<urn:uuid:78685936-5519-4576-8dfc-c42b1a9be833> a prov:Activity,
        slr:SystematicReview ;
    dcterms:title "Computation-based autism prediction from behavioral data: a systematic review of study design, conduct and reporting" ;
    prov:generatedAtTime "2026-08-15"^^xsd:date ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy [ a prov:Activity ;
            prov:wasAssociatedWith [ a prov:SoftwareAgent ;
                    schema1:name "SynthScholar" ] ] ;
    slr:assessed_eligibility 170 ;
    slr:assessed_on_abstract_only 0 ;
    slr:assessed_on_full_text 170 ;
    slr:duplicates_removed 1 ;
    slr:evidence_spans [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The prevailing belief in the literature was that facial attention differences are very clear in ASD, so we hypothesized that distraction ratios across an emotion recognition task alone were enough to distinguish ASD vs NC reliably. We now believe that integrating further subtlety through robust gaze-tracking hardware will be required to design a phenotyping marker from this effect." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.2196/13810> ;
            slr:claim_label "The authors explicitly revise the field assumption that facial attention differences in autism are clear enough to support reliable discrimination." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Using these techniques, we achieved an accuracy (area under the curve [AUC]) of 76% (SD 3%) and sensitivity of 76% (SD 4%) for identifying atypical children from among developmentally delayed children, and an accuracy (AUC) of 85% (SD 5%) and sensitivity of 76% (SD 6%) for identifying children with ASD from those predicted to have other developmental delays." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:5991dace03f8> ;
            slr:claim_label "The stacked rater-weighted classifiers achieved AUC 0.76 for atypical versus typical and AUC 0.85 for ASD versus other developmental delay." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "At this threshold, the averages of precision was 70%, sensitivity 87%, speciﬁcity 60%, error 25% and accuracy 88%." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1109/CBMS52027.2021.00062> ;
            slr:claim_label "The reported metrics are mutually inconsistent, with an error rate of 25% implying 75% accuracy against a reported 88%." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Noticeably, prior studies on IMC in autistic individuals often relied on expert ratings, potentially introducing biases and requiring substantial time. ... However, the issue of lack of objectivity remains if individuals' behavior is rated by experts." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:12accba36eb4> ;
            slr:claim_label "A sharper diagnosis than most papers here offer of what automation is for: machine-learning work that merely shortens ADOS or ADI-R addresses time cost while leaving the objectivity problem untouched, because the underlying observations remain expert-rated." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.87"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "YOLOv8 effectively identified non-human inputs such as a dog and a leaf, showcasing superior robustness and filtering capability. In contrast, YOLOv9 misclassified these irrelevant objects, predicting a dog as \"Autistic\" and a leaf as \"Nonautistic\"" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.47310/jpms2026150119> ;
            slr:claim_label "An out-of-distribution probe shows one model assigning an autism label to a photograph of a dog - the only test of a non-face input anywhere in the fifteen-study facial-image branch." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.99"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Joint attention −0.7883/p < 0.5" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/fnhum.2021.651349> ;
            slr:claim_label "Among seven gaze paradigms, joint attention showed no significant group difference, while unfamiliar own-race face viewing showed the largest." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The variables shoulder, hip, and trunk are important since they explain the balance status of children with high autistic trait." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:9c6671adbfae> ;
            slr:claim_label "SHAP attribution and model selection converge on upper-body and waist coordination with the base of support, giving a specific mechanistic reading rather than an undifferentiated balance deficit - and the conventional sway measures from prior literature performed less well than these novel correlation features." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.91"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The dataset used in the experiments consists of 42 subjects (21 ASD and 21 TD children) aged between 3 and 6 years. No statistical differences in age and gender are observed between ASD and TD groups." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/fnhum.2021.651349> ;
            slr:claim_label "The entire participant description is two group sizes, a shared age range and an unquantified assertion of age and sex matching, with no diagnostic information." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "MWL, computed using the number of letters, is not an accurate metric for predicting ASD due to the number of letters in a word not being strictly related to the complexity of the word." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:a9c2bcedd414> ;
            slr:claim_label "A negative feature result interpreted correctly rather than ignored - characters are the wrong unit for lexical complexity, and phonemes or morphemes would be the right one." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.85"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "It is important to highlight that, in the method evaluation, no information of subjects used for testing (\"Diagnosis phase\") is used in the learning phase, once the cross-validation was performed over the subjects." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-89023-8> ;
            slr:claim_label "Cross-validation was performed over subjects, with no test-participant information entering model learning." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Perceiver 10% 74.5% 73.7% 73.2% | Perceiver 20% 74.8% 73.5% 73.3% ... Perceiver 75.2% [5-fold]" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:0a8191e6e542> ;
            slr:claim_label "A stable mid-seventies figure across three evaluation configurations under subject-disjoint splitting - against the 81% to 99.8% reported by five Kaggle-corpus studies charted in this review under image-level splitting." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.94"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "as there are significant differences between children's and adults' gaze patterns [25], the success may not directly translate to ASD diagnosis among children." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:e51e9878eb2c> ;
            slr:claim_label "The authors caution that a 92%-accuracy adult eye-tracking result does not transfer to children, a caveat other papers citing the same work omit." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.8"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "A statistically more robust analysis based on the sampling of 10-min epochs from combined data of two episodes confirmed equivalent and high within-individual reliability in the ASD and TD groups (all correlations significantly different from zero; Spearman's ρ > 0.635, p < 0.001... Furthermore, the reliability estimates did not differ between the groups (p > 0.333" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1186/s13229-022-00517-2> ;
            slr:claim_label "Autistic gaze is as reliable within individuals as control gaze at every epoch duration tested, ruling out the noise explanation for group variability." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "On 500 epoch counts, the EDLM-ASDDFI approach has recognized 1279 samples as normal and 1350 samples as autism. Also, on 1000 epoch counts, the EDLM-ASDDFI models have recognized 1338 samples as normal and 1362 samples as autism." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.32604/cmc.2025.062266> ;
            slr:claim_label "The confusion-matrix counts account for essentially the whole stated dataset, indicating evaluation over all images rather than a held-out subset, and imply about 1,400 per class rather than the stated 2,926 total." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.98"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
