        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "in the medical context, interpretability is crucial because we are dealing with human lives. Therefore, it is not possible to make an unconditional judgment of which model is better." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:e15a1551c489> ;
            slr:claim_label "The authors decline to rank their models by accuracy, retaining an interpretable linear model on the ground that interpretability and accuracy are not commensurable in clinical use - the only such refusal in this corpus." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.93"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Previous efforts to build ASD-detection AI models showed promising results. However, due to a lack of automation, the requirements for specialized and expensive equipment, calibration, trained personnel, dependence on human rating of autistic behaviors, and low precision and recall, they are unsuitable for the development of ASD detection tools. The low precision and recall scores of these models may be due to a lack of targeted behavioral biomarkers that can readily discriminate ASD from TD." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:6131bfb6ef7c> ;
            slr:claim_label "An explicit critique of the prior computational literature and a statement of the study's alternative strategy - instrument a validated clinical construct rather than search for a statistical signature in available data." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.93"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The results obtained in the performed experiments are promising even if they show room for improvement." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:4c9af7f322c3> ;
            slr:claim_label "The authors present their results as preliminary with acknowledged room for improvement." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.8"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "To obtain the data to encode in task models in form of observation probabilities, we surveyed experienced ASD clinicians to provide their estimates of the rates in which some events in the diagnosis procedure occur." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:a21f317ce506> ;
            slr:claim_label "Observation probabilities were elicited from a survey of clinicians rather than estimated from data." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.85"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The Government Medical College, Guntur Ethics Committee, provided ethical approval for this study (Decision Reference Number: IRB-2024-0057). Written informed consent was obtained from all parents prior to their children's participation. Additionally, written informed consent was obtained from all participants involved in the study for both their participation and the publication of their details and any accompanying images." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:420caf43bd93> ;
            slr:claim_label "Two-tier consent covering both participation and publication of images places this study at the responsible end of the corpus's ethics gradient." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.92"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "All machine learning classiﬁers successfully identiﬁed ASD postural control patterns based on the COP features with high accuracy rates (>0.800). The naïve Bayes method was the optimal means to identify ASD postural control with the highest accuracy rate (0.900), speciﬁcity (1.000), precision (1.000), F1 score (0.898) and satisfactory sensitivity (0.826)." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:12377c334ffd> ;
            slr:claim_label "All six classifiers exceeded 0.800 accuracy, with naive Bayes reaching 0.900 accuracy, perfect specificity and precision, and 0.826 sensitivity." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "A significant limitation of this study lies in the small sample size... Additionally, the participants were selected based on convenience sampling, which may introduce selection bias" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:b4a900945ede> ;
            slr:claim_label "Sample size and convenience sampling are identified as threats to external validity, alongside four further named limitations." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Class/ASD Traits Boolean The class label represents whether ASD-related traits are observed, with a value of '0' indicating no traits present and '1' indicating that such traits are present" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3390/diagnostics15151859> ;
            slr:claim_label "The outcome is a questionnaire-derived Boolean, not a clinical diagnosis; no reference standard exists in the study." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.98"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Te outputs of the third hidden layer achieved the best test UAR of 44.06 %, which is a relative increase of 10.40 % over the Linear SVM baseline" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1145/3079452.3079492> ;
            slr:claim_label "With eGeMAPS features the best GAN representation reached 44.06% test UAR, a 10.4% relative gain over the linear SVM baseline." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Tao and Shyu used eye-gaze data from 28 children (5-12 years old) looking at 300 images and were able to achieve 74.22% accuracy on the validation set, although their performance on the testing set was reduced to 55.66% due to over-fitting problem." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1145/3539226> ;
            slr:claim_label "The paper's literature review identifies validation-to-test collapse from overfitting in prior scan-path work - a critique that applies with equal force to its own 100% training accuracy and image-level splitting." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.89"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "This threshold of 20 frames was deﬁned using the Youden method applied in the Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC)." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1109/CBMS52027.2021.00062> ;
            slr:claim_label "The decision threshold was fitted by Youden index on the same ROC curve used to characterise performance." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Therefore, the ASD subject age is very important for detecting the condition. Thus, selecting appropriate features for modeling ﬁxation behaviour of subjects require adaptation on basis of age. However, the proposed RM3ASD model does not include age information since this feature is not available in the given dataset." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:4c9af7f322c3> ;
            slr:claim_label "Age strongly moderates ASD fixation behaviour but was unavailable in the dataset, limiting the model." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "TU [39] 0.59 ... RM3ASD 0.59 ... SP-ASDNET_1 [40] 0.55 ... UCK_3 [41] 0.53" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:5908df2cffca> ;
            slr:claim_label "All eight competing methods on the shared Saliency4ASD test set fell between 0.53 and 0.59 accuracy." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "All the autistic children who participated in the test had a facial recognition rate of less than 60%. Therefore, if the accuracy rate of facial expression diagnosis by the system was less than 60%, the tester would have a tendency to suffer from autism. The lower the recognition rate, the higher the tendency of autism." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:0f2f3a5e7d29> ;
            slr:claim_label "The diagnostic rule is a 60% threshold on expression recognition rate, derived from the same twenty children on which it is reported." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The models multilayer perceptron and convolutional neural networks achieved 83% and 84% accuracy, respectively ... logistic regression, SVMs, K-NN, and random forests all achieved accuracies in the range 77%-80%" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:a9c2bcedd414> ;
            slr:claim_label "Modest, honestly reported accuracies benchmarked against three prior studies at 70-80% - a credible range for this task, and a useful calibration point against the 95-100% figures produced elsewhere in this review by leaked or confounded designs." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The classification accuracy reached 79.49% with only one feature: total session length." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.2196/29328> ;
            slr:claim_label "Total conversation length alone classified autism at 79.49%, exceeding what any single gaze feature achieved." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "To assess the performance and stability of the models, we employed a 5-fold nested cross-validation strategy combined with bootstrap-based 95% confidence intervals estimation. Each fold preserved class balance and ensured a participant-independent split." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1145/3806026> ;
            slr:claim_label "Nested cross-validation with participant-independent folds and bootstrap intervals - the most complete validation design in the charted corpus." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.99"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "DT ALL 0.938 0.939 0.938 0.938 2.223 Chi2 0.969 0.969 0.969 0.969 1.113" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.48084/etasr.12999> ;
            slr:claim_label "The log-loss column - the only calibration-sensitive measure in the charted corpus - exposes badly miscalibrated Decision Tree probabilities that accuracy alone would conceal." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.88"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "In the ablation studies, we find that the performance of using scan-path as input rivals those produced by more sophisticated methods that rely on both the scan-path and the input image stimuli. This is a surprising result as the scan-path itself contains no direct information about the visual stimuli." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:e51e9878eb2c> ;
            slr:claim_label "Scanpath-only classification matched scanpath-plus-image classification, contradicting the social-saliency premise of the approach." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.98"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The accuracy increases from 81.08% to 93.24% when we combined the HFA and the LFA groups into a single ASD group and performed binary classiﬁcation. Our study thus went a step further to mimic the real diagnosis situations where heterogeneity of ASD individuals poses a challenge" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:0e322379d4f9> ;
            slr:claim_label "Collapsing the two autistic subgroups raised accuracy from 81.08% to 93.24%, quantifying what binary classification gains by ignoring heterogeneity." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The academic authors LM, HM, PW, AM, PR, CG and JD-B are members of the trial steering and management committees and declare no financial interest in this product or the funding company, Harimata sp. z o.o. Coauthors AA and KS are board members of the Harimata sp. z o.o. that intends to commercialise the Play.Care assessment technology. AA and KS have options vesting in the company." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjopen-2018-026226> ;
            slr:claim_label "Commercial interest managed structurally rather than only disclosed - academic investigators holding no equity, company board members identified individually, a stated governance structure, and the analysis blinded to the developers." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.93"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Though restricted and repetitive behavior (RRB) is one of the cardinal symptoms of ASD, no study has been conducted to investigate whether restricted kinematic features (RKF) could be used to identify ASD." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:ea9c4425e2e9> ;
            slr:claim_label "The study is the first to test whether kinematic features derived from restricted and repetitive behaviour can identify ASD." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The data was collected after the Marmara University Medical Faculty Ethical Advisory Board Approval was obtained (Protocol code no: 09.2014.0194, reference: 70737436-050.06.04-140023995) Parental consent forms were signed by the parents of the children." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:f7f1df920e13> ;
            slr:claim_label "Ethics approval and parental consent were obtained and documented with protocol identifiers." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.75"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The features were extracted at the sample level and following the standard diagnostic tool for ASD - Autism Diagnostic Observation Schedule (ADOS). The features represented the stereotypic language behaviours exhibited in CwA like echolalia, inability to use pronouns and other semantic-pragmatic features including discourse coherence, extend of vocabulary, back and forth interchange in conversation, asking questions, building upon statements and so on." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:6de639c98328> ;
            slr:claim_label "Features are specified against a clinical instrument rather than selected empirically, so every measure has a clinical referent and model predictions can be explained in terms a clinician would recognise - the design principle underlying the study's interpretability claim." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.96"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "TD participants were recruited from various classes and without specific assessment of their pre-existing abilities. The only criterion for their selection was the absence reported by parents and teachers of neurodevelopmental disorders." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:03e7999e2993> ;
            slr:claim_label "The comparison group received no assessment of any kind, so in a fine-motor task with 3-6 year olds a general developmental explanation for the group difference cannot be excluded." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.92"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "the utterance is associated with the intonational phrase, the most fundamental unit of prosody argued to be universal across all languages" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:c6e9187abd76> ;
            slr:claim_label "The analytic unit was chosen for cross-linguistic commensurability, in contrast to the language-specific syllabic units used in the group's prior single-language work." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.85"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Ten normal children and ten autistic children were recruited for the comparative test to verify the accuracy of the system.After testing, the accuracy of facial expression recognition of this system reaches 81.4%." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:0f2f3a5e7d29> ;
            slr:claim_label "The abstract presents 81.4% - the expression recognition rate in typically developing children - as the system's accuracy, on a twenty-child evaluation." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Data from all participants were used to validate the model; therefore, the reported results correspond to the cross-validation process." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1002/aur.2636> ;
            slr:claim_label "The authors state that no independent sample was tested and all reported figures derive from cross-validation on the full dataset." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "All these data were then aggregated to find the average values for each task. The final dataset consisted of the mean value for all the features (Table 1) divided per task (five difficulty levels). The participants performed 25 tasks, 5 for each difficulty level, so our final dataset was composed by 1,500 x 12 observations. The target dataset is instead a simple vector of 1500 x 1 labels where the diagnosis of the participants is reported, 0 for TD participants and 1 for ASD." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:06efef346587> ;
            slr:claim_label "The modelled dataset repeats each child's diagnosis across 25 task rows, so the 1,500 observations are not independent - this is the structure that makes observation-level cross-validation leaky." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.98"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "we found that the significant group differences can be observed with epochs sampled from the video that are as short as 2 min" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1186/s13229-022-00517-2> ;
            slr:claim_label "Moderate group differences and above-chance individual identification both survive down to 2-minute samples, supporting brief naturalistic protocols." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.85"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "If this risk control measure were removed, the sensitivity for all study completers would fall to 51.6% (63/122) (95% CI 42.4%, 60.8%), and specificity would fall to 18.5% (56/303) (95% CI 14.3%, 23.3%)." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-022-00598-6> ;
            slr:claim_label "The authors report what performance would be without the abstention mechanism, quantifying how much of the headline accuracy depends on declining to classify two thirds of cases." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.98"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The number of distinct word roots contributes the most toward the diagnosis status of the child, indicating that the extent of vocabulary is an effective indicator in distinguishing CwA from TD. This is consistent with the previous literature exploring the linguistic behaviour in CwA." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:6de639c98328> ;
            slr:claim_label "The single largest SHAP contributor is a canonical expressive-vocabulary index - which is consistent with the autism literature the authors cite, and equally consistent with the autistic group extending down to 12 months while the comparison group starts at 3 years." ;
