        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The study utilizes an open-source movement dataset available via Mendeley Data, comprising 44 examples evenly distributed between individuals diagnosed with ASD (22 examples) and non-autistic individuals (22 examples)." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1007/s40747-025-01790-3> ;
            slr:claim_label "The entire empirical basis is 44 examples from an uncited public dataset, with no demographic or diagnostic information reported." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.97"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "In general, the first proposed system using the FFNN and ANN classifiers achieved the best performance amongst the proposed systems." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics11040530> ;
            slr:claim_label "Hand-engineered texture descriptors outperformed fine-tuned deep networks, consistent with the signal residing in low-level plot morphology." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.85"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "With only around 20 above-threshold samples and highly imbalanced sample overall, we were forced to use LOOCV to avoid polarization of prediction toward the Low autistic trait group." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:9c6671adbfae> ;
            slr:claim_label "The class imbalance is acknowledged and drives the choice of validation scheme - and at 15.1% prevalence the majority-class baseline is 84.9%, against which the reported 1.000 should be read, though the paper does not compute it." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.92"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "while larger f0 range has been repeatedly reported in English-speaking ASD groups, Thongseitratch and colleagues found a lower f0 range in the Thai-speaking ASD group. A lower f0 range associated with ASD was also found in a study of Japanese speakers." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:c6e9187abd76> ;
            slr:claim_label "Prior f0 findings conflict across languages, which the cross-linguistic design is built to resolve." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "since our proposed models are relatively lightweight, they could feasibly be deployed at home on mobile devices, allowing for private offline symptom detection as well as privacy-preserving federated learning approaches" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:44d2304739ef> ;
            slr:claim_label "Privacy is addressed substantively through proposed on-device inference and federated learning, motivated by autism's stigmatisation." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.8"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Non ASD 74 97 84 33 78 ASD 92 50 65 22 Macro average 83 73 74 55" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:bb59da535587> ;
            slr:claim_label "The support column reveals a 55-image test set (33 non-ASD, 22 ASD) - the only place the evaluation size appears - placing roughly plus or minus 12 points of sampling noise on every reported figure." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.98"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The participants ranged in age from 2 to 15 years" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:b694f7dc9504> ;
            slr:claim_label "The sample spans thirteen years of childhood with no group-specific ages, tests or covariate adjustment reported." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.85"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Out of 148 ASD instances and 144 non-ASD instances, all were classified correctly, yielding zero false positives and zero false negatives. This indicated that the model attained 100% sensitivity and 100% specificity, confirming its suitability for reliable clinical screening applications." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.48084/etasr.12999> ;
            slr:claim_label "A flawless 292-of-292 classification is read as confirmation of clinical suitability rather than as a diagnostic of the design." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.98"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "we have separated all the images and the associated scanpaths into two groups: the training group has 240 images and the associated 5542 scanpaths while the testing group has 60 images with 1411 scanpaths." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:10ab04b596b1> ;
            slr:claim_label "The internal split was partitioned by image rather than by participant." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "the specificity of the combined model was maintained at the same level as that of the language model, i.e. the addition of voice features did not increase misclassification among non-ASD children, despite that modality's tendency towards false positives." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1002/aur.2733> ;
            slr:claim_label "Adding voice raised sensitivity from 0.77 to 0.90 without importing the voice model's false-positive tendency, the structural signature of genuine complementarity." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Based on the above, the top two features of importance are face 12 (disgust) and face 21 (anger), but we do not think that feature importance has any particular significance. These results suggest that there is no difference in Sz by facial expression, but the face gaze scanning pattern may be different from that of the control group." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2023.952433> ;
            slr:claim_label "The authors decline to interpret a feature-importance ranking because the independent statistical analysis does not support it - a restraint absent elsewhere in this corpus, and one that makes the two interpretations they do offer more credible." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.93"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "diagnosing ASD often requires speech-based tests. However, at the early detection stage, many young children may not yet be able to speak, making the diagnosis more challenging." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:3316ae46ffa0> ;
            slr:claim_label "Identifies a genuine coverage gap that the language-based studies in this review cannot address: movement-based measurement reaches pre-verbal and non-speaking children, who are precisely the population early detection targets." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.91"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The model accurately identified 131 children with ASD as True Positives (TP) and correctly recognized 136 children without ASD as True Negatives (TN). It misclassified 9 children without ASD as having ASD (False Positives, FP) and incorrectly identified 4 children with ASD as not having it (False Negatives, FN)." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:ef9df8726301> ;
            slr:claim_label "The confusion matrix sums to 280 images, matching neither the stated 200-image validation set nor the 100-image test set - an unexplained discrepancy that leaves the denominator of the headline accuracy undetermined." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.94"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The data generated and analyzed in this study indeed include videos and audio recordings that contain identifiable features such as faces and voices of participants, and as such cannot be shared integrally. To facilitate open science and reproducibility, we however share a de-identified subsample of pose-estimation videos." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:dac3e7a03f26> ;
            slr:claim_label "A considered middle position between unrestricted sharing of identifiable child data and blanket unavailability, made possible by the study's own skeletal representation - with training code additionally published on GitHub." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.87"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "we split the data according to speaker rather than samples, to ensure that samples from each child belong to one fold only (training, development, or testing sets). This step is initiated to prevent the model from over-fitting by learning speaker or recording idiosyncrasies, and therefore deceptively excelling." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2025.1274675> ;
            slr:claim_label "The clearest statement of the leakage principle in this review, correctly implemented - and a demonstration that speaker disjointness does not address a confound operating at the class level, since a model that has learned clinic acoustics transfers across any speaker boundary." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.96"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Diagnoses were confirmed (ASD group) or ruled out (TD group) using the Clinical Best Estimate process [11] informed by the Autism Diagnostic Observation Schedule – Second Edition (ADOS-2; [5]) and adhering to DSM-V criteria for ASD [12]. Groups were matched on Full Scale IQ estimates (Wechsler Abbreviated Scale of Intelligence – 2nd Edition; [13]), verbal and nonverbal IQ estimates, and sex ratio" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:80626146cad2> ;
            slr:claim_label "Diagnosis was established by Clinical Best Estimate with ADOS-2 under DSM-5 for both confirming ASD and ruling it out in controls, with groups matched on three IQ measures and sex ratio." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "On the held-out test set drawn from the ABIDE-I dataset, a 20% split with about 247 subjects, the constructed system achieved an accuracy of 99.40%, precision of 100%, recall of 98.84%, an F1-score of 99.42%, and an ROC-AUC of 99.99%." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3390/diagnostics15172232> ;
            slr:claim_label "A ROC-AUC of 99.99% for autism diagnosis is a figure that should prompt a search for leakage; here the leakage is identifiable in the paper's own feature ranking, and the subject count matches neither of the two split descriptions given." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.96"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "We used the data of our 24 TD children and 26 HFA children to train the KNN model and used the data of the 24 LFA children as the test data. We found that the classiﬁer could correctly label these LFA children as children with ASD with an accuracy of 100%. On the other hand, if we used the data of our 24 TD children and 24 LFA children as the training set and used the data of the 26 HFA children as the test set, the overall accuracy dropped to 53.85%." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:0e322379d4f9> ;
            slr:claim_label "Cross-group transfer was strongly asymmetric - a model trained on the harder contrast generalised perfectly to unseen severely affected children, while the reverse transfer performed near chance." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The IJA-based model showed an AUROC of 90.3% (95% CI, 88.8%-91.8%) ... The low-level RJA-based model showed an AUROC of 84.4% ... The high-level RJA-based model showed an AUROC of 84.2%" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:6131bfb6ef7c> ;
            slr:claim_label "Severity prediction is markedly weaker than detection and shows a clear task ordering favouring initiation over response - but the three classes include non-autism, so the figure substantially re-solves the detection problem and no within-autism grading result is reported." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.94"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "ADOS was administered to all participants in both groups to corroborate clinical diagnosis and capture variation of symptoms severity across the ASD group." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cognition.2021.104652> ;
            slr:claim_label "The gold-standard diagnostic instrument was applied to both groups, with individual scores tabulated per participant." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "As shown in Fig. 14(a), the more participants there are, the more unclear and atypical cases there are, and it is more challenging to identify children with ASD. When the number of subjects is less than 100, the test cannot accurately reflect the performance of the model." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:5e6c6c482e7c> ;
            slr:claim_label "An explicit methodological criticism of the small-sample norm in this literature, supported by visualisation from a 333-child dataset - directly relevant to interpreting the many sub-100-participant studies charted in this review." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
