        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Our Model 91 94 88 95.67 ... Xception [23] 92.01 90.97 - 96.30 ... MobileNet-V1 [30] 90.67 90.67 90.67 90.70" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.22266/ijies2025.0430.21> ;
            slr:claim_label "On a 200-image test set the 95% interval around 91% spans roughly 86-95%, containing nearly every comparator, so the reported ranking is not statistically supported." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.93"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Between the two modes, mean value from EEG is related to all other eye-tracking features... Synchronization feature is related to exotic face, the 11th and 12th features (joint attention, whether there was gaze following), and the 13th feature (native strange face 1)." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/fnhum.2021.651349> ;
            slr:claim_label "The covariance graph reveals specific cross-modal relationships between EEG features and particular gaze paradigms despite asynchronous recording." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The dataset consists of a total of 1500 images from 30 male children aged between 5 and 15 years. The images are categorized as follows: 500 images captured from 10 students during outdoor play, 600 images taken within the classroom setting involving 12 students, 200 images recorded in the dining area from 4 students, and 200 images taken on the school veranda from 4 students." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:c846224c5959> ;
            slr:claim_label "Fifty images per child across four physically different thermal environments, with no severity-by-setting breakdown - so ambient temperature and exertion are confounded with the label, and the effective sample is 30 rather than 1,500." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.94"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The idea of use an human-robot interaction (HRI) to diagnose individuals with autism spectrum disorders (ASD) among preschool children implies the need of close cooperation between psychologists and engineers." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:9a63d236d24c> ;
            slr:claim_label "Frames autism assessment as a joint psychology-engineering design problem, with user requirements and evaluation criteria delivered as a co-equal research output - a stance that treats the autistic child as a user rather than only as a data source." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.88"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "This should be achieved by having close and extensive cooperation with the target users, including children, psychiatrists/psychologists and therapists." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:55050e1dc1bd> ;
            slr:claim_label "Names children themselves among the users whose cooperation is required to improve the paradigm - the only participatory-design commitment anywhere in this corpus." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.89"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The dataset was divided into 80% for training and validation, 80%:20%, respectively (350:88 images), and 20% for testing (109 images)." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics11040530> ;
            slr:claim_label "The partition operates on images with no participant grouping, so images from the same child span training and test." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.98"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "However, given the relatively high performance on participant metadata (age and gender), we could not conclude that gaze and expression recognition features enhance classification." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.2196/13810> ;
            slr:claim_label "The authors decline to claim that gaze and emotion features add classification value, because the demographic baseline performed comparably." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "parents consented to share their videos with our research team and completed a survey to provide the age, sex, and diagnostic status of their children." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:b694f7dc9504> ;
            slr:claim_label "Diagnostic status rests on unverified parent report, not acknowledged as a limitation in this paper." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "It is worth noting that our developed method has the following limitations. On the one hand, to deploy our model in practice, multimodal data from EEG and ET modalities must be available simultaneously. On the other hand, our model is a two-step approach containing three SDAE models, which requires much more computational costs" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:218fb0c15541> ;
            slr:claim_label "The stated limitations are two engineering constraints; no threat to validity is raised." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.85"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "We collected the audio data of 39 infants who were assessed using seven multiple instruments, consisting of (1) ADOS, second edition (ADOS-2), (2) the autism diagnostic interview, revised (ADI-R), (3) the behavior development screening for toddlers interview (BeDevel-I), (4) the behavior development screening for toddlers play (BeDevel-P), (5) the Korean version of the childhood autism rating scale (K-CARS) reﬁned from CARS-2, (6) the social communication questionnaire (SCQ), and (7) the social responsiveness scale (SRS)" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:63f4f1af3671> ;
            slr:claim_label "All 39 infants were assessed with seven diagnostic and severity instruments." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The ASD group comprised 10 children (mean age: 4 years +/- 0.94 SD) diagnosed with ASD. The TD group included 10 typically developing children (mean age: 3 years and 9 months +/- 0.1SD)." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:03e7999e2993> ;
            slr:claim_label "Twenty children in total - the smallest sample among included studies - with the autistic group's age standard deviation nearly ten times the comparison group's, so the groups differ markedly in developmental spread despite similar means." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.89"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The selection of the stimuli and the gradual exposition to the three stimuli conditions depended on the hyper-and-hypo sensitivities to sensory stimuli of the ASD population." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3390/jcm9051260> ;
            slr:claim_label "The cumulative sensory stimulus design was motivated directly by documented autistic hyper- and hypo-sensitivities." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.85"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Our best-performing classiﬁer achieved 0.75 classiﬁcation accuracy for a combination of selected web pages using all gaze features." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1145/3192714.3192819> ;
            slr:claim_label "The best classifier reached 0.75 accuracy using gaze features from selected web pages." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The comparison demonstrates significant data loss in the ASD group when \"minLength = 4\". ... we can conclude that the increase in BG difference is mainly attributable to using complete time information instead of filtering out short data." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:5e6c6c482e7c> ;
            slr:claim_label "A preprocessing threshold is analysed specifically because filtering by pattern length would preferentially discard autistic children's data, since autistic children produce fewer fixations - recognition of an interaction between preprocessing and the group difference being measured that is almost absent elsewhere in this corpus." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.93"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The CPSD has 13 female participants making 334 samples and 54 males making 1,388 samples" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2025.1274675> ;
            slr:claim_label "Sixty-seven participants in a corpus whose control group is elsewhere stated as 64 children, leaving three for all three atypical classes. The counts cannot both be right, the per-class child counts are never given, and this determines how much protection the speaker-disjoint split actually provides." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.91"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Some of the first systems developed to assist ASD therapists and make diagnosis based on robotic devices have primarily been open loop and remotely operated systems. However, these approaches are unable to perform autonomous feedback to enhance the interaction" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1007/s10846-018-00975-y> ;
            slr:claim_label "An accurate diagnosis of the gap in prior robot-assisted work, and the design brief for the closed-loop architecture that couples measurement to robot behaviour." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.87"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "one of the major drawbacks of using facial images is the invasion of privacy, especially for minors, and the need to collect their images over prolonged periods of their childhood" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:8f4dcae4a760> ;
            slr:claim_label "A direct published critique of the facial-image approach on privacy grounds, applying to the sixteen facial-image studies charted in this review." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.96"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "they may lack some critical acoustic details, such as prosodic features, which are often indicative of atypical speech characteristics in individuals with ASD... which are difficult to capture solely through textual representation." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:8f4dcae4a760> ;
            slr:claim_label "The information cost of the privacy-motivated modality choice is acknowledged rather than elided." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "the development of a Hybrid WQPSO with FMRCNN for improving the recognition of altered facial expressions in individuals with ASD ... where nuanced facial cues may be misinterpreted or overlooked" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.18280/ts.410628> ;
            slr:claim_label "The conclusion states an autism-specific achievement for a study in which no autistic person's face was analysed - the clearest case in the review of autism framing attached to work containing no autism data." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.98"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The absence of statistically significant differences in our McNemar test results does not definitively indicate the absence of a performance difference between models, particularly in the context of small sample sizes. This aligns with broader discussions on statistical power and interpreting non-significant results in research." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:e15a1551c489> ;
            slr:claim_label "Model comparisons are tested formally with McNemar and non-significance is explicitly not read as equivalence - a statistical discipline absent everywhere else in this review, where model rankings resting on one or two participants are routinely presented as findings." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Compared with the TD children, the children with ASD showed deﬁcits in responding to directional cues with varying levels of social components, suggesting that their deﬁcits in visual orienting are not only tied to social cues but appear to be domain-general." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:0e322379d4f9> ;
            slr:claim_label "Visual-orienting deficits appeared for non-social as well as social cues, supporting a domain-general rather than social-specific account." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "To avoid incorporating information from the distribution of the training data into the test set (i.e., \"data leakage\"), we carried out the following preprocessing steps separately for each test-train split of the data. The steps included, in order: outlier removal, imputation, standardization, upsampling." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:902ddbc85707> ;
            slr:claim_label "Leakage is named explicitly and all preprocessing including imputation and SMOTE is performed inside each fold rather than on the full dataset." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "NB 0.733 0.500 0.621 0.442 0.733 0.267 0.733 0.267 ... SVM 0.733 0.500 0.621 0.442 0.733 0.267 0.733 0.267" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1109/ICREST51555.2021.9331152> ;
            slr:claim_label "Two classifiers in cluster 1 return identical metrics with 26.7% specificity and exactly 0.500 AUC, the signature of single-class prediction, which is not flagged." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Therefore, the atypical oculomotor performance in the children with ASD was inseparable from visual attention in our study. Future investigations are encouraged to separate the inherent oculomotor function from visual attention deficits in unconstrained viewing settings (e.g., face-to-face interactions)." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1007/s10803-022-05685-x> ;
            slr:claim_label "The authors concede that the construct their study was designed to isolate - oculomotor behaviour as distinct from visual attention - is confounded in their data." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.91"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "511 speech data of ASD children and 506 data of normal children were randomly selected from the available data, of which 80% of the data were used as the training set and 20% of the data were used as the test set" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:ceb7a2194e61> ;
            slr:claim_label "The split is over recordings rather than children, with more than 80 autistic and 60 comparison children contributing roughly six utterances each." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "the features utilized for classiﬁcation are still highly subjective and not independent of the respective clinician who bases the diagnostic decision on just those features (circularity). Importantly, when using subjectively inﬂuenced data, resulting classiﬁcation algorithms must be validated in an independent sample in order to prevent circularity." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/frobt.2019.00132> ;
            slr:claim_label "Studies training on clinician-derived diagnostic features are criticised for circularity and require independent-sample validation." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The average recognition rates of the seven expressions from test child 1 to test child 10 were 28.5%, 28.5%, 28.5%, 42.9%, 42.9%, 57.1%, 28.5%, 42.9%, 28.5%, 14.3%, and the average recognition rate is 34.3%." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:0f2f3a5e7d29> ;
            slr:claim_label "Mean expression recognition rate in the autistic group was 34.3%, with per-child rates from 14.3% to 57.1%." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The classification performance remained similar, indicating that the PositiveEnergy' was not powerful enough to add additional value to the proposed integrated entropy. In fact, while using the affect data as the only input to the classifier, the performance was not up to the mark as it was while using only the integrated entropy as an input." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:665e8c7a855d> ;
            slr:claim_label "A discriminant-validity test rather than an accuracy chase: the complexity measure and the affect measure derived from the same video are shown by correlation, classification and odds ratios to carry largely independent information, with complexity the stronger." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.92"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "the binary variables A1_Score to A10_Score in the toddler dataset hold responses to the ASD screening questions based on the Quantitative Checklist for Autism in Toddlers (Q-CHAT-10) while the other three datasets were based on the Autism Spectrum Quotient (AQ-10) screening tool" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:74a48b2cecd0> ;
