        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "due to camera motion and variation in the location of the smartphone relative to the parent's face, the gaze fixation maps are difficult to interpret qualitatively, and AOIs cannot be definitely matched to a parent's specific facial regions." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:b694f7dc9504> ;
            slr:claim_label "The authors concede that the measurement frame prevents mapping gaze regions to facial features, so the significant finding cannot be connected to eye contact or any other construct." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "We have collected and analyzed data from 39 autistic Chinese school children (29 boys and 10 girls; age ranges from 4 to 10) in Shanghai Putuo District Zhanyi Children Cultivation Service Center and 53 normal Chinese school children (27 boys and 26 girls; age ranges from 2 to 15)." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:7df517928741> ;
            slr:claim_label "The entire participant description is group sizes, sexes and age ranges, with no diagnostic instrument and markedly different age spans between groups." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Limitations of this study include: landmarks and affect detection were based on algorithms trained primarily on adults, although our previous work showed it was still reliable for toddlers; other measures of complexity might be more robust than the MSE in their ability to discriminate children with and without ASD; and the study sample, while relatively large, still has a limited number of ASD participants and did not have sufficient power to determine the impact of demographic characteristics on the results." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:665e8c7a855d> ;
            slr:claim_label "Three accurate limitations, including an implicit acknowledgement of the unadjusted demographic imbalances - though the interaction between adult-trained detectors and the 30-point racial composition difference is not drawn out." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.89"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The classification accuracy reached a maximum accuracy of 96.7 % (specificity 93.8 % and sensitivity 100 %) by using seven features selected by the Fisher discriminant ratio-based technique. Overall mean accuracy, specificity, and sensitivity rates were also calculated over a number of selected features ranging from one to 17 (the whole number of features). The overall mean classification accuracy (specificity/sensitivity) was 84.9 % (mean specificity 89.1 % and mean sensitivity 82.2 %)." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:fb313da99be3> ;
            slr:claim_label "The headline 96.7% is a maximum across feature-subset sizes; the mean across all subset sizes is 84.9%." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Diagnostic classification was conducted externally by qualified specialists (neuropediatricians or clinical psychologists) prior to inclusion in the study." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1145/3806026> ;
            slr:claim_label "Labels are clinician-made and independent of the gaze data, unlike the checklist thresholds and folder labels that supply outcomes elsewhere in this review." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.94"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Males 194 (48.9%) / 31 (77.5%) ... White/Caucasian 308 (77.7%) / 19 (47.5%) ... Hispanic/Latino 29 (7.3%) / 13 (32.5%) ... Age in months 20.62 3.21 / 24.21 4.72" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:665e8c7a855d> ;
            slr:claim_label "Three substantial demographic differences between groups - 3.6 months of age, 29 points of sex ratio, 30 points of racial composition - all reported plainly and none adjusted for, in a study that runs ANCOVA for two other covariates." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.94"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "After performing a pilot study, involving 6 ASD children, out of a total of 11 considered in the clinical setup, we conducted a long-term study. This study empirically proves that the proposed assessment system represents the attention state of the patient with 82.4% accuracy." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1007/s12369-020-00639-8> ;
            slr:claim_label "The 82.4% is agreement between the automated attention trend and the therapist's ratings across 34 sessions, not a diagnostic accuracy - and it is computed over sessions clustered within six children, with 5 of 11 children unaccounted for." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.93"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The reason might be that the CNN needs a large number of labeled data for training, while the existing dataset is still small. One of our main tasks in future is to further enrich the dataset." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:ceb7a2194e61> ;
            slr:claim_label "Dataset size is the only limitation acknowledged, and only as an explanation for the CNN's weaker performance." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.8"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "the comparisons of Sensitivity and Specificity show that the proposed identification model is more likely to make an inaccurate judgment for TD children than ASD children... One possible reason is that some TD children may also have become distracted and not been able to complete the entire experiment. As a result, they may generate meaningless eye movement." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1145/3440067.3440078> ;
            slr:claim_label "Errors concentrate on typically developing children, which the authors attribute to distraction producing uninformative gaze data." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The lowest sensitivity was obtained in the 5-6 a old groups, as some children carried out systematic rehabilitation training to help them pay attention to the social information in scenes, with decreasing scores. Therefore, the model is more suitable for the initial screening of children who have not undergone rehabilitation training." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:5e6c6c482e7c> ;
            slr:claim_label "The measured signal is erased by intervention: autistic children trained to attend to social information score lower and are missed more often, which means the proposed AI-marker indexes something modifiable rather than a fixed trait." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.98"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "This augmentation stage is carried out through coding, and the study does not record the total number of augmentations applied in the code." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:49963b028b7f> ;
            slr:claim_label "The volume of augmented data entering training was not tracked, so the effective training-set size is unknown." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.86"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "In the test experiment, with the help of the Shenzhen Autism Society (SAS), we recruited 22 participants already diagnosed with ASD. All these participants were young males (average age = 12.68 ± 7.61 years)." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1111/jcap.12346> ;
            slr:claim_label "The test sample was all male with a mean age of 12.68 years, against a training sample averaging 24.84 years and evenly split by sex." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Decision Trees 11,229 8,690 2,540 41 0.72 61.2%" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:7d280d7f3a95> ;
            slr:claim_label "The decision tree recorded 41 false negatives against 11,229 true positives, meaning it assigned nearly every tweet to the autistic class - 99.6% sensitivity at 22.6% specificity, behaviour that its reported 61.2% accuracy and 0.72 F1 conceal and that only the published confusion counts reveal." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.93"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The model with a k-fold cross-validation provides a steady accuracy of 0.9284 +/- 0.0039-0.9294 +/- 0.0038, which means that the model is robust and consistent across the folds." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/fncom.2026.1626315> ;
            slr:claim_label "Per-fold standard deviations below 0.004 over clips from 75 videos are read as robustness, when near-identical fold results are equally consistent with folds containing the same recordings." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Upon reviewing the performance of all classifiers across multiple feature subsets, it was observed that the results obtained were not entirely satisfactory." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1088/2631-8695/addb0b> ;
            slr:claim_label "An unusually candid assessment in a literature where every reported result is framed as a success." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.85"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The study also shows that even if traditional parametric statistics methods show differences between groups with large effect sizes this does not necessarily translate to high ML classiﬁcation performance." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:61028ef44814> ;
            slr:claim_label "Large group-level effect sizes did not translate into high individual-level classification accuracy." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "parents seem to have high acceptance of eye tracking as part of the clinical evaluation because the visual results are easier to understand than is the ADI cutoff, for example. In fact, for some parents, a lack of an objective measure can lead to delayed or diminished acceptance of the clinical diagnosis." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.2196/27706> ;
            slr:claim_label "Objective visual measures are argued to aid parental acceptance of a clinical diagnosis, not only clinician decision-making." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.85"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Especially, for ASD with its highly heterogeneous phenotype, leave-one-out creates overly variable test sets, rendering model outcomes unstable" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/frobt.2019.00132> ;
            slr:claim_label "Leave-one-out cross-validation is argued to produce unstable model outcomes given autism's phenotypic heterogeneity." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The studies involving humans were approved by The Child Pathological Speech Database and De-Engima Project administrations ... Written informed consent for participation was not required from the participants or the participants' legal guardians/next of kin" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2025.1274675> ;
            slr:claim_label "Approval attributed to dataset custodians rather than a research ethics committee, and no consent for secondary use of recorded child speech - in a paper whose introduction reflects at length on ethics boards as safeguards for exactly this kind of data." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.85"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The authors do not have permission to share data." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:0a8191e6e542> ;
            slr:claim_label "The new continuous arousal and valence annotations for autistic children, presented in the abstract as a dataset contribution, cannot be obtained - removing the principal reusable output of the work." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.84"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "This was seen in our combination of the objective measures with the AQ, where predictive accuracy rose to 92%... This is a substantial improvement upon both subjective (83% max accuracy for the matched sample) and objective measures (74% max accuracy for the matched sample) alone in our dataset" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1038/s41398-026-03823-y> ;
            slr:claim_label "All three predictor configurations are reported, so the increment attributable to multimodal fusion (9 points over the questionnaire alone) is visible and checkable." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.96"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "It should be noted that feature normalization is ﬁrst performed on the training set and accordingly, the testing set is then rescaled according to the maximum and the minimum value of the training set" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:218fb0c15541> ;
            slr:claim_label "Normalisation parameters were fitted on training data only, avoiding a common source of optimistic bias." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "This study has some limitations, including the small sample size and a statistically significant difference in IQ between children with ASD and TD. IQ affects one's ability to perform behavioral tasks; however, joint attention is a strong trait differentiating ASD from TD even after controlling for IQ. External validation using a larger sample of IQ-matched cohorts with ASD and TD is still warranted." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:6131bfb6ef7c> ;
            slr:claim_label "The IQ confound is acknowledged candidly, correctly diagnosed as affecting task performance, and paired with an appropriate remedy - though no adjustment is attempted in the present analysis." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "adding age and gender to the model did not improve the performance and adding IQ-related measures rather worsened the performance. This seems to be because participants in our data were purposefully matched on age, gender, and IQ." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:80626146cad2> ;
            slr:claim_label "Demographic and IQ variables did not improve and IQ worsened model performance, consistent with the groups being matched on them." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
