            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Examining the videos of toddlers with high levels of head movements in the present study revealed that the movement involves not just the head but also the upper body including trunk and shoulders, which were not captured by our computer vision algorithm, as we focused solely on the face in this study." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:dbb27be8e821> ;
            slr:claim_label "The authors report that the measured signal may reflect whole-body postural instability rather than head movement specifically." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "ASD diagnosis was a good clinical estimate, as neither the ADOS-2 nor the Autism Diagnostic Interview-Revised (ADI-R) was employed in the assessment process." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:aa6615a55671> ;
            slr:claim_label "The reference standard was deliberately kept independent of the index test, which is what makes the algorithm's disagreement with clinical judgement interpretable rather than circular - the failure mode this review documents in studies whose labels derive from their own inputs." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.97"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "A total of 35 children (including 9 TD children, 18 children diagnosed with ASD, and 8 at-risk children who are younger siblings of ASD children, but have not been diagnosed, e.g., due to their young age) in the United States participated in the data collection. ... The ages of the subjects range from 3 months to 17 years at the time of recruitment." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1145/3233547.3233574> ;
            slr:claim_label "The sample comprised 18 ASD, 9 TD and 8 at-risk children aged from 3 months to 17 years at recruitment." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "A variety of devices and recording locations such as homes (12.90% ASD sample, 45.16% TD sample), autism centers (87.10% ASD sample) and health centers (54.84% TD sample) were used to avoid bias and increase generalizability." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1007/s10803-025-06811-1> ;
            slr:claim_label "Recording setting is strongly correlated with class - nearly nine in ten autistic cries from autism centres against a comparison group split between health centres and homes - and is presented as a bias-reducing design choice. All three analysed measures are sensitive to room acoustics and background noise." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.98"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "In the classiﬁcation of ASD (40 samples) and its comparison group of TD (43 samples), each subject within these 83 samples was used for testing the model that was trained on the rest 82 samples." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/fped.2020.00290> ;
            slr:claim_label "Model evaluation used leave-one-out cross-validation over 83 samples." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Our approach was evaluated using a leave-one-subject-out protocol, in which one subject is used for testing and the rest of the subjects are used for training." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:2859eee06613> ;
            slr:claim_label "Evaluation used leave-one-subject-out cross-validation." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.85"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The dataset of face images for ASD was obtained from Kaggle and data from an autism center in Semarang, with 1.468 labeled as ASD and 1.468 labeled as normal." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:7663a08834eb> ;
            slr:claim_label "The only study in the branch to mix the public web-scraped corpus with clinic-sourced images - and it reports neither the proportion from each source nor their distribution across classes, leaving open whether the addition reduces the provenance confound or aligns ancestry, camera and setting with the label." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.97"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "the classifier could discriminate ASD from TD in toddlers with an accuracy of 80%, a sensitivity of 80%, and a specificity of 82%; and could discriminate ASD from TD in preschoolers with an accuracy of 71%, a sensitivity of 81%, and a specificity of 61%." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyt.2022.899521> ;
            slr:claim_label "Classification was better in the younger cohort, and the preschool model's specificity of 61% would flag nearly two in five typically developing children." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.85"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "In addition, greater mediolateral COP sample entropy was observed during the eyes-closed conditions compared to the eyes-open conditions. No difference was found in anteroposterior COP sample entropy." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:12377c334ffd> ;
            slr:claim_label "Sample entropy complexity measures differed by visual condition but did not differentiate the diagnostic groups, contrary to prior reports of reduced complexity in ASD." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "A total of 2,866 photographs were collected of children with and without autism. These pictures were taken from websites and Facebook pages that included autism-related content." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:e03dbcffcbc9> ;
            slr:claim_label "The paper states its own confound: the autism class is defined by having been harvested from autism-related web content, so the label records image provenance rather than any property of the child. This is the fifth study in the review built on this corpus and the first to describe the harvesting so explicitly." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.98"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Inclusion criteria were as follows: (i) diagnosis of ASD according to both DSM-V clinical criteria and to the Autism Diagnostic Observation Schedule (ADOS-2) (Lord et al., 2012), (ii) age range between 5 and 10 years, and (iii) no existing comorbidities." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:06efef346587> ;
            slr:claim_label "A dual-criterion reference standard combining DSM-5 with ADOS-2 - among the strongest in this corpus - though comorbidity exclusion narrows the population and ADOS-2 scores are never reported." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.92"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Due to data augmentation, the original dataset was increased by 1300% in size in both classes." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:3dc286a9aa92> ;
            slr:claim_label "The dataset was expanded thirteenfold by flipping, translation and blurring before evaluation." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Inclusion criteria were: diagnostic criteria for ASD by Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM) 5, CARS greater than 30 and stable condition (no medication or no change for at least three months). Exclusion criteria were: calibration problems, eye tracking record rate less than 80% and age over 17 years." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1109/CBMS52027.2021.00062> ;
            slr:claim_label "Inclusion required DSM-5 diagnosis, CARS above 30 and three months of clinical stability, with data quality gated at an 80% record rate." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.85"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Declarations. Ethical Approval. Not required." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:c846224c5959> ;
            slr:claim_label "The entire ethics statement for a study that prospectively thermally imaged 30 disabled children aged 5 to 15 at a named residential special school, with no consent or assent statement anywhere, in work proposing to automate their psychiatric medication dosing." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.99"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "For Severe ASD, the dataset contains 9 video recordings (Samsung Note 9) without Kinect V2 data. Creators could not set up Kinect V2 in controlled environments for severe cases, so they collected videos in clinical settings." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyt.2026.1751654> ;
            slr:claim_label "The depth-sensor modality proposed as an objective severity measure could not be deployed for the children with the most severe autism - a finding about deployability that generalises beyond this study." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.96"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Having more page-speciﬁc AOIs for Study 2 may have increased the level of noise and decreased accuracy. These results suggest a trade-off between having sufﬁciently detailed AOIs and not introducing too much noise. The optimal number of AOIs is best deﬁned empirically." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1109/TNSRE.2020.2991675> ;
            slr:claim_label "Area-of-interest granularity involves a trade-off between page detail and added noise, with the optimum determined empirically." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.85"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "We report results based on nested cross-validation, where the only hyper-parameter that is being optimized is the time window Tw, and we optimize over values of Tw = 1, 2, 4, 6 seconds. ... The results for the AI method are obtained via 10-fold cross validation (repeated 100 times with shuffling participant order)." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:7adb257effec> ;
            slr:claim_label "Participant-level nested cross-validation with a single tuned hyperparameter, no feature selection, default regularisation, and 100 repetitions with shuffling - the most disciplined validation design in this corpus." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.97"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "the class probability for ASD calculated by the classiﬁer scaled positively with participants' ADOS score (rs = 0.48, p < 0.0001, see Fig. 2) and with participant's age (rs = 0.35, p = 0.001, see Fig. 3)." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-020-0227-5> ;
            slr:claim_label "Predicted class probability correlated with ADOS severity at rs = 0.48 and with participant age at rs = 0.35." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "It is useful to underline that in the dataset there is no strict correspondence between the ﬁxation ﬁle index and the subject: the subject generating the ﬁxation data 1 for image 1 may not be the one who generated the ﬁxation data 1 for image 2." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:4c9af7f322c3> ;
            slr:claim_label "Subject identity is not consistent across images in the dataset, precluding subject-level partitioning." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "CAT-Q-ES R .85 .82 .75 .58 .54 MAE 5.55 5.36 6.94 4.55 4.00 MAPE (%) 9.2 8.9 11.6 16.9 19.1" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:58f57df91746> ;
            slr:claim_label "Under a clean validation design with dimensional outcomes, the best achievable correlations are .85 for social-trait outcomes and .54-.58 for affective symptoms - the realistic performance envelope against which this literature's 99-100% figures should be read." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.98"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "We used nested leave-one-participant-out cross-validation to evaluate our experiments. In each run, one participant was left out as testing data, while the rest were used for training. ... The learning rate was optimized by nested leave-one-participant-out cross-validation." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2023.952433> ;
            slr:claim_label "Participant-disjoint splitting with the tuned hyperparameter selected in the inner loop - correct nesting, stated explicitly, and structurally preventing the trial-level leakage that inflates most eye-tracking studies in this corpus." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.96"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Among the six eye-tracking paradigms, Dyadic Bids is the most useful one identified by all three models. Surprisingly, Social Referencing has the largest possibility to be discarded by the machine learning algorithms." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1145/3379155.3391334> ;
            slr:claim_label "Dyadic Bids was the paradigm most consistently retained across all three selection methods, while Social Referencing was most often discarded." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The code, alongside with a small sample data, are publicly available from the OSF Science Foundation (https://osf.io/t5v62)." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:58f57df91746> ;
            slr:claim_label "The only study in the charted corpus to deposit working code." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Given the videos analyzed in the present study were recorded by a fixed camera in a stable lighting condition, the sole factor that could lead to pixel changes between image frames was the human movement." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:12accba36eb4> ;
            slr:claim_label "The validity of the measurement method is derived explicitly from the acquisition conditions rather than assumed - and the whole pipeline requires only a consumer camcorder with no markers, sensors or calibration." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.88"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "In a reaching-grasping paradigm, children with ASD showed reduced coupling between DoF, which correlated with socio-communicative symptoms severity." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cognition.2021.104652> ;
            slr:claim_label "Reduced inter-joint coupling was observed in autistic children and correlated with symptom severity." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The videos are recorded from another 54 children under the same condition described in Figure 1. There is no overlap between these 54 children and those 41 children in our database." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1109/ICPR48806.2021.9412766> ;
            slr:claim_label "The expression model's fine-tuning data came from 54 children with no overlap with the clinical evaluation cohort." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "We chose SVM since it is popular when used on relatively smaller datasets, and also a cross-validation was done to minimize the risk of overoptimistic classification (Vabalas et al., 2019)." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:dbb27be8e821> ;
            slr:claim_label "Leave-one-out cross-validation was adopted with explicit citation to the literature on validation bias in small-sample machine learning." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "D-FT Mixed-Gender 94.8 94.7 | Female 43.6 86.5 | Male 61.5 94.2 | W2V2-FT Mixed-Gender 92.3 90.8 | Female 24.4 92.3 | Male 53.8 100" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2025.1274675> ;
            slr:claim_label "The most consequential subgroup result in the review: pooled sensitivity of 94.8% falls to 43.6% for girls and 61.5% for boys on held-out gender partitions, and the second model falls to 24.4% for girls. Almost no other study here evaluates any subgroup, so how far their pooled accuracies conceal comparable deficits is unknown." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.99"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "This somewhat advanced age was indispensable here because in our region there were not enough diagnosed children younger than 6 years, and the time it takes to consult a doctor to make a diagnosis can be as long as 2 years." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.2196/27706> ;
            slr:claim_label "Regional diagnostic waiting times of up to two years prevented recruiting the younger sample the study aimed at." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "We achieved an accuracy of 92% (95% CI 88%-97%) on US videos using a classifier built on five features." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:5991dace03f8> ;
            slr:claim_label "The same pipeline achieved 92% accuracy on US videos, establishing the magnitude of the cross-cultural performance drop." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "the best-performing random forest model... achieved a test accuracy of 81.8%, sensitivity of 77.1%, specificity of 84.1%, and F1 of 77.9% for the full sample. For the age/gender-matched sample, these values were 74.0%, 69.2%, 79.2%, and 73.5%" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1038/s41398-026-03823-y> ;
            slr:claim_label "Sensitivity and specificity are reported separately for both samples, showing that the matched model misses roughly a third of autistic adults at its operating point." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.94"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "However, the impact of our results is limited due to the small number of subjects included in this first feasibility study. A significant performance difference between an SVM approach and a deep learning approach might only become evident on a dataset with a considerably higher number of vocalisations." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.21437/interspeech.2017-1007> ;
            slr:claim_label "The authors limit their claims to feasibility and attribute the SVM-versus-BLSTM tie to insufficient data." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The eye tracking system used in this study was custom-built and has not been evaluated on a standard dataset or compared with standard eye trackers. ... The mount occluded roughly 15% of the field of view in the lower region for most participants, which may have had a greater distracting effect on participants in the ASD group with tactile sensitivities." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.2196/13810> ;
            slr:claim_label "The custom eye tracker was never validated against a standard device and physically occluded part of the visual field, possibly differentially by group." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Each participant performed 10 walking motions, and the most optimal trial was selected based on the disease and the doctor's recommendation, resulting in a total of 100 samples." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:3316ae46ffa0> ;
            slr:claim_label "The single modelled sample per child was chosen with knowledge of that child's diagnosis - selection on the outcome, operating entirely upstream of cross-validation and unaddressable by any splitting scheme." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.97"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "A computational analysis of motor pattern variables, rather than social and emotional variables, is employed." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjopen-2018-026226> ;
            slr:claim_label "A deliberate construct choice away from social and emotional measurement toward motor control, grounded in the hypothesis that movement disruption is a core component of autism - reachable in pre-verbal children whom the language-based measures in this review cannot assess." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.92"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The database used in this study is constructed with 79 Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) samples, 77 Down Syndrome (DS) and 99 normal samples. All the speech samples are the phonation of sustained vowel /ah/ and are collected from six ASD children (three boys, three girls), six DS children (two boys, four girls) and seven controls (three boys, four girls). All children are aged between 5–14 years." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:9b39e593462a> ;
            slr:claim_label "255 voice samples derive from only 19 children, roughly 13 phonations each, with ages given only as a collective 5-14 year range." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "including VO6 (duration of socially oriented vocalizations and verbalizations) (OR 0.940, 95% CI: 0.907-0.970, p < 0.001), FP1 (total counts of functional play) (OR 0.639, 95% CI: 0.529-0.760, p < 0.001)" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1186/s12888-024-06129-9> ;
            slr:claim_label "The model's discriminative information spans two behavioural channels - socially directed speech and functional object play - with every coefficient reported as an interpretable odds ratio with a confidence interval. The strongest predictor is a raw speech duration, which is confounded with expressive language level that was never measured." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.93"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The model had six variables, which was a large number compared to the number of samples; hence, the AUC may have increased due to overfitting. Therefore, additional validation of the model using external data is required." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:aa6615a55671> ;
            slr:claim_label "The authors name overfitting as the explanation for their own validation drop and state the remedy - a form of self-assessment almost absent from this corpus, where perfect scores are frequently reported with no held-out check at all." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The number of incorrectly classified vocalisations of subjects from the ASD group indicates that, a certain proportion of vocalisations of an individual later diagnosed with ASD does not bear atypicalities in the acoustic signal domain." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.21437/interspeech.2017-1007> ;
            slr:claim_label "Not all vocalisations of infants later diagnosed with ASD carry acoustic atypicality, motivating subject-level rather than utterance-level judgements." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.85"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "To evaluate the predictive ability of the two models in classifying motor trajectories, we used k-fold cross-validation. Given the relatively small size of our dataset, we opted for a four-fold cross-validation approach. The validation test consists of 110 samples, precisely 15% of the entire dataset (738)." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:03e7999e2993> ;
            slr:claim_label "Cross-validation partitions trajectories, not children: 738 trajectories from 20 children means each validation trajectory has roughly 36 siblings from the same child in training, and no participant-level result is reported anywhere." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.98"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Result signiﬁcance was assessed with permutation testing. The labels of the data samples were randomly permutated 1000 times and empirical p-statistic calculated" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:61028ef44814> ;
            slr:claim_label "Statistical significance was established by 1000-iteration label permutation testing." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "such behavioural pattern suggests that autistic children have more deficits among automatic turn-taking (spontaneous, without instruction) as compared to intentional turn-taking (after being instructed)" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:9a63d236d24c> ;
            slr:claim_label "A construct decomposition nothing else in this corpus attempts: separating prompted from unprompted performance distinguishes capacity from spontaneous deployment, a distinction most behavioural measures in this review collapse." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.96"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "In addition, two features, age and sex, were obtained from personal information, providing eleven features in total." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1111/jcap.12346> ;
            slr:claim_label "Age and sex were included as model features, on which the test sample differs systematically from the training population." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The proposed model has several limitations firstly, the reliance on the Yeo-Johnson transformation for noise removal may not be sufficient for all types of data. Additionally, the complexity of the model may lead to overfitting, especially with smaller datasets." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.compbiolchem.2025.108619> ;
            slr:claim_label "The only stated limitations concern the model; the circular label, absent sample size and absent validation protocol go unmentioned, while a full table catalogues the disadvantages of prior studies." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.85"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Baseline LSTM (unidirectional) 0.932 ... BiLSTM + CGBO (core configuration) 0.985 ... BiLSTM + CGBO + SHAP (final model) 0.987" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1007/s42452-026-08352-3> ;
            slr:claim_label "The ablation credits the addition of SHAP with a 0.002 accuracy gain, which a post-hoc explanation method applied to an already-trained model cannot produce." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.88"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "This audio quality (average SNR of child speech was 12 ± 5dB) can be considered as one limitation of this study. In addition, since it is a DNN-based system, which benefits from a large dataset, it needs a minimum duration of child speech; this was set to 20s (at least 50 vocalizations)." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:894b14e604e6> ;
            slr:claim_label "Signal-to-noise ratio and a minimum-speech requirement are both stated as limitations, the latter excluding the least verbal children." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Data used in this paper are available in S1 File for other researchers." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:8da61cddafb9> ;
            slr:claim_label "The kinematic dataset was released with the paper for reuse." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.85"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Applying equation 12 from Raudys (40) to our problem, in which p = 3 (number of parameters), delta = 3.83 (the distance between normalized class centers), and n = 13 (the approximate number of samples per class for training in the 3-fold cross-validation scheme), we obtained a mean expected classiﬁcation error of 5.9%." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bpsc.2020.09.001> ;
            slr:claim_label "The authors quantify expected classification error at 5.9% against a 2.8% infinite-sample floor to argue their sample size is adequate for the model's parameter count." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Subsequently, the dataset was partitioned into three subsets: 2,536 for training, 200 for validation, and 100 for testing purposes. ... The Kaggle dataset comprising 2,836 facial images (1,418 autistic and 1,418 non-autistic children) underwent meticulous augmentation techniques." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:ef9df8726301> ;
            slr:claim_label "The augmentation is described as applied to the full 2,836-image dataset after the partition is described, leaving the ordering ambiguous - and if augmentation preceded splitting, transformed copies of the same photograph would sit on both sides of it." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.93"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "In the IJA task, which was designed to trigger the participant to initiate social interaction, individuals with TD showed a triadic gaze pattern - gaze shifting from the toy object to the examiner, then back to the toy object - while the individual with ASD failed to gaze on either the toy object or the examiner ... In the high-level RJA task, individuals with TD immediately turned around to view the poster and then turned back to face the examiner as if seeking approval, while those with ASD showed delayed or no response." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:6131bfb6ef7c> ;
            slr:claim_label "Coupled attention-weight and Grad-CAM analysis shows the models responding to the specific behavioural constructs the tasks were designed to elicit, rather than to unspecified correlates - the most thorough interpretability verification in this review." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.97"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Mean ADOS score of each group had to be within the range of −10% and +10% of the total sample mean. • The standard deviation of ADOS scores in each group had to be within the range of −10% and +10% of the total sample standard deviation. • Kurtosis of the ADOS score distribution of each group had to be within the range of −10% and +10% of the kurtosis of the total sample distribution." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:f0fbf02fd04e> ;
            slr:claim_label "Train/test splits were accepted only if the mean, standard deviation and kurtosis of ADOS scores matched the whole sample within 10%, with skewness bounded." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Without velocity-based color mapping AVE 99.81% 71.74% 78.99% | With velocity-based color mapping AVE 100.00% 80.25% 83.86%" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1145/3539226> ;
            slr:claim_label "Encoding gaze velocity as colour adds 8.5 accuracy points and 0.05 AUC over position alone in the same time window - a controlled demonstration that a rendered-trajectory representation can recover dynamics it would otherwise discard." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The ROC AUC for the boys was 0.8901... which was not significantly different from the ROC AUC for the SVM with our full sample (p = 0.4428). The ROC AUC for the girls was 0.8067... also not significantly different" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1002/aur.2733> ;
            slr:claim_label "The significant sex imbalance was addressed by fitting sex-stratified models, neither of which differed from the full sample." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Garcés et al. [23] 2022 ... Power spectrum and functional connectivity 212/199 56% to 64%" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.46604/aiti.2024.13951> ;
            slr:claim_label "Within the paper's own comparison table, the single study with a sample an order of magnitude larger than the rest reports by far the lowest accuracy - independent corroboration, assembled by these authors without that intention, of the inverse relationship between sample size and reported performance that this review traces across the behavioural branches." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.97"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "To ensure speaker identity is not a confounding factor, this partitioning was done in an completely child independent fashion i. e., all uterance from any given child are contained completely within one partition. All parameters of the models were optimised on the validation set, whereas the test partition is solely used for the purpose of performance evaluation on unseen children." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1145/3079452.3079492> ;
            slr:claim_label "Partitioning was child-independent with all parameter tuning on a separate validation set and the test set reserved for unseen children." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "We used 10-fold group-wise (by individual) cross-validation for development of ASD detection and ASD symptom severity assessment systems." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:6131bfb6ef7c> ;
            slr:claim_label "Cross-validation is grouped by participant and stated unambiguously, so all of a child's up-to-fifteen video clips from a single session remain in one fold - the safeguard most of this corpus omits." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.96"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "We found no signiﬁcant evidence for group differences regarding gaze behavior... An explanation for our null-result might lie in the fact that the appearance-based gaze estimation used here, in comparison to eye tracking methods that were used previously, was not sensitive enough to detect the subtle gaze dysfunctions present in ASD." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-020-0227-5> ;
            slr:claim_label "Gaze produced no group differences, attributed to appearance-based webcam estimation lacking the precision of dedicated eye trackers." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Since the requirement of the motor task was to perform the utmost variant movement and lack of invariance is the hallmark symptom of ASD, we hypothesized that the restricted feature of being unable to perform variant movement would be manifested in the execution of this speciﬁc motor task." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:ea9c4425e2e9> ;
            slr:claim_label "The task was designed so that restrictedness manifests as inability to comply with an instruction to move variably." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "This study utilizes a publicly available dataset from the Roboflow platform, which includes 14,352 annotated facial images of children that have been labelled as autistic or non-autistic... The dataset is divided into three groups for testing, validation and training." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.47310/jpms2026150119> ;
            slr:claim_label "No class counts, split ratios, partition sizes or count of distinct children is reported, so no result can be located to a sample." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.93"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The classifications with rhythm-relevant features were significant for both English (median AUC = 0.900, p < 0.001...), and Cantonese (median AUC = 0.962, p < 0.001...). In contrast, classifications with intonation-relevant features were significant only for English (median AUC = 0.695, p = 0.007...) but not Cantonese (median AUC = 0.620, p = 0.507...)" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:c6e9187abd76> ;
            slr:claim_label "Rhythm-based classification generalised across two typologically distinct languages while intonation-based classification did not." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "0.822 (0.010) 0.790 (0.014) 0.793 (0.014) 0.804 (0.014) 0.793 (0.013) Convolutional neural network" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:44d2304739ef> ;
            slr:claim_label "The best model reached 79.3% accuracy and 0.822 AUROC on uncontrolled home recordings under child-disjoint folds." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "In Figure 2, A1, A2, A6, A8, and A9 were found to have a high correlation with Q-CHAT-10 score; thus, they were excluded from the dataset to improve the model's capacity to generalize to novel data, simplifying it and minimizing repetition." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3390/diagnostics15151859> ;
            slr:claim_label "Five of the ten checklist items were deleted for correlating with the score they are summed into, which removes half the label's own summands rather than removing redundancy." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.99"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "For classification into Control and Condition group, we obtain a very high classification accuracy of 96.4%. Similarly, for classification into Comorbid(ASD+ADHD) and ASD only group, we obtain a high classification accuracy of 93.9%." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:2859eee06613> ;
            slr:claim_label "Reported accuracies were 96.4% and 93.9% for the two classification tasks." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "We did not try to have a similar number of boys and girls since it has been shown that the play complexity and toy engagement of children with ASD in both genders for car-like toys are almost similar [13,30]." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3390/su15107790> ;
            slr:claim_label "Sex is neither balanced nor reported, on the argument that toy engagement does not differ by sex - but declining to balance a variable does not remove the need to report it, so no reader can check the groups' composition." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.82"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "All children were equipped with communicative verbal skills, and diagnosed with one of the following conditions: autism disorders (AD; 11 children), pervasive developmental disorders not otherwise specifed (PDD-NOS; 10 children), or specifc language impairment (SLI; 13 children), according to DSM–IV criteria [1]." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1145/3079452.3079492> ;
            slr:claim_label "The clinical sample comprised 11 autism-disorder, 10 PDD-NOS and 13 specific-language-impairment children, all verbally able, diagnosed by DSM-IV criteria." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "It is observable that there were no large differences in mean saccade latencies between the control and ASD groups. However, there is greater trial variability in the ASD group." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:0c3188260dff> ;
            slr:claim_label "Mean saccade latency did not differ between groups while trial-to-trial variability was elevated in autism - reported without any supporting statistic." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Specifically, a time-series with higher variations that are highly periodic will result in extremely low values of the entropy measure. Similar is the case when the time-series data is almost stable with very low variations, the entropy value will still be low (which was the case for our TD participants). In contrast, for a time-series with higher variability, irregular and non-periodic movements, the entropy value will be high." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:665e8c7a855d> ;
            slr:claim_label "An explicit account of what the measure captures - predictability, not amplitude or variability - backed empirically by the weak correlation between integrated entropy and landmark variation." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.91"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Results show that four out of these five human mispredictions were correctly predicted by the AI, including the first participant in the list, whose diagnosis was predicted correctly by only 21% of the human raters. In other words, participants that were difficult for most human raters to accurately classify were not particularly difficult for the AI." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:7adb257effec> ;
            slr:claim_label "The paper's actual finding: AI and human errors barely overlap, so the model carries complementary information rather than duplicating clinician judgement." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.97"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "clinicians currently rely on the so-called \"clinical gaze\", the implicit knowledge based on experience, which is demanded to be used in some clinical investigations such as the autism diagnostic observation schedule (ADOS). Practitioners need many years of training to acquire the necessary expertise, which is also difﬁcult to verbalize, teach, quantify, standardize, and validate." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-020-0227-5> ;
            slr:claim_label "The motivating problem is the implicit, hard-to-standardise clinical expertise on which social interaction assessment currently depends." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "In this paper, the standard facial expression database FER2013 and CK + are used as the main training samples for autism diagnosis model.The facial expression image data of 16 Chinese children were collected as supplementary training samples." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:0f2f3a5e7d29> ;
            slr:claim_label "The models were trained on general facial expression datasets plus images from sixteen typically developing children, never on autism labels." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The limitations of the study include the still relatively small sample size for the patient population, especially for female participants with ASD. Because ASD may be less prevalent in women, recruiting female participants represents a particular challenge, and male and female participants with ASD may have different patterns of spatial distribution in language." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:fcc0cc14d937> ;
            slr:claim_label "The authors identify the shortage of women with ASD as a limitation and note possible sex differences in language patterns." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.85"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "one limitation in using face stimuli and structured recognition task that it is highly dependening on the knowledge about ASD which in response limits the model generalizability to other clinical populations or young children who may fail to understand or fulfill with the task instruction." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:6a3b6c2a276b> ;
