            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Differentiating facial features from normally developing children is a clear way to identify children diagnosed with ASD." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1145/3660853.3660888> ;
            slr:claim_label "A physiognomic claim considerably stronger than the craniofacial literature the paper cites supports, and asserted as established rather than tested - while no morphometric quantity is measured anywhere in the study." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.92"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Although existing literature provides promising results for automatic classification of autism and related conditions, their focus is often on the types of features [6], classification of other conditions such as Parkinson's Disease [17] or classification comparison between machine learning techniques [18]. The present study expands on these studies by looking at how each subject is represented in the data and how this impacts classification." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:51915c87b5e5> ;
            slr:claim_label "The paper identifies data representation - how each subject is represented - as an unexamined variable in the autism gait classification literature." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.85"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The first reviewing specialist agreed with the diagnosing specialist 79% of the time. The remaining 21% of subjects required a review by a second independent specialist clinician... the diagnosing specialist clinician was 'completely certain' 67% of the time when diagnosing ASD" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-022-00598-6> ;
            slr:claim_label "The reference standard's own reliability is quantified, bounding what any device validated against it can be shown to achieve." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "the ASD group reached a low level of STH and high level of DC... the average length of their trajectories (MeanLength) was lower than that of the TD group, indicating more fragmented movements... they showed big values of MeanSpeed and MaximumSpeed, but low values of MinimumSpeed." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.635696> ;
            slr:claim_label "Autistic trajectories were shorter, less straight, more redirected and wider in speed range, interpreted as repeated online correction." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.85"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Inevitably applied 5-fold cross-validation to evaluate the model's capacity for generalization to novel data. The dataset was partitioned into five subsets, with each fold employing 80% of the data for training and 20% for testing. The model consistently attained elevated accuracy and F1 scores across all folds, exhibiting minimal variance." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.32604/cmc.2025.062266> ;
            slr:claim_label "Cross-validation is asserted with no per-fold value, mean or standard deviation reported, and cannot be reconciled with the confusion matrices that span the whole dataset." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.96"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "To ensure that no participants with a high incidence of autistic traits were included in the control group, all control participants completed the 50-item Autism Quotient (AQ) test [21]." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1109/TNSRE.2020.2991675> ;
            slr:claim_label "Control status was positively screened with the AQ-50 rather than assumed, and one participant was excluded on that basis." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "it would be necessary that all the participants wear it simultaneously from their mouths to ensure that all the variations are due to the actual speech production." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:602daaeb6643> ;
            slr:claim_label "The authors correctly identify fixed head-mounted microphones as the requirement for attributing acoustic variation to speech rather than to acquisition." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Results obtained on simulated trajectories ... considering an acceleration range between -5 cm^2/s and +5 cm^2/s, showed a moderately positive correlation (r = 0.69) between the average acceleration of the trajectories and the predictions. On the other hand, the four-features model reached a stronger relationship between average trajectory acceleration and predictions (r = 0.82). ... As the average acceleration value increases, the model is more likely to predict belonging to the ASD group." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:03e7999e2993> ;
            slr:claim_label "Causal manipulation rather than post-hoc attribution: synthetic trajectories with systematically varied acceleration establish the direction and strength of the feature's effect on predictions - and the two-feature model, which never receives acceleration as input, still correlates with it at 0.69, showing the information is recoverable from raw coordinates." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.97"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Physically, it is observed that children with autism develop just like children without autism." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <urn:slr:article:4a5b35736368> ;
            slr:claim_label "A comparative developmental claim is asserted without any statistical comparison or effect size." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.8"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
