            slr:decision "include" ;
            slr:decision_rationale "Random-forest classification of ASD versus typically developing subjects from eye-tracking scanpaths over natural images, combining image content, fixation and centre-bias features - computational model on behavioral (gaze) data with an autism identification target." ;
            slr:screening_stage "title_abstract" ],
        [ a prov:Activity,
                slr:ScreeningDecisionRecord ;
            dcterms:title "AI-Powered Human-Computer Interaction Assisting Early Identification of Emotional and Facial Symptoms of Autism Spectrum Disorder in Children: &#x201C;A Deep Learning-Based Enhanced Facial Feature Recognition System&#x201D;" ;
            bibo:pmid "local_261afa51" ;
            slr:assessed_on "full_text" ;
            slr:decision "include" ;
            slr:decision_rationale "Included at the boundary of the review's modality scope and charted as a negative exemplar. The study has two components: classification of autism from static facial photographs, which is facial morphology rather than behaviour; and a facial expression recognition module trained on AffectNet, which is behavioural. The paper frames the whole as identifying 'emotional and facial symptoms' and positions the combined system as an autism detection tool, so it meets the criterion of a computational model with an autism classification target applied to human facial data. It is retained because the review needs to characterise this branch of the literature - autism classification from scraped face photographs - and because its dataset construction, in which autistic images are scraped from autism-focused websites and non-autistic images gathered randomly from the internet, exemplifies a validity failure that recurs across that branch." ;
            slr:full_text_source "user_supplied_pdf" ;
            slr:screening_stage "full_text" ],
        [ a prov:Activity,
                slr:ScreeningDecisionRecord ;
            dcterms:title "Exploiting Visual Behaviour for Autism Spectrum Disorder Identification" ;
            bibo:pmid "local_05ef29e5" ;
