                    slr:supporting_quote "One row per respondent and 5-fold cross-validation, so the split unit is the person and no within-participant leakage arises. But which features survived the engineering stage is not reported, so whether the summed score entered the model cannot be determined from the paper, and no class distribution is given." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "high" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<https://doi.org/10.46604/aiti.2024.13951> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "This study aims to leverage a promising avenue for the precise and early detection of Autism. Autism is a multifaceted neurodevelopmental condition marked by challenges in social interaction, communication, and repetitive behaviors. Traditional diagnosis relies on time-consuming behavioral assessments, necessitating reliable and non-intrusive biomarkers for early and accurate detection. This paper analyzes eleven linear and non-linear features across time and frequency domains from an EEG dataset. Four neural network models, such as convolutional neural network (CNN), deep neural network (DNN), long short-term memory (LSTM), and a custom neural network are employed for classification. The CNN achieves the lowest accuracy at 89.02%, while the custom neural network reaches the highest accuracy at 94.02%, and the DNN and LSTM achieve 91.98% and 93.83% accuracy, respectively. Other metrics such as precision, recall, specificity, and F1-score, are also evaluated. This research underscores the efficacy of neural network in detecting Autism, advancing diagnostic tools." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Hasan et al. (2024). Performance Evaluation of Neural Network Models for Autism Detection Using EEG Data [REC04023]. **. https://doi.org/10.46604/aiti.2024.13951" ;
    dcterms:creator "Hasan et al." ;
    dcterms:title "Performance Evaluation of Neural Network Models for Autism Detection Using EEG Data [REC04023]" ;
    bibo:doi "10.46604/aiti.2024.13951" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_0b0960c4" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2024 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Confounder control" ;
                            slr:justification "No age distribution by group across a 9-to-16 window in which EEG spectral characteristics change substantially; no medication record, although antiepileptics and antipsychotics alter EEG directly and are more common in autistic samples. Sex is fully controlled by the sample being all-male." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Handling of missing data and attrition" ;
                            slr:justification "Recording at rest is described as minimising artefact, but no artefact rejection, epoch exclusion or channel interpolation procedure is reported, and the number of analysed segments is never given." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Comparison group adequacy" ;
                            slr:justification "Four typically developing children - the smallest comparison group in the review, and too small to characterise typical variation in any EEG measure or to support the reported specificity." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participant selection and spectrum representativeness" ;
                            slr:justification "Thirteen autistic boys with no ascertainment described, no severity characterisation, no cognitive or medication information, and an all-male sample." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome measurement blinding" ;
                            slr:justification "Feature extraction and classification are fully automated from stored recordings." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Index test definition and prespecification" ;
                            slr:justification "Four architectures are specified with layer sizes and explicit activation equations, but the eleven features are not individually identified and no hyperparameters or training details are given." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reference standard quality" ;
                            slr:justification "No instrument, criteria, clinician or date for the autistic group, and no screening procedure for the comparison group." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Measurement standardisation" ;
                            slr:justification "A single acquisition chain applied identically to both groups, with montage, ground, reference, filter settings and sampling rate all specified - the most completely reported acquisition in the review." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "unclear" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance estimation and chance level" ;
                            slr:justification "Five metrics reported for four architectures with no confidence intervals, no confusion matrices and no baseline - although the 13:4 participant ratio places a trivial all-autistic classifier at 76.5%." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Sample size adequacy for the modelling" ;
                            slr:justification "Four neural architectures fitted on data from 17 children, four of them controls. The paper's own comparison table shows most of the branch operating at comparable scale." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Preprocessing and feature selection nesting" ;
                            slr:justification "Eleven features are extracted from stated domain families with no selection step described; whether extraction parameters were fitted on all data is not reported." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data splitting and leakage control" ;
                            slr:justification "No split, cross-validation scheme or partitioning unit is stated anywhere. With 17 participants the reported accuracies must be epoch-level, and with four controls a participant-disjoint arrangement is barely constructible." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data, code and protocol availability" ;
                            slr:justification "The corpus is identified with its access URL and the provider is acknowledged by name; no code is released." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Acknowledgement of limitations" ;
                            slr:justification "No limitations section. The conclusion states that the work 'underscores the efficacy of neural network in detecting Autism, advancing diagnostic tools'." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Ethics, consent and conflicts" ;
                            slr:justification "'For this type of study, statement of human rights is not required' and 'informed consent is not required', stated without qualification and without reference to the originating approvals, for EEG recordings of identifiable children obtained from another institution. Competing interests declared as none." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Completeness of methods reporting" ;
                            slr:justification "The acquisition chain, group sizes and age ranges, feature domain families and all four architectures with layer equations are reported. No validation scheme, segment count, feature identity, hyperparameters or diagnostic procedure is given." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reporting of results and uncertainty" ;
                            slr:justification "Accuracy, precision, recall, specificity and F1 for every architecture - including specificity, which is appropriate at this class ratio and more than several studies here provide. No intervals, confusion matrices or baseline." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Sharply divided. Fully reported: the data source with its URL and a named acknowledgement of the provider, both group sizes and age ranges, the complete electrode montage with ground and reference positions, the filter settings and sampling rate, the recording condition, the count of features and their domain families, all four network architectures with layer sizes and activation functions given as explicit equations, and five performance metrics for each model. Entirely absent: any train-test split, cross-validation scheme or partitioning unit; the number of EEG segments analysed and how they distribute across the 17 participants; the identity of the eleven features; hyperparameters; and any account of how autism was ascertained. A reader could replicate the recording and the architectures and could not evaluate or reproduce the result." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-123" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Almost entirely absent. Sex is fully controlled by the sample being all-male; age is given only as two overlapping ranges (10-16 and 9-16) with no distribution or group comparison; and no cognitive, language, comorbidity, medication or socioeconomic information exists. Two specific risks follow. EEG spectral characteristics change substantially across the 9-to-16 window - the alpha peak rises in frequency, slow-wave power declines, and the maturational trajectory is well documented - so an age difference between a 13-child and a 4-child group would be sufficient to separate them on spectral features, and nothing reported allows the check. And medication is unrecorded, although antiepileptics and antipsychotics both alter EEG spectra and are more common in an autistic than in a comparison sample." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-123" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None, by design. The EEG was recorded 'during participants' relaxed states to ensure artifact-free data', so no task was administered, no stimulus presented and no behaviour elicited. This is the defining difference between the electrophysiological branch and the behavioural studies charted elsewhere in this review, and it cuts both ways. Resting-state acquisition is maximally undemanding - it requires no cooperation with instructions, no verbal ability and no social engagement, which is why it reaches participants that task-based paradigms cannot - and it also means there is no behavioural or cognitive event to which any observed difference can be anchored. A group difference in resting spectral power is a difference in ambient neural state, interpretable only against a physiological literature, whereas a group difference in gaze during a social scene or in utterance length during play is interpretable directly." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-123" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. A single electrophysiological modality throughout, with no behavioural, imaging or clinical data fused. The eleven features span time and frequency domains, which is within-modality diversity rather than fusion." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-123" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Seventeen boys: 13 autistic aged 10 to 16 and 4 typically developing aged 9 to 16, drawn from the global autism EEG corpus held by the brain-computer interface group at King Abdulaziz University and used with permission. The comparison group of four is the smallest in the review and is too small to characterise typical variation in any EEG measure, let alone to support a claim about detection. The sample is entirely male, which removes sex as a confound and means nothing reported can be assumed to apply to autistic girls. No age distribution by group is given beyond the two ranges, and none of the descriptors this review usually records - cognitive level, language ability, comorbidity, medication, socioeconomic background - is available. The 13:4 ratio also sets the participant-level majority-class baseline at 76.5%, against which the reported 89-94% should be read and never is." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-123" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional secondary analysis with no longitudinal element. The ethics declarations read in full: 'For this type of study, statement of human rights is not required' and 'For this type of study, informed consent is not required.' The data are EEG recordings of identifiable children obtained from another institution's research group with permission, so the declaration concerns secondary use rather than the original collection - but the paper does not distinguish the two, gives no reference to the originating approvals, and states the exemption in absolute terms. The data provider is acknowledged by name with the corpus URL, and competing interests are declared as none." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-123" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The most completely specified acquisition chain in the review. A g.tec EEG cap with 16 Ag/AgCl electrodes, a g.tec GAMMAbox and g.tec USBamp, driven by BCI2000; electrodes at FP1, FP2, F7, F3, Fz, F4, F8, T3, C4, Cz, C3, T5, Pz, O1, Oz and O2 under the international 10-20 system; AFz as ground and the right earlobe as reference; bandpass 0.1-60 Hz with a 60 Hz notch; digitised at 256 Hz. Montage, ground, reference, filter settings and sampling rate are all given, which is more acquisition detail than most behavioural studies in this review report for their own instruments and is exactly what would be needed to replicate the recording. The corresponding practical point is that this apparatus - a research EEG amplifier, a gel cap, a trained operator and a cooperative child who will sit still - is at the opposite end of the deployment spectrum from the webcam-and-toys protocols charted elsewhere, and the review's synthesis should note that the electrophysiological branch shares the behavioural branch's motivation while requiring the equipment that motivation exists to avoid." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-123" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, precision, recall, specificity and F1 for all four architectures. Reporting specificity is appropriate at a 13:4 class ratio and more than several studies in this review provide. Three gaps: no confidence intervals on figures derived from 17 participants; no confusion matrices or class-wise breakdowns; and no chance or majority-class baseline, although the participant-level ratio places a trivial all-autistic classifier at 76.5%, so the reported 89-94% represents a much smaller increment over the trivial solution than the raw numbers suggest." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-123" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No feature-importance, attribution, spectral-band or electrode-localisation analysis is reported. In a study whose features are interpretable physiological quantities and whose electrodes have known anatomical correspondences, this is the clearest missed opportunity: a ranking of the eleven descriptors, or a topographic map of discriminative channels, would have been comparable across the branch's literature and would have survived any subsequent revision of the accuracy figure." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-123" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "There is no limitations section. What should have been stated, in order of consequence: no validation scheme of any kind is described, so the reported 89-94% cannot be placed relative to any evaluation design, and with 17 participants those figures must derive from epoch-level evaluation where a participant-disjoint arrangement is barely constructible from four controls. The participant-level majority-class baseline is 76.5% and is never stated. The comparison group of four children is too small to characterise typical EEG variation. No age distribution by group is given across a 9-to-16 window in which EEG spectra change substantially, and medication - which alters EEG directly and differs between such groups - is unrecorded. No diagnostic ascertainment is described. No feature-importance analysis identifies which of the eleven descriptors separates the groups, which would have been the study's most transferable finding. And the paper's own comparison table displays a pattern it does not comment on, discussed below." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-123" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No primary collection. Recordings were made by the originating group under controlled laboratory conditions with participants at rest, described as ensuring artefact-free and reliable data. Both groups were recorded on the same equipment under the same protocol, so the acquisition asymmetry that undermines several branches of this review - clinic against school, autism websites against general internet - does not arise here. That is a genuine structural advantage of purpose-built physiological corpora over the assembled behavioural ones." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-123" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None stated. The steps this study most needs are participant-disjoint evaluation with the scheme reported, a stated majority-class baseline, feature-importance analysis over the eleven descriptors, and a comparison group larger than four." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-123" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None described. The 13 autistic participants are stated to be 'diagnosed with ASD' with no instrument, criteria, clinician qualification, setting or date, and no screening procedure is reported for the 4 comparison children. The labels derive from the originating corpus and this paper transmits none of its documentation. The consequence is the same as for several studies in this review: the quantity the models predict is a label of unknown provenance, and no reported figure can be located relative to any clinical standard." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-123" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No severity, cognitive, adaptive or language measure exists for any participant, so no gradient analysis is possible and the models' outputs can be related only to group membership." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-123" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The study reports no validation scheme at all, and the sample structure makes the omission decisive. With 13 autistic and 4 typically developing participants, accuracies of 89.02% to 94.02% cannot have been computed over people - 17 cases cannot produce those percentages - so they must be computed over EEG epochs or windows, and the paper never states how many segments were extracted, how long they were, how they distribute across participants, or how they were partitioned. With four control children, any participant-disjoint evaluation would place at most one control in a held-out set, which would make the reported specificity uninterpretable; the natural reading is therefore that segments from the same children appear on both sides of the boundary, and that the networks are separating individuals rather than groups. This is the same failure the review has documented for scanpath images, video frames, thermal images and cry instances, arising here in a fifth modality, and it is compounded by the absence of a stated majority-class baseline: at 13 against 4, a classifier that predicts autism for everything scores 76.5% at participant level, so the headline numbers represent a far smaller gain than they appear to. The paper's own comparison table is the most useful evidence in the study and it points the same way. It lists participant counts of 16/46, 10/5, 34/11, 10/10, 15/0 and 13/4 alongside accuracies of 92.9%, 93%, 72.77%-90.57%, 81%, 91.12% and 89.02%-94.02% - and one outlier at 212/199 participants reporting 56% to 64%. The single study in the table with a sample an order of magnitude larger than the rest reports by far the lowest accuracy, and the authors reproduce this pattern without comment. That inverse relationship between sample size and reported performance is the same one this review has traced through the behavioural branches, and its appearance in a table assembled by the authors themselves makes it the study's most transferable contribution." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-123" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Advances in Technology Innovation 9(4):287-300, 2024, doi 10.46604/aiti.2024.13951; received 1 July 2024, revised 31 July, accepted 2 August 2024 - a 32-day cycle. A technology-innovation venue, and the reviewing pattern is consistent with that: the acquisition chain, the feature families and the four architectures with their layer equations are all documented, while the complete absence of any validation scheme, of any majority-class baseline, of a limitations section and of any account of diagnostic ascertainment passed without comment, as did an ethics declaration stating in absolute terms that no human-rights statement or informed consent is required." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-123" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Published 2024 by the School of Computer Engineering and Mathematical Sciences at the Defence Institute of Advanced Technology, Pune, with a collaborator at Manipal University Jaipur, using data provided by the brain-computer interface group at King Abdulaziz University. The framing is identical to that of the behavioural studies charted throughout this review - diagnosis 'predominantly depends on subjective and time-consuming behavioral assessments', so a 'reliable and non-intrusive biomarker' is needed - which is itself worth recording, because the two branches share a motivation while differing entirely in what they measure and in what they require. For the synthesis the study contributes three things. It is the review's representative of the electrophysiological branch, and it makes clear both that branch's advantage - a purpose-built corpus with both groups recorded on identical equipment under an identical protocol, removing the acquisition confound that undermines the scraped-image and clinic-versus-school studies - and its cost, since a research EEG amplifier, a gel cap, a trained operator and a child who will sit still is precisely the apparatus that the webcam-and-toys approaches exist to avoid. Second, it reproduces the methodological pattern the review has traced across five behavioural modalities: 17 participants, accuracies necessarily computed over epochs, no stated partitioning rule, and a majority-class baseline of 76.5% that goes unmentioned. Third, and most usefully, its own comparison table supplies independent evidence for the review's central quantitative observation. The table lists six prior studies with participant counts from 15 to 80 reporting accuracies of 81% to 93%, and one study with 411 participants reporting 56% to 64% - so within a table the authors assembled themselves, the single study with a sample an order of magnitude larger than the rest reports by far the lowest performance. The inverse relationship between sample size and reported accuracy that this review documents in the scanpath, facial-image and speech branches is therefore visible in the electrophysiological branch too, and visible in a summary constructed without any intention of demonstrating it." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-123" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The aim is to compare a broader range of neural architectures on EEG-derived features than prior work has done, on the argument that existing studies have explored a limited set of models and feature combinations. Four architectures were fitted to eleven linear and non-linear time- and frequency-domain features: a custom neural network reached 94.02% accuracy, an LSTM 93.83%, a deep neural network 91.98% and a convolutional network 89.02%, with precision, recall, specificity and F1 reported for each. The comparison is even-handed and includes the lowest performer rather than only the winner. What the study cannot establish is what those figures mean. With 17 participants the accuracies must have been computed over EEG epochs rather than people, no validation scheme of any kind is described, and with four control children a participant-disjoint evaluation would leave at most one control in any held-out set. The paper's own comparison table is the most useful thing in it for this review, and its pattern is discussed below." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-123" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Conventional clinical framing, brief and largely accurate. Autism is 'a complex neurodevelopmental condition characterized by a spectrum of challenges, including difficulties in communication, response, attention, social behavior, and repetitive activities', with the addition that autistic people 'exhibit atypical neural activity patterns'. The diagnostic argument is the branch standard: current practice 'predominantly depends on subjective and time-consuming behavioral assessments', so an objective non-intrusive biomarker is needed. Person-first phrasing is used throughout and no pathologising excess appears. No neurodiversity framing is present and no autistic person is reported as involved. The framing is worth recording for the synthesis because it is identical to that of the behavioural studies charted elsewhere in this review - the same dissatisfaction with behavioural assessment motivates both branches, while the electrophysiological branch requires equipment and expertise that the behavioural work is generally chosen to avoid." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-123" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "An eleven-dimensional vector of linear and non-linear time- and frequency-domain descriptors per EEG segment. The features are individually interpretable and have an established electrophysiological literature, which makes the absence of any feature-importance analysis a real loss - identifying which descriptors and which electrode positions separate the groups would have produced a transferable finding independent of the classification accuracy. The number and duration of segments and their distribution across participants are not reported, which is the missing information needed to judge the sample structure." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-123" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No behaviour is observed, elicited or measured at any point: the recordings capture spontaneous cortical electrical activity while participants rest. The construct is neurophysiological - spectral power, signal complexity, fractal and entropy characteristics of the resting EEG - and the paper's motivating premise is that 'individuals with ASD exhibit atypical neural activity patterns'. For the review this places the study in a parallel evidence stream: it addresses the same clinical problem as the behavioural work and answers it with a different kind of measurement, one that is more direct physiologically and further from anything a clinician, parent or teacher observes. The related-work section shows the branch's feature vocabulary clearly - recurrence quantification analysis, power spectrum, wavelet transform, fast Fourier transform, fractal dimension, correlation dimension, Lyapunov exponent, entropy, detrended fluctuation analysis and synchronisation likelihood - none of which corresponds to an observable behaviour." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-123" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Four architectures compared on a common feature set: a convolutional network, a three-layer dense network (128 ReLU, 64 ReLU, single sigmoid, with the layer equations written out explicitly), an LSTM, and a custom design. Presenting four architectures with their formulations, and reporting the weakest alongside the strongest, is more even-handed than much of this corpus. The comparison's informativeness is limited by the spread being small - 89.02% to 94.02%, five points across four quite different architectures - which on a 17-participant sample is well within the range that resampling variation alone would produce, and by no hyperparameter or training detail being reported. The pattern nonetheless matches the one found in the skeleton-movement study charted elsewhere in this review, where six architectures spanned two percentage points on one dataset: in this literature the data determine the result and the architecture rarely does." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-123" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: Autism, detection, EEG, machine learning, neural network. Compact and accurate, naming the population, the task, the modality and the method. The keyword set would place the study in searches for computational autism detection alongside the behavioural work, which is how it entered this corpus, and the review should note that the two branches are not distinguished at the indexing level." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-123" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:study_design "Retrospective algorithm-development study on an existing resting-state EEG corpus, comparing four neural architectures" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: ; comparison: 4 typically developing boys aged 9 to 16" ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "e3dd2585ff49c270bd5088ec847ee632e0bf3d76a5bbdbbce9f9295f0c712e6a" ;
