    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Gold-standard diagnosis at 36 months by a blinded experienced clinician using medical history, ADOS and ADI-R, made independently of the acoustic analysis." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "unclear" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Index data at 18 months, reference standard at 36 months - an appropriate prospective interval - but no accounting of participants excluded from the original cohort or of vocalisations discarded for adult-speech overlap." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Convenience subset of a high-risk infant-sibling cohort rather than a consecutive or random clinical series; ASD group is enriched by sibling recruitment, so the case mix does not match a screening population." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Classifier thresholds and hyperparameters were tuned on the same 43-participant dataset used for reporting; performance comes from cross-validation over vocalisation instances with no stated subject-independent partitioning, so the index test result is not blind to the data it is evaluated on." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "high" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<urn:slr:article:9a63d236d24c> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Current research on autism raises an important methodological issue concerning reliable measurement tools to diagnose abnormal social interactions within autistic children. Here we presents proposal of a interdisciplinary approach combining psychology and robotics yielding Human-Robot interaction to recognize autism symptoms based on a pre-schoolers’ play with the social robot (’Touch me’ and ‘Dance with me’). The observational measures combined with competent raters technique indicated abnormal interaction patterns in children with autism during interactive games with NAO robot. Our research, by linking psychological and engineering perspectives provides promising preliminary results on application of robot to study autism based on HRI and results in collection of user’s requirements and evaluation’s criteria for a robot design. I. INTRODUCTION A. Linking psychological and engineering research The idea of use an human-robot interaction (HRI) to diagnose individuals with autism spectrum disorders (ASD) among preschool children implies the need of close cooperation between psychologists and engineers. This cooperation includes both identiﬁcation of a robot’s behaviour within social environment and its expected effects on children from psychological perspective as well as user’s requirements and evaluation criteria from the robotics engineering perspective. This implies series of psychological experiments needing to engage a human and a robot including also pilot studies. Positive outcomes from the psychological experiments should result in effective interactive scenarios for a a social robot that either meets the user’s requirements, the evaluation’s criteria for the whole experimental setup or the ﬁnal assessment that ensures that whole diagnostic and constructing process is well and effectively designed. Our article presents a part of such complex process for building an interaction diagnosis system that takes into account preliminary results from psychological experiments on use of a social robot with preschool children with ASD and engineering speciﬁcations highlighting typical HRI problems due to the main research problem, and the proposed speciﬁc robotic solutions. B. Theory of mind (mentalization approach) as a theoretical basis In experimental psychology, cognitive and social theories can be used to describe human behaviour in interaction with a robot. In contrast to biological systems (human-human interaction), desired behaviour of robot results from implemented functionalities in this artiﬁcial system [1]. Therefore, development of the HRI interaction depends mostly on social imitation which is a bulk of cognitive and social learning mechanisms implemented in the robot design [2]. The ability to imitate human behavior predominantly engages social cognition like perception of other, shared attention, attribution as well as recognition of mental states of the other people, including the understanding of non-verbal communication o" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Arent K; Kruk-Lasocka J; Niemiec T; Szczepanowski R (2019). Social robot in diagnosis of autism among preschool children. *2019 24th International Conference on Methods and Models in Automation and Robotic*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Arent K",
        "Kruk-Lasocka J",
        "Niemiec T",
        "Szczepanowski R" ;
    dcterms:title "Social robot in diagnosis of autism among preschool children" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_c6f1209b" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2019 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participant selection and spectrum representativeness" ;
                            slr:justification "Sample size, ages, sex and clinical characteristics unreported in the retrieved text; recruitment through a single integrative kindergarten with no stated selection procedure." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Handling of missing data and attrition" ;
                            slr:justification "No attrition or excluded sessions reported, and behaviour counts are normalised by the number of behaviours each game required - but participant numbers are not stated." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Confounder control" ;
                            slr:justification "The paired within-subject design holds every child-level factor constant across the two compared conditions - general ability, motivation, familiarity with the robot and rater leniency all cancel. This is the design's principal strength." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Comparison group adequacy" ;
                            slr:justification "No comparison group of any kind. The authors identify this as their first limitation and specify the remedy, which the group subsequently carried out." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Index test definition and prespecification" ;
                            slr:justification "The two conditions - instructed and spontaneous - are defined by the paradigm's structure in advance, and normalisation by required behaviours is specified. The questionnaire items themselves are not reproduced." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome measurement blinding" ;
                            slr:justification "All measurement is by human raters and neither blinding nor inter-rater reliability is reported for this study." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Measurement standardisation" ;
                            slr:justification "Robot-delivered elicitation gives identical stimulus behaviour to every child across both games, with a fixed dialogue opening and a common setting." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reference standard quality" ;
                            slr:justification "No instrument, criteria, clinician or date described in the retrieved text." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data splitting and leakage control" ;
                            slr:justification "Not applicable; no model is fitted and no individual receives a predicted class." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Sample size adequacy for the modelling" ;
                            slr:justification "Unstated in the retrieved text, but the paired design extracts an interpretable finding from a pilot sample far too small for any classifier - a point worth noting against the similarly sized between-group studies elsewhere in this review." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance estimation and chance level" ;
                            slr:justification "No diagnostic accuracy computed or claimed, which is appropriate. The single reported statistic lacks an effect size and confidence interval, so the magnitude of the dissociation is uncharacterised." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Preprocessing and feature selection nesting" ;
                            slr:justification "Not applicable. The two conditions and the normalisation rule were specified by the paradigm design." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "unclear" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Completeness of methods reporting" ;
                            slr:justification "Games, dialogue opening, robot platform with specifications and software environment, rater backgrounds, coding procedure, normalisation rule, statistical test and result are all reported. Sample size, ages, sex, diagnostic ascertainment and the questionnaire items are not." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reporting of results and uncertainty" ;
                            slr:justification "One test statistic with a p-value; no effect size, confidence interval or inter-rater reliability." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Ethics, consent and conflicts" ;
                            slr:justification "Research ethics committee approval obtained and informed parental consent gained, both stated explicitly. Funding and competing interests are not stated." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data, code and protocol availability" ;
                            slr:justification "No data statement and no code, which is expected for a study with no computational component; the questionnaire is not reproduced." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Acknowledgement of limitations" ;
                            slr:justification "Two limitations named, both correct: the absence of a comparison group, and the need to test against children with other developmental disorders - the latter identifying the differential-diagnosis problem that almost no study in this review commits to addressing." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "unclear" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two interactive games with the NAO robot, 'Touch me' and 'Dance with me', preceded by a dialogue phase in which a multiple-choice voice menu encouraged the child to choose a game or request a demonstration. Three properties of this paradigm bear on the review. The elicitation is delivered by a machine, so the social partner's behaviour is identical for every child - a form of standardisation no human examiner can supply, and one that removes examiner variability from a construct (reciprocal interaction) where the examiner is half the dyad. The voice-menu opening gives the child a choice of activity, which is a design decision oriented to engagement rather than to experimental control and is consistent with the group's later commitment to scenarios attractive to children. And the two games differ in a way the group's 2022 paper makes explicit: one uses a stationary robot issuing instructions, the other a moving robot inviting imitation, so that the same physical behaviour has different meanings in each. Here that structure is used to define the two conditions of the within-subject contrast - instructed against spontaneous - which is the paradigm's payoff." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-033" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No computational or machine-learning model is fitted. The analysis is a Wilcoxon signed-rank test on paired within-subject proportions, which is the correct choice for small-sample paired ordinal data and is used appropriately. For a review of computation-based approaches, the study sits immediately upstream of the modelling work: it establishes that a robot-delivered paradigm produces a measurable and theoretically interpretable behavioural dissociation, which is the finding that a subsequent automated system would need to reproduce." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-033" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Clinical and engineering registers combined, with the paper's distinctive framing being that autism assessment is a joint design problem. The opening argument is that using human-robot interaction diagnostically 'implies the need of close cooperation between psychologists and engineers', covering both the robot's behaviour and its psychological effects on the one hand, and user requirements and evaluation criteria on the other - so the study treats the autistic child as a user whose requirements shape the technology, which is a different stance from the corpus norm of treating the child as a source of data. Terminology is standard for the period ('children with autism', 'autistic children', 'autistic population'), and the language of 'abnormal social interactions' and 'abnormal interaction patterns' is used in the abstract. The theoretical framing is theory of mind and mentalisation, which locates the construct in social cognition rather than in deficit enumeration. No autistic person is reported as involved, though the group's 2022 follow-up names children among the target users to be consulted." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-033" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The stated aim is twofold and unusual: to test whether play with a social robot reveals abnormal interaction patterns in autistic preschoolers, and simultaneously to collect user requirements and evaluation criteria for designing such a robot. The psychological finding is specific and well constructed: autistic children performed significantly more behaviours when the robot instructed them to than they performed spontaneously in imitation of the robot (Wilcoxon signed-rank W = 21, p = 0.028), from which the authors conclude that 'autistic children have more deficits among automatic turn-taking (spontaneous, without instruction) as compared to intentional turn-taking (after being instructed)'. That is a dissociation within the construct rather than a difference in its overall level, and it is exactly the kind of claim that a paired within-subject design can support and a between-group design at this scale could not. The engineering finding - a set of requirements and evaluation criteria for robot design derived from the experimental experience - is presented as a co-equal output, which no other study in this review does." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-033" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Proportions of required behaviours performed, computed separately for instructed and spontaneous conditions in each game. Normalising by the number of behaviours the game required of the child is the right construction, since it removes differences in game length and in how many opportunities each scenario afforded, and it makes the two conditions commensurable within a child." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-033" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three directions, two of them named as limitations and one as an engineering output. Compare autistic children's robot interaction against that of typically developing children - subsequently done. Observe interactions with children who have other developmental disorders, to address the differential-diagnosis problem the paper identifies as the hardest. And apply the collected user requirements and evaluation criteria to robot design, which the paper treats as a research product in its own right." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-033" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2019 24th International Conference on Methods and Models in Automation and Robotics (MMAR), IEEE. A robotics and automation venue, and the paper is written for that audience - its second stated output is a set of user requirements and evaluation criteria for robot design, framed as an engineering deliverable rather than a clinical one. The venue explains both the paper's strengths, in its explicit treatment of the assessment problem as a joint psychology-engineering design task, and its omissions, since participant characteristics and diagnostic ascertainment would be expected in a clinical venue and are absent here." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-033" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. A single video stream coded by human raters, with the robot's own sensors unused for measurement. The paper's engineering framing points toward using them - it is explicitly gathering requirements for an 'interaction diagnosis system' - but no automated sensing is implemented here." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-033" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Not reported. The retrieved text gives no sample size, age, sex, cognitive level, language ability, socioeconomic background or comorbidity information for the participating children. For a paired within-subject design the omissions are less damaging than they would be in a group comparison - the contrast being tested is internal to each child, so demographic differences cannot confound it - but they prevent any statement about which children the finding applies to, and they leave developmental level unmeasured in a study whose construct is spontaneous imitation of a robot." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-033" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional pilot, single session per child, with no follow-up or test-retest. Ethics reporting is brief but complete on the essentials: approval of the committee of research ethics was obtained to carry out the experiment, and informed consent was obtained from the children's parents. Funding and competing interests are not stated in the retrieved text; the participating kindergarten is acknowledged by name." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-033" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Not applicable in the machine-learning sense; no model is fitted and no individual receives a predicted class. The design's validity rests instead on the paired within-subject comparison, and this is its principal strength: because each child's instructed-behaviour rate is compared against their own spontaneous-behaviour rate, general ability, motivation, familiarity with the robot, session-specific factors and rater leniency are all held constant across the two conditions. That is a genuine methodological advantage at pilot scale, and it is why a study with no comparison group and an unreported sample size can nonetheless support a specific and interpretable claim. Three limits bound it. No between-group inference is possible, which the authors state as their first limitation. Inter-rater reliability is not reported here, although the competent-raters technique is the measurement instrument and the group quantified it in the 2022 follow-up at Kendall's W = 0.65. And no effect size or confidence interval accompanies the test statistic, so the magnitude of the dissociation is not characterised. Set against the modelling studies in this review, the design illustrates a point worth carrying into the synthesis: a within-subject contrast can extract an interpretable finding from a sample far too small for any classifier, and several of the between-group studies charted here with comparable sample sizes would have been better served by the same approach." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-033" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "An integrative kindergarten in Wroclaw, named in the acknowledgements. Assessing children in their own everyday setting, with a portable robot brought to them, is the deployment scenario the work envisages, so the pilot data were collected under the conditions in which the method would be used. Integrative kindergartens in Poland enrol autistic and typically developing children together, which is also the setting that would make the group's stated next step - comparison against typically developing peers - straightforward to arrange without changing anything else about the protocol." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-033" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "One inferential statistic - Wilcoxon signed-rank W = 21, p = 0.028 - and no diagnostic accuracy metric of any kind, which is appropriate given that no classification is attempted. What is missing is an effect size and a confidence interval, which on a small paired sample would have conveyed the magnitude of the dissociation rather than only its statistical detectability, and inter-rater reliability for the coding on which the statistic rests." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-033" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Preschool children with autism recruited through an integrative kindergarten in Wroclaw, which is named in the acknowledgements. The retrieved text does not state the sample size, ages or sex composition, and no cognitive, language, socioeconomic or comorbidity information is reported. There is no comparison group of any kind - a design feature the authors identify explicitly as their first limitation. The absence of controls means the study cannot support any between-group inference, and it is not presented as doing so: the finding is a within-subject dissociation, where each child's rate of instructed behaviour is compared against their own rate of spontaneous imitation, so every child serves as their own control and the sample size constrains precision rather than validity." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-033" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Presented in 2019 at an automation and robotics conference by a Polish collaboration between the Department of Cybernetics and Robotics at Wroclaw University of Science and Technology and the Research Unit for Cognitive and Applied Psychology at the University of Lower Silesia - the same group whose 2022 follow-up is charted separately in this review. The framing is distinctive: rather than presenting a method, the paper presents a stage in an interdisciplinary development process, arguing that diagnostic human-robot interaction 'implies the need of close cooperation between psychologists and engineers' and delivering both a psychological result and a set of engineering requirements as co-equal outputs. For the synthesis the study contributes three things. Its finding is a within-construct dissociation rather than a group difference: autistic children performed significantly more behaviours when instructed than spontaneously in imitation (W = 21, p = 0.028), which the authors read as a deficit in automatic rather than intentional turn-taking. That is a claim about the structure of the difficulty, and the measurement principle behind it - separating prompted from unprompted performance to distinguish capacity from spontaneous deployment - has wide application in a corpus where most behavioural measures collapse the two. Its design demonstrates that a paired within-subject contrast can extract an interpretable result from a sample far too small for any classifier, which several similarly sized between-group studies charted here might usefully have adopted. And its stated future direction identifies the field's hardest problem correctly: 'the especially challenging task is to differ autism from other similar disorders', with a commitment to observe robot interactions with children who have other developmental conditions. Almost no study in this review makes that commitment, and the one included study that does test autism against another clinical group found the field's gold-standard observational instrument achieving an AUC of 0.562 - which is the evidence that this priority is the right one. What the study cannot supply is any detection result: no comparison group, no model, no classification, no accuracy, an unreported sample size and no diagnostic ascertainment described." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-033" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Not described in the retrieved text. Children were recruited through an integrative kindergarten and are stated to have autism, with no instrument, criteria, clinician or date given. The group's 2022 study on a related sample reports that diagnoses were delivered by a multidisciplinary team of a psychiatrist, a psychologist and a pedagogue and recorded in kindergarten files, which is likely the same route; if so the underlying ascertainment is genuine clinical judgement, well above the folder-membership and questionnaire-threshold labels charted elsewhere in this review. As reported here, however, the standard is undocumented. The absence matters less than usual because the study makes no diagnostic claim - it does not attempt to distinguish autistic from non-autistic children, and its within-subject finding does not depend on the label being correct for any classification purpose." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-033" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Complete, because nothing is opaque: every value is a human rater's mark against a questionnaire item describing an observable behaviour. The interpretation offered is a psychological claim - a dissociation between automatic and intentional turn-taking - anchored to prior literature on automatic turn-taking deficits, rather than a statement about what a model responded to." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-033" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Turn-taking, decomposed into two forms - and the decomposition is the study's substantive contribution to the review. Behaviour performed in response to an explicit robot instruction is treated as intentional turn-taking; behaviour performed spontaneously, imitating the robot without being told to, is treated as automatic turn-taking. Each is expressed as a proportion of the behaviours required of the child in that game, so the two are directly comparable within a child. The finding is that autistic children reliably do what they are asked and rarely do it unprompted, which the authors read as a specific deficit in automatic rather than intentional turn-taking. Two things make this valuable for the synthesis. It is a within-construct dissociation rather than a level difference, so it says something about the structure of the difficulty rather than only its presence - which almost no other included study attempts. And it identifies a measurement principle with wide application: a paradigm that separates prompted from unprompted performance can distinguish capacity from spontaneous deployment, a distinction that most behavioural measures in this corpus collapse." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-033" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Adequate for a short conference paper and incomplete on participants. Reported: both games, the voice-menu dialogue opening, the robot platform with physical specifications and software environment, the professional backgrounds of the competent raters, the questionnaire-based coding procedure, the normalisation of behaviour counts to proportions of behaviours required in each game, the statistical test with statistic and p-value, ethics approval, parental consent, and the participating kindergarten by name. Not reported in the retrieved text: sample size, ages, sex, diagnostic ascertainment, the questionnaire items themselves, and inter-rater reliability - the last being a real gap given that the competent-raters technique is the study's measurement instrument, and one the group closed in its 2022 follow-up by reporting Kendall's W and subscale alpha." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-033" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A NAO humanoid robot from Aldebaran Robotics - 57 cm tall, 5.4 kg - with its SDK and desktop applications for authoring animations, behaviours and dialogues, plus video recording. The paper notes the availability of ASK NAO (Autism Solutions for Kids), a commercial application pack 'inspired by commonly practiced special education teaching methods (ABA, PECS, TEACCH, DENVER, SCERTS)', which is a useful observation for the review: the platform already carries an autism-specific software ecosystem built around established educational approaches, so robot-mediated assessment can be layered onto infrastructure that exists in practice rather than requiring a purpose-built device. The equipment cost remains high relative to the webcam-and-toys protocols charted elsewhere here, and what it buys is exact reproducibility of the social partner." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-033" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two limitations are stated and both are correctly identified. The first is the absence of a comparison group - 'we observed only autistic children interacting with the robot' - with the remedy given as comparison against typically developing children, which the group subsequently carried out. The second is more valuable and is rare in this corpus: 'the especially challenging task is to differ autism from other similar disorders. Thus, the other direction of our future studies is to observe the interactions performed by the robot and the children with other disorders.' Committing to a non-autistic clinical comparison is a commitment almost no study in this review makes, and the one included study that does test autism against another clinical population found the field's gold-standard observational instrument achieving an AUC of 0.562 - which is the evidence that this stated priority is the right one. What is not addressed: sample size and participant characteristics are unreported; no diagnostic ascertainment is described; inter-rater reliability is not quantified for this study; rater blinding to the hypothesis is not discussed; and no developmental measure separates general cognitive level from autism as an explanation for reduced spontaneous imitation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-033" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No formal keyword list appears in the retrieved text. Terms carried through the paper: human-robot interaction, social robot, NAO, turn-taking, automatic and intentional turn-taking, competent raters, theory of mind, mentalisation, user requirements, evaluation criteria." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-033" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No severity, cognitive, adaptive or language measure was obtained." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-033" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:study_design "Pilot observational study within a robot-mediated interaction paradigm, with human-rated behavioural coding and a within-group non-parametric comparison; also framed explicitly as …" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: ; comparison: None. The authors identify this directly: 'One of the limitation of this study regards to the fact that we observed only autistic children interacting with the robot.'" ] ] ;
