<https://doi.org/10.1145/3079452.3079492> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Machine learning paradigms based on child vocalisations show great promise as an objective marker of developmental disorders such as Autism. In conventional detection systems, hand-crafed acoustic features are usually fed into a discriminative classifer (e. g., Support Vector Machines); however it is well known that the accuracy and robustness of such a system is limited by the size of the associated training data. Tis paper explores, for the frst time, the use of feature representations learnt using a deep Generative Adversarial Network (GAN) for classifying children’s speech afected by developmental disorders. A comparative evaluation of our proposed system with diferent acoustic feature sets is performed on the Child Pathological and Emotional Speech database. Key experimental results presented demonstrate that GAN based methods exhibit competitive performance with the conventional paradigms in terms of the unweighted average recall metric." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Deng J; Cummins N; Schmitt M; Qian K; Ringeval F; Schuller B (2017). Speech-based Diagnosis of Autism Spectrum Condition by Generative. *Proceedings of the 2017 Inte*. https://doi.org/10.1145/3079452.3079492" ;
    dcterms:creator "Cummins N",
        "Deng J",
        "Qian K",
        "Ringeval F",
        "Schmitt M",
        "Schuller B" ;
    dcterms:title "Speech-based Diagnosis of Autism Spectrum Condition by Generative" ;
    bibo:doi "10.1145/3079452.3079492" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_36aa3ace" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2017 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "11 children with autism disorders against 68 controls for a four-way diagnostic claim; minimally verbal children excluded by design." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a clinically informative comparison group included?" ;
                            slr:justification "Specific language impairment as a separate class directly probes specificity, and PDD-NOS is separated from autism disorders." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Group sizes and recruitment sites only; no age, age range, SD or gender for any group." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched?" ;
                            slr:justification "Matching on age, sex, academic grades and lexical abilities is asserted for patients, and 2:1 age/sex matching for controls, but no numbers or tests are given." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "No recording hardware, format, sampling rate, environment, elicitation prompts or segmentation procedure are described; the reader is referred to the corpus papers." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "Acoustic features are extracted automatically by openSMILE and the GAN is trained without labels." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the feature extraction pipeline transparent?" ;
                            slr:justification "Published open feature standards extracted with an open-source toolkit, explicitly for transparency and reproducibility." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "DSM-IV clinical diagnosis without a standardised instrument or reliability statistics; controls screened only by teacher questionnaire." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is there external or independent validation?" ;
                            slr:justification "Single corpus; no second dataset." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is statistical comparison between systems performed?" ;
                            slr:justification "One-sided z-tests with significance markers on the results tables." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the validation scheme free of subject leakage?" ;
                            slr:justification "Partitioning is explicitly child-independent, with all utterances from a child confined to one partition." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the metric appropriate for the class distribution?" ;
                            slr:justification "UAR chosen explicitly for the imbalanced four-class task, with confusion matrices published." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is model selection separated from performance estimation?" ;
                            slr:justification "A dedicated validation partition tunes all parameters, but the reported best discriminator layer differs between feature sets in a way that tracks test rather than validation UAR." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are absolute performance limits reported honestly?" ;
                            slr:justification "UAR is reported against an explicit 25% chance level and confusion matrices reveal the near-floor autism-class recall rather than concealing it." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code or an implementation available?" ;
                            slr:justification "No code or seeds, though architectures and hyperparameters are fully specified." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics, consent and privacy handling reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No ethics approval, consent or anonymization statement for clinical child speech recordings, despite publication at a digital health venue and the presence of a competing-interests declaration." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are sample bias and imbalance discussed?" ;
                            slr:justification "Data scarcity and the confound risk of small-corpus results are discussed at field level, but this study's own class imbalance and demographic gaps are not." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "CPESD is named and cited but no availability, licence or access route is given." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No acquisition hardware is described - microphone, recording format, sampling rate and environment are all absent. Software: openSMILE for eGeMAPS and ComParE extraction, selected explicitly 'for transparency and reproducibility'; GAN generator and discriminator as feed-forward networks trained with SGD (mini-batch 128, LeakyReLU, batch normalisation, tanh output, binary cross-entropy); linear-kernel SVM for classification. The deep-learning framework is not named." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-010" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Reported by the authors: small corpus size in ASC research generally is the paper's motivating problem, and they cite prior work warning that high accuracies on small datasets 'may lead to potential confounds'. Identifiable in review: severe class imbalance (68 TD versus 11 AD children, 4547 versus 149 test-set utterances by the confusion matrices) with the reported UAR concealing near-floor autism recognition; no age or sex data reported at all, so demographic bias cannot be assessed; matching is asserted without numbers; single-country, monolingual, verbally able children only, which excludes minimally verbal autistic children entirely; evaluation is at utterance rather than child level; and the best-layer selection differs between feature sets in a way that suggests the layer choice is being read off test performance rather than validation - the eGeMAPS validation UARs are essentially flat across layers (39.33-40.92%) while the test UARs vary by more than a point." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-010" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Mixed clinic and school setting - patients recruited in two Paris university child and adolescent psychiatry departments, controls from elementary schools. Existing dataset: the Child Pathological and Emotional Speech Database (CPESD), used entirely as secondary data; no new collection." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-010" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Mixed. Strong on the modelling side: the feature sets are published open standards extracted with an open-source toolkit chosen for reproducibility, full network architectures are specified (layer counts, hidden units, activations, normalisation, objective, optimiser, batch size), the SVM complexity parameter is stated to be tuned on validation, and partition sizes are given. Weak on the data side: CPESD access conditions are never stated, the elicitation protocol and recording setup are absent, no code is released, no random seeds are reported, and the exact child-to-partition assignment is not published, so the child-independent split cannot be reproduced." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-010" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Four groups: autism disorders (n=11), PDD-NOS (n=10), specific language impairment (n=13) and typically developing (n=68), totalling 102 children. Group sizes are reported. This is one of the few corpus studies to include a non-autistic clinical comparison group - and notably one (SLI) chosen to probe exactly the specificity question that most ASD-versus-TD studies leave open. It is also one of the few to separate autism disorders from PDD-NOS as distinct classes." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-010" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; nearly 12 hours of audio in total but no per-child session duration, number of sessions or follow-up. The data are identifiable child speech recordings from a clinical population. This paper contains no ethics approval, no consent statement and no anonymization or data-protection provision, deferring entirely to the source corpus citations - a notable gap for a paper published at a digital health venue. A competing-interests declaration is present (none declared), which makes the absence of an ethics statement more conspicuous." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-010" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Speech: spontaneous child vocal production analysed paralinguistically through prosodic, spectral, cepstral and voice-quality descriptors. The introduction explicitly argues that linguistic markers may be unreliable because language skills vary across spectrum subtypes, and that paralinguistic cues are better suited to automatic detection - so linguistic content is deliberately excluded." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-010" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Sample sizes are given (11 AD, 10 PDD-NOS, 13 SLI, 68 TD) but no age, no age range, no standard deviation and no gender distribution is reported anywhere in the paper. Matching is asserted - patients matched among themselves for age, sex, academic grades and lexical abilities, and TD children matched to patients for age and sex at a 2:1 ratio - but with no numbers and no statistical verification. Absence of any age data makes the study impossible to place in the early-diagnosis timeline." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-010" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Unweighted average recall only, chosen explicitly because the four-class task is imbalanced - the methodologically correct choice, and reported on both validation and test partitions. Full four-by-four confusion matrices are published for the best system under each feature set, which is unusually transparent and is what makes the near-floor autism-class performance visible. One-sided z-tests compare systems. No accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, F1 or AUC, and no per-child metric." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-010" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Generalisability is addressed by design: partitioning is 'completely child independent', with all utterances from a given child confined to one partition, and a dedicated validation partition is used for all parameter optimisation while the test partition is 'solely used for the purpose of performance evaluation on unseen children'. This is the correct structure and better than most of the corpus. Class imbalance is addressed at the metric level (UAR) but not by resampling or class weighting, and the confusion matrices show the imbalance is not in fact overcome. No external validation on a second corpus, no held-out site, no bias mitigation, no calibration, and no real-time or deployment analysis." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-010" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Spontaneous speech recordings designed to induce three emotional valence categories (positive, neutral, negative). The elicitation procedure itself is not described in this paper - no prompts, stimuli, interlocutor or session structure are given, only the valence categories and the resulting utterance counts. Recordings were segmented into 6380 utterances by an unspecified procedure." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-010" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "DH'17 - the 2017 ACM International Conference on Digital Health, London. A technology/health-informatics venue rather than an autism or clinical journal, though more health-oriented than the pure signal-processing venues elsewhere in this corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-010" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Single modality; no fusion. Two acoustic feature sets are compared rather than combined, and no combination with clinical, cognitive or demographic data is performed. The future-work section proposes extending the approach to video and physiological signals, but no multimodal work is done here." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-010" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Apply the GAN-based system to other health tasks such as depression detection; extend GAN representation learning to other behavioural signals including video descriptors and to physiological signals such as electrocardiogram and electrodermal activity; and compare and combine GAN-based feature learning with alternative unsupervised representation-learning approaches such as bag-of-words. All the proposed directions are methodological rather than clinical - no validation, replication or population-extension work is proposed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-010" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2017. The paper characterises the field as showing that 'high levels of accuracy can be achieved' for discriminating typically developing children from children with ASC, but immediately qualifies that these results come from 'rather small datasets, and may lead to potential confounds', naming corpus size as 'a major obstacle in the development of robust models which are sufficiently reliable for clinical practice'. It positions itself as the first application of GAN representation learning in computational paralinguistics. This is one of the corpus's most explicit internal critiques of its own literature's headline accuracies." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-010" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No feature importance, saliency, attention or attribution analysis is performed, and no acoustic property is identified as characteristic of any group. The only interpretability-adjacent result is which discriminator hidden layer produces the most useful representation, which speaks to network depth rather than to behaviour. This is one of the least interpretable studies in the corpus, and unusually the paper makes no interpretive claim it cannot support." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-010" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Lexical abilities and academic grades are named as matching variables for the patient groups, but no scores are reported for any group and they are not used in modelling. All children are stated to have communicative verbal skills - an important inclusion constraint that excludes minimally verbal autistic children. Comorbidities are not described beyond the exclusion of learning disorders from the TD group." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-010" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Clinical diagnosis under DSM-IV criteria in two university departments of child and adolescent psychiatry in Paris. Control status established by teacher questionnaire excluding learning disorders. No standardised autism instrument (no ADOS, ADI-R or questionnaire measure) is reported, and no inter-rater reliability is given for the clinical diagnoses that serve as ground truth for a four-way task." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-010" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two-stage representation: hand-crafted acoustic functionals (eGeMAPS 88-dimensional, or ComParE 6373-dimensional) computed per utterance, then transformed into deep embeddings by taking the activations of one of four GAN discriminator hidden layers (256 units each). The comparison across layers is itself a study of representation depth, and yields different optima for the compact and the brute-forced feature set." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-010" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Unsupervised deep representation learning combined with supervised classification. Algorithms: a generative adversarial network whose discriminator acts as a non-linear feature extractor, with hidden-layer activations fed to a linear-kernel SVM. Baselines: linear SVM, RBF-kernel SVM and a four-hidden-layer multilayer perceptron. Notably the GAN is used purely for representation learning, not for the data augmentation that the introduction motivates - the generator's synthetic samples are never used to enlarge the training set." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-010" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with ASC', 'children with autism', 'children suffering from ASC', 'children with AD', 'patients', 'typically developing (TD) children', 'control group'. Person-first but with an explicitly medical framing - 'patients', 'suffering from', 'pathological' (in the corpus name), 'automatic diagnosis'. The paper uses 'Autism Spectrum Conditions' rather than 'Disorders', a comparatively neutral choice, alongside otherwise clinical vocabulary." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-010" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Author keywords: Autism Spectrum Condition; automatic diagnosis; generative adversarial networks; representation learning. CCS concepts: Applied computing - Life and medical sciences, Health informatics; Computing methodologies - Neural networks. Task vocabulary: 'speech-based diagnosis', 'objective marker', 'computational paralinguistics', 'data scarcity', 'representation learning'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-010" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: automatic diagnosis framed as a four-way classification, with the methodological objective of testing GAN representation learning against hand-crafted acoustic features under data scarcity. Main findings: best test UAR 46.93% with ComParE-derived GAN representations and 44.06% with eGeMAPS-derived ones, both significantly above the corresponding linear-SVM baselines (42.83% and 39.91%) at p<0.05, against a 25% chance level. The optimal discriminator layer differed by feature set (bottom layer for ComParE, third layer for eGeMAPS). The autism-disorders class was recognised at close to floor in both systems." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-010" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Almost 12 hours of audio in total across 102 children (7h38m TD, 1h35m autism disorders, 1h12m PDD-NOS, 1h56m SLI), segmented into 6380 utterances; per-child session duration not stated" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional multi-class classification study; secondary analysis of an existing speech corpus, framed as a methods paper" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism disorders, PDD-NOS, and specific language impairment" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 34 (11 autism disorders, 10 PDD-NOS, 13 specific language impairment); comparison: 68" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Chance UAR is 25%. With eGeMAPS: linear SVM 39.91%, RBF-SVM 39.09%, MLP 40.97% test UAR; best GAN representation (third hidden layer) 44.06% test UAR, a 10.40% relative improvement over the linear SVM baseline, significant at p<0.05. With ComParE: linear SVM 42.83%, RBF-SVM 40.40%, MLP 37.61%; best GAN representation (first hidden layer) 46.93%, significant. Confusion matrices show the autism-diso",
                        "GAN-learned representations of standard acoustic feature sets modestly but significantly outperform hand-crafted features fed directly to SVMs or an MLP on a four-way clinical speech classification task, reaching 46.93% UAR against a 25% chance level. The authors present this as the first use of GAN representation learning in computational paralinguistics. The confusion matrices, however, show per" ;
                    slr:outcome "Unweighted average recall (chosen explicitly for the imbalanced four-class task), reported on both validation and test partitions, with full four-by-four confusion matrices and significance markers" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Chance UAR is 25%. With eGeMAPS: linear SVM 39.91%, RBF-SVM 39.09%, MLP 40.97% test UAR; best GAN representation (third hidden layer) 44.06% test UAR, a 10.40% relative improvement over the linear SVM baseline, significant at p<0.05. With ComParE: linear SVM 42.83%, RBF-SVM 40.40%, MLP 37.61%; best GAN representation (first hidden layer) 46.93%, significant. Confusion matrices show the autism-diso; GAN-learned representations of standard acoustic feature sets modestly but significantly outperform hand-crafted features fed directly to SVMs or an MLP on a four-way clinical speech classification task, reaching 46.93% UAR against a 25% chance level. The authors present this as the first use of GAN representation learning in computational paralinguistics. The confusion matrices, however, show per" ] ] ;
