                    slr:supporting_quote "Evaluation on an organiser-held blind test set is a genuine strength and the models are fully specified, but the internal split is partitioned by image rather than participant, so the same child's scanpaths appear in both internal training and validation." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "high" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<urn:slr:article:2859eee06613> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Attention Deﬁcit Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) and Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) are neurodevelopmental conditions which impact on a signiﬁcant number of children and adults. Currently, the diagnosis of such disorders is done by experts who employ standard questionnaires and look for certain behavioural markers through manual observation. Such methods for their diagnosis are not only subjective, difﬁcult to repeat, and costly but also extremely time consuming. In this work, we present a novel methodology to aid diagnostic predictions about the presence/absence of ADHD and ASD by automatic visual analysis of a persons behaviour. To do so, we conduct the questionnaires in a computer-mediated way while recording participants with modern RGBD (Colour+Depth) sensors. In contrast to previous automatic approaches which have focussed only on detecting certain behavioural markers, our approach provides a fully automatic end-to-end system to directly predict ADHD and ASD in adults. Using state of the art facial expression analysis based on Dynamic Deep Learning and 3D analysis of behaviour, we attain classiﬁcation rates of 96% for Controls vs Condition (ADHD/ASD) groups and 94% for Comorbid (ADHD+ASD) vs ASD only group. We show that our system is a potentially useful time saving contribution to the clinical diagnosis of ADHD and ASD. I. INTRODUCTION The last 5 years have seen a steady progress in automatic expressive behaviour analysis, with the detection and tracking of faces [1][2][3][4], recognition of facial muscle actions [5][6][7], and accurate head pose estimation [1][8] all now possible under mild environmental constraints. This has renewed the interest of researchers to employ such behaviour analysis in the medical domain, targeting socalled behaviomedical conditions that alter one’s expressive behaviour [9]. In this paper we use state of the art facial expression analysis and RGBD head motion analysis to help in the diagnosis of Attention Deﬁcit Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) and Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD). ADHD is a neurodevelopmental condition affecting a large number of people and it has been estimated that at least 2.5% of the general adult population is affected by it [10]. ADHD is characterized by symptoms such as hyperactivity, impulsivity, inattention, etc. [11][12]. It usually begins in early childhood and quite often the symptoms persists into adulthood [13]. It is widely believed that both genetic [14] and environmental inﬂuences [15] contribute to the underlying cause of this disorder. Presently, the diagnosis of ADHD is made following the criteria of the DSM-5 [16], Fig. 1: Overview of our system. A participant follows instructions on a screen while being recorded by a Kinect 2 camera. Deep Learning and RGB-D behaviour analysis of each video segment leads to successful ASD/ADHD classiﬁcation. which involve mechanisms to validate hyperactivity, attention deﬁcit and impulsivity. The diagnosis is made by experts using a combin" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Jaiswal S; Valstar MF; Gillott A; Daley D (2017). Automatic Detection of ADHD and ASD from Expressive Behaviour in RGBD Data. *2017 12th IEEE International Conference on Automatic Face & Gesture Recognit*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Daley D",
        "Gillott A",
        "Jaiswal S",
        "Valstar MF" ;
    dcterms:title "Automatic Detection of ADHD and ASD from Expressive Behaviour in RGBD Data" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_3a331973" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2017 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "55 adults across four groups, with cells of 4 and 11; the ADHD-only group had to be abandoned." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched?" ;
                            slr:justification "No matching procedure and no comparability testing on age or gender." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Ages and gender distributions appear only as bar charts; group sizes must be inferred from results tables; prior diagnostic route unspecified." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is control status adequately established?" ;
                            slr:justification "Dual screening on ASRS Part A (threshold 4) and AQ-10 (threshold 6) with explicit cut-offs - a genuine strength." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the elicitation task standardised across participants?" ;
                            slr:justification "Fully computer-mediated with pre-recorded audio, deliberately removing interviewer variability." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Sensor, task, stimulus delivery, segmentation, histogram bin counts, angle ranges, smoothing window and normalisation are all specified." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "AU, animation-unit, pose and motion features are computed automatically by pre-trained detectors." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "Controls screened rigorously; clinical labels rest on unspecified prior diagnoses." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample size adequate for the feature dimensionality?" ;
                            slr:justification "Six feature families across 12 segments (several hundred dimensions) against 55 subjects, 33 for the second task." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is overfitting risk reasoned about?" ;
                            slr:justification "Explicitly - raw-pixel deep learning rejected and feature selection motivated on overfitting grounds, even though the selection itself is not validated correctly." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is there external or independent validation?" ;
                            slr:justification "None." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is class imbalance accounted for in the reported metric?" ;
                            slr:justification "Accuracy alone on 18-vs-37 and 11-vs-22 splits, with no majority-class baseline stated." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the validation scheme free of leakage?" ;
                            slr:justification "Greedy forward feature selection not stated to be nested within the leave-one-subject-out folds; perfect majority-class recall is consistent with selection leakage." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code or an implementation available?" ;
                            slr:justification "None; SVM hyperparameters and the feature-selection stopping criterion are unreported." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are effect directions reported for the informative features?" ;
                            slr:justification "Discriminative features are named and plotted but which group shows more or less of each is never stated." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "KOMAA is announced as a contribution and a community resource but no access route, licence or contact is given." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are sample bias, imbalance and limitations discussed?" ;
                            slr:justification "No limitations section; only the ADHD-only group's small size is acknowledged, and then only as a reason to drop it." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics, consent and privacy handling reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No ethics approval, no consent statement and no anonymization or access control, despite collecting and announcing an identifiable facial-video and voice database of clinically diagnosed adults." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None proposed. The conclusion asserts high potential for facial expressions to predict ADHD, ASD 'and other neurodevelopmental disorders' but sets out no specific research programme, no validation plan and no open questions - unusual even by the standards of a short conference paper." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-008" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Weak. The KOMAA dataset is announced as a contribution but no access conditions, licence, repository or contact route is stated - so a dataset presented as a community resource is in practice unavailable. No code release. Feature construction is described in good detail (histogram bin counts, AU list, angle ranges, smoothing window, normalisation) and would be re-implementable, but SVM hyperparameters, the feature-selection stopping criterion and the final feature-vector dimensionality are not given, and nothing states whether feature selection was nested in the cross-validation. Not reproducible as reported." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-008" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; a single ~12-minute recording per participant with no follow-up. The data are highly sensitive - identifiable RGB facial video plus depth and recorded voice responses from clinically diagnosed adults. Despite this, and despite announcing the resulting database as a contribution to the research community, the paper contains no ethics-approval statement, no participant consent statement, and no anonymization, access-control or data-protection provision. Only funding bodies are acknowledged." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-008" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Poorly reported. Total n=55 adults over 18. Group sizes are recoverable (18 control, 22 ASD only, 4 ADHD only, 11 comorbid) but only from the results tables. Median age per group and gender distribution per group are presented solely as bar charts (Figs. 2 and 3) with no numeric values, no standard deviations and no age range. No matching procedure, and no statistical test of age or gender comparability between groups, is reported. Adults only, so no early-diagnosis relevance." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-008" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Six behavioral feature families are combined by concatenation into a single normalised vector before classification - early/feature-level fusion - with temporal structure preserved by computing each family separately per story segment and concatenating across the 12 segments, which the authors describe as trading a twelvefold dimensionality increase for retained temporal information. No integration with clinical scores or demographics." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-008" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2017. The paper characterises computer vision for ADHD and ASD as 'still in its infancy', with prior work confined to detecting predefined behavioural markers (AOSI markers, self-stimulatory behaviour, social games, engagement) whose 'effectiveness in predicting the actual ADHD and ASD diagnosis still remains to be seen'. It positions itself as one of the first to pose diagnosis directly as a machine-learning problem and, unusually for this corpus, targets adults rather than children." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-008" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Classification accuracy only, supported by raw per-class correct/incorrect counts (which is at least transparent enough to recover the confusion matrix). No sensitivity, specificity, precision, recall, F1, AUC or confidence intervals - a significant gap given the 18-vs-37 and 11-vs-22 class imbalances, where the reported accuracies of 96.4% and 93.9% cannot be compared against the relevant majority-class baselines of 67% and 67% without recomputation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-008" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No cognitive, linguistic or adaptive-functioning measures are reported for any group. ASRS and AQ-10 are used only as control-admission gates and their scores are never reported for the ASD, ADHD or comorbid groups. Comorbidity, by contrast, is handled seriously - ADHD+ASD co-occurrence is a defined group and the subject of the second classification task, motivated by the observation that treatment differs across the three clinical groups." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-008" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Supervised learning. The diagnostic classifier is an RBF-kernel SVM with greedy forward feature selection. A deep CNN (a modified dynamic deep-learning AU architecture) is used upstream as a pre-trained facial action unit detector. The authors explicitly reject end-to-end deep learning on raw pixels because the small training set would cause severe overfitting - a deliberate and well-reasoned choice to use deep learning for representation but not for diagnosis." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-008" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No formal keyword list. The paper's vocabulary: 'automatic detection', 'expressive behaviour', 'RGBD data', 'behaviomedical conditions', 'diagnostic predictions', 'facial action units', 'animation units', 'Strange Stories'. The term 'behaviomedical' - conditions that alter expressive behaviour - is distinctive to this line of work and does not recur elsewhere in the corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-008" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Greedy forward feature selection identifies the discriminative features, with the top three per task visualised and the top 30 ranked by individual classification power. Informative features: head-motion speed (stories 1-2) and lip-corner depressor (story 10) for controls vs condition; inner-brow raiser (story 1), lip-corner puller (story 3) and head yaw (story 8) for comorbid vs ASD-only. Notably the features are segment-specific, implying that particular Strange Stories items carry the signal - an interpretable finding the paper does not pursue. The direction of each group difference is never stated." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-008" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A controlled recording setting with a screen and Kinect camera, with clinical recruitment via an NHS Asperger and ADHD service. Newly collected data - the study contributes the KOMAA dataset and no existing dataset is used." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-008" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Clinical groups: prior clinical diagnosis of ADHD and/or ASD, recruited through the Nottingham City Asperger Service and ADHD Clinic; the diagnostic instruments and criteria behind those prior diagnoses are not described. Controls: actively screened out using two validated instruments with stated thresholds - Adult ADHD Self-Report Scale Part A (threshold 4) and AQ-10 (threshold 6). The control screening is more rigorously specified than the case ascertainment, which is the reverse of the usual pattern in this corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-008" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No limitations section exists, so nothing is acknowledged by the authors beyond the ADHD-only group being too small to model. Identifiable in review: severe class imbalance (18 controls vs 37 condition) reported only as accuracy, where always predicting 'condition' would already score 67%; group sizes of 4 and 11 in two of the four cells; ages and genders never reported numerically and never matched or tested, so demographic confounding cannot be assessed at all; a very high-dimensional feature vector (six feature families x 12 segments) against 55 subjects with greedy forward selection not stated to be nested inside the leave-one-subject-out loop - the single most likely explanation for 37/37 and 22/22 perfect recall on the majority classes; single-site recruitment; and no ethnicity data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-008" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Hardware: Microsoft Kinect 2.0 RGBD sensor capturing high-resolution colour and depth; a computer screen with speakers for stimulus delivery. Software: Kinect v2 library for animation units and 3D head pose; a modified deep CNN facial-action-unit detector (the Jaiswal & Valstar architecture with the BLSTM removed); custom landmark-centroid head-speed computation; SVM with RBF kernel and greedy forward feature selection." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-008" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: a fully automatic end-to-end diagnostic aid predicting presence/absence of ADHD and ASD in adults directly from video, explicitly contrasted with prior work that only detected predefined behavioural markers. Main findings: 96.4% accuracy for controls vs condition (16/18 controls and 37/37 condition correct) and 93.9% for comorbid vs ASD-only (9/11 and 22/22 correct); head-motion speed and lip-corner depressor activity were most discriminative for the first task, inner-brow raiser, lip-corner puller and head yaw for the second." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-008" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'subjects who have been diagnosed with ASD', 'individuals with ADHD', 'people with ASD', 'subjects with ADHD/ASD', 'the ASD group', 'the Condition group', 'healthy controls', 'healthy individuals', 'control group'. Person-first for the clinical groups, but the comparison group is repeatedly framed as 'healthy', which implicitly positions autism as ill-health rather than difference - a framing choice worth noting against the more neutral 'typically developing' used elsewhere in this corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-008" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A computer-mediated version of the Strange Stories task: 12 short stories displayed on screen and simultaneously read aloud by a pre-recorded voice, each followed by 2-3 questions answered aloud. The design deliberately simulates a human interviewer while removing the interviewer, so that the stimulus is identical across participants. Video was manually segmented into the 12 story periods for feature aggregation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-008" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Four groups: controls (n=18), ASD only (n=22), ADHD only (n=4) and comorbid ADHD+ASD (n=11), totalling 55 adults. Group sizes must be inferred from the classification tables and a bar chart rather than a participant table. The ADHD-only group was explicitly too small (n=4) to train a classifier for, so the reported analyses collapse to controls-vs-condition and comorbid-vs-ASD-only. This is one of very few corpus studies with both a non-autistic clinical group and an explicit comorbid group." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-008" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Leave-one-subject-out cross-validation, which is subject-independent at the outer level. Overfitting is explicitly reasoned about in two places - rejecting raw-pixel deep learning, and motivating feature selection because 'any classifier trained directly on the entire feature-set is most likely to overfit'. But there is no statement that feature selection was nested within folds, no external or independent validation, no held-out site, no class-imbalance handling or metric, no statistical testing, and no deployment or real-time evaluation despite the time-saving clinical framing. The comorbid classifier is trained on 33 subjects total." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-008" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Histogram-based aggregation of frame-level descriptors: AU intensities, animation-unit intensities, head-pose angles and head speeds are each binned into per-segment histograms; cumulative distance and response times enter as scalars; everything is length-normalised by frame count and Z-scored. This is a handcrafted statistical representation built on top of deep-learned per-frame features rather than a learned embedding of the video." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-008" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "IEEE International Conference on Automatic Face and Gesture Recognition (FG) 2017 - a technology/computational venue (computer vision), not an autism or clinical venue." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-008" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Facial expression (FACS action units and Kinect animation units), head pose in 3D, head-motion speed and cumulative head displacement, plus response latency to spoken questions. Voice is recorded for the answers but no acoustic or linguistic analysis of it is performed - only the time taken to respond is used." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-008" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Single session; approximately 12 minutes of video per participant, segmented into 12 story segments" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional case-control classification study with a newly collected dataset (KOMAA)" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder, ADHD, and comorbid ADHD+ASD" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 37 in the combined condition group (ASD only n=22 by the classification tables, ADHD only n=4, comorbid ADHD+ASD n=11); comparison: 18 (from the classification table: 16 correct + 2 incorrect)" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Controls vs Condition (ADHD/ASD/both): 96.4% accuracy - controls 16/18 correct, condition 37/37 correct. Comorbid (ADHD+ASD) vs ASD-only: 93.9% accuracy - comorbid 9/11 correct, ASD-only 22/22 correct. The ADHD-only group (n=4) was too small to model",
                        "Facial expression, head pose and head motion extracted automatically from 12 minutes of RGBD video during a Strange Stories task discriminated adults with ADHD and/or ASD from screened controls at 96.4% accuracy, and separated comorbid ADHD+ASD from ASD-only at 93.9%. The authors present this as the first work to learn models predicting ADHD/ASD diagnosis directly from high-level facial features r" ;
                    slr:outcome "Classification accuracy only, reported alongside raw correct/incorrect counts per class" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Controls vs Condition (ADHD/ASD/both): 96.4% accuracy - controls 16/18 correct, condition 37/37 correct. Comorbid (ADHD+ASD) vs ASD-only: 93.9% accuracy - comorbid 9/11 correct, ASD-only 22/22 correct. The ADHD-only group (n=4) was too small to model; Facial expression, head pose and head motion extracted automatically from 12 minutes of RGBD video during a Strange Stories task discriminated adults with ADHD and/or ASD from screened controls at 96.4% accuracy, and separated comorbid ADHD+ASD from ASD-only at 93.9%. The authors present this as the first work to learn models predicting ADHD/ASD diagnosis directly from high-level facial features r" ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "81278ed75822c3a417eb9f8a11662accac96437c9586cd7b2a467662e0c96a7d" ;
