<https://doi.org/10.1007/s12369-020-00639-8> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "We propose a robot-mediated therapy and assessment system for children with autism spectrum disorder (ASD) of mild to moderate severity and minimal verbal capabilities. The objectives of the robot interaction sessions is to improve the academic capabilities of ASD patients by increasing the length and the quality of their attention. The system uses a NAO robot and an added mobile display to present emotional cues and solicit appropriate emotional responses. The interaction is semi-autonomous with minimal human intervention. Interaction occurs within an adaptive dynamic scenario composed of 13 sections. The scenario allows adaptive customization based on the attention score history of each patient. The attention score is autonomously generated by the system and depends on face attention and joint attention cues and sound responses. The scoring system allows us to prove that the customized interaction system increases the engagement and attention capabilities of ASD patients. After performing a pilot study, involving 6 ASD children, out of a total of 11 considered in the clinical setup, we conducted a long-term study. This study empirically proves that the proposed assessment system represents the attention state of the patient with 82.4% accuracy." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Alnajjar F; Cappuccio M; Renawi A; Mubin O; Loo CK (2021). Personalized Robot Interventions for Autistic Children: An Automated Methodology for Attention Assessment. *International Journal of Social Robot*. https://doi.org/10.1007/s12369-020-00639-8" ;
    dcterms:creator "Alnajjar F",
        "Cappuccio M",
        "Loo CK",
        "Mubin O",
        "Renawi A" ;
    dcterms:title "Personalized Robot Interventions for Autistic Children: An Automated Methodology for Attention Assessment" ;
    bibo:doi "10.1007/s12369-020-00639-8" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_9ca9b2cc" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2021 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participant selection and spectrum representativeness" ;
                            slr:justification "Eleven autistic boys from one hospital, restricted to mild-to-moderate CARS2 severity and to children with at least yes/no verbal capability - a narrow band, and one that excludes the non-speaking children the abstract's phrase 'minimal verbal capabilities' would suggest." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Comparison group adequacy" ;
                            slr:justification "No comparison group and no control condition of any kind, so the longitudinal attention increase has nothing to be compared against." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Confounder control" ;
                            slr:justification "No control for familiarisation with the robot, task practice, maturation or study attention over a seven-week course - all of which would raise a score whose components reward orienting toward and responding to a novel object." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Handling of missing data and attrition" ;
                            slr:justification "Five of eleven children did not continue into the long-term study and no reason is given; if disengagement drove attrition, the reported attention trajectory is computed on children already engaging." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reference standard quality" ;
                            slr:justification "Clinical diagnosis with a reported CARS2 band defines the population well. The therapist's 0-100 attention ratings serving as the validation standard have no stated procedure, blinding or reliability estimate." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Measurement standardisation" ;
                            slr:justification "A fixed 13-section scenario delivered by the same robot with the same detectors - but the scenario adapts per child by design, and the scoring constants are operator-adjustable per patient, so the instrument is deliberately non-uniform across children." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Index test definition and prespecification" ;
                            slr:justification "All three scoring rules and their constants are stated explicitly (+5 face detected, -1 not detected, +15 joint-attention head turn, linear speech-latency function), with a stated update rate." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome measurement blinding" ;
                            slr:justification "The automated score is computed without human input, but the therapist ratings against which it is validated were made by the treating therapist with no blinding described." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "unclear" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Sample size adequacy for the modelling" ;
                            slr:justification "Six children completing a seven-week course, contributing 34 sessions - adequate for a feasibility demonstration and not for either the agreement estimate or the intervention claim." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance estimation and chance level" ;
                            slr:justification "An uncorrected agreement rate of 82.4% over 34 sessions clustered within 6 children, with no chance-corrected statistic, no clustering adjustment, no confidence interval and no reliability estimate for the comparison standard." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data splitting and leakage control" ;
                            slr:justification "Not applicable; nothing is fitted, so there is no training set and no partitioning question." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Preprocessing and feature selection nesting" ;
                            slr:justification "Not applicable. Detectors are off-the-shelf and scoring constants are set a priori by the operator rather than derived from the data." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "unclear" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Ethics, consent and conflicts" ;
                            slr:justification "Approval from the Social Sciences Research Ethical Committee of UAE University and from Al Ain Hospital, with signed parental consent letters. Funding and competing interests are not stated." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Completeness of methods reporting" ;
                            slr:justification "Eligibility criteria, CARS2 band, mean age and SD, recruitment site, robot and chest-mount engineering, camera configuration, detector types, all scoring rules and constants, update rate and scenario structure are reported. Session duration, the content of the 13 sections, the therapist rating procedure and the reason for attrition are not." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Acknowledgement of limitations" ;
                            slr:justification "A well-judged analysis of the field's constraints, a reported practical failure with its fix, and the causal claim explicitly labelled a hypothesis. The absence of a control condition, the unexplained attrition and the uncorrected agreement statistic are not addressed." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reporting of results and uncertainty" ;
                            slr:justification "A single uncorrected agreement figure with its numerator and denominator, and session-level attention scores, with no chance-corrected statistic, confidence interval or clustering adjustment anywhere." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data, code and protocol availability" ;
                            slr:justification "No data or code availability statement and no preregistration - though the scoring rules are specified precisely enough to reimplement." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "unclear" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Predominantly medical, with 'ASD patients' used throughout and 'recovery progress' appearing in the discussion of what current systems cannot track - a framing that treats autism as something one recovers from. Against this, the introduction opens with a genuinely thoughtful question about what social robots are for, asking whether robots might help people 'establish richer relationships with other humans, fulfilling their inherent empathic potential', and framing the issue as most pressing for 'individuals who are prevented by clinical factors from establishing typical social relations with others'. The clinical reasoning is also person-centred in a practical way: the authors note that assessment is usually done at enrolment and repeated occasionally, that more frequent assessment would let therapists adapt treatment and would help 'patients and their parents ... be better motivated to withstand long, repetitive therapy sessions', and that success depends on knowing which stimuli hold a particular child's attention. That is an argument for measurement in service of individualised therapy rather than for classification, and it is why the study has no diagnostic target. No autistic person is reported as involved in the design." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-067" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Clinical diagnosis established through the recruiting hospital, with severity quantified by CARS2 and reported as a 30-36.5 range for all participants. This is genuine clinical ascertainment, and specifying the severity band is a real strength - it makes the population interpretable in a way that a bare 'diagnosed with ASD' does not, and it bounds the claims to children with mild-to-moderate presentation. What is not reported is the instrument set beyond CARS2, the assessors, or the dates. Because the study makes no diagnostic claim, the reference standard's role here is to define the population rather than to serve as a target, and for that purpose it is adequate." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-067" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Longitudinal in design - a seven-week course of sessions per child, with 34 sessions contributing to the agreement analysis - which distinguishes it from the almost uniformly cross-sectional remainder of this review. Repeated measurement within children is the study's methodological point rather than an incidental feature. Ethics reporting is complete: approval by the Social Sciences Research Ethical Committee of UAE University and by Al Ain Hospital, with signed parental consent letters. Funding and competing interests are not stated in the retrieved text. Attrition is substantial and unexplained: 6 of 11 children completed the long-term study." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-067" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Eleven autistic boys recruited from Al Ain Hospital in the United Arab Emirates, all with CARS2 scores between 30 and 36.5 - the mild-to-moderate band - under 16 years of age with a mean of 9.03 years (SD 2.56), and all required to have at least minimal verbal capability, being able to understand verbal communication and answer yes or no. Six of the eleven continued into the long-term study, with no explanation given for the other five. There is no comparison group of any kind, which is consistent with the design: the study measures attention within an intervention rather than discriminating between populations. Two features of the sample are worth recording for the review. Reporting a CARS2 range as an eligibility criterion is more severity characterisation than most included studies provide, and it bounds the population precisely - but because only the range is given rather than per-child scores, within-sample severity variation cannot be related to any outcome. And the eligibility requirements taken together - male, mild to moderate, verbally responsive - define a narrow population, so the system's behaviour with non-speaking or more severely affected children is untested despite the abstract describing the target group as having 'minimal verbal capabilities'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-067" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "One agreement figure - 82.4%, from 6 mismatches in 34 sessions against the therapist's 0-100 attention ratings - plus session-by-session attention scores for the longitudinal arm. The metric is the right kind for the claim, since concurrent agreement with a clinician's judgement of the same construct is what a therapy-support measure needs to demonstrate, and the paper describes it correctly as matching therapist assessments rather than as diagnostic accuracy. Three gaps: no chance-corrected statistic such as Cohen's kappa, so the proportion of agreement attributable to the marginal distribution of therapist ratings is unknown; no adjustment for the clustering of 34 sessions within 6 children; and no reliability estimate for the therapist ratings themselves, so the achievable ceiling is unknown." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-067" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The introduction contains an unusually clear analysis of the field's constraints, which functions as a limitations discussion of the wider literature: most preprogrammed robot systems 'have fixed behavior (i.e., they are unable to autonomously perform adaptable closed-loop interactions), are not tailored to the individual needs of patients, and cannot keep track of their recovery progress', while adaptive systems 'need high-performance hardware, such as GPUs chips, to process real-time data' and 'are not yet reliable outside controlled research' settings. The discussion reports a practical failure - children handling the chest display in early sessions, depressing scores, addressed by fixing the display - and the causal claim about attention improvement is labelled a hypothesis. What is not addressed: there is no control condition, so the seven-week rise in attention scores cannot be separated from familiarisation with the robot, task practice, maturation or study attention, and the score's own components would be expected to rise as a child grows comfortable with the apparatus. Attrition of 5 of 11 children is unexplained, and if disengagement drove it then the trend is computed on the children who were already engaging. The 82.4% agreement is computed over sessions clustered within children with no adjustment, no chance-corrected statistic and no confidence interval, and no reliability estimate exists for the therapist ratings serving as the standard. Scoring constants are operator-set per child, so scores are not comparable across children. And equating a detected face with attending is a strong assumption for children who may attend without orienting." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-067" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "An Aldebaran/SoftBank NAO robot with a custom-designed chest mount holding a mobile phone, the phone camera configured as an IP camera through a mobile application, and Haar cascade classifiers performing face and ear detection at approximately 1 Hz. The design point worth recording is that the sensing is the robot's own - the camera that measures the child is mounted on the thing the child is attending to - so the measurement apparatus and the social stimulus are the same object. That is more elegant than the arrangements charted elsewhere in this review, where a separate camera observes a child interacting with something else, and it means the face-detection geometry directly encodes orientation toward the robot. The cost is that the measure is only as good as the assumption behind it, and a low-frame-rate Haar cascade detecting a face is a coarse proxy for attending." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-067" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: Autism, Robotics, Assessment, Attention, Therapy. Accurate and complete - naming the population, the technology, the purpose, the construct and the application. Unusually, the keyword set correctly signals that the study is about assessment within therapy rather than about diagnosis, which is not true of several titles in this corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-067" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The authors propose sharing assessment data 'locally, within the same team or clinic, or globally as integrated clinical dataset sharable by professionals in different places through interoperable systems', and continued development of semi-autonomous adaptive robots that recognise behavioural cues and respond accordingly. The steps the study most needs are a control condition to separate intervention effect from familiarisation, an account of attrition, a chance-corrected agreement statistic with clustering adjustment, and reliability data for the therapist ratings." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-067" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "An autism rehabilitation centre, with participants recruited from Al Ain Hospital - a clinical service setting, and the same setting in which the system would be deployed. That alignment between study setting and intended use setting is shared by only a few studies in this review. The clinical framing is specific: the authors note that assessment is typically performed at enrolment and repeated during long-term therapy, and that if it 'could be repeated more frequently to trace the severity and development of symptoms, then therapists could implement more efficacious diagnostic and therapeutic options' - so the setting defines the problem, which is the cost in expert time of repeated assessment." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-067" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three streams combined into one running score - face detection, ear-based head orientation, and speech-response latency - fused by weighted accumulation with operator-set constants rather than by a learned combiner. The weights encode a clinical judgement about relative importance (joint attention worth three times face attention, non-attention penalised lightly), which is transparent and adjustable but not empirically derived. No ablation examines what each stream contributes to the 82.4% agreement, which would have been informative about whether the joint-attention component - the most clinically specific and the hardest to detect from ear cascades - is carrying its weight." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-067" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Complete and by design. Every component of the score is a stated rule with a stated constant, adjustable per child by the operator, so a therapist can read the derivation of any score directly and can tune the measure to a particular patient. Against the learned models charted throughout this review, this is the opposite design point: nothing is fitted, so nothing requires post-hoc explanation, and the trade-off is that calibration is asserted rather than derived. For a system intended to run inside a clinical session and inform a therapist's next move, that trade is defensible." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-067" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Attention and engagement, decomposed into three operationally defined components with explicit rules. Face attention: orientation toward the robot's chest display, detected by Haar cascade, scoring +5 when the face is detected and -1 when it is not. Joint attention: a head turn matching the robot's directed gaze, detected through left or right ear detection, scoring +15 - the largest weight in the system, and appropriately so, since joint attention is the more specific construct. Sound response: latency from prompt to vocal response, converted by a fixed linear formula. The construct choice is well matched to the clinical purpose the paper sets out, which is not diagnosis but therapy targeting: the authors argue that 'one of the most important preconditions for the success of these interventions is the knowledge and availability of the kinds of stimuli that usually draw the attention of the child and increase his or her engagement over time'. Attention is what a therapist needs to know moment by moment to steer a session, and it is the quantity the system measures and feeds back. The weakness is the operationalisation of face detection as attention, which equates orienting with attending and will misclassify a child who listens without looking." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-067" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Strong on the system and incomplete on the study. Reported: eligibility criteria including the CARS2 band and the verbal-response requirement, mean age with standard deviation, recruitment site, both ethics approvals, the parental consent procedure, the robot and the custom chest-mount with its mass-distribution justification, the phone-as-IP-camera configuration, the detector types, all three scoring rules with their exact constants (+5, -1, +15 and a linear latency function), the 1 Hz update rate, the 13-section scenario structure, the adaptive personalisation mechanism, the number of sessions in the agreement analysis and the exact number of mismatches, and a practical failure mode with its fix. Not reported: session duration, the content of the 13 sections, why 5 of 11 children did not continue, the therapist rating procedure and its reliability, funding and competing interests. A reader could rebuild the scoring system and could not reproduce the study." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-067" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The aim is a robot-mediated therapy and assessment system that measures a child's attention automatically, adapts its own interaction scenario to that measurement, and thereby increases engagement over a course of sessions. Two results are reported. The automated attention score agreed with the treating therapist's assessment on 28 of 34 sessions, giving 82.4%, which the conclusion describes accurately as 'matching over 80% of therapist assessments'. And attention scores rose across a seven-week course in most of the six children who continued, from which the authors draw an explicitly hypothetical conclusion - 'our hypothesis is that a properly designed robot intervention system can increase the attention levels'. Neither claim concerns autism detection: all participants are autistic, there is no comparison group, and no classification is attempted. For this review the study contributes a different kind of evidence - a demonstration that an automated behavioural measure can track a clinician's judgement of the same construct well enough to be usable in a therapy loop, which is the closed-loop application that most of the corpus's detection-oriented work does not consider." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-067" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Age is reported as a mean with standard deviation and severity as a CARS2 eligibility range, both of which exceed the corpus norm. Sex is not a variable, since all eleven participants are male. What is absent is cognitive and language measurement: the verbal-response criterion is a functional floor rather than an assessed level, so the children's language ability is bounded below and not otherwise characterised, and no IQ, adaptive or developmental measure was obtained. Since the outcome is attention during a verbally mediated robot interaction, and the sound-response component of the score is a speech-latency measure, language ability is directly implicated in the measurement and is unmeasured. No socioeconomic, ancestry or comorbidity information is reported." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-067" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A semi-autonomous adaptive scenario in 13 sections, delivered by a NAO robot with a mobile phone mounted on a purpose-built chest holder that displays facial expressions through an Emotions Selector application, presenting emotional cues and soliciting emotional responses. The paradigm's distinctive property is that it is closed-loop: the scenario adapts across sessions according to each child's attention score history, so the measurement feeds back into the intervention. No other study in this review implements that loop - the rest measure behaviour and stop. The engineering description is also careful in a way that matters practically: the chest mount was designed so that the added weight 'barely affects the center of mass of the robot body, hence the changes in the moment of inertia are negligible', preserving the robot's balance and its built-in interactive behaviours, which is the kind of detail that determines whether a modified platform actually works in a clinic. What is not reported is the session duration or the content of the 13 sections, so the paradigm cannot be reproduced from the paper." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-067" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Published 2021 by a group at United Arab Emirates University with Al Ain Hospital, and collaborators at RIKEN, the University of New South Wales, Western Sydney University and the University of Malaya. The framing separates it from most of this corpus: the problem identified is not detection but the cost of repeated assessment during therapy - assessment 'is typically performed at the time of enrollment and repeated during long-term therapy to measure progress', and if it 'could be repeated more frequently to trace the severity and development of symptoms, then therapists could implement more efficacious diagnostic and therapeutic options, and patients and their parents would be better motivated to withstand long, repetitive therapy sessions'. For this review the study contributes three things that the detection-oriented literature does not. It closes the loop: the automated attention measure feeds back into the interaction scenario, which adapts to each child's score history across sessions, so measurement serves intervention rather than terminating in a classification - no other study in the corpus implements this. It demonstrates what is achievable without any learned model: three transparent rules with operator-adjustable constants, running at 1 Hz on a phone camera mounted to the robot the child is attending to, agreeing with the treating therapist's judgement on 28 of 34 sessions. And it is longitudinal, tracking six children across a seven-week course, in a corpus that is otherwise almost entirely cross-sectional. Its evidential weight is nonetheless limited in ways that should be stated: there is no comparison group and no control condition, so the observed rise in attention cannot be separated from familiarisation, practice or maturation; 5 of 11 children did not complete and no reason is given; the 82.4% is an uncorrected agreement rate over sessions clustered within six children, against a therapist standard of unknown reliability; and the population is narrow - male, mild to moderate, verbally capable - despite the abstract describing the target as children with minimal verbal capabilities. The authors' own restraint is worth noting: the causal claim is presented as a hypothesis, and the system is positioned as a tool for therapists rather than as a diagnostic instrument." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-067" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "CARS2 is used as an eligibility range rather than as a per-child variable, so no severity gradient is available for analysis - a missed opportunity, since relating attention trajectories to baseline severity across six children in a seven-week course would have been the natural individualisation question and the data existed to ask it. The study's own outcome measure, the automated attention score, functions as a repeated within-child measure and is used that way: its whole design rationale is that assessment could be 'repeated more frequently to trace the severity and development of symptoms'. That is a severity-tracking argument, and it is the clearest statement in this review of why continuous behavioural measurement might matter clinically for reasons other than diagnosis." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-067" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Not applicable in the classification sense - no model is trained, no individual is assigned a class, and no autism prediction is attempted. The relevant validation question is whether the automated attention measure tracks the construct it claims to measure, and the study answers it with concurrent validation against an independent human standard: agreement with the treating therapist's 0-100 attention ratings on 28 of 34 sessions, reported as 82.4%. That is the right design for the claim and it is described accurately, with the conclusion stating that the system matches 'over 80% of therapist assessments' rather than presenting the figure as diagnostic performance. Four qualifications bound it. The 34 sessions come from 6 children and are therefore clustered, with no adjustment reported, so the effective sample is closer to 6 than to 34. No chance-corrected agreement statistic is given, so how much of the 82.4% reflects the marginal distribution of the therapist's ratings is unknown. No reliability estimate exists for those ratings, so the ceiling on achievable agreement is unknown - and a measure agreeing 82.4% with a standard of unknown reliability could be tracking either the construct or that particular therapist. And the scoring constants are operator-adjustable per child, which is clinically sensible but means the score is not a fixed instrument and its agreement with a clinician may partly reflect tuning. The separate longitudinal claim - that attention rose across seven weeks - has no control condition at all, so it cannot be separated from familiarisation with a novel robot, practice on a repeated task, maturation, or the attention of being in a study; and with 5 of 11 children not completing and no reason given, the trend is computed on a self-selected subgroup. The authors label the causal claim a hypothesis, which is the correct standing for it." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-067" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A scalar attention score accumulated at approximately 1 Hz across a session, with session-level trends used both for the agreement analysis and for adapting the next session's scenario. Every increment is traceable to a detection event and a stated constant." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-067" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No model is trained. The system is rule-based throughout: off-the-shelf Haar cascade classifiers perform face and ear detection as measurement infrastructure, and the attention score is accumulated by hand-specified increments with operator-adjustable constants. There is no training set, test set, cross-validation or fitted parameter anywhere. For a review of computational approaches this is a useful boundary case, because it shows what can be achieved without learning: a transparent, adjustable, real-time measure that tracks a clinician's judgement on 82.4% of sessions and closes a therapy loop. The corresponding limitation is that the constants are asserted rather than derived, so the score's calibration rests on the operator's judgement rather than on evidence." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-067" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "International Journal of Social Robotics 13:67-82, 2021; accepted 25 February 2020, published online 6 March 2020. A social robotics venue, and the article reads accordingly - the system engineering, the chest-mount mass distribution, the detector configuration and the scoring rules are all specified precisely, while the clinical evaluation carries no control condition, no chance-corrected agreement statistic and no account of attrition. The venue's orientation also explains the study's most valuable feature, the closed measurement-intervention loop, which is a robotics design idea rather than a clinical-trial one." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-067" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:study_design "Single-arm clinical pilot and longitudinal intervention study, with concurrent validation of an automated attention-measurement system against therapist ratings" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: ; comparison: None. There is no non-autistic comparison group of any kind, which is consistent with the study's purpose - it measures attention within an intervention rather than discriminating between groups." ] ] ;
