    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "The predictor set contains the A1-A10 items and the composite Q_SCORE while the target is a four-level discretisation of a weighted sum of those same items. Domain weights were calibrated on the full dataset before cross-validation, and SHAP-based feature pruning - which requires a fitted model - was also applied to the whole dataset." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Secondary use of four harmonised public screening datasets with no recruitment, eligibility, setting or dates described; per-cohort and per-class sample counts are not reported anywhere, and sex, ethnicity, country and respondent type were removed during feature pruning." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "unclear" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Stratified 10-fold cross-validation at record level with early stopping and class weighting, plus quantified sensitivity analyses on imputation, thresholds and weights. But no class counts, no per-cohort sample sizes and no confidence intervals or across-fold variance for any accuracy." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "There is no clinician-annotated severity label, a fact the paper states as its motivating gap. The single external anchor is a kappa of 0.72 against expert assessments on a randomly sampled subset, with the number of records, the experts' qualifications and the instrument used all unreported." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "high" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bpsc.2020.09.001> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "BACKGROUND: Imitation deﬁcits are prevalent in autism spectrum conditions (ASCs) and are associated with core autistic traits. Imitating others’ actions is central to the development of social skills in typically developing populations, as it facilitates social learning and bond formation. We present a Computerized Assessment of Motor Imitation (CAMI) using a brief (1-min), highly engaging video game task. METHODS: Using Kinect Xbox motion tracking technology, we recorded 48 children (27 with ASCs, 21 typically developing) as they imitated a model’s dance movements. We implemented an algorithm based on metric learning and dynamic time warping that automatically detects and evaluates the important joints and returns a score considering spatial position and timing differences between the child and the model. To establish construct validity and reliability, we compared imitation performance measured by the CAMI method to the more traditional human observation coding (HOC) method across repeated trials and two different movement sequences. RESULTS: Results revealed poorer imitation in children with ASCs than in typically developing children (ps , .005), with poorer imitation being associated with increased core autism symptoms. While strong correlations between the CAMI and HOC methods (rs = .69–.87) conﬁrmed the CAMI’s construct validity, CAMI scores classiﬁed the children into diagnostic groups better than the HOC scores (accuracyCAMI = 87.2%, accuracyHOC = 74.4%). Finally, by comparing repeated movement trials, we demonstrated high test-retest reliability of CAMI (rs = .73–.86). CONCLUSIONS: Findings support the CAMI as an objective, highly scalable, directly interpretable method for assessing motor imitation differences, providing a promising biomarker for deﬁning biologically meaningful ASC subtypes and guiding intervention. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bpsc.2020.09.001 Imitating others’ actions is crucial for social bond formation and learning (1–3), with atypical imitation indicating socialcommunicative impairments in autism spectrum conditions (ASCs) (4–8). The current standard in imitation assessment is manual human observation coding (HOC), which is subjective and time-consuming and requires intensive coder training. These drawbacks render HOC impractical for use in clinics and home settings. Automatic assessment of imitation is challenging because human motion data are highly heterogeneous (e.g., range of movements is virtually unlimited) and high dimensional (motion data involve spatial and temporal aspects), and human supervision (e.g., expert knowledge) is limited and error prone. Addressing these issues, we present a Computerized Assessment of Motor Imitation (CAMI) that can improve diagnosis and treatment efforts by providing an objective, continuous, and scalable score of imitation performance. Examining imitation performance with HOC methods requires identifying individual steps involved in an action, the action’s style, the" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Tunçgenç B; Pacheco C; Rochowiak R; Nicholas R; Rengarajan S; Zou E; Messenger B; Vidal R; Mostofsky SH (2020). Computerized Assessment of Motor Imitation as a Scalable Method for Distinguishing Children With Autism. **. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bpsc.2020.09.001" ;
    dcterms:creator "Messenger B",
        "Mostofsky SH",
        "Nicholas R",
        "Pacheco C",
        "Rengarajan S",
        "Rochowiak R",
        "Tunçgenç B",
        "Vidal R",
        "Zou E" ;
    dcterms:title "Computerized Assessment of Motor Imitation as a Scalable Method for Distinguishing Children With Autism" ;
    bibo:doi "10.1016/j.bpsc.2020.09.001" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_157d6266" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2020 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is general motor ability controlled for?" ;
                            slr:justification "MABC-2 administered explicitly to account for autism-associated differences in general motor ability, separating imitation-specific from general motor difficulty." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched?" ;
                            slr:justification "Group comparison test statistics are tabulated but the values are not recoverable here." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "48 children aged 8-12 with IQ at or above 80; the authors concede the sample is relatively small given phenotypic heterogeneity." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Recruitment route, inclusion criteria, IQ threshold and instrument battery are specified; the demographic table values are not recoverable from the extracted text." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the task designed to elicit the specific construct?" ;
                            slr:justification "Unfamiliar, goal-less, multi-limb movements chosen on the basis of prior evidence that autistic imitation difficulty is most pronounced for such actions." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "Joint extraction, importance estimation and DTW alignment are fully automatic; metric-learning weights are learned on training folds only." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Camera model, frame rate, dual placement, display setup, training phase, movement sequence composition and selection rationale, trial structure and speed restriction all specified." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "DSM-5 confirmed on site by research-reliable assessors using ADOS-2, ADI-R and SRS-2, with ADOS module standardised." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the scoring computed independently of the comparator?" ;
                            slr:justification "CAMI scores are calculated on held-out folds without reference to the HOC scores they are validated against." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is there external or independent validation?" ;
                            slr:justification "None; single cohort." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is reliability established?" ;
                            slr:justification "Test-retest correlations of r = .73-.86 across repeated trials of two movement sequences." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are sensitivity and specificity reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Only accuracy is given for the classification comparison." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is sample-size adequacy argued quantitatively?" ;
                            slr:justification "1-in-10 rule for the three-variable metric learning and Raudys's expected-error formula yielding 5.9% against a 2.8% floor." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the cross-validation design justified and checked?" ;
                            slr:justification "Fold count justified against bias-variance literature and analyses replicated at ten folds with the same findings." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No data availability statement in the extracted text." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the comparison against the existing standard made directly?" ;
                            slr:justification "CAMI and human observation coding are compared head to head on the same children, with the algorithm outperforming expert coding." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are limitations addressed substantively?" ;
                            slr:justification "Sample size addressed with two quantitative arguments and comparison against named prior studies; scalability limitation conceded regarding Kinect manual processing." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Algorithm equations and parameter values are supplied in a supplement; no code release stated." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the method validated psychometrically as well as computationally?" ;
                            slr:justification "Construct validity against the expert standard, test-retest reliability, and convergent validity with two independent symptom instruments." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics, consent and assent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Named IRB approval prior to commencement, written guardian consent, verbal child assent, and participant compensation." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "27 ASC and 21 TD children aged 8-12 years. Participant characteristics including means, standard deviations, ranges and group comparison test statistics are presented in Table 1, but the values are not recoverable from the extracted text, so age and sex matching cannot be independently verified in this charting. IQ is controlled by inclusion criterion - full-scale IQ of at least 80 or at least one WISC-V index score of at least 80 - which restricts the sample to children of broadly average or above cognitive ability. General motor ability was additionally measured with the Movement Assessment Battery for Children-2 specifically 'to account for autism-associated differences in general motor abilities', which is an unusually thoughtful covariate choice for an imitation study since it separates imitation-specific from general motor difficulty." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-039" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Single modality, with a designed internal fusion: spatial distance and two temporal asynchrony measures (lag and advance) are combined by learned linear weights into one continuous score, so spatial and temporal aspects of imitation are integrated rather than treated separately. The authors argue this integration is necessary 'given the importance of timing in social coordination and in characterizing autism-specific imitation impairments'. Separately, the CAMI score is validated against and correlated with independent clinical instruments (ADOS-2, SRS-2, MABC-2), which is convergent validation rather than fusion." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-039" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No data or code release is stated in the extracted text, and substantial methodological detail - the CAMI equations, parameter learning formula, parameter values, full inclusion and exclusion criteria, study setup, and supplementary ten-fold replication - is deferred to a supplement. What is in the main text is precise: the five-step algorithm is described in sequence, joint importance estimation is explained, the three features are defined, the cross-validation scheme and its rationale are given, and the software and hardware are named. The approach would be reimplementable with the supplement." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-039" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No formal keyword list in the extracted text. Vocabulary: 'Computerized Assessment of Motor Imitation', 'construct validity', 'test-retest reliability', 'promising biomarker', 'biologically meaningful ASC subtypes', 'scalable'. The psychometric vocabulary - validity, reliability, construct - is distinctive within a corpus dominated by accuracy-centric machine-learning language." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-039" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Strong. Three-fold cross-validation with class-balanced folds, and the metric-learning weights are learned only on training folds so that CAMI scores are computed 'completely independently from and without reference to' the HOC scores against which they are validated - an explicit leakage control on the validation comparison itself. The fold count is justified against the bias-variance literature and the analysis is replicated with ten folds in supplementary results. Test-retest reliability across repeated trials provides a form of stability evidence that cross-validation cannot. The small-sample concern is addressed with two quantitative arguments rather than dismissed. What is absent: external or independent validation on a separate cohort, sensitivity and specificity reporting, and any deployment evaluation - though the authors are candid that the current pipeline is not yet fully automatic." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-039" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Classification accuracy for the diagnostic comparison, but the evaluation is much broader than classification: Spearman correlations for construct validity against the expert standard, correlations across repeated trials for test-retest reliability, correlations with two independent symptom instruments for clinical validity, and group comparison significance testing. Reporting reliability alongside validity and accuracy is close to unique in this corpus and reflects a psychometric rather than purely machine-learning framing. No sensitivity, specificity or AUC is reported, which is the main metric gap." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-039" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: develop an objective, continuous, scalable measure of motor imitation, validated on four fronts - construct validity, test-retest reliability, diagnostic discrimination, and clinical association. Main findings: CAMI classified diagnostic groups at 87.2% versus 74.4% for human observation coding; a single one-minute trial reached 84.6%; CAMI-HOC correlations r = .69-.87; test-retest r = .73-.86; poorer imitation in ASC (p < .005); and poorer imitation correlated with greater symptom severity on SRS-2 (r = -0.50 total) and ADOS-2." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-039" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Interpretability is architectural rather than post hoc. The three features have direct behavioural meanings (spatial deviation, lag, advance), joint importance is estimated automatically from the model's own displacement so that the score reflects the joints that actually matter for each movement, and the output is a bounded continuous score interpretable as 'how well the children performed with respect to a model'. The authors explicitly contrast this with methods that 'classify children into diagnostic groups based on broad differences in movement patterns', arguing those cannot distinguish general motor abnormality from imitation-specific impairment - a specificity critique that applies to several other motor studies in this corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-039" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2020. The paper characterises the field precisely along two axes. On imitation assessment, it notes that human observation coding remains the standard but is 'subjective and time-consuming and requires intensive coder training', rendering it 'impractical for use in clinics and home settings', and that prior automated approaches are either rule-based with 'very limited generalizability' or DTW-based but reduced to dichotomous or categorical outputs that waste the continuous distance metric. On machine learning it makes a specificity critique that bears directly on this corpus: prior studies classifying autism from motor patterns 'did not directly assess imitation ability even when some tasks involved imitation', so 'it is unclear if the observed differences represent general motor abnormalities or specific imitation impairments' - a limitation that 'restricts the use of these methods for intervention purposes'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-039" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Reported by the authors, and addressed unusually rigorously rather than merely acknowledged. On sample size, they concede that 27 ASC and 21 TD 'can be considered relatively small' given phenotypic heterogeneity, then argue quantitatively that the method does not suffer from a sample size problem: invoking the 1-in-10 rule for the three-variable metric learning (30 samples needed, approximately 90 used), and applying Raudys's formula for SVM expected error with p=3, delta=3.83 and n=13 per class to derive a 5.9% expected error against a 2.8% infinite-sample floor. They also note their parameter-to-sample ratio is more favourable than two named prior autism motor studies (40 parameters from 30 subjects, and 7 parameters from 30 subjects). On scalability they concede that Kinect imperfections 'require some manual processing that can be time-consuming'. Identifiable in review: 48 children means 87.2% is roughly 42 correct; the headline is the better of two scoring methods evaluated on the same folds; IQ at or above 80 and ages 8-12 bound generalisability; and the demographic table is not recoverable from the extracted text so matching cannot be verified." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-039" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A three-dimensional interpretable representation derived from 20-joint 3D trajectories: a joint-importance-weighted DTW distance capturing spatial fidelity, and two durations capturing how long the child lagged and led the model. Producing a continuous score in [0,1] rather than a categorical judgement is an explicit design goal, argued as necessary for populations with high behavioural variability where 'categorical outputs can only capture relatively large variations'. This is one of the lowest-dimensional and most interpretable representations in the corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-039" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Biological Psychiatry: Cognitive Neuroscience and Neuroimaging - a clinical neuroscience and psychiatry journal, neither computational nor autism-specific, and one of the more clinically prestigious venues in this corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-039" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Extensive. Full-scale and index IQ measured with WISC-V and used as an inclusion criterion; general motor ability measured with the Movement Assessment Battery for Children-2 explicitly to separate imitation-specific from general motor differences; ADOS-2 and SRS-2 administered and used quantitatively in correlational analyses of symptom severity. Full inclusion and exclusion criteria are deferred to a supplement. This is among the fullest functional characterisations in the corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-039" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A hybrid of classical algorithm and learning: dynamic time warping provides temporal alignment, metric learning determines how the three derived features combine, and a support vector machine performs diagnostic classification. The design rationale is stated explicitly - the authors deliberately impose structure to reduce learnable parameters using expert-derived guidance, arguing that 'using expert knowledge to guide the features improved CAMI's interpretability, while deviance from HOC as a result of automatized learning processes improved CAMI's diagnostic discriminative ability'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-039" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Whole-body imitation of dance-like movements performed by a young woman on a large TV screen, deliberately without music or sound. Sequences comprised 14-18 individual movement types selected to be 'relatively unfamiliar (e.g., moving arms up and down like a puppeteer)', to lack an end goal, and to require moving multiple limbs simultaneously - chosen on the basis of prior research showing that autistic imitation difficulties are most pronounced for meaningless, goal-less actions. A brief training phase preceded testing. Of 14 collected trials at varying speeds, only the 4 at 100% speed are analysed to avoid confounding by speed, comprising two sequences each presented twice, which is what enables the test-retest analysis." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-039" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Motor behavior: whole-body 3D joint trajectories during imitation, evaluated for both spatial fidelity to a model and temporal synchrony with it. The construct measured is specifically imitation ability - the correspondence between the child's movement and a target - rather than movement quality in the abstract, which the authors argue distinguishes their approach from prior motor classifiers." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-039" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups: 27 children with autism spectrum conditions and 21 typically developing children, total 48. Group sizes are clear and participant characteristics are tabulated with group comparison test statistics, though the values are not recoverable from the extracted text. No other-diagnosis comparison group." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-039" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Research centre setting at a neurodevelopmental research institute, with community and school recruitment. Data were collected as part of a wider study of imitation skills in autism, so this is a focused analysis within a larger programme rather than a standalone collection." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-039" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The most complete diagnostic ascertainment in the corpus alongside Cho et al. (2019): DSM-5 criteria confirmed on site by research-reliable assessors using ADOS-2 (module 3 for all participants, ensuring comparability), the Autism Diagnostic Interview-Revised, and the parent-report SRS-2. Using all three of the field's principal instruments, applied by research-reliable assessors, and standardising the ADOS module across participants, is a materially stronger reference standard than the 'prior clinical diagnosis' that most of this corpus relies on." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-039" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Hardware: two Microsoft Kinect Xbox depth cameras at 30 frames per second positioned in front of and behind the child, requiring no sensors or special clothing - an explicit design advantage for this population; a large TV screen for stimulus display. Software: iPi Motion Capture Software for extracting 20 joint coordinates from depth recordings; a custom dynamic time warping and metric learning pipeline with equations supplied in a supplement; SVM for classification." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-039" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with autism spectrum conditions (ASCs)', 'children with ASCs', 'typically developing (TD) children', 'TD peers'. Consistently person-first, and the paper uses 'autism spectrum conditions' rather than 'disorder' throughout - one of a minority in this corpus to do so. The framing is developmental and functional rather than pathologising, discussing imitation's role in 'social bond formation and learning' rather than deficit alone, though 'imitation deficits' and 'impairments' are used." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-039" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; two-day visits with repeated trials within session, and no longitudinal follow-up - though repeated trials within session provide the test-retest estimate. Data are depth-camera recordings reduced to skeletal joint coordinates, so identifiability is moderate rather than high. Ethics governance is complete: Johns Hopkins University School of Medicine IRB approval prior to commencement, written informed consent from all legal guardians, verbal assent from all children, and $100 compensation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-039" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Improve scalability by exploring capture technologies beyond Kinect, whose motion-tracking imperfections currently require time-consuming manual processing. The authors also frame CAMI as a candidate biomarker 'for defining biologically meaningful ASC subtypes and guiding intervention', and note it can 'track performance during interventions designed to improve social-communicative function through imitation training' - positioning it as a measurement instrument for intervention research rather than only a diagnostic tool." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-039" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Brief one-minute imitation trials; 14 trials at varying speeds were collected, of which 4 at 100% speed are analysed here - the first two and their repetitions at the end - across two movement sequenc" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional case-control instrument-development and validation study, with classification as one of four evaluation strands" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum conditions" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 27; comparison: 21" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "A one-minute Kinect-based imitation task scored by a DTW and metric-learning algorithm distinguished children with autism spectrum conditions from typically developing children at 87.2% accuracy, outperforming expert human observation coding on the same data (74.4%) while correlating strongly with it (r = .69-.87). Test-retest reliability across repeated trials was high (r = .73-.86), imitation wa",
                        "CAMI classification accuracy 87.2% versus HOC 74.4%. A single one-minute trial without HOC scores (trial 2b) classified at 84.6%. Construct validity correlations between CAMI and HOC r = .69-.87. Test-retest reliability r = .73-.86. Group difference in imitation p < .005. Imitation-symptom correlations: SRS-2 total r = -0.50 (p < .05) for CAMI versus -0.43 for HOC; ADOS-2 total within the ASC grou" ;
                    slr:outcome "Classification accuracy for CAMI versus HOC scores; Spearman correlations for construct validity against HOC and for test-retest reliability; correlations with ADOS-2 and SRS-2 for clinical validity; group comparison p-values" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "CAMI classification accuracy 87.2% versus HOC 74.4%. A single one-minute trial without HOC scores (trial 2b) classified at 84.6%. Construct validity correlations between CAMI and HOC r = .69-.87. Test-retest reliability r = .73-.86. Group difference in imitation p < .005. Imitation-symptom correlations: SRS-2 total r = -0.50 (p < .05) for CAMI versus -0.43 for HOC; ADOS-2 total within the ASC grou; A one-minute Kinect-based imitation task scored by a DTW and metric-learning algorithm distinguished children with autism spectrum conditions from typically developing children at 87.2% accuracy, outperforming expert human observation coding on the same data (74.4%) while correlating strongly with it (r = .69-.87). Test-retest reliability across repeated trials was high (r = .73-.86), imitation wa" ] ] ;
