<https://doi.org/10.1038/s41398-026-03823-y> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Traditional subjective measures are limited in the insight they provide into underlying behavioral differences associated with autism and, accordingly, their ability to predict diagnosis. Performance-based measures offer an attractive alternative, being designed to capture neuropsychological constructs more directly and objectively. However, due to the heterogeneity of autism, differences in any one speciﬁc neuropsychological domain are inconsistently detected. Meanwhile, protracted wait times for diagnostic interviews delay access to care, highlighting the importance of developing better methods for identifying individuals likely to be autistic and understanding the associated behavioral differences. We administered a battery of online tasks measuring multisensory perception, emotion recognition, and executive function to a large group of autistic and non-autistic adults. We then used machine learning to classify participants and reveal which factors from the resulting dataset were most predictive of diagnosis. Not only were these measures able to predict autism in a late-diagnosed population known to be particularly difﬁcult to identify, their combination with the most popular screening questionnaire enhanced its predictive accuracy (reaching 92% together). This indicates that performance-based measures may be a promising means of predicting autism, providing complementary information to existing screening questionnaires. Many variables in which signiﬁcant group differences were not detected had predictive value in combination, suggesting complex latent relationships associated with autism. Machine learning’s ability to harness these connections and pinpoint the most crucial features for prediction could allow optimization of a screening tool that offers a unique marriage of predictive accuracy and accessibility." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Van der Burg E; Jertberg RM; Geurts HM; Chakrabarti B; Begeer S (2026). Finding the forest in the trees: Using machine learning and online cognitive and perceptual measures to predict adult autism diagnosis. **. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41398-026-03823-y" ;
    dcterms:creator "Begeer S",
        "Chakrabarti B",
        "Geurts HM",
        "Jertberg RM",
        "Van der Burg E" ;
    dcterms:title "Finding the forest in the trees: Using machine learning and online cognitive and perceptual measures to predict adult autism diagnosis" ;
    bibo:doi "10.1038/s41398-026-03823-y" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_7b19a959" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2026 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Confounders addressed" ;
                            slr:justification "Cognitive ability matched in both samples; age and gender imbalance addressed by a 100% matched subsample of 125 per group, reported as the primary analysis with the inflation of the unmatched estimate stated explicitly." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data source and eligibility described" ;
                            slr:justification "Recruitment sources named with a justification for the comparison-group platform; language and naivety criteria stated; 407 exclusions counted with reasons." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participants appropriate to the question" ;
                            slr:justification "Adults with clinician-made diagnoses from a national register, deliberately a late-diagnosed and majority-female sample - the population existing screening instruments are least able to identify." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Model development guarded against optimism" ;
                            slr:justification "Cross-validated grid search with an explicit train-test gap criterion for overfitting, no resampling, and a label-randomisation control falling to chance." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Predictors independent of the outcome" ;
                            slr:justification "Task performance is independent of diagnosis; the AQ's inclusion is the explicit object of a separate comparison rather than a hidden feature." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Predictors defined and measured appropriately" ;
                            slr:justification "Fifty-four dependent variables from three published experimental batteries spanning multisensory perception, emotion recognition and executive function." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome definition and ascertainment" ;
                            slr:justification "Self-reported formal clinician diagnosis via a national research register, not independently verified." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome determined independently of predictors" ;
                            slr:justification "Diagnosis predates the online tasks and is unrelated to task performance." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "unclear" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Analysis unit matches the clinical claim" ;
                            slr:justification "One row per adult; splitting is at the person level." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Validation design guards against optimism" ;
                            slr:justification "Matched and unmatched analyses run in parallel with the matched result reported as primary; label-randomisation control; explicit overfitting criterion." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance measures adequate" ;
                            slr:justification "Accuracy, sensitivity, specificity and F1 reported separately for every configuration and both samples, with chance established empirically - but no confidence intervals or fold variance on the final estimates." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Fusion claim checkable" ;
                            slr:justification "All three configurations reported (tasks 74%, AQ 83%, combined 92%) so the increment from fusion is visible, with feature-importance profiles compared across configurations." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Limitations and overinterpretation" ;
                            slr:justification "Demographic inflation of the unmatched estimate stated and acted on; generalisability of the feature ranking across ages flagged; and the methodological failures of higher-scoring prior studies diagnosed precisely." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "unclear" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The stated goal is to test whether online performance-based measures can predict adult autism diagnosis, whether they add to the most widely used screening questionnaire, and which behavioural variables carry the predictive signal. Headline findings on the age- and gender-matched sample: objective task measures alone reach 74% accuracy (sensitivity 69.2%, specificity 79.2%, F1 73.5%); the AQ alone reaches 83%; the two combined reach 92%. On the unmatched full sample the objective measures reach 81.8%, which the paper states is inflated by including age and gender as features. A label-randomised control performs at chance. Several variables with no significant group difference under Bonferroni correction nevertheless contributed predictive value in combination." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-164" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Entirely online, in Dutch, via the Neurotask platform, on participants' own devices - which the paper presents as the point rather than a compromise, arguing that online performance-based measures offer a marriage of more objective data types with the accessibility of surveys, against neuroimaging approaches that are costly, time-consuming and require laboratory attendance. The battery is described as briefer than a typical movie." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-164" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Feature importance is used as a scientific instrument rather than as decoration. The headline interpretive result is that several variables showing no significant group difference even under highly conservative Bonferroni correction nonetheless carried predictive value in combination - a finding that only a multivariate model can produce and that the authors read as evidence of complex latent relationships associated with autism rather than as a discovery of new markers. Reaction-time variables ranked consistently high; executive function was informative in this adult sample; and the near-identical importance profile with and without the AQ supports the claim that objective and subjective measures contribute differently. The authors also state the limit: the ranking may not generalise across ages." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-164" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Translational Psychiatry 16:129 (Nature Portfolio) - a clinical and translational psychiatry journal, and the only such venue in the charted corpus. The disciplinary fit is evident throughout: matched-subsample analysis, Bonferroni-corrected group comparisons, a label-randomisation control, sensitivity and specificity reported separately, deposited analysis code, documented data-access procedures, an explicit language statement citing community preference, and a discussion that diagnoses the methodological failures of the prior literature it is compared against." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-164" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, sensitivity, specificity and F1 for both samples and each configuration, plus a label-randomised control model. The specificity/sensitivity split is reported rather than collapsed into accuracy (matched sample: sensitivity 69.2%, specificity 79.2%), which shows that the model misses roughly a third of autistic adults at the reported operating point - visible precisely because the paper reports it. Missing: confidence intervals, cross-validation variance on the final estimates, and calibration. The chance level is established empirically by the randomisation control rather than asserted." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-164" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2026. The framing is screening-support and is bounded at every point: the measures may be a promising means of predicting autism, providing complementary information to existing screening questionnaires; the value proposition is triage for expedited assessment given protracted diagnostic wait times, not replacement of clinical assessment; and the reported gain is stated as 9 percentage points over the most popular adult screening questionnaire, with the authors noting that this matters in the context of an estimated 1% population prevalence. The paper is also unusual in reporting a result that undercuts its own headline - that the same models reach 81.8% on the unmatched sample and 74.0% once age and gender are matched - and in choosing the lower figure as its primary estimate." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-164" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Not captured in the extracted text." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-164" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Complete and analytically load-bearing. Age, gender, ICAR-16 abbreviated IQ, AQ total and age at diagnosis are tabulated for both samples with means, standard deviations and Mann-Whitney tests. The full sample carries large group differences in age (44.2 versus 32.2 years) and gender (71.0% versus 48.9% women), both significant at p<0.001; the matched subsample is exact on both. Cognitive ability is matched in both samples (ICAR 0.54 versus 0.53). The autistic sample is 71% women and late-diagnosed at a mean of 35.6 years - a demographic profile that is the opposite of almost every other autism study in this corpus, and one the paper identifies as particularly difficult to identify. Nothing on ethnicity or socioeconomic status." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-164" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "High, and complete on the axes that matter. The analysis script is publicly deposited; the recruitment sources are named with a justification for the platform chosen; the 407 exclusions are counted with their reasons; the matching procedure is described; demographic, cognitive and AQ characteristics are tabulated with standard deviations and significance tests for both samples; the tuned hyperparameters are enumerated; the tuning procedure and the overfitting criterion are stated; the decision not to resample is stated with its reasoning; and a label-randomisation control is reported. The primary data are restricted by register privacy rules, with the request procedure documented - the correct handling for a national register rather than a gap." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-164" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional. Ethics reporting is complete: ethical committee approval from the Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam with reference VCWE-2020-041R1, compliance with the Netherlands Code of Conduct for Research Integrity and the revised Declaration of Helsinki, and informed consent from all participants prior to the experiments. Data governance is also handled properly - the register data are restricted for privacy reasons, and the procedure for requesting access through a research proposal and Data Sharing Agreement is documented." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-164" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Distributed through the discussion rather than isolated, and unusually incisive. The paper states that the unmatched sample's higher accuracy is inflated by including age and gender as features and that the matched estimate should be preferred; that the feature ranking may not generalise across participant ages; and that the work represents initial efforts with limits on what can be concluded. Its most valuable limitation, though, is directed outward and applies to a large part of this review's corpus: it notes that studies reporting higher accuracy did so either by using the AQ-10 items to predict whether participants fell above or below an AQ cut-off score rather than whether they received an official diagnosis, or by using groups that were not balanced on demographic features included as predictors - and that this risks sampling biases inflating performance, which the authors accounted for through their matched analysis. That is a precise, published statement of the two mechanisms this review has documented across the questionnaire branch." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-164" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A battery of online tasks spanning three neuropsychological domains - multisensory perception, emotion recognition and executive function - reused from three prior published experiments with overlapping participants, yielding 54 dependent variables. The design rationale is stated clearly: because autism is heterogeneous, differences in any one neuropsychological domain are inconsistently detected, so the study looks across domains rather than within one." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-164" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "AQ-28 total, ICAR-16 abbreviated intelligence, and age at diagnosis, all reported by group with standard deviations and significance tests. The AQ is additionally used as a comparator predictor and a fusion partner, so its role in the modelling is explicit rather than hidden inside a feature vector - the distinction that separates this study from the corpus's instrument-inclusion leakage cases." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-164" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Use feature ranking to remove uninformative measures and shorten the battery; design manipulations that accentuate reaction-time differences in the most sensitive domains, since certain emotional categories and stimulus types are harder to categorise and leave more room for RT differences; and test whether the sensitive measures differ across age groups." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-164" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Genuine, declared and quantified multimodal fusion, and the study's central result. Objective task measures and the AQ questionnaire are evaluated separately and together on the same matched sample: 74% for tasks alone, 83% for the AQ alone, 92% combined. Because each configuration is reported, the increment attributable to fusion is visible (9 points over the questionnaire alone), and because the feature-importance profile is very similar with and without the AQ, the authors can argue that the two data types contribute information in different ways rather than merely duplicating each other. This is the only study in the charted corpus to report all three configurations and thereby make the fusion claim checkable." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-164" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The most carefully handled in the entire corpus, and explicitly so. The paper inserts a parenthetical statement in its opening paragraph that it alternates between person-first and identity-first language to address the varying preferences of the international autism community, citing a published recommendation. Its terminology is consistently difference-based rather than deficit-based - behavioural differences, differences from the norms seen in the non-autistic population - and it justifies its choice of algorithm partly in those terms, noting that random forests capture any patterns that exist in the data and are therefore difference focused rather than deficit focused. It is the only study in the review to make a language decision explicitly, to cite community preference as the reason, and to connect that framing to a methodological choice." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-164" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Self-reported formal diagnosis by a registered clinician, with participants drawn from a national research register (NAR) rather than a convenience sample, and non-autistic participants reporting no autism diagnosis. Diagnosis therefore predates and is independent of the task data. The population is explicitly characterised as late-diagnosed, which the paper treats as a strength for the research question - it is precisely the group that existing screening instruments miss - rather than glossing over it." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-164" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Random forest, selected with reasons given: capacity to detect sparse signals in high-dimensional data, provision of feature-importance rankings usable for battery optimisation, and the property of being difference-focused rather than deficit-focused. No deep learning and no architecture competition - the modelling is subordinated to the scientific question." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-164" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Neuropsychological performance across three domains rather than a single behavioural marker: multisensory perceptual integration, emotion recognition (with accuracy and reaction time varying by emotional category and stimulus type), and executive function. The substantive behavioural findings are specific - reaction-time variables consistently ranked high in predictive value, and executive function emerged as informative in this adult sample - and the paper is careful that the ranking is sample-dependent." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-164" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "One row per participant containing 54 task-derived dependent variables, with AQ total and demographic variables added in specified configurations. No dimensionality reduction or feature selection is applied, and no clinical instrument is smuggled in - the AQ's presence is the explicit object of one of the three comparisons." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-164" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two recruited groups of adults: 286 autistic and 266 non-autistic in the final dataset, from 959 initially recruited, with 407 excluded for missing data from at least one contributing experiment (per-study exclusion criteria, technical issues, attrition). Autistic participants came from the Netherlands Autism Register and non-autistic participants principally from Prolific Academic. A second, fully age- and gender-matched subsample of 125 per group was drawn by random selection from the unbalanced sample, and it is this matched analysis the paper reports as its primary result. The analysis unit is the person throughout. The attrition is large and is stated plainly with its causes." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-164" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Participants' own devices, with tasks programmed and delivered online through Neurotask. Analysis in Python with scikit-learn's RandomForest and GridSearchCV, with the script deposited on OSF." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-164" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "This is one of the two most rigorously evaluated studies in the charted corpus, and its distinctive contribution is the demographic-confounding analysis. The design uses a train/test split with 3-fold cross-validated grid search for hyperparameters and monitors overfitting by the train-test performance gap; adds a label-randomisation control that falls to chance, empirically establishing the baseline rather than assuming it; and declines to resample, stating that neither SMOTE nor undersampling was used. Its decisive move is to run the entire analysis twice - once on the full unbalanced sample and once on a subsample matched 100% on age and gender at 125 per group - and then to report the matched result as primary, stating that performance in the unmatched sample is inflated by the inclusion of age and gender as features given the disparity between groups in those factors. The difference is large: 81.8% unmatched against 74.0% matched, so roughly eight accuracy points in this study were attributable to demographic confounding alone. That number is directly relevant to the rest of this review, in which sex imbalances of 32 points (local_4ceee075) and 43 points (local_7432ffd7) between groups are reported and left unadjusted, and it is the only quantification of that effect anywhere in the corpus. Residual limitations: no confidence intervals or fold variance accompany the final estimates; the matched subsample is 250 people; diagnosis is self-reported rather than verified, albeit through a national register; and 407 of 959 recruited participants were excluded for missing data, which may not be at random. None of these undermines the study's central results, and the authors identify the most important of them themselves." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-164" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Three predictor configurations compared on the matched sample - objective task measures alone (74% maximum accuracy), the AQ alone (83%), and the two combined (92%) - plus a label-randomisation contro" ;
                    slr:study_design "Secondary analysis pooling three prior online experiments with overlapping participants; supervised binary classification of autism diagnosis from performance-based measures, run s…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 286 autistic adults in the full sample (mean age 44.2, SD 13.6; 71.0% women; AQ 83.4, SD 10.7; mean age at diagnosis 35.6, SD 15.1); 125 in the matched subsample.; comparison: 266 non-autistic adults (mean age 32.2, SD 12.0; 48.9% women; AQ 60.1, SD 11.6); 125 in the matched subsample." ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Full sample: accuracy 81.8%, sensitivity 77.1%, specificity 84.1%, F1 77.9%. Age- and gender-matched sample (the primary result): accuracy 74.0%, sensitivity 69.2%, specificity 79.2%, F1 73.5%. On the matched sample, the AQ alone reached 83% and the combination of objective measures with the AQ reached 92%. The label-randomised model performed at chance." ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy, sensitivity, specificity and F1 for both the full and matched samples, for each predictor configuration, alongside the label-randomised control. Feature importance rankings are reported and compared across configurations." ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Full sample: accuracy 81.8%, sensitivity 77.1%, specificity 84.1%, F1 77.9%. Age- and gender-matched sample (the primary result): accuracy 74.0%, sensitivity 69.2%, specificity 79.2%, F1 73.5%. On the matched sample, the AQ alone reached 83% and the combination of objective measures with the AQ reached 92%. The label-randomised model performed at chance." ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "af2f30d93f2dbffe47ff9ddb04f12ab02adf0596bd204f04221e0e8619ca033e" ;
