    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "unclear" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "One row per participant with person-level splitting; a label-randomisation control establishing chance empirically; and parallel matched and unmatched analyses. However, 407 exclusions for missing data may not be at random, and no confidence intervals or fold variance accompany the final estimates." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Autistic participants recruited through a national autism register with clinician-made diagnoses; non-autistic participants through a platform selected on documented data-quality grounds; cognitive ability matched across groups; and the demographic imbalance in the full sample addressed directly by a fully age- and gender-matched subsample reported as the primary analysis. The main residual concern is that 407 of 959 recruited participants were excluded for missing data." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Fifty-four task-derived variables with no selection or engineering; hyperparameters tuned by cross-validated grid search with an explicit overfitting criterion; no resampling applied, with the decision stated; and the AQ's role made explicit as one of three compared configurations rather than concealed within the feature vector." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "unclear" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Diagnosis is a self-reported formal diagnosis by a registered clinician, obtained through a national research register rather than a convenience sample, and independent of the task data - but not independently verified by the investigators." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "some" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-65384-4> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Autism is a developmental condition currently identified by experts using observation, interview, and questionnaire techniques and primarily assessing social and communication deficits. Motor function and movement imitation are also altered in autism and can be measured more objectively. In this study, motion and eye tracking data from a movement imitation task were combined with supervised machine learning methods to classify 22 autistic and 22 non-autistic adults. The focus was on a reliable machine learning application. We have used nested validation to develop models and further tested the models with an independent data sample. Feature selection was aimed at selection stability to assure result interpretability. Our models predicted diagnosis with 73% accuracy from kinematic features, 70% accuracy from eye movement features and 78% accuracy from combined features. We further explored features which were most important for predictions to better understand movement imitation differences in autism. Consistent with the behavioural results, most discriminative features were from the experimental condition in which non-autistic individuals tended to successfully imitate unusual movement kinematics while autistic individuals tended to fail. Machine learning results show promise that future work could aid in the diagnosis process by providing quantitative tests to supplement current qualitative ones." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Vabalas A; Gowen E; Poliakoff E; Casson AJ (2020). Applying Machine Learning to Kinematic and Eye Movement Features of a Movement Imitation Task to Predict Autism Diagnosis. *Scientific Reports, 10(1), Arti*. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-65384-4" ;
    dcterms:creator "Casson AJ",
        "Gowen E",
        "Poliakoff E",
        "Vabalas A" ;
    dcterms:title "Applying Machine Learning to Kinematic and Eye Movement Features of a Movement Imitation Task to Predict Autism Diagnosis" ;
    bibo:doi "10.1038/s41598-020-65384-4" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_638860ef" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2020 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "Matched for age, gender, handedness and IQ with all comparisons tested and non-significant within both samples." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "44 adults with a 14-participant independent test set; both samples high-IQ." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Age, IQ, gender and handedness reported with matching statistics for both samples; recruitment route and diagnostic route are not." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is demographic drift between samples acknowledged?" ;
                            slr:justification "The 20% versus 57% female difference between development and testing samples is stated explicitly and discussed." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the task designed to isolate the construct of interest?" ;
                            slr:justification "Movement style is manipulated across conditions so that failure to imitate an unusual style is separable from general motor difference." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "No diagnostic information appears in this paper." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Apparatus and protocol details are deferred entirely to a prior publication." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "Kinematic and gaze features are computed automatically; selection occurs inside cross-validation folds." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is statistical significance assessed?" ;
                            slr:justification "p-values accompany the reported accuracies." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is feature selection stability quantified?" ;
                            slr:justification "Kuncheva's index reported alongside accuracy for every algorithm, and a novel selector developed specifically to maximise it." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is feature selection nested within the validation loop?" ;
                            slr:justification "Feature selection performed inside each of ten nested cross-validation folds at development." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is there independent validation on a hold-out sample?" ;
                            slr:justification "A pre-specified sample of 7 autistic and 7 non-autistic adults took no part in model development." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the generalisation claim checked empirically?" ;
                            slr:justification "Development and independent-test accuracies are reported side by side and shown to be comparable." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample size adequate for the feature dimensionality?" ;
                            slr:justification "Several hundred features against 30 development participants; the paper's methodology is designed to cope with this rather than to remove it." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the fusion benefit quantified?" ;
                            slr:justification "73% kinematic, 70% gaze, 78% combined, obtained under independent testing." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the field's methodology critiqued and corrected?" ;
                            slr:justification "Prior kinematic ML studies are named as pooling training and validation data, not testing on independent data, and not assessing significance - and the paper implements all three corrections." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No data availability statement in the extracted text." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Deferred to the source protocol publication with no statement in this paper." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "None, though algorithms and their operating rules are specified in detail." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Handcrafted kinematic and gaze summary features, structured hierarchically: 20 kinematic parameters computed as means and standard deviations for each of three movement conditions within each of two instruction blocks, plus 24 gaze means and 24 gaze standard deviations per block. This produces a feature space far larger than the 30-participant development sample, which is precisely the small-sample high-dimensional regime the paper's methodology is designed to handle." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-035" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; a single session with no follow-up. Data are numeric kinematic and gaze traces, not identifiable media. Ethics and consent details are not restated in this paper, being referred to the source protocol publication - a reporting gap even if the underlying governance was presumably adequate." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-035" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Not described in this paper. The experimental protocol, stimuli and by implication the diagnostic ascertainment are referred to a prior publication by Gowen et al. This is the study's principal reporting gap - as with the same group's 2019 paper, the diagnostic basis for the class labels is not stated in the analysis paper itself." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-035" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups totalling 44 adults, split across two independently constituted samples: a development sample of 15 autistic and 15 non-autistic adults, and a hold-out testing sample of 7 autistic and 7 non-autistic adults, each internally matched. Having a pre-specified independent test sample rather than only cross-validation folds is rare in this corpus. No other-diagnosis comparison group." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-035" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Explicit multimodal fusion with quantified benefit: kinematic features alone gave 73%, eye movement features alone 70%, and combined features 78%. The authors state that the modalities 'provided complementary information for predictions', and the 5-point gain over the better single modality is one of the clearest quantified fusion benefits in the corpus, obtained under independent testing rather than internal cross-validation. Features are combined at feature level before selection and classification." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-035" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: predict autism diagnosis from imitation kinematics and gaze, with methodological reliability as the explicit primary focus. Main findings: 73% accuracy from kinematics, 70% from eye movements, 78% from combined features; development and independent-test accuracies comparable, demonstrating that nested cross-validation controlled overfitting; the general instruction block outperformed the attention instruction block across all data types; t-test feature selection outperformed SVM-RFE, ReliefF and mRMR on both accuracy and stability; and seven of ten selected kinematic features came from the elevated-amplitude condition, where autistic adults imitated with lower vertical amplitude and earlier peak acceleration." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-035" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Supervised learning with SVM-RBF as the fixed classifier, so that the experimental variable is feature selection rather than model family. Four established selectors are benchmarked (t-test filter, SVM-RFE, ReliefF, mRMR) and three variants developed (ensemble, bagging, wrapped t-test). Holding the classifier constant while varying selection is a clean design that isolates the contribution the paper is actually studying." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-035" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Laboratory setting at a UK university. The data come from an experimental protocol previously published by the same group, so this is a machine-learning analysis of an existing behavioural dataset rather than a new collection." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-035" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Not set out as a discrete future-work section in the extracted text. The introduction's framing implies the priority - the paper notes that adult diagnostic instruments 'have only been validated for use with children', that with adults 'clinicians rarely rely on standardised diagnostic methods making diagnosis less accurate, more subjective and lengthier', and that researching adult diagnostic criteria is listed among the top ten priorities by a UK autism research charity. The work positions itself as contributing quantitative tests to supplement current qualitative ones in that under-served context." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-035" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The paper's framing is itself a critique of prior work's limitations rather than only its own. On the field: prior kinematic ML studies 'had small sample sizes and achieved high classification accuracy', but 'models developed with small sample/high dimensional data are prone to fit noise and not necessarily an underlying pattern separating classes', and those studies 'did not avoid pooling training and validation data while developing their ML models and did not take other steps to control for the fitting of random noise', were 'not tested with independent data', and did not assess statistical significance. On their own work they acknowledge the difficulty of small-sample high-dimensional pattern recognition requiring careful handling of both validation and feature selection. Identifiable in review: 44 adults total with a 14-participant test set means 78% is roughly 11 of 14 correct; the diagnostic ascertainment is entirely absent from this paper; both samples are high-IQ adults; the reported headline is the best across several feature-selection variants; and the marked sex-composition difference between samples, while turned into a generalisation argument, also means the independent test provides limited evidence about performance in a comparably composed sample." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-035" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two modalities: hand movement kinematics (velocity, acceleration, deceleration profiles with their timings, amplitudes, jerk) and eye movement behaviour during the same imitation task. Measuring gaze and movement concurrently during imitation is distinctive - it captures both what the participant looked at while observing the model and how they subsequently moved." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-035" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'autistic adults', 'autistic individuals', 'non-autistic adults', 'non-autistic individuals', 'individuals living with autism'. Consistently identity-first and paired with 'non-autistic' rather than 'healthy', 'normal' or 'typically developing' - among the most neurodiversity-aligned terminology in the corpus, consistent with the same Manchester group's other entries here." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-035" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Movement imitation with style manipulation: participants watched a video of a hand movement sequence pointing between four locations and then imitated it, with only movements between two designated locations analysed. Three movement styles were used - elevated amplitude, fast-direct, and direct - crossed with two instruction blocks, a general instruction block and an attention instruction block that directed attention to movement style. The elevated condition is the theoretically critical one, since imitating an unusually elevated trajectory requires adopting a movement style outside one's habitual repertoire." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-035" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Hardware: motion tracking and eye tracking apparatus, with specific models deferred to the source protocol publication; a screen for video stimulus presentation. Software: implementations of SVM-RBF, four feature selection algorithms, nested cross-validation, and the authors' novel bagging and wrapped t-test variants; Kuncheva's index for stability quantification." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-035" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Interpretability is pursued as an explicit design goal rather than a by-product: feature selection methods were chosen and developed 'aimed at selection stability to assure result interpretability', and the wrapped t-test runs until it converges on an identical feature set across 100 consecutive iterations. The resulting stable ten-feature set supports a specific behavioural interpretation - seven of ten features come from the elevated-amplitude condition, where autistic adults produced lower vertical amplitude and consequently earlier peak acceleration, indicating a failure to adopt the model's unusual movement style rather than a general motor difference. This is corroborated by conventional parametric tests showing significant group differences in the same condition." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-035" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The strongest validation design in the corpus. Nested cross-validation at development, with feature selection performed inside each of ten nested folds; then a genuinely independent hold-out sample of 14 participants who took no part in development; then direct comparison of development and testing accuracies to verify that nesting controlled overfitting. Statistical significance is assessed. Feature selection stability is quantified with Kuncheva's index. The generalisation claim is additionally stress-tested by the incidental fact that the two samples differ substantially in sex composition, so the independent result holds across a demographic shift. What remains absent: external validation on a different site or protocol, sensitivity and specificity, and any deployment analysis." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-035" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No data or code release stated. Method reporting is strong on the analytic side - the full 20-parameter kinematic definition list, the feature structure across conditions and blocks, the four baseline selectors, the three developed variants with their operating rules (100 subsamples of size n/2 for bagging; t-statistic adjustment up or down at a 50% accuracy threshold with termination at Kuncheva's index of 1 across 100 consecutive iterations for the wrapped t-test), the nested cross-validation structure with feature selection inside each of ten folds, and the classifier. Weak on the experimental side, where apparatus, protocol details and diagnostic ascertainment are all deferred to a prior publication." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-035" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Scientific Reports (Nature Portfolio) - a broad multidisciplinary journal, neither autism-specific nor computational." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-035" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No formal keyword list in the extracted text. Vocabulary: 'reliable machine learning application', 'nested validation', 'selection stability', 'independent data sample', 'Kuncheva's index', 'quantitative tests to supplement current qualitative ones'. The methodological-reliability vocabulary dominates and is the paper's identity." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-035" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Development sample: 15 autistic (3 female, 12 male), mean age 33.1, mean IQ 122.0; 15 non-autistic (3 female, 12 male), mean age 32.2, mean IQ 124.8. Age p = 0.73, IQ p = 0.51. Independent test sample: 7 autistic (4 female, 3 male), mean age 28.16, mean IQ 122.14; 7 non-autistic (4 female, 3 male), mean age 27.90, mean IQ 126.29. Age p = 0.95, IQ p = 0.46. Groups are matched for age, gender, handedness and IQ within each sample, with all comparisons tested and reported - unusually thorough. The two samples differ in sex composition (20% female in development versus 57% in testing) and marginally in age (p = 0.06), which the authors flag explicitly and turn into a strength: generalisation held despite the demographic shift. Both samples are high-IQ (means 122-126) adults, which bounds the population addressed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-035" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Classification accuracy at both development and independent-testing stages, reported side by side per algorithm and per instruction block - the side-by-side presentation is what makes the generalisation claim checkable. Kuncheva's index quantifies feature selection stability as a second reported outcome, treated as co-equal with accuracy. Statistical significance p-values accompany the accuracies, and accuracy distributions are shown as box plots. No sensitivity, specificity or AUC." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-035" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2020. The paper gives one of the corpus's sharpest field characterisations. On the clinical problem: UK average waiting time between initial concerns and clinical diagnosis exceeds three years, adult diagnostic instruments 'have only been validated for use with children', and adult diagnosis is 'less accurate, more subjective and lengthier'. On the methodological problem: 'only a handful of ML studies used kinematic data for autism prediction. All studies had small sample sizes and achieved high classification accuracy', but such models 'are prone to fit noise', and to the authors' knowledge those studies 'did not avoid pooling training and validation data', were not tested with independent data, and did not assess statistical significance. This paper is the corrective demonstration that follows from that diagnosis." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-035" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "IQ is reported for all four subgroups and used as a matching variable with statistical verification, and handedness is also matched. No language, adaptive-functioning or autism-severity measures are reported, and no comorbidity information. IQ means of 122-126 indicate a sample well above population average." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-035" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Single session; imitation trials organised into a general instruction block and an attention instruction block, each covering three movement conditions (elevated, fast-direct, direct)" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional case-control classification study with a pre-specified independent hold-out test sample" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autistic adults" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 22 total - 15 in the development sample and 7 in the independent test sample; comparison: 22 total - 15 in the development sample and 7 in the independent test sample" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Final models: 73% accuracy from kinematic features, 70% from eye movement features, and 78% from combined features. Baseline t-test selection in the general instruction block gave 66% development accuracy with Kuncheva's index 0.66 and 68% on independent testing. Development and independent-testing accuracies were comparable throughout, which the authors present as evidence that nested cross-valid",
                        "Kinematic and eye-movement features of a movement imitation task predicted autism diagnosis at 73%, 70% and 78% respectively for kinematic, gaze and combined data, with combined data providing complementary information. The central methodological result is that development-stage and independent-test accuracies were comparable, demonstrating that nested cross-validation was sufficient to control ov" ;
                    slr:outcome "Classification accuracy at both development and independent testing stages, reported per algorithm and per instruction block, with Kuncheva's index quantifying feature selection stability and p-values for statistical significance; accuracy distributions shown as box plots" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Final models: 73% accuracy from kinematic features, 70% from eye movement features, and 78% from combined features. Baseline t-test selection in the general instruction block gave 66% development accuracy with Kuncheva's index 0.66 and 68% on independent testing. Development and independent-testing accuracies were comparable throughout, which the authors present as evidence that nested cross-valid; Kinematic and eye-movement features of a movement imitation task predicted autism diagnosis at 73%, 70% and 78% respectively for kinematic, gaze and combined data, with combined data providing complementary information. The central methodological result is that development-stage and independent-test accuracies were comparable, demonstrating that nested cross-validation was sufficient to control ov" ] ] ;
