<https://doi.org/10.1038/srep31107> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Autism is a developmental disorder evident from infancy. Yet, its clinical identification requires expert diagnostic training. New evidence indicates disruption to motor timing and integration may underpin the disorder, providing a potential new computational marker for its early identification. In this study, we employed smart tablet computers with touch-sensitive screens and embedded inertial movement sensors to record the movement kinematics and gesture forces made by 37 children 3–6 years old with autism and 45 age- and gender-matched children developing typically. Machine learning analysis of the children’s motor patterns identified autism with up to 93% accuracy. Analysis revealed these patterns consisted of greater forces at contact and with a different distribution of forces within a gesture, and gesture kinematics were faster and larger, with more distal use of space. These data support the notion disruption to movement is core feature of autism, and demonstrate autism can be computationally assessed by fun, smart device gameplay." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Anzulewicz A; Sobota K; Delafield-Butt JT (2016). Toward the Autism Motor Signature: Gesture patterns during smart tablet gameplay identify children with autism. *Scientific Reports, 6(1), Article 31107. http*. https://doi.org/10.1038/srep31107" ;
    dcterms:creator "Anzulewicz A",
        "Delafield-Butt JT",
        "Sobota K" ;
    dcterms:title "Toward the Autism Motor Signature: Gesture patterns during smart tablet gameplay identify children with autism" ;
    bibo:doi "10.1038/srep31107" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_e404a2a3" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2016 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "Both age- and gender-matched, but IQ explicitly uncontrolled - acknowledged and partially mitigated by the clinician functioning classifications." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Recruitment sites, diagnostic composition (including Asperger's and intellectual-impairment subgroups), exclusions and completion are all reported at individual-count level." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "n=82 is above corpus norm for 2016 and the age band (3-6) suits early identification, but the authors concede the sample reflects one institutional subgroup rather than the spectrum." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "Features are computed automatically from device sensor streams by a fixed engine." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the task appropriate and ecologically valid for the population?" ;
                            slr:justification "Deliberately designed to be engaging and instruction-light; the free-play game outperformed the structured one, and the ecological rationale is argued explicitly against laboratory motion capture." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "ICD-10 clinical diagnosis without ADOS/ADI-R; control status by teacher questionnaire only." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Device, OS version, sampling rate, table height and device placement, game structure, training/test timings and the full data pipeline are specified; the 262-feature list is supplied as supplemental material." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is there external or independent validation?" ;
                            slr:justification "None; the authors state future work must test whether the algorithms remain predictive in the general population or require retraining." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the validation scheme appropriate and stable?" ;
                            slr:justification "10 repetitions of 10-fold cross-validation with AUC standard deviations reported and 740 aggregated predictions for the ROC." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is feature importance derived from the model?" ;
                            slr:justification "Importance is approximated by univariate Kolmogorov-Smirnov distance, which the authors describe as 'a best approximation' rather than a model-internal measure." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is model selection justified with respect to overfitting?" ;
                            slr:justification "SVMs and GBMs explicitly rejected as overfitting-prone given p>>n and high feature correlation; redundant features (r>0.9) collapsed; a demographics-free variant run as a check." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample size adequate for the number of features?" ;
                            slr:justification "247 features retained against 82 children." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are competing interests declared?" ;
                            slr:justification "Two authors are affiliated with Harimata Sp. z o.o. and the paper states an ambition to commercialise the paradigm, but no competing-interests statement appears in the extracted text - the commercial relationship is disclosed only through affiliation." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are sample bias, generalisability and deployment limits discussed?" ;
                            slr:justification "An exemplary limitations section covering the IQ confound, institutional selection bias and its possible inflation of classifier power, sample-size sensitivity, and the fact that the comparison does not address the real clinical question." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics, consent and privacy handling reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Written parental consent, Declaration of Helsinki and named ethics-committee approval are reported, but transmission and storage of children's gameplay data in third-party commercial cloud infrastructure is described only as architecture, never as a data-protection matter." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code or an implementation available?" ;
                            slr:justification "None, though hyperparameters and the full feature list are published." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No data availability statement; commercial context." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Clinical diagnosis of Childhood Autism per ICD-10 (2010 Edition) by medical practitioners in specialist clinics, with a clinician questionnaire on symptom severity and intellectual/social functioning; children with uncertain diagnosis were excluded. No ADOS, ADI-R or other standardised observational instrument was used - a notable weakness given the strength of the rest of the design." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-006" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: early screening and characterisation - can motor information from tablet gameplay differentiate autism, and which movements do so. Main findings: best AUC 0.932 (RGF2, two-game average), 0.926 for the Creativity game alone, with 83% sensitivity at 85% specificity; the unstructured game outperformed the structured one; the signature is greater contact force with altered force distribution, faster/larger/more distal gestures, and shorter minimum tap duration, interpreted as disrupted prospective motor control." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-006" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "AUC as the primary metric, reported with standard deviation across repetitions - a rarity in this corpus - plus sensitivity and specificity quoted at two explicitly stated thresholds (0.50 and 0.55) and full ROC curves. Headline: AUC 0.932 average, 0.926 Creativity, 0.848 Sharing; 83% sensitivity / 85% specificity at threshold 0.50 on Creativity. No accuracy figure is emphasised, though the abstract translates the result as 'up to 93% accuracy', conflating AUC with accuracy." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-006" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two tablet games, each with a 2-minute guided training phase and a 5-minute unassisted test phase. 'Sharing': tap a food item to slice it into four, then drag pieces onto four cartoon children's plates, with characters reacting positively or negatively, plus non-task distractor objects. 'Creativity': choose a toy or animal shape, swipe to fill in its dotted outline, then select a colour and paint freely with no rules. The experimenter demonstrated verbally and gesturally during training but did not intervene during testing; the clinician or teacher was present and intervened only for challenging behaviour, in which case data were omitted." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-006" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Applied real-world settings - specialist therapeutic centres for the autism group and standard kindergartens for the controls, explicitly chosen over a university laboratory because laboratory motion-capture paradigms with reflective markers 'under the watchful gaze of curious scientists' are difficult for autistic children and may invalidate the data. Newly collected data; commercially available games were adapted with logging code." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-006" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2016. The paper positions itself as the first to use smart-device serious games to study motor patterns in autism, and notes only one prior machine-learning study of autism-specific motor patterns (Crippa et al., optical motion capture, n=30) - which it critiques directly for SVM plus leave-one-out cross-validation being 'prone to over-fitting'. This is the corpus's clearest early example of methodological self-correction within the field, and it documents the shift from laboratory optical motion capture to consumer-device sensing." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-006" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Scientific Reports (Nature Portfolio) - a broad multidisciplinary science journal, neither autism-specific nor computational." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-006" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two behavioral data streams from the same device - touch and inertial - are combined by feature concatenation into a single 247-dimensional vector (early/feature-level fusion). Predictions from the two games are additionally averaged per child, a decision-level fusion across tasks that produced the best AUC (0.932) - higher than either game alone. Age and gender are included as features in ET, RF and RGF, and deliberately excluded in RGF2, which performed best, showing the result does not depend on demographic inputs." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-006" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups: children clinically diagnosed with Childhood Autism (n=37 recruited, 35 with usable data) and typically developing controls (n=45). Group composition is reported in unusual detail - 30 'uncomplicated' autism, 1 high-functioning, 2 Asperger's Syndrome, 4 with co-morbid intellectual impairment (2 completing). No non-autistic clinical comparison group, which the authors identify as the key gap." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-006" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A questionnaire on level of intellectual and social functioning and on prior mobile-device experience was administered to clinicians (autism group) and teachers (control group), but no scores are reported. IQ was explicitly not controlled - the authors state they 'were unable to exclude intelligence as a potential confounder' and mitigate by noting most children were clinician-classified as average (n=29) or high functioning (n=4), with only 2 low-functioning children contributing final data. Comorbidity is reported at the individual level (intellectual impairment in 4)." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-006" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Reported by the authors, unusually thoroughly: IQ was not controlled and could confound; recruitment from particular specialist institutions gives only a subgroup of the spectrum and this 'inherent selection bias in our autism group may have facilitated machine learning differential power'; machine learning is sensitive to sample size so wider recruitment and larger numbers are needed; and the comparison is against children with no concern for ASD rather than against the clinically relevant ASD-versus-ASD-like contrast. Identifiable in review: 247 features against 82 children; feature importance assessed univariately by KS distance rather than from the models; the reported AUC is the best of four algorithm variants across two games plus their average; and two authors are affiliated with the commercial developer with explicit commercialisation ambitions, disclosed by affiliation but not as a competing-interests statement." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-006" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No data availability statement and no code release - unsurprising given the commercial affiliation and stated commercialisation ambition. Reproducibility support is nonetheless comparatively good: the full 262-feature list is provided in a supplemental table, algorithm hyperparameters are given (RGF 500 trees, L2=sL2=1.0, square loss; ET and RF 5000 trees), the correlation-based feature reduction threshold (r>0.9) is stated, the cross-validation scheme is fully specified, and classification thresholds (0.50 and 0.55) are reported explicitly rather than optimised silently. The games are commercially available. Method-reproducible, data-irreproducible." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-006" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Supervised learning with decision-tree ensembles: ExtraTrees, Random Forest and Regularized Greedy Forest (plus an RGF variant without demographics). The authors explicitly rejected SVMs and gradient boosting machines as overfitting-prone given more features than observations and strong inter-feature correlations - a deliberate model-selection rationale that is rare in this corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-006" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No formal keyword list in the extracted text; the paper's own vocabulary is distinctive: 'autism motor signature', 'computational marker', 'bio-behavioural marker', 'serious games', 'smart tablet', 'prospective motor control', 'motor agency', 'early identification'. The framing of a 'computational bio-behavioural marker' as an alternative to purely biological biomarkers is a notable conceptual contribution." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-006" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with autism', 'children with ASD', 'individuals with autism', 'the Autism group', 'children developing typically', 'typically developing children', 'the Control group', 'neurotypical individuals'. Predominantly person-first; the phrase 'children developing typically' is used in preference to deficit framing, and 'neurotypical' appears once." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-006" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Handcrafted statistical features over sensor streams: touch data are segmented into atomic gestures and summarised by kinematic and form variables; inertial data are summarised across the session by statistical moments, zero-crossing counts and inter-axis correlations. All per-feature values are reduced to their session mean before modelling. No raw time series, images or learned embeddings." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-006" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Autism n=37 (12 female / 25 male), mean age 4 years 5 months (SD 11 months), range 3-6 years. Control n=45 (13 female / 32 male), mean age 4 years 7 months (SD 11 months). Explicitly both age-matched and gender-matched - one of relatively few corpus studies to match on both. Female representation (32%) is unusually high for this literature. Very young sample, directly relevant to early identification, though all children were already diagnosed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-006" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Hardware: iPad mini (Apple) running iOS 7.0, positioned on a 65 cm children's table within 10 cm of the edge so that sensor readings reflect touch forces rather than device movement; tri-axial accelerometer, gyroscope and magnetometer sampled via iOS Core Motion at 10 Hz; standard iOS touch-event acquisition. Software: two Duckie Deck commercial children's games wrapped in a bespoke app that sequenced and time-boxed the games and disabled the home button; gzip/JSON transfer to Microsoft Azure Table Storage; a bespoke cloud-side feature-computation engine." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-006" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Kolmogorov-Smirnov distance between group distributions, with the ten highest-distance features per game reported, described and boxplotted. Most informative: inertial force features dominate (all ten in the Sharing game), joined by touch-based gesture velocity, area, height and minimum duration in the Creativity game. The interpretation is substantive rather than decorative - greater impact force, altered angular force distribution, faster and larger, more distally placed gestures and faster taps - and is tied to a mechanistic account of disrupted prospective/feed-forward motor control." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-006" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Test differentiation between ASD and other developmental psychopathologies (developmental coordination disorder, ADHD) - framed as 'the ultimate clinical question'; substantiate with a larger, more generalised population and test whether the algorithms transfer or need retraining; apply the paradigm to younger, not-yet-diagnosed children; couple gameplay with other psychometric tests, with the face-forward camera for social-intelligence and emotional-reaction measures, and with sensorised toys; and shift toward individual functional assessment rather than broad diagnostic categorisation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-006" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "10 repetitions of 10-fold cross-validation with AUC standard deviations reported, aggregating 740 predictions for the ROC; algorithm choice explicitly motivated by overfitting risk; redundant features (r>0.9) collapsed; a demographics-free model variant (RGF2) run to check that age and gender were not driving the result. Bias and generalisability are addressed narratively (selection bias, IQ confound, institution-specific sample, need for retraining on the general population) rather than by mitigation. No external or independent test set, no held-out site and no real-time/deployment evaluation, though commercial deployment as a screening device is an explicit ambition." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-006" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; a single ~14-minute session per child with no follow-up. Data are touch coordinates and inertial signals - low identifiability, no video or audio. Written informed parental consent, Declaration of Helsinki compliance and University of Strathclyde Ethics Committee approval are reported. However, the data pipeline transmits gameplay data over the web to Microsoft Azure cloud storage operated in connection with a commercial company; the paper documents this as architecture but never as a privacy, anonymization or data-protection question." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-006" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Motor behavior in two forms: gesture kinematics and form from the touch screen (velocity, area, height, duration, tap counts) and contact/angular forces from inertial sensors (accelerometer, gyroscope, magnetometer). No gaze, speech or facial data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-006" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Single session; each of two games comprised a 2-minute training phase followed immediately by a 5-minute test phase (14 minutes total per child)" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional case-control classification study (proof-of-concept)" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Childhood Autism (ICD-10)" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 37 recruited; 35 engaged in gameplay, 2 discarded for not focusing or resigning; comparison: 45 (all engaged in gameplay)" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Best model RGF2: AUC 0.848 (SD 0.025) for Sharing, 0.926 (SD 0.013) for Creativity, 0.932 (SD 0.016) for the two-game average. Creativity at threshold 0.50 gave 83% sensitivity and 85% specificity; at 0.55, 80% sensitivity and 88% specificity. ExtraTrees and Random Forest reached average AUCs of 0.881 and 0.885",
                        "Motor patterns extracted from ordinary iPad gameplay identified children with autism at up to AUC 0.93. The unstructured free-colouring game outperformed the structured sharing game, which the authors argue shows the signal is motor rather than attentional. The characterised signature is greater contact force with an altered force distribution, faster and larger gestures placed more distally, and " ;
                    slr:outcome "AUC (primary, with standard deviation across repetitions), sensitivity and specificity at two explicit thresholds (0.50 and 0.55), ROC curves" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Best model RGF2: AUC 0.848 (SD 0.025) for Sharing, 0.926 (SD 0.013) for Creativity, 0.932 (SD 0.016) for the two-game average. Creativity at threshold 0.50 gave 83% sensitivity and 85% specificity; at 0.55, 80% sensitivity and 88% specificity. ExtraTrees and Random Forest reached average AUCs of 0.881 and 0.885; Motor patterns extracted from ordinary iPad gameplay identified children with autism at up to AUC 0.93. The unstructured free-colouring game outperformed the structured sharing game, which the authors argue shows the signal is motor rather than attentional. The characterised signature is greater contact force with an altered force distribution, faster and larger gestures placed more distally, and " ] ] ;
