<https://doi.org/10.1088/2631-8695/addb0b> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) is a complex neuropsychiatric developmental condition that signiﬁcantly affects social interaction, communication, and learning skills. Early and precise diagnosis is essential for timely intervention but remains challenging due to the variability in symptoms and reliance on time consuming clinical assessments. This study proposes machine learning (ML) classiﬁers for autism trait screening using a toddler dataset with 19 attributes, emphasizing optimized feature selection and hyperparameter tuning. Feature selection techniques, including Chi-squared tests, ML based feature importance, and pairwise correlation analysis, identify key predictive features. Multiple subsets of features are systematically evaluated to optimize classiﬁer performance. Notably, considering only the Q1–Q10 behavioural features resulted in a 41% reduction in features, enhancing classiﬁcation performance while reducing computational complexity. The Logistic Regression and k-nearest neighbors (LR-KNN) ensemble achieved an accuracy of 99%, followed by the Artiﬁcial Neural Network (ANN) at 98%, Logistic Regression (LR) at 97%, and k-nearest neighbors (KNN) at 94%. Through a systematic analysis of feature selection, an optimal feature subset is identiﬁed that balances model complexity and predictive power. These ﬁndings highlight the role of feature selection in improving ML based screening, offering a scalable and cost effective approach for early detection and personalized intervention strategies. This approach not only streamlines the screening process but also facilitates the way for integrating ML techniques into clinical settings." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Sankari M S (2025). Predicting autism traits using Q-CHAT behavioral features: a machine learning approach. *Engineering Research Express, 7(2), Article 025269*. https://doi.org/10.1088/2631-8695/addb0b" ;
    dcterms:creator "Sankari M S" ;
    dcterms:title "Predicting autism traits using Q-CHAT behavioral features: a machine learning approach" ;
    bibo:doi "10.1088/2631-8695/addb0b" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_14df790d" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2025 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Sample size justified" ;
                            slr:justification "No justification; and the split sizes are never stated, with the test partition recoverable only from confusion-matrix totals that disagree across models." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participants appropriate to the question" ;
                            slr:justification "App-submitted records with no verification of respondent or child and no recruitment procedure." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data source and eligibility described" ;
                            slr:justification "Dataset cited with class counts, age range, sex composition within the autism group and an eleven-group ethnicity description - fuller than most of the branch - but no eligibility criteria, collection dates or response rate." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Predictors assessed blind to outcome" ;
                            slr:justification "Chi-squared, tree-importance and correlation selection all rank features against the class label, with no statement of nesting inside the training partition." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Preprocessing transparently reported" ;
                            slr:justification "Preprocessing is published as a numbered algorithm specifying every encoding and column operation, and no resampling is applied - the 69/31 imbalance is left in place." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Predictors defined and measured appropriately" ;
                            slr:justification "The Q-CHAT items and the Q-CHAT total are both in the feature pool and both determine the label; the paper notes the redundancy without pursuing it." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome accurately described" ;
                            slr:justification "The title and abstract describe the target as autism traits rather than autism diagnosis, which is accurate and rare in this branch." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome determined independently of predictors" ;
                            slr:justification "It is not - the label is a threshold on the sum of items supplied as features." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome definition and ascertainment" ;
                            slr:justification "Outcome is the Q-CHAT-10 screening result from app-submitted responses; no clinical ascertainment for any record." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Limitations and overinterpretation" ;
                            slr:justification "No limitations section, but two candid remarks - that results across subsets were not entirely satisfactory, and that retaining both the Q-CHAT total and its items could mislead the model. Claims are confined to screening support rather than diagnosis." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Validation design guards against optimism" ;
                            slr:justification "Split proportions and cross-validation k are unstated, no per-fold results are given, selection and tuning nesting is unstated, and tuning is described as followed by training and evaluation on the dataset." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance measures adequate" ;
                            slr:justification "Accuracy, precision, F1, sensitivity, specificity, MCC and confusion matrices for every model, with MCC included deliberately because of imbalance - the fullest metric set in the branch. But no confidence intervals, no chance level (69.1% majority class), and confusion matrices implying different test partitions across models." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Analysis unit matches the clinical claim" ;
                            slr:justification "One row per toddler, matching the claim's subject." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The stated goal is to identify a minimal feature subset for ASD trait screening in toddlers through systematic feature selection and hyperparameter tuning, and to map Q1-Q10 and the Q-CHAT total onto DSM-5 behavioural domains. Headline findings: using Q1-Q10 alone (a 41% feature reduction) gives the best performance across all classifiers, with the LR-KNN ensemble at accuracy 0.99, precision 1.00, specificity 1.00 and MCC 0.98, followed by ANN 0.98, LR 0.97 and KNN 0.94. Q9, Q6 and Q5 rank consistently highest across chi-squared, tree-importance and correlation methods; Q8 and Q10 lowest." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-150" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Parent-reported early social-communication behaviours from the Q-CHAT: response to name, ease of eye contact, pointing to express desires, and the remaining seven items, each mapped to a DSM-5 behavioural domain. The item-level analysis produces a consistent ordering - Q9, Q6 and Q5 most informative, Q8 and Q10 least - which is the paper's substantive behavioural claim." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-150" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, precision, F1, sensitivity, specificity, MCC and a confusion matrix for every model, plus per-subset comparisons and an iterative feature-addition curve. The metric set is the most complete in this branch: MCC is included deliberately because of the class imbalance, and its motivation - that incorrect diagnoses have significant implications for families - is stated. It also does real work, separating LR (accuracy 0.97, MCC 0.92) from ANN (0.98, 0.98). Two problems. No confidence interval accompanies any figure. And the confusion matrices are mutually inconsistent: LR, KNN and LR-KNN each have class totals of 105 and 212, while the ANN's are 110 and 207, so the four models were not all evaluated on the same 317-record partition." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-150" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Not available - the extracted text ends mid-discussion and contains no conclusion or future-work section." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-150" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No limitations section, but two candid remarks stand out. The author writes that on reviewing performance across multiple feature subsets, the results obtained were not entirely satisfactory - an unusual admission in a literature where every result is a success. And, discussing Subset 2, that it included both the Qchat-10-Score and Q1-Q10 features, which could sometimes mislead the model results. That second sentence is the closest any of the six Q-CHAT-based studies charted in this review comes to naming the circularity at the centre of the branch: the total and its own summands are both in the feature set, and the label is a threshold on the total. Having noticed it, the paper treats it as a redundancy nuisance and moves on." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-150" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No setting. A single-author secondary analysis of a downloaded file; no computing environment or runtime is described." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-150" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "machine learning; feature selection; hyperparameter tuning; logistic regression; k-nearest neighbors; artificial neural network. Six terms, all naming technique - autism, ASD, Q-CHAT, toddlers and screening appear in none of them, which is unusual and leaves the keyword list disconnected from the paper's subject." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-150" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2025, published 28 May, 132 days from submission to acceptance. Framing is screening-support rather than diagnostic replacement and is among the most measured in the corpus - the title claims to predict autism traits, the abstract offers a scalable and cost-effective approach for early detection and personalized intervention strategies, and the discussion admits that results across subsets were not entirely satisfactory. The overreach is confined to the closing claim of facilitating the way for integrating ML techniques into clinical settings. Its distinguishing contribution is analytical rather than predictive: by systematically varying which parts of the Q-CHAT arithmetic enter the feature set, it produces the branch's most complete map of why these accuracies are what they are." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-150" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No cognitive, adaptive, language or severity instrument. The Qchat-10-Score total appears as a predictor in two feature subsets rather than as a participant characteristic." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-150" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The validation design is under-reported - no split proportions, no value of k, no per-fold results, no statement of whether feature selection and tuning were nested inside the training partition, and a passage stating that the manually tuned model is then trained and evaluated on the dataset to ensure generalization, which describes tuning and evaluating on the same data. The confusion matrices imply a 317-record test partition but disagree on its class composition across models (105/212 for three of them, 110/207 for the ANN). Underneath that, the estimand is the branch's usual one: Class/ASD traits is the Q-CHAT-10 outcome and Q1-Q10 are its summands, so predicting the former from the latter is arithmetic recovery. What makes this study analytically valuable to the review is that its own feature-subset experiment maps the circularity almost completely without naming it. Subsets 3 and 4 retain the Qchat-10-Score and discard the items; Q1-Q10 retains the items and discards the total; Subset 2 retains both, and the author observes that this could sometimes mislead the model results. Every configuration containing either the total or its summands performs well, and the reported conclusion - that Q1-Q10 alone gives optimal performance with 41% fewer features - is a statement about which encoding of the label's own arithmetic is most compact. The single most revealing number in the paper is in the iterative analysis: with only the two least significant features, age and sex, sensitivity was 99.5%. With 69.1% of records positive, that is what a near-all-positive classifier produces, and the surrounding text treats it as a baseline from which adding Q1 (sensitivity 97.6%) and Q2-Q4 (92.8%, 91.3%) represents degradation. Read correctly it demonstrates that sensitivity alone is uninformative on this dataset and that the majority-class baseline is 69.1% accuracy - neither point is made, and no chance level appears anywhere in the article." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-150" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. The outcome is the Class/ASD traits field, the Q-CHAT-10 screening result, applied to app-submitted responses with no clinical assessment, no clinician, no instrument beyond the checklist and no verification of who completed it. The paper's title and abstract are accurate about this - it predicts autism traits, not autism - and that accuracy distinguishes it from several neighbours in the corpus that describe the same label as a diagnosis." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-150" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No task or paradigm. The instrument is the ten-item Quantitative Checklist for Autism in Toddlers, administered through a screening app on a five-point frequency scale from never to always, collapsed to binary. Table 1 maps each question to its behavioural construct - response to name, eye contact, finger pointing and so on - which is more transparency about the instrument than most of the branch offers." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-150" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Standardised tabular vectors in five configurations: the default 19-attribute set; Subset 1 (chi-squared, ordered by Q1-Q10 with Q9, Q5, Q6 top); Subset 2 (tree importance, retaining both Qchat-10-Score and Q1-Q10); Subset 3 (correlation-based: Qchat-10-Score, sex, jaundice, age, family history, ethnicity, respondent); Subset 4 (minimal: Qchat-10-Score, age, sex, jaundice); and Q1-Q10 alone. The configurations are the study's real object, and their comparison is where its diagnostic value lies - Subsets 3 and 4 keep the Q-CHAT total and drop the items; Q1-Q10 keeps the items and drops the total; Subset 2 keeps both." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-150" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "ASD, autism spectrum disorder, autism traits, ASD traits, toddlers, individuals with ASD, controls, non-ASD individuals. The register is careful and the title is the most honest in this branch of the corpus: predicting autism traits, not diagnosing autism. ASD is described as a lifelong neurodevelopmental condition, controls rather than normals are used for the comparison class, and the stated motivation includes that incorrect diagnoses can have significant implications for families. One slip: the opening sentence calls ASD a complex neuropsychiatric developmental condition." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-150" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No fusion - a single questionnaire modality with demographic covariates. The systematic subset comparison is a study of feature configurations within one modality, not multimodal integration." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-150" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Four supervised learners: logistic regression, k-nearest neighbours, a feed-forward artificial neural network, and a soft LR-KNN ensemble presented as the contribution. No tree-based or boosted classifier is used for prediction, though RF and Extra Trees are used for feature ranking." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-150" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Moderate, and the strongest in this branch alongside local_ed30e24c. The preprocessing is published as a numbered algorithm specifying every encoding and column operation; the dataset is public; the composition of each of the four feature subsets is listed; the statistical tests and their exact p-values are tabulated; and confusion matrices are printed for all four final models. Missing: split proportions, the value of k in the cross-validation, any per-fold result, final hyperparameter values, the ANN architecture, the ensemble's combination rule, a seed, and code. The extracted PDF also ends mid-discussion after Table 7, so no conclusion or funding statement is available." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-150" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Better than most of the branch, though still descriptive only. Age averages 25 to 28 months with a note that labelled cases rise between 25 and 30 months and become more noticeable by 35-36 months. Sex is given only as 69.7% male within the autism group, with no control-group figure. Ethnicity is reported across eleven groups with White Europeans most represented followed by Asians, and the paper states there were no significant ethnic differences in diagnosis rates - the one ethnicity analysis in this branch. Jaundice, family history and respondent type are present. No performance is broken down by any of these, so the demographic detail informs the sample description but not the evaluation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-150" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional secondary analysis. The ethics statement is the notable feature: the dataset was collected via an ASD test screening application, with no direct human interaction, with responses submitted by parents and other stakeholders, ensuring ethical approval for research purposes. Absence of contact between researcher and participant is presented as itself supplying ethical approval. No committee, protocol number, consent statement or data-governance arrangement appears, and no consideration is given to whether app users agreed to research reuse of their children's screening responses." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-150" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Engineering Research Express 7(2):025269 (IOP Publishing) - an open-access general engineering journal. A 132-day review including one revision round did not surface the mutually inconsistent confusion matrices, the missing split proportions and cross-validation k, the unstated chance level, or an ethics statement that treats absence of direct human interaction as constituting ethical approval." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-150" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The most thorough feature analysis in this branch: chi-squared scores, RF and Extra Trees impurity importance, Pearson and Spearman correlations, variance inflation factors, Kruskal-Wallis association tests with exact p-values, a DSM-5 domain mapping for each item, and an iterative curve tracking sensitivity and specificity as Q1 through Q10 are added one at a time. Three items (Q9, Q6, Q5) rank highest under all three methods. The analysis is careful and internally consistent; what limits it is that the label is a threshold on the sum of the very items being ranked, so the Kruskal-Wallis p-values of order 1e-100 and the convergence across three ranking methods are arithmetic consequences rather than empirical discoveries about which behaviours matter." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-150" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No instrumentation. Analytical components: StandardScaler, one-hot and label encoding, SelectKBest with chi-squared, RF and Extra Trees importance, Pearson and Spearman correlation, variance inflation factor, Kruskal-Wallis tests, logistic regression, KNN, a feed-forward ANN and an LR-KNN ensemble. No library names, versions, seed or hardware." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-150" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No participants. 1,054 records from the public toddler Q-CHAT dataset, 728 labelled autism and 326 control - a 69.1% positive rate that the paper reports and, unusually for this branch, leaves in place rather than resampling. The records were submitted through an ASD screening application by parents and other stakeholders, so neither respondent nor child was verified. The confusion matrices imply a test partition of 317 records, though no split proportion is stated; the row totals also differ across models within the same table (105/212 for LR, KNN and LR-KNN but 110/207 for the ANN), which they should not if all four were evaluated on the same partition." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-150" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Internal: four classifiers across five feature configurations. External: literature-quoted accuracies in Table 8, none re-run." ;
                    slr:study_design "Retrospective secondary analysis of a single public tabular screening dataset; four classifiers evaluated across a default feature set and four selected subsets, with hyperparamete…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 728 records labelled autism (69.1%); comparison: 326 records labelled control (30.9%)" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "With Q1-Q10 alone: LR-KNN ensemble accuracy 0.99, precision 1.00, F1 0.99, MCC 0.98, sensitivity 0.99, specificity 1.00; ANN 0.98/0.98/1.00/0.98/1.00/0.98; LR 0.97/0.97/0.98/0.92/1.00/0.90; KNN 0.94/0.96/0.95/0.85/0.93/0.92." ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy, precision, F1, sensitivity, specificity, Matthews correlation coefficient and a confusion matrix for every model in the final configuration, plus per-subset accuracy, sensitivity and specificity in a bar chart, and an iterative feature-addition analysis of sensitivity and specificity. The " ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "With Q1-Q10 alone: LR-KNN ensemble accuracy 0.99, precision 1.00, F1 0.99, MCC 0.98, sensitivity 0.99, specificity 1.00; ANN 0.98/0.98/1.00/0.98/1.00/0.98; LR 0.97/0.97/0.98/0.92/1.00/0.90; KNN 0.94/0.96/0.95/0.85/0.93/0.92." ] ] ;
