                    slr:supporting_quote "Forty-five children from an existing purpose-collected corpus with specialist-confirmed diagnoses and both groups characterised on age, SCQ, nonverbal intelligence and receptive vocabulary. Recruitment procedure is not described in this paper, sex is unreported, the sample is small, and the ASD group's age variability is more than double the comparison group's." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "some" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<https://doi.org/10.1186/s11689-025-09668-8> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Background Movement differences in autism have attracted growing attention in recent years. Anecdotally, autistic movement has been likened to that of Parkinson’s Disease (PD). Given that PD assessments are primarily movementbased, it is important to ensure that autistic individuals are not scoring highly on PD diagnostic criteria due to autismrelated movement differences. Quantifying overlap in movement profiles and identifying distinguishing features is essential, particularly given increased PD diagnosis rates in the autistic population. Methods We conducted the first direct comparison study of autistic and parkinsonian movement. Autistic individuals (N = 31), individuals with PD (N = 32) and control participants (N = 31) completed a Shapes Tracing Task and a Reaction Time Task. Kinematic features were compared between groups and classification algorithms were run to distinguish between groups. Results Groups were distinguishable based on kinematic features. The autistic group differed from both PD and control groups in speed modulation and sub-movements, and from the PD group in reaction time. Classification algorithms for clinical (autism and PD) versus non-clinical groups, and for autism versus PD, were most accurate when combining kinematic and questionnaire data. There were no kinematic similarities between autism and PD that were also distinct from controls. Conclusions Whilst kinematic features did not appear similar between autism and PD, they were informative for group classification. This proof-of-concept study highlights that movement-based metrics may aid in identifying whether someone belongs to a clinical group, and which one – suggesting potential for refining diagnostic approaches for both autism and PD." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Hickman LJ; Fraser DS; Galea JM; Happé F; Cook JL (2026). An assessment of autistic and parkinsonian movement profiles to inform selective classification algorithms. *Journal of Neurodevelopmental*. https://doi.org/10.1186/s11689-025-09668-8" ;
    dcterms:creator "Cook JL",
        "Fraser DS",
        "Galea JM",
        "Happé F",
        "Hickman LJ" ;
    dcterms:title "An assessment of autistic and parkinsonian movement profiles to inform selective classification algorithms" ;
    bibo:doi "10.1186/s11689-025-09668-8" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_69b10fe4" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2026 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data source and eligibility described" ;
                            slr:justification "Recruitment channels named, diagnostic evidence required, exclusions prespecified, recruitment order and matching strategy reported." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participants appropriate to the question" ;
                            slr:justification "Three groups including a second movement-affecting clinical condition, with documented formal diagnoses and mutual exclusion; near sex-balanced autistic sample." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Confounders addressed" ;
                            slr:justification "Gender and non-verbal reasoning matched; age, depression and anxiety entered as control variables and residualised from classification inputs; PD participants tested before morning medication." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Predictors independent of the outcome" ;
                            slr:justification "Kinematics are task-derived; where questionnaire traits are used they are declared as a separate predictor set rather than mixed silently into a kinematic model." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Feature selection guarded against optimism" ;
                            slr:justification "Discriminatory feature sets were selected by group-difference significance computed on all participants, including those later held out." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Predictors defined and measured appropriately" ;
                            slr:justification "Nine standard kinematic features across four shapes spanning the angular-frequency spectrum, with outlier percentages and transformations reported per variable." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "unclear" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome definition and ascertainment" ;
                            slr:justification "Documented formal clinical diagnoses of ASD or PD, verified through clinician letters or research databases." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome limitations acknowledged" ;
                            slr:justification "Undiagnosed cases and generational under-diagnosis of autism in the older comparison groups are both named." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome determined independently of predictors" ;
                            slr:justification "Diagnoses predate the study and are unrelated to the tracing task." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Validation design guards against optimism" ;
                            slr:justification "Participant-level 80/20 split with all three algorithms and the full configuration grid reported, but test partitions of roughly 13 to 19 participants and feature selection performed on the full sample." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Analysis unit matches the clinical claim" ;
                            slr:justification "One row per participant; the split partitions people." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Limitations and overinterpretation" ;
                            slr:justification "Five mechanistic limitations including cohort effects in ascertainment; work labelled proof-of-concept; kinematics proposed alongside rather than in place of clinical assessment." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance measures adequate" ;
                            slr:justification "Chance is used as the explicit reference and every configuration including the two at-chance models is published, but no confidence interval accompanies any accuracy; the 95% interval around 0.93 spans roughly 0.66 to 1.00." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Evidence for the null properly handled" ;
                            slr:justification "Bayesian ANOVAs with pre-stated BF01 bands are used to distinguish absence of evidence from evidence of absence, and the authors decline to claim similarity where Bayes factors are low." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "unclear" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Fine motor kinematics during constrained tracing: speed, acceleration, jerk, minimum and maximum speed, number of sub-movements, speed modulation (how sharply speed adapts to trajectory curvature), smoothness via SPARC, and tracing error, plus simple reaction time. The discriminating features are specific and directional - autistic participants showed greater speed modulation and more sub-movements than both other groups, and differed from PD in reaction time - and the paper is careful about mechanism, noting that reported increases in jerk in autism may be epiphenomenal on speed differences because the two quantities are mathematically linked." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-158" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Autism; Parkinson's disease; Movement; Classification. Four terms, both conditions named, the behavioural domain named, and the analytic task named - with no architecture, no algorithm and no accuracy language." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-158" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A genuinely analytical limitations section with five distinct items, each carrying a mechanism: diagnosis-based grouping cannot exclude undiagnosed cases; the PD and control groups come from a generation in which autism diagnoses were not prevalent, so undiagnosed autistic people may be present in them; stylus resistance and tablet sampling and spatial resolution may have suppressed group differences relative to motion tracking; kinematic features are context-dependent and the results may be task-specific; and the autistic sample was older than those in the existing kinematic literature, so apparent contradictions with prior findings may be age effects. Two further issues go unstated: the classification test partitions contain roughly 13 to 19 participants, and the discriminatory feature sets were selected using group-difference tests computed on all participants." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-158" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A Shapes Tracing Task with four shapes chosen to span the angular-frequency spectrum (clover, petals, ellipse, rounded square) plus a Reaction Time Task, both stylus-based. The shape design is deliberate and yields a methodological result the review should record: the ellipse was the least useful shape for distinguishing groups, and the authors point out that most drawing tasks in the literature index only elliptical or spiral movements, recommending that future studies span the full angular-frequency range. That is a concrete, actionable finding about task design derived from data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-158" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Comprehensive and instrument-based: UPDRS Part II for parkinsonian traits, RAADS and AQ for autistic traits, PHQ for depression, GAD for anxiety, MaRs-IB for non-verbal reasoning, and years since diagnosis. All are reported by group with ANOVA and post-hoc comparisons, and the ordering is as expected (autistic traits ASD>PD>CTRL; parkinsonian traits PD>ASD>CTRL). Crucially the trait instruments are used both as sample descriptors and, in some model configurations, as declared predictors alongside kinematics - so their role is explicit rather than hidden." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-158" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two aims, one confirmatory and one applied. First, to test directly whether autistic and parkinsonian movement resemble each other - a comparison previously possible only by juxtaposing separate studies with different designs and demographics. Second, to assess whether kinematic features can classify clinical versus non-clinical status and, within the clinical groups, ASD versus PD. Findings: autistic participants differed from both PD and controls in speed modulation and sub-movements, and from PD in reaction time; classification reached a best mean test accuracy of 0.73 for clinical versus non-clinical and 0.93 for ASD versus PD, in both cases from kinematics combined with questionnaires; and, most importantly, no kinematic feature was found to be similar between ASD and PD while also differing from controls. The anecdotal likeness between autistic and parkinsonian movement is not supported." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-158" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Substantive and directional. The study identifies which features discriminate (speed modulation, sub-movements, reaction time), in which direction, and between which pairs of groups; which task conditions are informative (the ellipse least so); and where an apparent effect may be an artefact of another (jerk as a possible epiphenomenon of speed). It also uses Bayesian analysis to distinguish absence of evidence from evidence of absence, reporting that low Bayes factors mean there is not strong evidence to conclude that movements were the same between groups where differences were not found - a distinction almost never drawn in this literature." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-158" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Test accuracy per algorithm and averaged across the three, with the full grid published as a heat map colour-coded against chance and ordered by mean accuracy - so the reader sees every configuration, including the two that performed at chance, rather than only the maximum. For the group-difference analyses the reporting is fuller still: F statistics, p-values, beta estimates with 95% confidence intervals, and Bayes factors quantifying evidence for the null with interpretive bands stated in advance. What is absent is any interval on the classification accuracies, which matters because the test partitions hold roughly 13 to 19 participants; the 95% interval around the 0.93 headline spans approximately 0.66 to 1.00, and around 0.73 approximately 0.49 to 0.90." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-158" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Journal of Neurodevelopmental Disorders 18:8, a specialist neurodevelopmental journal, with a 180-day review - the longest among the charted 2026 studies. The disciplinary fit is visible throughout: formal group matching with equivalence tests, mixed models with appropriate random-effect structure, Bayesian evidence for the null, pre-specified interpretive thresholds, deposited data and scripts, complete ethics reporting including participant remuneration, and a limitations section that reasons about cohort effects in ascertainment." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-158" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The validation design is participant-level and honestly reported, with three qualifications that the review should record rather than treat as disqualifying. The structure is correct: an 80/20 split of participants, with test accuracy computed on held-out people and reported for each of three algorithms and as their mean, and with the entire configuration grid published so that the reported maximum sits visibly among its alternatives, including two models at chance. The qualifications are, first, size - 63 participants in the ASD-versus-PD contrast and 94 in clinical-versus-non-clinical give test partitions of roughly 13 and 19 people, so a 0.93 mean accuracy corresponds to about 12 of 13 correct and carries a confidence interval running from roughly 0.66 to 1.00; no interval is reported. Second, selection - the discriminatory kinematic feature sets were defined by group-difference significance from mixed models fitted on all participants, so configurations using those sets saw the held-out participants' labels during feature selection and are optimistically biased; the all-kinematic-features configurations are unaffected, and the paper's own observation that variability was seen in whether discriminatory or all features yielded stronger accuracy is consistent with the selection buying less than it might. Third, no external cohort, which the authors acknowledge by describing the work as proof-of-concept. Set against the corpus, what makes this study valuable is not its accuracy figure but its structure: the negative result is the primary finding, it is supported by Bayesian evidence for the null rather than by absence of significance, and the classification is offered as a secondary demonstration rather than as a diagnostic claim." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-158" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Home-based testing on a supplied tablet over two days no more than three days apart, following an online screening form, questionnaire battery and reasoning task. The PD group completed the tasks before their first dopaminergic dose each morning. Home administration is presented as an advantage for scalability - the protocol could be run in community settings without laboratory kinematic equipment - and its cost is acknowledged in the limitations, where stylus resistance and the tablet's sampling and spatial resolution are named as possible suppressors of group differences." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-158" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Employ a range of shapes across the angular-frequency spectrum rather than only ellipses or spirals; investigate kinematic features for early screening in conjunction with clinical assessments; and separate clinically driven movement decline from normal ageing, a need the study itself establishes by demonstrating age effects on several kinematic features." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-158" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The highest in the charted corpus alongside local_4da2b941. Data and analysis scripts are deposited on OSF; all software is named with versions; the device and its screen specification are given; the outlier rule is stated with the exact percentage removed for each of the nine features; the normalising transformations are listed per variable; the mixed-model formulae are written out including random-effect structure; the feature-selection rule is specified precisely, including how group-by-shape interactions were handled; and the full classification grid is published as a heat map rather than reduced to a headline. The only gaps are the composition of the individual 80/20 draws and whether JASP's parameter optimisation was nested inside the split." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-158" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Ninety-four adults in three groups: 31 autistic adults, 32 adults with Parkinson's disease and 31 controls. Both clinical groups required documentary evidence of a formal clinical diagnosis or came from research databases where such evidence had been verified, with mutual exclusion between the two conditions and exclusion of co-occurring movement or developmental disorders. Recruitment order is reported and is itself methodologically informative - the PD group was recruited first as an opportunity sample, autistic adults were then recruited to match it, and controls to match both. The analysis unit is the participant throughout, and the 80/20 classification split partitions people. What limits the classification estimates is size rather than structure: the ASD-versus-PD test partition contains roughly 13 people and the clinical-versus-non-clinical partition roughly 19." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-158" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Clinically ascertained with documentation, and honestly bounded. Participants had to supply evidence of a formal clinical diagnosis such as a clinician's letter, or be drawn from databases where that evidence had been verified. The limitations section then names the two residual problems precisely: absence of a diagnosis does not exclude undiagnosed cases, and the PD and control participants belong to a generation in which autism diagnoses were not prevalent, so undiagnosed autistic people may sit in the comparison groups. That second observation is a genuine insight about ascertainment in older cohorts that appears nowhere else in this corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-158" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Samsung Galaxy Tab A7 with a 10.4-inch 2000x1200 touchscreen and stylus; tasks programmed in PsychoPy and delivered via Pavlovia (PsychoJS 2021.1.4); MaRs-IB on Gorilla. Analysis in R Studio 2022.07.2 (matching), MATLAB 2022a with fitlme (mixed models) and JASP 0.17.2.1 (Bayesian analyses and classification). Every package is named with a version." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-158" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2026, accepted 15 December 2025 after 180 days. The framing is unusual for this corpus in that its primary contribution is negative and clinically protective rather than a performance claim. The motivating concern is that PD assessments are primarily movement-based, so autistic people may score highly on PD diagnostic criteria because of autism-related movement differences - a differential-diagnosis risk that matters given rising PD diagnosis rates in autistic populations. The study then finds no kinematic similarity between autism and PD that is also distinct from controls, which addresses that concern directly. The classification work is labelled proof-of-concept, kinematics are proposed for use in conjunction with clinical assessments rather than instead of them, and the applied suggestion - that a tablet-and-stylus protocol could support community-scale assessment without laboratory equipment - is proportionate to what was demonstrated." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-158" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The most complete adult-sample reporting in the corpus, with formal matching tests. Gender is matched across groups (chi-squared(4)=3.25, ns) and the autistic sample is close to sex-balanced at 14M/16F/1 other - a composition no other autism study in this review achieves. Age: ASD 55.52 (8.01), PD 63.16 (7.60), CTRL 59.00 (9.96); both clinical groups matched to controls but ASD and PD not matched to each other (p<0.001), which the authors handle by entering age as a control variable and residualising it from the classification inputs. Non-verbal reasoning (MaRs-IB) is matched. Depression and anxiety differ significantly and are controlled for. Years since diagnosis, psychoactive medication use, and UPDRS, RAADS and AQ trait scores are all reported by group with post-hoc comparisons." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-158" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Autistic individuals, autistic adults, autistic movement, individuals with PD, control participants, non-autistic individuals. Identity-first language is used consistently and without hedging, movement differences are described as differences rather than deficits or abnormalities, and the framing throughout treats autistic movement as a characteristic to be measured rather than a pathology to be detected. The paper also cites the proposal that movement differences be added as specifiers to autism diagnoses - condition-relevant but non-selective - which is a precise and non-inflationary way of positioning the finding." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-158" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional with two testing days; no longitudinal follow-up, which the authors identify as limiting their ability to know whether autistic participants later develop PD. Ethics reporting is complete: two Research Ethics Committee approval numbers (ERN_18-1800B and ERN_16-0281AP5), Declaration of Helsinki compliance, fully informed consent, and payment of GBP 10 per hour - remuneration being reported at all is uncommon in this corpus and is relevant to the ethics of research participation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-158" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three standard classifiers - KNN, random forest and linear-kernel SVM - selected on the explicit ground that these three have been used together in comparably sized studies of parkinsonian movement. No deep learning and no novel architecture; results are reported for all three separately and as a mean, so no single algorithm's best run stands in for the finding." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-158" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Genuine and declared multimodal fusion, and the study's most useful applied finding. Kinematic features and self-report questionnaire scores are combined in an explicit grid of predictor configurations - kinematics alone, questionnaires alone (ASD, PD, or both) and their combinations - and the combination outperforms either alone for both classification problems (0.73 clinical versus non-clinical; 0.93 ASD versus PD). Because the questionnaire sets are named and separated, the contribution of each modality is visible rather than buried, and the finding that both trait questionnaires together beat either individually is directly interpretable." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-158" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "One row per participant. Thirty-seven kinematic input variables (nine features across four shapes plus one reaction time value), computed as residuals after controlling for age, depression and anxiety and averaged across trials and days; plus three questionnaire sets. A reduced discriminatory kinematic feature set was defined by retaining features, or feature-shape combinations, that showed significant group differences in the mixed models. That selection rule is stated precisely - including that a group-by-shape interaction yielding a difference on only one shape contributes only that shape's value - which is both good reporting and the point at which the design leaks, since the significance tests were computed over all participants including those later held out." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-158" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Systematic: three algorithms crossed with predictor configurations spanning all kinematic features, discriminatory kinematic features, three questionnaire sets and their combinations, for two classifi" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional three-group comparison with primary kinematic data collection over two testing days, followed by linear mixed-model analysis of group differences and supervised cla…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 31 autistic adults (14 male, 16 female, 1 other; mean age 55.52, SD 8.01; mean 4.23 years since diagnosis); comparison: Two comparison groups: 32 adults with Parkinson's disease (19 male, 13 female; mean age 63.16, SD 7.60; 3.69 years since diagnosis) and 31 controls (15 male, 16 female; mean age 59.00, SD 9.96). The second clinical group is the study's defining feature - the comparison is against another movement-affecting condition rather than only against non-clinical controls." ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Clinical (ASD+PD) versus non-clinical: best mean test accuracy 0.73, from discriminatory kinematic features combined with both questionnaire sets. ASD versus PD: best mean test accuracy 0.93, again from kinematics plus both questionnaires, with discriminatory kinematic features alone reaching 0.80 under SVM. All models were above chance except two, which performed at chance (0.50). The principal n" ;
                    slr:outcome "Test accuracy per algorithm and averaged across algorithms, displayed as a heat map with chance-level colour coding. For the group-difference analyses: F statistics, p-values, beta estimates with 95% confidence intervals, and Bayes factors (BF01) quantifying evidence for the null with interpretive t" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Clinical (ASD+PD) versus non-clinical: best mean test accuracy 0.73, from discriminatory kinematic features combined with both questionnaire sets. ASD versus PD: best mean test accuracy 0.93, again from kinematics plus both questionnaires, with discriminatory kinematic features alone reaching 0.80 under SVM. All models were above chance except two, which performed at chance (0.50). The principal n" ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "e84a8cca66f22b7729fc75aefa22ccda68c477e56f09c8e7d92827f2cf6f947e" ;
