<https://doi.org/10.32604/cmc.2025.062266> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Autism spectrum disorder (ASD) is a multifaceted neurological developmental condition that manifests in several ways. Nearly all autistic children remain undiagnosed before the age of three. Developmental problems affecting face features are often associated with fundamental brain disorders. The facial evolution of newborns with ASD is quite different from that of typically developing children. Early recognition is very significant to aid families and parents in superstition and denial. Distinguishing facial features from typically developing children is an evident manner to detect children analyzed with ASD. Presently, artificial intelligence (AI) significantly contributes to the emerging computer-aided diagnosis (CAD) of autism and to the evolving interactive methods that aid in the treatment and reintegration of autistic patients. This study introduces an Ensemble of deep learning models based on the autism spectrum disorder detection in facial images (EDLM-ASDDFI) model. The overarching goal of the EDLM-ASDDFI model is to recognize the difference between facial images of individuals with ASD and normal controls. In the EDLM-ASDDFI method, the primary level of data pre-processing is involved by Gabor filtering (GF). Besides, the EDLM-ASDDFI technique applies the MobileNetV2 model to learn complex features from the pre-processed data. For the ASD detection process, the EDLM-ASDDFI method uses ensemble techniques for classification procedure that encompasses long short-term memory (LSTM), deep belief network (DBN), and hybrid kernel extreme learning machine (HKELM). Finally, the hyperparameter selection of the three deep learning (DL) models can be implemented by the design of the crested porcupine optimizer (CPO) technique. An extensive experiment was conducted to emphasize the improved ASD detection performance of the EDLM-ASDDFI method. The simulation outcomes indicated that the EDLM-ASDDFI technique highlighted betterment over other existing models in terms of numerous performance measures." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Balasubramani J; Rajendran S; Zakariah M; Alnuaim A (2025). Ensemble of Deep Learning with Crested Porcupine Optimizer Based Autism. *Computers, Materials & Con*. https://doi.org/10.32604/cmc.2025.062266" ;
    dcterms:creator "Alnuaim A",
        "Balasubramani J",
        "Rajendran S",
        "Zakariah M" ;
    dcterms:title "Ensemble of Deep Learning with Crested Porcupine Optimizer Based Autism" ;
    bibo:doi "10.32604/cmc.2025.062266" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_13c1f46d" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2025 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data source and eligibility described" ;
                            slr:justification "Kaggle URL and access date given, but no provenance, eligibility, demographics or collection method; stated sample size contradicted by the results." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Sample size justified" ;
                            slr:justification "No justification; image count treated as sample size, and the stated 2,926 does not match the roughly 2,800 implied by the confusion matrices." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participants appropriate to the question" ;
                            slr:justification "No participants; unlabelled web-scraped photographs of unknown children, with the number of distinct individuals unknown." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Predictors assessed blind to outcome" ;
                            slr:justification "Feature extraction is automatic and label-independent, but hyperparameters were selected by a metaheuristic against an unstated objective on an unstated data partition." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Predictors defined and measured appropriately" ;
                            slr:justification "Facial texture and shape features from photographs of unstated resolution (the placeholder X, Y pixels survives); an LSTM is applied to single static images with no sequential structure and no justification." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Preprocessing applied without leakage" ;
                            slr:justification "Augmentation, class weighting and normalisation are all described as dataset-level preprocessing with no split in view." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome determined independently of predictors" ;
                            slr:justification "Formally independent, but the label's own validity is unestablished, so independence carries no weight." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome definition and ascertainment" ;
                            slr:justification "Outcome is a Kaggle folder label; no clinical ascertainment, and the ethics statement declares the study exempt on grounds of the dataset's public availability." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Comparison appropriate" ;
                            slr:justification "All seven comparators are figures quoted from other publications on unstated data and protocols; none was re-run on this dataset or split." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance measures adequate" ;
                            slr:justification "No specificity, no numeric AUC, no calibration, no predictive values, no confidence intervals, and no fold variance despite cross-validation being claimed." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Analysis unit matches the clinical claim" ;
                            slr:justification "Claims concern diagnosing children; metrics are computed over images on a corpus with unknown per-child counts." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Limitations and overinterpretation" ;
                            slr:justification "No limitations section, no future work, no caveat of any kind; the closing claim is unqualified superiority, with computer-aided diagnosis framed as helping parents past superstition and denial." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Validation design guards against optimism" ;
                            slr:justification "Confusion matrices span the whole dataset with no split described; an asserted 5-fold cross-validation is reported without any per-fold value; the headline is the best of five epoch settings." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2025, published 16 April, 81 days from submission to acceptance. Framing is unqualified computer-aided diagnosis, with an unusual and revealing justification: early recognition is said to be significant to aid families and parents in superstition and denial, and AI is credited with aiding the treatment and reintegration of autistic patients. The contribution is expressed purely as a higher number than seven quoted baselines. There is no limitations section, no future work, and no acknowledgement that any part of the claim is provisional - the paper's closing position is that the method is superior, full stop." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-145" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Computers, Materials & Continua 83(2) (Tech Science Press) - a broad-scope open-access computing journal covering materials, computing and engineering. This is the review's clearest venue-mismatch case: a diagnostic claim about children, resting on unlabelled web photographs and an explicit ethics exemption, published in a journal with no clinical review capacity, with no limitations section and no future work, in 81 days." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-145" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No behaviour is observed, elicited or measured. The target is static facial appearance - edges, contours and textures at multiple Gabor orientations and scales, described as capturing intricate details of face characteristics including the eyes, nose and mouth." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-145" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, precision, recall, F-score and G-measure, per class and averaged, at 500 through 2,500 epochs; ROC curves described qualitatively; a Gabor ablation with training time and a computational-cost index whose units are not given. No specificity, no numeric AUC, no calibration, no predictive values at a stated prevalence, and no confidence interval anywhere. The reported average of 96.43% is not one measurement but the best of five epoch settings, carried into both the abstract and the comparison table against baselines evaluated under unknown conditions in other papers." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-145" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None of the trained model. The Gabor ablation shows that texture filtering helps (96.43% versus 92.80%) but says nothing about what the classifier attends to. No saliency, no attribution, no per-class error analysis beyond the raw confusion counts. In a study whose premise is that specific facial characteristics carry diagnostic information, no facial characteristic is ever identified." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-145" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No task or paradigm. Static photographs with no stimulus, interaction or session. The construct is facial morphology and texture, justified by the premise that developmental problems affecting face features are often associated with fundamental brain disorders." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-145" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Gabor-filtered RGB face crops at an unstated resolution, passed through MTCNN detection, median denoising and Z-score channel normalisation, then encoded as MobileNetV2 features. Augmentation adds rotation, horizontal flip, cropping and brightness variation, described as a preprocessing stage rather than as a training-time operation. Class weighting is applied on the stated grounds of imbalance, although the source Kaggle corpus is balanced by construction and the paper's own confusion matrices imply near-equal class sizes." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-145" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The stated goal is to distinguish facial images of individuals with ASD from normal controls with an ensemble pipeline: Gabor filtering, MobileNetV2 feature extraction, an ensemble of LSTM, deep belief network and hybrid-kernel extreme learning machine, and Crested Porcupine Optimizer hyperparameter selection. Headline findings: average accuracy 96.43%, precision 96.44%, recall 96.43%, F-score 96.43% and G-measure 96.43% at 1,000 epochs, against literature-quoted comparators between 89.55% and 92.64%. The Gabor ablation - the paper's one genuinely executed experiment - gives 96.43% with filtering against 92.80% without, at less than half the training time." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-145" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. The article contains no limitations section, no threats-to-validity discussion, and no caveat of any kind. Nothing is said about the dataset's web provenance, the absence of diagnostic labels, the evaluation counts that span the whole dataset, the epoch-wise selection of the reported figure, or the fact that all comparator numbers are quoted rather than measured." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-145" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A four-stage pipeline: Gabor filtering, MobileNetV2 feature extraction, a heterogeneous ensemble of LSTM, deep belief network and hybrid-kernel extreme learning machine, and metaheuristic hyperparameter selection by the Crested Porcupine Optimizer. This is the corpus's most elaborate architecture stack applied to its simplest and least trustworthy data. Only one component's contribution is actually tested (Gabor filtering); the others are argued for in the conditional - removing MobileNetV2 may result in diminished performance, evaluating individual classifiers against the ensemble might reveal improvements - without those experiments being run." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-145" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No cognitive, adaptive, language or severity measure, and none obtainable from photographs." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-145" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Autism spectrum disorder; ensemble learning; crested porcupine optimizer; facial images; computer-aided diagnosis. Four of five terms name the machinery; none names participants, validation or setting." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-145" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The validation account is internally contradictory, and this is the study's central defect. The experimental section reports confusion matrices at five epoch settings whose correct-classification counts - 1,338 normal plus 1,362 autism at 1,000 epochs, and comparable figures at the other settings - account for essentially the entire stated dataset. No train-test division is described anywhere in that section. If those matrices are computed over all available images, then every reported metric is a resubstitution figure on the training data. Separately and much later, the comparison discussion asserts that 5-fold cross-validation was applied with 80% training and 20% testing per fold, and that the model consistently attained elevated accuracy and F1 scores across all folds, exhibiting minimal variance - yet not one per-fold number, mean or standard deviation is given, so the claim cannot be checked and does not reconcile with the confusion-matrix counts. On top of that: the reported headline is the best of five epoch settings with no selection criterion or held-out basis; the Crested Porcupine Optimizer searched hyperparameters against an unstated objective on an unstated partition; augmentation is described before any split; and the split, whatever it is, is at the image rather than the child level on a corpus known to repeat faces. The comparison in Table 3 sets this study's figure against seven baselines taken from other publications on unstated data and protocols, which is a comparison of numbers rather than of methods." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-145" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No setting. Images were downloaded from Kaggle on 22 November 2024; the compute environment is not described at all, and the only timing information is an unlabelled training-time column (10.5 versus 26.0) in the Gabor ablation table." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-145" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None whatsoever. No age, sex, ethnicity, geography, socioeconomic status or recruitment source - the dataset carries none and the paper requests none. This is notable in comparison with local_986436f2, which reused a sibling of the same corpus and supplied an elaborate demographic table that the data cannot support; here the same absence is simply left unremarked. The abstract's claim about the facial evolution of newborns with ASD is particularly untethered, since nothing in the data identifies any subject's age, let alone a neonate." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-145" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. There is no future-work section. The paper closes on the claim of superiority over existing methods." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-145" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No participants. The unit is the image: a stated 2,926 facial samples in two classes, Normal and Autism, downloaded from Kaggle. The number of distinct children is unknown and never raised. The stated total does not survive contact with the results: the confusion matrix at 1,000 epochs reports 1,338 correctly recognised normal and 1,362 correctly recognised autism images, which against the per-class accuracies of 95.57% and 97.29% implies about 1,400 images in each class and about 2,800 overall. Those counts also span essentially the whole dataset, which means the reported confusion matrices are not computed on a held-out subset at all." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-145" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional. Ethics is handled by explicit exemption: the study did not involve direct experiments on humans or the use of human tissue samples, and publicly available Kaggle datasets were used instead. That reasoning treats identifiable photographs of children - carrying a disability label that no clinician assigned - as outside the scope of human-subjects consideration entirely. It is the clearest statement in the corpus of the assumption that makes this branch of the literature possible, and it is stated as a formal declaration rather than in passing." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-145" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None for acquisition. Processing components named: Gabor filter bank, MTCNN face detection, median filter, Z-score colour normalisation, bicubic interpolation, MobileNetV2, LSTM, DBN built from restricted Boltzmann machines, hybrid-kernel ELM, Crested Porcupine Optimizer. No software, versions, seed, hardware or runtime environment is given, and the target image resolution is left as the literal placeholder (X, Y pixels)." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-145" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Very low. Some preprocessing choices are specified with real precision - rotation range -15 to 15 degrees, horizontal flips, random crops to 10%, brightness 0.8 to 1.2, inverse-frequency class weights, Z-score channel normalisation - and the source dataset is properly cited with an access date. Everything needed to actually rebuild the system is missing: no code, no libraries, no seed, no image size, no layer configuration, no ensemble combination rule, no CPO search space or budget, no split specification, and a stated sample size that the reported confusion matrices contradict." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-145" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No fusion of modalities - a single image source. What the paper calls an ensemble is three classifiers (LSTM, DBN, HKELM) over one shared MobileNetV2 feature vector from one photograph. The LSTM's inclusion is unexplained: there is no temporal or sequential structure in a single still image for a recurrent model to exploit, and the paper never addresses this." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-145" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "ASD, autism spectrum disorder, autistic children, autistic patients, individuals with ASD, normal controls, Normal class, typically developing children. The pathologising register is unusually strong even for this branch: autism is described as a set of intricate neuro-developmental brain diseases, the comparison class is labelled Normal throughout, and the stated benefit of early recognition is to aid families and parents in superstition and denial. Elsewhere autistic people are said to require treatment and reintegration. There is no autistic or community perspective anywhere in the article." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-145" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. Labels are Kaggle folder names on photographs of unknown origin. No clinical assessment, instrument, criterion, assessor, date or provenance record exists for a single image, and the paper does not acknowledge the issue at any point - including in its ethics statement, which cites the dataset's public availability as the reason no ethical review was required." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-145" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Literature-quoted figures for MobileNet (90.24%), VGG-16 with logistic regression (89.55%), random forest (91.00%), decision tree (90.11%) and gradient boosting (92.64%), multi-kernel SVM (91.98%) and" ;
                    slr:study_design "Retrospective secondary analysis of a public Kaggle facial-image dataset; supervised binary image classification with a metaheuristic hyperparameter search and a reported 5-fold cr…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: Not stated. Inferable as approximately 1,400 from the confusion-matrix arithmetic.; comparison: Not stated; approximately 1,400 by the same inference. The paper simultaneously asserts that the dataset suggests a possible class imbalance requiring consideration and applies inverse-frequency class weights, although the source dataset is balanced by construction." ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Average accuracy 96.43%, precision 96.44%, recall 96.43%, F-score 96.43%, G-measure 96.43% at 1,000 epochs. Per class at that setting: Normal accuracy 95.57%, precision 97.24%; Autism accuracy 97.29%, precision 95.65%. The Gabor ablation reports 96.43% with filtering against 92.80% without." ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy, precision, recall, F-score and G-measure, per class and averaged, at five epoch counts; ROC curves; a Gabor filtering ablation with training time and a computational-cost index. Specificity, AUC as a number, calibration, predictive values and confidence intervals are all absent." ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Average accuracy 96.43%, precision 96.44%, recall 96.43%, F-score 96.43%, G-measure 96.43% at 1,000 epochs. Per class at that setting: Normal accuracy 95.57%, precision 97.24%; Autism accuracy 97.29%, precision 95.65%. The Gabor ablation reports 96.43% with filtering against 92.80% without." ] ] ;
