<https://doi.org/10.32604/cmes.2025.074627> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Artificial Intelligence (AI) is changing healthcare by helping with diagnosis. However, for doctors to trust AI tools, they need to be both accurate and easy to understand. In this study, we created a new machine learning system for the early detection of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) in children. Our main goal was to build a model that is not only good at predicting ASD but also clear in its reasoning. For this, we combined several different models, including Random Forest, XGBoost, and Neural Networks, into a single, more powerful framework. We used two different types of datasets: (i) a standard behavioral dataset and (ii) a more complex multimodal dataset with images, audio, and physiological information. The datasets were carefully preprocessed for missing values, redundant features, and dataset imbalance to ensure fair learning. The results outperformed the state-of-the-art with a Regularized Neural Network, achieving 97.6% accuracy on behavioral data. Whereas, on the multimodal data, the accuracy is 98.2%. Other models also did well with accuracies consistently above 96%. We also used SHAP and LIME on a behavioral dataset for models’ explainability." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Alshammeri M; Tariq N; Jhanji NZ; Humayun M; Khan MA (2026). Explainable Ensemble Learning Framework for Early Detection of Autism Spectrum Disorder: Enhancing Trust, Interpretability and Reliability in AI-Driven Healthcare. **. https://doi.org/10.32604/cmes.2025.074627" ;
    dcterms:creator "Alshammeri M",
        "Humayun M",
        "Jhanji NZ",
        "Khan MA",
        "Tariq N" ;
    dcterms:title "Explainable Ensemble Learning Framework for Early Detection of Autism Spectrum Disorder: Enhancing Trust, Interpretability and Reliability in AI-Driven Healthcare" ;
    bibo:doi "10.32604/cmes.2025.074627" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_1d856900" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2026 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data source and eligibility described" ;
                            slr:justification "Dataset 1 is cited to a prior study with class balance and missing-value counts tabulated; Dataset 2 has no source, citation, country or institution." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Sample size justified" ;
                            slr:justification "Dataset 2 rests on 100 original samples inflated to 400 by augmentation, with 4,220 features and a 40-sample test partition." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participants appropriate to the question" ;
                            slr:justification "Dataset 1 is a public download with no demographics reported; Dataset 2 contains no non-ASD comparison and no participant description whatsoever." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Predictors assessed blind to outcome" ;
                            slr:justification "Correlation filtering and ANOVA F-test selection follow the split in the published algorithm, but the partition on which the F-scores were computed is not stated." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Preprocessing applied without leakage" ;
                            slr:justification "SMOTE is explicitly confined to the training set and triggered only below a stated class-ratio threshold, with the paper naming leakage prevention as the reason - correct ordering that several comparable studies get wrong." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Predictors defined and measured appropriately" ;
                            slr:justification "Dataset 1's features include four autism instruments (AQ, SRS, Q-CHAT, CARS) used to predict an autism-traits label." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome definition and ascertainment" ;
                            slr:justification "No diagnostic procedure for either dataset; Dataset 2's severity classes have no named instrument behind them." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome determined independently of predictors" ;
                            slr:justification "Dataset 1's outcome is predicted from a feature set containing four instruments that determine it." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome matches the stated claim" ;
                            slr:justification "Dataset 2's 98.2% is four-class severity classification with no non-ASD class, reported in the abstract alongside a binary detection accuracy." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Analysis unit matches the clinical claim" ;
                            slr:justification "Dataset 2's unit is an augmented copy rather than a person; 400 rows derive from 100 originals." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance measures adequate" ;
                            slr:justification "No confidence intervals or fold variance; precision, recall and F1 omitted for both neural-network rows; the 63.28% majority-class baseline computable from the paper's own table is never stated." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Validation design guards against optimism" ;
                            slr:justification "Dataset 2's split and cross-validation are applied after fourfold augmentation, so transformed duplicates cross the partition." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Interpretability appropriately targeted" ;
                            slr:justification "SHAP and LIME applied only to the behavioural dataset, omitting the multimodal dataset where modality-level attribution would have been the informative result." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Limitations and overinterpretation" ;
                            slr:justification "No limitations section; a named limitation is assigned to each of roughly fifteen prior studies; machine learning is described as a suitable replacement for routine and expensive screening tests." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Twelve keywords including ethical AI and healthcare trustworthiness. The list is longer than any other in the corpus and names trust, interpretability, ethics and reliability - all values the paper's Dataset 2 handling does not exemplify." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-163" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Dataset 1 carries AQ, SRS, Q-CHAT and CARS as features rather than as reported participant characteristics - no mean, range or class-wise distribution appears for any of them. Dataset 2's severity categories are the prediction target with no underlying measure described." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-163" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No setting is described for either dataset. Dataset 1 is a public download; Dataset 2's 100 original clinical samples have no stated collection site, country, institution or period, which is difficult to reconcile with the claim that an Institutional Review Board granted approval for their collection." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-163" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No task or paradigm for either dataset. Dataset 2's modality descriptions imply that facial images, 10-30 second voice recordings, body-keypoint motion and wearable physiological signals were captured simultaneously with synchronisation errors below 100 ms, which requires a structured session protocol - none is described." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-163" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Five base learners (random forest, XGBoost, gradient boosting, SVM, logistic regression), soft-voting and stacking ensembles with a logistic-regression meta-learner, and two neural networks - a regularised MLP with L2, batch normalisation and dropout, and a deeper variant with early stopping and adaptive learning-rate scheduling. Predictions are combined by ensemble weighting proportional to validation performance." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-163" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Thin for Dataset 1 and absent for Dataset 2. Dataset 1 lists age, sex, ethnicity, jaundice and family history among its features and reports none of their distributions; seven comorbidity indicators are similarly listed as features with no prevalences. Dataset 2 reports no demographic information of any kind for its 100 clinical samples, despite claiming IRB approval and caregiver consent for their collection." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-163" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, precision, recall, F1 and ROC-AUC for eight configurations on Dataset 1, plus accuracy and F1 for six ablation configurations on both datasets. Three problems. Precision, recall and F1 are omitted for both neural-network rows, so the two models carrying the headline figures are reported on accuracy and AUC alone. No confidence interval or cross-validation variance accompanies any value. And the reported logistic-regression row - recall 100.00%, precision 87.20%, ROC-AUC 1.000 - is the signature of a model that separates the classes perfectly on the test partition, which on a dataset containing four autism instruments as features is expected rather than notable, and which the paper reads as displaying more false-positive tendencies. The 63.28% majority-class baseline is computable from the paper's own table and is never stated." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-163" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None for either dataset. Dataset 1's target is a binary ASD-traits field with no diagnostic procedure described, and the dataset's own feature list includes the Autism Spectrum Quotient, Social Responsiveness Scale, Q-CHAT score and Childhood Autism Rating Scale - four instruments used to determine autism status, supplied as predictors of it. This is the same instrument-inclusion leakage documented in local_183eaef4, which used the same 1,985-record dataset and reached accuracy 1.0000. Dataset 2's four severity categories are asserted with no instrument named; clinical experts are said to have verified annotation consistency at Cohen's kappa 0.92, but what they were rating and against what standard is not stated." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-163" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "ASD, autism spectrum disorder, children with ASD, ASD traits. The register is clinical and unremarkable; the framing is service-oriented, opening on the need for clinicians to trust AI tools and closing on healthcare trustworthiness. One sentence in the introduction overreaches in a way worth recording - that machine learning algorithms assess the likelihood of a child being diagnosed with autism from behavioural information, making them a suitable replacement for routine and expensive screening tests. No autistic or community perspective appears." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-163" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two datasets with very different standing. Dataset 1 is 1,985 instances with 28 attributes from public ASD screening databases - the same 1,985-record ASD Children Traits collection analysed by local_183eaef4 - and here, unlike there, its class balance is reported: 729 ASD-positive (36.72%) against 1,256 negative (63.28%), with 283 instances carrying missing values. Dataset 2 consists of 100 original clinical samples augmented fourfold to 400, distributed evenly across four severity categories with no non-ASD class at all, and with no source, citation, country or institution given for the original 100. The number of distinct children behind either dataset is never stated." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-163" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Dataset 1: screening-questionnaire responses and comorbidity flags, none of them examined individually. Dataset 2: the most behaviourally specific description in the paper - velocity, acceleration, jerk, movement range, coordination and repetitive behavioural patterns from body keypoints, alongside voice quality and autonomic arousal - describing exactly the kind of multimodal behavioural measurement this literature needs, for a dataset whose existence the paper does not evidence." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-163" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Dataset 2 is presented as genuine four-modality fusion - image, audio, motion and physiological features concatenated into a 4,220-dimensional vector - and the ablation attributes the final gain from 97.80% to 98.2% to multimodal integration. Three things make that uninterpretable. There are 4,220 features for 400 samples, of which only 100 are original. The 400 include augmented copies split across training and test, so the multimodal gain is measured on partly duplicated data. And no modality-level attribution is reported: SHAP and LIME are applied to Dataset 1 only, so the paper cannot say what any modality contributed. A 0.4-point accuracy gain on a 40-sample test set is a single case reclassified." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-163" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "SHAP and LIME are applied, and applied only to Dataset 1 - a choice the abstract states plainly. The consequence is that the paper's interpretability contribution covers the dataset where the answer is predetermined by the presence of AQ, SRS, Q-CHAT and CARS among the features, and omits the dataset where attribution would have been genuinely informative, namely which of four fused modalities carries the signal. No SHAP or LIME output is described in the extracted text." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-163" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Computer Modeling in Engineering & Sciences 146(1):41 (Tech Science Press), 55 days from submission to acceptance. A modelling and engineering venue; review did not surface an entirely unsourced clinical dataset carrying an IRB claim with no committee or institution, a split performed over augmented copies, a four-class severity result reported alongside a binary detection result, or four autism instruments sitting in the predictor set." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-163" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Not captured in the extracted text." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-163" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No limitations section appears in the extracted text. A comparison table instead assigns a named limitation to each of roughly fifteen prior studies (few participants, results may not apply widely; tested only in lab settings, not in daily life situations; works best only on similar data, not tested in real-world settings). None of that scrutiny is turned inward. Unmentioned: that four autism instruments sit in Dataset 1's feature set alongside the autism label; that Dataset 2's split partitions augmented copies rather than original samples; that Dataset 2's test partition holds 40 samples against 4,220 features; that Dataset 2 contains no non-ASD class, so its 98.2% is four-class severity rather than detection; that the 63.28% majority-class baseline on Dataset 1 is never stated; and that Dataset 2 has no provenance." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-163" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Dataset 1: a reduced tabular vector after correlation filtering above 0.90 and ANOVA F-test selection, drawn from 28 attributes that include the AQ, SRS, Q-CHAT and CARS scores alongside the ASD-traits target. Dataset 2: a 4,220-dimensional concatenation dominated by 4,040 image features, against 400 samples - a ratio of more than ten features per sample, and more than forty per original clinical sample." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-163" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The two datasets require separate verdicts. On Dataset 1 the pipeline ordering is right and is unusually well documented: a stratified 60:20:20 split precedes SMOTE, SMOTE is applied only to the training portion and only when the class ratio falls below 0.8, and the paper states this explicitly as preventing data leakage - the ordering that local_ed30e24c got wrong and that most of this branch does not discuss. What the ordering cannot repair is the feature set, which contains the Autism Spectrum Quotient, the Social Responsiveness Scale, the Q-CHAT score and the Childhood Autism Rating Scale as predictors of a binary ASD-traits label. The same dataset produced accuracy 1.0000 for local_183eaef4; here it produces 97.60%, and the difference is attributable to preprocessing choices rather than to anything about autism. Dataset 2 is worse in kind. Its 100 original clinical samples were augmented fourfold to 400, and the stratified 80/10/10 split and 5-fold cross-validation were applied to the augmented set - so rotated, pitch-shifted, coordinate-scaled and noise-added copies of the same clinical sample sit on both sides of the split. The test partition therefore holds 40 samples, of which perhaps ten correspond to distinct originals, evaluated against a 4,220-dimensional feature vector. The reported 98.2% is roughly 39 of 40 correct on data the model has seen transformed versions of. It is also not a detection result: Dataset 2 contains four severity classes and no non-ASD class, so the abstract's presentation of 98.2% accuracy alongside the behavioural detection figure conflates a four-way severity task with binary ASD identification. Neither dataset carries a confidence interval, and the 63.28% majority-class baseline available in the paper's own Table 2 is never used." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-163" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "For Dataset 2, vision-based pose estimation and wearable sensors are named without models; ResNet50, VGG16 and MobileNetV2 supply image features at 224x224 with ImageNet normalisation; audio features include MFCC, spectral, temporal, rhythmic and voice-quality measures such as jitter, shimmer and harmonics-to-noise ratio. No software, version, seed or compute environment is given anywhere." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-163" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The stated goal is an ensemble framework that is both accurate and interpretable enough for clinicians to trust, evaluated on a behavioural dataset and a multimodal one. Headline findings: 97.6% accuracy on behavioural data from a regularised neural network (ROC-AUC 0.998) and 98.2% on multimodal data, with all other models above 93%; and an ablation showing accuracy rising from 89.40% (no feature selection, no resampling) through feature selection, SMOTE and ensemble fusion to 97.60% with neural-network regularisation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-163" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2026, published 29 January. The framing is trust-centred - the stated goal is a model that is not only good at predicting ASD but also clear in its reasoning, with the title promising to enhance trust, interpretability and reliability in AI-driven healthcare. Against that, the paper reports a 98.2% figure from a dataset it does not source, whose split partitions augmented duplicates, and whose task is not the one the abstract implies; it applies its interpretability methods only to the dataset where they are least informative; and it reproduces the branch's characteristic overreach in describing machine learning as a suitable replacement for routine and expensive screening tests. Its genuine methodological contribution - explicitly confining SMOTE to the training partition and publishing the whole pipeline as an algorithm - is real and is the part of the paper most worth borrowing." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-163" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Sharply divided between the two datasets. Dataset 1's pipeline is one of the better-documented in this branch: the whole sequence is published as a numbered algorithm specifying label encoding, z-score normalisation, the 0.90 correlation-filtering threshold and its retention rule, the 60:20:20 stratified split, the exact SMOTE trigger condition (minority-to-majority ratio below 0.8) with the interpolation formula, the ANOVA F-score expression, and the SHAP and LIME definitions - and the dataset's instance count, feature count, missing-value count and class balance are all tabulated. Dataset 2 is unreproducible in principle: no source, citation, country, institution, ethics committee, participant description or access route exists for the 100 original samples, and its characteristics are conveyed entirely through asserted quality figures (complete data availability, synchronisation below 100 ms, inter-rater kappa 0.92). Neither dataset is accompanied by code, seeds, base-learner hyperparameters or a stated fold count." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-163" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional. Ethics is claimed only for Dataset 2, in a short paragraph stating that all participant data were anonymised, written consent was obtained from caregivers, and IRB approval was granted for data collection and analysis, with secure storage, encryption and restricted access. No committee, protocol number, institution or country accompanies any of this, and no ethics statement covers Dataset 1 at all. Given that Dataset 2 is otherwise entirely unsourced, the ethics paragraph is the only evidence offered that its 100 clinical samples exist as described." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-163" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Eight model configurations reported side by side on Dataset 1, plus an ablation adding one pipeline component at a time, plus a state-of-the-art comparison table with named limitations for each prior " ;
                    slr:study_design "Retrospective analysis of two datasets under a common pipeline: a public behavioural screening dataset (binary ASD traits) and a smaller multimodal dataset (four severity classes),…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: Dataset 1: 729 ASD-positive instances (36.72%). Dataset 2 has no non-ASD class - all 400 samples are distributed across four severity categories.; comparison: Dataset 1: 1,256 ASD-negative instances (63.28%), which sets the majority-class baseline at 63.28%. Dataset 2: none." ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Dataset 1: regularised neural network 97.60% accuracy (ROC-AUC 0.998); voting classifier 96.90%; SVM, logistic regression and stacking 96.10%; random forest and gradient boosting 94.50%; XGBoost 93.70%. Logistic regression reports recall 100.00% with precision 87.20% and ROC-AUC 1.000. Dataset 2 multimodal: 98.2%. Ablation on Dataset 1: 89.40% baseline, 92.80% with feature selection, 94.60% with S" ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy, precision, recall, F1 and ROC-AUC per model on Dataset 1; accuracy and F1 for each ablation configuration on both datasets. No confidence intervals, no cross-validation variance, and precision, recall and F1 are omitted for the two neural network rows." ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Dataset 1: regularised neural network 97.60% accuracy (ROC-AUC 0.998); voting classifier 96.90%; SVM, logistic regression and stacking 96.10%; random forest and gradient boosting 94.50%; XGBoost 93.70%. Logistic regression reports recall 100.00% with precision 87.20% and ROC-AUC 1.000. Dataset 2 multimodal: 98.2%. Ablation on Dataset 1: 89.40% baseline, 92.80% with feature selection, 94.60% with S" ] ] ;
