            slr:overall_judgment "high" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<https://doi.org/10.3389/frobt.2019.00132> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "PERSPECTIVE published: 29 November 2019 doi: 10.3389/frobt.2019.00132 Frontiers in Robotics and AI | www.frontiersin.org 1 November 2019 | Volume 6 | Article 132 Edited by: Cigdem Beyan, Italian Institute of Technology (IIT), Italy Reviewed by: Lori-Ann Rosalind Sacrey, University of Alberta, Canada Concetto Spampinato, University of Catania, Italy *Correspondence: Alexandra Livia Georgescu alexandra.georgescu@kcl.ac.uk Jana Christina Koehler jana.koehler@med.uni-muenchen.de †These authors have contributed equally to this work Specialty section: This article was submitted to Human-Robot Interaction, a section of the journal Frontiers in Robotics and AI Received: 03 March 2019 Accepted: 13 November 2019 Published: 29 November 2019 Citation: Georgescu AL, Koehler JC, Weiske J, Vogeley K, Koutsouleris N and Falter-Wagner C (2019) Machine Learning to Study Social Interaction Difﬁculties in ASD. Front. Robot. AI 6:132. doi: 10.3389/frobt.2019.00132 Machine Learning to Study Social Interaction Difﬁculties in ASD Alexandra Livia Georgescu 1,2*†, Jana Christina Koehler 3*†, Johanna Weiske 3, Kai Vogeley 2,4, Nikolaos Koutsouleris 3 and Christine Falter-Wagner 3,5 1 Department of Psychology, Institute of Psychiatry, Psychology and Neuroscience, King’s College London, London, United Kingdom, 2 Department of Psychiatry and Psychotherapy, University Hospital of Cologne, Cologne, Germany, 3 Department of Psychiatry and Psychotherapy, Medical Faculty, LMU Munich, Munich, Germany, 4 Institute of Neuroscience and Medicine, Cognitive Neuroscience (INM-3), Research Center Juelich, Jülich, Germany, 5 Institute of Medical Psychology, Medical Faculty, LMU Munich, Munich, Germany Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) is a spectrum of neurodevelopmental conditions characterized by difﬁculties in social communication and social interaction as well as repetitive behaviors and restricted interests. Prevalence rates have been rising, and existing diagnostic methods are both extremely time and labor consuming. There is an urgent need for more economic and objective automatized diagnostic tools that are independent of language and experience of the diagnostician and that can help deal with the complexity of the autistic phenotype. Technological advancements in machine learning are offering a potential solution, and several studies have employed computational approaches to classify ASD based on phenomenological, behavioral or neuroimaging data. Despite of being at the core of ASD diagnosis and having the potential to be used as a behavioral marker for machine learning algorithms, only recently have movement parameters been used as features in machine learning classiﬁcation approaches. In a proof-of-principle analysis of data from a social interaction study we trained a classiﬁcation algorithm on intrapersonal synchrony as an automatically and objectively measured phenotypic feature from 29 autistic and 29 typically developed individuals to differentiate those individuals with ASD from those without ASD. Parameters included nonverbal motion energy values from 116 videos of social interactions. As opposed to previous studies to date, our classiﬁcation approach has been applied to non-verbal behavior objectively captured during naturalistic and complex interactions with a real human interaction partner assuring high external validity. A machine learning approach lends itself particularly for capturing heterogeneous and complex behavior in real social interactions and will be essential in developing automatized and objective classiﬁcation methods in ASD." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Georgescu AL; Koehler JC; Weiske J; Vogeley K; Koutsouleris N; Falter-Wagner C (2019). Machine Learning to Study Social Interaction Difficulties in ASD. *Frontiers in Robotics and AI, 6, Article 132*. https://doi.org/10.3389/frobt.2019.00132" ;
    dcterms:creator "Falter-Wagner C",
        "Georgescu AL",
        "Koehler JC",
        "Koutsouleris N",
        "Vogeley K",
        "Weiske J" ;
    dcterms:title "Machine Learning to Study Social Interaction Difficulties in ASD" ;
    bibo:doi "10.3389/frobt.2019.00132" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_ba5fbda7" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2019 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Recruitment sources and diagnostic route are given, but no ages, sexes or demographic characteristics appear anywhere; these are deferred to a companion manuscript under revision." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "58 high-functioning adults, described by the authors themselves as a 'rather limited sample size'." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched?" ;
                            slr:justification "No matching procedure or comparability testing is reported in this paper." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "Motion energy is computed automatically, but regions of interest are drawn manually - a subjective component the authors acknowledge explicitly." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "Two medical specialists independently confirmed diagnosis under ICD-10 with supplementary neuropsychological examination." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Camera model and geometry, lighting, seating distance, task structure, region-of-interest definitions and the full MEA and cross-correlation parameterisation are specified." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the elicitation ecologically valid?" ;
                            slr:justification "Unscripted dyadic interaction with a real human partner, chosen deliberately over the staged movement tasks the authors criticise in prior work." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the error profile reported transparently?" ;
                            slr:justification "Raw true/false positive and negative counts published alongside sensitivity and specificity, exposing that 13 of 29 controls were misclassified." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is nested cross-validation used with parameter optimisation separated from evaluation?" ;
                            slr:justification "Repeated nested k-fold with 10x5 outer and 5x5 inner cycles, optimisation confined to CV1 and evaluation to CV2." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is there external or independent validation?" ;
                            slr:justification "None; explicitly named as the immediate next step." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is dimensionality reduction fitted inside the validation loop?" ;
                            slr:justification "PCA retaining 80% cumulative variance is applied within each CV1 fold." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample size adequate for the feature dimensionality?" ;
                            slr:justification "4016 features against 58 participants, mitigated by PCA but still extreme." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Sharing is explicitly refused with a stated reason - identifiable patient data from a sample that did not consent to sharing - the most careful such statement in the corpus." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "None, though the named open-source tools rMEA and NeuroMiner and the full parameterisation support re-implementation." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are field-level methodological problems identified?" ;
                            slr:justification "Circularity in clinician-derived features, over-optimism from simple cross-validation, and instability from leave-one-out are each named and argued, with best-practice standards codified in a box." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics, consent and compensation reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Written informed consent under the Declaration of Helsinki, named ethics committee approval, 50 Euro compensation and debriefing all reported." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are results reported without inflation?" ;
                            slr:justification "75.9% presented as 'a conservative estimate on the basis of a state-of-the art ML approach', with poor specificity shown rather than concealed." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: proof-of-principle demonstration that full-body non-verbal behaviour from naturalistic interaction can classify autistic adults, alongside a methodological argument for cross-validation standards. Main findings: 75.9% accuracy, 96.6% sensitivity, 55.2% specificity, AUC 0.71, with 28/29 autistic participants correctly identified but 13/29 controls misclassified. The authors present 75.9% as a deliberately conservative estimate produced by a rigorous validation procedure." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-027" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Single modality in this analysis. The parent study measured interpersonal coordination between dyad members, but this classification uses intrapersonal synchrony only. Features from four separate interaction tasks are aggregated per participant, a form of fusion across contexts. The future-work section explicitly proposes multimodal fusion with eye-gaze, facial expression and clinical questionnaire data, and Box 1 lists 'combination of objective variables and standard diagnostic measures as input features' as a minimum requirement for reliable clinical application." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-027" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Automatically computed high-dimensional synchrony representation: 1004 lagged cross-correlations per window between head and body motion energy time series, z-standardised and aggregated across four interaction conditions into 4016 features per participant, then projected onto principal components explaining 80% of variance within each inner fold. No handcrafted kinematic parameters and no manual behavioural coding." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-027" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Laboratory setting with standardised lighting and fixed camera and seating geometry, but with naturalistic unscripted conversation content. The data come from an existing study on interpersonal coordination in dyadic interactions by the same group, so this is a secondary analysis for classification purposes." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-027" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Supervised learning: linear-kernel support vector machine, with PCA used unsupervised for dimensionality reduction. A single algorithm with no best-of-many comparison, consistent with the paper's methodological conservatism." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-027" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Extensive neuropsychological examination is stated to have supplemented the diagnostic interviews for the autistic group, but no scores are reported and none are used in analysis. No IQ, language or adaptive measures for either group, no autism severity instrument, and no comorbidity information." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-027" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Frontiers in Robotics and AI, in the Human-Robot Interaction specialty section, published as a Perspective article - a technology venue, though the author list is dominated by psychiatry departments and the content is clinical-methodological rather than robotic." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-027" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Apply the trained algorithm to a completely new and larger dataset, and test transdiagnostic specificity across different psychiatric disturbances; combine multiple non-verbal communication parameters with clinical data such as questionnaires to improve prediction and detect cross-domain associations; extend to eye-gaze and facial expression; and move toward fully automated computer-vision pose and gaze estimation to remove the manual region-of-interest step - while cautioning that such tools' validity against standard motion capture is not yet demonstrated and that they vary in susceptibility to tracking failure and in the video types they support." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-027" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The methodological centrepiece. The paper argues explicitly that most autism machine-learning studies rely on simple cross-validation, which 'increases the likelihood of choosing an overly optimistic model', and that leave-one-out validation - used by several studies it names - 'creates overly variable test sets, rendering model outcomes unstable' given autism's heterogeneity. It implements repeated nested k-fold cross-validation instead: 10 outer folds with five permutations, a 5-by-5 inner cycle, participants shuffled before each fold definition, parameter optimisation confined to the inner cycle and performance evaluation confined to the outer, with PCA fitted inside each inner fold. It then presents 75.9% as a conservative estimate obtained under this regime, and codifies the standards in a Box listing minimum requirements. What remains absent: external validation on an independent sample (named as the immediate next step), transdiagnostic specificity testing, and any deployment analysis." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-027" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'autistic individuals', 'individuals with ASD', 'autistic adults', 'patients', 'typically developed (TD) individuals', 'TD adults'. Mixed identity-first and person-first, with 'typically developed' rather than 'normal' or 'healthy'. The paper also refers to autism as 'an umbrella term for neurodevelopmental conditions' and uses 'conditions' alongside 'disorder'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-027" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Diagnosis confirmed independently by two medical specialists in clinical interviews according to ICD-10 criteria, supplemented by extensive neuropsychological examination, with the sample restricted to F84.0 high-functioning autism and F84.5 Asperger syndrome. Independent double confirmation by two specialists is stronger than most corpus studies, though no standardised observational instrument is named. Control status is asserted with no screening instrument reported." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-027" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, AUC and raw true/false positive and negative counts, plus a decision-score plot showing each participant's position relative to the decision boundary. Reporting the raw counts (28/16/13/1) is what makes the error profile legible - the model's 75.9% accuracy comes with 96.6% sensitivity but only 55.2% specificity, meaning 13 of 29 controls were called autistic. No confidence intervals." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-027" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Data sharing is refused on explicit consent grounds and no code is released, but the analysis is described in unusual procedural detail: the MEA pipeline and its source, the region-of-interest definitions, the cross-correlation window length (60 s), lag range (plus or minus 5 s) and step size (0.04 s), the non-overlapping window rule, the resulting feature counts (1004 lagged cross-correlations, 4016 features per participant), the PCA variance threshold (80%) and its placement inside the inner fold, the feature scaling range, the full nested cross-validation structure (10 folds x 5 permutations outer, 5-by-5 inner), and the eight C values searched. The named open-source tools (rMEA, NeuroMiner) further support re-implementation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-027" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Motor behavior: whole-body non-verbal movement captured as frame-differencing motion energy in head and body regions, converted into intrapersonal synchrony - the temporal coordination between a person's own head and body movement. This is a distinctive construct in the corpus: not movement quantity, speed or trajectory, but the internal temporal coupling of one's own body segments during social interaction." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-027" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None from the model - no feature importance, SVM weight inspection or ablation is reported, which is a real gap given 4016 features reduced by PCA to an uninterpretable subspace. The only interpretive output is the per-participant decision-score plot, which shows the distribution of confidence and highlights that only one autistic participant fell on the wrong side of the boundary. Substantive behavioural interpretation is deferred to the companion manuscript." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-027" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups of 29: high-functioning autistic adults (ICD-10 F84.0 or F84.5) and typically developed adults. Dyads were composed in three configurations - two typically developed, two autistic, or mixed - which is a design feature no other corpus study uses, since it means each participant's behaviour is measured while interacting with partners of both diagnostic statuses. No other-diagnosis group, though transdiagnostic specificity testing is named as required future work." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-027" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Author keywords: autism spectrum disorder, machine learning, nonverbal synchrony, support vector machine, motion energy analysis, classification, intrapersonal synchrony, nested cross-validation. Including 'nested cross-validation' as a keyword is itself notable and signals the paper's methodological intent. Task vocabulary: 'behavioral marker', 'automatized and objective classification', 'external validity', 'predictive psychiatry', 'transdiagnostic specificity'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-027" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Reported by the authors: the sample size is 'rather limited', so the next step must be applying the algorithm to a completely new and larger dataset; transdiagnostic specificity across psychiatric conditions is untested; and Motion Energy Analysis retains 'a subjective component' because regions of interest are drawn manually, even though pixel-change detection is automatic. The paper also criticises the wider literature for circularity where classifiers are trained on clinician-derived features. Identifiable in review: no participant ages, sexes or matching information are reported in this paper at all; specificity of 55.2% means the model misclassifies nearly half of controls, so the headline accuracy rests on an unbalanced error profile; the dyadic design means each participant's synchrony was measured while interacting with partners of differing diagnostic status, a potential source of variance that is not analysed; one entire task was dropped because a single dyad misunderstood instructions; and the sample is high-functioning adults only." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-027" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; four analysed 5-minute interactions per dyad in a single session with no follow-up. The data are video of identifiable adults in extended conversation - among the most sensitive in the corpus. Handling is the most careful encountered: written informed consent under the Declaration of Helsinki, local ethics committee approval, 50 Euro compensation, debriefing, and an explicit data availability statement refusing sharing because the data are 'identifiable patient data from a sample that did not consent to their data being shared in any form'. That is a substantive privacy-protective decision rather than an unexplained absence." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-027" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2019. The paper provides one of the corpus's most explicit critical surveys of its own field. It notes that early machine-learning work in autism aimed at shortening ADOS and ADI-R administration by item reduction, 'yielding a classification accuracy of autism vs. typically-developing individuals of up to 99.9%', but warns that in such studies 'the features utilized for classification are still highly subjective and not independent of the respective clinician who bases the diagnostic decision on just those features (circularity)'. It catalogues the kinematic strand (96.7%, 92.5%, 93% from staged movement tasks) and observes that 'the movements under investigation were staged, thus, highly unnatural', arguing for a move to naturalistic interaction. And it identifies simple cross-validation and leave-one-out as sources of unstable and optimistic estimates. Together these amount to a diagnosis of three distinct methodological problems in the 2015-2019 literature that this corpus documents." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-027" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Five 5-minute dyadic social interaction tasks - an ice-breaker, two debating tasks, a meal-planning task and a roleplay - with dyads composed as TD-TD, ASD-ASD or mixed. The ice-breaker task was abandoned entirely because one mixed dyad did not understand the instructions, reducing 145 videos to 116. Recording used a fixed high-definition camera 320 cm from chairs placed 60 cm apart under standardised artificial lighting. Two regions of interest per participant (head; body including legs) were drawn manually, and grayscale change within them produced two continuous movement time series per person." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-027" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Hardware: Panasonic DV C Pro HD P2 high-definition video camera on a tripod at a fixed 320 cm distance, chairs 60 cm apart, standardised artificial lighting. Software: Motion Energy Analysis following the Ramseyer and Tschacher pipeline; the rMEA R package for smoothing, scaling and lagged cross-correlation; NeuroMiner for PCA, SVM training and nested cross-validation. Notably no wearable sensors are used, which the authors flag as an advantage given hypersensitivity in autism." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-027" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Only the group sizes are reported in this paper: 29 autistic and 29 typically developed adults. No ages, no age ranges, no standard deviations, no sex distribution, and no matching information or comparability testing - all deferred to a companion manuscript described as under revision. For a Perspective article this is understandable, but it means the classification result cannot be assessed for demographic confounding from this paper alone. Adults only, so no early-diagnosis relevance." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-027" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Five 5-minute social interaction tasks per dyad, of which four were analysed after one task was abandoned; 116 videos entered final analysis from an original 145" ;
                    slr:study_design "Perspective article containing a cross-sectional proof-of-principle classification analysis of data from an existing social interaction study" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "High-functioning autism and Asperger syndrome, adults" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 29; comparison: 29" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Accuracy 75.9%, sensitivity 96.6%, specificity 55.2%, AUC 0.71, with 28 true positives, 16 true negatives, 13 false positives and 1 false negative. The classifier correctly identified all but one autistic participant but misclassified 13 of 29 controls",
                        "Intrapersonal motion synchrony - the temporal coordination between a person's own head and body movement - extracted automatically from video of naturalistic conversation classified autistic from typically developed adults at 75.9% accuracy under repeated nested cross-validation. Sensitivity was very high (96.6%, all but one autistic participant identified) but specificity poor (55.2%), so the mod" ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, AUC, and raw true/false positive and negative counts, plus a per-participant decision-score plot" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Accuracy 75.9%, sensitivity 96.6%, specificity 55.2%, AUC 0.71, with 28 true positives, 16 true negatives, 13 false positives and 1 false negative. The classifier correctly identified all but one autistic participant but misclassified 13 of 29 controls; Intrapersonal motion synchrony - the temporal coordination between a person's own head and body movement - extracted automatically from video of naturalistic conversation classified autistic from typically developed adults at 75.9% accuracy under repeated nested cross-validation. Sensitivity was very high (96.6%, all but one autistic participant identified) but specificity poor (55.2%), so the mod" ] ] ;
