                    slr:supporting_quote "No per-group age or sex data are reported, so it cannot be established whether the ASD and non-ASD groups are comparable on variables that strongly affect gaze; recruitment differs systematically by group (specialty clinics versus mainstream schools) with no matching." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "high" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<urn:slr:article:34584d91747b> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Autism is a behavioral neurological disorder affecting a signiﬁcant percentage of worldwide population. It especially starts manifesting at very low ages, but it is difﬁcult to early diagnose it since there is not a speciﬁc exam or trial that is able to spot it safely. Its detection is in fact mainly dependent from the medical expertise used to assess the patient behavior during direct interviews. This work aims at providing an automatic objective support to the doctor for the assessment of (early) diagnosis of possible autistic subjects by only using video sequences. The underlying idea and rationale come from the psychological and neuroscience studies claiming that the executions of simple motor acts are different between pathological and healthy subjects, and this can be sufﬁcient to discriminate between them. To this end, we devised an experiment in which we recorded, using a standard video camera, patient and healthy children performing the same simple gesture of grasping a bottle. By only processing the video clips depicting the grasping action using a recurrent deep neural network, we are able to discriminate with a good accuracy between the 2 classes of subjects. The designed deep model is also able to provide a sort of attention map in which the zones in the video of major interest are identiﬁed in space and time: this “explains” in a certain way which areas the model deems more relevant to the classiﬁcation purpose, which could also be used by the doctor to make the diagnosis. In the end, this work constitutes a ﬁrst step towards the development of an automatic computational system devoted to the early diagnosis of autistic subjects, providing the medical expert of a supportive objective method, potentially simple to use in clinical and also more open settings. I. INTRODUCTION Autism Spectrum Disorders (ASD) represent a heterogeneous set of neurodevelopmental disorders characterized by deﬁcits in social communication and reciprocal interactions as well as stereotypic behaviors. The prevalence of ASD has been increasing over the past two decades, with a global population prevalence of about 1% worldwide. Converging evidence indicates that early diagnosis followed by intensive intervention is paramount in order to optimize the outcome of the therapy, especially for children. Yet, diagnosis of ASD remains a complex problem. Typically, it relies on specialist medical expertise with diagnostic instrumentation that depends on interpretative coding of child observations, parent interviews, and manual testing. Recently, advanced computational and engineering methodologies have been employed to meet the needs of psychology and psychiatry applications [1]. In particular, computer vision (CV) and machine learning (ML) methods have demonstrated quite a success in several areas such as affective computing [2], medical imaging [3], and bioinformatics [4]. Actually, there is a clear aid that CV and ML techniques can provide also in the study o" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Zunino A; Morerio P; Cavallo A; Ansuini C; Podda J; Battaglia F; Veneselli E; Becchio C; Murino V (2018). Video Gesture Analysis for Autism Spectrum Disorder Detection. *2018 24th International C*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Ansuini C",
        "Battaglia F",
        "Becchio C",
        "Cavallo A",
        "Morerio P",
        "Murino V",
        "Podda J",
        "Veneselli E",
        "Zunino A" ;
    dcterms:title "Video Gesture Analysis for Autism Spectrum Disorder Detection" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_d8dc996a" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2018 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "Age (p=0.510), IQ (p=0.193), gender and executive function (p=0.999) all matched with tests reported." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Recruitment sites, group composition, age, gender, IQ, executive function, handedness and exclusion criteria all reported with statistics." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "39 analysed children, restricted to ASD without intellectual impairment and IQ-matched to controls." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "DSM-5 with ADOS-2 and ADI-R administered by two experienced professionals." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are incidental cues controlled?" ;
                            slr:justification "Gaussian smoothing applied specifically to remove arm and hand appearance so the model must rely on kinematics." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "Features are learned end-to-end from video by a pre-trained CNN and an LSTM, with no manual coding." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Table and chair dimensions, object dimensions and weights, start posture, distances, trial counts, block randomisation, camera resolution and frame rate, and the video trimming rule are all specified." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample size adequate for a deep model?" ;
                            slr:justification "39 subjects and 1837 clips for an LSTM over CNN features; the authors themselves note deep methods require large training data." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a meaningful baseline included?" ;
                            slr:justification "Dense trajectories with HOG/HOF and VLAD encoding, reported as performing at random chance." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the validation scheme subject-independent?" ;
                            slr:justification "Leave-one-subject-out with 39 separately trained models, explicitly chosen over cross-validation as the harder and more realistic protocol." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is there external or independent validation?" ;
                            slr:justification "None; single site, single camera viewpoint." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is decision confidence analysed?" ;
                            slr:justification "Detection accuracy plotted across confidence thresholds from 0.5 to 0.95, with the model declared untrustworthy above 0.9." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics, consent and privacy handling reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Named ethics committee, Helsinki compliance and written parental consent after detailed description; no anonymization or data-protection statement accompanying the public video release." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are limitations discussed?" ;
                            slr:justification "No limitations section; heterogeneity is presented as a substantive finding rather than as a constraint on the claim." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code or an implementation available?" ;
                            slr:justification "No code, no training hyperparameters, no seeds." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are per-subject results reported rather than only aggregates?" ;
                            slr:justification "A full per-subject accuracy table across four conditions and full data, revealing several confidently misclassified individuals." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "The video dataset is publicly released at a stated URL - the only released video dataset in this corpus - though licence and access terms are not described." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Supervised deep learning: a pre-trained GoogLeNet CNN as frame feature extractor feeding a recurrent LSTM with a learned soft spatial attention mechanism and a binary softmax classifier. A handcrafted alternative (dense trajectories with HOG/HOF and VLAD encoding) is included specifically as a baseline and is reported to perform at chance - an informative negative comparison that most corpus studies omit." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-016" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "ASD n=20 (18 male, 2 female), age 9.8 +/- 1.5 years, WISC-IV Full Scale IQ 98.5 +/- 11.1. TD n=20 (16 male, 4 female), age 9.5 +/- 1.5 years, Full Scale IQ 102.8 +/- 9.4. Matching is verified statistically on both age (t38 = -0.665, p = 0.510) and IQ (t38 = 1.325, p = 0.193), and gender is matched by design. This is among the best-matched samples in the corpus - age, gender and IQ all matched with tests reported - though both groups are male-dominated (90% and 80%) and the ASD group is restricted to children without intellectual impairment." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-016" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: automatic objective diagnostic support from video alone, framed explicitly as a first step toward a computer-aided tool usable in clinical and more open settings. Main findings: 0.82 detection accuracy (32/39 subjects) with full-data training, 0.72 (28/39) when training per intention condition; mean per-subject accuracy 0.75 +/- 0.22; ASD recall 78.95%, TD recall 85.00%; a dense-trajectories handcrafted baseline performed at chance; attention maps localised to hand and arm; per-subject variability was large and is interpreted as evidence of spectrum heterogeneity." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-016" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2018. The paper surveys machine learning applied to autism across quotient-based diagnostic tools, MRI, kinematic data, eye tracking and language acoustics, and argues that these modalities are 'quite invasive or require specialized personnel, often affecting the behavior of the subjects under study', and that most rely on 'handcrafted features and simplified linear models that often fail to handle the complexity of human behaviors in less controlled settings'. It positions itself as the first to cast autism diagnosis as a video-based computational problem and as part of the shift toward deep learning - a shift this corpus documents accelerating sharply from 2018 onward." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-016" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups of 20: children with ASD without accompanying intellectual impairment (18 male), and typically developing children (16 male). One ASD subject (subject 3) was discarded because all their non-corrupted videos came from a single condition, so 39 subjects enter the experiments. No other-diagnosis comparison group." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-016" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "There is no limitations section, so nothing is formally acknowledged, though the authors do report the difficulty candidly in the results - the large per-subject standard deviation is presented as evidence that 'finding a very general pattern in the video, that could be unique in distinguishing autistic subjects, is a challenging task', and TD subjects being classified better than ASD ones is read as 'evidence of the fact that autistic spectrum disorders comprise indeed a very wide spectrum of variability'. Identifiable in review: n=39 makes 0.82 equal to 32 of 39 children; the ASD group excludes intellectual impairment and is IQ-matched to controls, so results cannot extend to the broader spectrum; both groups are 80-90% male; the per-subject table shows several ASD children classified confidently wrong (subjects 7, 17 and 20 at 0.15, 0.16 and 0.31), which a single aggregate figure would conceal; the 0.5 threshold on averaged frame probabilities is a lenient decision rule and performance collapses below chance above a 0.9 threshold; and single-site recruitment with one fixed camera viewpoint leaves transfer entirely untested." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-016" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "DSM-5 criteria, with both ADOS-2 and ADI-R administered by two experienced professionals. This is the most complete diagnostic ascertainment encountered in the corpus to date - the two instruments that the literature identifies as having the highest sensitivity and specificity, both applied, by two independent experienced assessors." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-016" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Motor behavior captured as raw video: the kinematics of a reach-to-grasp movement, with visual appearance deliberately degraded by Gaussian smoothing so that only movement information remains available to the model. No markers, no motion capture, no handcrafted kinematic parameters - the paper positions this freedom from 'invasive sensory devices (e.g., MRI, microphones, eye tracker, Motion Capture landmarks)' as its central practical advantage." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-016" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; a single ~30-minute session per child with no follow-up. Video of identifiable children is inherently sensitive, though the analysis pipeline applies Gaussian smoothing to suppress appearance and the released clips show only a lateral view of a reaching arm. Ethics approval from the local committee (ASL3 Genovese) under the Helsinki Declaration is reported, and parents gave written informed consent after a detailed study description. No anonymization or data-protection statement accompanies the public dataset release, which is a gap given that the dataset is posted openly." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-016" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A learned soft spatial attention map, generated per frame by the LSTM from its hidden spatio-temporal state and visualised superimposed on the video, is the interpretability mechanism - and it is designed into the model rather than applied post hoc. The maps concentrate on the hand and arm during reaching, which the authors read as the network attending to gesture kinematics, and they propose the maps as feedback clinicians could use in interpreting a diagnosis. The interpretation stops at localisation: no kinematic property (velocity, jerk, trajectory shape) is extracted or named, so the explanation identifies where the model looks but not what it sees there." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-016" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Mixed but above corpus average. The dataset is publicly released at a stated URL - the only video dataset released in this corpus - and the experimental protocol is specified in exhaustive physical detail (table dimensions, object weights and positions, start posture, trial counts, block structure, camera settings, trimming rule). The model architecture is described concretely (GoogLeNet last conv layer, 7x7x1024 features, 7x7 attention map, 128-dim LSTM hidden state, 16-dim FC layer, 15-frame clips). What is missing: no code release, no training hyperparameters (learning rate, optimiser, epochs, regularisation), no random seeds, and no statement of how the model handles the 15-frame clip splitting at test time beyond averaging." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-016" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The 24th International Conference on Pattern Recognition (ICPR) 2018, Beijing - a technology/computational venue (pattern recognition and computer vision), not an autism or clinical venue, though the author list spans a computer vision institute, a cognition and motion neuroscience group, a university psychology department and a hospital child neuropsychiatry unit." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-016" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Hardware: Vicon VUE video camera at 1280x720 and 100 frames per second, lateral viewpoint; height-adjustable chair and a table of specified dimensions; a plastic bottle, box and glass of specified dimensions. Software: pre-trained GoogLeNet for CNN features; a custom LSTM with soft spatial attention; dense trajectories with HOG and HOF descriptors and VLAD encoding for the baseline. Deep-learning framework and training hyperparameters are not named." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-016" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A highly standardised reach-to-grasp protocol: fixed start posture with pincer grip on a marked point, a water-filled bottle of specified dimensions at 44 cm from the child's midline, and four intention conditions (grasp-to-place, grasp-to-pour, pass-to-place, pass-to-pour) with target objects at specified distances. 12 consecutive grasps per condition (48 total), initiated at natural speed after an auditory tone, with 2 practice trials before each block, pseudo-randomised block order, and a 2-minute pause between blocks to avoid fatigue. Crucially, video is trimmed at the moment of contact, so only the pre-grasp reaching phase is analysed - testing the neuroscientific claim that intention is encoded in the earliest stages of a motor act." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-016" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Exploit the intention labels already in the dataset to test whether intention classification itself is altered in ASD, which could provide an additional diagnostic cue; and refine the spatial attention model to more finely inspect the kinematic discriminants in the gestures. Both are concrete and grounded in the existing data rather than generic calls for larger samples." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-016" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Learned deep embeddings over raw video, with no handcrafted features at all: 7x7x1024 convolutional feature maps per frame from GoogLeNet, adaptively pooled by a learned 7x7 attention map into a 1024-dimensional vector, then consumed recursively by a 128-dimensional LSTM. Videos are split into 15-frame clips for training, with each clip treated independently and probabilities averaged across frames at test time. This is one of the few genuinely end-to-end representations in the corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-016" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Robustness is handled unusually seriously. Leave-one-subject-out with 39 independently trained models is used deliberately, with the authors stating that it is 'more challenging than the usual cross-validation whose classification scores are always higher' and that they consider it the correct procedure for a system meant to generalise. Appearance information is removed by Gaussian smoothing so the model cannot exploit incidental visual differences. A handcrafted state-of-the-art baseline is run and reported to fail at chance. A confidence-threshold sensitivity analysis shows detection accuracy degrading from 0.82 to 0.67 between thresholds 0.5 and 0.7 and dropping below chance above 0.9, with the authors stating plainly that the model 'is not more trustable after a threshold of 0.9'. What is absent: any external or independent dataset, any held-out site or camera viewpoint, any class-imbalance issue (19/20), and any real-time or deployment measurement despite clinical use being the stated aim." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-016" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Laboratory setting - a quiet room with fixed table and chair geometry and a standardised start posture - with clinical recruitment from a hospital child neuropsychiatry unit and control recruitment from primary schools. Newly collected data, released as the first public video dataset of ASD/TD gestures." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-016" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Full Scale IQ measured by WISC-IV for both groups and used as a matching variable; executive function assessed in both groups by the Tower of London test with no significant difference (TD 29.35 +/- 3.54, ASD 29.35 +/- 2.80, t38 = 0, p = 0.999); handedness assessed by the Edinburgh Handedness Inventory (all but two children right-handed). Comorbidity is handled by explicit exclusion of pharmacological treatment, dyslexia, epilepsy and any other neurological or psychiatric condition. This is unusually thorough functional characterisation for a computer-vision paper." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-016" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with ASD', 'autistic subjects', 'ASD subjects', 'typically developing (TD) children', 'healthy subjects', 'patient and healthy children', 'pathological and healthy subjects', 'autism disease', 'this pathology'. Markedly medicalised framing - autism is repeatedly called a 'disease' and a 'pathology', and the comparison group is 'healthy' - which is at the opposite end of the corpus's terminological range from the identity-first, 'neurotypical' usage of studies like Li et al. (2017)." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-016" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No formal keyword list. The paper's vocabulary: 'autism disease diagnosis', 'video gesture analysis', 'attention map', 'computer-aided diagnostic algorithms', 'objective quantitative tool', 'motor signals of Autism', 'first screening test', 'pathological vs healthy subjects'. The recurrent 'disease'/'pathology' framing is distinctive and contrasts sharply with the neurodiversity-aligned vocabulary elsewhere in this corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-016" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Single modality, no fusion. There is a form of temporal-spatial integration internal to the model - the LSTM's learned attention map weights spatial CNN features at each time step based on its accumulated hidden state, so spatial and temporal information are combined adaptively rather than by fixed pooling. Frame-level probabilities are averaged into a per-subject decision, a late fusion across frames and clips. No combination with clinical scores, IQ or demographics, despite those being available." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-016" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Per-subject averaged accuracy with standard deviation, detection accuracy at a stated threshold, a full confusion matrix with per-class precision and recall (ASD 83.33% precision / 78.95% recall; TD 80.95% precision / 85.00% recall), and a threshold sensitivity curve from 0.5 to 0.95. No AUC or confidence intervals, but the reporting is more complete than accuracy-only studies and the full per-subject accuracy table is published, which allows a reader to see the distribution rather than only its mean." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-016" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Single session of approximately 30 minutes per participant; 12 consecutive grasps per condition across four conditions, giving 48 movements per child, with 2 practice trials before each block and a 2-" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional case-control classification study with a newly released video dataset" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder without accompanying intellectual impairment" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 20 recruited (18 male); one subject discarded from experiments because all their non-corrupted videos came from a single condition, leaving 19 analysed; comparison: 20 (16 male); one discarded for the same data reason, leaving 20 in the reported per-subject table with subject 3 removed from the ASD side" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "A CNN-LSTM with spatial attention classified whether a simple bottle-grasping movement was performed by an autistic or typically developing child from video alone at 0.82 detection accuracy, using only the pre-contact reaching phase and with visual appearance deliberately suppressed. A state-of-the-art handcrafted action-recognition baseline (dense trajectories with HOG/HOF and VLAD) performed at ",
                        "Full-data model: 0.82 detection accuracy (32 of 39 subjects correctly classified), mean per-subject accuracy 0.75 +/- 0.22. Per-intention models: 0.72 detection accuracy (28 of 39), mean accuracy 0.69 +/- 0.22, with individual conditions ranging from 0.59 (pass-to-pour) to 0.77 (pouring). Confusion matrix: ASD recall 78.95% (15/19), TD recall 85.00% (17/20), ASD precision 83.33%, TD precision 80.9" ;
                    slr:outcome "Per-subject averaged accuracy, detection accuracy (fraction of subjects whose averaged probability exceeds a threshold), standard deviations across subjects, a confusion matrix with precision and recall per class, and a sensitivity analysis of detection accuracy as the confidence threshold varies fr" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Full-data model: 0.82 detection accuracy (32 of 39 subjects correctly classified), mean per-subject accuracy 0.75 +/- 0.22. Per-intention models: 0.72 detection accuracy (28 of 39), mean accuracy 0.69 +/- 0.22, with individual conditions ranging from 0.59 (pass-to-pour) to 0.77 (pouring). Confusion matrix: ASD recall 78.95% (15/19), TD recall 85.00% (17/20), ASD precision 83.33%, TD precision 80.9; A CNN-LSTM with spatial attention classified whether a simple bottle-grasping movement was performed by an autistic or typically developing child from video alone at 0.82 detection accuracy, using only the pre-contact reaching phase and with visual appearance deliberately suppressed. A state-of-the-art handcrafted action-recognition baseline (dense trajectories with HOG/HOF and VLAD) performed at " ] ] ;
