<urn:slr:article:363f034a75b7> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Autism spectrum disorder (ASD), similar to many other developmental or behavioral conditions, is difﬁcult to be precisely diagnosed. This difﬁculty increases when the subjects are young children due to the huge overlap between the ASD symptoms and typical behaviors of young children. Therefore, it is important to develop reliable methods that could help distinguish ASD from normal behaviors of children. In this paper, we implemented a computer vision based automatic ASD prediction approach to detect autistic characteristics in a video dataset recorded from a mix of children with and without ASD. Our target dataset contains 555 videos, out of which 8349 episodes (each approximately 10 seconds) are derived. Each episode is labeled as an atypical or typical behavior by medical experts. We ﬁrst estimate children pose in each video frame by re-training a state-of-the-art human pose estimator on our manually annotated children pose dataset. Particle ﬁlter interpolation is then applied on the output of the pose estimator to predict the locations of missing body keypoints. For each episode, we calculate the children motion pattern deﬁned as the trajectory of their keypoints over time by temporally encoding the estimated pose maps. Finally, a binary classiﬁcation network is trained on the pose motion representations to discriminate between typical and atypical behaviors. We were able to achieve a classiﬁcation accuracy of 72.4% (precision=0.72 and recall=0.92) on our test dataset." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Vyas K; Ma R; Rezaei B; Liu S; Neubauer M; Ploetz T; Oberleitner R; Ostadabbas S (2019). Recognition Of Atypical Behavior In Autism Diagnosis From Video Using Pose Estimation Over Time. *2019 IEEE*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Liu S",
        "Ma R",
        "Neubauer M",
        "Oberleitner R",
        "Ostadabbas S",
        "Ploetz T",
        "Rezaei B",
        "Vyas K" ;
    dcterms:title "Recognition Of Atypical Behavior In Autism Diagnosis From Video Using Pose Estimation Over Time" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_a45f39e9" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2019 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "The stated target is ASD prediction but the labelled unit is a 10-second behavioural episode, and the number of children is unknown." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "No participant counts, ages, genders or diagnostic status; only video and time-stamp counts." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched?" ;
                            slr:justification "No demographic information exists; the comparison is between behaviour labels, not participant groups." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "Expert physician annotation through an established assessment platform, but no diagnostic instrument, no per-child outcome and no inter-rater agreement despite multiple annotators." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is domain adaptation of the upstream model performed and evaluated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Mask R-CNN was retrained on manually annotated child poses and the improvement quantified as 9-14% more successfully annotated frames." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Activity categories, video durations, frame rate and the episode construction rule are specified; recording devices, conditions and per-child protocols are not." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "Pose estimation, imputation and PoTion encoding are fully automatic and label-independent." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is there external or independent validation?" ;
                            slr:justification "None; single corpus from a single commercial source." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the validation partitioned at participant or video level?" ;
                            slr:justification "Partitioning basis unstated; 8349 episodes derive from 555 videos and an unknown number of children." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is class imbalance addressed?" ;
                            slr:justification "5265 atypical versus 3084 typical stamps with no rebalancing; 0.92 recall against 0.66 AUC indicates majority-class bias." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a meaningful baseline included?" ;
                            slr:justification "A conventional Inception V3 single-frame approach with majority voting, representing standard video classification practice." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ablation results reported consistently with the narrative?" ;
                            slr:justification "Three imputation conditions produce identical accuracy, precision and recall and AUC differing by 0.02, yet the text states the classifier 'benefits a lot from interpolation'." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics, consent and privacy handling reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No ethics approval, no parental consent and no anonymization, despite parent-recorded home video of children during play and mealtimes routed through a commercial platform." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Commercially held home-video corpus with no availability statement." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the classification target clearly distinguished from diagnosis?" ;
                            slr:justification "The paper frames episode-level atypical behaviour classification as 'automatic ASD prediction' and glosses typical/atypical as normal/ASD throughout." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are limitations discussed?" ;
                            slr:justification "No limitations section; only two technical extensions proposed." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "The authors state code 'will be released' on their website - a commitment rather than a release." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Hardware: parents' own recording devices (unspecified) at 30 fps. Software: Behavior Imaging's NODA platform for collection and expert annotation; 2D Mask R-CNN with a ResNet base, region proposal network and RoI Align, extended with a keypoint estimation head, retrained on manually annotated child poses using a semi-automatic annotation tool the authors describe as enabling researchers to build their own annotated datasets; particle filter for keypoint imputation; a modified PoTion generator; a convolutional classifier; Inception V3 for the baseline." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-030" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Mixed. The authors state that 'the code for our proposed method will be released in our website', which is a commitment rather than a completed release. The representation is described in reproducible detail - 15 named keypoints, Gaussian kernel size 12x12 with variance 1.0, the three-colour body-region scheme, intensity ramping across the episode, 256x256x3 output, the 8-seconds-before and 2-seconds-after episode rule, and 30 fps giving up to 301 frames. Dataset statistics are tabulated per set. What is missing: no participant information, no train/test split specification, no classifier hyperparameters, no random seeds, and no access route for the video corpus, which is commercially held." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-030" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: automatic prediction of ASD-related atypical behaviour from home video, with the stated design constraint that any practical algorithm be cost-efficient and unobtrusive enough for long-term monitoring in the child's natural environment. Main findings: 0.72 accuracy, 0.72 precision, 0.92-0.93 recall and 0.66 AUC for the pose-based approach, against 0.66 accuracy and 0.62 AUC for an RGB single-frame baseline; retraining the pose estimator on child poses improved frame annotation rates by 9-14%; and imputation method made essentially no difference to performance." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-030" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No formal keyword list in the extracted text. Vocabulary: 'atypical behavior', 'behavior recognition', 'pose estimation over time', 'PoTion representation', 'appearance agnostic', 'long-term behavioral monitoring', 'cost-efficient and unobtrusive'. The monitoring-and-deployment framing, and the explicit design requirement of unobtrusiveness for the natural living environment, are distinctive." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-030" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No feature importance or saliency analysis, and no behavioural characteristic is identified as distinguishing atypical from typical episodes - so the study produces a classifier without a finding about autistic movement. Two ablations are reported and are informative in different ways: the pose-versus-RGB comparison supports the architectural claim that appearance-agnostic pose carries more signal than raw frames, while the imputation ablation inadvertently undercuts its own narrative by showing three conditions performing identically on three of four metrics." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-030" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Nothing reported. No participant counts, no ages, no age ranges, no gender distribution, no matching information, and no diagnostic breakdown. The only quantitative description of the corpus is at video and time-stamp level: four sets comprising 235, 101, 177 and 42 video clips (555 total) yielding 4465, 2048, 1545 and 291 time stamps (8349 total), split 3084 typical and 5265 atypical. Since the number of contributing children is never stated, even the ratio of episodes to participants is unknown." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-030" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Motor behavior: full-body pose, represented as the trajectories of 15 skeletal keypoints over 10-second windows. The authors position this explicitly against the field's focus on faces and eyes, noting that prior work has concentrated on 'analyzing RGB frames and pertaining high level information from the video as well as focusing on specific body parts of children (e.g. face)', whereas 'none of them focused on studying full body pose movement over time as an indicator of atypical behaviors'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-030" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Supervised deep learning throughout, in a three-stage pipeline: a retrained Mask R-CNN detector for pose estimation, a classical particle filter for state estimation of missing keypoints, and a convolutional binary classifier over the resulting motion images. A conventional video-classification approach (Inception V3 on single frames with majority voting) serves as a baseline, which is a genuine comparator rather than a strawman since it represents standard practice." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-030" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The weakest dimension. A train/test split is used but its partitioning basis is never stated, and with 8349 episodes derived from 555 videos - and an unknown but certainly smaller number of children - episodes from the same video and child will span the split unless deliberately prevented, which is not claimed. There is no cross-validation, no repeated splits, no permutation testing, and no class-imbalance handling despite a 5265-to-3084 imbalance that the recall-AUC divergence suggests is driving results. There is no external validation and no participant-level evaluation. The retraining of the pose estimator on the authors' own annotated child data is a sensible domain-adaptation step and is evaluated on its own terms (9-14% more annotated frames), which is a strength within the pipeline even though it does not address classification validity." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-030" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Not reported at participant level. Expert physicians reviewed videos through the NODA platform and tagged time stamps as typical or atypical behaviour, and each video is stated to have been 'annotated by several expert physicians' - but no diagnostic instrument, criteria, or per-child diagnostic outcome is described, and no inter-rater agreement statistic is given despite multiple annotators. The ground truth for classification is therefore expert behavioural judgement rather than diagnosis." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-030" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Combine RGB and audio modalities alongside pose to improve accuracy; and develop a 3D-trajectory version of the PoTion representation to add spatial information to the pipeline. Both are technical extensions; no validation, population-characterisation or participant-level evaluation work is proposed, despite these being the study's principal gaps." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-030" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Single modality in this work. Within it, the PoTion representation performs a spatiotemporal fusion: keypoint heatmaps from up to 301 frames are colourised by body region and by relative time, then aggregated into one image, so a 10-second motion sequence is compressed into a single static RGB frame that a standard convolutional classifier can consume. The colour channels carry body-region identity and the intensity gradient carries temporal order, which is an elegant encoding of three information types into three channels. Future fusion with RGB appearance and audio is proposed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-030" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, precision, recall and AUC, reported consistently across three imputation conditions and against the RGB baseline, plus frame-annotation rates before and after pose-estimator retraining. Reporting AUC alongside accuracy is what makes the result interpretable: 0.72 accuracy with 0.92 recall but only 0.66 AUC indicates a classifier operating close to the majority class rather than discriminating well, which the accuracy figure alone would conceal." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-030" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Unstructured everyday home activity, categorised as play alone, play with others, meal time, and parents' concerns, with videos ranging from 2 to 10 minutes. The episode construction rule is explained and justified: 8 seconds before and 2 seconds after each expert time stamp, 'because physicians usually labeled a typical or atypical time stamp after they observed the behavior for a while, and a 10-second window could approximately record the behavior in full'. This is a thoughtful accommodation of how human annotation actually occurs." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-030" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; single videos per session with no longitudinal modelling, though the platform is designed for ongoing remote assessment. The data are the most privacy-sensitive in the corpus - parent-recorded video inside family homes capturing children during play and meals, routed through a commercial company to an academic laboratory. There is no ethics approval statement, no parental consent statement, no anonymization procedure and no data-protection or retention provision anywhere in the paper." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-030" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The paper does not report participant groups at all. The corpus is described only as 555 videos 'recorded from a mix of children with and without ASD', with no participant counts, no breakdown by diagnostic status, and no indication of how many children contributed the videos. The analytic groups are instead behavioural: 3084 expert-labelled typical time stamps and 5265 atypical time stamps across four video sets. This is a fundamentally different unit of analysis from the rest of the corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-030" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Home setting - videos recorded by parents during the child's daily activities and submitted through a commercial remote assessment platform. Existing data: the NODA corpus was collected and expert-annotated by Behavior Imaging and provided to the authors, so this is secondary analysis of industry data. A manually annotated children's pose dataset was newly created by the authors for retraining the pose estimator." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-030" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with ASD', 'people with ASD', 'the patients', 'ASD patients', 'patient's condition', 'children with and without ASD', 'typical (i.e. normal) behavior', 'atypical (i.e. ASD) behavior', 'normal behaviors of children'. Markedly medicalised - children are 'patients' whose 'condition tends to deteriorate with increasing age' - and the typical/atypical distinction is repeatedly glossed as normal/ASD, which conflates a behavioural label with a diagnostic one." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-030" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No dedicated limitations section. The authors note only that the approach could be improved by combining RGB and audio modalities and by moving to 3D trajectories. Identifiable in review and more consequential: the classification target is episode-level expert-labelled behaviour rather than participant diagnosis, so the reported figures are not diagnostic accuracy despite the paper's 'ASD prediction' framing; no participant counts, ages, genders or diagnostic status are reported, so the corpus composition and any demographic confounding are unknowable; the train/test partitioning basis is unstated with 8349 episodes from 555 videos, making episode-level leakage across the split highly likely; class imbalance (5265 atypical to 3084 typical) is unaddressed and is consistent with the 0.92 recall against 0.66 AUC, indicating a majority-class-biased classifier; the imputation ablation shows three conditions performing identically on accuracy, precision and recall yet the text claims 'the classifier benefits a lot from interpolation'; multiple expert annotators are mentioned with no inter-rater agreement reported; and there is no ethics or consent statement for home video of children." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-030" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "An engineered spatiotemporal image representation consumed by a learned classifier. The PoTion encoding is the distinctive element: rather than feeding a frame sequence to a recurrent or 3D convolutional model, it collapses the entire episode into a single 256x256x3 image where spatial position encodes keypoint location, colour hue encodes body region, and colour intensity encodes time within the episode. This makes a temporal classification problem tractable with a standard 2D convolutional network." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-030" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "An IEEE conference (2019), venue name truncated in the source metadata - a signal-processing or computer-vision venue rather than an autism or clinical one, consistent with the paper's technical framing and industry collaboration." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-030" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2019. The related-work survey characterises the field as having grown 'in the last couple of decades' around home-video behaviour analysis, infant observation scales, facial expression quantification with IQ-matched controls, caregiver-child interaction analysis, and multimodal eye-movement classification - and then identifies a specific gap: 'none of them focused on studying full body pose movement over time as an indicator of atypical behaviors', with most work 'focusing on specific body parts of children (e.g. face)'. This is an accurate characterisation of the corpus, where face and gaze dominate and full-body pose is rare. The commercial NODA platform also documents the emergence of remote video-based autism assessment as a deployed service by 2019." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-030" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None reported. No cognitive, linguistic, adaptive or severity measures and no comorbidity information for any participant." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-030" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Videos range from 2 to 10 minutes; each labelled time stamp is expanded into a 10-second episode taking 8 seconds before and 2 seconds after the stamp, giving at most 301 frames at 30 fps" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional classification study on an industry-collected annotated video corpus; the unit of analysis is the 10-second behavioural episode" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Mixed cohort of children with and without ASD" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: Not reported - the dataset is described as videos 'collected from children with and without ASD' with no participant counts; comparison: Not reported" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Full-body pose trajectories encoded as PoTion motion images classified expert-labelled atypical versus typical 10-second behavioural episodes from home video at 0.72 accuracy with 0.92 recall but only 0.66 AUC, outperforming a conventional RGB single-frame baseline (0.66 accuracy, 0.62 AUC). Retraining the pose estimator on manually annotated child poses raised successful frame annotation by 9-14%",
                        "Proposed approach: 0.72 accuracy, 0.72 precision, 0.92-0.93 recall, 0.66 AUC. Imputation ablation: no interpolation 0.72/0.72/0.93/0.64; linear 0.72/0.72/0.92/0.66; particle filter 0.72/0.72/0.93/0.66 - accuracy, precision and recall are effectively identical across all three, with AUC differing by only 0.02. RGB single-frame baseline: 0.66 accuracy, 0.70 precision, 0.77 recall, 0.62 AUC. Retraini" ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy, precision, recall and AUC, reported across three imputation conditions and against the RGB baseline; separately, the percentage of frames successfully annotated with pose is compared before and after retraining" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Proposed approach: 0.72 accuracy, 0.72 precision, 0.92-0.93 recall, 0.66 AUC. Imputation ablation: no interpolation 0.72/0.72/0.93/0.64; linear 0.72/0.72/0.92/0.66; particle filter 0.72/0.72/0.93/0.66 - accuracy, precision and recall are effectively identical across all three, with AUC differing by only 0.02. RGB single-frame baseline: 0.66 accuracy, 0.70 precision, 0.77 recall, 0.62 AUC. Retraini; Full-body pose trajectories encoded as PoTion motion images classified expert-labelled atypical versus typical 10-second behavioural episodes from home video at 0.72 accuracy with 0.92 recall but only 0.66 AUC, outperforming a conventional RGB single-frame baseline (0.66 accuracy, 0.62 AUC). Retraining the pose estimator on manually annotated child poses raised successful frame annotation by 9-14%" ] ] ;
