<urn:slr:article:420caf43bd93> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "This article aims to improve the detection of ASD in children using ET data and advanced ML techniques. ASD, a neurodevelopmental disorder characterized by impairments in social communication, interaction, and repetitive behaviors, typically manifests before the age of three. Early diagnosis is crucial for timely and effective interventions. ET data have revealed distinct gaze patterns in children with ASD, such as diminished attention to social cues and increased ﬁxation on repetitive stimuli. However, current methods of ET data analysis are largely manual and lack scalability for widespread clinical screening. To address these limitations, we propose a novel, scanpath-based ASD detection method that identiﬁes atypical gaze behaviors through dynamic changes in visual attention. We extract four sequential features from scanpaths and analyze variations in feature space across different ASD severity levels low, mild, moderate, and severe using the MultiMatch and DTW similarity metrics. Our analysis reveals that children with ASD exhibit unique and highly individualized gaze patterns when compared to TD children. Notable differences are observed in attention duration and vertical gaze distribution, providing key insights into ASD-related visual behaviors. For classiﬁcation, we employ a hybrid CNNRNN model, which signiﬁcantly outperforms traditional ML methods. The CNN-RNN model achieves an accuracy of 97%, recall of 98.24%, and an F1-score of 97.04% using the feature set (x, d, y). In comparison, models based on GRU and 2-LSTM networks show competitive accuracies of 92% and 90%, respectively. However, RFC and XGBoost models underperform, with accuracies ranging between 70.25 and 80.80%. These ﬁndings demonstrate the efﬁcacy of DL approaches, particularly the CNN-RNN hybrid model, in accurately classifying ASD based on ET data, emphasizing their potential to enhance diagnostic accuracy. The proposed scalable method for ASD detection holds promise for improving early diagnosis and intervention, which is critical for better long-term outcomes in children with ASD. Further evaluation on an independent dataset will assess the generalizability and precision of the model." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Cheekaty S; Muneeswari G (2025). Enhanced multilevel autism classification for children using eye-tracking and hybrid CNN-RNN deep learning models. *Neural Computing and Applications, 37(33), 27631-27654*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Cheekaty S",
        "Muneeswari G" ;
    dcterms:title "Enhanced multilevel autism classification for children using eye-tracking and hybrid CNN-RNN deep learning models" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_bb4d1f7a" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2025 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data source and eligibility described" ;
                            slr:justification "Recruitment site, age band and CARS eligibility threshold are stated prospectively, and the reason for using DSM-5 plus CARS rather than ADOS/ADI-R is explained." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Sample size justified" ;
                            slr:justification "No justification; forty children yield 4,000 analysis units by frame and eye multiplication, and an unretracted statement elsewhere claims 8-10 children aged x years." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participants appropriate to the question" ;
                            slr:justification "Single special school, all participants already diagnosed, no TD group although the abstract reports a TD comparison; sex not recorded." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Predictors defined and measured appropriately" ;
                            slr:justification "Scanpath features x, y, duration and velocity are clearly defined, extracted with standard fixation/saccade algorithms after calibration drift correction, and systematically ablated." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Predictors assessed blind to outcome" ;
                            slr:justification "Feature extraction is automatic and label-independent, but MIC-based feature scoring and the feature-set selection were performed over the full dataset." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Preprocessing applied without leakage" ;
                            slr:justification "Augmentation by rotation and shearing is described as a dataset-preparation step before the 70:15:15 partition." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome definition and ascertainment" ;
                            slr:justification "Diagnosis is ascertained by physicians using DSM-5 and CARS, but the modelled outcome is a four-level CARS band whose defined limits are impossible (High requires CARS>60 on a 15-60 scale) and inconsistent with the CARS>=30 eligibility criterion." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome determined independently of predictors" ;
                            slr:justification "Severity bands come from clinician-administered CARS, which is independent of the gaze features supplied to the model." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Limitations and overinterpretation" ;
                            slr:justification "Three specific and genuine limitations are stated, and the abstract itself concedes that generalisability is not yet assessed; but the leakage, the missing TD group and the impossible severity bands are unmentioned, alongside claims of unprecedented accuracy and setting a new standard." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance measures adequate" ;
                            slr:justification "Eight metrics are defined by equation and a four-class confusion matrix is presented and discussed by class, but specificity, FDR and FOR are never reported, no interval accompanies any figure, and the 25% chance level is unstated." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Validation design guards against optimism" ;
                            slr:justification "Image-level 70:15:15 split with fixed per-child counts guarantees participant overlap across all three subsets; cross-validation asserted four times but never quantified." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Analysis unit matches the clinical claim" ;
                            slr:justification "Claims concern classifying children; every reported metric is computed over eye-image crops, at 100 per child." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "External comparison appropriate" ;
                            slr:justification "Table 7 compares this study's four-class severity accuracies against Saliency4ASD entries solving a binary ASD-versus-TD task on different data, presenting the difference as a methodological advance." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional, single session, no follow-up. Ethics is a genuine strength: approval from the Government Medical College Guntur Ethics Committee with a stated decision reference (IRB-2024-0057), written parental informed consent before participation, and separate written consent for publication of participants' details and any accompanying images. That second, publication-specific consent places this study at the responsible end of the review's ethics gradient, alongside local_7d7f1fcc and well away from studies that report no ethics statement at all." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-144" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Supervised sequence classification. Primary model is a hybrid CNN-RNN combining convolutional feature extraction with GRU and LSTM recurrence; comparators are GRU, a two-layer LSTM, BiLSTM, random forest and XGBoost. LSTM layers use 128 hidden units into a fully connected softmax; batch size 64, cross-entropy loss, learning rate 0.0001. The classification section describes the output as softmax regression for class probability prediction in binary classification although the task is four-class throughout." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-144" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A single special school (Dakshinya Autistic School, Guntur) supplying participants, with ethics oversight from Government Medical College, Guntur, and analysis at VIT-AP University. Recording used a five-point calibration then passive viewing. Setting is described adequately for the acquisition but the recording room, examiner and session conditions are not characterised, and there is no discussion of whether recording conditions varied across severity groups." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-144" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Neural Computing and Applications 37(33):27631-27654, in a special issue drawn from the 2023 India International Congress on Computational Intelligence. Special-issue routing is relevant to the review's venue analysis: the impossible CARS bands, the absent TD group behind a TD claim in the abstract, the surviving x placeholder in the participant count, and a literature review containing building-energy and distributed-generation citations all passed review in 53 days." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-144" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No multimodal fusion - eye tracking alone. There is a within-modality fusion of two representations of the same recording (scanpath sequences for the RNN path, cropped eye images for the CNN path), which is what the hybrid designation refers to. The Gesell Developmental Scale was collected and could have supported a genuine behavioural-clinical fusion, but no GDS result is reported." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-144" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Thin. Age is given only as the 8-12 eligibility band, with no mean, SD or per-class distribution. Sex is not reported at all - not even a total male/female count - despite forty children being individually recruited and consented. Nothing on socioeconomic status, language, verbal ability or schooling level, and no per-severity-class characteristics of any kind. The Gesell Developmental Scale is said to have been used to relate eye-tracking data to behavioural and clinical outcomes, but no GDS number appears anywhere in the article." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-144" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The strongest interpretability work among the eye-tracking studies charted so far, and the reason this paper is worth more than its headline number. Maximal information coefficient scores rank individual scanpath features and the similarity measures; a systematic four-way feature-set ablation shows accuracy moving from 90.00% ((x,y,v)) to 97.00% ((x,d,y)) to 95.23% ((x,y,d,v)); and from that pattern the authors argue that velocity is uninformative for severity while fixation duration and vertical gaze position carry the signal. They further contrast the two similarity metrics, finding MultiMatch less sensitive to subtle temporal or spatial change and DTW more sensitive to vertical gaze distribution. These are claims about gaze behaviour that could be falsified by another laboratory, which is rare in this corpus. Their evidential weight is nonetheless limited by the same leakage that inflates the accuracies: an ablation computed under an image-level split ranks features by how well they support participant recognition as much as by how well they support severity discrimination." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-144" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Passive viewing of a ten-second silent movie in which a young Asian woman articulates the English letters, chosen on the reasoning - well argued from the prior literature reviewed - that a speaking face is a more discriminating stimulus than a static one, since gaze duration on inner facial features is reduced specifically while the face is speaking. The stimulus choice is one of the better-motivated in the corpus. Against that, the workflow section describes classifying autism severity from a 2-min ET video, which contradicts the ten-second stimulus and is never reconciled." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-144" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Forty children, all with an existing ASD diagnosis, recruited from Dakshinya Autistic School in Guntur, India, and divided into four severity classes of exactly ten each: low, mild, medium and high. There is no typically developing comparison group, which matters because the abstract nonetheless reports that children with ASD exhibit unique and highly individualized gaze patterns when compared to TD children - a comparison the design cannot support. The only TD data in the paper belong to the separate public Saliency4ASD set (14 ASD, 14 TD) used in a side experiment. Two internal inconsistencies affect the count: the introduction states that in the initial training we enrolled a group of 8-10 children aged x years, with the placeholder x left in the published text, and the analysis unit is not the child but the cropped eye image - 50 frames x 2 eyes x 40 children = 4,000 images, which is what the 70:15:15 split partitions." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-144" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, precision, F1, false discovery rate, false omission rate and AUC are all defined by equation, which reads as thorough; only accuracy, recall and F1 are then reported for the within-study experiments, and specificity, FDR and FOR appear as numbers nowhere. A four-class confusion matrix is shown and discussed sensibly - low and medium separate better than mild and high. The Saliency4ASD table reports accuracy and AUC together (95.93/95.82 and 97.95/97.84). The 25% four-class chance level is never stated, and no metric carries an interval." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-144" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Autism spectrum disorder; CNN-RNN hybrid network; Early diagnosis; Eye-tracking data; Machine learning; Scanpath analysis. Method- and modality-centred, with scanpath analysis correctly signalling the paper's actual analytic contribution; nothing about severity, participants or validation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-144" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Well handled and honestly reported. Diagnoses were made by experienced physicians applying DSM-5 criteria together with CARS scores, and the paper states plainly why gold-standard instruments were not used: ADI-R and ADOS evaluations are not widely available in India. That candour about a real infrastructural constraint compares favourably with the many studies in this corpus that simply assert diagnosis or inherit labels from a download. What undermines it is the severity banding rather than the diagnosis: eligibility required CARS >=30, yet Table 3 assigns ten children to a Low class defined as CARS <=20 and ten more to a Mild class spanning 20 to 40, and a High class defined as CARS >60 when the scale's maximum is 60. The four equal groups cannot have been generated by the stated rule, and the actual CARS distribution is never shown." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-144" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Visual attention during viewing of a speaking face, decomposed into fixation position (x, y), fixation duration and inter-fixation velocity, and into scanpath shape compared by DTW and MultiMatch. The behavioural claims that emerge are specific and testable: attention duration and vertical gaze distribution differ most across severity levels, velocity least, and children with ASD show highly individualised rather than merely reduced gaze patterns." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-144" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The stated goal is to replace largely manual, unscalable eye-tracking analysis with an automated scanpath-based method that classifies autism severity into four levels, and to characterise how gaze features vary across those levels using DTW and MultiMatch similarity. Headline findings: CNN-RNN with features (x, d, y) reaches accuracy 97.00%, recall 98.24%, F1 97.04%, against GRU 92%, 2-LSTM 92%, XGBoost 80.25% and RFC 80.80%. The more interesting finding is the feature ablation: velocity contributes least, and the authors conclude that it is the continuous attention process, as opposed to the transition phases, that reflects atypical visual behaviour - with differences concentrated in attention duration and vertical gaze distribution." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-144" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "ASD, autism spectrum disorder, children with ASD, ASD children, autism applicants, participants, TD children, neurotypical. The paper is one of the few in the corpus to define neurotypical explicitly and to note that it refers to individuals whose neurological development conforms to societal norms and expectations - a formulation that recognises the norm as social rather than natural. Elsewhere the register slips (autism applicants as a table heading; the severity ladder low/mild/medium/high applied to children rather than to symptoms). The introduction is unusual in this corpus for framing the research motivation partly around reducing stigma, promoting inclusion, and improving educational and employment outcomes rather than solely around detection." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-144" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three limitations are stated: small-scale datasets typical of ASD research; the practice of retaining valid scanpaths across paradigms, which produces inconsistent sample sizes across subjects; and eye-tracking reliability problems from calibration precision, subject cooperation and equipment limits. All three are real and specific. Unmentioned: that the 70:15:15 split is at the eye-image level so every participant appears in all three subsets; that there is no TD group despite the abstract's TD comparison; that the CARS bands defining the four classes are arithmetically impossible and inconsistent with the eligibility threshold; that the external comparison is against a different task on a different dataset; that specificity, FDR and FOR are defined but never reported; and that the asserted cross-validation is never quantified." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-144" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Mixed. The acquisition protocol is reported in enough detail to be repeated - device, sampling rate, calibration procedure, stimulus, display parameters, eligibility criteria - and the four feature sets and their ablation are fully specified, so the study's most interesting result could be re-tested. Everything downstream is opaque: no code, no seed, no MATLAB version, no convolutional architecture, no epoch count, no fold count for the cross-validation that is repeatedly asserted but never reported, and no access route for the 4,000-image dataset the paper announces as a contribution for researchers and developers to leverage. A further reproducibility concern is the literature review, which contains a run of citations on building energy consumption, distributed generation, solar-equipped neighbourhoods and electric vehicles - and the abbreviation list correspondingly defines DB as Design builder, DG as Distributed generation and ICME as Integrated Computational Materials Engineering. These belong to a different paper and indicate that portions of the manuscript were assembled rather than written for this study." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-144" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The design is a 70:15:15 partition of 4,000 eye images derived from 40 children at exactly 100 images per child (50 frames, left and right eye). Because the per-child image count is constant and the split is at the image level, every participant is present in the training, validation and test subsets simultaneously; more finely still, the left and right eye of the same video frame - two crops of one instant of one recording - can land on opposite sides. This is frame-level leakage of the same kind documented in the video, EEG-epoch, thermal, cry and facial-image branches, and it is sufficient to explain a 97% four-class result from 40 children. Three further weaknesses compound it. Augmentation by rotation and shearing is described as a dataset-preparation step applied before partitioning. Cross-validation is asserted four separate times as ensuring robustness, but no fold count, no per-fold result and no variance is ever reported, so it cannot be assessed and may not have been run on the final model. And the external comparison in Table 7 places this study's four-class severity accuracies (95.93%, 97.95%) beside Saliency4ASD entries (74.22%, 65.41%, 61.62%) that solve a different, binary ASD-versus-TD task on different data - the numbers are not commensurable, and the higher figure is not evidence of a better method. To the authors' credit, the abstract itself concedes that further evaluation on an independent dataset will assess the generalizability and precision of the model, which is the correct reading of their own result." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-144" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Received 12 August 2024, accepted 4 October 2024, published online 4 December 2024, issued 2025. Framing is assistive-to-transformative: the work is said to show how ML and eye tracking can transform the diagnosis and treatment of autism and to set a new standard in the field, with the method described as capturing spatial and temporal dynamics with unprecedented accuracy. Set against that rhetoric, the paper is also one of the few in the corpus whose abstract states outright that generalisability has not yet been assessed, and whose introduction motivates the work partly by stigma reduction, inclusion and long-term quality of life rather than solely by detection throughput." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-144" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Weight DTW by fixation duration to improve scanpath alignment; address calibration precision and data-integrity problems in eye-tracking to improve reliability and accessibility for screening; and evaluate on an independent dataset to assess generalizability and precision. The last is stated in the abstract itself, which is an unusually forthright acknowledgement that the reported figures are not yet generalisation estimates." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-144" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "CARS is the only reported measure, and it is the label rather than a covariate. The Gesell Developmental Scale is named but never reported. No IQ, adaptive-behaviour, language or comorbidity data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-144" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "SMI RED250 portable eye tracker at 250 Hz, 1024x768 display, stated spatial resolution 0.05, five-point calibration, with calibration drift correction applied in preprocessing. Fixation/saccade segmentation by dispersion-threshold and velocity-threshold algorithms. Classification implemented in MATLAB on Windows 10 with an Intel Core i5 and 4 GB RAM; frame segmentation in Python. The hardware is implausibly modest for the described deep models and no training time is given." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-144" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two representations. (i) Variable-length scanpath sequences over four features - x, y, fixation duration d, velocity v - padded to maximum length with a masking mechanism, evaluated in four combinations. (ii) 4,000 cropped eye images at 416x416, fifty frames per child times left and right eye, each labelled with subject ID, frame number, severity band and eye side. The image-level representation is what the 70:15:15 split partitions, and it is where the validity problem originates: with a fixed 100 images per child, the split cannot separate participants even in principle." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-144" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Within-study: GRU, 2-LSTM, RFC, XGBoost across four feature sets. Across-study: Saliency4ASD/ICME 2019 entries (Tao and Shyu 74.22%, Wu et al. 65.41% and 61.62%, Startsev and Dorr AUC 75 and 76.9), ag" ;
                    slr:study_design "Prospective single-site primary data collection followed by supervised four-class classification of autism severity, with a 70:15:15 image-level split. A secondary comparison exper…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: All 40 participants have an ASD diagnosis, allocated 10 each to low, mild, medium and high severity classes.; comparison: None. There is no typically developing group in the study, although the abstract states that children with ASD exhibit unique and highly individualized gaze patterns when compared to TD children. The only TD data in the paper come from the separate public Saliency4ASD set (14 ASD, 14 TD children) used in a side comparison." ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "CNN-RNN with feature set (x,d,y): accuracy 97.00%, recall 98.24%, F1 97.04%. Other feature sets: (x,y,d,v) 95.23%, (y,x) 94.00%, (x,y,v) 90.00%. Comparators at the best feature set: GRU 92%, 2-LSTM 92%, XGBoost 80.25%, RFC 80.80%. On the Saliency4ASD comparison the proposed method is reported at 95.93% accuracy / 95.82% AUC (full dataset) and 97.95% / 97.84% (eye dataset)." ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, precision, F1, false discovery rate, false omission rate and AUC are all defined by formula. Only accuracy, recall and F1 are actually tabulated for the within-study comparison; specificity, FDR and FOR are never reported as numbers. A four-class confusion matrix " ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "CNN-RNN with feature set (x,d,y): accuracy 97.00%, recall 98.24%, F1 97.04%. Other feature sets: (x,y,d,v) 95.23%, (y,x) 94.00%, (x,y,v) 90.00%. Comparators at the best feature set: GRU 92%, 2-LSTM 92%, XGBoost 80.25%, RFC 80.80%. On the Saliency4ASD comparison the proposed method is reported at 95.93% accuracy / 95.82% AUC (full dataset) and 97.95% / 97.84% (eye dataset)." ] ] ;
