    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Predictors are the 25 raw CAT-Q-ES items with no selection, no engineering and no label-informed preprocessing. Hyperparameters were tuned on a validation partition inside each cross-validation trial and never on the test partition. Fifty algorithms were compared under one common protocol, with packages and grids published." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "unclear" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Convenience online recruitment through autism organisations and social media produces a sample that is 78% women, more educated than the general population and self-selected for interest in camouflaging - the authors identify this, explain the mechanism and name it as the study's most notable limitation. Exclusions are prespecified and counted; both groups are characterised in full on age, gender, education and comorbidity, with between-group differences tested." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "All 601 participants entered the analysis; the four cross-validation partitions are participant-disjoint and stratified across the score range; missing data amounted to 0.30% and were imputed at person level with a stated rationale; predictors and outcomes were collected in a single administration, so no interval issue arises." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Outcomes are self-reported questionnaire scores (BAPQ, PHQ-9, GAD-7) completed by the same participant in the same sitting as the predictors, so predictor and outcome share method variance and, for the BAPQ social subscales, overlapping construct content. Autism diagnosis is self-reported, though restricted to clinician-made diagnoses with self-diagnosis excluded and the residual uncertainty acknowledged." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "some" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<urn:slr:article:5991dace03f8> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Background: Autism spectrum disorder (ASD) is currently diagnosed using qualitative methods that measure between 20-100 behaviors, can span multiple appointments with trained clinicians, and take several hours to complete. In our previous work, we demonstrated the efficacy of machine learning classifiers to accelerate the process by collecting home videos of US-based children, identifying a reduced subset of behavioral features that are scored by untrained raters using a machine learning classifier to determine children’s “risk scores” for autism. We achieved an accuracy of 92% (95% CI 88%-97%) on US videos using a classifier built on five features. Objective: Using videos of Bangladeshi children collected from Dhaka Shishu Children’s Hospital, we aim to scale our pipeline to another culture and other developmental delays, including speech and language conditions. Methods: Although our previously published and validated pipeline and set of classifiers perform reasonably well on Bangladeshi videos (75% accuracy, 95% CI 71%-78%), this work improves on that accuracy through the development and application of a powerful new technique for adaptive aggregation of crowdsourced labels. We enhance both the utility and performance of our model by building two classification layers: The first layer distinguishes between typical and atypical behavior, and the second layer distinguishes between ASD and non-ASD. In each of the layers, we use a unique rater weighting scheme to aggregate classification scores from different raters based on their expertise. We also determine Shapley values for the most important features in the classifier to understand how the classifiers’ process aligns with clinical intuition. Results: Using these techniques, we achieved an accuracy (area under the curve [AUC]) of 76% (SD 3%) and sensitivity of 76% (SD 4%) for identifying atypical children from among developmentally delayed children, and an accuracy (AUC) of 85% (SD 5%) and sensitivity of 76% (SD 6%) for identifying children with ASD from those predicted to have other developmental delays. Conclusions: These results show promise for using a mobile video-based and machine learning–directed approach for early and remote detection of autism in Bangladeshi children. This strategy could provide important resources for developmental health in developing countries with few clinical resources for diagnosis, helping children get access to care at an early age. Future J Med Internet Res 2019 | vol. 21 | iss. 4 | e13822 | p. 1 http://www.jmir.org/2019/4/e13822/ (page number not for citation purposes) Tariq et al JOURNAL OF MEDICAL INTERNET RESEARCH XSL•FO RenderX research aimed at extending the application of this approach to identify a range of other conditions and determine the population-level burden of developmental disabilities and impairments will be of high value. (J Med Internet Res 2019;21(4):e13822) doi: 10.2196/13822" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Tariq Q; Fleming SL; Schwartz JN; Dunlap K; Corbin C; Washington P; Kalantarian H; Khan NZ; Darmstadt GL; Wall DP (2019). Detecting Developmental Delay and Autism Through Machine. **." ;
    dcterms:creator "Corbin C",
        "Darmstadt GL",
        "Dunlap K",
        "Fleming SL",
        "Kalantarian H",
        "Khan NZ",
        "Schwartz JN",
        "Tariq Q",
        "Wall DP",
        "Washington P" ;
    dcterms:title "Detecting Developmental Delay and Autism Through Machine" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_4b031d44" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2019 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched?" ;
                            slr:justification "Sex differs by 26 percentage points between ASD and SLC cohorts; income and residence differ sharply across all three groups; no matching or comparability testing." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "150-159 children aged 18 months to 4 years suits the early-detection aim, but all ASD and TD families were urban and predominantly higher-income, so the rural low-resource population the work targets is barely represented." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a clinically informative comparison group included?" ;
                            slr:justification "Speech and language conditions as a separate class directly tests specificity against another developmental delay." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Recruitment site, enrolment criteria, and an unusually rich demographic table covering age, sex, preterm birth, income, residence, religion and stunting." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "Raters were non-Bengali-speaking, clinically untrained and had no access to diagnostic status." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is measurement consistent across groups?" ;
                            slr:justification "All videos rated through the same platform by the same rater pool under identical criteria." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "MCHAT plus two-clinician evaluation, with ADOS on only 28 of 50 ASD children and the reason disclosed." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Video content criteria, quality-inspection process, rater training duration, rater quality checks against gold-standard scores, and the 31-question instrument are all described." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is cross-cultural generalisation empirically tested?" ;
                            slr:justification "Previously published US-validated classifiers applied unchanged to the Bangladeshi data, with the resulting AUC 0.75 reported against 92% on US videos." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is class imbalance handled?" ;
                            slr:justification "Balanced class weights applied in the elastic-net models." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is fold partitioning at child level stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Not explicitly stated, though with one video per child the practical risk is limited." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is specificity reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Only AUC and sensitivity are given for both layers, so the false-positive burden of the sensitivity-optimised triage layer cannot be assessed." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are demographic confounds controlled or adjusted for?" ;
                            slr:justification "Sex and income differences between groups are visible in the demographic table but are not adjusted for or discussed." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is feature importance derived from the model and interpreted?" ;
                            slr:justification "Shapley values at both individual-rater and ensemble level, with cross-cultural feature comparison and a plausible mechanism." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No data availability statement, though identifiable child video could not reasonably be released." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the performance drop on transfer reported honestly?" ;
                            slr:justification "The fall from 92% accuracy on US videos to AUC 0.75 on Bangladeshi videos is reported prominently and diagnosed in three specific ways." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "None, but all packages are named with exact version numbers and the mathematical formulations are given." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics, consent and competing interests reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Dual ethics clearance, Bengali-language consent, secure transmission, HIPAA-compliant rating platform, and a disclosed conflict of interest for the senior author as founder of a paediatric digital health company." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Parents submitted brief unstructured videos, quality-screened against three content criteria: capturing the child's face and hands, involving social interaction or attempts at it, and involving interaction between the child and a toy or object. All 159 submitted videos passed quality inspection. Nine clinically untrained, non-Bengali-speaking US raters then answered 31 multiple-choice questions per video after a one-hour training session, with quality control comparing a 10-video subset against gold-standard scores agreed by two experienced clinical research coordinators." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-021" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Fusion occurs across annotators rather than across modalities, and this is the paper's methodological contribution. Each rater's annotations train a separate random forest, and predictions are combined by a softmax-weighted ensemble whose weights reflect each rater's model accuracy relative to a majority-vote baseline. The authors argue this lets the ensemble exploit each rater's strengths (one may rate eye contact well, another language ability) while discounting their weaknesses, and note it permits distributed annotation where each rater sees only a subset of videos without requiring imputation. A second form of composition is the stacked two-layer architecture, where layer one's atypical classifications feed layer two." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-021" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "AUC and sensitivity with standard deviations for both layers, and AUC with standard deviation for the source-classifier transfer test, plus ROC curves. Reporting sensitivity alongside AUC is appropriate for a screening framing, and reporting standard deviations across folds is better than corpus norm. However specificity is not reported for either layer, which matters given that layer one is explicitly optimised for sensitivity - so the false-positive burden of the first-stage triage is not quantifiable from the paper." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-021" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Journal of Medical Internet Research - a digital health and health informatics journal, clinically oriented rather than computational or autism-specific." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-021" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Behavioural observation mediated by human rating rather than direct sensing: 31 categorical judgements of autism-relevant behaviours (eye contact, social participation, stereotyped speech, sensory seeking, stereotyped interests and actions, and others) made by watching short video. This is the only approach in the corpus where the 'features' are human perceptual judgements of behaviour rather than automatically extracted signals - which is deliberate, since it removes any need for specialised sensing hardware in the deployment setting." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-021" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: scale a validated US video-based screening pipeline to another culture and to developmental delays beyond autism, for deployment in settings lacking clinical resources. Main findings: US-trained classifiers transferred to Bangladeshi videos at maximum AUC 0.75 (SD 0.06) against 92% accuracy on US videos; retrained stacked classifiers with rater-adaptive weighting achieved AUC 76% (SD 3%) and sensitivity 76% (SD 4%) for atypical versus typical, and AUC 85% (SD 5%) and sensitivity 76% (SD 6%) for ASD versus other developmental delay; and feature importance differed by culture, with eye contact more prominent for Bangladeshi videos and social participation and stereotyped speech more prominent for US videos." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-021" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; a single short video per child with no follow-up. The data are among the most sensitive in the corpus - identifiable home video of very young children, transmitted internationally and viewed by nine non-clinical raters in another country. Governance is reported reasonably fully: dual ethical clearance from the Bangladesh Institute of Child Health / Dhaka Shishu Children's Hospital and the Stanford IRB, consent obtained in Bengali, secure transmission, and a HIPAA-compliant rating platform. What is absent is any retention, deletion or re-use policy, and any discussion of the ethics of routing identifiable child video to non-clinical raters abroad." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-021" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "MCHAT (Bangla version) administered to all children, followed by clinical evaluation by a developmental psychologist and a child health physician to assign ASD, SLC or TD. ADOS was administered to only 28 of 50 ASD children, with the reason given explicitly - families could not commit the time required, 'a common problem for families in low-resource areas'. This partial gold-standard coverage is disclosed transparently and is itself an informative observation about diagnostic feasibility in the target setting. MCHAT scores are reported per group (13.5 ASD, 2 TD, 0.08 SLC), giving some quantitative separation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-021" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Expand to larger samples across Bangladesh and other low-resource settings; recruit raters who share the children's linguistic and sociocultural background, hypothesised to close the performance gap with US results; accumulate a large annotated video corpus so that convolutional and recurrent networks can learn features directly from raw video and audio rather than from human annotations; and use location-tagged videos to generate epidemiological prevalence estimates that could inform policy and resource allocation - a public-health framing of future work that is unique in this corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-021" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2019. The paper documents the field's cross-cultural blind spot directly: empirically validated tools like ADOS and ADI-R 'are not always used in different countries, particularly in developing countries, as these tools are expensive, require trained clinicians to administer, and may be limited by available translations and cultural adaptations', and it notes that global autism prevalence is 'largely unknown' with estimates of 0.15-0.8% in Bangladesh and around 3% in Dhaka, most cases in semiurban and rural areas going unnoticed. It situates itself in a programme of prior work on rapid mobile ASD detection from short home videos, and its central empirical contribution - measuring how far those US-validated classifiers transfer - is the kind of external validation this corpus otherwise almost entirely lacks." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-021" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with autism', 'children with ASD', 'the ASD cohort', 'children with an SLC', 'children with neurotypical development (TD)', 'children in need of therapeutic interventions', 'children with developmental delays'. Person-first throughout, and notably 'neurotypical development' rather than 'normal' or 'healthy'. The framing is service-access oriented - autism as a condition requiring diagnosis for intervention access - rather than deficit-focused." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-021" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The paper is unusual in this corpus for treating generalisation as an empirical question rather than an assumption. It applies previously published classifiers unchanged to a new cultural population and reports the resulting performance drop (92% accuracy on US videos to AUC 0.75 on Bangladeshi videos), then trains models on each dataset and tests on the other to compare feature reliance. Class imbalance is handled with balanced class weights. Three-fold cross-validation with reported training and test demographics gives some assurance of split stability. What is missing: whether cross-validation folds were partitioned at child level is not explicitly stated (though with one video per child this is largely moot); specificity is not reported; and no prospective or external validation of the retrained Bangladeshi models is performed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-021" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "MCHAT total scores and ADOS subscores (social affect, restricted and repetitive behaviour, composite) reported for the assessed subset. Additional health and social indicators are reported that no other corpus study collects: preterm birth, stunted growth, family income band, urban/semiurban/rural residence, and religion. Stunting information is missing for the entire TD cohort, which prevents its use as a covariate. No IQ, language or adaptive-functioning scores." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-021" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Home setting for data capture - parents recorded brief videos on mobile phones - combined with clinical setting for diagnosis and recruitment at a Dhaka children's hospital, and remote US-based rating. Newly collected data. The design deliberately exploits Bangladesh's 95% mobile phone subscription rate as the deployment substrate." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-021" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Supervised learning. Random forests per rater with softmax ensemble weighting for the main classification; logistic regression with elastic net regularisation and balanced class weights for the cross-cultural transfer analysis. The choice of a two-layer stack is motivated conceptually - layer one optimised for sensitivity so no atypical case is missed, layer two optimised for both sensitivity and specificity to separate ASD from other delays - which links model design to intended clinical use more explicitly than most of the corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-021" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three groups: 55 videos of children with ASD, 50 with speech and language conditions, and 54 typically developing, totalling 159 videos (150 with complete demographic data). The inclusion of a non-autistic clinical comparison group - and the explicit design of a second classification layer to separate ASD from other developmental delay - directly addresses the specificity question that most ASD-versus-typical studies in this corpus leave open. SLC diagnoses are further broken down: 74% had both receptive and expressive language delay, 12% receptive and expressive language disorder, 10% expressive delay alone, 4% receptive delay alone." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-021" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Author keywords: autism; autism spectrum disorder; machine learning; developmental delays; clinical resources; Bangladesh; Biomedical Data Science. Task vocabulary: 'risk scores', 'reduce-to-practice testing', 'rater-adaptive weighting', 'triage', 'cross-cultural validity', 'health care disparity'. The health-equity and health-systems vocabulary ('clinical resources', 'population-level burden', 'policy change') is distinctive in this corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-021" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Categorical human ratings as features: 31 multiple-choice responses per video per rater. No signal-level representation of the video is used at all. The authors note in future work that a sufficiently large annotated corpus would allow convolutional and recurrent networks to learn features directly from raw video and audio - explicitly positioning the current human-rating approach as a bootstrapping stage." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-021" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Hardware: parents' own mobile phones for video capture - deliberately, since the deployment argument rests on mobile ubiquity. Software: a secure HIPAA-compliant web rating platform; Python 3.6.7 with pandas 0.23.4, scikit-learn 0.20.0 for models, hyperopt 0.1.1 for hyperparameter tuning, shap 0.24.0 for Shapley values, and matplotlib 3.0.1. Version numbers are given throughout, which is better practice than corpus norm." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-021" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Reported by the authors in a dedicated limitations section that diagnoses the transfer gap in three specific ways: the source classifiers were trained on clinical scoresheets rather than features obtained from live video; those scoresheets came from formal clinical assessments of US children and 'do not capture a culturally diverse set of behavioral nuances'; and the classifiers were built to separate typical development from autism whereas this dataset contains other delays including SLCs. They also hypothesise that raters sharing the children's linguistic and sociocultural background would improve performance. Identifiable in review: sex is markedly imbalanced between the ASD and SLC cohorts (72% versus 46% male), directly confounding the second classification layer; family income differs sharply across groups (96% of ASD families above 30,000 taka versus 26% of TD families), as does residence; stunting data are missing entirely for the TD cohort; ADOS covered only 28 of 50 ASD children; the demographic table's gender percentages are inconsistent with their counts; and the senior author founded a company developing paediatric digital health solutions, disclosed as a conflict of interest." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-021" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Among the most thorough in the corpus. Shapley values are computed for all 159 videos in the second layer using the shap package, exploiting local accuracy so each video's predicted probability decomposes exactly into per-feature contributions with both magnitude and direction, and the same rater weights are applied to derive ensemble-level Shapley values. This permits interpretation at the level of an individual child's individual annotation. Separately, features selected most often during elastic-net tuning are compared across the US and Bangladeshi models, yielding the substantive cross-cultural finding that eye contact dominates for Bangladeshi videos while social participation and stereotyped speech dominate for US videos - with a plausible mechanism offered, namely that raters could evaluate language-based behaviours only when they shared the language." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-021" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No data availability statement and no code release. Method reporting is otherwise strong: all software packages with exact versions, the rater-weighting softmax formulation, the Shapley local-accuracy property and its ensemble extension, the elastic-net loss with its two hyperparameters and the grid-search procedure, the balanced class weighting, the three-fold cross-validation split sizes, and demographic breakdowns for both training and test sets. Full mathematical detail is deferred to a multimedia appendix. The pipeline is re-implementable in principle; the videos, being identifiable child media, could not reasonably be released." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-021" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "ASD n=50 (36 male, 72%), mean age 2.51 (0.70) years. SLC n=50 (31 male, 46%), mean age 2.73 (0.51). TD n=50 (23 male, printed as 62% though the count implies 46%), mean age 2.40 (0.59). Ages are close across groups but no matching or comparability testing is reported. Sex is substantially imbalanced between the two clinical groups (72% versus 46% male), which is a material confound for the ASD-versus-SLC layer. The demographic table also shows a marked socioeconomic gradient: 96% of ASD families earned over 30,000 taka versus 80% of SLC and only 26% of TD families, and 100% of ASD and TD families were urban versus 78% of SLC. Very young sample (18 months to 4 years), directly relevant to early detection." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-021" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Brief parent-submitted videos averaging 3 minutes 11 seconds (SD 1 minute 57 seconds), recorded during clinical evaluation visits; each rater scored videos in under approximately 6 minutes based on th" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional development and validation study with a three-class design and stacked classification layers" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder, speech and language conditions, and typical development" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 55 ASD videos (50 children in the demographic table) plus 50 with speech and language conditions; comparison: 54 videos (50 children in the demographic table)" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "A video-based machine-learning screening pipeline developed and validated on US children transferred to Bangladeshi children with substantially reduced but non-trivial performance - AUC 0.75 directly, improved to 0.85 for ASD versus other developmental delay after retraining with a novel rater-adaptive ensemble. The most consequential finding for the field is the size of the cross-cultural drop: t",
                        "Layer one (atypical versus typical among developmentally delayed children): AUC 76% (SD 3%), sensitivity 76% (SD 4%). Layer two (ASD versus other developmental delay): AUC 85% (SD 5%), sensitivity 76% (SD 6%). Previously published US-trained classifiers applied directly to Bangladeshi videos achieved a maximum AUC of 0.75 (SD 0.06), against the 92% accuracy (95% CI 88-97%) those same classifiers a" ;
                    slr:outcome "AUC and sensitivity with standard deviations for both classification layers; AUC with standard deviation for the source-classifier transfer test" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Layer one (atypical versus typical among developmentally delayed children): AUC 76% (SD 3%), sensitivity 76% (SD 4%). Layer two (ASD versus other developmental delay): AUC 85% (SD 5%), sensitivity 76% (SD 6%). Previously published US-trained classifiers applied directly to Bangladeshi videos achieved a maximum AUC of 0.75 (SD 0.06), against the 92% accuracy (95% CI 88-97%) those same classifiers a; A video-based machine-learning screening pipeline developed and validated on US children transferred to Bangladeshi children with substantially reduced but non-trivial performance - AUC 0.75 directly, improved to 0.85 for ASD versus other developmental delay after retraining with a novel rater-adaptive ensemble. The most consequential finding for the field is the size of the cross-cultural drop: t" ] ] ;
