    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "unclear" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "MCHAT plus clinical evaluation by a developmental psychologist and child health physician is a reasonable standard for the setting, but ADOS was completed for only 28 of 50 ASD children, and no equivalent structured instrument underpins the SLC diagnoses that form the layer-two comparator." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "unclear" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Three-fold cross-validation with reported split demographics, balanced class weights and Shapley attribution, but child-level fold partitioning is not explicitly stated, specificity is not reported for either layer, and rater weights are derived from model accuracy on held-out validation data whose relationship to the test folds is not fully specified." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "All 159 videos passed quality inspection and were rated; 9 subjects with missing demographic data were excluded and reported; training and test set demographics are given per layer." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "The two clinical groups compared in layer two differ markedly in sex (72% versus 46% male) and family income (96% versus 80% above 30,000 taka), and the TD group differs from both on income (26%) and residence, so demographic differences are confounded with the classification target." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "some" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<urn:slr:article:61028ef44814> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Autism is a developmental condition primarily identiﬁed by social and communication deﬁcits. However, over 70% of autistic individuals also show motor function deﬁcits, which are evident even when simple stereotyped movements are performed. In this study, we have asked 24 autistic and 22 non-autistic adults to perform pointing movements between two markers 30 cm apart as quickly and as accurately as they can for 10 seconds. Motion tracking was employed to collect data and calculate kinematic features of the movement and aiming accuracy. At the group level, the results showed that autistic individuals performed pointing movements slower but more accurately compared to non-autistic individuals. At the individual level, we have used Machine Learning methods to predict autism diagnosis. Nested result Cross-Validation was used, which in contrast to commonly used K-fold CrossValidation avoids pooling training and testing data and provides robust performance estimates. Our developed models achieved a statistically signiﬁcant classiﬁcation accuracy of 71% and showed that even a simple and short motor task enables discrimination between autistic and non-autistic individuals. I. INTRODUCTION Autism Spectrum Condition (ASC) is a group of complex developmental conditions primarily characterised by social and communication deﬁcits. Stereotyped and repetitive movements are also recognised as a symptom but receive little attention in identifying ASC [1]. Nonetheless, growing research interest for motor impairment in ASC demonstrated that balance, gait, praxis and visuomotor functions are implicated and deﬁcits show large, highly signiﬁcant effect sizes [2]. Even very simple movement tasks like reach to grasp [3], repetitive hand pronation-supination and ﬁnger tapping [4] show differences between ASC and typically developing (TD) individuals. In this study we have asked adult ASC and TD participants to perform a simple and short movement task — to point between two points as quickly and as accurately as they can. The movements were objectively measured using motion tracking and Machine Learning (ML) methods were applied to predict ASC diagnosis. The strength of ML is the ability to ﬁnd complex interactions between multiple variables and this makes it well A. Vabalas and A. J. Casson are with the School of Electrical and Electronic Engineering, The University of Manchester, UK. Email: {andrius.vabalas, alex.casson}@manchester.ac.uk E. Gowen and E Poliakoff are with the School of Biological Sciences, The University of Manchester, UK. Email: {emma.gowen, ellen.poliakoff}@manchester.ac.uk A. Vabalas was supported by the UK Engineering and Physical Sciences Research Council and its Doctoral Training Partnership with the University of Manchester. suited for identiﬁcation of ASC biomarkers as ASC is a complex and heterogeneous condition which can have different expressions between affected individuals. Additionally, the current diagnostic process is long an" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Vabalas A; Gowen E; Poliakoff E; Casson AJ (2019). Kinematic features of a simple and short movement task to predict autism diagnosis. *2019 41st Annual International Conference of the IEEE Engineering i*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Casson AJ",
        "Gowen E",
        "Poliakoff E",
        "Vabalas A" ;
    dcterms:title "Kinematic features of a simple and short movement task to predict autism diagnosis" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_785ba418" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2019 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched?" ;
                            slr:justification "IQ matching claimed and mean ages nearly identical, but no values or tests reported." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "46 adults; 71% accuracy corresponds to roughly 33 of 46 correct." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Group sizes, sex counts and mean ages only; no IQ values despite IQ matching being claimed, no SDs, no ranges, no recruitment route." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "No diagnostic instrument or procedure reported." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "Kinematic features are computed automatically, but 1.4% of data points were replaced with group means before analysis." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Tracker, sensor placement, sampling rate, target geometry, instruction wording, task duration and both hands specified, with the full measure table published." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the task appropriate and minimal-burden for the population?" ;
                            slr:justification "A 10-second pointing task with a single spoken instruction - the least burdensome protocol in the corpus." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is performance variability characterised?" ;
                            slr:justification "Nested cross-validation repeated 100 times with accuracy distributions plotted per pipeline." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is significance against chance tested?" ;
                            slr:justification "1000-permutation testing, with two of four pipelines correctly reported as non-significant." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the full pipeline nested within the validation loop?" ;
                            slr:justification "Normalisation, feature selection and parameter tuning all performed inside each training fold, stated explicitly and justified by citation." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is there external or independent validation?" ;
                            slr:justification "None, but explicitly named as planned future work with newly collected independent data." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample size adequate for the feature dimensionality?" ;
                            slr:justification "60 features against 46 participants, reduced to 10 by the filter methods." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No data availability statement." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "None, though all libraries and parameters are named." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are non-significant and unfavourable results reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Two of four pipelines reported as non-significant with their p-values, and the headline 71% is well below the field's published figures." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the field's methodology critiqued and corrected?" ;
                            slr:justification "Prior 86.7-96.7% results are attributed to K-fold cross-validation on small samples, and nested cross-validation is adopted in response." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Named ethics committee with a specific approval reference number; no consent statement." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2019. The paper explicitly characterises the state of kinematic autism classification: three prior studies using tablet gameplay sensors, reach-and-throw tracking and movement imitation achieved 'high classification accuracy rates of 86.7% to 96.7%', but 'these studies used K-fold Cross-Validation, which was demonstrated to produce overoptimistic performance estimates, especially when a sample size is small' and did not test significance against chance. This is the corpus's most direct statement that its own literature's headline accuracies are methodologically inflated, and the paper's 71% is offered as the corrected estimate for a comparable task." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-025" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'autistic individuals', 'autistic adults', 'ASC participants', 'non-autistic adults', 'non-autistic individuals', 'typically developing (TD) individuals', 'affected individuals'. Predominantly identity-first ('autistic individuals') paired with 'non-autistic' rather than 'healthy' or 'normal', and the paper uses 'Autism Spectrum Condition' rather than 'Disorder' throughout - among the more neurodiversity-aligned terminology in the corpus, consistent with the same Manchester group's other entries here." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-025" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A deliberately minimal task: point between two 8 mm stickers 30 cm apart on a table with the index finger, as quickly and accurately as possible, for 10 seconds, repeated with the dominant and non-dominant hand. The brevity and simplicity are the point - the paper tests whether a task this short can carry diagnostic signal, in contrast to the longer imitation and gameplay protocols elsewhere in the corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-025" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups: 24 autistic adults and 22 non-autistic (typically developing) adults, IQ-matched. Both group sizes and sex counts are reported. No other-diagnosis comparison group." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-025" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No per-feature importance ranking from the final models. Interpretability comes from the group-level behavioural analysis, which reports the five most discriminative measures with means, t-statistics, p-values and Cohen's d, and from averaged velocity and vertical-amplitude profiles plotted across movement progression for both groups. The substantive finding is a speed-accuracy trade-off: autistic participants were slower but roughly twice as spatially precise (30.87 vs 64.01 mm2 scatter area), which is a difference in movement strategy rather than an impairment - a framing that contrasts with most motor studies in this corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-025" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No dedicated limitations section, but the paper's entire framing is a limitation critique aimed at its own field: prior kinematic studies achieving 86.7-96.7% 'used K-fold Cross-Validation, which was demonstrated to produce overoptimistic performance estimates, especially when a sample size is small' and did not assess whether performance differed significantly from random guessing. The conclusion adds that large parametric effect sizes 'do not necessarily translate to high ML classification performance'. Identifiable in review: no diagnostic instrument named; IQ matching claimed without values; no age standard deviations or ranges; adults only; n=46 so 71% is roughly 33 of 46 correct, with no confidence interval reported though accuracy distributions are plotted; the best of four pipelines is highlighted, though all four including the two non-significant ones are reported in full; and outlier replacement with group means (1.4% of data points) uses group information, a minor leakage route not discussed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-025" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Motor behavior: 3D index-finger kinematics during repetitive pointing (velocity, acceleration, deceleration, jerk, amplitude and their timings) together with spatial endpoint accuracy derived from where the finger contacted the table." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-025" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Extend the approach to kinematic data from more complex movement imitation tasks, with preliminary results described as promising - which is exactly what the same group's 2020 Scientific Reports paper in this corpus goes on to do; and validate the models on newly collected independent data to establish robustness, directly addressing the absence of external validation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-025" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The strongest validation design among the motor studies here. Nested 10-fold cross-validation with normalisation, feature selection and hyperparameter tuning all performed inside each training fold, so test data never informs model development; the whole procedure repeated 100 times with random splits to yield performance distributions; and permutation testing with 1000 label shuffles to establish that accuracy differs from chance. The authors cite Varma and Simon on nested cross-validation giving 'almost unbiased' estimates and K-fold producing 'significantly overoptimistic results'. Absent: external or independent validation (explicitly named as future work), any held-out site, class-imbalance handling (24 vs 22 is near-balanced), and any deployment analysis. Outlier replacement with group means is performed before the folds, a small residual leakage path." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-025" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "ASC n=24 (9 female, 37.5%), mean age 31.5 years. TD n=22 (7 female, 31.8%), mean age 30.8 years. Groups are stated to be IQ matched, but no IQ values, no age standard deviations, no age ranges and no statistical comparisons are reported. Female representation is comparatively high for this literature. Adults only, so no early-diagnosis relevance." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-025" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: predict autism diagnosis at the individual level from a short simple motor task, with the stated methodological aim of producing unbiased performance estimates. Main findings: 71% accuracy (75% sensitivity, 66% specificity, p=0.021) with t-test feature selection and 70% with ReliefF, while SVM-RFE (62%) and mRMR (60%) did not reach significance; autistic participants moved more slowly but pointed about twice as accurately; and large group-level effect sizes did not translate into high classification accuracy." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-025" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Handcrafted kinematic summary features: 20 scalar movement measures per pointing movement plus spatial accuracy measures, aggregated to 60 features as means and standard deviations pooled across hands, then z-score normalised. No raw time series, images or learned embeddings." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-025" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Hardware: Polhemus Fastrak electromagnetic motion tracker with a single sensor on the distal phalange of the index finger, sampling X, Y and Z at 120 Hz; two 8 mm red target stickers on a table. Software: scikit-learn for SVM and grid search, scikit-rebate for ReliefF; outlier handling following the Van Selst and Jolicoeur non-recursive procedure." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-025" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, sensitivity, specificity and a permutation p-value for each of the four pipelines, plus accuracy distributions across the 100 nested cross-validation repetitions and confusion matrices for all four models. Reporting non-significant results with their p-values (0.113 and 0.148) alongside significant ones is rare in this corpus. No AUC or confidence intervals, though the plotted distributions convey spread." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-025" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Supervised learning. Algorithm: SVM with RBF kernel, with C and gamma grid-searched. Four feature-selection strategies are compared as complete pipelines: SVM recursive feature elimination (embedded), two-sample t-test (filter), ReliefF (filter with neighbour interactions) and mRMR (filter with redundancy penalty). The finding that the two simplest filters outperformed the two more sophisticated methods is reported without embellishment." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-025" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Laboratory setting at a UK university. Newly collected data using motion tracking; no existing dataset used." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-025" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "IQ matching is claimed but no IQ values are reported for either group, and no language, adaptive-functioning or autism-trait measures are given. Comorbidities are not described. This is unusually sparse given that the paper is otherwise meticulous about its analysis." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-025" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; a single session of a few seconds of task data per hand, with no follow-up. Data are numeric 3D position traces, not identifiable media, so sensitivity is low. Ethics approval from the University of Manchester research ethics committee is reported with the specific reference number (2017-2541-4204); no consent statement or anonymization procedure is separately described." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-025" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Single modality, but with two distinct feature families combined - movement kinematics and endpoint accuracy - which is what allows the speed-accuracy trade-off to emerge. Features from dominant and non-dominant hands are pooled into means and standard deviations, a form of within-modality aggregation. No integration with clinical, cognitive or demographic data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-025" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No formal keyword list in the extracted text. The paper's vocabulary: 'predict autism diagnosis', 'ASC biomarkers', 'automated algorithms assisting with ASC identification', 'nested Cross-Validation', 'overoptimistic performance estimates', 'statistically significantly different from random guessing'. The validation vocabulary is unusually prominent and is effectively the paper's contribution." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-025" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Not reported. Participants are described simply as autistic adults with no diagnostic instrument, criteria, clinician or verification procedure named, and control status is asserted without screening. Given that diagnosis is the prediction target, this is the study's principal reporting gap - though the same research group's companion 2020 paper documents its ascertainment more fully." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-025" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The 41st Annual International Conference of the IEEE Engineering in Medicine and Biology Society (EMBC) 2019 - a biomedical engineering venue, technology-focused but clinically adjacent, not an autism-specific journal." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-025" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No data or code release and no random seeds, but the analytic pipeline is described in enough detail to reimplement: the full 20-measure table, the accuracy-feature definitions, the outlier-removal procedure and its citation, z-score normalisation, the four feature-selection methods with their parameters (ReliefF K=23, top 10 features for the filter methods), the SVM kernel and grid-searched parameters, the named libraries, the nested cross-validation structure with 10 folds repeated 100 times, and the 1000-permutation significance test. Analytically this is one of the more reproducible descriptions in the corpus; empirically it is not reproducible without the data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-025" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Single session; 10 seconds of continuous pointing per hand, performed with both dominant and non-dominant hands" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional case-control classification study" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum condition, adults" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 24 (9 female); comparison: 22 (7 female)" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "A 10-second pointing task discriminated autistic from non-autistic adults at 71% accuracy under nested cross-validation with permutation-tested significance. Autistic participants moved more slowly but pointed substantially more accurately - roughly half the spatial scatter - which is a speed-accuracy trade-off rather than a deficit. The paper's central methodological point is that group-level dif",
                        "t-test feature selection: 71% accuracy, 75% sensitivity, 66% specificity, p = 0.021. ReliefF: 70%, 72%, 68%, p = 0.027. SVM-RFE: 62%, 65%, 59%, p = 0.113 (not significant). mRMR: 60%, 64%, 55%, p = 0.148 (not significant). Behavioural group differences were significant with medium-to-large effect sizes (Cohen's d 0.69 to 0.82)" ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy, sensitivity, specificity and a permutation-derived p-value for every one of the four pipelines, plus accuracy distributions and confusion matrices for all four" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "t-test feature selection: 71% accuracy, 75% sensitivity, 66% specificity, p = 0.021. ReliefF: 70%, 72%, 68%, p = 0.027. SVM-RFE: 62%, 65%, 59%, p = 0.113 (not significant). mRMR: 60%, 64%, 55%, p = 0.148 (not significant). Behavioural group differences were significant with medium-to-large effect sizes (Cohen's d 0.69 to 0.82); A 10-second pointing task discriminated autistic from non-autistic adults at 71% accuracy under nested cross-validation with permutation-tested significance. Autistic participants moved more slowly but pointed substantially more accurately - roughly half the spatial scatter - which is a speed-accuracy trade-off rather than a deficit. The paper's central methodological point is that group-level dif" ] ] ;
