<urn:slr:article:a21f317ce506> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Since the diagnosis of autism spectrum disorder (ASD) relies heavily on behavioral observations by experienced clinician, we seek to investigate whether parts of this job can be autonomously performed by a humanoid robot using only sensors available on-board. To that end, we developed a robot-assisted ASD diagnostic protocol. In this work we propose the Partially observable Markov decision process (POMDP) framework for such protocol which enables the robot to infer information about the state of the child based on observations of child’s behavior. We extend our previous work by developing a protocol POMDP model which uses tasks of the protocol as actions. We devise a method to interface protocol and task models by using belief at the end of a task to generate observations for the protocol POMDP, resulting in a hierarchical POMDP framework. We evaluate our approach through an exploratory study with ﬁfteen children (seven typically developing and eight with ASD)." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Petric F; Kovacic Z (2019). Hierarchical POMDP Framework for a Robot-Assisted ASD Diagnostic Protocol. *2019 14th ACM/IEEE International Conference on Human-Robot Interaction (HRI). IEEE*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Kovacic Z",
        "Petric F" ;
    dcterms:title "Hierarchical POMDP Framework for a Robot-Assisted ASD Diagnostic Protocol" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_9528024b" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2019 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "15 children, with the ASD group all in daily intervention and habituated to similar tasks." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched?" ;
                            slr:justification "Mental-age matching claimed with no values or test; gender explicitly not considered." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Group sizes only; no ages, no genders, no recruitment route, no diagnostic route." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Room layout, robot placement, task structure and recording arrangement are described, but social cue coding criteria and the offline video analysis procedure are not." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the elicitation protocol standardised?" ;
                            slr:justification "Four ADOS-derived tasks each performed exactly once, with a therapist ensuring imitation demonstrations always succeeded." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "No diagnostic instrument; the comparator is post-hoc expert judgement of de-identified transcripts." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "A human operator supplied critical observations in real time during sessions, and remaining observations were coded offline from video by the research team." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is implausible model confidence addressed?" ;
                            slr:justification "The authors deliberately introduced confidence scaling at 0.8 after judging that four tasks producing near-certain belief was invalid given ADOS's scale." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the appropriateness of the method itself questioned?" ;
                            slr:justification "The authors concede that with four tasks the robot makes only three decisions, which 'does not necessarily warrant a use of POMDP'." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is any held-out or cross-validated evaluation performed?" ;
                            slr:justification "None; belief outcomes are compared qualitatively against expert behavioural categories." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are model parameters estimated from data?" ;
                            slr:justification "Observation probabilities come from a clinician survey; transition matrices are identity by assumption." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a quantitative performance metric reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No accuracy, sensitivity, specificity or AUC anywhere." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are divergences between diagnosis and observed behaviour reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "The authors report plainly that typically developing children sometimes behaved autistically and vice versa, and restructured their analysis around behaviour as a result." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are session failures and attrition reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Robot failures, children's refusal and four terminated sessions are disclosed narratively but not accounted for per child." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No data availability statement." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code or the model specification available?" ;
                            slr:justification "No code, but the POMDP state space, partitioning scheme, full observation probability matrices, scaling equation and reward rules are published in sufficient detail to reimplement." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics, consent and privacy handling reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No ethics approval, no parental consent statement and no data-protection provision, despite two-way-mirror and onboard-camera video of preschool children." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Four ADOS-derived tasks, each performed exactly once as in ADOS: response to name call (positive if eye contact detected), joint attention (transferring attention to a second robot that activates its lights, verified by eye contact), play request (eliciting vocalisations and eye contact coordinated with hand and body gestures to test simultaneous multi-channel communication), and functional imitation with real objects - a toy frog and a cup - which the authors distinguish from the simple motoric imitation used in comparable work. In every task the robot first performs an action intended to provoke a child response. A therapist placed objects into the robot's hand and triggered demonstrations via head tactile sensors, specifically to eliminate failed grasps and guarantee the imitation always occurred." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-023" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Hardware: two NAO humanoid robots, one facing the child with its back to the two-way mirror and one on the floor to its left with lights off except during the joint attention task; a high-definition camera behind the two-way mirror; an overhead microphone in the examination room; the robot's onboard camera. Software: hierarchical POMDP models solved with the SARSOP point-based solver, with protocol and task controllers and the operator interface running on a remote computer." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-023" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Reported by the authors, with notable candour: the sample size 'is not large'; because each task runs once the robot makes only three decisions, which 'does not necessarily warrant a use of POMDP'; social cue detection algorithms are 'not accurate enough for fully autonomous execution' so the robot is only semi-autonomous with an operator supplying critical observations; not all children completed all tasks, some because of robot failures and some through fear or refusal; in four cases robot failure ended the session because children would not continue; and crucially, typically developing children 'sometimes exhibit autistic behavior, and vice versa', which the authors attribute to the ASD group being in daily intervention and unafraid of the robot while typically developing children were shy and wary. Identifiable in review: no ages, genders or diagnostic instrument; no ethics or consent statement despite two-way-mirror video of preschoolers; observation probabilities elicited by clinician survey rather than estimated from data; no quantitative performance metric whatsoever; and the confidence-scaling parameter was tuned until 'more appropriate' belief values emerged, which is a judgement call rather than a fitted value." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-023" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2019. The paper positions itself as 'one of the first applications of humanoid robots in the diagnostics procedure', distinguishing diagnostic from the much commoner therapeutic robot applications and noting that diagnostic use 'brings forth some additional requirements and challenges, especially in terms of data acquisition and reasoning'. It observes that the complexity of the diagnostic process 'imposes constraints on the robotic applications in form of the need for efficient processing and reasoning algorithms', which results in a 'low number of robotic applications in ASD diagnosis'. It also documents that most robot-autism research still uses 'some form of Wizard-of-Oz approach with various degrees of autonomy', and that observations are typically 'collected through analysis of video recordings, which is a common technique in this field' - an accurate characterisation of the robot strand of this corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-023" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Not machine learning in the supervised sense. The method is probabilistic sequential decision-making: a hierarchical POMDP with task-level and protocol-level models, solved by the SARSOP point-based value iteration solver. Model parameters are specified rather than learned - transition matrices for child states are identity (robot actions cannot change the child's condition, and no data existed on their effect on engagement or verbal activity), and observation probabilities were elicited from a clinician survey. The authors themselves note that comparison against trained classifiers is not yet possible for lack of data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-023" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Multiple cue types are combined within each task's observation set and jointly update a shared belief over child condition, engagement and verbal activity - so eye contact, utterances, imitation and gestures fuse probabilistically into one latent state estimate. The hierarchical structure adds a second fusion level: each task's terminal belief is discretised into a partition index that becomes an observation for the protocol-level model, so information accumulates across tasks. This belief-space-partitioning interface is the paper's central technical contribution and is a genuinely different fusion mechanism from the feature concatenation used everywhere else in the corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-023" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Index terms: POMDP; NAO; robot-assisted; autism; diagnostics. Task vocabulary: 'robot-assisted ASD diagnostic protocol', 'belief state', 'social cues', 'semi-autonomous', 'Wizard-of-Oz'. The decision-theoretic vocabulary (belief, policy, reward, observation probability) is unique in this corpus and reflects a robotics rather than pattern-recognition framing." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-023" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; a single session per child, with repeated sessions deliberately avoided to prevent learned behaviours. The data are video and audio of identifiable preschool children, recorded both by the robot's onboard camera and by a high-definition camera concealed behind a two-way mirror - among the most privacy-sensitive arrangements in the corpus. There is no ethics approval statement, no parental consent statement and no anonymization or data-protection provision anywhere in the paper. The only privacy-adjacent measure is that transcripts shared with the expert assessors omitted child identification." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-023" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups: 8 preschool children already diagnosed with ASD and 7 typically developing children, total 15. Group sizes are reported. No other-diagnosis group. Notably the analysis is ultimately regrouped by expert-assessed behaviour rather than by diagnosis, producing four categories (ASD-like behaviour n=5, typical behaviour n=3, ambiguous n=4, session too short n=3) that cut across the original diagnostic grouping." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-023" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Behaviour is represented as discrete observation symbols drawn from a defined set per task, and the child is represented as a probability distribution (belief) over a factored latent state space of condition, engagement and verbal activity. Between hierarchy levels, a continuous belief simplex is discretised into 16 partitions whose centroids define observation probabilities. There are no feature vectors, embeddings or time series." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-023" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None reported. No cognitive, linguistic or adaptive measures, no severity instrument, and no comorbidity information. Mental age is used for matching but its values are not given. The one clinically relevant characteristic reported is that all ASD-group children were undergoing intervention and trained on similar tasks almost daily - which the authors correctly identify as a confound affecting their behaviour toward the robot." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-023" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "There is no validation in the machine-learning sense - no cross-validation, no held-out data, no independent test set. Robustness is addressed through two design decisions instead. First, the authors noticed that four tasks were driving belief close to certainty and judged this implausible given ADOS uses five modules of at least ten tasks each, so they introduced a confidence-scaling transformation and set it to 0.8 to deliberately reduce the information each task contributes - actively degrading apparent performance on principled grounds. Second, they validated against expert-assessed behaviour rather than diagnostic label, because they had observed the two diverging in their sample. Neither is a generalisation estimate. There is no external validation, no bias analysis, no class-imbalance consideration, and the system is not autonomous, so deployment readiness is explicitly not established." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-023" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Not reported. Children are described as 'already diagnosed with ASD' with no instrument, criteria, clinician or date, and typically developing status is asserted without screening. The protocol tasks are modelled on ADOS, but ADOS itself was not administered as a reference standard. Instead, the validation comparator is a post-hoc expert judgement of behaviour from de-identified transcripts - which is a reasonable choice given the authors' observation that behaviour and diagnosis diverged, but it means no gold-standard diagnostic anchor exists in the study." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-023" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The 14th ACM/IEEE International Conference on Human-Robot Interaction (HRI) 2019 - a robotics and human-robot interaction venue, not an autism, clinical or machine-learning venue." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-023" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: enable a humanoid robot to reason about a child's unobservable state during an ADOS-derived diagnostic protocol and to select tasks adaptively, addressing the reported problem of inter-clinician disagreement arising from the need to simultaneously observe, code and interpret many behaviours. Main findings: belief distributions separated expert-assessed ASD behaviour clearly and typical behaviour less decisively; behaviour experts judged ambiguous was assigned to high-functioning ASD; and sessions too short for expert assessment produced near-uniform beliefs (approximately 0.27-0.43), correctly signalling insufficient information. No accuracy figure is reported." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-023" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Run more clinical sessions to obtain data on child engagement and verbal activity during robot-administered tasks, which would enable richer reward models, permit evaluation of the robot's task-selection policy, and allow belief-based classification to be compared against conventional classifiers that currently cannot be trained for lack of data; continue developing social cue detection toward full autonomy; increase the number of protocol tasks; and investigate whether children behave differently when diagnostic tasks are administered by a robot rather than a human examiner - a question that bears directly on whether robot-administered protocols measure the same construct as clinician-administered ones." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-023" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Social-interaction behaviour observed as discrete cues: eye contact, vocalisations, imitation and gestures, each tracked in specified tasks. These are categorical behavioural events rather than continuously sensed signals, and in this study they were largely human-coded (in real time by the operator for critical observations, offline from video for the rest) rather than automatically detected." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-023" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The model is inherently interpretable in structure - beliefs over named clinical states, observations tied to specific social cues, and reward rules stated in plain language (penalise name-call if the child is already engaged; penalise joint attention if the child is not engaged; reward play request if disengaged; reward imitation if engaged). But no analysis identifies which cues or tasks were most informative, and the observation probabilities encode clinician priors rather than empirical relationships, so nothing about autistic behaviour is learned from the data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-023" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with ASD', 'people with ASD', 'child with low-functioning ASD', 'child with high-functioning ASD', 'typically developing children'. Person-first throughout, with the paper framing its motivation in terms of 'successful inclusion of people with ASD into the society'. The POMDP state labels (sLA, sHA, sNA for low-functioning, high-functioning and typically developing) formalise a three-level functioning taxonomy as latent model states." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-023" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, precision, F1 or AUC is reported, and no confusion matrix. The outputs are belief distributions over three latent states, plotted as frequency histograms per expert-assigned behavioural category. This means the study contributes no diagnostic accuracy estimate, and it should be excluded from any quantitative synthesis of performance - a point worth recording explicitly since its framing as a diagnostic protocol might otherwise invite inclusion." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-023" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Laboratory setting - a purpose-built examination and observation suite with a two-way mirror, fixed camera position and overhead microphone, with a therapist in the room and a second therapist plus robot operator behind the mirror. Newly collected session data; no existing dataset used." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-023" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No data or code release. The model specification is however documented in unusual detail: the state factorisation, the belief-space partitioning scheme (16 partitions over the condition simplex, 3 each over engagement and verbal activity), the full 3x16 observation probability matrix for the condition states, the 2x3 matrices for engagement and verbal activity, the centroid-based procedure for deriving those probabilities, the confidence-scaling equation with its chosen value of 0.8, the reward-shaping rules, and the solver used. The model is therefore reimplementable; the sessions are not reproducible, and the clinician survey that supplied the underlying observation rates is not documented." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-023" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Only group sizes are reported: 8 ASD and 7 typically developing preschool children, matched by mental age. No chronological ages, no age ranges, no standard deviations, and gender is explicitly disregarded - 'Gender was not considered'. Mental-age matching is claimed but no values or comparison are given. The research targets ages 2-6, so the sample is directly relevant to early diagnosis, but nothing about its composition can be verified." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-023" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Single session per child; four tasks each performed exactly once, mirroring ADOS structure. No repeated sessions were held, deliberately, to avoid learned behaviours" ;
                    slr:study_design "Exploratory proof-of-concept study evaluating belief-state outcomes from robot-administered sessions, with no formal classification evaluation" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder, preschool" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 8; comparison: 7" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "A hierarchical POMDP allowed a semi-autonomous humanoid robot to maintain and update a belief over whether a child was typically developing, high-functioning autistic or low-functioning autistic across an ADOS-derived four-task protocol, and to select the next task based on inferred engagement. Across 15 preschool children the resulting beliefs corresponded reasonably to independent expert judgeme",
                        "Experts classified the 15 sessions as: 5 children showing ASD-characteristic behaviour, 3 showing typically developing behaviour, 4 ambiguous, and 3 sessions too short for meaningful assessment. The framework's belief distributions separated the ASD-behaviour group clearly, separated the typical-development group less decisively with overlap between high-functioning-ASD and typical beliefs, assign" ;
                    slr:outcome "None in the classification sense. Belief distributions over the three child-condition states are plotted per expert-assigned behavioural category; no accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, AUC or confusion matrix is reported" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Experts classified the 15 sessions as: 5 children showing ASD-characteristic behaviour, 3 showing typically developing behaviour, 4 ambiguous, and 3 sessions too short for meaningful assessment. The framework's belief distributions separated the ASD-behaviour group clearly, separated the typical-development group less decisively with overlap between high-functioning-ASD and typical beliefs, assign; A hierarchical POMDP allowed a semi-autonomous humanoid robot to maintain and update a belief over whether a child was typically developing, high-functioning autistic or low-functioning autistic across an ADOS-derived four-task protocol, and to select the next task based on inferred engagement. Across 15 preschool children the resulting beliefs corresponded reasonably to independent expert judgeme" ] ] ;
