    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Labels inherited from a public image collection with no diagnostic instrument, criterion, assessor or date reported, and no summary of the originating study's ascertainment." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Single image-level split on a dataset with multiple images per child, so images from the same child appear on both sides. The test set holds 55 images, a figure the paper never states, and four architectures trained identically span 78% to 100% on it." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "No participants. Images from an unnamed public repository with no accession, no recruitment, no eligibility, no demographics and no statement of how many children contributed the 547 scanpaths; the abstract additionally reports image counts as child counts." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "unclear" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Four transfer-learning classifiers with fully tabulated hyperparameters, and augmentation correctly confined to training after the split. However, rotation and flipping of scanpath images produce traces that correspond to no possible viewing behaviour, and no ablation isolates the hybrid model." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "high" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<urn:slr:article:bf28de6e32db> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Individuals with Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) have been shown to have atypical scanning patterns during face and emotion perception. While previous studies characterized ASD using eye-tracking data, this study examined whether the use of eye movements combined with task performance in facial emotion recognition could be helpful to identify individuals with ASD. We tested 23 subjects with ASD and 35 controls using a Dynamic Affect Recognition Evaluation (DARE) task that requires an individual to recognize one of six emotions (i.e., anger, disgust, fear, happiness, sadness, and surprise) while observing a slowly transitioning face video. We observed differences in response time and eye movements, but not in the recognition accuracy. Based on these observations, we proposed a machine learning method to distinguish between individuals with ASD and typically developing (TD) controls. The proposed method classiﬁes eye ﬁxations based on a comprehensive set of features that integrate task performance, gaze information, and face features extracted using a deep neural network. It achieved an 86% classiﬁcation accuracy that is comparable with the standardized diagnostic scales, with advantages of efﬁciency and objectiveness. Feature visualization and interpretations were further carried out to reveal distinguishing features between the two subject groups and to understand the social and attentional deﬁcits in ASD. I. INTRODUCTION Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) is a broad spectrum neurodevelopmental disorder characterized by difﬁculties in social interaction and communication, as well as restricted, repetitive, and stereotyped behaviors and interests [1]. As a core deﬁcit, social impairment is one of the most studied aspects of ASD [2]. It is theorized that atypical viewing of socially relevant stimuli may contribute to these deﬁcits. The addition of eye-tracking technologies and techniques within the ﬁeld has dramatically enhanced our ability to investigate atypical visual attention in ASD objectively. Starting in infancy, the high saliency and importance of social information processing and emotion recognition can be observed [3]. Indeed, atypical visual processing was noted in toddlers (10–49 months) with ASD [4]. Similar ﬁndings that individuals with ASD dwelled longer on nonsocial versus social stimuli was also noted in adolescents with the disorder [5]. As noted in a systematic review by Black et al. [6], results have varied across studies. Previous research has reported decreased gazing upon the eye region either with or without an increase in attention towards 1M. Jiang and Q. Zhao are with the Department of Computer Science and Engineering, University of Minnesota, Minneapolis, MN. 2S. M. Francis, D. Srishyla, C. Conelea, and S. Jacob are with the Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences, University of Minnesota, Minneapolis, MN. *Q. Zhao and S. Jacob contributed equally to this paper. other facial regions, such as the mouth [7]–[11]. In" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Jiang M; Francis SM; Srishyla D; Conelea C; Zhao Q; Jacob S (2019). Classifying Individuals with ASD Through Facial Emotion Recognition and Eye-Tracking. *2019 41st Annual International Conference of*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Conelea C",
        "Francis SM",
        "Jacob S",
        "Jiang M",
        "Srishyla D",
        "Zhao Q" ;
    dcterms:title "Classifying Individuals with ASD Through Facial Emotion Recognition and Eye-Tracking" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_96c87d36" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2019 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Recruitment sources, group sizes, ages, sexes, IQ range and control exclusion criteria all reported, with group comparability formally tested." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "Age and sex differences tested as non-significant, but control age SD is more than double the ASD SD and IQ is not controlled despite being flagged as an influence in the paper's own introduction." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "58 recruited (53 analysed) spanning ages 8-34, with the control range far exceeding the ASD range." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Eye trackers, sampling rates, calibration procedure and error tolerance, displays, viewing distance, stimulus source, resolution and trial structure all specified." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "Multi-source diagnostic ascertainment confirmed by clinical research staff." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "Gaze, task and OpenFace features are all computed automatically, with no manual area-of-interest annotation." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the measurement apparatus consistent across groups?" ;
                            slr:justification "All ASD subjects recorded at 300 Hz, 29 of 35 controls at 60 Hz - a group-confounded apparatus difference affecting the model's most important features." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is class imbalance handled?" ;
                            slr:justification "Balanced class weights used explicitly to offset the 23-versus-35 group sizes." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the validation subject-independent?" ;
                            slr:justification "Leave-one-subject-out with each subject tested once." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample size adequate for the feature dimensionality?" ;
                            slr:justification "648 features per fixation against 53 analysed subjects, mitigated by fixation-level sampling but not by subject-level independence." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is there external or independent validation?" ;
                            slr:justification "None, despite multi-site collection that would have permitted a held-out site." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the contribution of each component quantified?" ;
                            slr:justification "Every feature family evaluated separately and combined, at fixation, trial and subject level, in a single comparable table." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No data availability statement." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are null and dissociating results reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Equal recognition accuracy between groups is reported prominently rather than suppressed, and task features' low standalone sensitivity is shown explicitly." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are limitations and confounds discussed?" ;
                            slr:justification "No limitations section; the two-eye-tracker confound is dismissed in one sentence without analysis, and the group-only exclusion of five ASD subjects is not examined." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is feature importance derived from the model and interpreted?" ;
                            slr:justification "Random-forest importances averaged across folds, face-feature channels visualised anatomically, and independent pixel-wise fixation-density testing performed." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "None, though libraries, network layer and hyperparameter choices are specified." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "IRB approval stated for all recruitment; no consent statement or competing-interests declaration." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "AUC, accuracy, sensitivity and specificity, reported for every feature family at every unit of analysis - a 3x4 results table that is among the most complete in the corpus and permits direct assessment of what each component contributes. Headline subject-level combined: AUC 0.935, accuracy 86.2%, sensitivity 91.3%, specificity 82.9%. No confidence intervals or confusion matrices. The printed formulas define sensitivity as TN/(TN+FP) and specificity as TP/(TP+FN), which reverses the conventional definitions and is presumably a transcription error, though it leaves the labelling of the reported values formally ambiguous." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-020" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups: 23 individuals with ASD (5 later excluded for eye-tracking failure) and 35 typically developing controls, total 58 recruited. Group sizes, sexes and ages are reported, and control status is defined by explicit exclusions. No other-diagnosis comparison group, though the discussion proposes extending the paradigm to schizophrenia and ADHD." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-020" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Diagnosis based on testing with standardized instruments, review of diagnostic history and prior evaluations, and DSM criteria confirmed by clinical research staff - a multi-source ascertainment described in more procedural detail than most corpus studies, though the specific instruments are not named. Control status established by requiring no psychiatric history, no developmental delay and no previous special education." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-020" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No data availability statement and no code release. Method reporting is otherwise detailed: eye tracker models and sampling rates, calibration procedure and error tolerance, display specifications, viewing distance, stimulus source and resolution, trial counts, feature definitions and dimensionalities, the exact OpenFace layer used (inception-4a, 640 channels at 6x6), the Gaussian kernel for fixation density maps (sigma=15 pixels), balanced class weighting, and the cross-validation scheme. The pipeline is re-implementable in principle; the data are not accessible." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-020" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The Dynamic Affect Recognition Evaluation task: silent videos of a face transitioning slowly from neutral into one of six emotions, with the participant pressing the spacebar upon recognition and then selecting the emotion from six labels. Two practice trials (two choices) preceded twelve test trials (six choices), with practice videos not reused. Videos lasted 19-33 seconds. The design yields both a behavioural measure (how quickly recognition occurs, and whether it is correct) and a gaze measure over the same stimulus - which is what enables the accuracy-versus-latency dissociation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-020" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The 41st Annual International Conference of the IEEE Engineering in Medicine and Biology Society (EMBC) 2019 - a biomedical engineering venue, technology-focused but clinically adjacent." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-020" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Mixed clinic and community setting - recruitment spanned university clinics, websites, registries, local advertising and the 2015 Minnesota State Fair, with testing at more than one location using different displays and different eye trackers. Newly collected data; the face stimuli come from the existing Cohn-Kanade database." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-020" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; a single ~10-minute session with no follow-up. Data are gaze coordinates and button-press latencies over third-party stimulus faces, so participant-identifiable content is minimal. Institutional Review Board approval from the University of Minnesota is reported for all recruitment. No participant or parental consent statement, no anonymization procedure and no competing-interests declaration appear." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-020" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: classify ASD from combined task performance and eye movements during facial emotion recognition, and interpret the features to understand social and attentional differences. Main findings: recognition accuracy did not differ between groups (77.89% vs 77.14%, p=0.841) but response time and every eye-movement measure did; combined features gave subject-level AUC 0.935, accuracy 86.2%, sensitivity 91.3%, specificity 82.9%; task features alone had only 56.5% sensitivity, rising to 91.3% when gaze and face features were added." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-020" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No formal keyword list in the extracted text. Vocabulary: 'atypical scanning patterns', 'atypical visual attention', 'social and attentional deficits', 'facial emotion recognition', 'Dynamic Affect Recognition Evaluation', 'comparable with the standardized diagnostic scales', 'efficiency and objectiveness'. The explicit benchmarking of classifier performance against standardised diagnostic scales is a distinctive framing." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-020" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Leave-one-subject-out cross-validation repeated so each of the subjects is tested once, with balanced class weights addressing the 23-versus-35 imbalance, and results reported at three aggregation levels so the effect of the decision unit is visible rather than hidden. What is absent: external or independent validation, any held-out site despite multi-site collection, and any analysis of the apparatus confound. Most seriously, generalisability is undermined rather than tested by the design - the eye tracker is confounded with diagnostic group, and the model's most important features are sampling-rate-sensitive. No calibration analysis, no real-time or deployment claim beyond noting efficiency advantages over standardized diagnostic scales." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-020" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2019. The paper surveys the eye-tracking-plus-machine-learning literature specifically, citing SVM classification of children from face-recognition scanpaths, deep-learning extraction of fixated-region features achieving 92% in adults, and gaze-plus-demographics classification across ASD risk levels. Its critical observation is that 'these studies only considered the eye-tracking data, mainly what was looked at, without jointly taking into account the task performance', which it attributes to behavioural tasks being 'general but not specific enough' - identifying the integration of task performance with gaze as the field's next step. It also cites a systematic review noting that eye-tracking results have varied across studies and are sensitive to sample size, age, developmental level, IQ and stimulus variation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-020" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No dedicated limitations section. The introduction acknowledges at field level that eye-tracking results 'can be impacted by sample size, age, developmental level and IQ, variation in stimuli, and task effort and objective', but the study does not then control for these. Identifiable in review: the two eye trackers are confounded with group - all ASD subjects used the 300 Hz TX300 while 29 of 35 controls used the 60 Hz X2-60 - and the most important model features (fixation frequency, fixation duration, fixation time, saccade amplitude) are exactly the measures a fivefold sampling-rate difference would bias, yet the authors dismiss this in one sentence; five ASD subjects were excluded for eye-tracking capture failure with no controls excluded, a group-dependent attrition that could itself reflect the behaviour being measured; control ages extend to 34 versus an ASD maximum of 17, with a control SD more than double the ASD SD; IQ is reported only as an ASD range and never controlled; two display sizes were used; and the reported equations define sensitivity in terms of TN and specificity in terms of TP, inverting the standard definitions." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-020" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Well developed and multi-layered. Random-forest importance values are averaged across all cross-validation runs and normalised, showing that coarse temporal measures (relative response time, fixation time, fixation frequency, response time) dominate while fine-grained spatial fixation statistics matter less. Face-feature channels are visualised by averaging network activations across frames and overlaying them on an average face, revealing that the informative channels correspond to distinct anatomical regions (forehead, eyes, cheeks, nose, ears). Separately, pixel-wise fixation density maps with t-tests localise where the groups differ - controls more on eyes and mouth, ASD subjects more on forehead, hair, ears and chin. This combination of model-derived importance, activation visualisation and independent statistical mapping is the most thorough interpretability treatment charted so far." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-020" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'individuals with Autism Spectrum Disorder', 'individuals with ASD', 'subjects with ASD', 'people with ASD', 'the ASD group', 'typically developing (TD) controls', 'patients with schizophrenia or ADHD'. Consistently person-first, with 'typically developing' rather than 'normal' or 'healthy' for the comparison group - and notably the paper frames its findings as revealing 'social and attentional deficits', a deficit framing, while simultaneously reporting that emotion recognition accuracy was unimpaired." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-020" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Hardware: two Tobii Pro eye trackers - the TX300 at 300 Hz for all ASD subjects and six controls, and the X2-60 at 60 Hz for the remaining controls - with Tobii Studio 3.3.2; two display configurations (19-inch 1680x1050 and 27-inch 1920x1080) depending on collection site; viewing distance approximately 65 cm (camera range 50-70 cm); 9-point calibration with error below 0.5 degrees for every subject. Software: OpenFace pre-trained on LFW for face features, Viola-Jones for face detection, scikit-learn for random forests." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-020" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "ASD n=23 (20 male, 87%), age 12.74 +/- 2.45 years, range 8-17, IQ range 58-137. TD n=35 (25 male, 71%), age 14.11 +/- 5.09 years, range 8-34. Neither age nor sex was matched by design, but both differences were tested and found non-significant (p=0.241 and p=0.171). The control age range nonetheless extends to 34 while no autistic participant exceeded 17, so the groups differ substantially in composition even though means do not differ significantly - the control SD is more than double the ASD SD. IQ is given only as a range for the ASD group with no control values and no use as a covariate." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-020" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Hybrid representation: handcrafted scalar features for task performance and gaze dynamics, combined with a 640-dimensional learned embedding of the fixated face region taken from an intermediate convolutional layer. This is a distinctive design in the corpus - it replaces manually annotated areas of interest with a learned encoding of stimulus content at the point of gaze, which the authors explicitly present as avoiding reliance on annotated AOIs." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-020" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Feature-level fusion across the three families into a single per-fixation vector, and the contribution of each is ablated separately at fixation, trial and subject level - so the complementarity claim is directly evidenced. The clearest demonstration is that task features alone reach 81.0% subject-level accuracy but only 56.5% sensitivity, while adding gaze and face features raises sensitivity to 91.3% at similar accuracy. Predictions are additionally fused across observations by soft voting from fixations to trials to subjects, with accuracy rising monotonically (72.5% to 75.6% to 86.2%) as the aggregation unit widens." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-020" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three streams: gaze (fixation location, timing, duration, frequency, saccade amplitude), behavioural task performance (response latency and recognition accuracy), and the visual content of what was fixated (deep embeddings of the face region at each fixation). The third is unusual - most corpus gaze studies use predefined anatomical areas of interest, whereas here the stimulus content at the point of gaze is encoded by a deep network without any manual region annotation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-020" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Extend to more subjects across a wider span of developmental ages; build a multimodal classifier incorporating demographic information, autonomic nervous system measures, functional MRI and other methodologies; and apply the same paradigm to schizophrenia and ADHD, which also show altered gaze patterns in visual tasks - a differential-diagnosis direction that addresses the specificity question left open by ASD-versus-TD designs." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-020" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Supervised learning: random forest with bootstrap sampling, majority voting and balanced class weights. A pre-trained deep convolutional network (OpenFace, 37 layers in 8 Inception blocks) is used upstream as a frozen feature extractor without fine-tuning, on the stated grounds that the LFW training set is sufficiently general." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-020" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "IQ score range (58-137) reported for the ASD group only, with no mean, SD or control comparison, and not used as a covariate despite the authors' own introduction noting that eye-tracking results 'can be impacted by sample size, age, developmental level and IQ'. No language or adaptive measures. Comorbidities not described for the ASD group; controls screened by exclusion of psychiatric history, developmental delay and special education." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-020" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Single session; the entire protocol (two practice trials and twelve test trials) lasted approximately 10 minutes, with individual videos of 19-33 seconds (mean 23.33 +/- 3.75 s)" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional case-control classification study with a preceding statistical analysis of group differences" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 23 recruited (20 male); data from 5 subjects with ASD excluded for failure to capture eye movements in at least six trials; comparison: 35 (25 male)" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Individuals with ASD recognised facial emotions just as accurately as controls but took significantly longer and moved their eyes differently - fewer, longer fixations with larger saccades, and more fixations on regions irrelevant to emotion (forehead, hair, ears, chin) rather than eyes and mouth. Combining task performance, gaze dynamics and deep features of the fixated face regions classified su",
                        "Subject level with combined features: AUC 0.935, accuracy 86.2%, sensitivity 91.3%, specificity 82.9%. Subject level by family: task alone AUC 0.789 / accuracy 81.0% but sensitivity only 56.5%; gaze alone AUC 0.904 / 84.5%; face alone AUC 0.917 / 84.5%. Trial level combined: AUC 0.824, accuracy 75.6%. Fixation level combined: AUC 0.743, accuracy 72.5%. Note the paper's equations define sensitivity" ;
                    slr:outcome "AUC, accuracy, sensitivity and specificity, reported separately for each feature family and their combination, at each of three units of analysis (fixation, trial, subject)" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Subject level with combined features: AUC 0.935, accuracy 86.2%, sensitivity 91.3%, specificity 82.9%. Subject level by family: task alone AUC 0.789 / accuracy 81.0% but sensitivity only 56.5%; gaze alone AUC 0.904 / 84.5%; face alone AUC 0.917 / 84.5%. Trial level combined: AUC 0.824, accuracy 75.6%. Fixation level combined: AUC 0.743, accuracy 72.5%. Note the paper's equations define sensitivity; Individuals with ASD recognised facial emotions just as accurately as controls but took significantly longer and moved their eyes differently - fewer, longer fixations with larger saccades, and more fixations on regions irrelevant to emotion (forehead, hair, ears, chin) rather than eyes and mouth. Combining task performance, gaze dynamics and deep features of the fixated face regions classified su" ] ] ;
