            slr:overall_judgment "high" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<urn:slr:article:ea9c4425e2e9> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Existing diagnosis of the autism spectrum disorder (ASD) heavily depends on the informant’s evaluation of the patient’s behavior, which is both time consuming and labor demanding. In order to develop a rapid diagnostic tool with high accuracy, machine learning (ML) approaches have been proposed to explore the feasibility of identifying ASD with a limited number of features extracted from behavioral evaluation, neuroimaging and kinematic data. Though restricted and repetitive behavior (RRB) is one of the cardinal symptoms of ASD, no study has been conducted to investigate whether restricted kinematic features (RKF) could be used to identify ASD. The present study aimed to address this question. Twenty children with high functioning autism and twenty-three children with typical development (TD) were recruited. They were instructed to perform a motor task that required the execution of the utmost variant movement. Entropy and 95% range of the movement amplitude, velocity and acceleration were computed as indices of RKF. Five ML classiﬁers were trained and tested including support vector machine (SVM), Linear Discriminant Analysis (LDA), Decision tree (DT), Random forest (RF), and K nearest neighbor (KNN). Results showed that the KNN algorithm (k = 1) yielded the highest classiﬁcation accuracy with four kinematic features (accuracy: 88.37%, speciﬁcity: 91.3%, sensitivity: 85%, AUC: 0.8815). Our study demonstrated that RKF could help robustly identify ASD. It is inferred that the application of ML on genetic, neuroimaging, psychological and kinematic features might pose a considerable challenge to the current diagnostic criteria of ASD, and might potentially lead to an automated and objective diagnosis of ASD." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Zhao Z; Zhang X; Li W; Hu X; Qu X; Cao X; Liu Y; Lu J (2019). Applying Machine Learning to Identify Autism With Restricted Kinematic Features. *IEEE Access, 7, 157614-157622*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Cao X",
        "Hu X",
        "Li W",
        "Liu Y",
        "Lu J",
        "Qu X",
        "Zhang X",
        "Zhao Z" ;
    dcterms:title "Applying Machine Learning to Identify Autism With Restricted Kinematic Features" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_361110f1" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2019 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched?" ;
                            slr:justification "Described as age-matched with a demographic comparison tabulated; non-verbal IQ controlled by inclusion criterion." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "43 children, high-functioning only, with ADHD, intellectual disability and schizophrenia excluded." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Recruitment site, inclusion and exclusion criteria and IQ assessment are specified, but demographic values sit in a table not reproduced in the text." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "Entropy and range are computed automatically from position traces by a fixed pipeline." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "Licensed psychiatrist DSM-IV diagnosis with IQ confirmation and comorbidity exclusion, but no standardised observational instrument." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the task well matched to the construct being measured?" ;
                            slr:justification "The task demands maximal movement variability so that restrictedness manifests directly as failure to comply - a well-reasoned operationalisation of a DSM criterion." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Apparatus, movement-zone geometry, instruction and demonstration procedure, trial count and duration, break structure, protocol-violation rules, filtering, interpolation and noise thresholds all specified." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is model comparison reported transparently?" ;
                            slr:justification "All five classifiers reported with their best accuracy, metrics and feature counts, plus an accuracy-versus-feature-count curve." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample size adequate for the modelling?" ;
                            slr:justification "43 participants, with the winning model a 1-nearest-neighbour classifier, the highest-variance option available." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is there external or independent validation?" ;
                            slr:justification "None." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is feature selection nested within the validation loop?" ;
                            slr:justification "The t-test screen used all labels before cross-validation and forward selection optimised the reported accuracy metric." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reported result free of selection optimism?" ;
                            slr:justification "Best of five classifiers, four k values and an incremental feature search." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are results reported consistently?" ;
                            slr:justification "Sensitivity and specificity values are swapped between the abstract and the discussion." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "None, though software versions and processing parameters are given." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are limitations discussed candidly?" ;
                            slr:justification "High-functioning-only sampling, unknown feasibility for low-functioning children, and an explicit statement that the task cannot serve early screening below age 2." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Not available, but stated explicitly rather than left silent - 'The dataset is not made publicly available'." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are effect directions reported for the informative features?" ;
                            slr:justification "Amplitude range direction reported and interpreted; velocity entropy and range directions, which drive three of four selected features, are not." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Written caregiver consent, Declaration of Helsinki conformity and institutional ethical guidelines all stated." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A deliberately inverted task design: participants perform one-dimensional oscillatory hand movement 'as complex as possible', with the experimenter demonstrating the contrast between simple periodic oscillation (mono amplitude and frequency) and complex unpredictable oscillation (variant amplitude and frequency), followed by practice trials until the requirement was understood. Three 60-second trials with the subdominant hand held behind the back, breaks of 2-5 minutes between trials, and trial reinitiation if the hand left the recording zone or movement stopped. The design is the paper's key idea - restrictedness is measured as failure to comply with an instruction to be variable." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-018" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Motor behavior: one-dimensional hand kinematics, specifically the statistical distributions of oscillation amplitude, velocity and acceleration rather than their means. The construct measured is behavioural variability/complexity rather than speed, accuracy or coordination - a different target from every other motor study in this corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-018" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Non-verbal IQ assessed by Raven's Advanced Progressive Matrices and used as an inclusion criterion (average ability), so cognitive level is controlled by restriction rather than by matching or covariate adjustment. No language or adaptive-functioning measures and no autism severity instrument. Comorbidity is handled by exclusion: ADHD, mental retardation and schizophrenia ruled out for the ASD group; controls reported no physical or mental disorders." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-018" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Index terms: Autism, entropy, kinematic feature, machine learning, restricted and repetitive behavior. Task vocabulary: 'restricted kinematic features', 'kinematic complexity', 'rapid diagnostic tool', 'automated and objective diagnosis', 'locked into a specific thought or behavior'. The entropy and complexity vocabulary is distinctive within the corpus's motor strand." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-018" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups: 20 children with high-functioning autism and 23 age-matched typically developing children. Group sizes are clear. No other-diagnosis comparison group - indeed ADHD, intellectual disability and schizophrenia were explicit exclusions - though the conclusion identifies ASD-versus-ADHD classification as the needed next step." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-018" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; three 60-second trials in a single session with no follow-up. Data are numeric hand-position traces, not identifiable media. Written informed consent was signed by caregivers, the study conformed to the Declaration of Helsinki, and it followed the ethical guidelines of Shenzhen University. No anonymization procedure is described, and none is materially required for this data type." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-018" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Supervised learning with a broad classifier comparison: SVM, linear discriminant analysis, decision tree, random forest and k-nearest neighbour with k in {1,3,5,7}. KNN with k=1 - effectively a nearest-neighbour lookup, the highest-variance choice available - gave the best result, which on 43 participants with four selected features warrants caution the paper does not express." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-018" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2019. The paper characterises machine learning in autism as having concentrated on two things: shortening existing diagnostic instruments by removing redundant ADOS and ADI-R items (citing a claimed 100% accuracy from an 8-item ADOS subset, later above 95% on a larger sample), and classifying from neuroimaging, eye gaze and kinematic data - with only 'a handful' of kinematic studies, none of which targeted restricted and repetitive behaviour. Its closing argument that integrating genetic, neurological, psychological and kinematic features 'might pose a considerable challenge to the current diagnostic criteria of ASD' is one of the corpus's more ambitious claims about where the field is heading." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-018" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Confirmed diagnosis of high-functioning autism by a licensed psychiatrist using DSM-IV criteria, with Raven's Advanced Progressive Matrices confirming average non-verbal intellectual ability and explicit exclusion of ADHD, intellectual disability and schizophrenia. No ADOS or ADI-R. The exclusion of common comorbidities is unusually explicit and is a design strength for internal validity, though it narrows the population." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-018" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "IEEE Access - a broad open-access engineering and computing journal, neither autism-specific nor clinical." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-018" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Handcrafted distributional features: Shannon entropy over a 101-bin normalised histogram and the central 95% range, computed for amplitude, velocity and acceleration and aggregated across three trials by minimum, median and maximum. This is a distribution-shape representation rather than the point-statistic representation (peak velocity, duration, jerk) used by other motor studies in the corpus, and is well matched to the construct of restrictedness." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-018" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: test whether restricted kinematic features derived from restricted and repetitive behaviour can identify ASD, providing an objective assessment of a symptom domain normally rated by informants. Main findings: KNN (k=1) with four features achieved 88.37% accuracy and AUC 0.8815; nine of 18 features differed between groups at p<0.1; three of the four best features derive from the velocity distribution; and all classifiers except the decision tree exceeded 75% accuracy with five or fewer features." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-018" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Single modality; no fusion. Within the modality, three kinematic quantities (amplitude, velocity, acceleration) are each summarised by two complexity measures (entropy and 95% range) and three cross-trial aggregates (min, median, max), giving an 18-feature grid, and the selected model combines amplitude-range and velocity-range/entropy features - so the fusion is across kinematic derivatives and trial aggregates. The discussion proposes future integration with genetic, neuroimaging and psychological data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-018" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Leave-one-out cross-validation is applied uniformly across all five classifiers, and the comparison across classifiers and feature counts is systematic. But the two selection stages are not nested: the t-test screen used the full labelled dataset, and forward feature selection optimised the same accuracy metric that is then reported, so the headline is a maximum over a search rather than an unbiased estimate - the same failure mode that Vabalas et al. (2019) in this corpus identify and correct. There is no external or independent validation, no permutation or significance testing, no class-imbalance handling (20 vs 23 is near-balanced), and no deployment analysis. Generalisability is addressed honestly in narrative terms - high-functioning only, unusable below age 2 - but not tested." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-018" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "ASD n=20, TD n=23, described as age-matched with an inclusion range of 6-13 years. Demographic details including ages and sexes are presented in Table 1, which is not recoverable from the extracted text, so exact means, standard deviations and sex ratios cannot be charted here. Non-verbal IQ was assessed with Raven's Advanced Progressive Matrices as an inclusion criterion (average ability required) but the values are likewise in the unrecoverable table. Age matching is claimed and a demographic comparison is tabulated, which is better practice than most of the corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-018" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Hardware: LeapMotion hand-tracking device connected to a Lenovo Legion R720-15IKBN computer, with a physical rig of two sticks and a string defining a 60 cm movement zone and a mat for comfortable hand positioning. Software: MATLAB R2017b for signal processing (piecewise cubic Hermite interpolation, second-order 5 Hz low-pass Butterworth filter, differentiation) and machine learning; SPSS 17.0 for statistical comparison." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-018" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Reported by the authors in a dedicated Limitations section that is notably candid: only high-functioning children were recruited to ensure task compliance, and whether low-functioning patients could perform the task 'remains an open question'; and 'obviously, this motor task would not work for children less than 2 years old, which suggests that this motor task would not function for the early screening of ASD' - an explicit concession that the method cannot serve early screening, which almost no other corpus study makes. They further note that motion-capture approaches can quantify low-level stereotyped movement but not higher-level RRB such as circumscribed interest. Identifiable in review: the t-test screen from 18 to 9 features used the full labelled dataset before cross-validation, and forward feature selection then selected the combination maximising classification accuracy, so 88.37% is a maximum over a search using the evaluation criterion; the best of five classifiers and four k values is reported; sensitivity and specificity are reversed between abstract and discussion; the direction of the velocity features carrying most of the signal is not reported; exclusion of ADHD, intellectual disability and schizophrenia makes the discrimination easier than the clinical task; and demographic detail resides in a table that is not reproduced in the running text." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-018" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Clinical recruitment (hospital child psychiatry department) with laboratory-style testing on a desktop LeapMotion apparatus. Newly collected data; no existing dataset used." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-018" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two-stage and reasonably transparent: independent t-tests identify nine discriminative features from 18, and forward feature selection then builds up the model incrementally, with the accuracy-versus-feature-count curve published for all five classifiers. The substantive interpretation is developed - three of four selected features derive from the velocity distribution, connected to prior work showing velocity distributions characterise subtle individual motor differences, and greater maximal amplitude range in ASD is linked to reported macrographia and fine motor control deficits. However the direction of the velocity entropy and range differences is never stated, so the central claim that autistic children are more restricted is not directly evidenced for the features that matter most." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-018" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, sensitivity, specificity and AUC for the best model of each of the five classifiers, plus a confusion matrix for the winning model and a plot of accuracy against number of features for all classifiers - which usefully exposes how performance varies with model size. Headline: 88.37% accuracy, AUC 0.8815. Sensitivity and specificity values (91.3% and 85%) are reported but with their labels swapped between the abstract and the discussion, leaving the assignment formally ambiguous. No confidence intervals or significance testing against chance." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-018" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Extend classification from ASD-versus-TD to ASD-versus-other-conditions, specifically ADHD; apply machine learning to adjacent problems such as engagement evaluation; integrate genetic, neurological, psychological and kinematic features toward automated objective diagnosis, while acknowledging the open problem of selecting relevant features from a very large pool; and develop ways to quantify higher-level restricted and repetitive behaviour such as circumscribed interest, which accelerometer and Kinect approaches to stereotyped movement cannot capture." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-018" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The dataset availability is stated explicitly and negatively - 'The dataset is not made publicly available' - which is at least transparent, and rarer in this corpus than silence. No code release. Method reporting is otherwise strong: apparatus, movement-zone geometry, trial structure and durations, interpolation and filter settings, noise thresholds for each kinematic quantity, the 101-bin histogram construction, the Shannon entropy and range equations, the two-stage feature-selection procedure with its p<0.1 threshold, the five classifiers with the k values tested, and the software versions. Re-implementable in principle; not reproducible without the data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-018" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with high functioning autism', 'children with ASD', 'patients with ASD', 'ASD patients', 'the patient', 'individuals with typical development (TD)', 'TD children', 'non-ASD individuals'. Consistently medicalised - 'patient' is the default noun throughout, and the framing is diagnostic rather than descriptive." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-018" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Single session; three trials of 60 seconds each per participant, separated by 2-5 minute breaks to avoid fatigue, with the first and last 3 seconds of each trial discarded" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional case-control classification study" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "High-functioning autism" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 20; comparison: 23" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Best model KNN (k=1) with four features: accuracy 88.37%, AUC 0.8815. The abstract reports specificity 91.3% and sensitivity 85%, while the discussion reverses these to sensitivity 91.3% and specificity 85% - an internal inconsistency. All classifiers except the decision tree exceeded 75% accuracy with no more than five features",
                        "Restricted kinematic features - entropy and spread of the amplitude, velocity and acceleration distributions during a task explicitly demanding variable movement - classified high-functioning autistic children from typically developing children at 88.37% accuracy with AUC 0.88 using just four features. Three of the four best features derive from the velocity distribution, which the authors connect" ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy, sensitivity, specificity and AUC, reported for the best model of each of the five classifiers together with the number of features in each best model, plus a confusion matrix for the winning model and an accuracy-versus-feature-count curve" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Best model KNN (k=1) with four features: accuracy 88.37%, AUC 0.8815. The abstract reports specificity 91.3% and sensitivity 85%, while the discussion reverses these to sensitivity 91.3% and specificity 85% - an internal inconsistency. All classifiers except the decision tree exceeded 75% accuracy with no more than five features; Restricted kinematic features - entropy and spread of the amplitude, velocity and acceleration distributions during a task explicitly demanding variable movement - classified high-functioning autistic children from typically developing children at 88.37% accuracy with AUC 0.88 using just four features. Three of the four best features derive from the velocity distribution, which the authors connect" ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "3f8de49f6385439d7248da9d6419cb208bf41046ad58b0663e005c16895786fa" ;
