<https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics11040530> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Eye tracking is a useful technique for detecting autism spectrum disorder (ASD). One of the most important aspects of good learning is the ability to have atypical visual attention. The eye-tracking technique provides useful information about children’s visual behaviour for early and accurate diagnosis. It works by scanning the paths of the eyes to extract a sequence of eye projection points on the image to analyse the behaviour of children with autism. In this study, three artiﬁcial-intelligence techniques were developed, namely, machine learning, deep learning, and a hybrid technique between them, for early diagnosis of autism. The ﬁrst technique, neural networks [feedforward neural networks (FFNNs) and artiﬁcial neural networks (ANNs)], is based on feature classiﬁcation extracted by a hybrid method between local binary pattern (LBP) and grey level cooccurrence matrix (GLCM) algorithms. This technique achieved a high accuracy of 99.8% for FFNNs and ANNs. The second technique used a pre-trained convolutional neural network (CNN) model, such as GoogleNet and ResNet-18, on the basis of deep feature map extraction. The GoogleNet and ResNet-18 models achieved high performances of 93.6% and 97.6%, respectively. The third technique used the hybrid method between deep learning (GoogleNet and ResNet-18) and machine learning (SVM), called GoogleNet + SVM and ResNet-18 + SVM. This technique depends on two blocks. The ﬁrst block used CNN to extract deep feature maps, whilst the second block used SVM to classify the features extracted from the ﬁrst block. This technique proved its high diagnostic ability, achieving accuracies of 95.5% and 94.5% for GoogleNet + SVM and ResNet-18 + SVM, respectively." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Ahmed IA; Senan EM; Rassem TH; Ali MAH; Shatnawi HSA; Alwazer SM; Alshahrani M (2022). Eye Tracking-Based Diagnosis and Early Detection of Autism Spectrum Disorder Using Machine Learning and Deep Learning Techniques. **. https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics11040530" ;
    dcterms:creator "Ahmed IA",
        "Ali MAH",
        "Alshahrani M",
        "Alwazer SM",
        "Rassem TH",
        "Senan EM",
        "Shatnawi HSA" ;
    dcterms:title "Eye Tracking-Based Diagnosis and Early Detection of Autism Spectrum Disorder Using Machine Learning and Deep Learning Techniques" ;
    bibo:doi "10.3390/electronics11040530" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_4f7e7eb3" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2022 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sex imbalance addressed?" ;
                            slr:justification "86.2% versus 43.3% male, restated in one sentence and never mentioned again." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the source dataset's sample characterised in substance?" ;
                            slr:justification "Only participant and image counts and sex are carried over from the source publication." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched?" ;
                            slr:justification "No matching described or assessable on any variable." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Counts and sex only; no ages, no recruitment route, no clinical detail." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Does the representation preserve any behavioural signal?" ;
                            slr:justification "Segmentation, morphological cleaning and texture descriptors leave no fixation, saccade or attention quantity." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "No diagnostic procedure reported or summarised." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "No stimulus, eye tracker, duration, sampling rate or protocol is reported at any point." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the image processing pipeline described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Filters, active contour formulation, morphological operations, feature extraction and network hyperparameters all specified with equations and tables." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are multiple configurations compared on identical data?" ;
                            slr:justification "Six configurations across three families on the same split, all reported." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reported headline a held-out figure?" ;
                            slr:justification "99.8% is described as an overall accuracy pooling phases; the test-phase figure for the same model is 98.7%." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is augmentation confined to training data?" ;
                            slr:justification "Training and validation expanded sevenfold and tenfold; the test set left unaugmented at 44 and 66 images." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the unit of analysis the participant?" ;
                            slr:justification "Images are split 80/20 with no participant grouping, at roughly 9.3 images per child." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is performance estimated over repeated splits?" ;
                            slr:justification "A single fixed partition with no cross-validation, repetition or confidence intervals." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is any baseline reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No chance level, permutation test or trivial comparator." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible?" ;
                            slr:justification "Public figshare URL for the source images given with an access date." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No statement anywhere, despite funding, conflict-of-interest and data availability sections being present." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are reported metrics internally consistent?" ;
                            slr:justification "GoogleNet's 93.6% accuracy with 99.48% AUC, and its per-class accuracies of 97.7% and 90.9% against stated sensitivity and specificity both of 94.5%, are not reconcilable as presented." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Not mentioned." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the result placed against the source dataset's participant-level finding?" ;
                            slr:justification "Cilia and colleagues' fall from roughly 90% image-level to approximately 71% per child is neither cited nor discussed." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are any behavioural or clinical findings reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No group differences, effect sizes or gaze statistics anywhere in the paper." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a limitations section present?" ;
                            slr:justification "None; no limitation is acknowledged at any point." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Nothing. No diagnostic instrument, criterion, clinician role, setting or timing is reported, and the source dataset's ascertainment procedure is not summarised even in a sentence. The class labels are treated as file attributes throughout. Under this review's classification this is dataset-label-only ascertainment in its least documented form. The omission is compounded by the paper's framing, which repeatedly describes the systems as tools for 'diagnosis' and 'early diagnosis' and proposes that they 'could help experts and autism treatment centres in the early detection of autism in children' - claims about diagnostic utility made against a reference standard whose nature is entirely unknown to the reader." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-088" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No cognitive, language, adaptive, severity, comorbidity or medication information for either group, and no exclusions described. Nothing therefore relates model output to any clinical dimension, and the possibility that the classifier separates developmental level rather than diagnosis cannot be examined." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-088" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with ASD', 'children with autism', 'people with autism', against 'TD' and 'typically developing children'. Person-first in the main, though 'patients with autism spectrum disorder' appears and autism is once described as 'autism disease' and elsewhere as something requiring 'effective treatments to improve responses in children with autism'. The framing is medical and treatment-oriented in a way that misdescribes the condition, referring to 'the heterogeneous response to treatment and the search for effective treatments' and to children 'who are less responsive to the currently available treatment methods'. The introduction also contains a confused clinical passage - 'Many symptoms of ASD appear, such as genetic, cognitive, neurological and cognitive factors' - listing aetiological factors as symptoms and repeating 'cognitive' twice, which is symptomatic of the paper's general disengagement from the clinical subject matter." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-088" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Not reported. The paper describes neither the room, the country, the recruitment route, the eye tracker nor the acquisition protocol for the source recordings. The only settings named are the four institutions where the analysis was carried out and the hardware on which it ran - a fifth-generation i5 processor with 8 GB RAM and a 4 GB GPU, specified more precisely than anything about the children." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-088" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two distinct problems, one shared with several studies in this corpus and one specific to this paper. The shared problem is the unit of analysis: 547 images come from 59 children at roughly 9.3 images each, and the 80/20 partition is by image with no participant grouping described, so images from the same child appear in both training and test. Cilia and colleagues, who produced this dataset, measured the consequence directly, reporting image-level accuracy near 90% falling to approximately 71% when recomputed per child; that correction is neither cited nor applied here. The specific problem is that the headline figure pools phases: the abstract and conclusion report 99.8% while the results text states the same model achieved 100% in training and validation and 98.7% in testing, and describes 99.8% as 'the overall accuracy'. One element is handled correctly and deserves credit - augmentation by flipping, rotation, displacement and shearing was applied only to the training and validation partitions, expanding them from 175 and 262 to 1,750 and 1,834 images, while the test set remained unaugmented at 44 and 66. This is the correct discipline and is precisely what Gaspar and colleagues violated on the same dataset by augmenting the testing data as well. Against that, there is a single fixed split with no repetition, no cross-validation, no confidence intervals and no external validation, so the reported differences among six configurations rest on one partition of 109 test images." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-088" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Entirely absent. Unlike Gaspar and colleagues on the same dataset, who at least state that participants 'were shown different videos containing stimulating scenarios for the movement of the eye in the entire screen', this paper reproduces nothing at all about what was viewed, for how long, or under what conditions. The reader is given no basis on which to judge what behaviour the scanpaths represent. Since the analysis proceeds by treating the resulting plot as a texture image, the absence is internally consistent with the method - the pipeline does not require knowing what generated the trace - but it means the study cannot connect its result to any behavioural construct, and the paper accordingly makes no attempt to." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-088" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Electronics 11(4):530 (2022), an MDPI open-access engineering journal. Authorship spans the Applied College at Najran University in Saudi Arabia, Dr Babasaheb Ambedkar Marathwada University in India, Bournemouth University in the UK and the University of Malaya in Malaysia, with no clinical, psychological or autism-research affiliation among the seven authors. The review timeline is compressed - received 15 January 2022, accepted 6 February, published 10 February, twenty-six days from submission to publication - and the paper contains no limitations section, no ethics statement and several internal inconsistencies in its reported metrics. Funded by the Najran University Scientific Research Deanship." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-088" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional secondary analysis of a public dataset; no follow-up. No ethics statement, consent statement or IRB approval appears anywhere, and no pointer is given to where the source dataset's approvals were obtained. A data availability statement supplies the figshare URL for the source images with an access date, funding is acknowledged from the Najran University Scientific Research Deanship under a named grant, and a conflict-of-interest declaration is present - so the absence of an ethics statement is not a matter of missing front matter but of the section not being written." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-088" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No acquisition equipment is described at any point. The analysis chain is specified in unusual mathematical detail: the average filter with its 5x5 window and neighbour-averaging equation, the Laplacian operator, the subtraction combining them, the active contour formulation including the level-set function, the energy terms, the Heaviside and Dirac delta smoothing functions, the sign convention determining whether the contour shrinks or expands, and the stopping criterion comparing successive masks. Morphological operations are named, LBP is given with its neighbourhood size and feature count, and the CNN training options are tabulated with optimiser, mini-batch size, epochs, learning rate, validation frequency and execution environment for both networks. The asymmetry is total: every arithmetic step is documented and nothing about the measurement is." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-088" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Single modality with no cross-modal fusion. Two within-pipeline combinations are performed. The first concatenates LBP and GLCM outputs into a single 216-dimensional vector, justified on the general ground that 'extracting the features from several methods and combining them are two of the most important recent methods that have an effect on accurate diagnosis' - an assertion rather than an argument, with no ablation showing what either family contributes alone. The second replaces each CNN's classification head with an SVM operating on its deep feature maps. That second comparison is the more informative because it is properly controlled: the same features are classified two ways, and the result is mixed - the SVM head improves GoogleNet from 93.6% to 95.5% but degrades ResNet-18 from 97.6% to 94.5%. The paper reports both directions without comment. Across all six configurations the pattern that hand-engineered texture beats learned representation is presented as the headline conclusion but never examined, though it is the observation most consistent with the discriminative signal residing in low-level plot morphology rather than in anything a pretrained visual network would find semantically salient." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-088" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Sex composition is restated from the source publication and is the only demographic information given: the autistic group comprises 25 males and 4 females (86.2% male) and the comparison group 13 males and 17 females (43.3% male). That 43-percentage-point difference, running in opposite directions between groups, is reported in a single sentence and never mentioned again - not analysed, not covaried, not listed as a limitation, and not discussed anywhere in a 27-page paper. It matters because the classifier operates on rendered scanpath images, and any systematic difference in viewing behaviour, screen coverage or trace density associated with sex would be absorbed directly into the texture features. No ages, no age range, no IQ, no language level, no severity and no matching of any kind. Given that the same dataset elsewhere in this corpus is analysed by Gaspar and colleagues with no participant information at all, the restatement here is an improvement in reporting that is not accompanied by any improvement in analysis." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-088" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Reproducible as an image-processing pipeline, not as a study. Fully specified: all filtering, segmentation and morphological steps with equations; LBP neighbourhood and feature count; the 216-dimensional concatenated vector; the network architectures with layer counts; both CNNs' complete training hyperparameters; the augmentation operations and the resulting counts before and after; the exact split into 350 training, 88 validation and 109 test images with per-class breakdowns; the evaluation formulae; and the compute hardware. Not reported: anything about the participants beyond counts and sex; the stimuli; the eye tracker; the diagnostic procedure; the final chosen contour parameters; whether the split was stratified or randomised and with what seed; and any code. The data availability statement points to the public source images." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-088" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: compare three artificial-intelligence approaches - shallow neural networks on hand-engineered texture features, transfer-learned CNNs, and CNN-plus-SVM hybrids - for classifying autism from gaze-path images. Main findings: FFNN and ANN on concatenated LBP and GLCM features reached 99.8% overall accuracy (98.7% at test phase); ResNet-18 reached 97.6% and GoogleNet 93.6%; the hybrids reached 95.5% and 94.5%; and the hand-engineered pipeline was concluded to be 'the best performance amongst the proposed systems'. No behavioural finding, group difference or gaze characteristic is reported." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-088" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2022. The framing is the standard early-detection argument with the usual supporting claims: prevalence of one in 160 per WHO, diagnosis typically delayed to age 4 despite symptoms appearing earlier, and eye tracking recommended as 'fast, inexpensive, easy to analyse and applicable to all ages'. The related-work section is a list of prior studies with their reported accuracies and no critical assessment, and it includes an error that indicates limited engagement with the clinical literature - the ADOS is attributed to Lord et al. as 'a method... for assessing autism' consisting of 'unstructured observational tasks', when the instrument is explicitly semi-structured and standardised, and the CARS is described as being 'based on the doctor's assessment of children's behaviour through two scales, a social communication questionnaire and a social responsiveness scale', which conflates three distinct instruments. One passage is worth noting because it states a caveat the paper then ignores: 'evidence regarding positive eye-tracking outcomes as a strong predictor of long-term outcomes in children with ASD is lacking'. Having noted that the predictive validity of the modality is unestablished, the paper proceeds to report 99.8% accuracy and to propose the systems as aids for diagnosis and early detection without returning to the point." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-088" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "There is no limitations section, and no limitation is stated anywhere in the paper. Nothing is acknowledged about the image-wise split, the pooled headline accuracy, the absence of participant information, the sex imbalance restated in the methods, the lack of diagnostic provenance, the absence of any behavioural interpretation, or the single fixed split without repetition or confidence intervals. The conclusion instead asserts that all three systems 'achieved superior results' and 'promising results', and the future work proposes only to concatenate the feature families - an architectural extension that would address none of the above. This is the least self-critical paper in the corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-088" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Nominally visual attention, but no behavioural construct survives the pipeline. The source dataset encodes gaze coordinates and their derivatives as a rendered plot; this study then applies average filtering, Laplacian edge enhancement, active contour segmentation of the trace region, morphological hole-filling, and finally local binary pattern and grey-level co-occurrence texture descriptors. What reaches the classifier is a 216-dimensional summary of the local texture statistics of a processed image of a line drawing. No fixation, saccade, dwell time, area of interest, coverage measure or any other quantity with a gaze interpretation is computed at any stage, and consequently the paper reports no group difference in viewing behaviour, no effect direction and no interpretation. The introduction's extensive discussion of social versus non-social orientation, social attention deficits and visual biomarkers has no connection to anything the method actually measures - a gap of several sections between the stated construct and the operationalisation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-088" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The representational chain is the study's core and its central problem. Four preprocessing stages precede feature extraction - average filtering, Laplacian enhancement, active contour segmentation, morphological cleaning - each documented with equations and each progressively removing information that might have carried gaze meaning. Segmentation isolates 'the eye-tracking path region', converting a trajectory into a binary or near-binary shape; morphological cleaning then modifies that shape further. LBP and GLCM are then computed over the result. Local binary patterns encode the relative intensity of a pixel against its neighbours and were developed for texture and face recognition; grey-level co-occurrence matrices summarise second-order intensity statistics. Neither has any relationship to where a child looked. The consequence is visible in the paper's own output: 27 pages containing six accuracy tables, multiple confusion matrices and ROC curves, and not one statement about what distinguishes autistic children's scanning from that of their peers." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-088" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, precision, sensitivity, specificity and AUC for all six configurations, with confusion matrices and, for the neural network pipeline, separate training, validation and test figures. Reporting the three phases separately is genuinely useful and is what allows the headline to be diagnosed: the ANN achieved 100% on training and validation and 98.7% at test, and the abstract's 99.8% is described as 'the overall accuracy' - so it pools performance on data the model was fitted to with performance on held-out data. The correct held-out figure is 98.7%, on 109 images. Two internal inconsistencies also appear: GoogleNet is reported with 93.6% accuracy but 99.48% AUC while ResNet-18 has higher accuracy (97.6%) and lower AUC (97.56%), a pattern that would require explanation and receives none; and GoogleNet's per-class accuracies of 97.7% and 90.9% are difficult to reconcile with its stated 94.5% sensitivity and 94.5% specificity. No confidence intervals, no repeated splits, no significance tests and no baseline of any kind accompany any figure." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-088" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Six configurations spanning three families, all reported, which is a reasonable comparative design in itself. The shallow networks use 216 input neurons and 10 hidden layers, an unusually deep configuration for a fully connected network on 216 features and 547 samples, with no justification, no hidden-layer widths and no regularisation described. The CNN training is more conventionally specified with full hyperparameters for both networks. The SVM is unspecified as to kernel or parameters. What is missing throughout is any baseline: no chance level, no permutation test, no simple comparator such as image mean intensity or trace pixel count, and no repetition across splits - so a 6.2-point spread between the best and worst configuration is reported without any means of judging whether it exceeds split-to-split variation on 109 test images." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-088" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: autism spectrum disorder, eye tracking, machine learning, neural networks, convolutional neural network, GLCM, local binary pattern. Five of seven are computational and two of those name specific texture descriptors, which accurately identifies the paper as a texture-classification study that happens to use autism-labelled images." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-088" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "One direction: 'to extract features using CNN models and combine them with features extracted by LBP and GLCM algorithms into a single feature vector for each image and classify it using all three ANN, FFNN and SVM algorithms.' This proposes more feature concatenation on the same 547 images with the same split, and addresses none of the study's substantive weaknesses. No proposal is made to group by participant, obtain demographic or diagnostic metadata, validate externally, interpret the features behaviourally, or test on an independent dataset." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-088" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None, and none is possible. There is no feature importance, attribution, saliency or ablation, and the representation - texture codes over a segmented, morphologically cleaned rendering of a gaze trace - admits no behavioural interpretation even in principle. No group means, effect sizes, directions or descriptive statistics about viewing behaviour appear anywhere. The paper therefore contributes six accuracy figures and no knowledge about autism, gaze or visual attention, despite an introduction devoting several pages to social orientation, attention biomarkers and the clinical significance of atypical visual attention. That disconnection between the stated motivation and the delivered content is the study's defining characteristic." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-088" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups inherited from the Carette et al. dataset: 29 autistic children contributing 219 images and 30 typically developing children contributing 328 images, 547 images from 59 children in total. This paper is more informative than Gaspar and colleagues on the same data, which reported only image counts, since it restates the participant counts and sex composition from the source publication. But the participant structure is then entirely disregarded in the analysis: the 80/20 partition operates on images, so children are not the unit at any stage and images from the same child necessarily span the training and test sets at roughly nine images per participant. No other-diagnosis or developmental-delay comparison group. The class imbalance of 219 to 328 is addressed by differential augmentation of the training and validation partitions - sevenfold for the autistic class, tenfold for the comparison class - rather than by any participant-level consideration." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-088" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Not reported; recording durations are properties of the source dataset and are not restated" ;
                    slr:study_design "Secondary analysis of a public image dataset, structured as a three-way comparison of modelling pipelines with a single fixed image-wise train/validation/test split" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Children from the Carette et al. gaze-path image dataset" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 29 children (25 male, 4 female) contributing 219 images; comparison: 30 children (13 male, 17 female) contributing 328 images" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Neural networks on LBP-plus-GLCM features: FFNN and ANN both 99.8% overall accuracy, with the ANN reaching 100% on all measures in training and validation and 98.7% accuracy, 100% precision, 100% sensitivity, 98.2% specificity and 99.77% AUC in testing; FFNN precision 99.8%, sensitivity 99.5%, specificity 100%, AUC 99.85%. Deep models: ResNet-18 97.6% accuracy (precision 97.5%, sensitivity 97%, sp",
                        "Six modelling pipelines were compared on 547 public gaze-path images, with shallow neural networks on concatenated local binary pattern and grey-level co-occurrence matrix texture features reaching 99.8%, transfer-learned ResNet-18 reaching 97.6%, GoogleNet 93.6%, and CNN-plus-SVM hybrids 95.5% and 94.5%. The result that hand-engineered texture descriptors outperform fine-tuned deep networks by tw" ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy, precision, sensitivity, specificity and AUC for each of six configurations, with confusion matrices and separate training, validation and test performance for the neural network pipeline" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Neural networks on LBP-plus-GLCM features: FFNN and ANN both 99.8% overall accuracy, with the ANN reaching 100% on all measures in training and validation and 98.7% accuracy, 100% precision, 100% sensitivity, 98.2% specificity and 99.77% AUC in testing; FFNN precision 99.8%, sensitivity 99.5%, specificity 100%, AUC 99.85%. Deep models: ResNet-18 97.6% accuracy (precision 97.5%, sensitivity 97%, sp; Six modelling pipelines were compared on 547 public gaze-path images, with shallow neural networks on concatenated local binary pattern and grey-level co-occurrence matrix texture features reaching 99.8%, transfer-learned ResNet-18 reaching 97.6%, GoogleNet 93.6%, and CNN-plus-SVM hybrids 95.5% and 94.5%. The result that hand-engineered texture descriptors outperform fine-tuned deep networks by tw" ] ] ;
