<urn:slr:article:51915c87b5e5> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) is a developmental disorder that is prevalent globally. Research into detecting autism traditionally focused on behavioural aspects of the condition, however, more recently, focus has shifted to more objective alternatives using techniques such as machine learning and gait analysis. Gait measurements, having been used for person identification, varies from person to person, introducing a lot of intra-subject variance. This applies to the 8 spatialtemporal features used in this study, representing the time that an individual spends in each phase of a gait cycle, collected using a Vicon motion tracking system. The features were averaged across each gait trial that the subjects performed, producing a second set of features with reduced intra-subject variance. Four common classifiers, a Support Vector Machine (SVM), KNearest Neighbour (KNN), Random Forests (RF) and a Decision Tree (DT) classifier, were all trained using the two feature sets and their classification rates were compared. The results show that for the RF classifier, reducing the intra-subject variance, was able to successfully increase the classification power. The KNN and DT classifiers experienced a minimal decrease in accuracy, where the SVM suffered the greatest loss when intrasubject variance was reduced. Results overall show that the effect intra-subject variance has on classification power depends heavily on the suitability of the classifier to the initial problem as well as size and class balance of the data." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Henderson B; Yogarajah P; Gardiner B; McGinnity M; Forster K; Nicholas B; Wimpory D; Wanigasinghe J (2020). Effects of Intra-Subject Variation in Gait Analysis on ASD Classification Performance in Machine Learning Models. **." ;
    dcterms:creator "Forster K",
        "Gardiner B",
        "Henderson B",
        "McGinnity M",
        "Nicholas B",
        "Wanigasinghe J",
        "Wimpory D",
        "Yogarajah P" ;
    dcterms:title "Effects of Intra-Subject Variation in Gait Analysis on ASD Classification Performance in Machine Learning Models" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_c79e100a" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2020 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is class balance addressed?" ;
                            slr:justification "The 12:9 imbalance is discussed as an explanation for the SVM's collapse, and synthetic minority generation proposed, but nothing is done about it here." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "21 subjects, and the authors note each represents a large portion of the dataset." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "No matching is reported or possible to assess; height, leg length and cadence directly determine the eight features." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Group sizes only - no ages, sexes, site, recruitment route or period." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are anthropometric determinants of the features controlled?" ;
                            slr:justification "Height, leg length and mass are neither reported nor normalised out of timing features they strongly determine." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "One clause with no instrument or criteria; control status unsupported." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Sampling rate, event definitions and feature derivation are fully specified; marker set, walkway, footwear, walking speed instruction and session protocol are not." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "Features are automatic event-timing differences with no label-dependent selection." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the compared conditions otherwise identical?" ;
                            slr:justification "Same subjects, features and classifiers, but the two datasets differ in size (278 versus 105) as well as in variance, confounding the comparison - as the authors themselves recognise." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the validation split at the participant level?" ;
                            slr:justification "Splitting is by gait cycle or trial; the authors state the same subject and trial can appear on both sides." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is model selection separated from performance estimation?" ;
                            slr:justification "Grid search optimised hyperparameters on the same cross-validation that produced the reported scores." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are enough metrics reported to detect degenerate models?" ;
                            slr:justification "Reporting recall and precision alongside accuracy is what exposed the always-autistic SVM." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the observed improvement tested for significance?" ;
                            slr:justification "68.75% to 76.67% on 105 data points, with no confidence interval, per-fold variance or statistical test." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the acknowledged leakage addressed rather than rationalised?" ;
                            slr:justification "Named explicitly, judged 'potentially limited', and left in place." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No release or availability statement." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Library named; no code, no grid ranges, no selected hyperparameters, no seed." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are feature definitions fully specified?" ;
                            slr:justification "All eight features named by event pair and mapped to gait phase, with sampling rate and computation stated." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No approval, consent or governance statement anywhere, for a study of clinically diagnosed participants of unstated age." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a degenerate or negative result reported honestly?" ;
                            slr:justification "The SVM's 100% recall is identified as single-class prediction and declared useless in application rather than presented as a strength." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "An IEEE conference proceedings paper (IEEE Xplore, 2020), venue name not captured in the extracted text. A short methodological contribution rather than a clinical study, which matches the depth of participant reporting." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-041" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Eight temporal gait-phase durations per gait cycle, computed as frame-count differences at 250 Hz between detected foot strike and foot off events, then L2-normalised per feature vector to equalise phases of naturally different duration. The two representations compared are the raw per-cycle vector (Dataset A) and its mean across all cycles within a trial (Dataset B). The averaging is the intervention under study, and the paper demonstrates its effect directly by plotting the same two features before and after for six subjects." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-041" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Mixed. The feature definitions are exemplary - all eight are named explicitly by their event pairs (RS-LS, LS-RS, LO-LS, RO-RS, LS-LO, RS-RO, LS-LS, RS-RS), mapped to their gait phases, with the sampling rate and the frame-difference computation stated, so the feature set is fully reproducible. The dataset construction is also precisely specified, down to the cycle and trial counts in each condition. Everything else is missing: no grid-search ranges or selected hyperparameters for any classifier, no random seed, no code or data release, no ethics statement, and no participant characteristics whatsoever." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-041" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Include objective measures of intra-subject gait variance directly in the feature vectors, on the basis of the observed greater variability in autistic subjects; pair variance reduction with synthetic data generation for the minority class to counter the imbalance that averaging exacerbates and the overfitting seen in the SVM; and examine more generally when the loss of dataset size outweighs the gain from reduced variance." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-041" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2020. The framing is a shift in the field's evidential basis: research on autism detection 'traditionally focused on behavioural aspects' but has moved toward 'more objective alternatives' such as gait analysis, on the argument that instrumented measurement 'can identify ASD with more confidence when compared to behavioural assessments recorded by human beings'. The paper situates itself against a set of prior results it reports at face value - 95%, 89% and 100% for random forest, KNN and SVM on adolescent scan data, 96.2% for a PART classifier on gaze and demographic data - and against gait datasets of 60 subjects (30/30) and 44 subjects (12/32). Its own contribution is to notice that these studies differ in how each subject is represented and that this choice has not been examined, which makes it one of the few papers in the corpus whose motivation is a defect in the literature's methodology rather than a clinical need." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-041" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Acknowledged with unusual directness in one respect and not at all in others. The authors state that training and testing folds 'can both contain data points from the same subject and even from the same trial', judge the impact 'potentially limited', and choose 10-fold over leave-one-out on that basis - naming the leakage and then reasoning past it rather than eliminating it by splitting on participants. They also acknowledge the small dataset, the class imbalance, that averaging reduces the data available for training, and that the SVM's apparent 100% recall was a degenerate single-class predictor with 'no use in real world applications'. Not acknowledged: that grid search selected hyperparameters on the same cross-validation whose scores are reported, so every figure is optimistically biased; that no participant characteristic is reported, so nothing rules out age, sex or stature as the discriminated variable in features that are all durations; that no diagnostic instrument is named; and that no ethics approval is stated. The paper describes itself as a 'preliminary investigation', which is a fair self-assessment." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-041" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No IQ, developmental, language, adaptive or severity measure is reported, and no comorbidity information. Nor are the anthropometric variables that directly determine gait phase durations - height, leg length, body mass - which for a gait study is a substantive omission independent of clinical characterisation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-041" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No model interpretability method is applied, and neither the random forest nor the decision tree feature importances are extracted, which is a missed opportunity given both were trained. Interpretation is instead pursued through direct data visualisation: scatter plots of two features for three autistic and three typically developed subjects, before and after averaging, used to show that averaging compresses within-subject spread most for the subjects who varied most, and incidentally that autistic subjects vary more than typically developed ones. This is transparency about the data rather than about the model, and it is the source of the paper's only substantive clinical observation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-041" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Overground walking trials with lower-limb marker placement - a minimum of five per subject, each containing a variable number of gait cycles, with the analysis restricted to the five-trial common minimum so that every subject contributes equally. Restricting to a common trial count is a deliberate balancing decision at the subject level, and is the only such control the design applies." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-041" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, recall, precision and F1, with accuracy and F1 designated primary and the other two used for extended analysis. Reporting all four is what makes the SVM's degeneration detectable - accuracy 50% with recall 100% and precision 50% is diagnostic of a single-class predictor, and the authors correctly identify it as such. Precision was also the grid-search selection metric, chosen on the stated grounds that the application is identifying autistic from non-autistic people. Missing: AUC, confusion matrices, per-fold variance, confidence intervals, and any statistical test of the difference between Dataset A and Dataset B results - so the headline improvement from 68.75% to 76.67% on 105 data points is reported without any indication of whether it exceeds noise." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-041" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A single clause: subjects were 'clinically diagnosed as having ASD'. No instrument, no criteria, no diagnostician, no date. Typically developed status carries no stated basis at all. Given that the entire classification target rests on these labels, this is the study's most consequential reporting gap." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-041" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; a single capture session per subject with no follow-up. Data are numeric marker trajectories and event timings, so low sensitivity in themselves, but no ethics approval, consent procedure or data governance statement appears anywhere in the paper. Since ages are never reported, it cannot even be determined whether the participants were adults or children." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-041" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No multimodal fusion. A single modality throughout. The paper's structural comparison is instead between two aggregation levels of the same modality - the gait cycle and the walking trial - which is a distinct axis from the sensor-fusion comparisons elsewhere in the corpus and arguably a more fundamental one, since it concerns what a data point is rather than what it contains." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-041" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Not stated. Author affiliations span Ulster University (Northern Ireland), Bangor University (Wales) and the University of Colombo (Sri Lanka), but no collection site, recruitment route, period or setting is identified, and no ethics approval or consent statement appears." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-041" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups, 21 subjects: 12 clinically diagnosed autistic and 9 typically developed. No other-diagnosis comparison group. The analysis unit is not the subject but the gait cycle (Dataset A: 167 autistic and 111 typically developed cycles) or the walking trial (Dataset B: 60 and 45), so 21 people generate 278 or 105 data points respectively. The authors themselves note that with a dataset this small 'the impact of variation in the subjects may be magnified due to the large portion of the dataset each subject represents'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-041" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The weakest link, and knowingly so. Splitting is by gait cycle or by trial, never by participant, across 10 folds; the authors state outright that the same subject's and even the same trial's data can fall on both sides of a split, and proceed on the judgement that the effect is limited. With 21 subjects generating 278 cycles, and with the eight features being timing measures strongly determined by individual stature and habitual cadence, the classifiers can succeed by recognising people rather than by recognising autism - and the paper's own introduction concedes the premise, noting that gait analysis is used for 'person identification' and that 'gait representations are unique to individual people'. Compounding this, hyperparameters were grid-searched on the same cross-validation that produced the reported scores. There is no held-out participant set, no external cohort, no repeated cross-validation, and no significance testing." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-041" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'subjects clinically diagnosed as having ASD', 'subjects with ASD', 'those with autism', 'typically developed (TD)', 'people with the condition'. Predominantly person-first, with 'typically developed' rather than 'healthy' or 'normal'. Autism is framed as 'a permanent neurological condition' rather than a disease, and the motivating argument is that objective motor measurement can supplement subjective behavioural assessment - a relatively neutral register." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-041" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: Machine Learning, Autism Spectrum Disorder, automatic classification, intra-subject, variation. 'Intra-subject variation' as an indexed keyword is what distinguishes this paper - the object of study is a property of the data rather than a clinical construct or an algorithm." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-041" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Vicon infrared motion tracking system sampling at 250 Hz for marker capture and gait event detection; the scikit-learn Python library for L2 normalisation, the four classifiers, grid search, 10-fold cross-validation and the four reported metrics. No custom software and no bespoke instrumentation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-041" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: determine how the choice of data representation - individual gait cycles versus trial averages - affects autism classification performance, as a methodological question in its own right. Main findings: averaging reduced intra-subject variance, most for subjects with the greatest initial variance; random forest improved from 68.75% to 76.67% accuracy; decision tree and KNN showed little accuracy change but substantial F1 loss; the SVM degenerated into an always-autistic predictor; autistic subjects showed visibly greater intra-subject gait variability than typically developed subjects; and the benefit of variance reduction depends on classifier suitability, dataset size and class balance." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-041" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Walking gait, reduced to the temporal structure of the gait cycle: step time, stance time, swing time and step-to-step interval, each measured separately for the left and right leg. Only timing is used - no kinematics, no joint angles, no ground reaction forces, and no spatial measures - which makes the feature set narrow but exceptionally well specified." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-041" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None reported. The paper gives group sizes (12 autistic, 9 typically developed) and nothing else: no ages, no age ranges, no sex distribution, no ethnicity, no socioeconomic information, no country of collection, and no statement about matching on any variable. This is the least participant characterisation of any included study in the corpus, and it means the reported group difference cannot be attributed to autism rather than to age, sex, height, leg length or any other determinant of gait timing - all of which the eight features measure directly." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-041" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Four classical supervised classifiers held as the experimental variable: SVM, KNN, random forest and decision tree, each optimised by grid search so that comparisons are between best-configured models rather than arbitrary defaults. The selection rationale is stated per classifier - KNN as a simple baseline suited to small data, SVM as the field's default for small datasets and for autism classification specifically, and random forest and decision tree for their handling of non-linearly separable data. No deep learning, appropriately for 105 or 278 data points with eight features." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-041" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "A minimum of five walking trials per subject, each trial containing a varying number of gait cycles; analysis restricted to the five-trial common minimum for every subject" ;
                    slr:study_design "Methodological comparison study: the same participants, features and classifiers under two data representations" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Not characterised beyond diagnosis" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 12; comparison: 9" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Averaging gait-phase timings across a walking trial visibly reduces intra-subject variance, most sharply for subjects whose variance was largest to begin with. Its effect on classification, however, is classifier-dependent rather than uniformly beneficial: random forest improved from 68.75% to 76.67% accuracy, decision tree and KNN changed little in accuracy but lost substantial F1, and the SVM co",
                        "Dataset A (individual gait cycles, 167 autistic and 111 typically developed): random forest 68.75% accuracy / 80.39% recall / 73.21% precision / 76.63% F1; SVM 63.75 / 69.57 / 85.71 / 76.80; decision tree 63.75 / 76.47 / 69.64 / 72.90; KNN 58.75 / 78.05 / 57.14 / 65.98. Dataset B (trial averages, 60 autistic and 45 typically developed): random forest 76.67 / 86.67 / 72.22 / 78.79; decision tree 63" ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy, recall, precision and F1, all from scikit-learn's metrics module, reported for the best grid-search model of each classifier under each dataset. Accuracy and F1 are designated the primary criteria. No AUC, no confusion matrices, no confidence intervals, no statistical comparison between co" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Dataset A (individual gait cycles, 167 autistic and 111 typically developed): random forest 68.75% accuracy / 80.39% recall / 73.21% precision / 76.63% F1; SVM 63.75 / 69.57 / 85.71 / 76.80; decision tree 63.75 / 76.47 / 69.64 / 72.90; KNN 58.75 / 78.05 / 57.14 / 65.98. Dataset B (trial averages, 60 autistic and 45 typically developed): random forest 76.67 / 86.67 / 72.22 / 78.79; decision tree 63; Averaging gait-phase timings across a walking trial visibly reduces intra-subject variance, most sharply for subjects whose variance was largest to begin with. Its effect on classification, however, is classifier-dependent rather than uniformly beneficial: random forest improved from 68.75% to 76.67% accuracy, decision tree and KNN changed little in accuracy but lost substantial F1, and the SVM co" ] ] ;
