<https://doi.org/10.1002/aur.2636> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "The core symptoms of autism spectrum disorder (ASD) mainly relate to social communication and interactions. ASD assessment involves expert observations in neutral settings, which introduces limitations and biases related to lack of objectivity and does not capture performance in real-world settings. To overcome these limitations, advances in technologies (e.g., virtual reality) and sensors (e.g., eye-tracking tools) have been used to create realistic simulated environments and track eye movements, enriching assessments with more objective data than can be obtained via traditional measures. This study aimed to distinguish between autistic and typically developing children using visual attention behaviors through an eye-tracking paradigm in a virtual environment as a measure of attunement to and extraction of socially relevant information. The 55 children participated. Autistic children presented a higher number of frames, both overall and per scenario, and showed higher visual preferences for adults over children, as well as specific preferences for adults’ rather than children’s faces on which looked more at bodies. A set of multivariate supervised machine learning models were developed using recursive feature selection to recognize ASD based on extracted eye gaze features. The models achieved up to 86% accuracy (sensitivity = 91%) in recognizing autistic children. Our results should be taken as preliminary due to the relatively small sample size and the lack of an external replication dataset. However, to our knowledge, this constitutes a first proof of concept in the combined use of virtual reality, eye-tracking tools, and machine learning for ASD recognition. Lay Summary Core symptoms in children with ASD involve social communication and interaction. ASD assessment includes expert observations in neutral settings, which show limitations and biases related to lack of objectivity and do not capture performance in real settings. To overcome these limitations, this work aimed to distinguish between autistic and typically developing children in visual attention behaviors through an eye-tracking paradigm in a virtual environment as a measure of attunement to, and extraction of, socially relevant information. K E Y W O R D S autism spectrum disorder, behavioral biomarker, eye tracking, machine learning, multivariate supervised learning, virtual reality Received: 26 May 2021 Accepted: 28 October 2021 DOI: 10.1002/aur.2636 This is an open access article under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits use, distribution and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited. © 2021 The Authors. Autism Research published by International Society for Autism Research and Wiley Periodicals LLC. Autism Research. 2022;15:131–145. wileyonlinelibrary.com/journal/aur" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Alcañiz M; Chicchi‐Giglioli IA; Carrasco‐Ribelles LA; Marín‐Morales J; Minissi ME; Teruel‐García G; Sirera M; Abad L (2021). Eye gaze as a biomarker in the recognition of autism spectrum disorder using virtual reality and machine learning: A proof of concept for diagnosis. *Autism Research, 15(1), 131-145*. https://doi.org/10.1002/aur.2636" ;
    dcterms:creator "Abad L",
        "Alcañiz M",
        "Carrasco‐Ribelles LA",
        "Chicchi‐Giglioli IA",
        "Marín‐Morales J",
        "Minissi ME",
        "Sirera M",
        "Teruel‐García G" ;
    dcterms:title "Eye gaze as a biomarker in the recognition of autism spectrum disorder using virtual reality and machine learning: A proof of concept for diagnosis" ;
    bibo:doi "10.1002/aur.2636" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_36617ec5" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2021 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Group sizes, ages with SDs, recruitment sources and ocular exclusion criteria given; sex, IQ and severity scores absent." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "55 children against 144 features, with no independent sample, acknowledged by the authors as limiting." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the functional level of the autistic sample characterised?" ;
                            slr:justification "ADOS-2 module 1 indicates minimally verbal participants, but no scores or language measures are given." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is sex composition reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Sex is not reported for either group anywhere in the paper." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "Six-month age difference untested, and the authors state groups were not matched on sociodemographics, IQ or cognitive ability." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "ADOS-2 with module specified and ADI-R, with reliabilities documented and stated to have been applied to both groups." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "All 16 scenes with content and duration, CAVE geometry and hardware, child positioning, counterbalancing, calibration and acceptance verification, and the head-motion compensation mechanism." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "Wrapper feature selection is label-driven and shares the cross-validation used for reporting." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the paradigm designed around the population's needs?" ;
                            slr:justification "Forest interludes inserted to prevent sensory overload, glasses acceptance verified before entry, and researcher intervention limited to device failure or cybersickness." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is overfitting guarded against in the selection?" ;
                            slr:justification "Selection capped at ten features and default hyperparameters used deliberately to avoid an extra search dimension." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is kappa reported alongside accuracy?" ;
                            slr:justification "Reported consistently for every model, appropriate given the 35:20 imbalance." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is an independent sample tested?" ;
                            slr:justification "Stated explicitly by the authors that all participants were used in validation and no independent sample exists." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is performance estimated over repeated resampling?" ;
                            slr:justification "Five-fold cross-validation repeated four times with standard deviations on all six metrics." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is variability across iterations interpreted?" ;
                            slr:justification "The significant-features model is identified as most consistent on the basis of lowest standard deviations." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is feature and model selection separated from performance estimation?" ;
                            slr:justification "Both chosen using the same repeated cross-validation, across five algorithms and ten datasets." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Software named; no code, and selected feature identities deferred to a supplementary table." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a contrary result reported and defended?" ;
                            slr:justification "The reversal of the expected social-attention direction is stated plainly, situated against a meta-analysis and explained by social-content level and paradigm ecology." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Ethics committee approval, informed consent from relatives, and separate explicit consent to video recording of the child." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No release or availability statement." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are deployment barriers stated honestly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Glasses may be rejected by young children, and both the glasses and CAVE assembly are described as prohibitively expensive for clinical use." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are limitations stated candidly?" ;
                            slr:justification "No independent sample, unmatched groups, macro-area limitations, computational cost and hardware cost all named." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A three-wall CAVE of 4 x 4 x 3 m at the Institute for Research and Innovation in Bioengineering, Polytechnic University of Valencia, with autistic participants from the Red Cenit Neurocognitive Development Center and comparison children recruited by a management company. Funded by the Spanish Ministry of Economy, Industry and Competitiveness project 'Immersive Virtual Environment for the Evaluation and Training of Children with Autism Spectrum Disorder: T Room'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-063" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No cross-modal fusion of sensor streams, but the stimulus is genuinely multisensory - visual, auditory and olfactory channels are synchronised, with odour presence and intensity under programmatic control. The analytic combination is across feature families, and the hypothesis-contrast approach makes it systematic: eight predefined families are modelled separately so their discriminative value can be compared. The resulting ordering is informative. Coarse social-versus-non-social contrasts perform best - people versus items at 84%, adults versus items at 83%, children versus items at 82% - while fine-grained within-social contrasts perform worst, with adults' faces versus bodies reaching only 66% and kappa 0.25. The authors draw the correct conclusion, that macro-area definitions capture the signal but cannot resolve face-internal patterns, and recommend adding eye and mouth areas." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-063" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: autism spectrum disorder, behavioral biomarker, eye tracking, machine learning, multivariate supervised learning, virtual reality. 'Behavioral biomarker' is the organising concept - the paper's framing is that gaze can serve as an implicit, unconscious indicator complementing explicit symptom observation, situated alongside genetic, neural and physiological biomarker classes." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-063" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Strong on protocol, weaker on results provenance. Reported: every scene with its content and duration; CAVE dimensions, projector, audio and olfactory hardware; child positioning; the counterbalancing and the sensory-overload interludes; the calibration and acceptance procedure; the eye tracker's head-motion compensation; the hierarchical area-of-interest structure; all five feature families with construction rules including the difference features; the normality testing and choice between t-test and Mann-Whitney; the step backward wrapper with its ten-feature cap; the repeated cross-validation scheme; and six metrics with standard deviations for all ten datasets. Missing: participant sex, ADOS-2 and ADI-R scores, the identity of the selected features in the winning models (deferred to a supplementary table), any data or code release, and reconciliation of how many total model fits the reported maxima were selected from." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-063" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "144 frame-count features spanning general, background, area-of-interest, person and item families, each computed globally and per scene, plus explicitly constructed difference features contrasting faces against bodies, children against adults, avatar against other characters, dynamic against static items, and people against items. Building the contrasts directly into the feature set rather than leaving them for the model to discover is a sensible choice at this sample size. The unit throughout is the number of frames in which something was seen, which measures how much of the session was spent looking at a category but discards fixation structure, dwell duration and sequence - a limitation the authors partly recognise in calling for finer area definitions." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-063" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Five classifiers - naive Bayes, XGBoost, kNN, random forest and SVM - trained on each of ten datasets, with the best per dataset reported. Default hyperparameters were used throughout, a decision the authors justify explicitly as avoiding an additional layer of search complexity. That is defensible and unusually candid, though it means the algorithm comparison reflects default behaviour rather than tuned potential, and different algorithms win on different datasets (SVM on two, kNN on two, naive Bayes on two, random forest and XGBoost on one each), which is what one would expect when nothing is tuned and sample sizes are small." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-063" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Acknowledged with unusual completeness. The sample is small, the feature count high, and 'the models were not applied to an independent sample'; the authors state directly that 'data from all participants were used to validate the model; therefore, the reported results correspond to the cross-validation process', offering the 20 iterations and low standard deviations as partial mitigation. Groups were not matched on sociodemographics, IQ or cognitive ability. The macro-level area-of-interest approach 'presented some limitations in terms of recognition of specific eye gaze patterns related to specific areas, such as face areas (i.e., eyes and mouth)'. Eye-tracking glasses may not be accepted by young children and both they and the CAVE are expensive, limiting clinical deployment. Training took about 20 hours even parallelised. Not acknowledged: the feature subset and model were both chosen on the same repeated cross-validation that produces the reported metrics, across five algorithms and ten datasets, so the headline is a maximum over roughly fifty fits; participant sex is never reported; and the minimally verbal composition implied by ADOS-2 module 1 may bound the generality of the reversed social-attention finding." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-063" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Interpretability comes from two complementary analyses rather than from any model-explanation method. Univariate testing with normality checking identifies 13 significantly differing features and localises them to specific scenes, and the eight hypothesis-contrast datasets rank feature families by discriminative value. Together these support a specific claim: coarse social-versus-non-social attention differentiates the groups while fine facial detail does not, at least at the area granularity used. The interpretation of the reversed direction is the most substantive interpretive work in the paper, connecting the finding to social-content level, to the static-versus-dynamic stimulus distinction, and to the argument that 'findings related to offline social cognition may not be generalizable across contexts'. The authors position their paradigm as 'an effective middle point' between contrived screen tasks and uncontrollable real settings." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-063" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; a single 24-minute session per child with no follow-up. Data are first-person video of identifiable children moving through a monitored environment. Governance is properly reported: informed consent from relatives after explanation of objectives and procedure, separate explicit consent to video recording of the child, ethics committee approval at the Polytechnic University of Valencia, and Helsinki conformity. A researcher remained present to monitor the child but 'attempted to intervene as little as possible (only in situations of device failure or cybersickness)' - the only mention of cybersickness in the corpus and a real consideration for immersive paradigms with children." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-063" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Larger samples with testing on an independent dataset; treatment of sociodemographics, IQ and cognitive ability as moderators; refinement of areas of interest to include eyes and mouth, and adoption of a bottom-up data-driven definition rather than predefined macro-areas; development of a portable system with integrated remote eye tracking to replace glasses that young children may reject and a CAVE that is prohibitively expensive to assemble; and inclusion of other neurodevelopmental conditions such as ADHD to sharpen discrimination and characterise severity gradations." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-063" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Visual attention to social and non-social content during free exploration of a rich environment, decomposed hierarchically: people versus items versus background; within people, the main interacting avatar versus other characters, adults versus children, faces versus bodies; within items, dynamic versus static. Because features are computed both globally and per scene, the analysis can locate where in the 16-scene sequence group differences arise - and the significant differences cluster in the hall, the electronics shop and the game centre rather than being distributed evenly, which is information a whole-session summary would lose." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-063" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Autistic group: 35 children, mean age 5.26 +/- 0.51 years. Typically developing group: 20 children, mean age 4.75 +/- 0.77 years. Sex is not reported for either group anywhere in the paper - a striking omission in autism research. The six-month age difference is untested. Most importantly, the authors state directly that 'experimental groups were not matched on sociodemographics, IQ, or cognitive ability, limiting the generalization of the model', and recommend treating these as moderators in future work. The only stated exclusion criteria were wearing glasses or having an ocular alteration or pathology. ADOS-2 module 1 administration indicates the autistic sample is minimally verbal, which is important context for the study's central finding." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-063" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: determine whether gaze in an immersive virtual environment distinguishes autistic from typically developing children and which parameters do so. Main findings: 86% accuracy with 91% sensitivity and AUC 0.91 using all features; autistic children looked at social elements in more frames than typically developing children, contrary to most prior work; they also attended more to static non-social items; people-versus-items contrasts discriminated best at 84% while adults'-faces-versus-bodies discriminated worst at 66%; and the model restricted to the 13 statistically significant features was less accurate but the most stable across iterations." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-063" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups totalling 55 children aged 4-7: 35 autistic recruited from the Red Cenit Neurocognitive Development Center in Valencia and 20 typically developing recruited by a management company through calls and mailings to families. The 35:20 imbalance is not addressed by any balancing procedure. Both groups are stated to have received the same evaluation scales and procedure before the experiment. No other-diagnosis comparison group, though the authors name ADHD inclusion as a priority for future work specifically to 'identify with greater precision the degrees of severity in each disorder'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-063" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "ADOS-2 and ADI-R, both described in detail with their psychometric properties - ADOS-2 test-retest reliability of 0.87 for social impairment, 0.64 for repetitive behaviour and 0.88 for the global index; ADI-R ranging 0.93-0.97 - and the paper states that both groups were 'individually evaluated with the same scales and procedure prior to the experiments'. This is a strong ascertainment on paper. The gap is that no scores are reported for any participant or group, so neither the severity distribution nor the separation between groups can be inspected, and the assertion that typically developing children received the same instruments cannot be checked against data. ADOS-2 module 1 is specified, which locates the autistic sample among children who do not use coherent phrase language." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-063" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "ADOS-2 and ADI-R administered but with no scores reported and no severity-stratified analysis. No IQ, cognitive, adaptive or language measures, and the authors identify the absence of IQ and cognitive matching as a limitation requiring attention in future work. The only functional indicator available is the choice of ADOS-2 module 1." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-063" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'autistic children', 'children with ASD', 'typically developing (TD) children', 'autistic participants'. Predominantly identity-first ('autistic children') rather than person-first, which is unusual in this corpus and aligns with neurodiversity-community preference. The framing is measurement-oriented rather than deficit-oriented, centred on the argument that clinical assessment 'relies on children's observable behaviors and parents' interview responses, which clinicians rate according to their expertise and subjectivity', and that 'social desirability bias has been found to affect the veracity of parents' responses'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-063" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Six metrics - accuracy, kappa, AUC, F1, sensitivity and specificity - each with a standard deviation across 20 cross-validation iterations, reported for the best model on every one of ten datasets. Including kappa alongside accuracy is appropriate given the 35:20 imbalance and is done consistently. The standard deviations are used substantively: the model on the 13 significant features is noted to have 'the lowest standard deviations, showing the highest consistency', which is a real finding distinct from mean accuracy. Missing: confusion matrices, confidence intervals, and any statistical comparison between the ten datasets, so whether 0.86 differs meaningfully from 0.84 on 55 children is not established - and the standard deviation of 0.10 on the best model spans that gap." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-063" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A three-wall CAVE with three ultrashort-throw projectors, Logitech Z906 5.1 audio and a wireless Olorama olfactory system regulating odour presence and intensity; Tobii Pro Glasses 2 with a scene camera, two cameras per eye using a 3D eye model, and an accelerometer and gyroscope allowing head and eye movement to be separated so that head motion does not contaminate gaze data - a technical requirement for free movement that screen-based trackers cannot meet. Glasses were fitted and calibrated in an adjacent room, with researchers verifying both correct operation and that 'the subject did not reject them' before the child entered the CAVE. Analysis in R 3.6.1." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-063" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The most elaborate elicitation paradigm in the corpus: a 24-minute 45-second multisensory virtual mall with 16 scripted scenes and synchronised visual, auditory and olfactory channels. The scenes are designed to sample distinct social demands - an avatar greeting the child and asking their name, peekaboo through shop displays, a choice offered between an apple and a lollipop, explicit joint-attention bids where the avatar indicates three times verbally and by gesture that the child should look left, an emotional display of disgust at a trash can, and a startling event where a train nearly runs over the avatar. Two design decisions deserve note: forest scenes with relaxing music were inserted at the start and between stores specifically 'to avoid cognitive and sensory overload', and scene order was counterbalanced across participants. This is elicitation designed around the population's sensory needs rather than only around the constructs being measured." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-063" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Autism Research, 15(1), 131-145 - the journal of the International Society for Autism Research, published open access under CC BY. Publishing in a specialist autism venue rather than an engineering one is consistent with the paper's substantive engagement with the social-attention literature and its willingness to contest an established finding." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-063" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2021. The framing combines three arguments. On objectivity, that assessment 'relies on children's observable behaviors and parents' interview responses, which clinicians rate according to their expertise and subjectivity', with the specific addition that 'social desirability bias has been found to affect the veracity of parents' responses'. On ecological validity, that assessment 'occurs in laboratories or clinical settings lacking ecological validity that could offer a deeper understanding of real-life abilities'. And on timing, that 'many ASD dimensions are internal and do not manifest until 2-3 years of age', lengthening diagnosis. Its most valuable contribution to the corpus's framing is a careful review of the inconsistency in the social-attention literature: it catalogues studies finding reduced social attention, four finding increased social preference, and eight finding no difference, and identifies the moderators as static versus dynamic stimuli and level of social content - citing meta-analytic evidence that autistic children attended less to social stimuli specifically 'when the social content was higher (i.e., the number of people exceeded one)'. Its own contrary result is then argued within that framework rather than against it." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-063" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Five-fold cross-validation repeated four times with means and standard deviations reported, which is better practice than single-split evaluation and gives some sense of variability. The limitation is stated by the authors themselves - no independent sample exists and all participants contributed to validation - and compounded by the fact that both the feature subset and the model were selected on that same repeated cross-validation, across five algorithms and ten datasets. The reported per-dataset maxima are therefore optimistically biased, and the general-approach figure of 0.86 is the best of roughly fifty fits. The ten-feature cap on selection is a sensible guard against overfitting given 144 candidates and 55 participants, and using default hyperparameters removes one further source of selection. There is no external site, no independent cohort and no held-out replication." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-063" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "One session per child of 24 minutes 45 seconds, comprising 16 scenes ranging from 10 to 180 seconds each" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional case-control classification with a general modelling approach and a parallel hypothesis-contrast approach testing eight predefined feature families" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Preschool children with autism, minimally verbal" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 35; comparison: 20" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Gaze recorded in a 24-minute multisensory virtual mall distinguished autistic from typically developing preschoolers at up to 86% accuracy with 91% sensitivity. The substantive finding runs against the prevailing literature: autistic children looked at social elements in more frames than typically developing children, both overall and within scenes, and also attended more to static non-social item",
                        "General approach: all 144 features with SVM and nine selected features gave 0.86 accuracy (SD 0.10), kappa 0.69, AUC 0.91, F1 0.87, sensitivity 0.91, specificity 0.82; restricting to the 13 statistically significant features and using kNN gave 0.77 accuracy, kappa 0.52, AUC 0.83, sensitivity 0.78, specificity 0.85 - notably with the lowest standard deviations of any model. Hypothesis-contrast appr" ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy, Cohen's kappa, AUC, F1, sensitivity and specificity, each with a standard deviation across the 20 iterations, reported for the best model on each of the ten datasets" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "General approach: all 144 features with SVM and nine selected features gave 0.86 accuracy (SD 0.10), kappa 0.69, AUC 0.91, F1 0.87, sensitivity 0.91, specificity 0.82; restricting to the 13 statistically significant features and using kNN gave 0.77 accuracy, kappa 0.52, AUC 0.83, sensitivity 0.78, specificity 0.85 - notably with the lowest standard deviations of any model. Hypothesis-contrast appr; Gaze recorded in a 24-minute multisensory virtual mall distinguished autistic from typically developing preschoolers at up to 86% accuracy with 91% sensitivity. The substantive finding runs against the prevailing literature: autistic children looked at social elements in more frames than typically developing children, both overall and within scenes, and also attended more to static non-social item" ] ] ;
