<https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cognition.2021.104652> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "In autism spectrum disorder (ASD), socio-communicative impairments and stereotypical behaviours are paral­ leled by sensorimotor deficits. Individuals with ASD show an altered selection of motor parameters, resulting in clumsy and fragmented actions. Here, we investigated inter-joint coordination and motor synergies as a potential substrate of motor control problems in ASD. Synergies enable co-controlling redundant motor degrees of freedom (DoF, e.g. joint angles, muscles) by mapping behavioural goals into a flexible and low-dimensional set of vari­ ables. This mechanism simplifies motor control and helps to find unambiguous solutions for motor tasks. In a reaching-grasping paradigm, children with ASD showed reduced coupling between DoF, which correlated with socio-communicative symptoms severity. Impaired synergies may help to frame well-established motor problems in ASD, including impaired motor sequencing and abnormal trial-to-trial motor variability. On the other hand, synergies also provide an effective and compact coding system of observed actions. Impaired synergies may thus jeopardize motor interaction by initiating bottom-up cascade effects, leading to pervasive impairments of social behaviour. Finally, we trained an automatic classification algorithm to distinguish between ASD and typically developing (TD) participants based on reaching-grasping kinematics. Classification accuracy reached up to 0.947. This result corroborates and expands previous accounts claiming that motor-based early recognition is feasible and effective in ASD." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Emanuele M; Nazzaro G; Marini M; Veronesi C; Boni S; Polletta G; D'Ausilio A; Fadiga L (2021). Motor synergies: Evidence for a novel motor signature in autism spectrum disorder. *Cognition, 213, Article 104652*. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cognition.2021.104652" ;
    dcterms:creator "Boni S",
        "D'Ausilio A",
        "Emanuele M",
        "Fadiga L",
        "Marini M",
        "Nazzaro G",
        "Polletta G",
        "Veronesi C" ;
    dcterms:title "Motor synergies: Evidence for a novel motor signature in autism spectrum disorder" ;
    bibo:doi "10.1016/j.cognition.2021.104652" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_0ab41681" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2021 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Per-participant table giving ADOS total and module, age, sex, ICD-10 diagnosis code, comorbidity and handedness for all 26 children." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the comparison group assessed with the same instrument?" ;
                            slr:justification "ADOS administered to all typically developing children, all scoring below threshold with ten scoring zero." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "Age formally matched and tested; sex differs by 31 percentage points untested; no cognitive measures obtained." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comorbidities reported rather than excluded?" ;
                            slr:justification "Four children with language disorder and one with epilepsy retained and identified individually." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "13 per group, acknowledged by the authors as unable to capture autism's heterogeneity." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the measurement instrument high-fidelity?" ;
                            slr:justification "Three-camera optoelectronic capture at 200 Hz with visual verification of marker labelling." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the construct measured directly rather than by proxy?" ;
                            slr:justification "Motor synergy is operationalised as variance accounted for by principal components across nine measured joint angles." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "All 17 features are pre-specified physical quantities computed automatically, with no label-driven selection." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "ICD-10 and DSM-5 clinical diagnosis corroborated by ADOS in both groups." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Task geometry, stimulus dimensions, block structure, camera count and sampling rate, filter parameters, marker anatomy, kinematic model and movement-boundary criteria all specified." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is robustness under deployment constraints tested?" ;
                            slr:justification "Performance evaluated under reduced test-trial counts and with features from the ulnar marker alone." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are non-normal outcomes handled appropriately?" ;
                            slr:justification "Shapiro-Wilk testing followed by permutation tests with 5,000 permutations for the two non-normal measures." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the operating point independent of the evaluation?" ;
                            slr:justification "Sensitivity and specificity are reported at the optimal point of the same ROC used to characterise performance." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are preprocessing and hyperparameters confined to training folds?" ;
                            slr:justification "Normalisation fitted on training data and applied to test data using training statistics; grid search performed at each iteration." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the distribution of per-participant performance reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Accuracy banded across children, showing five of 26 classified below 0.6." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the validation split at the participant level?" ;
                            slr:justification "Leave-one-subject-out with all of a participant's trials held out together, explicitly justified as simulating a new patient." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Written informed consent from parents or guardians, Helsinki-compliant design, and local ethics committee approval with a full reference number." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No release or availability statement." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the finding linked to symptom severity rather than group label alone?" ;
                            slr:justification "All four kinematic measures correlated against ADOS totals within the autistic group, with only the synergy measure significant." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are limitations stated candidly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Sample size, absent cognitive measures and absent other-diagnosis comparison groups all named, with specific rationale for each." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are reported figures labelled accurately?" ;
                            slr:justification "The abstract describes 0.947 as classification accuracy when it is the subject-level AUC; trial-level accuracy is 0.739." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Libraries named and the pipeline fully described; no code." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cognition, 213, article 104652, published open access in a special issue honouring the journal's founding editor - a cognitive science venue rather than a clinical or engineering one, which explains the depth of theoretical development relative to the modelling." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-066" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: Autism spectrum disorder, Motor synergies, Machine learning, Kinematics, Early diagnosis, Neurodevelopmental disorders. 'Motor synergies' is the novel term and the paper's claim - the authors state their result indicates 'for the first time, that motor synergies are impaired in ASD'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-066" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Leave-one-subject-out, correctly implemented and explicitly motivated as simulating 'the diagnosis of a new patient in everyday life clinical settings'. Normalisation is fitted on training folds and applied to test data using training statistics, and hyperparameters are grid-searched at each iteration rather than fixed globally. Two robustness analyses extend the validation usefully - varying the number of trials available in the test set, and restricting features to those derivable from the ulnar marker alone - both framed around deployment constraints rather than as statistical exercises. The residual issues are that the ROC operating point is chosen on the evaluation curve, and that no independent cohort or external site is tested, which the small sample precludes." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-066" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Autistic group: 13 children, 8 male and 5 female, aged 44-82 months, mean 66.23 +/- 11.56. Typically developing group: 13 children, 4 male and 9 female, aged 43-71 months, mean 60.08 +/- 9.54. Age matching is formally tested and confirmed (t(24) = 1.480, p = 0.152). Sex is not: 38% female against 69% female, an inversion of the usual direction, reported per participant but never tested or mentioned. Handedness was assessed behaviourally by asking children to draw and confirmed by caregivers or clinicians, with two left-handed children and two of unclear handedness, and left-hand trials from three autistic children were discarded because markers could not be placed on their left arms due to compliance problems. Per-participant ADOS totals, modules, ages, sexes, ICD-10 codes, comorbidities and handedness are all tabulated - the most complete individual-level characterisation in the corpus. No IQ or cognitive measures, which the authors name as a limitation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-066" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Measure intellectual disability as a source of inter-subject variability affecting motor planning and coordination; include developmental coordination disorder and ADHD comparison groups to test whether impaired synergies represent 'a common pathogenetic mechanism for these conditions' rather than an autism-specific marker; and extend the synergy framework to speech and language, on the argument that 'speech and language coordination share key similarity with upper limb action' and that language difficulties are common in autism." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-066" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Acknowledged: the sample was 'relatively small and likely not able to capture the full heterogeneity of ASD'; intellectual disability was not measured despite being a known source of variability affecting motor planning and coordination; and only autism and typical development were contrasted, whereas motor difficulties occur across neurodevelopmental disorders so additional diagnostic classes would 'facilitate a dimensional acknowledgment of atypical development'. Practical constraints are also disclosed, including that left-arm markers could not be placed on three autistic children due to compliance problems. Not acknowledged: the abstract reports 0.947 as classification accuracy when it is the subject-level AUC, with trial-level accuracy being 0.739; the sensitivity and specificity are taken at an operating point optimised on the same ROC used to characterise performance; the sex imbalance of 38% versus 69% female is tabulated but never tested or discussed; and the severity correlation rests on 13 observations with a visibly influential high-ADOS point." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-066" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with ASD', 'individuals with ASD', 'typically developing (TD) children', 'neurotypical subjects', 'participants with ASD'. Consistently person-first with 'neurotypical' used for the general comparison. The framing is developmental and mechanistic rather than deficit-cataloguing - autism's heterogeneity is described at length, aetiology is acknowledged as 'far from being understood', and the theoretical argument runs from low-order sensorimotor processing upward to social cognition rather than treating social impairment as primary." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-066" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "ADOS total scores individually reported for all 26 participants with the module used, plus ICD-10 diagnostic subtype and comorbid diagnoses per child. Severity is used analytically rather than descriptively - correlated against all four kinematic outcomes - which is what produces the paper's key dissociation. The acknowledged gap is intellectual functioning: no IQ or developmental level was measured, and the authors state that future research 'will have to evaluate intellectual disability, which is an important source of inter-subject variability in ASD that seems to impact motor planning and coordination'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-066" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Upper-limb reaching and grasping, analysed at two levels. Conventional kinematics capture the surface properties of movement - speed, velocity-profile shape, endpoint variability. The synergy analysis captures something different in kind: how tightly the nine joint angles covary, indexed by how much of their joint variance three principal components can absorb. That second level is the paper's contribution, and it targets a construct - co-control of redundant degrees of freedom - that no other study in this corpus measures." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-066" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups of 13 children each. The autistic group spans three ICD-10 diagnoses - six with F84.9 pervasive developmental disorder unspecified, six with F84.0 autistic disorder and one with F84.5 Asperger syndrome - and includes four children with comorbid mixed receptive-expressive language disorder and one with epilepsy, all reported per participant rather than excluded. No other-diagnosis comparison group, though the authors identify developmental coordination disorder and ADHD as necessary additions for establishing whether the motor finding is autism-specific or shared across neurodevelopmental conditions." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-066" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Diagnosis by an experienced clinician under both ICD-10 and DSM-5, with specific ICD-10 subcodes reported per child. ADOS was then administered to every participant in both groups, with autistic children meeting the diagnostic cut-off and typically developing children scoring below it - ten of the thirteen scored zero, and the highest comparison score was 3. Applying the gold-standard instrument to both groups and publishing individual scores is stronger practice than almost anything else in the corpus. The authors also report ADOS's own published operating characteristics (sensitivity 0.94, specificity 0.80 across 12 studies) and use them as the benchmark against which to judge their classifier." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-066" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Qualisys optoelectronic motion capture with three infrared cameras at 200 Hz - the highest-fidelity motion measurement in the corpus - with ten passive markers on the upper limbs and one on the sternum, low-pass filtered at 10 Hz with a third-order Butterworth filter. Marker labelling was verified by offline visual inspection, and the movement onset and offset criteria were themselves validated by visual inspection of individual trials. The robustness analysis using only the ulnar marker is a direct response to the practical constraint that autistic children may not tolerate a full marker set." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-066" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Excellent. Reported: task geometry with all distances, stimulus dimensions, block structure and randomisation; camera count, sampling rate, filter type and cutoff; marker placement anatomy; the five-segment kinematic model with all nine joint angles and their derivation including the pronosupination computation; movement onset and offset criteria with their thresholds and durations; PCA preprocessing including time-normalisation to 200 points and per-trial z-scoring with its stated rationale; the justification for retaining three components; the full ANOVA model with both factors; permutation testing for non-normal outcomes with iteration count; all 17 classifier features defined individually in a table; the SVM kernel and full hyperparameter grid; the leave-one-subject-out procedure including how normalisation was applied across the split; and per-participant clinical data. Supplementary material covers pronosupination derivation, trial-count robustness, feature-restricted robustness and per-participant feature importance. Missing only: data or code release, and IQ measures." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-066" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A single radial-basis-function SVM with hyperparameters grid-searched at each leave-one-subject-out iteration. No model comparison is reported, which is appropriate given that classification is explicitly secondary to the mechanistic question and the paper makes no claim about optimal architecture. The design decision that matters is aggregation: the classifier operates on trials, and subject-level inference is obtained by taking each participant's proportion of positive trial responses as an estimate of diagnosis probability, from which the ROC is built." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-066" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Reaching for and grasping a felt-tip pen to complete a printed figure - a task chosen because it is ecological and needs no explanation to a preschooler, with pen orientation varied across four positions specifically to prevent stereotyped movement. Forty trials per child across eight blocks in two sessions, with block order randomised and free choice of hand and grip. The design decisions are all in service of the mechanistic question: enough trials to estimate trial-to-trial variability and to fit trial-wise PCA, enough orientation variation to require genuine motor parameter selection rather than a rehearsed movement, and enough naturalness that task comprehension is not a confound." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-066" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Interpretability is the paper's organising purpose. Every feature is a physical quantity with a motor-control meaning, the group differences are reported with effect directions and interpreted against specific literatures on feedforward versus feedback control, and the synergy result is developed into an explicit mechanistic account - that impaired co-control forces either learning motor commands for each degree of freedom separately, which is prohibitively slow, or mapping each behavioural goal to a whole set of muscle activations, which prevents generalisation, with either extreme producing the idiosyncratic and fragmented motor strategies observed clinically. The correlation analysis then provides the discriminating evidence, showing that only the synergy measure tracks severity. Per-participant feature importance is computed and reported, and the authors verify that it agrees with the univariate findings." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-066" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: test whether motor synergies - the co-control of redundant degrees of freedom - are impaired in autism, and relate this to symptom severity. Main findings: autistic children moved faster with more symmetrical velocity profiles, showed greater trial-to-trial spatial variability, and showed significantly reduced variance accounted for by the first three principal components across nine joint angles; only the synergy measure correlated with ADOS severity (rho = -0.650, p = 0.016); and leave-one-subject-out SVM classification reached AUC 0.947 with 0.846 sensitivity and 0.923 specificity at the optimal operating point, on trial-level accuracy of 0.739." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-066" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2021. The framing is developmental and causal rather than diagnostic-efficiency driven. It argues that 'low-order impairments of sensory processing and motor coding during the early stages of life may impact the acquisition of more complex abilities, including socio-communicative skills and cognitive functions', and assembles evidence of pervasive sensorimotor impairment across posture, balance, gait, aiming movements, eye-hand coordination, force control and handwriting - proposing that these 'scattered disturbances presumably arise from a limited number of common dysfunctional mechanisms'. Its most distinctive contribution to the corpus's framing is a methodological criticism aimed at its own field: that standardised symptom assessments 'are often only used to assign group labels (i.e. ASD vs. neurotypical)', and that 'this approach has prevented understanding the impact of motor differences in the heterogeneous expression of ASD symptoms'. The paper then acts on that criticism by correlating every kinematic measure against ADOS totals within the autistic group, which is what yields its central dissociation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-066" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No multimodal fusion. What the study combines is levels of description within a single modality, and it does so to informative effect: the 17 classifier features include both conventional kinematics and the three synergy measures, and the per-participant feature importance analysis finds that spatial displacement measures and the cumulative variance accounted for by three components both contribute. The authors note that this 'substantially confirmed the results obtained with univariate statistics', so the classifier's internal weighting agrees with the group-level analysis - a convergence that few studies in the corpus check and that here supports treating the classification as reflecting the same phenomenon as the hypothesis test rather than an unrelated pattern." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-066" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Trial-level accuracy reported as a mean and in bands across participants, plus a subject-level ROC with AUC and the sensitivity and specificity at its optimal operating point. Reporting the distribution of per-child accuracy rather than only the mean is valuable - it shows five of 26 children classified below 0.6, which a summary figure would hide. The weakness is presentational: the abstract's 'classification accuracy reached up to 0.947' conflates AUC with accuracy, no subject-level accuracy is given directly, and the operating point yielding 0.846 sensitivity and 0.923 specificity is selected on the same curve. Comparison against ADOS's own published sensitivity and specificity is a useful external anchor that few studies provide." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-066" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; two sessions per child with no follow-up. Data are numeric marker trajectories, with per-participant clinical data published in tabular form but no identifiers. Governance is complete: written informed consent from parents or legal guardians, Helsinki-compliant design, and local ethical committee approval with a full reference number." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-066" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Laboratory motion capture at the University of Ferrara and the Istituto Italiano di Tecnologia's Center for Translational Neurophysiology of Speech and Communication, with autistic children recruited from a local outpatient neurodevelopmental clinic and comparison children from a local kindergarten. Funded by two Italian Ministry of Health young-researcher grants and an EU H2020 project. No competing interests declared." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-066" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Seventeen trial-level features, each defined explicitly in a table, spanning movement timing, peak dynamics and their relative timing, spatial displacement at both peak velocity and movement offset, trajectory length, and the three cumulative variance-accounted-for measures. The representation is compact, physically interpretable and includes the theoretically motivated coordination variables alongside the descriptive ones. The PCA preprocessing is carefully specified - trial-wise application after time-normalisation to 200 points and per-trial z-scoring, the latter adopted specifically to account for the differing ranges of motion across joints, with the choice of three components justified by reference to prior work on three-dimensional movement." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-066" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Eight blocks of five trials each across two sessions of four blocks, giving 40 trials per child - ten per pen orientation" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional case-control study combining group-level kinematic comparison, within-group severity correlation, and single-subject automatic classification" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Preschool children with autism spectrum diagnoses" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 13; comparison: 13" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Autistic preschoolers showed faster, more ballistic reaching with a more symmetrical velocity profile, greater trial-to-trial spatial variability, and - the paper's central and novel result - weaker coupling between joint degrees of freedom, indicating impaired motor synergies. Only the synergy measure correlated with autism severity (rho = -0.650 against ADOS total), while the conventional kinema",
                        "Average trial-level accuracy 0.739, with accuracy above 0.7 in 15 of 26 children, between 0.6 and 0.7 in 6, and below 0.6 in the remaining 5. At subject level the ROC gave an AUC of 0.947 with sensitivity 0.846 and specificity 0.923 at its optimal operating point. The abstract describes this as 'classification accuracy reached up to 0.947', which conflates AUC with accuracy; the trial-level accura" ;
                    slr:outcome "Trial-level classification accuracy averaged across participants and reported in bands, plus a subject-level ROC built from each participant's proportion of positive trial responses with area under the curve and sensitivity and specificity at the optimal operating point" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Average trial-level accuracy 0.739, with accuracy above 0.7 in 15 of 26 children, between 0.6 and 0.7 in 6, and below 0.6 in the remaining 5. At subject level the ROC gave an AUC of 0.947 with sensitivity 0.846 and specificity 0.923 at its optimal operating point. The abstract describes this as 'classification accuracy reached up to 0.947', which conflates AUC with accuracy; the trial-level accura; Autistic preschoolers showed faster, more ballistic reaching with a more symmetrical velocity profile, greater trial-to-trial spatial variability, and - the paper's central and novel result - weaker coupling between joint degrees of freedom, indicating impaired motor synergies. Only the synergy measure correlated with autism severity (rho = -0.650 against ADOS total), while the conventional kinema" ] ] ;
