<https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-89023-8> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "An advantage of using eye tracking for diagnosis is that it is non-invasive and can be performed in individuals with different functional levels and ages. Computer/aided diagnosis using eye tracking data is commonly based on eye fixation points in some regions of interest (ROI) in an image. However, besides the need for every ROI demarcation in each image or video frame used in the experiment, the diversity of visual features contained in each ROI may compromise the characterization of visual attention in each group (case or control) and consequent diagnosis accuracy. Although some approaches use eye tracking signals for aiding diagnosis, it is still a challenge to identify frames of interest when videos are used as stimuli and to select relevant characteristics extracted from the videos. This is mainly observed in applications for autism spectrum disorder (ASD) diagnosis. To address these issues, the present paper proposes: (1) a computational method, integrating concepts of Visual Attention Model, Image Processing and Artificial Intelligence techniques for learning a model for each group (case and control) using eye tracking data, and (2) a supervised classifier that, using the learned models, performs the diagnosis. Although this approach is not disorder-specific, it was tested in the context of ASD diagnosis, obtaining an average of precision, recall and specificity of 90%, 69%" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Oliveira JS; Franco FO; Revers MC; Silva AF; Portolese J; Brentani H; Machado-Lima A; Nunes FLS (2021). Computer-aided autism diagnosis based on visual attention models using eye tracking. *Scientific Reports, 11, 10131*. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-89023-8" ;
    dcterms:creator "Brentani H",
        "Franco FO",
        "Machado-Lima A",
        "Nunes FLS",
        "Oliveira JS",
        "Portolese J",
        "Revers MC",
        "Silva AF" ;
    dcterms:title "Computer-aided autism diagnosis based on visual attention models using eye tracking" ;
    bibo:doi "10.1038/s41598-021-89023-8" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_241ec2c2" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2021 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "No age comparison reported at all, on a paradigm where biological-versus-geometric preference is developmentally graded." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Group sizes, sexes, recruitment site and per-participant clinical data given; no group-wise ages across a 3-18 year range." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is class imbalance addressed?" ;
                            slr:justification "76:30 ratio unaddressed, which inflates the reported 90% precision relative to a balanced sample." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is comorbidity handled with a stated rationale?" ;
                            slr:justification "Comorbid participants deliberately retained, argued from prevalence data on anxiety and ADHD co-occurrence and their contribution to differing trajectories." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "106 participants is among the larger eye-tracking cohorts in the corpus, and its heterogeneity is argued as supporting generalisability." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Stimulus construction, counterbalancing, viewing distance, calibration and failure policy, capture-rate threshold and the caregiver blindfold control all specified." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is data quality gated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Participants excluded after a second calibration failure, and only those with over 80% video capture retained." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "DSM-5 psychiatric evaluation with CARS applied to both groups and neuropsychological assessment by a trained specialist." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a source of caregiver influence controlled?" ;
                            slr:justification "Adults holding lap-seated children wore a blindfold to avoid cueing the child's gaze." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "Genetic algorithm selection is group-specific and label-driven, with no statement of nesting within folds." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is feature selection nested within folds?" ;
                            slr:justification "Reported once per group with no indication of repetition per fold, although the selected sets are the main interpretive finding." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is stimulus independence addressed?" ;
                            slr:justification "Fifty frames withheld from model learning for diagnosis only, with the same-video contamination objection raised and mitigated by the authors themselves." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the weakest metric discussed?" ;
                            slr:justification "Sensitivity of 69% is reported but never analysed, despite being the figure that governs screening utility." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the decision threshold independent of the evaluation?" ;
                            slr:justification "The 28-vote cut-off was chosen by Youden index on the cross-validated ROC used to report performance." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are alternative learners and selectors compared?" ;
                            slr:justification "Six combinations of classifier and feature selection reported with mean AUC and standard deviation." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the validation split at the participant level?" ;
                            slr:justification "Stated explicitly that no test-subject information enters the learning phase, with cross-validation performed over subjects." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Toolboxes, network specification and hyperparameters given; no code." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Named ethics committee approval with full protocol number, informed consent, Helsinki conformity, and separate consent for publication of identifiable stimulus footage." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are stimulus-specific parameters flagged as such?" ;
                            slr:justification "The optical-flow aggregation threshold is explicitly noted as video-dependent and requiring review for different stimuli." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a nuanced negative result reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Feature selection improved the neural network's stability far more than its mean AUC, and substantially degraded the SVM." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Per-participant clinical data deposited as supplementary material; eye-tracking data not released, with an intention to release stated." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Interpretability is delivered by the per-group feature comparison rather than by any post hoc explanation method, and it works well because the features are semantically meaningful image properties. Three findings are drawn out and connected to prior literature: the frame-centre feature entering only the autistic model matches reports of centre fixation 'even when there was nothing in the center'; Saliency Toolbox features entering both models is read as evidence of biologically general attentional mechanisms; and biological movement entering only the autistic model is explained by redundancy with face and person features in the typically developing model. The absence of any group-level statistics on the features themselves limits how far these can be pushed, and the interpretive claims rest on which features a genetic algorithm happened to retain, which is a noisier basis than a direct comparison would be." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-062" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Visual attention during competition between biological and geometric motion, analysed not as dwell proportions but as a mapping from image properties to fixation probability. The behavioural quantity modelled is which visual features attract a group's gaze, decomposed across low-level saliency (steerable pyramids, colour, intensity, orientation), semantic content (faces, people, skin, horizon), spatial position (distance to screen and scene centres) and motion (optical flow magnitude, biological versus geometric scene membership)." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-062" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'individuals with ASD', 'ASD subjects', 'subjects with and without ASD', 'Typical Development (TD)', 'ASD patients'. Mixed person-first and abbreviated group labels, with 'patients' used when discussing comorbidity prevalence. The framing is neutral and mechanism-oriented, grounding the work in bottom-up and top-down attentional circuits and describing autism through 'alterations in early precursor of social behavior as biological motion, human face preference, and joint attention' rather than through deficit or burden." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-062" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Twenty-eight pixel-level image features reduced to 15 per group by genetic algorithm, computed by averaging feature values across the frames aggregated into each group. The aggregation procedure is the paper's more distinctive representational contribution: because individual video frames contain too few fixations to support learning, consecutive frames are merged wherever mean optical-flow motion falls below 0.33, a threshold derived by visual inspection of when successive frames were nearly identical, and explicitly flagged as needing revision for any different stimulus. Training pixels are then sampled as the 350 most-fixated coordinates against 350 randomly chosen zero-fixation coordinates, giving a balanced 700 per map." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-062" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups totalling 106 children and adolescents: 76 autistic recruited from the Psychiatry Institute of the University of Sao Paulo School of Medicine and 30 typically developing. The class ratio of roughly 2.5:1 favouring autism is not addressed by any balancing procedure and matters for interpreting the reported 90% precision. Notably, participants with psychiatric and neurological comorbidities were deliberately retained rather than excluded, with the authors reasoning from co-occurrence prevalence that excluding them would misrepresent the population. No separate other-diagnosis group, though extending to autism-versus-ADHD discrimination is named as a future direction." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-062" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Clinical evaluation by a child psychiatrist against DSM-5 criteria with CARS-measured severity, plus neuropsychological assessment by a trained neuropsychologist using WISC, the Repetitive Behavior Scale and Vineland scales where possible. CARS was also administered to the comparison group and confirmed below the diagnostic threshold of 30, so typical development is verified rather than assumed. Per-participant clinical information is published as supplementary material. What is absent is ADOS or ADI-R, and no account is given of who performed the CARS or with what reliability." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-062" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Laboratory eye tracking with autistic participants recruited from the Psychiatry Institute of the University of Sao Paulo School of Medicine, in collaboration between computing, psychiatry and bioinformatics units. Ethics approval from the University of Sao Paulo under protocol 57185516.9.0000.5390." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-062" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two small neural networks, one per group, each acting as a generative model of where that group looks, plus a similarity-vote decision rule - the same architecture as the severity study from the same laboratory but applied to diagnosis. SVM was tested as an alternative learner and performed worse (0.775 against 0.818 without selection). The comparison table across six classifier-and-selection combinations is genuinely informative: genetic algorithm selection raised the network's mean AUC only from 0.818 to 0.822 but cut its standard deviation from 0.053 to 0.015, while the same selection dropped the SVM from 0.775 to 0.695. Reporting a case where feature selection helps stability rather than accuracy, and helps one learner while harming another, is more nuanced than most of the corpus manages." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-062" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Continue building the dataset toward public release, with the observation that assembling eye-tracking signals alongside expert clinical evaluation 'is not a trivial task'; evaluate further machine learning techniques and additional features; and extend from case-control comparison to discrimination between two disorders, specifically naming autism versus ADHD - the differential question the corpus almost never addresses." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-062" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Scientific Reports, 11, 10131 - a broad open-access multidisciplinary journal, with authorship spanning a computing school, a university psychiatry department and a bioinformatics postgraduate programme." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-062" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Group sizes and sex splits are given - 76 autistic (49 male, 27 female) and 30 typically developing (20 male, 10 female) - with a single 3-18 year age range for the whole sample. No group-wise ages, means, standard deviations, matching statement or statistical test appears anywhere. The authors treat the breadth as a virtue, describing the dataset as 'heterogeneous in terms of age, gender and CARS' and citing this as a strength relative to comparison studies, which is a legitimate external-validity argument. But a fifteen-year age span with no group comparison is a gap on a paradigm where preference for biological over geometric motion is developmentally graded. Per-participant clinical data including WISC, Repetitive Behavior Scale and Vineland where obtainable are deposited in supplementary material, which is better practice than most of the corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-062" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2021. The framing is a precise methodological critique rather than a general appeal to objectivity. Region-of-interest methods carry two costs: annotation burden, since every ROI must be marked on every frame of every video, and information loss, since aggregating within a region discards 'which visual features of sub parts of an image had a more fixed gaze'. The paper grounds its alternative in attention theory, distinguishing bottom-up circuits driven by image properties from top-down circuits using prior knowledge and social rules, and traces the computational lineage from the Koch model through Itti's implementation to the supervised saliency models of Borji and Judd. It positions its own advance as extending these from static images to video, citing the argument that video 'can contribute with more discoveries because the videos have temporal information'. It also situates itself quantitatively against prior work, tabulating AUCs of 0.71, 0.71 and 0.86 from three published studies against its own 0.82, and noting that its sample is larger and more heterogeneous in age, sex and severity than the comparators." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-062" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Precision, sensitivity, specificity and AUC with fold-wise standard deviation, ROC curves for all five folds, a six-row comparison of classifier and selection combinations, and a table of published AUCs from three comparable studies for external context. Reporting standard deviation across folds is what reveals the stability benefit of feature selection. The gap is that sensitivity of 69% is reported but never discussed, and no confusion matrix or confidence interval accompanies it - on a sample where 76 of 106 participants are autistic, missing 31% of them is the most consequential number in the paper." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-062" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No formal keyword list. Vocabulary: 'visual attention model', 'region of interest', 'saliency map', 'fixation map', 'stimulus independence', 'computer-aided diagnosis'. The recurring conceptual pair is fixation map versus saliency map - what a person actually looked at against what a model predicts a group would look at - and classification is the comparison between them." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-062" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Very good. Reported: stimulus construction with clip counts, durations, resolution and counterbalancing; positioning, calibration procedure and the two-failure exclusion rule; the 80% capture-rate inclusion threshold; the blindfold control for lap-seated children; frame preprocessing including edge removal, resizing to 200 x 350 and transition-frame deletion; the optical-flow aggregation threshold of 0.33 with its empirical derivation and an explicit note that it is video-specific and 'should be reviewed' for different stimuli; the 5 x 5 Gaussian smoothing kernel; the 350-positive and 350-random-negative pixel sampling; all 28 candidate features with their sources; the full network specification including loss, optimiser, learning rate and stopping criteria; the vote-based classification rule; and the subject-level cross-validation with the 50 held-out diagnosis frames. Missing: group-wise ages, whether genetic algorithm selection was nested within folds, and any code or eye-tracking data release." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-062" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The biological-versus-geometric preference paradigm rendered as nine sequential 6-second clips totalling 54 seconds, with the screen split between children interacting and fractal movement, and with side and ordering varied to prevent conditioning. Two protocol details matter: calibration was repeated once on failure and the participant excluded after a second failure, and only subjects with more than 80% of total video time successfully captured were retained - a quantified data-quality gate. Where a child had to sit on an adult's lap, the adult wore a blindfold 'in order to avoid influencing the signals acquired', which is a control against caregiver gaze cueing that no other study in the corpus reports." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-062" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: replace region-of-interest annotation with learned per-group visual attention models over video stimuli, and classify individuals by adherence to those models. Main findings: AUC 0.822 with 90% precision, 93% specificity and 69% sensitivity; genetic algorithm selection retained distance-to-centre and biological movement only for the autistic model and colour only for the typically developing model; selection improved the neural network's fold-to-fold stability far more than its mean AUC while degrading the SVM substantially." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-062" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; a single 54-second viewing session per participant with no follow-up. Data are gaze coordinates over video stimuli. Governance is complete and detailed: named ethics committee approval with full protocol number, informed consent from participants or legal guardians, Helsinki conformity, and separately obtained consent for publication of identifying images from the individuals appearing in the stimulus videos - the last being a consideration almost never addressed elsewhere in this corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-062" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Acknowledged with unusual directness on one point. The authors anticipate the objection that using the same video for training and diagnosis constitutes contamination, state it themselves - 'although it could be interpreted as contamination and biasing of learning' - and describe their mitigation: only 700 sampled pixels per map enter learning, 50 frames are withheld entirely for diagnosis, and negatives are randomly drawn, which they argue 'approximates a scenario of usage of different videos'. They also note the optical-flow threshold is stimulus-specific, identify dataset construction as the field's binding constraint, and flag intent to release data. Not acknowledged: sensitivity of 69% is not discussed at all despite being the weakest metric and the one that matters for screening; the 2.5:1 class ratio inflates precision and is unaddressed; genetic algorithm selection is not stated to be nested within folds although the selected feature sets are the paper's main interpretive claim; the 28-vote threshold was fitted by Youden on the reported ROC; and no group-wise ages are given across a 3-18 year span." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-062" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No cross-modal fusion. The combination is across feature families within a single modality, and the genetic algorithm's per-group selection is what makes it interpretable: the two models retain overlapping but non-identical feature sets, with distance-to-centre and biological-movement indicators appearing only in the autistic model and colour appearing only in the typically developing model. The authors' explanation for the biological-movement asymmetry is subtle and worth recording - the feature covers the whole region containing biological movement, and since typically developing attention 'is specifically more focused on the regions with people and faces' which are already captured by dedicated features, the broader region adds nothing for that group. That is a redundancy argument rather than an absence-of-effect argument, and it distinguishes a feature being uninformative from being subsumed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-062" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Tobii Pro TX300 with five-point calibration and a documented failure and exclusion policy; monitor viewed from 50-70 cm; MATLAB 2015a; Saliency Toolbox for Itti-model features; a gist-descriptor mixture of linear regressions for horizon detection; face and person detectors; optical flow for motion estimation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-062" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Five-fold cross-validation over subjects, stated explicitly and unambiguously - 'no information of subjects used for testing is used in the learning phase, once the cross-validation was performed over the subjects' - which is the correct unit and is rarely stated this plainly. A second partition operates over frames, with 50 held out from model learning for diagnosis only, deliberately simulating stimulus independence. The authors also pre-empt and address the same-video contamination objection. Two elements nonetheless sit outside the loop: the genetic algorithm feature selection is presented as a single result per group with no indication it was repeated per fold, and the 28-vote decision threshold was chosen by Youden index on the cross-validated ROC. There is no independent cohort or external site." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-062" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Among the better-characterised samples in the corpus: CARS for all participants, plus WISC intelligence, Repetitive Behavior Scale and Vineland adaptive behaviour for autistic participants where obtainable, all deposited per participant. The limitation is that none of these measures is used analytically - there is no severity-stratified analysis, no relation between CARS or IQ and classifier output, and no examination of whether comorbidity status affects classification, despite comorbid participants having been deliberately retained." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-062" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "One session per child viewing nine sequentially displayed videos of approximately 6 seconds each, 54 seconds in total" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional case-control classification with a two-phase model-learning and diagnosis architecture, evaluated by subject-level cross-validation" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Children and adolescents with autism, deliberately including those with comorbidities" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 76; comparison: 30" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Average precision 90%, specificity 93%, sensitivity 69%, with AUC 0.822 (SD 0.015) for neural networks with genetic algorithm selection. Comparisons: neural network without selection 0.818 (0.053), with ReliefF 0.782 (0.026); SVM without selection 0.775 (0.027), with genetic algorithm 0.695 (0.023), with ReliefF 0.695 (0.042). Feature selection therefore helped the neural network only marginally i",
                        "Learning a separate visual attention model for each group - each a small network predicting whether any given pixel will be fixated, from 15 genetically selected image features - and classifying individuals by which group's predicted saliency their own fixation pattern more closely matches, achieved AUC 0.822 with 90% precision and 93% specificity but only 69% sensitivity. The approach removes the" ;
                    slr:outcome "Precision, sensitivity/recall, specificity and AUC with standard deviation, plus ROC curves for all five folds and a comparison table of AUC across six classifier-and-feature-selection combinations, and a table of AUCs from three related published studies" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Average precision 90%, specificity 93%, sensitivity 69%, with AUC 0.822 (SD 0.015) for neural networks with genetic algorithm selection. Comparisons: neural network without selection 0.818 (0.053), with ReliefF 0.782 (0.026); SVM without selection 0.775 (0.027), with genetic algorithm 0.695 (0.023), with ReliefF 0.695 (0.042). Feature selection therefore helped the neural network only marginally i; Learning a separate visual attention model for each group - each a small network predicting whether any given pixel will be fixated, from 15 genetically selected image features - and classifying individuals by which group's predicted saliency their own fixation pattern more closely matches, achieved AUC 0.822 with 90% precision and 93% specificity but only 69% sensitivity. The approach removes the" ] ] ;
