<https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-020-0227-5> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "ARTICLE OPEN Towards the automatic detection of social biomarkers in autism spectrum disorder: introducing the simulated interaction task (SIT) Hanna Drimalla 1,2,3✉, Tobias Scheffer4, Niels Landwehr 4,5, Irina Baskow1,6, Stefan Roepke6, Behnoush Behnia6,7 and Isabel Dziobek1,2,7 Social interaction deﬁcits are evident in many psychiatric conditions and speciﬁcally in autism spectrum disorder (ASD), but hard to assess objectively. We present a digital tool to automatically quantify biomarkers of social interaction deﬁcits: the simulated interaction task (SIT), which entails a standardized 7-min simulated dialog via video and the automated analysis of facial expressions, gaze behavior, and voice characteristics. In a study with 37 adults with ASD without intellectual disability and 43 healthy controls, we show the potential of the tool as a diagnostic instrument and for better description of ASD-associated social phenotypes. Using machine-learning tools, we detected individuals with ASD with an accuracy of 73%, sensitivity of 67%, and speciﬁcity of 79%, based on their facial expressions and vocal characteristics alone. Especially reduced social smiling and facial mimicry as well as a higher voice fundamental frequency and harmony-to-noise-ratio were characteristic for individuals with ASD. The time-effective and cost-effective computer-based analysis outperformed a majority vote and performed equal to clinical expert ratings. npj Digital Medicine (2020)3:25 ; https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-020-0227-5" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Drimalla H; Scheffer T; Landwehr N; Baskow I; Roepke S; Behnia B; Dziobek I (2020). Towards the automatic detection of social biomarkers in autism spectrum disorder: introducing the simulated interaction task (SIT). **. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-020-0227-5" ;
    dcterms:creator "Baskow I",
        "Behnia B",
        "Drimalla H",
        "Dziobek I",
        "Landwehr N",
        "Roepke S",
        "Scheffer T" ;
    dcterms:title "Towards the automatic detection of social biomarkers in autism spectrum disorder: introducing the simulated interaction task (SIT)" ;
    bibo:doi "10.1038/s41746-020-0227-5" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_16a77fb0" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2020 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Recruitment clinic, full inclusion and exclusion criteria, diagnostic instruments with per-instrument counts, and all exclusions with reasons." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "Age differs by 3.75 years with a wider autistic range, and the classifier's output correlates with age at rs = 0.35." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "80 adults is reasonable for this design but small for a diagnostic claim; the cohort is high-functioning adults with existing diagnoses." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is sex representation adequate?" ;
                            slr:justification "49% female in the autistic group and 51% in controls - the best sex balance in the corpus - with gender entered as a model feature and per-gender results reported." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Full task structure with segment durations, topic rationale, actress instruction, laboratory conditions, tracking thresholds and achieved tracking rates." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "ICD-10 specialist diagnosis with ADOS in 35 and ADI-R in 21, plus AQ for all participants." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "Primary features were fixed a priori from theory and a separate EMG study in a different sample." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the stimulus itself verified rather than assumed?" ;
                            slr:justification "The actress's own action units were quantified with the same pipeline, confirming the intended emotional structure of each segment." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the validation split at the participant level?" ;
                            slr:justification "Leave-one-participant-out with an explicit statement that no test-participant data entered training." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are confounds in model output tested for?" ;
                            slr:justification "Correlation with age identified at rs = 0.35 and named as an age bias requiring careful confounder testing - though not corrected in the reported accuracy." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are repeated measures avoided in the feature representation?" ;
                            slr:justification "One aggregate feature vector per participant, stated explicitly." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is hyperparameter tuning nested within training folds?" ;
                            slr:justification "Three-fold cross-validation inside each training set, with full grids reported." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a trivial baseline included?" ;
                            slr:justification "McNemar test against a majority vote, which the classifier significantly beat (X = 9, p = 0.014)." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Does the model output track clinical severity?" ;
                            slr:justification "Predicted class probability correlated with ADOS score at rs = 0.48, p < 0.0001." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a human benchmark included?" ;
                            slr:justification "Eight experienced psychologists and psychiatrists rated the same videos, mean accuracy 0.71, range 0.56-1.00." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a null result reported and diagnosed?" ;
                            slr:justification "Gaze produced no surviving group differences and the weakest classifier, attributed to appearance-based estimation lacking precision, with better hardware recommended." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the intended use scoped honestly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Explicitly positioned to supplement rather than substitute clinical assessment, with system-level costs and risks of screening discussed." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Withheld for video privacy but available on reasonable request; the SIT instrument scheduled for open release from 2024 under CC BY-NC-ND." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Analysis code publicly available on GitHub." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are internal results consistent?" ;
                            slr:justification "The text reports higher harmonics-to-noise ratio in autism while the tabulated medians are lower in both sexes." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Charite ethics committee approval, Helsinki conformity, written informed consent, and separate consent for individuals in published figures." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Prior ICD-10 diagnoses of Asperger syndrome, atypical autism or childhood autism, made through a specialist autism outpatient clinic, with ADOS administered to 35 of 37 participants and ADI-R to the 21 for whom parental informants were available. Autism-Spectrum Quotient was collected for all participants in both groups and the Wortschatztest confirmed verbal intelligence. Control status was established prospectively by excluding any history of psychiatric disorder, with three participants excluded on that basis. Both groups were also screened for current antipsychotic and anticonvulsant medication and comorbid neurological disorders. This is among the more rigorous ascertainments in the corpus, and the ADOS scores are used analytically rather than merely reported." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-050" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'individuals with ASD', 'adults with ASD', 'autism spectrum disorder', 'healthy controls (HC)', 'neurotypical (NT)', 'individuals without autism'. Consistently person-first, with both 'healthy controls' and 'neurotypical' used. The framing locates the problem in clinical practice rather than in patients: clinicians rely on 'the so-called clinical gaze, the implicit knowledge based on experience', which is 'difficult to verbalize, teach, quantify, standardize, and validate', and 'interrater-variability may account for inconsistencies regarding diagnostic accuracy'. Camouflaging is described neutrally as a strategy adults develop to compensate, and is identified as a reason for late diagnosis rather than as a deficit." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-050" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Compare SIT behaviour against a live face-to-face condition with the same topic and script to validate the simulation; make the actress's responses adaptive to participants' answer lengths; provide interaction partners matched on gender, age or ethnic background since similarity enhances mimicry; add automated camera and microphone checks and evaluate accuracy in home settings, since self-administration often diminishes accuracy; use state-of-the-art eye tracking to establish whether gaze carries information in this paradigm; and test sensitivity and specificity in larger clinical samples spanning other psychiatric conditions, since social interaction deficits are common to psychosis, depression, bipolar disorder and substance use disorders." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-050" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: build and validate a standardised, automatically scored measure of social interaction behaviour capable of supporting autism diagnosis in adults. Main findings: combined AUC 0.78 with 73% accuracy, 67% sensitivity and 79% specificity; voice was the strongest modality (0.77), facial expression next (0.74), gaze weakest (0.63) with no differences surviving correction; autistic adults showed less social smiling and less mimicry and higher fundamental frequency; the classifier beat a majority vote and matched eight clinical experts whose own accuracy ranged from 0.56 to 1.00 and scaled with experience; and predicted probability correlated with both ADOS severity (rs = 0.48) and age (rs = 0.35), the latter identified as a confound." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-050" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2020. The framing is distinctive in locating the problem in the measurement instrument rather than in access or cost alone. For general cognition, standardised tests exist - the Montreal Cognitive Assessment, the Mini Mental State Examination - but 'quantifying social interaction deficits is still in its infancy', so clinicians rely on the clinical gaze, which 'practitioners need many years of training to acquire' and which is 'difficult to verbalize, teach, quantify, standardize, and validate'. The paper's own expert-rating data then substantiate this claim empirically: eight experienced clinicians rating the same videos ranged from 0.56 to 1.00 accuracy, with performance correlating with months of experience. The population argument is equally specific - adults with average or above-average intelligence are diagnosed late because they develop camouflaging strategies, and 'to the best of our knowledge, no existing approach focuses on adults with ASD and normal intelligence levels despite the high need for access to diagnosis'. The paper is also careful about its own claim, stating that the SIT 'is not meant to substitute traditional clinical assessment but rather to allow additional information about the client's social communication abilities at an early stage of the diagnostic process'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-050" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups in the main study: 37 autistic adults without intellectual disability recruited via the autism outpatient clinic of Charite Universitatsmedizin Berlin, and 43 healthy controls, with one autistic participant excluded from the combined classifier for missing audio. A separate preparatory study used 40 healthy males with facial EMG to select regions of interest before the main analysis - a two-stage design in which feature selection is performed on an entirely different sample, which is stronger than any within-study selection procedure. No other-psychiatric-diagnosis comparison group, though the authors identify testing 'in larger clinical samples with different psychiatric conditions' as necessary since the social interaction deficits targeted are common to many disorders." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-050" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Autistic group: n = 37, 18 female (49%), mean age 36.89 years, range 22-62. Control group: n = 43, 22 female (51%), mean age 33.14 years, range 18-49. The sex balance is the most striking feature - essentially even in both groups, against the 4:1 male predominance typical of autism cohorts including most in this corpus - and it enables the paper's per-gender analysis showing better detection of women (33 of 39 versus 25 of 40, p = 0.048), which the authors interpret through the finding that women require more severe symptoms to receive a diagnosis. Age is not matched, with the autistic group 3.75 years older and extending 13 years further, and the classifier demonstrably picks this up (class probability correlated with age at rs = 0.35, p = 0.001). Verbal intelligence was confirmed sufficient by the Wortschatztest but no IQ values are reported. No ethnicity or socioeconomic data; single clinic recruitment." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-050" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Acknowledged with unusual thoroughness. On the age bias: 'older participants in both groups were more often labeled as autistic than young participants by the machine-learning classifiers, as the classifier was probably picking up a tendency of higher age in the ASD group. This age bias points to the strong need of careful testing for confounders and biases in training data for all kind of machine-learning diagnostic methods.' On gaze: the null result is attributed to appearance-based estimation being 'not sensitive enough to detect the subtle gaze dysfunctions present in ASD', with a caution that the null 'has to be interpreted with caution'. On the paradigm: the prerecorded actress cannot adapt to answer length, and future versions should provide tailored responses and partners matched on gender, age or ethnicity since similarity enhances mimicry. On the HNR finding: interpretation 'is not straightforward, given the lack of reference studies', with conflicting prior findings acknowledged and the possibility raised that groups differ in speaking-to-pause ratios. On multiplicity: the authors note that automated analysis 'allows the comparison of many different aspects of behavior' so 'the problem of multiple testing remains', which is why they favour the multivariate machine learning approach. Not acknowledged: the reported direction of the HNR effect contradicts the tabulated medians, which are lower rather than higher in autism; and the age confound, though identified, is not adjusted for in the headline accuracy." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-050" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A single random forest of 1,000 trees with maximum depth and minimum samples per leaf tuned over seven values each, applied identically to each modality and to the combination. Model choice is deliberately conservative given 80 participants, and an earlier conference paper by the same group compared alternative approaches. The more consequential modelling decision is what enters the model: features were restricted 'strictly to features based on domain-knowledge or on the EMG preparatory study' for the primary analyses, which is a principled constraint that sacrifices accuracy for interpretability and is quantified by the parallel all-action-unit result." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-050" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Among the best in the corpus. Reported: all recruitment, inclusion and exclusion criteria; the full SIT structure with segment durations and the reasoning behind topic choice; the actress's verified non-verbal behaviour; frame- and participant-level tracking confidence thresholds (0.75 confidence, 90% of frames) with actual achieved tracking rates (99% of frames, mean confidence 0.99, no group difference at p = 0.97); feature construction at both primary and secondary levels with counts per modality; the classifier, ensemble size and full hyperparameter grids; the nested cross-validation scheme with an explicit statement about the absence of repeated measures; the expert rating protocol; and all statistical tests with effect sizes. Analysis code is public on GitHub, and the SIT instrument was scheduled for open release from 2024 under a Creative Commons licence at a named repository. Video data are withheld for privacy but available on request." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-050" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The Simulated Interaction Task - the corpus's most carefully constructed elicitation paradigm. A prerecorded actress conducts a small-talk conversation about food preferences and dinner preparation, structured into neutral, positive and negative segments of 24-26 seconds each, alternating between her speaking and the participant answering while she displays empathic listening (nodding, social smiling). The design reasoning is stated in unusual detail: food preferences were chosen because they elicit positive and negative emotion quickly without personal or sensitive content, and because response lengths vary less than for topics such as favourite films, which aids standardisation. To generate natural behaviour, the actress actually conversed with the film director during shooting rather than reading to camera. Naturalness was supported by informing participants the actress's part was prerecorded, by instructing and rehearsing three-to-four-sentence answers, and by opening with a practice exchange. Crucially, the actress's own behaviour was then verified with the same OpenFace pipeline, confirming she expressed more disgust and less joy when discussing disliked food and smiled more when listening than speaking - so the stimulus is quantified rather than assumed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-050" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Leave-one-participant-out cross-validation with nested three-fold cross-validation inside each training set for hyperparameter tuning, and an explicit assurance that no test-participant data entered training. The paper states directly that each participant contributes a single aggregate feature vector so that 'the data for the machine-learning analysis did not include any nested repeated measures' - an explicit repudiation of the segment-level splitting that inflates results throughout this literature. Beyond internal validation, the study adds two external reference points that most of the corpus lacks: a majority-vote baseline, beaten significantly, and eight practising clinicians rating the same videos, whose performance the classifier matched. What remains absent is an independent cohort or external site, and the age confound is detected but not corrected in the reported figures." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-050" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A quiet laboratory with constant lighting at Humboldt-Universitat zu Berlin, with participants recruited through the autism outpatient clinic of Charite Universitatsmedizin Berlin. The experimenter left the room before the task began, so the participant was alone with the screen. Ethics approval from the Charite ethics committee, Helsinki conformity, written informed consent from all participants and separate consent for figure publication." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-050" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "npj Digital Medicine 3:25 - a Nature Portfolio digital health journal, open access, with authorship spanning psychology, computer science and psychiatry across Humboldt-Universitat, University of Potsdam, Hasso Plattner Institute and Charite." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-050" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Primary features chosen a priori from theory and from an independent EMG study, then expanded into secondary summary statistics (mean, standard deviation, minimum, maximum, time of maximum, skewness, kurtosis) computed separately for each of the seven conversation parts - yielding 715 facial, 85 gaze and 58 voice features. Segment-wise computation preserves the paradigm's designed structure, so the representation encodes not just how much a participant smiled but when relative to the emotional content of the conversation. Each participant contributes exactly one feature vector, which the authors state explicitly rules out nested repeated measures. Gender is included as a feature throughout so that the model can weight sex-dependent voice features appropriately." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-050" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A clean modality comparison with an informative asymmetry. Voice alone reached AUC 0.77, facial expression 0.74 with all 17 action units and 0.65 with the domain-selected subset, gaze 0.63, and all three combined 0.78 - so fusion added essentially nothing over voice alone, and the combined result is carried by the two strong modalities. The gaze failure is diagnosed rather than glossed: appearance-based estimation from a webcam is judged insufficiently precise for the subtle differences reported in the eye-tracking literature, and the authors explicitly recommend state-of-the-art eye trackers to test whether gaze carries information in this paradigm. The comparison between domain-selected facial features (0.65) and all available action units (0.74) is also instructive - constraining features for interpretability cost about 9 AUC points, a trade-off the authors accepted deliberately for 'transparent and explainable diagnostic decisions'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-050" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "AUC per modality and combined, accuracy, sensitivity and specificity at a stated threshold, McNemar tests against a majority-vote baseline and against expert raters, correlations of predicted class probability with ADOS severity and with age, per-gender classification breakdowns, and supplementary confusion matrices and ROC curves for every classifier. Two of these are almost unique in the corpus. Comparing against a majority-vote baseline establishes that the accuracy exceeds trivial prediction. Correlating predicted probability with ADOS establishes that the model's continuous output has clinical meaning beyond the binary decision - and correlating it with age reveals the confound." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-050" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Deliberately minimal: a standard computer with a camera and microphone, which the paper contrasts against comparators using 'high-cost apparatus, not suited for large-scale testing'. Analysis used OpenFace for action units, landmarks and gaze with a trained confidence network, Librosa for MFCCs and energy, Parselmouth Praat scripts for fundamental frequency, jitter and shimmer, and scikit-learn. The preparatory study used facial EMG over zygomaticus major, corrugator supercilii and levator labii to establish which action units to target." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-050" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; one session per participant with no follow-up, though the stated applications include monitoring disorder course and treatment outcome. Data are video and audio of identifiable adults. Governance is complete and includes an unusual element - explicit separate consent from individuals appearing in published figures - and the data availability statement withholds the dataset specifically 'due to privacy restrictions of the video data' while making it available on reasonable request. The discussion also engages the downstream consequences of deployment, noting that 'especially screenings and unsupervised feedback might result in more individuals seeking care and lead to higher costs for the health system' and that 'it is warranted to carefully define the area of use and evaluate benefits, costs, and risks' - one of very few papers in the corpus to consider system-level effects of its own tool." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-050" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "ADOS scores for 35 participants, Autism-Spectrum Quotient for all participants in both groups, and Wortschatztest verbal intelligence as an inclusion screen. The ADOS data are used substantively: the classifier's predicted class probability correlates with ADOS score at rs = 0.48, p < 0.0001, establishing that the model's output tracks symptom severity and not merely group membership. No full-scale IQ, adaptive functioning or comorbidity scores are reported." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-050" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three behavioural channels measured simultaneously during a single interaction: facial expression (action unit occurrence and intensity, and critically mimicry - the time-shifted correlation between the actress's and participant's expression time series), gaze (angle, deviation from personal median, speed and acceleration), and voice (fundamental frequency, jitter, shimmer, energy, harmonics-to-noise ratio and MFCCs). Measuring mimicry rather than expression alone is the distinctive choice: it captures a dyadic property that requires both partners' behaviour, and it is only possible because the partner's behaviour is identical for every participant." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-050" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No formal keyword list. Vocabulary: 'social biomarkers', 'simulated interaction task', 'clinical gaze', 'camouflaging', 'social phenotypes', 'time-effective and cost-effective'. The framing term that does most work is 'clinical gaze' - the implicit expertise the tool is designed to externalise and standardise." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-050" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Interpretability is designed in rather than added afterwards. Features were restricted to those with prior theoretical or EMG-based justification specifically 'for transparent and explainable diagnostic decisions', so every input has a stated behavioural meaning. Each is then reported as a univariate group comparison with effect size, so the multivariate result can be read against the individual behaviours: less social smiling in mouth and eye regions, reduced mimicry of the actress's cheek-raise, more intense frowning during negative content, higher fundamental frequency. The cost of this constraint is measured - all 17 action units reached AUC 0.74 against 0.65 for the selected subset. The clinical validity of the model's output is separately established by its correlation with ADOS score, and its principal bias by its correlation with age." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-050" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "One session per participant; the simulated interaction task lasts about 7 minutes total, comprising a 3.08-minute warm-up and instruction phase (not analysed) and a 3.2-minute analysed recording" ;
                    slr:study_design "Two-part study: a facial EMG preparatory study in healthy males to select regions of interest, followed by a cross-sectional case-control classification study with parallel clinica…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Adults with autism without intellectual disability" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 37 (36 in the combined classifier, one excluded for missing audio); comparison: 43" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "A seven-minute prerecorded video conversation, scored automatically from a webcam and microphone, detected autism in adults without intellectual disability at 73% accuracy (67% sensitivity, 79% specificity, AUC 0.78) - significantly better than a majority vote and statistically indistinguishable from eight experienced clinicians rating the same videos, whose own accuracy ranged from 0.56 to 1.00 a",
                        "Combined facial, gaze and voice features gave AUC 0.78, accuracy 73%, sensitivity 67% and specificity 79%. By modality: voice AUC 0.77, facial expression 0.74 using all 17 action units and 0.65 using domain-selected features, gaze 0.63. The combined classifier was significantly better than a majority vote (McNemar X = 9, p = 0.014) and not significantly worse than clinical experts (p > 0.05). Eigh" ;
                    slr:outcome "AUC per modality and combined, plus accuracy, sensitivity and specificity at a 0.5 class probability threshold; McNemar tests against a majority-vote baseline and against clinical experts; correlation of predicted class probability with ADOS score and with age; and per-gender breakdown of classifica" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Combined facial, gaze and voice features gave AUC 0.78, accuracy 73%, sensitivity 67% and specificity 79%. By modality: voice AUC 0.77, facial expression 0.74 using all 17 action units and 0.65 using domain-selected features, gaze 0.63. The combined classifier was significantly better than a majority vote (McNemar X = 9, p = 0.014) and not significantly worse than clinical experts (p > 0.05). Eigh; A seven-minute prerecorded video conversation, scored automatically from a webcam and microphone, detected autism in adults without intellectual disability at 73% accuracy (67% sensitivity, 79% specificity, AUC 0.78) - significantly better than a majority vote and statistically indistinguishable from eight experienced clinicians rating the same videos, whose own accuracy ranged from 0.56 to 1.00 a" ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "a8de15a20349c4d7b247f315f61cf2cd0008bb5e9566d31322f4f81f4c5fd864" ;
