<https://doi.org/10.1109/CBMS52027.2021.00062> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Computer-aided diagnosis using eye tracking data is classically based on regions of interest in the image. However, in recent years, the modeling of visual attention by saliency maps has shown better results. Wang et al., considering 3layered saliency model that incorporated pixel-level, objectlevel, and semantic-level attributes, showed differences in the performance of eye tracking in autism spectrum disorder (ASD) and better characterized these differences by looking at which attributes were used, providing meaningful clinical results about the disorder. Our hypothesis is that the context interpretation would be worse according to the severity of ASD, consequently, the eye tracking data processed based on visual attention model (VAM) could be used to classify patients with ASD according to gravity. In this context, the present work proposes: 1) based on VAM, using Image Processing and Artiﬁcial Intelligence to learn a model for each group (severe and non-severe), from eye tracking data, and 2) a supervised classiﬁer that, based on the models learned, performs the severity diagnosis. The classiﬁer using the saliency maps was able to identify and separate the groups with an average accuracy of 88%. The most important features were the presence of face and skin color, in other words, semantic features." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Revers MC; Oliveira JS; Franco FO; Portolese J; Cardoso TV; Silva AF; Machado-Lima A; Nunes FLS; Brentani H (2021). Classification of Autism Spectrum Disorder Severity Using Eye Tracking Data Based on Visual Attention Model. *2021 IEEE 34th International Symposium on Computer-Based Medical Systems (CBMS), 142-147*. https://doi.org/10.1109/CBMS52027.2021.00062" ;
    dcterms:creator "Brentani H",
        "Cardoso TV",
        "Franco FO",
        "Machado-Lima A",
        "Nunes FLS",
        "Oliveira JS",
        "Portolese J",
        "Revers MC",
        "Silva AF" ;
    dcterms:title "Classification of Autism Spectrum Disorder Severity Using Eye Tracking Data Based on Visual Attention Model" ;
    bibo:doi "10.1109/CBMS52027.2021.00062" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_30c622f2" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2021 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the severity split independently corroborated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Vineland adaptive behaviour scores significantly lower in the severe group (p = 0.0006)." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Recruitment site, inclusion and exclusion criteria, group sizes, ages, sexes, CARS ranges and means, plus Vineland and RBS comparisons." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "Sex differs significantly (p = 0.02) with the severe group majority female, unadjusted, on a model built around face and skin features." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "88 autistic participants across a CARS range of 30-46.5 is a reasonable basis for a within-autism severity study." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is clinical stability controlled?" ;
                            slr:justification "No medication or no medication change for at least three months required for inclusion." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "DSM-5 by a child psychiatry specialist with CARS-based severity, corroborated by Vineland." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the paradigm accessible across the severity range?" ;
                            slr:justification "Short, audio-free and requiring no verbal instruction, so usable with nonverbal participants at any age." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Tracker, sampling rate, latency, screen size, viewing distance, calibration procedure, stimulus duration, frame rate and resolution all specified." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "ReliefF selection is group-specific and not stated to be nested within folds." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is data quality gated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Participants with eye-tracking record rate below 80% excluded, along with those with calibration problems." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the decision threshold independent of the evaluation?" ;
                            slr:justification "The 20-frame cut-off was chosen by Youden index on the ROC curve used to report performance." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is feature selection nested within folds?" ;
                            slr:justification "Described once per group with no statement of nesting, although the resulting feature sets are the paper's principal finding." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the validation split at the participant level?" ;
                            slr:justification "Stated explicitly - classification on 20% of subjects, model learning on the remainder, across five proportionally balanced folds." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is uncertainty quantified?" ;
                            slr:justification "Mean and maximum AUC across folds are given, but no confidence intervals on any metric." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the reported metrics internally consistent?" ;
                            slr:justification "Error of 25% implies 75% accuracy, and sensitivity 87% with specificity 60% implies about 72%, against a reported 88% that matches the maximum AUC." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is specificity adequate for the intended use?" ;
                            slr:justification "Specificity of 60% means two in five non-severe participants would be labelled severe." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Software, toolboxes and all network hyperparameters named; no code." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Research ethics board approval with a full protocol number and Helsinki conformity." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No release or availability statement." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are limitations stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Sample size, age restriction, sex imbalance, need for different visual inputs and absence of IQ all named." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the substantive finding connected to prior literature?" ;
                            slr:justification "The semantic-feature dissociation is discussed against work on face looking, face perception and severity, and skin-face redundancy." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: test whether autism severity can be classified from gaze via a visual attention model, and whether semantic top-down attentional guidance degrades with severity. Main findings: mean ROC AUC 0.85 with sensitivity 87% and specificity 60%; presence of face and presence of skin were selected as predictive features only for the non-severe group; the non-severe model retained more features overall (15 versus 12); and the CARS severity split was corroborated by significantly lower Vineland scores in the severe group." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-058" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; a single 54-second viewing session per participant with no follow-up. Data are gaze coordinates over video stimuli. Governance is properly reported: research ethics board approval with a full protocol number and Helsinki conformity. Clinical stability over the preceding three months was required for inclusion." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-058" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "An unusual architecture for this corpus: rather than training one discriminative classifier on participant-level features, the paper trains one small generative model per severity group - each a ten-neuron single-hidden-layer network with Bayesian regularisation, predicting whether a pixel was fixated by that group - and then classifies a new participant by which group's saliency map their own fixation map more closely resembles. This is a nearest-prototype scheme in saliency space. It has two consequences worth noting: the model is small enough to be well conditioned on the available data, and the comparison of the two fitted models is itself the scientific output, which is why the feature-selection difference is reported as the main finding rather than as an ablation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-058" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Visual attention during competition between social and geometric content, analysed not as how much is looked at but as what predicts where gaze lands. The unit of analysis is the pixel: for each pixel, a 27-dimensional feature vector describing its image properties at pixel, object and semantic levels, with the model learning which properties predict fixation for each severity group. This reframes the behavioural question from a preference measure to an attentional-guidance measure, which is what allows the top-down versus bottom-up interpretation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-058" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Tobii Pro TX300 at 300 Hz with stated processing latency of 1-3.3 ms; 23-inch screen at 50-70 cm; Tobii Pro Studio; five-point calibration with animated figures at the corners and centre; MATLAB 8.5 R2015a with mexopencv for the attention model; RStudio for statistics. Data quality was enforced as an exclusion criterion - participants with an eye-tracking record rate below 80% were excluded, which is a stricter and more explicit quality gate than most of the corpus applies." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-058" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The Psychiatry Institute of the University of Sao Paulo School of Medicine, with acknowledgement to the Autism Spectrum Disorder Program of the Clinics Hospital (PROTEA-HC). Funded by CAPES and the Brazilian National Program to Support Health Care for Persons with Disabilities. Ethics approval from the School of Medicine research ethics board under protocol 17556019.8.0000.0068." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-058" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Twenty-seven image features at three levels of abstraction, computed per pixel, reduced by ReliefF to 15 for the non-severe model and 12 for the severe model. The representation describes the stimulus rather than the participant, with the participant entering only through which pixels they fixated - an inversion of the usual arrangement in this corpus, where features describe gaze behaviour directly. Frame reduction from 1,590 to 135 groups by a 33% pixel-difference criterion is a sensible efficiency measure for 54 seconds of video, and discarding the ten frames either side of each video transition removes the change-detection artefacts that would otherwise dominate." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-058" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'individuals with ASD', 'patients with ASD', 'ASD children', 'individuals with typical development'. Mixed person-first and 'patients', the latter reflecting recruitment from a psychiatry institute. The framing is clinical and severity-oriented, describing autism as classifiable by severity 'based on the degree of impairment in social communication, repetitive and restricted behavior patterns and also according to the need for support', and motivating the work by the claim that 'the categorization of individuals with ASD into levels contributes to the intervention, which directly impacts the prognosis'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-058" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Test the method with different visual inputs to validate which features are genuinely relevant rather than paradigm-specific; extend the age range beyond 3-16 given that autism is lifelong; enlarge the sample and address the sex imbalance; and incorporate intelligence quotient as an additional model feature." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-058" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No cross-modal fusion - gaze only. The combination that matters is across levels of image representation, and the design makes this an explicit test rather than an implicit one. The three feature levels map onto the two mechanisms the paper is interested in: pixel-level features index bottom-up attention driven by image statistics, semantic-level features index top-down attention driven by prior knowledge, and object-level features sit between. The finding that the two semantic features survive selection only in the non-severe group, while pixel-level features survive in both, is a direct within-modality dissociation supporting the hypothesis that severity impairs top-down more than bottom-up attentional guidance." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-058" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Non-severe group: 49 participants, 27 male and 22 female, aged 3-16 with mean 8.8 +/- 3.2, CARS 30-36 with mean 33.1 +/- 2.0. Severe group: 39 participants, 12 male and 27 female, aged 3-16 with mean 8.7 +/- 3.9, CARS 36.5-46.5 with mean 39.4 +/- 2.6. Age is well matched (p = 0.900) with identical ranges. Sex is not: 55% male against 31%, p = 0.02, so the severe group is majority female against a population sex ratio of roughly 3:1 male. The authors engage with this substantively, citing literature both supporting and contesting greater severity in autistic females and noting that the ratio falls to 2:1 when restricted to low-functioning or intellectually disabled subgroups - but they do not adjust for sex in the model. Vineland scores were significantly lower in the severe group (p = 0.0006) while Repetitive Behavior Scale scores did not differ (p = 0.104). No IQ data, which the authors identify as a limitation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-058" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Good. Reported: full stimulus specification with duration, frame rate and resolution; viewing distance, screen size and calibration procedure; the exclusion criteria including the 80% record-rate threshold; all 27 candidate features itemised by level; the frame preprocessing chain including edge removal, resizing, transition-frame deletion and the 33%-difference grouping rule that reduced 1,590 frames to 135; both pixel-sampling strategies with counts; the complete neural network architecture, activation functions, training algorithm, learning rate and stopping criteria; the 0.75 binarisation threshold; the 20-frame decision rule and how it was chosen; the cross-validation scheme with its subject-level unit; and the statistical tests with normality checking. Missing: whether ReliefF was nested within folds, the identity of the selected features beyond their counts by category, any data or code release, and reconciliation of the inconsistent performance figures." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-058" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2021. The framing is that severity classification is under-explored relative to diagnosis - 'although we know little about its potential use in severity classification, in literature there are some important clues about the use of eye tracking in symptom severity' - and it assembles those clues carefully: a reported correlation of r = -0.52 between fixation on social stimuli and ADOS social scores; evidence that geometric preference predicts later symptom severity and joint-attention gaze shifting; and a finding that autistic children preferring the geometric side scored worse on ADOS, Mullen and Vineland alike. Its methodological argument is that region-of-interest analysis, which dominates the field, discards information: 'although using all the image information and visual attention models... could be more informative'. It builds directly on a 2015 three-level saliency study that found semantic features important in characterising autistic viewing and 'suggesting top-down impairments', and extends that from diagnosis to severity - which is a genuinely incremental and well-posed research question rather than a reapplication of an existing method to a new dataset." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-058" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The geometric-versus-social preference paradigm - nine videos totalling 54 seconds at 30 fps and 1920 x 1080, with social scenes of children playing or doing yoga on one side of the screen competing against randomly varying fractals on the other. The paradigm is chosen for reasons of clinical reach rather than theoretical richness, and the reasons are stated: it 'has the advantage of being short and does not require audio, it can be used in nonverbal and at any age'. This matters for a severity study, since a paradigm requiring verbal instruction or sustained compliance would systematically exclude the severe end of the range being measured." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-058" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Assessment by a specialist in child and adolescent psychiatry against DSM-5 criteria, with CARS above 30 required for inclusion, so every participant carries both a categorical diagnosis and a dimensional severity score. Clinical stability was additionally required - no medication or no change for at least three months - which controls a source of variability almost no other study in this corpus addresses. The severity split at CARS 36 follows a cited convention. What is missing is any account of who performed the CARS, with what training or reliability, and whether ADOS or ADI-R were available." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-058" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Precision, sensitivity, specificity, error and accuracy plus a ROC curve with mean and maximum AUC - a full set on paper, but the values do not reconcile. Error of 25% implies accuracy of 75%; sensitivity of 87% with specificity of 60% on a 39-severe, 49-non-severe split implies roughly 72%; and the headline 88% matches the separately reported maximum AUC of 0.88 exactly. The internally consistent reading is that the classifier achieves roughly 75% accuracy with 87% sensitivity and 60% specificity, and that mean AUC is 0.85. That specificity of 60% is the more informative figure for a severity-grading tool: two in five non-severe participants would be labelled severe." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-058" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Interpretability is the paper's organising purpose rather than an add-on. By fitting a separate attention model per severity group and comparing their retained feature sets, the analysis yields a directly interpretable dissociation - presence of face and presence of skin predict fixation for non-severe participants but not for severe ones - which is then connected to the wider literature on reduced face and eye-region looking in autism and on face perception relating to social-impairment severity. The authors correctly note the partial redundancy between skin and face features as reinforcing rather than duplicating the finding, and identify the single differing non-semantic feature as a steerable pyramid component that indexes edges, texture and orientation and 'is not related to object recognition'. The conclusion drawn - that top-down mechanisms differentiate severity more than bottom-up ones - is well supported by the structure of the result, subject to the caveat that the feature selection may not have been nested within folds." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-058" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Index terms: Autism Spectrum Disorder, Severity Level, Supervised Machine Learning, Classifier, Eye Tracking. 'Severity Level' as a headline term marks this as one of the few corpus studies whose target is gradation rather than detection." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-058" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Strong for a severity study. CARS for all participants with group ranges and means; Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales and the Repetitive Behavior Scale-Revised administered 'when possible'. The Vineland result is used substantively - significantly lower adaptive functioning in the severe group corroborates the CARS-based split from an independent instrument, which is the correct way to validate a severity dichotomy. The absence of IQ is acknowledged as a limitation and proposed as a future model feature." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-058" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Five-fold cross-validation with splitting stated explicitly at the subject level, which is the correct unit and is clearly described. Folds were proportionally balanced. Two elements fall outside the loop. ReliefF feature selection is described once, per group, with no statement that it was repeated within folds - and since the group-specific feature sets are the paper's principal scientific claim, whether they were derived using all 88 participants or only training-fold participants materially affects their status. The 20-frame decision threshold was chosen by the Youden index applied to the ROC curve, which is the same curve used to characterise performance, so the operating point is fitted to the evaluation. There is no independent cohort, no external site and no held-out replication." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-058" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A single clinical population of 88 autistic children and adolescents, split by CARS into 39 severe and 49 non-severe, from 91 recruited. There is no typically developing comparison group and none is intended - the research question is whether severity can be graded within autism, which makes this one of very few studies in the corpus addressing gradation rather than detection. Exclusions were calibration problems, eye-tracking record rate below 80% and age over 17, applied to three participants." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-058" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2021 IEEE 34th International Symposium on Computer-Based Medical Systems (CBMS), pages 142-147 - a medical informatics conference, with authorship spanning a university psychiatry department, a bioinformatics programme and a computing school. The same institution and venue as another corpus record on joint attention trajectories, from an overlapping author group." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-058" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Acknowledged: that the saliency-map approach needs testing with different visual inputs 'in order to validate the relevant features'; that the age range is restricted to 3-16 while autism is lifelong; that the sample is small; and that the sex imbalance between groups is a limitation. Analysing IQ as an additional feature is proposed. Not acknowledged: the reported accuracy of 88% is inconsistent with the reported error rate of 25% and with the reported sensitivity and specificity, and coincides exactly with the separately reported maximum ROC AUC; ReliefF feature selection is not stated to be nested within cross-validation folds, so the group-specific feature sets that constitute the paper's main finding may have been chosen using all the data; and the 20-frame decision threshold was fitted by Youden index on the same ROC used for evaluation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-058" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "One session per participant viewing nine videos in sequence with no break, 54 seconds total at 30 frames per second" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional severity classification within a single clinical population, with no non-autistic comparison group" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Children and adolescents with autism across the severity range" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 88 analysed from 91 recruited - 39 severe, 49 non-severe; comparison: 0" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "A visual attention model fitted separately to severe and non-severe autism groups distinguished them from gaze during a 54-second geometric-versus-social preference video, with mean AUC 0.85 and sensitivity 87% against specificity 60%. The substantive result is which features each group's model retained: presence of face and presence of skin were selected only for the non-severe group, so semantic",
                        "Mean AUC 0.85 with maximum 0.88. At the Youden-selected threshold of 20 frames, the reported averages are precision 70%, sensitivity 87%, specificity 60%, error 25% and accuracy 88%. These figures are mutually inconsistent - an error rate of 25% implies accuracy of 75%, and sensitivity of 87% with specificity of 60% on a 39/49 split implies accuracy of roughly 72%. The headline 88% appears to be t" ;
                    slr:outcome "Precision, sensitivity, specificity, error and accuracy, plus a ROC curve with mean and maximum area under the curve" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Mean AUC 0.85 with maximum 0.88. At the Youden-selected threshold of 20 frames, the reported averages are precision 70%, sensitivity 87%, specificity 60%, error 25% and accuracy 88%. These figures are mutually inconsistent - an error rate of 25% implies accuracy of 75%, and sensitivity of 87% with specificity of 60% on a 39/49 split implies accuracy of roughly 72%. The headline 88% appears to be t; A visual attention model fitted separately to severe and non-severe autism groups distinguished them from gaze during a 54-second geometric-versus-social preference video, with mean AUC 0.85 and sensitivity 87% against specificity 60%. The substantive result is which features each group's model retained: presence of face and presence of skin were selected only for the non-severe group, so semantic" ] ] ;
