            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<https://doi.org/10.1109/ICPR48806.2021.9412766> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Autism spectrum disorder (ASD) is a neurodevelopmental disorder, which causes deﬁcits in social lives. Early screening of ASD for young children is important to reduce the impact of ASD on people’s lives. Traditional screening methods mainly rely on protocol-based interviews and subjective evaluations from clinicians and domain experts, which requires advanced expertise and intensive labor. To standardize the process of ASD screening, we design a ”Responsive Social Smile” protocol and the associated experimental setup. Moreover, we propose a machine learning based assessment framework for early ASD screening. By integrating speech recognition and computer vision technologies, the proposed framework can quantitatively analyze children’s behaviors under well-designed protocols. We collect 196 stimulus samples from 41 children with an average age of 23.34 months, and the proposed method obtains 85.20% accuracy for predicting stimulus scores and 80.49% accuracy for the ﬁnal ASD prediction. This result indicates that our model approaches the average level of domain experts in this ”Responsive Social Smile” protocol. I. INTRODUCTION Autism spectrum disorder (ASD) is a mental disorder that may signiﬁcantly impact people’s lives. Difﬁculties in social communication represent one category of the autistic syndromes [1]. Among the indicators that can reﬂect our sensing and understanding of the social environment, the smile is one of the most important factors. Especially for children between 1 to 3 years old, their responses to simple utterances, including a responsive social smile, can reﬂect the level of ASD in some widely used screening tests (e.g., ASD-G [2], ADOS-2 [3]). Although social smiling is essential in ASD screening, most existing methods still rely on clinicians’ subjective evaluation. According to screening sheets, clinicians can distinguish ASD and evaluate multi-dimensional abilities. Unlike other screening methods involving clear indicators and benchmarks (e.g., response to name, joint attention), the responsive social The ﬁrst two authors contributed equally. Fig. 1. The layout of the experimental environment and video recording example. smile is evaluated by emotion description that usually remains an ambiguous classiﬁcation result. It is especially harder for clinicians to assess children’s emotions on small faces and record them when distracted from carrying out experiments. Remembering every score for different social stimuli during the screening or perform the behavior scoring after the test is very subjective and labor-intensive, which requires experience and expertise. As top clinicians master the scoring skills with high accuracy, we are motivated to imitate experts and develop an intelligent, objective, and efﬁcient system that can recognize social stimuli, detect and quantify the corresponding emotion in ASD screenings. In this case, clinicians would be equipped with leading technology and rich experience and universally serve" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Pan Y; Cai K; Cheng M; Zou X; Li M (2020). Responsive Social Smile: A Machine-Learning Based Multimodal Behavior Assessment Framework towards Early Stage Autism Screening. *Proceedings of the 25th International Conference on Pattern Recognition (ICPR), 2240-2245*. https://doi.org/10.1109/ICPR48806.2021.9412766" ;
    dcterms:creator "Cai K",
        "Cheng M",
        "Li M",
        "Pan Y",
        "Zou X" ;
    dcterms:title "Responsive Social Smile: A Machine-Learning Based Multimodal Behavior Assessment Framework towards Early Stage Autism Screening" ;
    bibo:doi "10.1109/ICPR48806.2021.9412766" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_df438dd2" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2020 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "Cannot be assessed at all; age spans 12-32 months on an age-sensitive social behaviour." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "41 toddlers is small, but the 12-32 month age band is genuinely early and the comparison group is clinically referred rather than community-recruited." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the comparison group clinically realistic?" ;
                            slr:justification "Non-autistic children were assessed at the same specialist clinic, making the discrimination harder and more representative of screening practice." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Only pooled age, sex and group sizes; no group-wise demographics, recruitment route or referral basis." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "The expression model was trained on external corpora and a disjoint children's database, then applied blind." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "Stimulus scores have three independent clinicians with majority voting; the autism label has only unspecified DSM-IV clinical diagnosis." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Room layout, camera placement, frame rate, all six stimuli with their scripted keywords and speaker, repetition rule and response window are specified." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Does the measured quantity match the construct claimed?" ;
                            slr:justification "The system measures whether a smile occurred in a post-stimulus window, not whether it was a response to the stimulus - the failure analysis shows this is the dominant error mode." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is model selection separated from performance estimation?" ;
                            slr:justification "The decision tree was chosen as best of four classifiers on the same leave-one-out estimate reported as the headline." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is uncertainty quantified?" ;
                            slr:justification "No confidence intervals or tests; one child is worth 2.4 accuracy points on 41 participants." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are errors analysed rather than only counted?" ;
                            slr:justification "All 29 misscored trials examined, with the dominant failure mode identified and illustrated." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is perception training data disjoint from the evaluation cohort?" ;
                            slr:justification "15,000 clips from 54 children with explicitly no overlap with the 41 clinical participants." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the autism classification held out at the participant level?" ;
                            slr:justification "Leave-one-out over 41 children." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the contribution of each pipeline stage isolated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Running all four classifiers on clinicians' own stimulus scores quantifies the perception stage's error contribution at about 2.4 accuracy points." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are cross-study comparisons scoped honestly?" ;
                            slr:justification "The comparison table spans different populations and ages, but the paper presents it as context and notes its own cohort is the youngest." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Two new databases contributed, neither released nor given an availability statement." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Only a consent mention in the acknowledgements; no committee approval or consent procedure for frontal video of toddlers." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the conclusions proportionate to the evidence?" ;
                            slr:justification "The paper states that a single protocol cannot screen autism comprehensively and frames the result as approaching expert level within this protocol only." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Architecture fully specified; no code, no decision tree hyperparameters, no smile-count threshold." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with ASD', 'ASD children', 'autism children', 'non-ASD', 'autistic children'. Mixed person-first and 'ASD children'; the comparison group is 'non-ASD' rather than 'typically developing' or 'healthy', which is accurate given they were clinically assessed rather than screened as typical. Autism is described as 'a mental disorder that may significantly impact people's lives' and as causing 'deficits in social lives'. The paper's stated aim is to equip clinicians rather than replace them - 'clinicians would be equipped with leading technology and rich experience and universally serve autism children'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-046" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'Professional doctors diagnose all these 41 children' at the Third Affiliated Hospital of Sun Yat-sen University, according to DSM-IV standards. No instrument (ADOS, ADI-R, CARS or otherwise), no cut-off, no diagnostician count and no assessment date are reported, and the use of DSM-IV rather than DSM-5 is not explained. Separately and more strongly, the per-stimulus ground truth is well established: three clinicians independently watched the protocol videos and labelled each trial, with majority voting used as the final score - so the intermediate outcome the system is optimised against has a genuine consensus reference standard even though the diagnostic label does not." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-046" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Leave-one-out cross-validation at both stages, with the autism classification held out at the child level. The perception module's training data is cleanly disjoint from the evaluation cohort - the fine-tuning database comes from 54 children with explicitly no overlap with the 41 participants - which prevents the most serious form of leakage in video-based work. Two weaker points: the stimulus scoring evaluation is at the trial level across 196 samples from 41 children, so the same child appears in training and test folds for that stage; and the decision tree was chosen as best of four on the same leave-one-out estimate reported as the result. There is no independent cohort, no external site, and no held-out test set, which the sample size precludes." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-046" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Good on architecture, thin on protocol provenance. Reported: the full network architecture layer by layer for both branches with feature map dimensions, the 7-frame clip length and 224 x 224 input size, the 24 FPS and 20-second default window (about 480 frames per stimulus), the 0.9 smile decision threshold with its rationale, the ASR training corpus, the mean-imputation rule for missing stimuli, and the leave-one-out evaluation. Missing: decision tree hyperparameters, the smile-count threshold for stimulus scoring, group-wise demographics, ethics documentation, and any data or code release. A typographical error in the stimulus scoring description states that the score is marked 0 in both branches of the threshold test." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-046" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Responsive social smiling - whether a toddler produces a clear smile in response to a specific social overture, across five humour mechanisms plus a parent-delivered greeting. The behaviour is deliberately chosen because it is the hard case: unlike response to name or joint attention it 'is evaluated by emotion description that usually remains an ambiguous classification result', so it is the element of screening where automation has the most to offer and where a defensible ground truth is hardest to obtain. That the paper builds a three-clinician consensus for exactly this reason is a considered response to the problem." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-046" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A standardised low-cost room setup deployed at the collaborating Third Affiliated Hospital of Sun Yat-sen University, Guangzhou, with a fixed seating layout for child, clinician and parent and a single camera positioned to record the child's frontal face. Funded by the National Natural Science Foundation of China, the Key Research and Development Program of Jiangsu Province, the Six Talent Peaks project and a Guangzhou municipal livelihood science and technology plan." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-046" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No IQ, developmental quotient, language, adaptive or symptom severity measure is reported, and no comorbidity information. The six behaviour scores function as a within-protocol profile but are not related to any external severity measure." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-046" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Acknowledged: that a single protocol is insufficient - 'using the responsive social smile score alone cannot give a very comprehensive ASD classification' - and that fusing multiple protocols is needed; that autistic children are heterogeneous, making single-aspect screening hard; and, through the failure case study, that most residual errors are smiles that occurred but were not responses to the designed stimulus. Not acknowledged: that group-wise ages and sexes are never reported, so age and sex confounding cannot be excluded in a 12-32 month cohort; that the diagnostic reference standard is a bare DSM-IV clinical judgement with no instrument; that 80.49% is the best of four classifiers on 41 children where one child is worth 2.4 points - the same size as the gap the human-score ablation reveals; that stimulus scoring is evaluated at the trial level so the same child appears across folds; and that no ethics approval is reported." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-046" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2020. The framing is about clinician workload and the limits of subjective scoring in a specific, unusually difficult case. The paper argues that the responsive social smile differs from other screening indicators because it lacks clear benchmarks and is 'evaluated by emotion description', that assessing 'children's emotions on small faces' while simultaneously running the experiment is error-prone, and that 'remembering every score for different social stimuli during the screening or perform the behavior scoring after the test is very subjective and labor-intensive'. Its comparison table is a useful snapshot of the 2020 field - eye movement at 88.51%, hand imitation at 86.70%, prosody at 76.00%, neuroimaging at 70.00% - and its accompanying observation is that 'cost and age requirements prevent some accurate ASD screening methods among children', with neuroimaging singled out as both expensive and impractical for young children who cannot stay still. Its own cohort at 12-32 months is the youngest in that table." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-046" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A six-dimensional binary vector, one element per stimulus. This is a deliberately clinical representation - it mirrors exactly what a clinician records on a screening sheet, so the classifier's input is inspectable and the system's intermediate output is directly usable by a human even if the final classification is ignored. The cost is that all temporal, intensity and latency information about the smile is collapsed to a single bit per stimulus, and missing stimuli are mean-imputed within the child, which for a binary feature produces values the classifier was not otherwise trained on." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-046" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No attribution or saliency method, but the pipeline is interpretable by construction: every intermediate output is a clinically meaningful quantity - stimulus onset time, face crop, smile or non-smile, per-stimulus score - so a clinician can inspect where a decision came from without any explanation technique. The failure case study is the interpretive high point, examining all 29 misscored trials and finding that most involve genuine smiles that clinicians attributed to spontaneous emotion rather than to the social stimulus, with four examples shown. This identifies a specific conceptual limit: the system measures whether a smile occurred in a time window, not whether it was caused by the stimulus, and closing that gap requires modelling attention or gaze direction rather than better expression recognition." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-046" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Fuse scores and features from multiple complementary automated protocols - response to name and joint attention are named as the natural partners - to move from a single-aspect indicator to a composite screen, and address the distinction between spontaneous and socially responsive smiling that accounts for most remaining scoring error." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-046" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups, 41 toddlers: 20 diagnosed autistic and 21 non-autistic, all assessed at the same specialist hospital. The comparison group is therefore clinically referred rather than community-recruited, which makes the discrimination task harder and more clinically realistic than most of the corpus. A separate, non-overlapping group of 54 children under six supplied 15,000 labelled expression clips for training the perception module. No other-diagnosis comparison group." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-046" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Reported only for the pooled cohort: 41 children aged 12-32 months, mean 23.34 months, 33 boys and 8 girls. No group-wise ages, standard deviations, sex splits or matching tests are given, and an age-by-diagnosis figure is shown but its values are not tabulated. This is a material gap: with a 12-32 month range, responsive social smiling to scripted verbal prompts changes substantially with age, so an age difference between groups would directly produce the observed effect. The authors note the 4:1 male:female ratio matches autism epidemiology, which speaks to representativeness but not to between-group balance. No IQ, developmental, socioeconomic or ethnicity data; single site in Guangzhou." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-046" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Genuinely multimodal, but with the modalities serving different functions rather than being fused as parallel evidence: audio drives temporal segmentation (locating each stimulus onset from its scripted keyword) while video drives measurement (detecting the face and classifying the expression). This division is the framework's structural idea - speech tells the system when to look, vision tells it what it sees - and it is what allows an unsegmented clinical recording to be parsed automatically. Within the vision branch there is a second fusion, between a 3D-CNN capturing temporal dynamics and a 2D-CNN capturing spatial detail, concatenated to 1024 dimensions before classification. No accuracy comparison between the branches is reported, so the contribution of each is unquantified." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-046" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, sensitivity and specificity with full confusion matrices at both stages, and - most valuably - a four-classifier comparison run twice, once on predicted stimulus scores and once on clinicians' scores. That paired table is the paper's best methodological contribution: it decomposes total error into the perception stage and the protocol stage, showing the perception stage contributes about 2.4 accuracy points. No AUC, no confidence intervals, no statistical test, and no per-stimulus analysis. The cross-study comparison table against eye-movement, hand-imitation, prosody and neuroimaging methods is drawn from different populations and age ranges and cannot support relative claims, though the paper presents it as context rather than as competition, noting its own cohort is the youngest at 1-3 years." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-046" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: automate scoring of the responsive social smile, one element of clinical screening that lacks clear behavioural benchmarks, and use the resulting scores for autism classification. Main findings: 85.20% agreement with three-clinician consensus stimulus scores; 80.49% autism classification accuracy with 85.00% sensitivity and 77.27% specificity from a decision tree; substituting clinicians' own stimulus scores raises this only to 82.93%, locating the performance ceiling in the protocol rather than the perception pipeline; 92.60% smile classification after fine-tuning on a purpose-built children's expression database; and most residual scoring errors are correctly detected smiles that clinicians judged spontaneous rather than socially responsive." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-046" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Proceedings of the 25th International Conference on Pattern Recognition (ICPR), Milan, pages 2240-2245 - a computer vision and pattern recognition venue, with clinical co-authorship from the Third Affiliated Hospital of Sun Yat-sen University." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-046" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No formal keyword list. Vocabulary: 'responsive social smile', 'multimodal behavior assessment framework', 'early stage autism screening', 'standardize', 'stimulus scoring', 'temporal stimulus localization'. The recurring word is standardisation - the paper's claim is as much about making a protocol repeatable and machine-readable as about classification accuracy." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-046" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A single camera at 24 FPS and a microphone in a fixed layout, explicitly framed as low-cost. Software: Kaldi with an AISHELL-2-trained Mandarin ASR model; OpenCV-DNN face detector chosen for robustness on small toddler faces; a custom 2D-3D CNN built from ResNet-18 and pretrained 3D-ResNet-18 components; standard classifiers for the final decision. Standardising the physical setup is treated as a contribution in its own right." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-046" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A cascade rather than a single model: pretrained ASR, pretrained face detection, a fine-tuned two-branch expression network, and a shallow classifier over six binary scores. The design is well matched to the data regime - deep components are trained on large external corpora (AISHELL-2, Oulu-CASIA, and the purpose-built 15,000-clip children's database) while the only model fitted to the 41-child cohort is a decision tree over six features. Four classifiers are compared, with the decision tree best by a wide margin (80.49% against 70.73% for SVM), which for a six-dimensional binary feature space is plausible." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-046" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A purpose-designed protocol modelled on ADOS and organised around distinct humour mechanisms: greeting smile with the child's name, praise words, hide and seek, hints of tickling, actual tickling, and a parent-delivered greeting smile. Each stimulus opens with a scripted keyword phrase, which is what makes automatic temporal segmentation possible - the ASR system detects the phrase and thereby locates the response window, so protocol design and pipeline design are co-dependent by construction. Stimuli are repeated up to three times if the child does not respond, and children who do not complete every step contribute missing scores that are mean-imputed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-046" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; a single session per child with no follow-up. Data are frontal video and audio of identifiable toddlers with a parent and clinician present - among the most sensitive material in the corpus. Governance reporting is a single clause in the acknowledgements referring to participants' consent; no ethics committee approval, formal consent procedure, anonymisation or retention policy is stated, and no data availability statement is given." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-046" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "One session per child, comprising up to six scripted stimuli, each repeated at most three times if the child did not respond" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional case-control with dual evaluation: the system's per-stimulus scores are validated against clinician consensus labels, and the resulting score vectors are used to cl…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Toddlers referred for autism assessment" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 20; comparison: 21" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "A scripted six-stimulus social smile protocol, automatically segmented by speech recognition and scored by a two-branch 2D/3D expression CNN, reproduced clinician consensus stimulus scores at 85.20% and supported autism classification at 80.49% accuracy in toddlers averaging 23 months. The most informative result is the ablation against human scores: feeding clinicians' own stimulus ratings into t",
                        "Facial expression: 89.10% on Oulu-CASIA (against 81.46-91.67% for published comparators), then 92.60% for binary smile classification after fine-tuning on the children's database. Stimulus scoring against three-clinician majority-vote ground truth: 85.20% accuracy (confusion matrix 62 / 13 / 16 / 105 over 196 samples). Autism classification from predicted scores: decision tree 80.49% accuracy, 85." ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy, sensitivity and specificity with full confusion matrices for both stimulus scoring and autism classification, plus a comparison of all four classifiers under two conditions - using the system's predicted stimulus scores and using the clinicians' own scores - which isolates how much of the " ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Facial expression: 89.10% on Oulu-CASIA (against 81.46-91.67% for published comparators), then 92.60% for binary smile classification after fine-tuning on the children's database. Stimulus scoring against three-clinician majority-vote ground truth: 85.20% accuracy (confusion matrix 62 / 13 / 16 / 105 over 196 samples). Autism classification from predicted scores: decision tree 80.49% accuracy, 85.; A scripted six-stimulus social smile protocol, automatically segmented by speech recognition and scored by a two-branch 2D/3D expression CNN, reproduced clinician consensus stimulus scores at 85.20% and supported autism classification at 80.49% accuracy in toddlers averaging 23 months. The most informative result is the ablation against human scores: feeding clinicians' own stimulus ratings into t" ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "fa4a2351c8fb9cc19845d72a47bd27aaffb52a1c27e777feeff925c50a2cc530" ;
