<https://doi.org/10.1111/jcap.12346> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Problem: Autism spectrum disorders (ASD) are pervasive neurodevelopmental disorders and generally accompanied by social disorders, verbal or nonverbal communication defects, inability to concentrate and other negative symptoms that affect the autistic person's normal life. However, traditional screening methods are time‐ consuming and public health resources are limited. Methods: This study proposed a novel technique that combined eye‐movement data and machine learning algorithms for predicting autistic traits. We converted raw eye movement data into features, trained and tested a model for early screening. Findings: In the preliminary experiment, 107 participants (average age = 24.84 ± 5.24 years) wore HTC Vive to watch a VR scene for 15–20 s. We explored eight classification models, among which the ensemble model performed best, with 0.73 accuracy, 0.68 precision, 0.81 recall, 0.74 F1‐score, and an area under the curve of 0.90. And in the test experiment, 22 participants (average age = 12.68 ± 7.61 years) diagnosed as ASD took the experiment and the ensemble model showed a recall of 0.77. Eye movement data is an effectively distinguishable tool and we find that the proportion of time to observe figure and animal region continuously can distinguish participants with obvious and unobvious autistic traits effectively in the model. Conclusion: This study focuses on the detection of autistic traits, and proposes a more objective and faster method for undertaking early screening, which provides possibilities to save precious time to intervene and alleviate its symptoms before making a definite diagnosis. K E Y W O R D S autism spectrum disorders (ASD), early screening, eye movement, machine learning, virtual reality (VR) As increasingly common mental illnesses, autism spectrum disorders (ASD) seriously affect patients and their families (Meadan et al., 2010). Early screening and prevention are important for reducing the incidence of ASD and improving the treatment outcomes of autistic persons. Traditional methods of ASD diagnosis rely heavily on questionnaires and human assistance, and the screening process in traditional diagnosis is complex. However, studies have demonstrated that eye movement is available to observe abnormalities related to ASD and the combination of eye movement and machine learning provides timely and accurate diagnosis (Liu et al., 2016; Wan et al., 2019). Therefore, in our research, we proposed to record eye movement more realistically through virtual reality (VR) interaction and combine machine learning algorithms to predict autistic traits as the basis for early screening. 2 | ASD AND TRADITIONAL DIAGNOSTIC METHODS ASD are a group of pervasive development disorders (PDD) with a neurological basis, including autism, Asperger syndrome, Rett syndrome, childhood disintegrative disorder, pervasive developmental disorder not otherwise specified and other subtypes (Kidd, 2002). Though the severity levels are diffe" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Lin Y; Gu Y; Xu Y; Hou S; Ding R; Ni S (2021). Autistic spectrum traits detection and early screening: A machine learning based eye movement study. *Journal of Child and Adolescent Psychiatric Nursing, 35*. https://doi.org/10.1111/jcap.12346" ;
    dcterms:creator "Ding R",
        "Gu Y",
        "Hou S",
        "Lin Y",
        "Ni S",
        "Xu Y" ;
    dcterms:title "Autistic spectrum traits detection and early screening: A machine learning based eye movement study" ;
    bibo:doi "10.1111/jcap.12346" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_d8d3037b" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2021 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "The training sample contains no diagnosed autistic participants and its mean AQ of 21.0 sits well within the normal range." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Does the test sample permit assessment of discrimination?" ;
                            slr:justification "No negative cases, so only recall is computable and specificity in the diagnosed population is unknown." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "Well matched within the preliminary experiment; the test sample differs from the training population by roughly twelve years of mean age and entirely in sex." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Preliminary sample fully described with age, sex and AQ by group; test sample given only as 22 young males with a mean age and no further characterisation." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the stimulus rationale justified?" ;
                            slr:justification "Immersive scene chosen on the documented ground that social attention appears normal in autism for static or isolated social presentations." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "Gaze features are computed automatically, but age and sex are included as predictors and covary with group membership across experiments." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate?" ;
                            slr:justification "A median split of AQ at 21 in a non-clinical sample, against a clinical cut-off of 32, is labelled '1 (ASD)'." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Headset, tracking module, scene contents and exposure duration given; calibration procedure, gaze-point counts and data-quality criteria are not." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the second experiment a valid external validation?" ;
                            slr:justification "All-positive sample from a demographically different population, with labels not commensurable with the training labels." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is hyperparameter tuning confined to training data?" ;
                            slr:justification "Threefold cross-validated GridSearchCV within the 70% training portion." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are multiple models compared transparently?" ;
                            slr:justification "All eight models reported with five metrics each in the preliminary experiment and recall in the test experiment." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is model instability across populations reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Per-model recall change between experiments is tabulated and interpreted as evidence that the two populations differ." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are reported metrics internally consistent?" ;
                            slr:justification "Ensemble AUC of 0.90 exceeds every constituent model's AUC of 0.57-0.68 by a margin combination cannot produce." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is data cleaning specified?" ;
                            slr:justification "Abnormal values replaced by the mean of normal data with no outlier criterion and no statement of whether this preceded the split." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "IRB approval with reference number THUDP20 from the Department of Psychology, Tsinghua University, for both experiments." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the conclusions proportionate to the evidence?" ;
                            slr:justification "Performance obtained on a questionnaire median split is presented as evidence for an ASD screening method." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are hardware and acceptability limitations disclosed?" ;
                            slr:justification "aGlass accuracy described as insufficient and rejection of VR among autistic participants reported." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are internal results consistent?" ;
                            slr:justification "Table 2 reports the high-trait group's mean AQ as 16.6 and the low-trait group's as 25.7, which are transposed." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Models and tuned parameters named; no code and no selected hyperparameter values." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Available from the corresponding author on reasonable request." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Laboratory sessions at Tsinghua University's Shenzhen International Graduate School, with diagnosed participants recruited through the Shenzhen Autism Society. Funded by Shenzhen Humanities and Social Sciences Key Research Bases, the Guangdong Natural Science Foundation, the National Philosophy and Social Sciences Foundation of China and a Tsinghua SIGS foundation. IRB approval from the Department of Psychology, Tsinghua University (No. THUDP20) covering both experiments." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-054" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Acknowledged: aGlass stability and accuracy are insufficient; VR was rejected by some autistic participants; small samples in both experiments make bias inevitable; not all scenes containing social stimuli discriminate, so scene design should involve a specialist psychologist and several scenes should be compared. Most valuably, the authors report and interpret the recall instability across experiments, concluding that 'the data patterns of the adults we recruited in the preliminary experiment are somehow different from those autistic adults'. Not acknowledged: that the preliminary positive class is a median split of a general-population trait distribution well below the AQ clinical cut-off, so the models were never trained on autism; that Table 2's group AQ means are transposed; that age and sex are model features while the test sample differs sharply from the training population on both; that the test experiment has no negative cases so specificity is unmeasured and recall alone cannot distinguish a good model from one biased toward positive prediction; that the ensemble's AUC of 0.90 is inconsistent with constituent AUCs of 0.57-0.68; and that outlier replacement by the mean is unspecified in both criterion and timing." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-054" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A single Unity3D virtual scene - a couple in a garden with their dog - containing three semantic regions: human figures as social stimuli, an animal, and landscape. Free viewing for 15-20 seconds with no instruction. The design rationale is explicit and worth recording: previous Chinese eye-tracking work used 'merely a face on the computer screen', whereas static or isolated social presentations may fail to reveal group differences because 'for static images or those which present social information in isolation, social attention is normal in autism'. The 15-20 second exposure is by some margin the shortest stimulus in the corpus, which suits deployment but yields few gaze points per participant." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-054" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A single 70/30 split of 107 participants with threefold cross-validated GridSearchCV inside the training portion, followed by application to a separate group of 22 diagnosed participants. The two-stage structure is the right instinct - fitting on one sample and testing on another population is more than most of the corpus attempts - but its execution defeats the purpose. The test sample contains only positive cases, so nothing about specificity or overall discrimination can be established. It differs from the training population by roughly twelve years of mean age and entirely in sex composition, both of which are model inputs. And the labels are not commensurable: the training positive class is above-median AQ in a non-clinical adult sample, while the test positives are clinically diagnosed individuals whose AQ scores were never measured. There is no repetition over alternative splits, no confidence intervals and no independent replication." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-054" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Distribution of visual attention across three semantic region types in an immersive scene - human figures, an animal and landscape - characterised in three ways: total occupancy, continuity of occupancy, and transitions between region pairs. Including both total and continuous occupancy is the design's most productive choice, since the continuity features turned out to be the most important and total occupancy was not, which distinguishes sustained engagement from repeated brief glances - a distinction most gaze studies in this corpus collapse." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-054" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Feature importances from the tree-based models identify continuousnum1 and continuousnum2 - continuous looking at figures and at the animal - as the most significant features, supported by density-distribution plots showing these features differing most between groups while others such as continuous landscape looking overlap closely. The interpretation offered is behaviourally specific and plausible: what distinguishes the groups is how long attention is sustained on social and animate content, not how much total time is spent there. What is missing is any directional statement or statistical test - no group means, no effect sizes, no significance testing for any feature - so the reader cannot tell whether high-trait participants sustained more or less continuous looking at figures." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-054" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Nine gaze features plus age and sex. The gaze features are proportions rather than counts, which normalises for exposure length, and the three-way split into total, continuous and transition proportions gives a compact description of both where attention rests and how it moves. Whole-sample means show the landscape dominating at 76% of gaze points against 17% for figures and 7% for the animal. The inclusion of age and sex as predictors is the consequential decision: it improves fit within a homogeneous adult sample but makes the model's behaviour on a demographically different test population uninterpretable, since the classifiers may be responding to the demographic features rather than the gaze features." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-054" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "HTC Vive with an embedded aGlass infrared eye-tracking module, gaze coordinates delivered in a normalised unit square through the Unity console; Unity3D for scene construction and recording scripts; scikit-learn with GridSearchCV. The authors report the hardware's limitations candidly, stating that 'the stability and accuracy of aGlass are not high enough' and that they observed rejection of VR among autistic participants - two practical findings that bear directly on whether this modality is deployable with the target population." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-054" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Mixed. Reported: the scene contents, exposure duration, headset and tracking module, the K-means region segmentation procedure, all eleven features with definitions, the seven base models with the specific hyperparameters tuned for each and the rationale for PCA before logistic regression and naive Bayes, both ensemble combination rules, the 70/30 split with threefold cross-validated GridSearchCV, and full metric tables for both experiments. Not reported: the outlier criterion and the stage at which mean imputation was applied relative to the split, the selected hyperparameter values, the number of gaze points per participant, any calibration or data-quality statistics, the diagnostic basis of the test participants, and any code. Data are available on request." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-054" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "AQ total scores for the preliminary sample only, with the five subscale domains described but never analysed. No severity, IQ, language, adaptive or comorbidity measure for the diagnosed test participants, and no AQ administered to them - so it is not possible to establish whether the diagnosed group would even have scored above the study's own trait threshold." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-054" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Seven classical classifiers plus an ensemble, with each model's assumptions and their consequences discussed individually - PCA applied before logistic regression because of multicollinearity and before naive Bayes because of its independence assumption, normalisation before KNN because distance is scale-sensitive, standardisation before SVM for convergence. This model-by-model reasoning is more careful than most of the corpus. The ensemble is then justified on generalisation rather than accuracy grounds, which is the right argument and is the one the test experiment supports." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-054" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, precision, recall, F1 and AUC for all eight models in the preliminary experiment - a complete and transparent table - but recall alone in the test experiment, because the test sample contains no negative cases. That asymmetry matters: recall of 0.77 on an all-positive sample is uninformative about discrimination, since any model with a positive prediction bias scores well. The reported ensemble AUC of 0.90 against constituent AUCs of 0.57-0.68 is also internally implausible, since averaging or voting over models with AUC at most 0.68 cannot ordinarily yield 0.90." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-054" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The weakest ascertainment logic in the corpus, and it is structural rather than a reporting omission. In the preliminary experiment there is no diagnostic assessment at all: the positive class is created by dichotomising AQ at 21, a value chosen because 'this histogram was almost symmetrical in terms of the mean results of the questionnaire (21), with 55 out of 107 results lower than 21 and 52 results no less than 21'. The threshold is therefore a median split chosen for balance, not a clinical cut-off - the established AQ screening threshold is 32 - and the paper nonetheless labels the upper half '1 (ASD)'. In the test experiment, participants were 'already diagnosed with ASD' with no instrument, criteria, diagnostician or date reported, and no AQ was administered so their trait scores are unknown and the two experiments' labels are not commensurable." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-054" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: build a rapid VR-based gaze screening method for high autistic traits, deployable where clinical resources are limited. Main findings: an ensemble of seven classifiers reached 0.73 accuracy, 0.81 recall and reported AUC 0.90 on the median-split adult sample and recalled 77% of 22 diagnosed autistic participants; continuity of looking at the figure and animal regions was the most important feature family across tree-based models; and individual model recall shifted markedly between the two experiments, which the authors read as evidence that the two populations differ." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-054" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; one brief exposure per participant with no follow-up. Data are gaze coordinates within a synthetic scene, plus questionnaire scores and demographics - low sensitivity. Governance is properly reported: IRB approval with a reference number for both experiments and explicit attention to participant safety, which matters here because the authors note that some autistic participants rejected the VR headset." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-054" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two entirely separate samples serving different roles. The preliminary experiment used 107 non-clinical adults (49 women, 58 men, mean age 24.84 +/- 5.24) split by AQ score into 52 above-median and 55 below-median. The test experiment used 22 young autistic males (mean age 12.68 +/- 7.61) recruited through the Shenzhen Autism Society, with no comparison group. The two samples differ in age by roughly twelve years on average, in sex composition (mixed versus all-male) and in clinical status (trait-scored general population versus diagnosed), so the second is not a held-out sample from the first population but a different population entirely." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-054" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Preliminary experiment: 107 adults, 49 women and 58 men, mean age 24.84 +/- 5.24 years, mean AQ 21.0 +/- 5.2. The two label groups are closely matched - 25 women and 30 men at 24.7 +/- 5.2 years versus 24 women and 28 men at 25.0 +/- 5.3 - which is good practice, although the reported group AQ means of 16.6 for the high-trait group and 25.7 for the low-trait group are transposed relative to their labels. Test experiment: 22 participants, all male, mean age 12.68 +/- 7.61 years, with no age range, AQ, IQ, severity, comorbidity or socioeconomic data. The demographic gulf between the two samples is the study's central design flaw and is never addressed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-054" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'autistic persons', 'people with autism', 'patients', 'participants with obvious and unobvious autistic traits', 'typically developing (TD)', 'healthy children'. Predominantly person-first but with heavy disease framing - autism is repeatedly called 'a very complex disease' and 'a mental disease', and the aims include 'reducing the incidence of ASD', which conflates screening with prevention of an incident condition. The taxonomy given in the background is also outdated, listing Rett syndrome and childhood disintegrative disorder as ASD subtypes, which DSM-5 removed. Against that, the paper is careful to distinguish trait detection from diagnosis in its framing, positioning the work as identifying high autistic traits 'before making a definite diagnosis'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-054" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No cross-modal fusion. What is combined is models rather than modalities: seven classifiers ensembled by probability averaging and majority voting, with the stated rationale that 'the best model that matches the pattern of this group of data well does not mean that it can be generalized to fit another group of data', so combining reduces variance and improves generalisation. That reasoning is sound and is borne out empirically - the ensemble's recall held at 0.81 then 0.77 across the two experiments while individual models swung from 0.81 to 0.55 (gradient boosting) or 0.44 to 0.00 (logistic regression). This is the corpus's clearest demonstration that ensembling buys stability under population shift, even though the underlying task here is not what the paper claims." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-054" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Journal of Child and Adolescent Psychiatric Nursing, 35(1), 83-92 - a nursing journal, which is an unusual venue for a machine learning contribution and is consistent with the emphasis on deployability and screening infrastructure over methodological rigour." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-054" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2021. The framing is unusually specific about health-system context in China. It describes the two-stage screening structure recommended by the American Academy of Pediatrics, notes that 'approximately 50% of the time is carried out with staff assistance' even for parent-completed tools, and that assessment can 'continue for months if the symptoms are not sufficiently obvious'. On China specifically it observes that 'the study of mental diseases used to be marginalized due to the conservative culture and weak science consciousness', that 'a majority of child health care institutions in China have not incorporated early ASD screening into their daily work', and that parents and primary health care staff lack awareness of early behavioural characteristics. It also positions itself against two named Chinese eye-tracking studies reporting 88.51% and 85.1%, criticising both for using only a face on a computer screen, and grounds that criticism in a substantive claim from the literature - that social attention in autism appears normal when social information is presented statically or in isolation, which is the argument for immersive scenes." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-054" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Improve the accuracy and stability of the eye-tracking hardware and address VR acceptability among autistic participants; enlarge both samples to reduce small-sample bias; involve a specialist psychologist in scene construction since social stimuli do not automatically discriminate; and design multiple scenes and select the most discriminative." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-054" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: autism spectrum disorders (ASD), early screening, eye movement, machine learning, virtual reality (VR). 'Early screening' rather than diagnosis is the operative framing, and the paper is generally careful to describe its target as high autistic traits rather than autism - though the labelling of the above-median group as 'ASD' in the methods works against that care." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-054" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "One VR exposure of 15-20 seconds per participant" ;
                    slr:study_design "Two-stage study: a preliminary classification experiment on non-clinical adults dichotomised by questionnaire score, followed by a test experiment applying the fitted models to a s…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Two quite different groups: non-clinical adults dichotomised by autistic-trait questionnaire score in the preliminary experiment, and young diagnosed autistic males in the test experiment" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 52 above-median-AQ adults in the preliminary experiment; 22 diagnosed autistic participants in the test experiment; comparison: 55 below-median-AQ adults in the preliminary experiment; 0 in the test experiment" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Gaze recorded during 15-20 seconds of free viewing in a VR garden scene, reduced to nine region-occupancy and transition proportions plus age and sex, separated above-median from below-median autistic-trait scorers among 107 adults at 73% accuracy with an ensemble classifier, and recalled 77% of 22 separately recruited diagnosed autistic participants. The most informative features across all tree-",
                        "Preliminary experiment: ensemble best at accuracy 0.73, precision 0.68, recall 0.81, F1 0.74 and AUC 0.90; random forest and gradient boosting both 0.70 accuracy, 0.81 recall, 0.72 F1 and AUC 0.68 and 0.66; decision tree 0.70 accuracy and AUC 0.68; SVM 0.61 accuracy; logistic regression 0.61 with recall of only 0.44; naive Bayes 0.58; KNN 0.55. Test experiment recall: ensemble 0.77, naive Bayes hi" ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy, precision, recall, F1 and AUC for all eight models in the preliminary experiment, and recall only in the test experiment" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Preliminary experiment: ensemble best at accuracy 0.73, precision 0.68, recall 0.81, F1 0.74 and AUC 0.90; random forest and gradient boosting both 0.70 accuracy, 0.81 recall, 0.72 F1 and AUC 0.68 and 0.66; decision tree 0.70 accuracy and AUC 0.68; SVM 0.61 accuracy; logistic regression 0.61 with recall of only 0.44; naive Bayes 0.58; KNN 0.55. Test experiment recall: ensemble 0.77, naive Bayes hi; Gaze recorded during 15-20 seconds of free viewing in a VR garden scene, reduced to nine region-occupancy and transition proportions plus age and sex, separated above-median from below-median autistic-trait scorers among 107 adults at 73% accuracy with an ensemble classifier, and recalled 77% of 22 separately recruited diagnosed autistic participants. The most informative features across all tree-" ] ] ;
