                    slr:supporting_quote "Clinician-assigned diagnosis supported by ADOS severity, SRS-2, Vineland-3 and Stanford-Binet IQ administered by clinicians and trained psychometrists, with IQ obtained for all 64 participants." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "All 64 participants accounted for, the 30-of-32 coverage for ADOS, SRS and Vineland is stated explicitly, quality-control criteria and measured calibration error are reported, and missing data handling is specified." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "low" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<https://doi.org/10.1145/3440067.3440078> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Autism spectrum disorder (ASD) is a lifelong developmental disorder characterized by repetitive, restricted behavior and deficits in communication and social interactions. Early diagnosis and intervention can significantly reduce the hazards of the disease. However, the lack of effective clinical resources for early diagnosis has been a long-standing problem. In response to this problem, we apply the recent advances in deep neural networks on eye-tracking data in this study to classify children with and without ASD. First, we record the eye movement data of 31 children with ASD and 43 typically developing children on four categories of stimuli to construct an eye-tracking data set for ASD identification. Based on the collected eye movement data, we extract the dynamic saccadic scanpath on each image for all subjects. Then, we utilize the hierarchical features learned from a convolutional neural network and multidimensional visual salient features to encode the scanpaths. Next, we adopt the support vector machine to learn the relationship between encoded pieces of scanpaths and the labels from the two classes via supervised learning. Finally, we derive the scores of each scanpath and make the final judgment for each subject according to the scores on all scanpaths. The experimental results have shown that the proposed model has a maximum classification accuracy of 94.28% in the diagnostic tests. Based on existing research and calculation models, dynamic saccadic scanpaths can provide promising findings and implications for ASD early detection. Furthermore, integrating more information of the scanpaths into the model and developing a more in-depth description of scanpaths can improve the recognition accuracy. We hope our work can contribute to the development of multimodal approaches in the early detection and diagnosis of ASD." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Xia C; Chen K; Li K; Li H (2020). Identification of Autism Spectrum Disorder via an Eye-Tracking Based. *2020 7th International Conference on Bioinformatics Research and Appli*. https://doi.org/10.1145/3440067.3440078" ;
    dcterms:creator "Chen K",
        "Li H",
        "Li K",
        "Xia C" ;
    dcterms:title "Identification of Autism Spectrum Disorder via an Eye-Tracking Based" ;
    bibo:doi "10.1145/3440067.3440078" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_40168c96" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2020 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the control group screened?" ;
                            slr:justification "Typical status assumed from mainstream school attendance and absence of a prior diagnosis." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "74 children with a 35-child test set, where one subject is worth 2.86 accuracy points; the authors call for large-scale replication." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Group sizes, ages and recruitment institutions given; sex, IQ, socioeconomic status and any severity measure absent." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "Age differs in mean, standard deviation and range with no test or adjustment, on a task whose behaviour changes rapidly across 2-8 years." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "CCMD-3 clinical diagnosis is a recognised national standard but no instrument, diagnostician or date is reported and no ADOS or DSM confirmation was applied." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is data validity assessed?" ;
                            slr:justification "No proportion of valid gaze data or exclusion criteria reported, though the paper attributes classification errors to children producing 'meaningless eye movement'." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "CNN and saliency features are computed automatically from image content at fixation locations, with the CNN pretrained on external data." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Tracker, sampling rate, viewing distance, stimulus count, categories, size and timing all specified; calibration, fixation detection and data-loss handling are not." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the decision threshold independent of the test data?" ;
                            slr:justification "Otsu maximum between-class variance is applied to the score distribution being classified, so test scores set the boundary." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is uncertainty quantified?" ;
                            slr:justification "No confidence intervals, no confusion matrices, no statistical test; differences between conditions correspond to one or two test children." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are repeated splits reported in full rather than selectively?" ;
                            slr:justification "All five permutations tabulated, with the average reported alongside - though the abstract quotes the maximum." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are cross-study comparisons valid?" ;
                            slr:justification "Comparisons against 89% and 92% come from different datasets, stimuli and populations." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the validation split at the participant level?" ;
                            slr:justification "Disjoint sets of 15 + 20 training and 15 + 20 testing children." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the contribution of each component isolated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Ablation across four feature families with all three accuracy granularities plus sensitivity and specificity." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is participant flow accounted for?" ;
                            slr:justification "31 and 43 recruited become 30 and 40 analysed with no explanation." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "A new dataset is contributed but no availability statement is given." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No approval, consent or governance statement anywhere, for children as young as 1.8 recruited through a clinical service." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Architecture and toolbox named; no code, no SVM hyperparameters." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are limitations stated candidly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Sample size, class balance, absence of severity grading and data-validity problems are all raised explicitly, with the error analysis identifying distraction in controls as the specific cause of reduced specificity." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with ASD', 'ASD children', 'typically developing (TD) children', 'infants with ASD', 'subjects'. Mixed person-first and 'ASD children'. The framing is medical and burden-oriented - autism is 'a severe lifelong neurodevelopmental disorder', children with it 'usually struggle in school, which affects their quality of life', and families 'may also bear enormous psychological pressure and burden'. The paper also uses 'the hazards of the disease' and refers to a 'golden period' for treatment at ages 2-6." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-044" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A hybrid: a pretrained two-stream CNN as a fixed feature extractor (not fine-tuned on this data), scale-specific SVM regression models via LIBSVM, and Otsu thresholding for the final decision. Using a frozen pretrained CNN is a sensible response to 74 participants, avoiding the overfitting that end-to-end deep training would invite, and the SVM stage operates on a much larger effective sample because each child contributes many scanpath pieces. The multi-scale design - one model per piece length from 1 to 5 fixations - is the architectural contribution, and is explicitly contrasted with the alternative of zero-padding variable-length scanpaths to a fixed size." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-044" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "SciEye 2.0 eye tracker at 140 Hz; 1920 x 1080 monitor at 0.5 m; a pretrained two-stream CNN taken from prior published work rather than trained here; LIBSVM; a saliency pipeline incorporating Itti et al.'s conspicuity maps, Oliva-Torralba saliency, a trained horizon-line detector and face, person and car object detectors. Sampling rate, viewing distance, presentation timing and image dimensions are all specified; calibration procedure, data loss rate and fixation-detection algorithm are not." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-044" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No saliency maps, attention visualisation or attribution over the model's decisions, and no directional characterisation of what distinguishes the groups. Interpretability is confined to the feature-family ablation, from which the authors infer 'the importance of semantic high-level attributes in ASD recognition' - a reasonable but indirect inference, since the high-level features are face, person and car detector responses and their removal costs 5.7 accuracy points. Because the model scores image content at fixated locations rather than gaze geometry, a per-category or per-region analysis would have been straightforward and would have connected the classifier to the reduced-social-attention literature the paper reviews at length; it is not performed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-044" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Mixed. The model is described in enough detail to reimplement - patch sizes (79 x 79 centre, 158 x 158 surround resized to 79 x 79), the full 33-dimensional saliency breakdown with dimension counts per component, the multi-scale piece construction for k = 1 to 5 with the scoring equations, the aggregation formula, the thresholding method and the toolbox used. Stimulus timing, count, categories and apparatus are given. Missing: SVM kernel and hyperparameters, calibration and data-quality procedures, the identity of the CNN's pretraining data, any code or data release, any ethics documentation, and an account of the four recruited children who do not appear in the experiments." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-044" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Free viewing of 82 static images across four categories - facial images, social scenarios, animated characters and synthetic patterns - each shown for 3 seconds with a 1-second mask between images to suppress carry-over, at 0.5 m from a 1920 x 1080 monitor with no instruction beyond observing. The design choice is deliberate and argued: eye-tracking protocols for this population 'usually consider the limited attention of children with ASD, with each set of experiments lasting less than 10 minutes', and the paper contrasts its short protocol against comparators requiring 10 minutes or 10-45 minutes. Note that 82 images at 4 seconds each implies roughly 5.5 minutes of presentation, against the 'approximately 2.5 minutes for recording eye movement' claimed in the comparison - an unexplained discrepancy." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-044" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: identify autism from the dynamic structure of saccadic scanpaths rather than from static fixation statistics, using learned representations. Main findings: 93.15% mean subject-level accuracy across five participant-level permutations (94.29% maximum), with sensitivity 94.66% and specificity 92.00%; accuracy increases monotonically with aggregation from pieces (87.16%) to scanpaths (88.03%) to subjects (93.15%); CNN features contribute most and high-level semantic features next in ablation; and errors concentrate on typically developing children, attributed to distraction producing uninformative gaze." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-044" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Acknowledged and unusually specific: the sample of 31 and 43 children is small and needs large-scale replication; class balance 'is also a problem that needs attention in the validation of our machine learning based model'; severity subdivision is not addressed; and data validity is limited because distracted typically developing children 'may generate meaningless eye movement', which the error analysis identifies as the reason specificity trails sensitivity. Not acknowledged: the Otsu threshold is computed on the score distribution being classified, including at test time, so the decision boundary uses test data and would not be available when assessing a single child; the abstract's 94.28% is the best of five permutations rather than the average; comparisons against Liu and Jiang and Zhao are across different datasets and cannot establish superiority; age differs between groups in mean, spread and range without any test or adjustment; sex is unreported; the control group is unscreened; and no ethics approval is stated." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-044" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Participant-level splitting into disjoint training and testing sets, repeated over five random permutations with every permutation's result tabulated rather than only the best - a sound design, and better than most of the corpus. Two qualifications. First, the classification threshold is obtained by the maximum between-class variance method applied to the score distribution being classified, so at test time the boundary is set using the test subjects' own scores; this is transductive rather than inductive, and would not transfer to assessing an individual child, which is the deployment scenario the paper's cost and portability arguments envisage. Second, no independent cohort, external site or held-out stimulus set is used, and the cross-paper comparisons against 89% and 92% are drawn from studies on entirely different datasets and populations, so they establish nothing about relative performance." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-044" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy at three aggregation levels (piece, scanpath, subject) plus sensitivity and specificity, tabulated per permutation and per ablation condition. Reporting accuracy at all three granularities is a real methodological contribution: it makes the value of hierarchical evidence integration measurable (87.16 to 88.03 to 93.15 percent) rather than assumed, and it is the kind of reporting that would expose segment-level inflation in studies that report only the finest granularity. Missing: AUC, confusion matrices, confidence intervals, and any statistical comparison between conditions - on a 35-child test set, where one subject is worth 2.86 points, the differences between permutations and between some ablation conditions amount to one or two children." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-044" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Visual attention during free viewing, targeted specifically at its dynamic structure - the sequence of and transitions between fixations rather than where or how long the eye rests. This is the paper's defining choice and its stated critique of the field: existing methods 'tend to analyze the indicators based on individual static fixation, such as the fixation location and fixation duration. However, the actual eye movement is a dynamic process. The relationship between fixations also contains important information in visual attention.' Ironically, in operationalising the dynamic view the model discards fixation duration entirely, which the authors recognise and list as future work." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-044" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups: 31 autistic children recruited through the Children Rehabilitation Center at Shaanxi Rehabilitation Hospital and 43 typically developing children attending mainstream schools in Xi'an. The reported experiments use 30 and 40 respectively (15 + 20 training, 15 + 20 testing), so one autistic and three typically developing children are recruited but unaccounted for in the analysis, with no explanation given. No other-diagnosis comparison group; the authors themselves flag class balance as an issue needing attention." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-044" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Autistic children had 'confirmed clinical diagnoses of ASD according to the Classification and Diagnostic Criteria of Mental Disorders in China-Third Edition' (CCMD-3), with no instrument, diagnostician, date or severity measure reported. Using CCMD-3 rather than DSM-5 or ADOS/ADI-R is a legitimate national standard but limits comparability with the rest of this corpus, where ADOS confirmation is the norm. Typically developing status rests on mainstream school attendance and absence of a prior autism diagnosis, with no screening instrument applied - so the control group is unscreened." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-044" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Larger-scale replication with attention to class balance; extension from binary identification to severity scoring; protocol improvements to hold children's attention and increase the proportion of valid data; incorporation of fixation duration, which the authors observe 'most identification models have ignored'; replacement of fixed-length piece encoding with recurrent networks able to consume variable-length scanpaths directly; and transfer of the framework to other recognition tasks including other diseases, gender and age." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-044" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Autistic group: n = 31, aged 1.8-8 years, mean 4.67 +/- 1.59. Typically developing group: n = 43, aged 2.5-7 years, mean 4.15 +/- 0.98. Age is reported but not tested: the means differ by about half a year, the spreads differ substantially (SD 1.59 versus 0.98), and the ranges are asymmetric, with only the autistic group including children under 2.5 or over 7. Free-viewing behaviour changes rapidly across 2-8 years, and age is not entered into or controlled by the model. Sex is not reported at all for either group, nor is IQ, developmental level, socioeconomic status or ethnicity. All participants are from Xi'an." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-044" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD), Machine Learning, Eye-Tracking, Saccadic Scanpath, Support Vector Machine (SVM), Representation Learning. 'Saccadic scanpath' and 'representation learning' together capture the contribution - the object of study is how to represent a gaze trajectory, with autism as the classification task on which the representation is evaluated." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-044" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; a single session per child with no follow-up. Data are gaze coordinates over image stimuli. No ethics committee approval, parental consent procedure, anonymization or data governance statement appears anywhere in the paper, despite participants being children as young as 1.8 years recruited through a clinical rehabilitation service." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-044" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No IQ, developmental quotient, language, adaptive functioning or symptom severity measure is reported for either group, and no comorbidity information. Grading severity is named only as future work." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-044" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No cross-modal fusion - gaze only. There is within-modality feature fusion between learned CNN representations and handcrafted multi-level saliency features, and the ablation quantifies each family's contribution: removing CNN features costs 8.6 accuracy points, high-level semantic features 5.7, low-level 4.0 and mid-level 2.3. The paper closes by expressing the hope that the work 'can contribute to the development of multimodal approaches in the early detection and diagnosis of ASD', positioning gaze as one future component rather than a complete solution." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-044" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "ICBRA 2020, the 7th International Conference on Bioinformatics Research and Applications (ACM, Berlin) - a computing conference, published open access with support from King's College London and Northwestern Polytechnical University." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-044" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2020. The framing combines an access argument with a methodological one. On access: autism diagnosis 'usually relies on a multidisciplinary team of experts, which is relatively difficult to achieve, especially for economically underdeveloped areas', and eye trackers are 'affordable, portable, and easy to operate' so 'they can also be used in poor and remote areas'. On method: the paper's specific criticism of the existing eye-tracking literature is that models 'have usually utilized statistical analysis to discover the differences between the two classes' and so 'have high requirements for selecting the stimuli and indicators of eye movement for analysis', and that they analyse static fixations while 'the actual eye movement is a dynamic process'. Protocol duration is treated as a competitive dimension in its own right - the paper contrasts its short recording against comparators needing 10 or 10-45 minutes - reflecting a field increasingly aware that attention span, not accuracy alone, bounds what is deployable with young children." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-044" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Scanpath pieces of one to five consecutive fixations, each fixation represented by two-stream CNN features over centre and surround image patches concatenated with 33 saliency features spanning low-level (orientation subbands, colour, Itti conspicuity, colour histograms, Oliva-Torralba saliency), mid-level gist (distance to centre, horizon line) and high-level semantic (face, person and car detector responses) tiers. Crucially, features describe what was looked at - the image content at each fixation - rather than the gaze coordinates themselves, so the representation encodes the semantics of visual attention rather than its geometry. Fixation duration is deliberately excluded and its omission acknowledged." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-044" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A therapy room with at most two people present besides the child. Autistic participants came from the Children Rehabilitation Center at Shaanxi Rehabilitation Hospital, Xi'an; typically developing participants from mainstream Xi'an schools. Funded by the National Natural Science Foundation of China, the China Postdoctoral Science Foundation and the Natural Science Foundation of Shaanxi Province." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-044" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "One free-viewing session per child: 82 images presented for 3 seconds each with a 1-second mask between images" ;
                    slr:study_design "Case-control classification study with a purpose-built eye-tracking dataset and repeated random participant-level splits" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Preschool and early school-age children with autism" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 31 recruited; 30 used in the reported experiments (15 training, 15 testing); comparison: 43 recruited; 40 used in the reported experiments (20 training, 20 testing)" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Across five permutations, subject-level accuracy was 94.29, 91.43, 94.29, 94.29 and 91.43 percent, averaging 93.15% with mean sensitivity 94.66% and specificity 92.00%. Accuracy rises monotonically with aggregation: 87.16% at piece level, 88.03% at scanpath level, 93.15% at subject level. Ablation: without CNN features 84.57%, without high-level features 87.42%, without low-level 89.14%, without m",
                        "Encoding the dynamic structure of saccadic scanpaths - overlapping segments of one to five consecutive fixations, each represented by pretrained CNN features plus multi-level saliency features - and scoring them with scale-specific SVMs classified children as autistic or typically developing with 93.15% mean subject-level accuracy over five random participant splits. Accuracy improves monotonicall" ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy at three granularities (piece, scanpath, subject), plus sensitivity and specificity, reported for each of five permutations and for each ablation condition. Reporting accuracy at all three aggregation levels is a genuine contribution - it quantifies how much the hierarchical voting buys" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Across five permutations, subject-level accuracy was 94.29, 91.43, 94.29, 94.29 and 91.43 percent, averaging 93.15% with mean sensitivity 94.66% and specificity 92.00%. Accuracy rises monotonically with aggregation: 87.16% at piece level, 88.03% at scanpath level, 93.15% at subject level. Ablation: without CNN features 84.57%, without high-level features 87.42%, without low-level 89.14%, without m; Encoding the dynamic structure of saccadic scanpaths - overlapping segments of one to five consecutive fixations, each represented by pretrained CNN features plus multi-level saliency features - and scoring them with scale-specific SVMs classified children as autistic or typically developing with 93.15% mean subject-level accuracy over five random participant splits. Accuracy improves monotonicall" ] ] ;
