    slr:journal "2020 7th International Conference on Bioinformatics Research and Appli" ;
    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "unclear" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Stimulus timing and counts are precisely reported, but 31 and 43 recruited children become 30 and 40 in the experiments without explanation, and no data-quality or exclusion accounting is given despite the paper attributing errors to invalid gaze." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "unclear" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Confirmed clinical diagnoses under CCMD-3 with no instrument, diagnostician or date reported; control status established only by school attendance and absence of prior diagnosis, with no screening instrument." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "unclear" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Participant-level splitting repeated over five permutations with all results reported, but the decision threshold is computed by Otsu on the score distribution being classified, so test-set scores determine the boundary." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Autistic children recruited from a rehabilitation centre versus typically developing children from mainstream schools, so group is confounded with recruitment route; ages differ in mean, spread and range without test or adjustment; sex unreported; control group unscreened; and four recruited children are unaccounted for in the analysis." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "high" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<https://doi.org/10.1177/07356331211040405> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Several robot-mediated therapies have been implemented for diagnosis and improvement of communication skills in children with Autism Spectrum Disorder. The proposed research uses an existing model i.e., Multi-robot-mediated Intervention System (MRIS) in combination with Hidden Markov Model (HMM) to develop an infrastructure for categorizing the severity of autism in children. The observable states are joint attention type (low, delayed, and immediate) and imitation type (partial, moderate, and full) whereas the non-observable states are (level of autism i.e., (minimal, and mild). The research has been conducted on 12 subjects in which 8 children were in the training session with 72 experiments over 9 weeks, and the remaining 4 subjects were in the prediction test with 25 experiments for 6 weeks. The predicted category was compared with the actual category of autism assessed by the therapist using Childhood Autism Rating Scale. The accuracy of the proposed 1School of Mechanical and Manufacturing Engineering (SMME), National University of Sciences and T echnology (NUST), H-12, Islamabad, Pakistan 2National Center of Artificial Intelligence (NCAI), National University of Sciences and Technology, (NUST), H-12, Islamabad, Pakistan 3Shifa International Hospitals Limited and Director at Autism Resource Centre, Islamabad, Pakistan 4School of Computing, Mathematics and Digital Technology, Manchester Metropolitan University, Manchester, UK Corresponding Author: Sara Ali, School of Mechanical and Manufacturing Engineering (SMME), National University of Sciences and T echnology (NUST), H-12, Islamabad, Pakistan. Email: sarababer@smme.nust.edu.pk Journal of Educational Computing Research ! The Author(s) 2021 Article reuse guidelines: sagepub.com/journals-permissions DOI: 10.1177/07356331211040405 journals.sagepub.com/home/jec Ali et al. 723 model is 76%. Further, a statistically significantly moderate Kappa measure of agreement between Childhood Autism Rating Scale and our proposed model has been performed in which n ¼ 25, k ¼ 0.52, and p ¼ 0.009. This research contributes towards the usefulness of Hidden Markov Model integrated with joint attention and imitation modules for categorizing the level of autism using multi-robot therapies." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Ali S; Mehmood F; Ayaz Y; Sajid M; Sadia H; Nawaz R (2021). An Experimental Trial: Multi-Robot Therapy for Categorization of Autism Level Using Hidden Markov Model. *Journal of Educational Computing Resea*. https://doi.org/10.1177/07356331211040405" ;
    dcterms:creator "Ali S",
        "Ayaz Y",
        "Mehmood F",
        "Nawaz R",
        "Sadia H",
        "Sajid M" ;
    dcterms:title "An Experimental Trial: Multi-Robot Therapy for Categorization of Autism Level Using Hidden Markov Model" ;
    bibo:doi "10.1177/07356331211040405" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_b70dae32" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2021 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Per-child age, sex, autism type and mean performance are tabulated, but no cognitive, language, comorbidity or CARS score data appear." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the severity terminology consistent and defined?" ;
                            slr:justification "The introduction describes DSM levels 1-3 and states the study addresses levels 1 and 2, while the categories used are 'minimal' and 'mild' with no mapping given." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the test set composition adequate?" ;
                            slr:justification "Four children, of whom Table 2 indicates only one is mild, cannot generate the 12 mild actual categories reported across 25 test sessions." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "Twelve children with four in the test set, defended only by reference to the authors' own prior study using a similar number." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is measurement automated within the intervention?" ;
                            slr:justification "Gaze from robot cameras and imitation from Kinect are scored during therapy itself, requiring no separate assessment session." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "Binning thresholds were set in discussion with the therapist who also assigned the severity labels." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "CARS categories from centre clinicians with no scores, band definitions or inter-rater reliability." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Both modules specified with their cues and actions, robot placement and distances, sensors and SDK modules named, and binning thresholds stated." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the model parameters reported and internally valid?" ;
                            slr:justification "Transition probabilities as printed do not form valid distributions, and no fitting procedure is described." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the unit of statistical analysis appropriate?" ;
                            slr:justification "Kappa computed with n = 25 sessions drawn from only 4 children, treating repeated measures as independent." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the model's independence assumption justified?" ;
                            slr:justification "The posterior multiplies two conditionals although the imitation module is activated by joint attention and so cannot be independent of it." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are individual predictions inspectable?" ;
                            slr:justification "All 25 test cases tabulated with input categories, actual and predicted severity, allowing every error to be examined." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the train/test split at the participant level?" ;
                            slr:justification "Eight children for training and four held out for testing, stated explicitly." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are agreement statistics accompanied by uncertainty?" ;
                            slr:justification "Kappa 0.52 reported with a p value but no confidence interval, and no sensitivity, specificity or per-class breakdown." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "None; the HMM parameter estimation procedure is not described." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are limitations stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No limitations section; the only self-critical remark defends the sample size by reference to the authors' prior work." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Centre-level approval by the autism specialist and director board plus signed parental consent, but no named institutional review board and no data-handling policy." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No release or availability statement." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the conclusions proportionate to the evidence?" ;
                            slr:justification "The claim that the method 'does not need a therapist for intervention or categorization prediction' is undercut by every training label originating in a therapist's CARS assessment." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are internal results consistent?" ;
                            slr:justification "Table 2's per-child severity assignments cannot produce Table 4's distribution of actual categories across the 25 test sessions." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A single group of 12 autistic children from the Autism Resource Center in Islamabad, split into 8 for training and 4 for the prediction test. There is no non-autistic comparison group and none is intended - the task is severity categorisation within autism rather than case-control discrimination, which makes this one of the few studies in the corpus targeting a graded clinical outcome rather than a binary diagnosis. The analysis unit is the intervention session rather than the child, so 97 sessions arise from 12 individuals and 25 test predictions from 4." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-052" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with ASD', 'ASD children', 'autistic child', 'the patient', 'subjects'. Mixed person-first and 'ASD children', with 'patient' used for the severe end of the spectrum. The framing is medical and severity-graded, opening with an etymology of 'autism' as 'referring to the children with extreme inability to relate with others' and describing the spectrum as running from borders that 'overlap normality on one end' to 'extreme intellectual impairment caused by brain malfunctions on the other'. Robot-mediated therapy is justified on the ground that 'ASD children are more inclined towards robots because of their predictive behavior'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-052" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Longitudinal in structure - 9 sessions per training child over 9 weeks and about 6 per test child over 6 weeks - but the longitudinal dimension is not analysed. Sessions are treated as independent observations rather than as repeated measures within child, and no change over time in joint attention or imitation is examined despite the data being an intervention series. Data are video and depth recordings of identifiable young children, with a photograph of a child with the robots published. Governance is reported at centre level (autism specialist and director board approval, signed parental consent) but no institutional review board is named and no data-handling policy appears." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-052" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy (19 of 25, 76%) and Cohen's kappa (0.52, p = 0.009) against clinician CARS categorisation. Reporting kappa rather than accuracy alone is appropriate for an agreement study and is better practice than most of the corpus. But the kappa's n = 25 counts sessions rather than children, so its significance is overstated; no confidence interval accompanies it; and no sensitivity, specificity or per-class breakdown is given, though the individually tabulated 25 test rows allow these to be derived - three false-mild and three false-minimal errors, symmetric across classes." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-052" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: use performance data already generated during robot-mediated therapy to categorise autism severity level without requiring a therapist to do the categorisation. Main findings: an HMM over binned joint attention and imitation categories matched clinician CARS categories in 19 of 25 test sessions (76% accuracy) with moderate agreement (kappa 0.52, p = 0.009); average joint attention success across all children was 65.47% and imitation 76.19%; and most children fell in the delayed joint attention and moderate-to-full imitation bands." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-052" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A held-out split by participant, with 8 children and 72 sessions for training and 4 children and 25 sessions for testing, and test participants selected randomly. Splitting by child rather than by session is the correct choice and is stated clearly. Two failures follow it, though. Prediction and evaluation both operate at the session level, so the 25 test observations are roughly six repeated measures from each of four children, and the kappa treats them as independent - the effective sample is 4 children, not 25 sessions. And the test set's composition is unbalanced at the child level (one mild, three minimal by Table 2), which cannot generate the 12-mild, 13-minimal distribution of actual categories reported in Table 4, leaving the evaluation internally inconsistent. There is no cross-validation, no repetition over alternative splits, no independent cohort and no confidence interval." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-052" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: autism spectrum disorder (ASD), imitation, joint attention, Hidden Markov Model (HMM), robot-mediated therapies, Multi-robot-mediated Intervention System (MRIS). Two of the six name the specific clinical constructs targeted, which reflects the paper's grounding in established autism intervention targets rather than in generic behavioural sensing." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-052" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A two-state Hidden Markov Model with the severity level as the hidden state and the two binned behavioural categories as observables. The choice is defensible for an intrinsically sequential intervention series with a latent clinical state, and the model is inherently interpretable. But the temporal structure that motivates an HMM is not actually exploited - sessions are treated as independent observations rather than as a sequence within child, no state transitions over the course of therapy are examined, and the reported posterior computation is a static Bayes calculation over two conditionals rather than a sequential inference. In effect the model functions as a naive Bayes classifier over nine input cells, with the HMM framing adding formalism rather than capability." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-052" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Participants were recruited from an autism resource centre and 'evaluated clinically based on Childhood Autism Rating Scale Schedule (CARS) by the autism experts'. CARS then serves double duty as both the eligibility assessment and the reference standard for severity. No CARS scores are reported for any child, no cut-offs are given for the minimal and mild bands, no inter-rater reliability is established, and no DSM-5 or ADOS assessment is described. Because the model is evaluated purely by agreement with these CARS categories, the absence of any information about their reliability is the central weakness in the reference standard - a moderate kappa against an unquantified rater tells one little." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-052" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "CARS severity category only, as minimal or mild. No CARS total scores, no IQ, no language or adaptive measures, and no comorbidity information. The two performance measures the model consumes - joint attention and imitation success rates - are themselves clinically meaningful and are tabulated per child, but they are the model's input rather than an independent characterisation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-052" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two behavioural channels are combined, but through a probabilistic model rather than a learned fusion: the posterior for each severity category is computed as the product of P(category | joint attention) and P(category | imitation). No single-channel baseline is reported, so the contribution of combining them cannot be assessed, and no ablation is performed. More problematically, the product form assumes the two observables are conditionally independent given severity - an assumption the study's own architecture violates, since the imitation module only activates once the child has sustained gaze on a robot for five seconds, making imitation performance conditional on joint attention performance by construction. There are also two sensing modalities (robot camera gaze and Kinect depth), but these map one-to-one onto the two behaviours rather than being fused." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-052" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Journal of Educational Computing Research, 60(3), 722-741 - an educational technology journal rather than a clinical or engineering venue, which is consistent with the intervention framing and with the limited statistical scrutiny the analysis received." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-052" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Mixed. Well reported: the two intervention modules with their specific cues and actions, the physical setup with distances, the sensors and SDK modules used, the binning thresholds for both observable variables, the session and week counts for training and testing, and a per-child table of age, sex, autism type and mean performance. Poorly reported or absent: CARS scores and band definitions, how the HMM parameters were estimated from the 72 training sessions, the basis for the transition probabilities quoted, per-child test results, any code or data, and any statement of institutional ethics approval. The printed transition probabilities do not sum to valid distributions, and the posterior formula assumes conditional independence between joint attention and imitation without stating or justifying that assumption." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-052" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two modules of an existing multi-robot intervention, each targeting a core impairment. The joint attention module escalates cues in a least-to-most hierarchy - visual (cyclic eye colour change, blinking), then visual plus speech, then visual plus speech plus motion - which is drawn from established prompting practice and allows the level of support required to be read off the cue level at which the child responds. The imitation module is contingent rather than scripted: the child must fixate one of the two robots for five seconds to activate it, then imitates four movements. Using two robots is a deliberate design choice presented as 'representing a triad human communication scenario', on the reasoning that dyadic robot interaction does not exercise the same social configuration as everyday multi-party interaction." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-052" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two NAO humanoid robots with the Naoqi SDK, using the ALGazeDetection module of NaoqiPeoplesPerception to determine whether the child is looking at the robot and to compute eye-contact delay and duration; a Microsoft Kinect for real-time skeletal joint tracking to score imitation against the robot's action. The measurement is therefore fully automated within the intervention itself, requiring no separate assessment session - which is the practical strength of the approach." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-052" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None stated. The conclusion asserts the model's usefulness without identifying required replication, larger samples, per-child validation, or the reconciliation of its own tables." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-052" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2021. The framing combines prevalence growth (CDC estimates moving from 1 in 59 to 1 in 54) with a claim that autism is 'the fastest-growing developmental disorder in the USA with a 6% to 15% increase in rate per year'. Distinctively for this corpus, it invokes COVID-19 as a driver: 'especially under the current COVID-19 situation, the current research trends are focusing online for technology-based therapies', which situates robot-mediated intervention within a pandemic-era shift toward remote and technology-delivered care. The rationale for robots is given as controllability, accuracy, low cost and adaptability, plus the claim that autistic children are drawn to robots because of their predictable behaviour. The specific gap claimed is that existing robot therapies improve skills but cannot label severity, and the authors note a prior HMM attempt that 'failed to produce hidden Markov models that are indicative of a person's level of autism'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-052" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The Autism Resource Center, Islamabad, with the study approved by the centre's autism specialist and director board and parental consent obtained. Two robots were placed in an arc approximately 1 m from the child, who sat for the joint attention module and stood for imitation, with a Kinect behind the robots. The authors state that the participant setting is illustrated 'to make the intervention replicable'. No external funding was received." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-052" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The model is interpretable by construction: every input is a named clinical category, every hidden state is a severity level, and the posterior is an explicit product of two conditional probabilities that a clinician could in principle compute by hand. All 25 test cases are tabulated with their joint attention category, imitation category, actual category and predicted category, so every prediction and every error is individually inspectable - a level of transparency almost no other study in the corpus offers. What is missing is any account of the fitted probabilities, so the direction and strength of the learned relationships cannot be read, and no analysis of which of the nine input cells the model handles well or badly." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-052" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Essentially none. The paper has no limitations section, and the only self-critical remark is a defence of the sample size on the ground that 'the presented work is based on previous research that also uses a similar number of subjects'. Identifiable in review and unaddressed: Table 2 assigns each test child a single autism type (one mild, three minimal) while Table 4 records 12 mild and 13 minimal actual categories across 25 test sessions, which cannot be reconciled without an unexplained within-child change in CARS category, and until resolved makes the reported accuracy and kappa uninterpretable; the kappa treats 25 sessions from 4 children as independent, inflating the effective sample about sixfold; the two observables are binned to three levels each using thresholds set with the therapist who also supplied the labels; the conditional independence assumption is contradicted by the design, since the imitation module is activated by joint attention; the severity labels 'minimal' and 'mild' are never mapped to the DSM levels the introduction describes; and the claim that no therapist is needed is undercut by the fact that every training label comes from a therapist's CARS assessment." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-052" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Twelve children, 11 male and 1 female, aged 4.2 to 7.5 years with a mean of 6.5 and standard deviation 0.98, described as of Asian background. Per-child age, sex, autism type and mean joint attention and imitation performance are tabulated individually, which is good practice and is what makes the internal inconsistency between the tables detectable. No IQ, language, adaptive functioning, comorbidity or socioeconomic information. The sex ratio of 11:1 is more extreme than population estimates and the single girl is in the test set. Age is not related to performance anywhere in the analysis despite a 3.3-year span across which joint attention and imitation both develop substantially." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-052" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two behaviours, both DSM-relevant core impairments: joint attention, operationalised as the delay before establishing eye contact with a robot and the duration for which it is sustained under escalating cue support; and imitation, operationalised as the proportion of four gross motor actions correctly reproduced. Measuring both within a single interaction, and gating one on the other, is the design's distinctive feature - most robot studies in this corpus measure a single behaviour." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-052" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two continuous session-level percentages, each collapsed into three ordinal bands at 50% and 80% thresholds - so the entire model input for a session is one of nine discrete combinations. This is by far the coarsest representation in the corpus. The thresholds were 'deduced in discussion with the therapist and according to the child's performance', which gives them clinical meaning but also means they were chosen with sight of the data by the same source that supplied the labels. The per-child averages in Table 2 show how much is discarded: joint attention values from 43.43% to 85.46% and imitation from 59.9% to 92.19% collapse into a handful of cells." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-052" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "15 weeks total - 9 weeks and 9 sessions per child for the 8 training participants (72 experiments), and 6 weeks and approximately 6 sessions per child for the 4 test participants (25 experiments)" ;
                    slr:study_design "Prospective intervention study with severity categorisation; single group of autistic children split into a training set and a prediction test set, with agreement against clinician…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Children with autism at minimal and mild severity levels" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 12 (8 training, 4 test); comparison: 0" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "An HMM taking binned joint attention and imitation success rates from robot-mediated therapy sessions reproduced clinician CARS severity categories in 76% of 25 test sessions, with moderate agreement (kappa 0.52, p = 0.009). The practical claim is that severity categorisation can be derived from data the intervention already produces, so that 'this technique focuses on the prediction of the level ",
                        "The model matched the clinician's CARS category in 19 of 25 test sessions, giving 76% accuracy and a statistically significant moderate kappa of 0.52 (p = 0.009). The six errors are individually tabulated and are symmetric - three sessions predicted mild where CARS said minimal and three the reverse. The test data comprise 12 sessions with an actual category of mild and 13 with minimal, which is c" ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy (19 of 25 correct, 76%) and Cohen's kappa against clinician CARS categorisation (kappa = 0.52, p = 0.009, described as moderate agreement). No sensitivity, specificity, confusion matrix by class, confidence interval or per-child breakdown" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "The model matched the clinician's CARS category in 19 of 25 test sessions, giving 76% accuracy and a statistically significant moderate kappa of 0.52 (p = 0.009). The six errors are individually tabulated and are symmetric - three sessions predicted mild where CARS said minimal and three the reverse. The test data comprise 12 sessions with an actual category of mild and 13 with minimal, which is c; An HMM taking binned joint attention and imitation success rates from robot-mediated therapy sessions reproduced clinician CARS severity categories in 76% of 25 test sessions, with moderate agreement (kappa 0.52, p = 0.009). The practical claim is that severity categorisation can be derived from data the intervention already produces, so that 'this technique focuses on the prediction of the level " ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "cc264478aa3012c96fb0f553d6ac83082e3c0a367cca23d2500e04ecfd185060" ;
