    slr:journal "Journal of Educational Computing Resea" ;
    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Twelve children from one centre with 11 boys and 1 girl, no comorbidity or cognitive characterisation, and a test set of four children whose severity composition (one mild, three minimal) cannot account for the distribution of actual categories reported across the 25 test sessions." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Session counts and durations are reported, but the actual severity categories in the results table are irreconcilable with the per-child assignments in the participant table, and no per-child test results are given." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "CARS categories assigned by centre clinicians with no scores, no band definitions, no inter-rater reliability and no independent diagnostic confirmation, yet serving as the sole outcome against which the model is judged." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Two continuous measures binned to three levels each using thresholds set in discussion with the label-providing therapist; posterior computed under a conditional independence assumption contradicted by the design; printed transition probabilities do not form valid distributions; and no account of how model parameters were estimated." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "high" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<https://doi.org/10.2196/13810> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Background: Several studies have shown that facial attention differs in children with autism. Measuring eye gaze and emotion recognition in children with autism is challenging, as standard clinical assessments must be delivered in clinical settings by a trained clinician. Wearable technologies may be able to bring eye gaze and emotion recognition into natural social interactions and settings. Objective: This study aimed to test: (1) the feasibility of tracking gaze using wearable smart glasses during a facial expression recognition task and (2) the ability of these gaze-tracking data, together with facial expression recognition responses, to distinguish children with autism from neurotypical controls (NCs). Methods: We compared the eye gaze and emotion recognition patterns of 16 children with autism spectrum disorder (ASD) and 17 children without ASD via wearable smart glasses fitted with a custom eye tracker. Children identified static facial expressions of images presented on a computer screen along with nonsocial distractors while wearing Google Glass and the eye tracker. Faces were presented in three trials, during one of which children received feedback in the form of the correct classification. We employed hybrid human-labeling and computer vision–enabled methods for pupil tracking and world–gaze translation calibration. We analyzed the impact of gaze and emotion recognition features in a prediction task aiming to distinguish children with ASD from NC participants. Results: Gaze and emotion recognition patterns enabled the training of a classifier that distinguished ASD and NC groups. However, it was unable to significantly outperform other classifiers that used only age and gender features, suggesting that further work is necessary to disentangle these effects. Conclusions: Although wearable smart glasses show promise in identifying subtle differences in gaze tracking and emotion recognition patterns in children with and without ASD, the present form factor and data do not allow for these differences to be reliably exploited by machine learning systems. Resolving these challenges will be an important step toward continuous tracking of the ASD phenotype. (J Med Internet Res 2020;22(4):e13810) doi: 10.2196/13810 J Med Internet Res 2020 | vol. 22 | iss. 4 | e13810 | p. 1 http://www.jmir.org/2020/4/e13810/ (page number not for citation purposes) Nag et al JOURNAL OF MEDICAL INTERNET RESEARCH XSL•FO RenderX" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Nag A; Haber N; Voss C; Tamura S; Daniels J; Ma J; Chiang B; Ramachandran S; Schwartz J; Winograd T; Feinstein C; Wall DP (2020). Toward Continuous Social Phenotyping: Analyzing Gaze Patterns in an Emotion Recognition Task for Children With Autism Through Wearable Smart Glasses. *Journal of Medical Internet Research, 22(4), e13810*. https://doi.org/10.2196/13810" ;
    dcterms:creator "Chiang B",
        "Daniels J",
        "Feinstein C",
        "Haber N",
        "Ma J",
        "Nag A",
        "Ramachandran S",
        "Schwartz J",
        "Tamura S",
        "Voss C",
        "Wall DP",
        "Winograd T" ;
    dcterms:title "Toward Continuous Social Phenotyping: Analyzing Gaze Patterns in an Emotion Recognition Task for Children With Autism Through Wearable Smart Glasses" ;
    bibo:doi "10.2196/13810" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_f9a5195f" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2020 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Recruitment window and channels, full eligibility criteria for both groups, and every exclusion with its reason." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "81% versus 53% male, named by the authors as a bias potentially exacerbating known gender differences on emotion and gaze tasks." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "33 analysed children against 219 features per trial; the authors state more data are required for a reliable model." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is control status positively screened?" ;
                            slr:justification "Five exclusion criteria including SCQ threshold and sibling diagnosis of autism or schizophrenia." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the spectrum adequately represented?" ;
                            slr:justification "Only 3 of 16 autistic participants had ABIQ below 80, so findings may not reflect children across the spectrum." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Could the apparatus itself differ systematically between groups?" ;
                            slr:justification "A chinrest was added mid-study and more autistic participants were tested later, which the authors state 'may be exploited by the classifiers'; the mount also occluded about 15% of the lower visual field." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "Medical-record confirmation with SCQ corroboration, plus SRS-2 and ABIQ for all participants." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Hardware construction and cost, screen geometry, viewing distance, stimulus source and balancing, timings, feedback modality, calibration and validation devices all specified, with implementation detail in an appendix." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the measurement instrument validated?" ;
                            slr:justification "The custom eye tracker 'has not been evaluated on a standard dataset or compared with standard eye trackers', and much of the pupil and gaze annotation was ultimately manual." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is regularisation appropriate to the feature-to-sample ratio?" ;
                            slr:justification "Elastic net chosen explicitly because 219 features far exceed 33 participants, with logistic regression as a comparator." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the conclusions proportionate to the evidence?" ;
                            slr:justification "A gaze-only result of 83.9% with P = .002 is declined as a positive finding because the metadata baseline is high and correction removes significance." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is statistical significance of accuracy established?" ;
                            slr:justification "Monte Carlo shuffling tests over 2,500 iterations with convergence checking for every reported accuracy." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a null or trivial baseline included?" ;
                            slr:justification "An age-and-gender-only model, which matched the full model's 71.0% and is the basis of the paper's central conclusion." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the validation split at the participant level?" ;
                            slr:justification "Leave-one-participant-out for every classifier in the grid." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is multiplicity across the ablation grid accounted for?" ;
                            slr:justification "Bonferroni correction applied, and results that fail it are reported as failing." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are conflicts of interest declared?" ;
                            slr:justification "The senior author declares founding Cognoa, a digital child-health company." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the negative result reported without inflation?" ;
                            slr:justification "The abstract, results and conclusions all state that the features did not significantly outperform an age-and-gender baseline." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Implementation detail is in a supplementary appendix; no code released." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Written informed consent from parents and children under an approved Stanford IRB protocol." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a prior assumption of the field revised in light of the data?" ;
                            slr:justification "The authors state explicitly that they had believed facial attention differences were clear and now believe the effect is subtle and requires better hardware." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No release or availability statement." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Journal of Medical Internet Research, 22(4), e13810 - a digital health venue, appropriate for a wearable feasibility study, and one whose remit makes a negative feasibility result publishable in a way that a pure machine learning venue might not." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-049" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Emotion recognition under competing attentional demand - a face at centre flanked by two validated high-autism-interest nonsocial distractors, so that gaze allocation and emotion identification are measured in the same trial. The paper's rationale for combining them is theoretical rather than opportunistic: emotion recognition and social attention 'have mostly been studied in isolation' and both have been proposed separately as diagnostic indicators, so the authors 'hypothesized that they are deeply linked and studied them together'. The three-trial structure with feedback in trial 2 only is designed to emulate the Superpower Glass intervention, and it produces an unanticipated result - discrimination was best in trial 1, before any feedback or task adaptation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-049" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No model-explanation method, but the ablation grid functions as a structured interpretability analysis at the block level, and its results are interpreted carefully. Two patterns are drawn out. First, discrimination is best in trial 1 - 'the first trial conducted which was before receiving any feedback or adjusting to the task' - implying the signal is strongest on first exposure and attenuates as children adapt, which has direct implications for protocol design. Second, emotion confusion matrices alone collapse to below chance in later trials, so the emotion-response signal is unstable. The behavioural interpretation is explicitly bounded: the authors note that classical eye-tracking studies have found subtler effects such as increased mouth relative to eye attention, and state that 'the gaze data collected by our system was too noisy to test for these subtler effects'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-049" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Autistic group: n = 16, 13 male (81%) and 3 female, mean age 12.13 +/- 3.31 years, range 6-17; SCQ 18.86 +/- 6.43; ABIQ 102.75 +/- 19.54 (range 55-133); SRS-2 total 78.85 +/- 11.13. Neurotypical group: n = 17, 9 male (53%) and 8 female, mean age 11.53 +/- 2.48, range 8-17; SCQ 1.82 +/- 1.07; ABIQ 108.94 +/- 9.58 (91-129); SRS-2 total 44.41 +/- 8.11. Comorbidities and current medications are itemised for the 11 autistic participants who completed the survey - methylphenidate in 3, sertraline in 2, and single instances of arginine vasopressin, guanfacine, carbamazepine, aripiprazole and dexmethylphenidate, with only 4 of 11 unmedicated against 15 of 17 controls. This is the most complete participant characterisation in the corpus. The sex imbalance (81% versus 53% male) is named by the authors as a limitation, and the ABIQ distribution shows the autistic cohort is predominantly high-functioning with wider spread than controls." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-049" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Very good, with implementation detail deferred to a supplementary appendix rather than omitted. Reported in the paper: recruitment window and channels, all inclusion and exclusion criteria for both groups, every excluded participant with the reason, hardware construction and cost, screen size, resolution and viewing distance, stimulus source and balancing, image counts per trial, display durations, feedback modality and timing, the calibration validation device, the four-calibration scheme with per-calibration polynomial regression, the four-way gaze coding scheme, all four feature blocks with their dimensionalities, the classifier and its rationale, the cross-validation scheme, the shuffling test with iteration count and convergence checking, and a full 60-cell ablation grid. Pupil tracking algorithms, inter-labeller reliability and outlier exclusion criteria are in Multimedia Appendix 1. Not released: data or code." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-049" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2020. The framing is the gap between where social skills are practised and where they are measured: emotion recognition and eye contact skills 'are important to improve social functioning, but the methods currently used to measure and track such skills are delivered in clinical settings or via trained administrators outside the social context where these skills are practiced'. The proposed remedy is mobile and wearable machine learning platforms enabling continuous phenotyping during natural social interactions. The paper's most valuable contribution to the field's self-understanding is its retraction of a prior assumption, stated explicitly: 'The prevailing belief in the literature was that facial attention differences are very clear in ASD, so we hypothesized that distraction ratios across an emotion recognition task alone were enough to distinguish ASD vs NC reliably. We now believe that integrating further subtlety through robust gaze-tracking hardware will be required to design a phenotyping marker from this effect.' It also reports that later generations of the same research group's system abandoned the eye tracker entirely because of bulkiness and calibration problems - a rare disclosure of a technology's practical failure trajectory." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-049" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two behaviours measured simultaneously and by design: visual attention allocation between a central social stimulus and flanking nonsocial distractors, and emotion recognition accuracy and error structure. The distraction ratio - proportion of gaze frames on distractors rather than the face - is the primary behavioural measure, with a variant excluding frames coded 'nowhere in particular' to give a more direct face-versus-distractor comparison. Emotion responses are captured both as a 7 x 7 confusion matrix and as a per-face correct/incorrect sequence preserving performance over time." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-049" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Move acquisition into the home, with four named prerequisites: large-scale in-home data acquisition, augmented reality platforms with robust native eye tracking, game design for at-home analysis platforms, and clearly defined measurable experimental parameters. Extend feasibility testing to younger children. Balance cohorts for age and gender. And solve a human-computer interaction problem the authors state precisely - producing 'appropriate user interfaces and games that homogenize use in ways so as to lower the natural variability of measurement', since follow-up Superpower Glass studies showed heterogeneity in less-restricted collection. They also note that later generations of the system dropped the eye tracker entirely because of bulkiness and calibration issues, and expect native gaze tracking in commercial AR devices to resolve this." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-049" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: test the feasibility of wearable gaze tracking during an emotion recognition task and whether the resulting data distinguish autistic from neurotypical children. Main findings: autistic children showed a higher mean distraction ratio (0.0433 versus 0.0139) but not significantly at the participant level (P = .12); the full classifier reached 71.0%, exactly matching an age-and-gender-only baseline; gaze features alone reached 83.9% but did not survive Bonferroni correction; performance was best on trial 1 before feedback and worst on trial 2 during audio cueing; and the authors conclude the differences are too subtle and the data too noisy for reliable machine exploitation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-049" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Leave-one-participant-out cross-validation throughout, with Monte Carlo shuffling tests establishing significance against chance for every reported accuracy, hyperparameters grid-searched on training folds only, and Bonferroni correction applied when interpreting the grid. Most importantly, the design includes a null-model baseline using only age and gender, which is the element almost every other study in this corpus lacks and which is what converts a nominal 71% accuracy into an interpretable finding. What remains absent is an independent cohort or external validation, which the sample size precludes, and the acknowledged possibility that the mid-study chinrest change is confounded with group and could be exploited by the classifiers - a validity threat the authors identify but cannot remove." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-049" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "In-lab at Stanford University, with families recruited between 25 February 2015 and 26 January 2016 through the Autism and Developmental Disabilities Research Registry, referrals to the Autism and Developmental Disabilities Clinic, the Developmental Behavioral Unit of Lucile Packard Children's Hospital, and academic presentations. Funded by NIH grants 1R01EB025025-01 and 1R21HD091500-01 plus multiple foundation and Stanford programme grants. The senior author declares founding Cognoa, a company building digital child-health solutions." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-049" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups: 16 autistic and 17 neurotypical children, from 43 enrolled (23 autistic, 20 neurotypical). Exclusions are itemised with reasons: 4 controls received an early prototype version with visual rather than audio cues and could not read the cues or complete the task; 5 autistic participants received images in a different randomised order because of a stimulus ordering error; and 1 autistic participant had an unrelated health issue mid-session. No other-diagnosis comparison group. The cohort is predominantly high-functioning - only 3 of 16 autistic participants had an abbreviated IQ below 80." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-049" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Leave-one-participant-out accuracy paired with a Monte Carlo shuffling P value for every one of 60 grid cells (15 feature combinations by four trial conditions), plus cross-validation confusion matrices for the full model. Shuffling tests ran 2,500 iterations and were checked for convergence. Bonferroni correction is applied when interpreting significance across the grid, and the authors note which results survive it and which do not. Reporting a permutation-based significance test for every reported accuracy is unique in this corpus and is precisely what distinguishes an accuracy from a finding at this sample size. No sensitivity, specificity or AUC." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-049" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with autism', 'children with ASD', 'ASD participants', 'neurotypical controls (NCs)', 'individuals with autism'. Consistently person-first, and 'neurotypical' rather than 'normal', 'healthy' or 'typically developing' - the neurodiversity-aligned term. The framing acknowledges theoretical uncertainty rather than asserting deficits: 'There are several leading theories about facial processing in ASD, and the underlying biological mechanisms are not fully understood.' Autism is nonetheless introduced as 'one of the most important public health challenges we face today'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-049" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The most complete limitations section in the corpus, with five named items. Age range 6-17 'is not representative of children who are in critical periods of development for cognition and speech'. Gender imbalance of 13:3 in the autistic cohort against 9:8 in controls, with the authors noting that gender differences exist on emotion recognition and gaze tasks and 'may have been exacerbated by our imbalanced cohorts'. Limited spectrum coverage, since only 3 of 16 autistic participants had ABIQ below 80 so 13 are high-functioning. A chinrest added after the first four participants because of head movement, with the explicit admission that 'more ASD subjects took part in the study later on. This confound may be exploited by the classifiers'. And nonstandard eye tracking - the custom system was never evaluated against a standard tracker, the mount shifted requiring recalibration and extensive manual annotation, and it 'occluded roughly 15% of the field of view in the lower region for most participants, which may have had a greater distracting effect on participants in the ASD group with tactile sensitivities'. The discussion adds an ethological caution: static 6-second photographs are 'quite divergent from real-life social interactions with moving children and fleeting displays of facial emotion', and the field 'does not have enough information to know how divergent our experimental data might be from the ecologically valid situations'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-049" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Complete. SCQ, SRS-2 total and Stanford-Binet ABIQ for every participant in both groups, plus itemised comorbid psychological conditions and current medications for those who completed the survey. Uniquely in this corpus, the medication data are reported at drug level rather than as a summary count, which matters because several of the drugs listed - methylphenidate, guanfacine, aripiprazole - have documented effects on attention and eye movement." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-049" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; one 20-minute session per child with no follow-up, though the stated goal of continuous phenotyping is inherently longitudinal. Data include eye and world video of identifiable children with extensive manual annotation by independent labellers. Governance is reported: written informed consent from both parents and children under an approved Stanford IRB protocol. The conflict of interest is declared." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-049" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: autism spectrum disorder; translational medicine; eye tracking; wearable technologies; artificial intelligence; machine learning; precision health; digital therapy. The recurring frame is 'continuous social phenotyping' - measurement carried out repeatedly in natural settings rather than episodically in clinics - which positions the work as infrastructure for longitudinal characterisation rather than as a diagnostic test." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-049" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Official autism diagnosis confirmed via medical record, with parent-reported diagnosis corroborated by SCQ above 16, and syndromic autism excluded on the basis of genetic, metabolic or infectious aetiology in the record. The control group is screened more rigorously than anywhere else in the corpus, excluded on SCQ above 14, history of mood or personality disorder, a sibling diagnosed with autism or schizophrenia, history of seizures or neurological disorder, and vision impairment. Excluding on sibling diagnosis controls for the broader autism phenotype - a step no other study here takes. Stanford-Binet ABIQ was administered by a trained research assistant and SRS-2 completed by parents for every participant." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-049" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Elastic net as the primary classifier, chosen explicitly for the data regime: 219 features per trial against 33 participants means 'regularization was important to prevent model overfitting', and elastic net combines lasso and ridge penalties. Logistic regression with grid-searched l1 or l2 regularisation serves as an ablation comparator, included on the technical grounds that elastic net optimisers can be unstable at the edge cases where one penalty vanishes. Elastic net outperformed logistic regression in most tasks, which the authors read as confirming 'a need for regularization in this dataset'. No deep learning, correctly - the discussion notes that a larger, more balanced corpus 'would also enable the use of more complex statistical models, such as artificial neural networks', which is a statement of what the data cannot currently support." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-049" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "219 features per trial in four clearly separated blocks, which is what makes the ablation grid possible: a row-normalised 7 x 7 emotion confusion matrix flattened to 49 features; a per-face binary correctness sequence of 41 or 42 features preserving temporal structure; three gaze features per face (proportion of frames on the face rather than a distractor, proportion of fixation changes directed to the face, and frames elapsed before first looking at the face) giving 123 or 126; and two metadata features, age and gender. Including metadata as an explicit feature block rather than as covariates is the design decision that makes the paper's central finding visible." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-049" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Deliberately low-cost and open-hardware derived: Google Glass with a custom 3D-printed prism mount holding a Microsoft LifeCam HD-6000 webcam whose infrared filter was removed and whose indicator LEDs were replaced with infrared emitters, adapted from the open-source Pupil Project, at about US $35 for the add-on. Multiple mount variants were printed so the best eye view could be chosen per participant. Stimuli came from the CAFE dataset of child faces, balanced across five racial groups and by gender and emotion within each trial - the most carefully balanced stimulus set in the corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-049" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The fusion result is negative and instructive. Gaze features alone gave the best behavioural performance (83.9% on trial 1 and on all trials), emotion confusion matrices alone performed at or below chance in two of three trials (6.5% and 35.5%), and combining all feature blocks gave 71.0% - worse than gaze alone. Adding emotion features to gaze reduced accuracy in most cells of the ablation grid. This is a rare demonstration in the corpus that multimodal combination can degrade rather than improve performance when one modality carries little signal and the feature-to-participant ratio is extreme. The critical comparison, though, is not between modalities but against the metadata baseline: age and gender alone reached 71.0% with P = .04, the same accuracy as the full model with a marginally better P value, so no combination of behavioural features demonstrably beat two demographic variables." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-049" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "One 20-minute session per child comprising three successive trials of 41, 42 and 41 face images, with recalibration between trials" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional case-control feasibility and classification study with a systematic feature-ablation grid" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "School-age and adolescent children with autism, predominantly high-functioning" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 16 analysed (23 enrolled); comparison: 17 analysed (20 enrolled)" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "A US $35 eye tracker mounted on Google Glass could track gaze during an emotion recognition task, and autistic children were on average more distracted by nonsocial stimuli than neurotypical children. But the difference was not significant at the participant level (P = .12), and no classifier built on gaze and emotion features significantly outperformed one using only age and gender - both reached",
                        "The full model using all features achieved 71.0% across all trials, exactly matching the age-and-gender-only baseline of 71.0%, with the full model's shuffling P value of .05 no better than the baseline's .04. Gaze features alone reached 83.9% on trial 1 and on all trials (P = .00 and .002) and gaze-plus-emotion-confusion-details reached 80.6% and 83.9%, but these did not survive Bonferroni correc" ;
                    slr:outcome "Leave-one-out accuracy with a shuffling-test P value for every one of 60 cells in the ablation grid, plus cross-validation confusion matrices for the full model across all four trial conditions" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "The full model using all features achieved 71.0% across all trials, exactly matching the age-and-gender-only baseline of 71.0%, with the full model's shuffling P value of .05 no better than the baseline's .04. Gaze features alone reached 83.9% on trial 1 and on all trials (P = .00 and .002) and gaze-plus-emotion-confusion-details reached 80.6% and 83.9%, but these did not survive Bonferroni correc; A US $35 eye tracker mounted on Google Glass could track gaze during an emotion recognition task, and autistic children were on average more distracted by nonsocial stimuli than neurotypical children. But the difference was not significant at the participant level (P = .12), and no classifier built on gaze and emotion features significantly outperformed one using only age and gender - both reached" ] ] ;
