<https://doi.org/10.2196/27706> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Background: The early diagnosis of autism spectrum disorder (ASD) is highly desirable but remains a challenging task, which requires a set of cognitive tests and hours of clinical examinations. In addition, variations of such symptoms exist, which can make the identification of ASD even more difficult. Although diagnosis tests are largely developed by experts, they are still subject to human bias. In this respect, computer-assisted technologies can play a key role in supporting the screening process. Objective: This paper follows on the path of using eye tracking as an integrated part of screening assessment in ASD based on the characteristic elements of the eye gaze. This study adds to the mounting efforts in using eye tracking technology to support the process of ASD screening Methods: The proposed approach basically aims to integrate eye tracking with visualization and machine learning. A group of 59 school-aged participants took part in the study. The participants were invited to watch a set of age-appropriate photographs and videos related to social cognition. Initially, eye-tracking scanpaths were transformed into a visual representation as a set of images. Subsequently, a convolutional neural network was trained to perform the image classification task. Results: The experimental results demonstrated that the visual representation could simplify the diagnostic task and also attained high accuracy. Specifically, the convolutional neural network model could achieve a promising classification accuracy. This largely suggests that visualizations could successfully encode the information of gaze motion and its underlying dynamics. Further, we explored possible correlations between the autism severity and the dynamics of eye movement based on the maximal information coefficient. The findings primarily show that the combination of eye tracking, visualization, and machine learning have strong potential in developing an objective tool to assist in the screening of ASD. Conclusions: Broadly speaking, the approach we propose could be transferable to screening for other disorders, particularly neurodevelopmental disorders. (JMIR Hum Factors 2021;8(4):e27706) doi: 10.2196/27706" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Cilia F; Carette R; Elbattah M; Dequen G; Guérin JL; Bosche J; Vandromme L; Le Driant B (2021). Computer-Aided Screening of Autism Spectrum Disorder: Eye-Tracking Study Using Data Visualization and Deep Learning. *JMIR Human Factors, 8(4), e27706*. https://doi.org/10.2196/27706" ;
    dcterms:creator "Bosche J",
        "Carette R",
        "Cilia F",
        "Dequen G",
        "Elbattah M",
        "Guérin JL",
        "Le Driant B",
        "Vandromme L" ;
    dcterms:title "Computer-Aided Screening of Autism Spectrum Disorder: Eye-Tracking Study Using Data Visualization and Deep Learning" ;
    bibo:doi "10.2196/27706" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_4917150c" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2021 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the comparison group assessed rather than assumed?" ;
                            slr:justification "CARS administered to non-autistic children, returning the floor score of 15, plus consideration of parental concerns." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "7 years 7 months versus 8 years 0 months with near-identical spreads, and 19 males in each group." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Group sizes, sexes, chronological ages with SDs, CARS and ECSP scores, diagnostic route and the regional constraint on age composition all reported." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "59 children is small and the authors name it as the primary limitation; the school-age composition is explained by regional diagnostic delays." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the representation choice documented and justified?" ;
                            slr:justification "Three-step construction with mirroring correction, velocity bound and the empirically tested point threshold all reported." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Tracker and sampling rate, monitor, viewing distance, calibration and verification, room conditions, and the full stimulus battery with per-protocol durations and content." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "ADI-R and ADOS-G by health professionals in specialty clinics, with independent CARS and ECSP by the research team." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "No engineered features, but the 200-point drawing threshold was chosen because lower values 'poorly discriminate the 2 classes', which uses class information." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the effect of splitting unit quantified?" ;
                            slr:justification "Both regimes reported on the same model and data, showing roughly a 19-point difference." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is uncertainty quantified?" ;
                            slr:justification "AUC standard deviation across folds reported; no confidence intervals on accuracy." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is augmentation handled without crossing the split?" ;
                            slr:justification "Five synthetic samples per image, with no statement that augmented copies were confined to their source image's partition." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are metrics reported for both conditions?" ;
                            slr:justification "Accuracy, recall and precision for the image-level split; accuracy and AUC only for the participant-level split." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a participant-level validation reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Participants split first and images assigned by ID, giving approximately 71% accuracy." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is training adequate?" ;
                            slr:justification "Three epochs over three-fold cross-validation is very brief, with no early stopping or convergence criterion reported." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Libraries and layer counts named; no code and no full hyperparameter specification." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the severity correlation appropriately qualified?" ;
                            slr:justification "MIC computed per image across 547 observations from 59 children, on a sample whose entire comparison group sits at the CARS floor with zero variance." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are limitations stated candidly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Sample size, inaccessible ADI-R and ADOS-G scores, short scenarios, restricted age group and absence of a benchmark dataset all named." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Written parental consent, child assent, right to withdraw, Helsinki conformity, and an explicit account of why ethics committee authorisation was not required in France." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible?" ;
                            slr:justification "Stated to have been made freely available for other studies, though no repository link appears in the extracted text." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: autism spectrum disorder; screening; eye tracking; data visualization; machine learning; deep learning; AI; ASS; artificial intelligence; ML; screening; adolescent; diagnosis. 'Data visualization' is the distinguishing term - the contribution is a representation strategy rather than an architecture or a clinical finding." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-061" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No cross-modal fusion. What is fused is spatial and temporal information within a single representation: the scanpath image simultaneously carries where gaze travelled and how fast, with velocity encoded as greyscale gradient along each line. This is a genuinely different answer to the multimodality problem from the feature-concatenation approaches elsewhere in the corpus - the two information types are combined at the representation level before any learning occurs, and the MIC analysis is offered as evidence that the velocity channel carries real signal, since velocity correlates with CARS at 0.79 and is exactly what the colour encodes." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-061" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "CARS for severity and ECSP for social communication, both in French versions, administered to both groups by psychologists. CARS is used analytically in the correlation analysis rather than merely tabulated. ECSP is reported but not used, and its near-identical group means at what appears to be a ceiling limit its informativeness. No IQ, adaptive functioning or comorbidity measures, and ADI-R and ADOS-G scores were inaccessible." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-061" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups of 59 children: 29 autistic and 30 non-autistic, closely matched at 19 males each. The authors explain the age composition candidly rather than presenting it as a design choice - they wanted younger participants for early detection, 'but in our region there were not enough diagnosed children younger than 6 years, and the time it takes to consult a doctor to make a diagnosis can be as long as 2 years'. That is an unusually direct statement of how diagnostic waiting times constrain the very research meant to shorten them. No other-diagnosis comparison group." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-061" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "SMI RED250 remote eye tracker at 250 Hz - among the highest sampling rates in the corpus, and material here because the authors attribute conflicting literature findings on saccade dynamics partly to trackers running at 60 Hz and 24 Hz; a 17-inch monitor at 1280 x 1024 viewed from about 60 cm; SMI Experiment Center; five-point calibration followed by verification procedures. Analysis in Python with Matplotlib, OpenCV and Keras on a Tesla K80, with the Minerva R package for the MIC computation, and Azure ML hosting the deployed demonstration model." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-061" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A larger cohort spanning different ages with analysis of socio-cognitive and cognitive profiles alongside gaze; autism-specific data augmentation methods; longer video scenarios for richer gaze representation; adaptation of more recent deep network methodologies; and, more broadly, transfer of the approach to other neurodevelopmental disorders. The authors also identify the field-level need for a benchmark dataset, which their own open release partly addresses." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-061" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "JMIR Human Factors, 8(4), e27706 - a digital health and human factors venue, with all eight authors credited as contributing equally across psychology, computer science and anatomy research units at the University of Picardy Jules Verne." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-061" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: represent eye-tracking scanpaths as velocity-coloured images so that autism screening becomes an image classification problem. Main findings: approximately 90% accuracy with image-level splitting and approximately 71% with participant-level splitting; a maximal information coefficient of 0.79 between CARS score and average gaze velocity; and a working demonstration application linking eye tracking, visualisation and prediction through a hosted model." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-061" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The representation is the contribution. Scanpath images at 256 x 256 after cropping and rescaling, with three deliberate design decisions documented: a cap of 200 consecutive drawn points, selected empirically after finding that 100-150 'turned out to poorly discriminate the 2 classes' while higher values produced clutter; a velocity bound at a quarter of the screen diagonal on the grounds that faster movement 'would not be normal given the scenarios used'; and vertical mirroring to correct for the coordinate origin. Augmentation generated five synthetic variants per image. The heterogeneous stimulus battery is what yields an average of 9.27 images per child, which is simultaneously what makes the dataset large enough to train a CNN and what creates the leakage risk the paper then measures." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-061" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'individuals diagnosed with ASD', 'children with ASD', 'ASD-diagnosed individuals', 'non-ASD', 'children with typical development'. Consistently person-first with 'non-ASD' rather than 'normal' or 'healthy' for the comparison group. The framing acknowledges family impact - deficits 'can unfortunately place a considerable strain on their lives and their families' - but is otherwise neutral, and the discussion notes that parents find eye-tracking results easier to understand than ADI cut-offs and that 'a lack of an objective measure can lead to delayed or diminished acceptance of the clinical diagnosis', which frames objectivity as serving families rather than only clinicians." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-061" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Gaze trajectory and its dynamics across a heterogeneous battery spanning joint attention initiation, gaze following with head-plus-eye versus eye-only cues, static face-and-object viewing, and emotionally contingent versus non-contingent cartoon scenes. Rather than reducing these to fixation counts or dwell proportions, the study preserves the whole scanpath as a spatial object with velocity written into its colour - so the behavioural unit analysed is the shape of looking over time rather than any summary statistic of it." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-061" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "ROC curves with AUC and cross-fold standard deviation, plus accuracy, recall and precision, reported twice - once for each splitting regime - together with training and validation loss curves. Reporting the same metrics under both regimes is what makes the comparison legible and is the paper's key methodological contribution. Missing: confusion matrices, confidence intervals on the accuracy figures, and per-class breakdown for the participant-level condition, where only accuracy is quoted." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-061" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2021. The framing is conventional on diagnosis - hours of clinical examination, symptom variation, susceptibility to human bias - but two observations are distinctive. First, an unusually frank statement of how service capacity shapes research: the sample is school-aged rather than preschool because 'in our region there were not enough diagnosed children younger than 6 years, and the time it takes to consult a doctor to make a diagnosis can be as long as 2 years', so the very diagnostic delay the tool is meant to address prevented studying the age group where it would matter most. Second, an argument about acceptance rather than accuracy: 'parents seem to have high acceptance of eye tracking as part of the clinical evaluation because the visual results are easier to understand than is the ADI cutoff', and 'for some parents, a lack of an objective measure can lead to delayed or diminished acceptance of the clinical diagnosis'. That reframes the value of objective measurement as communicative as well as diagnostic, a consideration essentially absent elsewhere in this corpus. The paper also identifies the absence of a benchmark dataset as an obstacle to comparing methods, and responds by releasing its own." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-061" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The paper's most important element and the reason it matters to this review. Three-fold cross-validation is run twice on the same model and data under two splitting units. Pooling the 547 images regardless of origin gives approximately 90% accuracy. Splitting the 59 participants first and then assigning their images by ID - so that no child appears on both sides - gives approximately 71%. The authors describe the second explicitly as a further examination of model performance and note the decline as expected. With an average of 9.27 images per child, this constitutes a direct within-study quantification of segment-level inflation: roughly 19 accuracy points, on a design where images from one child are highly correlated. Residual issues: augmented samples presumably inherit their source image's participant, so the image-level figure may be further inflated; only accuracy is reported for the participant-level condition; three epochs is very few; and there is no independent cohort or external site." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-061" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Diagnosis confirmed by health professionals in multidisciplinary autism specialty clinics using ADI-R and ADOS-G - the field's gold standard, and among the better ascertainments in the corpus. The important qualification is stated openly: 'we did not get permission to read the children's medical files', so the ADI-R and ADOS-G scores were unavailable and unanalysed, and the authors list this among their limitations, noting that access 'could have provided further interpretation of the results'. Independent severity and communication measures were obtained directly by the research team using French CARS and ECSP for both groups. Non-autistic status rests on careful consideration of parental concerns plus a floor CARS score." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-061" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No saliency or attribution analysis over the CNN, and the visual representation is not decomposed to show which regions of a scanpath image drive classification - a missed opportunity given that the images are directly human-interpretable and are reproduced in the paper for one participant from each group. Interpretability is instead pursued externally through the MIC analysis, which establishes that the velocity information encoded in colour correlates strongly with CARS severity and thereby argues that the model has something real to learn from. The authors connect this to the wider literature on saccade dynamics in autism, while noting honestly that the direction of the effect 'does not appear to be settled and seems to depend largely on the equipment and data used'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-061" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Autistic group: 29 children, 19 male, chronological age 7 years 7 months (SD 2 years 6 months), CARS 32.9 +/- 6.4, ECSP total 141.1 +/- 50.3. Non-autistic group: 30 children, 19 male, chronological age 8 years 0 months (SD 2 years 8 months), CARS exactly 15 with zero variance, ECSP total 139.18 +/- 49.5. Ages and sex ratios are closely comparable though no test is reported. Two features of the clinical data deserve note: the non-autistic group's CARS of 15 with zero standard deviation is the instrument's floor, so the measure carries no within-group variance; and ECSP developmental ages of approximately 23-24 months in both groups reflect a ceiling effect of an instrument designed for much younger children, not a meaningful developmental estimate for eight-year-olds. No IQ, ethnicity or socioeconomic data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-061" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Acknowledged: the small participant count as the primary limitation; the inability to access ADI-R and ADOS-G scores, which 'limits the interpretation of our results'; the short duration of video scenarios, since 'perhaps longer scenarios might have allowed for a richer representation of the gaze behavior'; the restricted age group; the absence of a benchmark dataset in the autism literature making cross-study comparison difficult; and hardware and software costs as barriers to clinical adoption. The performance decline under participant-level splitting is reported as expected rather than hidden. Not acknowledged: the MIC of 0.79 is computed per image rather than per child, so 547 observations derive from 59 individuals; and because every non-autistic child scores the CARS floor of 15 with zero variance, that correlation largely reflects the between-group difference rather than a severity gradient within autism. Also unremarked: augmented copies of an image share its participant, which would compound leakage in the image-level condition, and three training epochs is very few." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-061" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Good. Reported: the full stimulus battery with per-protocol durations, counts and content; recording geometry, calibration and verification; the three-step visualisation procedure including the mirroring correction and the velocity bound at a quarter of the screen diagonal; the empirical selection of the 200-point drawing threshold with the tested alternatives and their outcomes; image cropping and rescaling to 256 x 256; the augmentation factor and resulting sample count; the CNN layer composition; the three-fold cross-validation under both splitting regimes with the participant-level procedure described step by step; training epochs and hardware; and the MIC method with its rationale and software. Missing: CNN hyperparameters beyond layer counts, filter sizes, the repository link for the released dataset, and ADI-R and ADOS-G scores which were inaccessible." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-061" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A composite battery assembled from several existing psychological protocols and described at unusual protocol-level detail: three 58-second joint attention initiation videos plus 18 matched 5-second photographs, opening with a hand-waving cartoon attention grabber before a woman greets the child and then looks, verbalises and points toward a target either present in or absent from the child's visual field; twelve 4-second gaze-following videos separating head-plus-eye shifts from eye-only shifts; static actor-plus-object photographs; and five minutes of cartoon scenes whose characters' emotional expressions are contingent or non-contingent on the preceding scene. Stimuli were counterbalanced and interleaved with a two-second central crosshair. The variety - dynamic versus static, human versus cartoon, human versus object - is deliberate, allowing 'analysis of the ocular activity of participants from different perspectives', and it is what produces multiple scanpath images per child." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-061" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; a single session of approximately 10 minutes per child with no follow-up. Data are gaze coordinates rendered as abstract scanpath images and released openly. Governance is fully reported and its regulatory basis explained: informed written consent from parents or guardians after explanation of objectives, tasks and the right to withdraw; assent from every child; parents permitted to remain in the room; Helsinki conformity; and an explicit statement that ethics committee authorisation was not required under French recommendations for psychological research. Explaining why approval was unnecessary, rather than simply omitting the matter, is better practice than the silence found in much of the corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-061" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A quiet room at the University of Picardy Jules Verne with white barriers to reduce visual distraction, in an interdisciplinary collaboration between psychology and artificial intelligence research units. Children were recruited regionally, with autistic participants diagnosed at multidisciplinary specialty clinics. Supported by a university research quality bonus; no conflicts declared." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-061" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A convolutional network with four convolutional layers, four pooling layers, two fully connected layers and dropout, trained for three epochs. The architectural choice follows directly from the representation decision: having converted gaze to images, the natural learner is a CNN, and the authors describe the whole approach as recasting diagnosis as 'a typical task of image classification'. No comparison against alternative classifiers or against engineered-feature baselines is reported, though the authors note this study builds on their own earlier work using fixation and saccade event features." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-061" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "One session per child of approximately 10 minutes, spanning multiple stimulus protocols" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional case-control classification study with an explicit comparison of image-level and participant-level validation, plus a correlational analysis against severity" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "School-aged children with autism" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 29; comparison: 30" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Image-level split: approximately 90% accuracy, 83% recall and 80% precision. Participant-level split: approximately 71% accuracy. The authors report the drop plainly - 'expectedly, the model performance declined' - and characterise 71% as 'still promising given the relatively small data set'. Separately, MIC between CARS score and average gaze velocity was 0.79",
                        "Rendering eye-tracking scanpaths as images in which line colour encodes gaze velocity, then classifying them with a convolutional network, achieved about 90% accuracy when images were split without regard to participant and about 71% when the split respected participant boundaries. Reporting both is the paper's most valuable contribution: the 19-point gap is a direct, within-study measurement of h" ;
                    slr:outcome "ROC curves with area under the curve and standard deviation across folds, plus accuracy, recall and precision, reported separately for the image-level and participant-level splits, and training and validation loss curves" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Image-level split: approximately 90% accuracy, 83% recall and 80% precision. Participant-level split: approximately 71% accuracy. The authors report the drop plainly - 'expectedly, the model performance declined' - and characterise 71% as 'still promising given the relatively small data set'. Separately, MIC between CARS score and average gaze velocity was 0.79; Rendering eye-tracking scanpaths as images in which line colour encodes gaze velocity, then classifying them with a convolutional network, achieved about 90% accuracy when images were split without regard to participant and about 71% when the split respected participant boundaries. Reporting both is the paper's most valuable contribution: the 19-point gap is a direct, within-study measurement of h" ] ] ;
