<https://doi.org/10.2196/29328> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Background: Previous studies have shown promising results in identifying individuals with autism spectrum disorder (ASD) by applying machine learning (ML) to eye-tracking data collected while participants viewed varying images (ie, pictures, videos, and web pages). Although gaze behavior is known to differ between face-to-face interaction and image-viewing tasks, no study has investigated whether eye-tracking data from face-to-face conversations can also accurately identify individuals with ASD. Objective: The objective of this study was to examine whether eye-tracking data from face-to-face conversations could classify children with ASD and typical development (TD). We further investigated whether combining features on visual fixation and length of conversation would achieve better classification performance. Methods: Eye tracking was performed on children with ASD and TD while they were engaged in face-to-face conversations (including 4 conversational sessions) with an interviewer. By implementing forward feature selection, four ML classifiers were used to determine the maximum classification accuracy and the corresponding features: support vector machine (SVM), linear discriminant analysis, decision tree, and random forest. Results: A maximum classification accuracy of 92.31% was achieved with the SVM classifier by combining features on both visual fixation and session length. The classification accuracy of combined features was higher than that obtained using visual fixation features (maximum classification accuracy 84.62%) or session length (maximum classification accuracy 84.62%) alone. Conclusions: Eye-tracking data from face-to-face conversations could accurately classify children with ASD and TD, suggesting that ASD might be objectively screened in everyday social interactions. However, these results will need to be validated with a larger sample of individuals with ASD (varying in severity and balanced sex ratio) using data collected from different modalities (eg, eye tracking, kinematic, electroencephalogram, and neuroimaging). In addition, individuals with other clinical conditions (eg, developmental delay and attention deficit hyperactivity disorder) should be included in similar ML studies for detecting ASD. (J Med Internet Res 2021;23(8):e29328) doi: 10.2196/29328" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Zhao Z; Tang H; Zhang X; Qu X; Hu X; Lu J (2021). Classification of Children With Autism and Typical Development Using Eye-Tracking Data From Face-to-Face Conversations: Machine Learning Model Development and Performance Evaluation. *Journal of Medical Internet Research, 23(8), e29328*. https://doi.org/10.2196/29328" ;
    dcterms:creator "Hu X",
        "Lu J",
        "Qu X",
        "Tang H",
        "Zhang X",
        "Zhao Z" ;
    dcterms:title "Classification of Children With Autism and Typical Development Using Eye-Tracking Data From Face-to-Face Conversations: Machine Learning Model Development and Performance Evaluation" ;
    bibo:doi "10.2196/29328" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_bfc088c7" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2021 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Recruitment sites, full inclusion and exclusion criteria, group sizes, ages, sexes and IQ all reported with test statistics." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the comparison group screened for the broader phenotype?" ;
                            slr:justification "Excluded for autism or ADHD diagnosis in first-degree relatives as well as personal disorder." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is sex representation adequate?" ;
                            slr:justification "Only four girls in total, named as a limitation with a specific rationale about sex differences in presentation." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "Age and sex matched and tested; IQ differs by 15.3 points (p = .02) with no adjustment." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "39 analysed children, restricted to ages 6-13 with at least average intelligence and minor severity, which the authors describe as proof-of-concept." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is interviewer consistency verified empirically?" ;
                            slr:justification "Interviewer movement quantified from video by image differencing and tested for a group difference." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Full question list, interviewer characteristics and blinding, seating distance, all devices with sampling rates, areas of interest, fixation definition with parameters, and the mapping and verification procedure." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the assessor blinded?" ;
                            slr:justification "The interviewer was blinded to group membership and required to behave consistently." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is measurement reactivity controlled?" ;
                            slr:justification "Participants were unaware the glasses recorded gaze, confirmed by post-experiment interview." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "Tiered psychiatric diagnosis with escalation on disagreement, and the ADOS/ADI-R gap disclosed." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a trivial baseline computed?" ;
                            slr:justification "Majority-class prediction of 51.3% calculated and compared explicitly." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are errors analysed rather than only counted?" ;
                            slr:justification "All three misclassified autistic children examined individually against the typically developing distribution on each selected feature." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the validation split at the participant level?" ;
                            slr:justification "Leave-one-out with a single participant held out per fold." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reported figure a pre-specified operating point?" ;
                            slr:justification "The maximum is quoted across 21 feature counts, four classifiers and three feature sets." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is uncertainty quantified?" ;
                            slr:justification "No confidence intervals; with 39 participants one misclassification is worth 2.6 accuracy points." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is feature selection nested within validation?" ;
                            slr:justification "Forward selection evaluates features by the same leave-one-out accuracy that is reported." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are single-modality baselines reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Fixation features alone and session length features alone each reach 84.62%, making the fusion gain readable." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Classifiers named; no code and no hyperparameters." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Helsinki principles, Shenzhen University ethical guidelines and written caregiver consent, but no committee approval number; the non-disclosure of gaze recording is not discussed as an ethical matter." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No release or availability statement." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a null result reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Eye-region fixation did not differ between groups in any of the four sessions, reported in full in the comparison table." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are limitations stated candidly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Severity, sex, age range, device intrusiveness, feature narrowness, absence of other clinical groups and uncontrollable interaction factors all named." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A clean and well-quantified fusion result, with both single-modality baselines reported: fixation features alone reach 84.62%, session length features alone reach 84.62%, and combined they reach 92.31% - roughly an 8-point gain. What makes it more informative than most fusion claims in the corpus is that the two feature families are conceptually distinct rather than two views of the same signal: one measures where the child looked, the other how long the interaction took. The best three-feature model draws on both, taking total session length plus two fixation features. The finding that session length alone reaches 79.49% is the most consequential single number in the paper, since it implies most of the discriminative information is available without an eye tracker at all." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-059" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Autistic group: 19 analysed, 17 male and 2 female, mean age 99.6 +/- 25.1 months, mean IQ 100.8 +/- 22.7. Typically developing group: 20 analysed, 17 male and 3 female, mean age 108.8 +/- 27.0 months, mean IQ 116.1 +/- 22.7. Age (p = .28) and sex ratio (p = .68) are well matched and formally tested. IQ is not - the groups differ by 15.3 points (p = .02) - and this is reported in the demographic table but not adjusted for anywhere in the modelling, which matters given that the single most informative feature is how long a child took to complete a conversation. Only four girls participated in total, which the authors name as a limitation with the specific rationale that autistic males and females differ in behavioural presentation, cognitive domains and emotions. No ethnicity or socioeconomic data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-059" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups: 20 autistic children recruited from the Child Psychiatry Department of Shenzhen Kangning Hospital and 23 typically developing children from local schools, with four excluded for eye-tracking technical problems leaving 19 and 20. No other-diagnosis comparison group, and the authors identify this as a substantive limitation rather than a scope note: 'it remains unclear whether eye-tracking data could effectively detect ASD from other clinical phenotypes (eg, developmental delay and ADHD)'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-059" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Journal of Medical Internet Research, 23(8), e29328 - a digital health venue, with authorship spanning a human factors and ergonomics institute, a district centre for disease control, and a hospital child psychiatry department." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-059" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: determine whether gaze during live face-to-face conversation classifies autism, and whether combining fixation features with conversation duration improves on either alone. Main findings: 92.31% accuracy with SVM using three combined features against 84.62% for either feature family alone; total conversation length alone achieved 79.49%; autistic children fixated less on mouth, face and body but not differently on the eyes; and the three misclassified autistic children fell within the typically developing range on all selected features." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-059" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Twenty-one features: sixteen fixation percentages (four areas of interest by four sessions) and five durations (four sessions plus total). The representation is compact, interpretable and clinically legible - every feature is a proportion of looking at a named body region during a named conversational activity, or a duration. Computing features per session rather than pooling across the conversation is what allows the temporal pattern to emerge, showing group differences concentrated in the earlier sessions. The authors note the representation's narrowness themselves, observing that fixation counts, entropy, revisits and oculomotor features were all available and unexploited." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-059" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Interpretability is served three ways. The forward selection ordering itself ranks features by contribution, showing that total session length alone reaches 79.49% and that only two fixation features are needed on top of it. The univariate table gives medians and effect statistics for every fixation feature, which is what reveals the eye-region null. And an error analysis examines the three misclassified autistic children individually, showing that on each of the three selected features they fell within the typically developing distribution - for instance, one had a total session length shorter than 75% of the typically developing group and mouth fixation higher than half of them. The authors read this as heterogeneity rather than model failure, which is the correct inference and is rarely drawn elsewhere in this corpus. The substantive interpretations offered - that the mouth matters because the task is conversational, and that longer conversations may reflect difficulty extracting social information - are plausible and appropriately hedged." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-059" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A tiered clinical procedure described in more detail than most of the corpus: DSM-IV diagnosis by a licensed psychiatrist with at least five years of experience, further evaluation by a senior psychiatrist, and escalation to at least two additional senior psychiatrists on disagreement. The unavailability of ADOS and ADI-R is disclosed explicitly as a resource constraint. Nonverbal IQ was screened by the psychiatrist and measured with Raven's Advanced Progressive Matrices, and ADHD, schizophrenia and other conditions were exclusions. The comparison group was screened for absence of mental or physical disorder and for absence of autism or ADHD in first-degree relatives - the latter controlling for the broader autism phenotype, a step few studies here take." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-059" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; one conversation per child with no follow-up. Data are first-person scene video from head-mounted glasses plus two external camera feeds of identifiable children in dyadic interaction. Governance is partially reported - Helsinki principles, Shenzhen University ethical guidelines, and written caregiver consent - but no ethics committee approval number is given. Participants were deliberately not told the glasses recorded gaze, with a post-experiment interview confirming none had realised; this is methodologically well motivated for behavioural naturalness but the deception is not discussed as an ethical consideration." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-059" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Tobii Pro Glasses 2 head-mounted eye tracker at 50 Hz with Tobii Pro Lab for dynamic-stimulus analysis, plus two video cameras at 25 and 30 Hz. Two practical details are unusual and worth recording: an accuracy test was performed at the end of any conversation in which the participant had moved the glasses, and the authors report verifying that the device 'was reliably accurate even if the glasses were moved by participants during the conversation'. They nonetheless flag head-mounted tracking as a limitation, noting that autistic individuals 'are more sensitive to wearing devices' and recommending contactless, calibration-free alternatives." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-059" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two behavioural channels from a single live interaction. Gaze allocation to a real conversational partner across four regions - eyes, mouth, whole face, whole body - and interaction duration, measured as the time taken to complete a fixed number of conversational exchanges. The second is not a gaze measure at all but an outcome of the interaction, and treating it as a feature alongside gaze is the design's most productive decision. The four-session structure adds a temporal dimension, since each feature is computed per session, allowing the analysis to show that group differences in mouth and face fixation are strong in the early sessions and attenuate later." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-059" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: autism spectrum disorder; eye tracking; face-to-face interaction; machine learning; visual fixation. 'Face-to-face interaction' is the distinguishing term and accurately identifies the contribution - the novelty is the recording context rather than the algorithm." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-059" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with ASD', 'individuals with ASD', 'children with typical development (TD)', 'healthy children'. Consistently person-first with 'typical development' as the comparison term, though 'healthy children' appears in the inclusion criteria. The framing locates the diagnostic problem in procedure rather than person: current diagnosis 'involves substantive subjective procedures that can be negatively impacted by various factors such as caregivers' reporting bias and clinicians' insufficient capability in differentiating ASD', and is 'highly labor- and time-demanding due to the shortage in clinical specialists'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-059" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Nonverbal IQ via Raven's Advanced Progressive Matrices for both groups, with at least average intelligence required for inclusion in the autistic group. No autism severity measure of any kind, which the authors state candidly: severity 'was not specifically measured in our study' and the sample 'could be viewed as representing individuals with minor severity'. No language, adaptive functioning or comorbidity scores. The IQ data are reported but, unusually, the significant group difference they reveal is not carried into the analysis." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-059" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Four classical classifiers - SVM, linear discriminant analysis, decision tree and random forest - each wrapped in forward feature selection, with the paper giving a short rationale for each including the observation that decision trees offer 'strong interpretability, but it is prone to overfitting'. The model comparison is informative in one respect: random forest needed sixteen features to reach 84.62% while SVM reached 92.31% with three, which speaks to how concentrated the signal is. The wrapper design is also what creates the study's main methodological weakness, since the selection criterion is the reported metric." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-059" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2021. The framing is a specific critique of ecological validity in the existing eye-tracking literature. The paper observes that prior data 'were primarily obtained by having participants watch images (ie, videos, pictures, web pages)' but that 'human gaze behavior is highly context-sensitive', that experimental setting and cognitive load influence visual attention, that face-to-face interaction is 'much more perceptually and cognitively difficult' than image watching, and that 'the presence of the social partner elicits a different pattern of both neural response and gaze behavior' - concluding that image-viewing findings 'could not be directly generalized to the scenario of natural social interaction'. It also situates itself on age, noting a meta-analytic mean diagnosis age of 60.48 months and arguing that future work should examine younger children than its own 6-13 range. And it is unusually explicit that the field has not yet addressed differential diagnosis: 'our study and most others only classified individuals with ASD and TD... More scientific endeavor is certainly required before a practical ML model that could detect ASD from different conditions is established.'" ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-059" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Strong. Reported: full question list for all four sessions; interviewer age, sex, blinding and consistency requirement; seating distance; both camera models with sampling rates; eye tracker model and rate; the four areas of interest; the real-world mapping procedure with human verification and manual correction; the velocity-threshold fixation definition with its 30 deg/s and 60 ms parameters; the fixation-percentage computation; all 21 features; the forward selection procedure described step by step; the leave-one-out scheme; and a table giving accuracy, sensitivity and specificity at every feature count from 1 to 21. Missing: ethics approval number, data or code release, and classifier hyperparameters. The interviewer-consistency check by image differencing is itself reported with its test statistic." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-059" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Comprehensive. Accuracy, sensitivity, specificity and AUC for every classifier under every feature set; a confusion matrix for the best model; an accuracy-versus-feature-count curve for all four classifiers; a table showing the incremental contribution of each successively added feature; and full Mann-Whitney comparisons for all 16 fixation features and all session lengths. Crucially, a majority-class baseline of 51.3% is computed and explicitly compared, which is the check almost every other study in the corpus omits. The one gap is that no confidence intervals accompany any figure, and with 39 participants a single misclassification is worth 2.6 accuracy points." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-059" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A laboratory dyadic setup at Shenzhen University with participants seated 80 cm from the interviewer, autistic children recruited from Shenzhen Kangning Hospital's Child Psychiatry Department and comparison children from local schools. Funded by seven Shenzhen and Guangdong municipal, medical-discipline and provincial research programmes. No conflicts declared." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-059" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Acknowledged extensively and specifically: the sample excludes severe presentations and children below 6 or above 13, so the work is 'proof-of-concept research'; only four girls participated, and autistic males and females differ across behavioural, cognitive and emotional domains; severity was not measured; head-mounted eye tracking may itself affect autistic children's behaviour and contactless methods are needed; only fixation percentage was computed while counts, entropy, revisits and oculomotor measures were available; only autism versus typical development was tested, leaving discrimination from other clinical phenotypes unknown; and live interaction introduces uncontrollable subjective factors, with the honest observation that 'no human being can be expected to behave exactly the same way when interacting with different people'. Not acknowledged: forward feature selection maximises the same leave-one-out accuracy that is reported, so the headline is not an unbiased estimate, and the maximum is quoted across 21 feature counts, four classifiers and three feature sets; and IQ differs significantly between groups with no adjustment, despite the leading feature being conversation duration." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-059" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A structured four-session conversation with a single blinded interviewer, each session designed to probe a different aspect of interaction: general questions for familiarisation, hobby sharing to test whether a self-selected topic changes gaze, yes-no questions to elicit head nodding or shaking, and question raising to examine initiative. The full question list is published, which makes the protocol reproducible in a way that most naturalistic-interaction studies are not. The design's key property is that the number of exchanges is fixed while the time taken is free, which is exactly what makes session length an interpretable behavioural measure rather than an artefact - and it is that measure which proved most discriminative." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-059" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Validate on a larger autistic sample varying in severity with balanced sex ratio; include other clinical conditions such as developmental delay and ADHD; recruit younger children, citing a meta-analytic mean diagnosis age of 60.48 months overall and 43.18 months among children aged 10 or under; develop contactless calibration-free gaze recording, citing recent webcam-plus-computer-vision work whose accuracy was comparable to manual coding; generate richer gaze features including fixation counts, entropy, revisits and oculomotor performance; and combine eye tracking with kinematic, EEG and neuroimaging data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-059" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Leave-one-out cross-validation at the participant level, explicitly motivated as overfitting protection. The defect is that forward feature selection evaluates candidate features by their classification accuracy, and that accuracy is the leave-one-out estimate itself, so features were chosen to maximise the number subsequently reported. Compounding this, results are reported for every feature count from 1 to 21 across four classifiers and three feature sets - 252 cells - and the maximum is quoted as the headline. There is no outer validation loop, no held-out cohort and no repetition. The saving grace is that the paper prints the entire selection trajectory, so a reader can see that accuracy plateaus at 92.31% from three features through eleven and that the single-feature result is 79.49%, which makes the shape of the result assessable even though the peak is optimistic." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-059" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "One conversation per child, comprising four chronologically arranged sessions of variable duration" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional case-control classification study using live dyadic interaction rather than screen-based stimuli" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "School-age children with autism of at least average nonverbal intelligence" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 20 enrolled, 19 analysed; comparison: 23 enrolled, 20 analysed" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Combined features: SVM best at 92.31% accuracy with three features (specificity 100%, sensitivity 84.21%, AUC 0.92), then LDA 89.74% with four features (AUC 0.92), decision tree 84.62% with two features (AUC 0.86) and random forest 84.62% with sixteen features (AUC 0.86). Visual fixation features alone: maximum 84.62% by LDA with three features and by decision tree with two (both AUC 0.86). Sessio",
                        "Gaze recorded during a live structured conversation classified autistic from typically developing children at 92.31% accuracy, higher than fixation features alone or conversation duration alone (both 84.62%). The most striking finding is which feature carried most of the signal: total conversation length alone achieved 79.49%, meaning that how long a child took to answer a fixed set of questions w" ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy, sensitivity, specificity and AUC for every classifier and feature set, a confusion matrix for the best model, accuracy as a function of feature count for all four classifiers, and a full table showing the incremental effect of each added feature. A majority-class baseline is also computed " ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Combined features: SVM best at 92.31% accuracy with three features (specificity 100%, sensitivity 84.21%, AUC 0.92), then LDA 89.74% with four features (AUC 0.92), decision tree 84.62% with two features (AUC 0.86) and random forest 84.62% with sixteen features (AUC 0.86). Visual fixation features alone: maximum 84.62% by LDA with three features and by decision tree with two (both AUC 0.86). Sessio; Gaze recorded during a live structured conversation classified autistic from typically developing children at 92.31% accuracy, higher than fixation features alone or conversation duration alone (both 84.62%). The most striking finding is which feature carried most of the signal: total conversation length alone achieved 79.49%, meaning that how long a child took to answer a fixed set of questions w" ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "51b52bd3f600b960b04af7b66f26b20b30c754300569d06f22f94b69d5d2acd0" ;
