<https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2025.1569464> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "TYPE Original Research PUBLISHED 21 May 2025 DOI 10.3389/fmed.2025.1569464 OPEN ACCESS EDITED BY SeongKi Kim, Chosun University, Republic of Korea REVIEWED BY Surbhi Bhatia Khan, University of Salford, United Kingdom Osman Ali Sadek Ibrahim, Minia University, Egypt Abhishek Singhal, Amity University, India *CORRESPONDENCE Theyazn H. H. Aldhyani taldhyani@kfu.edu.sa Sultan Ahmad s.alisher@psau.edu.sa RECEIVED 31 January 2025 ACCEPTED 16 April 2025 PUBLISHED 21 May 2025 CITATION Al-Nefaie AH, Aldhyani THH, Ahmad S and Alzahrani EM (2025) Application of artiﬁcial intelligence in modern healthcare for diagnosis of autism spectrum disorder. Front. Med. 12:1569464. doi: 10.3389/fmed.2025.1569464 COPYRIGHT © 2025 Al-Nefaie, Aldhyani, Ahmad and Alzahrani. This is an open-access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (CC BY). The use, distribution or reproduction in other forums is permitted, provided the original author(s) and the copyright owner(s) are credited and that the original publication in this journal is cited, in accordance with accepted academic practice. No use, distribution or reproduction is permitted which does not comply with these terms. Application of artiﬁcial intelligence in modern healthcare for diagnosis of autism spectrum disorder Abdullah H. Al-Nefaie1,2, Theyazn H. H. Aldhyani1,3*, Sultan Ahmad4* and Eidah M. Alzahrani5 1King Salman Center for Disability Research, Riyadh, Saudi Arabia, 2Department of Quantitative Methods, School of Business, King Faisal University, Hofuf, Saudi Arabia, 3Applied College in Abqaiq, King Faisal University, Hofuf, Saudi Arabia, 4Department of Computer Science, College of Computer Engineering and Sciences, Prince Sattam Bin Abdulaziz University, Al-Kharj, Saudi Arabia, 5Computer Science Department, Al-Baha University, Al-Bahah, Saudi Arabia" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Al-Nefaie AH; Aldhyani THH; Ahmad S; Alzahrani EM (2025). Application of artificial intelligence in modern healthcare for diagnosis of autism spectrum disorder. *Frontiers in Medicine, 12, Article 156*. https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2025.1569464" ;
    dcterms:creator "Ahmad S",
        "Al-Nefaie AH",
        "Aldhyani THH",
        "Alzahrani EM" ;
    dcterms:title "Application of artificial intelligence in modern healthcare for diagnosis of autism spectrum disorder" ;
    bibo:doi "10.3389/fmed.2025.1569464" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_0f5a351e" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2025 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participant selection and spectrum representativeness" ;
                            slr:justification "A public Kaggle image assembly with no ascertainment process, no spectrum description and no clinical setting; the number of distinct children behind 2,940 images is unreported." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Handling of missing data and attrition" ;
                            slr:justification "Class counts and split proportions are reported, but the evaluation partition's size and balance contradict them, and no image-quality exclusion criteria are described." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Confounder control" ;
                            slr:justification "No sex, ancestry or age distribution for either class, across a stated 2-to-14 age range in which facial appearance changes profoundly, and with a physiognomic premise resting on measurements that vary systematically with all three." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Comparison group adequacy" ;
                            slr:justification "Images 'randomly retrieved from web searches', so the comparison class differs from the index class in collection route rather than necessarily in any property of the children." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome measurement blinding" ;
                            slr:justification "Classification is fully automated with no human rater at inference." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Measurement standardisation" ;
                            slr:justification "Uncontrolled internet photographs with unknown and potentially class-correlated acquisition conditions; preprocessing standardises geometry and pixel range only." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reference standard quality" ;
                            slr:justification "No diagnostic instrument, clinician judgement or record for any image; the label is folder membership." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Index test definition and prespecification" ;
                            slr:justification "Three architectures compared on the same data with the reported figures drawn from the model-selection partition; no prespecified operating point; hyperparameters unspecified beyond the epoch cap." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance estimation and chance level" ;
                            slr:justification "No held-out test result despite a test partition being defined; no chance baseline against a 54.9% majority class; no confidence intervals; no prevalence-adjusted predictive value." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Preprocessing and feature selection nesting" ;
                            slr:justification "Augmentation is described as expanding the training set, which is correct, but the exactly balanced evaluation counts leave open whether it reached the evaluation partition; and all reported figures come from the partition used for model selection." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Sample size adequacy for the modelling" ;
                            slr:justification "Three deep architectures on 2,940 images from an unknown and possibly much smaller number of children." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data splitting and leakage control" ;
                            slr:justification "Image-level splitting with the number of distinct children unknown, in a corpus where one child may appear in several images." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Ethics, consent and conflicts" ;
                            slr:justification "No ethics or consent discussion appears in the main text for training data consisting of identifiable photographs of children collected from the internet; the declarations section was not present in the retrieved text and so cannot be assessed." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Completeness of methods reporting" ;
                            slr:justification "Corpus counts, augmentation parameters, split proportions, image dimensions, architectures, loss, optimiser and epoch cap are reported with complete per-class metrics and confusion matrices. Missing: participant counts, demographics, splitting unit, and code." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reporting of results and uncertainty" ;
                            slr:justification "Validation-partition figures presented as the study's results; support counts inconsistent with the stated split and class ratio; no intervals or baselines; and the abstract, introduction and results tables disagree about which model performed best." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Acknowledgement of limitations" ;
                            slr:justification "One honest sentence concedes that the research gap persists despite 98% and that clinical validation is necessary. Nothing else: the provenance confound, the absent reference standard, the missing demographics, the validation-only reporting and the non-existent feature analysis all go unmentioned." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data, code and protocol availability" ;
                            slr:justification "The corpus is public and identified; no code is reported, and the retrieved text ends before the journal's declarations so availability statements could not be assessed." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No study setting exists. The corpus is a third-party Kaggle assembly, and its two classes were built by different procedures - an autism-labelled collection on one side, general web search on the other. As with the other studies on this corpus charted in this review, the collection route is the confound rather than a background detail, because it is the only documented respect in which the two image populations are known to differ." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-131" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "RGB face images at 224x224x3 with pixel values rescaled to [0, 1], augmented by 10% shear, 10% zoom and horizontal flip. No landmark geometry and no morphometric derivation, so the craniofacial constructs invoked as motivation are not represented at all, and every source-dependent property of the image is retained." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-131" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: transfer learning, deep learning, diagnosis, disability, mental health. Entirely method- and domain-facing with no term naming autism, the population, the data source or the behaviour - so the paper is hard to locate from a clinically framed search, which is characteristic of this branch. Terms carried through the body: facial phenotype analysis, morphological characteristics, biomarkers, ImageNet, augmentation, confusion matrix, screening." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-131" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Clinical validation is named as necessary for adoption, which is the right requirement. No other direction appears in the retrieved text. Needed and not proposed: reporting of the demographic composition of each class, evaluation on a cohort with documented diagnoses, and any test of whether the classifier's decisions track facial phenotype rather than the collection route of the image." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-131" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. The autism label means an image was placed in the autistic subfolder of a Kaggle collection; the non-autistic label means an image was returned by a web search. No child in either class is known to have been assessed, no diagnostic instrument or clinician judgement is cited, and the paper does not identify the absence as a limitation - it reports the folder structure factually and moves on. This is the sixth study in the review resting on this corpus, and the position is unchanged: whatever separation the models achieve, it is a separation between two collections of internet images, and the paper supplies the description that makes this plain." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-131" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No task, elicitation or standardisation exists. The children were photographed by whoever took the pictures, for their own purposes, under uncontrolled conditions. This matters for the paper's premise in a specific way: the motivating literature concerns craniofacial morphometry - measurements that require controlled pose, distance and camera geometry to be meaningful - and none of those is available in web-sourced photographs, so even if the morphometric claims were correct the data could not test them." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-131" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Per-class precision, recall and F1 with support counts, overall accuracy, weighted averages, confusion matrices for all three models, training and validation curves, an ROC figure for Inception-V3 and a comparison table against ten prior studies on the same corpus. That is a fuller presentation than several papers in this branch. Three defects undercut it. Every table is headed 'Validation results' and the confusion matrices are described as validation-stage, so no held-out test figure is reported despite a test partition being defined. The support counts of 294 per class - 588 in total - are 20% of the corpus rather than the stated 10% validation share, and are exactly balanced although the corpus is 1,327 against 1,613, so the evaluation partition's composition cannot be reconstructed and it is unclear whether augmentation reached it. And no chance baseline, confidence interval or prevalence-adjusted predictive value appears, so a 98% figure has no reference point - at the corpus ratio a trivial majority classifier scores 54.9%, and at a realistic population prevalence the reported precision would not survive." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-131" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three ImageNet-pretrained architectures - ResNet50, Inception-V3 and VGG-19 - fine-tuned with categorical cross-entropy and SGD for up to 25 epochs. Transfer learning from ImageNet is a reasonable default for a 2,940-image corpus, and comparing three families is more informative than reporting one. Two problems attach. The training curves show Inception-V3 reaching 0.9992 training accuracy against 0.9779 validation, and VGG-19 exceeding 0.9854 in training before 'a significant decline in accuracy from epochs 24-25', both of which are signs of fitting to the training set that the paper reports without interpreting. And the introduction's statement that 'VGG16' was among the architectures compared, and that VGG19 achieved the best 98%, contradicts both the abstract and the results tables - an inconsistency about the study's own central claim." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-131" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The stated aim is to evaluate deep transfer-learning architectures for distinguishing autistic from non-autistic children from facial images, motivated by the cost and delay of conventional assessment and by the premise that autism has a distinct facial phenotype. Results: Inception-V3 98% accuracy, VGG-19 97%, ResNet50 96%, each with per-class precision and recall of 96-98% and confusion matrices on 588 evaluation samples. Two internal inconsistencies bear on how these should be read. The abstract names ResNet50, Inception-V3 and VGG-19 with Inception-V3 best; the introduction names 'ResNet, VGG16, and VGG19' and states that VGG19 achieved the superior 98%, contradicting the paper's own results tables. And all three result tables are headed 'Validation results', with the confusion matrices described as validation-stage - so the headline figures come from the partition used for model selection, while the 10% test partition defined in the methods is never reported on. The paper's own comparison table places this subliterature at 80.05% to 96% across ten prior studies on the same corpus, so a 98% validation figure sits above every published test figure for the same data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-131" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No limitations section appears in the retrieved text, but the introduction contains one honest sentence: 'The research gap in ASD identification using images persists, despite the proposed system achieving 98% accuracy on a benchmark dataset ... Ultimately, clinical validation is necessary to ensure the widespread adoption of this approach in healthcare settings and its practical applicability.' Conceding that a 98% benchmark result leaves the research gap open is unusual in this branch and should be credited. What is not stated: that the non-autistic class was assembled by web search and the autistic class from an autism collection, so the label encodes provenance; that no diagnostic ascertainment exists for any image; that no demographic information is available in a study classifying faces; that the split is at image level with the number of children unknown; that all reported figures come from the validation partition rather than the defined test partition; that the reported support counts are inconsistent with both the stated split proportions and the corpus class ratio; that no chance baseline is given; that the promised feature-importance analysis was not performed; and that the abstract, introduction and results tables disagree about which models were compared and which won." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-131" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The weakest element, and it fails in three independent ways. First, the split is at image level - 80/10/10 with no mention of participant-level partitioning and no statement of how many distinct children the 2,940 images represent - so in a web-assembled corpus where one child may appear repeatedly, images of the same child can fall on both sides of the boundary. This is the standard failure of this branch and the review has documented it in five prior studies on the same corpus family. Second, and more unusual, every reported figure comes from the validation partition: all three result tables are headed 'Validation results', the confusion matrices are described as validation-stage, and the training curves report validation accuracy as the endpoint, while the 10% test partition defined in the methods section is never reported on anywhere. Model selection and performance estimation therefore share data, and the headline 98% is a selection maximum rather than a held-out estimate. Third, the evaluation partition cannot be reconciled with the described design: 294 samples per class totalling 588 is 20% of 2,940 rather than the stated 10%, and exact class balance is impossible under a random split of a 1,327-to-1,613 corpus - so either the evaluation set was stratified to equal size, or augmentation reached it, or the numbers are misreported, and the paper does not say which. Against all of this sits the confound that no splitting scheme could address: the paper states that the non-autistic images were 'randomly retrieved from web searches' while the autistic images came from an autism collection, so a model that has learned to recognise the photographic character of one source will succeed on perfectly disjoint subjects. The paper's own comparison table, listing ten prior studies on this corpus at 80.05% to 96%, is the most useful thing in it for the review: it shows a subliterature clustered below this study's figure, and the difference between them is a matter of evaluation protocol rather than of signal." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-131" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Nominally craniofacial morphology; operationally, whatever separates an autism-labelled image folder from a folder of web-search results. The motivating claims are specific and measurable - broader upper faces, wider eyes, shorter nasal bridges, narrower cheeks - but nothing in the pipeline measures any of them: no landmarks are detected, no distances or ratios computed, and the models receive whole RGB images. What the networks can key on is unconstrained and includes apparent age, sex, ancestry, expression, pose, resolution, compression, lighting and background, all of which are free to differ between two folders built by different routes. The paper's third stated contribution was to analyse which facial features drive detection, which would have been the direct test of its own premise; that analysis does not appear." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-131" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Published May 2025 by groups at the King Salman Center for Disability Research in Riyadh together with King Faisal, Prince Sattam Bin Abdulaziz and Al-Baha Universities. The framing is the standard access argument - conventional diagnosis is time-intensive and costly, creating barriers 'particularly in resource-limited settings' - combined with a physiognomic premise about distinct facial morphology in autism. For this review the paper is the sixth built on the Kaggle facial corpus and adds three things to the record. It states the provenance confound more plainly than any of the others, in its own methods: the autistic folder holds images of autism while 'the non-autistic subfolder had images randomly retrieved from web searches'. It supplies, in its Table 1, the most complete catalogue of the subliterature in the corpus - ten prior studies on the same images reporting 80.05%, 84.66%, 86%, 87.9%, 89%, 89.87%, 90.5%, 90.67%, 91.3%, 92%, 93.24% and 96% - which makes visible both the scale of the effort invested in this dataset and the fact that its reported accuracies cluster below the figure claimed here. And it contains, in its introduction, the only passage in this branch that names the harms of autism identification as such: labelling that 'may impact the child's self-esteem and social relationships', and 'a risk of overdiagnosis or misdiagnosis, leading to unnecessary interventions and therapies'. That the paper then proposes artificial intelligence as the mitigation for those very risks, without explaining how a classifier trained on web-scraped images with no diagnostic ascertainment would reduce mislabelling, is the clearest illustration in the review of how the access argument absorbs its own counterarguments. Three cautions travel with the study: all reported figures are validation-partition results while the defined test partition is never reported on; the evaluation support counts are irreconcilable with both the stated split and the corpus class ratio; and the abstract, introduction and results tables disagree about which architectures were compared and which performed best." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-131" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Frontiers in Medicine 12:1569464, Original Research, open access under CC BY; received 31 January 2025, accepted 16 April 2025, published 21 May 2025, with editor and three reviewers disclosed by name. The venue is a general medical journal rather than an engineering one, which makes the pattern of what passed review notable: a physiognomic premise asserted without measurement, a corpus whose comparison class is described in the paper's own methods as web-search results, no demographic information of any kind, results reported from a validation partition while a defined test partition goes unreported, support counts inconsistent with the stated split, a claimed feature-importance analysis that does not exist, and an introduction that contradicts the abstract and the results tables about which models were compared. The retrieved text ends mid-discussion at Table 8, so the declarations could not be assessed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-131" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Adequate on the computational side and empty on the human side. Reported: the corpus with class counts and stated age range, the four augmentation parameters with their values, the 80/10/10 split, the image dimensions, the three architectures, the loss, optimiser and epoch cap, complete per-class precision, recall, F1 and support, confusion matrices, and training and validation curves with specific accuracy and loss trajectories. Not reported: the number of distinct children, any demographic composition, whether the split respected individuals, the composition of the evaluation partition in a form reconcilable with the stated split, hyperparameters beyond the epoch cap, and code. The stated claim of a facial-feature importance analysis has no corresponding section." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-131" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional secondary analysis with no longitudinal element. The retrieved text terminates within the discussion section before the declarations, so ethics, funding, data-availability and competing-interest statements could not be assessed from what was available. What can be said is that the paper contains no discussion anywhere in its main text of the ethics of the underlying material - identifiable photographs of children aged 2 to 14, collected from the internet without their families' knowledge or consent, one class specifically from autism-related sources. The introduction does raise the ethical hazards of autism identification in general terms, naming labelling, effects on self-esteem and social relationships, and overdiagnosis, which is more than the rest of this branch offers; it does not extend that concern to the provenance of its own training data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-131" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No severity, cognitive, adaptive or language measure exists for any image, and none is possible from a web-assembled corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-131" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "There are no participants. The units are 2,940 RGB images from the Kaggle autistic-children facial dataset, split 1,327 autistic and 1,613 non-autistic, described as children aged 2 to 14, organised into two folders. The number of distinct children behind either count is never stated. The decisive fact is the paper's own description of how the two folders were built: 'The autistic subfolder included images of ASD, while the non-autistic subfolder had images randomly retrieved from web searches.' The class label therefore records the collection route rather than any assessment of the child depicted - the same provenance confound this review has recorded for five other studies on this corpus family, stated here more plainly than in any of them. No age distribution, sex, ancestry, cognitive, language or clinical information is available for either class, and no demographic comparison between them is possible." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-131" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Absent apart from a stated age range of 2 to 14, and consequential for a facial classifier. No sex distribution, ancestry, socioeconomic, cognitive or clinical information exists for either class, and none could, given how the corpus was assembled. Three risks follow directly. Facial appearance changes profoundly between ages 2 and 14, so an age difference between a class drawn from autism-related material and a class drawn from general web search would be sufficient to produce the reported separation. Autism is diagnosed roughly four times more often in boys and facial classifiers key strongly on sex-linked morphology, so an unmeasured sex imbalance is an untestable alternative explanation. And ancestry composition is likely to differ between an autism-focused collection and a general web search, with no means of checking. The paper's motivating claims are morphometric - upper-face width, eye width, nasal bridge length, cheek narrowness - every one of which varies systematically with age, sex and ancestry, so the demographic gap is not incidental to the hypothesis but fatal to testing it." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-131" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Claimed and absent. The third listed contribution is 'Analysis of the specific facial features that contribute most significantly to accurate ASD detection', and the paper contains no such analysis - no saliency map, no attribution, no landmark or region analysis, no feature ranking. This is the one omission that most directly affects the paper's own argument: having asserted specific morphometric differences as the biological basis for the approach, the study had both the motive and the standard tools to test whether its models respond to those regions, and did neither. In their absence, the 98% is compatible with a facial phenotype and equally compatible with the model having learned the photographic character of images that appear in autism-related online collections - and this review has recorded four other studies on this corpus where saliency evidence, where offered, could not distinguish the two either." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-131" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Predominantly medical and physiognomic, with one passage of genuine ethical awareness that the paper then argues past. Autism is 'a mental condition characterized by challenges in communication, restricted behaviors, difficulties with speech, non-verbal interaction, and distinctive facial features in children', and is elsewhere called 'this disease'. The physiognomic framing is explicit and forms the study's premise: autistic people 'often present distinct facial morphological characteristics' including 'broader upper faces, wider eyes, shorter nasal bridges, and narrower cheeks', described as 'increasingly recognized as potential biomarkers'. Against this, the introduction contains a paragraph absent from every other paper in this branch, acknowledging the harms of early identification itself: 'A significant concern is the potential for labeling, which may impact the child's self-esteem and social relationships. There is a risk of overdiagnosis or misdiagnosis, leading to unnecessary interventions and therapies.' Naming labelling, self-esteem and overdiagnosis as risks of the enterprise the paper is contributing to is a real acknowledgement. What follows undoes it: the authors propose that these dangers may be mitigated by using artificial intelligence, without explaining how a facial classifier trained on web-scraped images with no diagnostic ascertainment would reduce the risk of mislabelling a child. No autistic person or organisation is reported as involved." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-131" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. A single image modality throughout, with no fusion of any kind." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-131" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No acquisition equipment. Analysis used three ImageNet-pretrained networks on 224x224x3 images with categorical cross-entropy, the SGD optimiser and a 25-epoch cap. Because the images come from unknown cameras under unknown conditions, every acquisition variable is free to vary and, critically, may vary systematically with class given that the two folders were assembled by different routes." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-131" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:study_design "Retrospective algorithm-development study comparing three pretrained convolutional architectures on a public image corpus" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: ; comparison: 1,613 images retrieved by web search" ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "c09e86f872354e7f7863c310c14939c121e9300d5760570674c3e33126fb779a" ;
