    slr:journal "Frontiers in Medicine, 12, Article 156" ;
    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "None exists. The autism label reflects folder membership in a public collection and the comparison label reflects a web search result. No diagnostic assessment, instrument or record is cited for any image." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Two image folders assembled by different routes - an autism-labelled collection against images 'randomly retrieved from web searches'. The number of distinct children is unknown, and no age distribution, sex, ancestry or clinical information exists for either class." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "An 80/10/10 image-level split with no participant-level partitioning and the number of children unstated; all reported figures come from the validation partition while the defined test partition is never reported; and the evaluation support counts of 294 per class are irreconcilable with both the stated 10% share and the 1,327-to-1,613 corpus ratio." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Whole RGB images from unknown cameras under uncontrolled conditions, with no control over pose, expression, illumination, resolution or compression, all of which may vary systematically with the collection route. No morphometric quantity is measured despite the morphometric premise." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "high" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<https://doi.org/10.3389/fnhum.2021.651349> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "ORIGINAL RESEARCH published: 25 May 2021 doi: 10.3389/fnhum.2021.651349 Frontiers in Human Neuroscience | www.frontiersin.org 1 May 2021 | Volume 15 | Article 651349 Edited by: Jingying Chen, Central China Normal University, China Reviewed by: Zhenhu Liang, Yanshan University, China Liang Liao, National Institute of Informatics, Japan Zheng Wang, The University of Tokyo, Japan *Correspondence: Dan Chen dan.chen@whu.edu.cn Specialty section: This article was submitted to Cognitive Neuroscience, a section of the journal Frontiers in Human Neuroscience Received: 09 January 2021 Accepted: 19 March 2021 Published: 25 May 2021 Citation: Zhang S, Chen D, Tang Y and Zhang L (2021) Children ASD Evaluation Through Joint Analysis of EEG and Eye-Tracking Recordings With Graph Convolution Network. Front. Hum. Neurosci. 15:651349. doi: 10.3389/fnhum.2021.651349 Children ASD Evaluation Through Joint Analysis of EEG and Eye-Tracking Recordings With Graph Convolution Network Shasha Zhang, Dan Chen*, Yunbo Tang and Lei Zhang School of Computer Science, Wuhan University, Wuhan, China Recent advances in neuroscience indicate that analysis of bio-signals such as rest state electroencephalogram (EEG) and eye-tracking data can provide more reliable evaluation of children autism spectrum disorder (ASD) than traditional methods of behavior measurement relying on scales do. However, the effectiveness of the new approaches still lags behind the increasing requirement in clinical or educational practices as the “bio-marker” information carried by the bio-signal of a single-modality is likely insufﬁcient or distorted. This study proposes an approach to joint analysis of EEG and eye-tracking for children ASD evaluation. The approach focuses on deep fusion of the features in two modalities as no explicit correlations between the original bio-signals are available, which also limits the performance of existing methods along this direction. First, the synchronization measures, information entropy, and time-frequency features of the multi-channel EEG are derived. Then a random forest applies to the eye-tracking recordings of the same subjects to single out the most signiﬁcant features. A graph convolutional network (GCN) model then naturally fuses the two group of features to differentiate the children with ASD from the typically developed (TD) subjects. Experiments have been carried out on the two types of the bio-signals collected from 42 children (21 ASD and 21 TD subjects, 3–6 years old). The results indicate that (1) the proposed approach can achieve an accuracy of 95% in ASD detection, and (2) strong correlations exist between the two bio-signals collected even asynchronously, in particular the EEG synchronization against the face related/joint attentions in terms of covariance." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Zhang S; Chen D; Tang Y; Zhang L (2021). Children ASD Evaluation Through Joint Analysis of EEG and Eye-Tracking Recordings With Graph Convolution Network. *Frontiers in Human Neuroscience, 15, Article 651349*. https://doi.org/10.3389/fnhum.2021.651349" ;
    dcterms:creator "Chen D",
        "Tang Y",
        "Zhang L",
        "Zhang S" ;
    dcterms:title "Children ASD Evaluation Through Joint Analysis of EEG and Eye-Tracking Recordings With Graph Convolution Network" ;
    bibo:doi "10.3389/fnhum.2021.651349" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_4d6fac7e" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2021 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is dataset provenance stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "The apparatus, age range, ethics committee and paradigms match a separately published cohort, but no provenance or citation is given." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "Matching asserted in a single clause with no figures or test statistics." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Two group sizes and a shared age range only; no ages, sexes, recruitment route or clinical detail." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "42 children with an implied test set of roughly 20 samples, and no limitations section addressing this." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "Random forest gaze feature selection and covariance-based graph construction both draw on the full dataset." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Seven gaze paradigms, presentation schedule, calibration requirement and 60 ms fixation threshold given; viewing distance, tracker model, stimulus details and data-quality criteria are not." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the electrode reduction justified scientifically?" ;
                            slr:justification "120 of 128 channels discarded 'in order to reduce computational overhead'." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "No instrument, criteria or clinician reported for either group." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the signal preprocessing fully specified?" ;
                            slr:justification "Referencing, filter band, sampling rate, ICA artefact removal, visual rejection, electrode selection and epoch length all stated." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are single-modality baselines reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "EEG alone 0.7368 and gaze alone 0.7895 using identical features and pipeline, making the fusion gain directly readable." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is any cross-validation or repetition performed?" ;
                            slr:justification "A single split with no folds, repetition or held-out tuning set." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is uncertainty quantified?" ;
                            slr:justification "No confidence intervals or between-model tests; with roughly 20 test samples the gap between 0.95 and 0.81 is about three samples." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the unit of the train/test split stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "'The ratio of training set against test samples is 8:2' with no definition of sample; reported accuracies imply roughly 20 test samples from 42 children." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are alternative fusion architectures compared?" ;
                            slr:justification "A feature-concatenation SVM pipeline (0.81) and a ResNet18 on concatenated features (0.70) both implemented." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the graph structure built independently of the test data?" ;
                            slr:justification "Covariance thresholds defining the adjacency matrix were computed across the whole dataset." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a result contrary to the paper's framing reported honestly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Eye tracking alone outperformed EEG alone, reported plainly and explained, despite the paper's neuroscience framing." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "No code, no GCN layer configuration, no training hyperparameters, no covariance threshold value." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Helsinki conformity, named ethics committee approval at Beijing Normal University, and written informed consent." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "The availability statement points to the article and supplementary material, which contain no participant-level data." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are limitations stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No limitations section and no caveats on sample size, validation design or generalisability." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The broadest gaze battery in the corpus - seven distinct tests targeting own-race unfamiliar faces (twice), other-race unfamiliar faces, joint attention, gaze following (twice) and social interaction, each photograph type presented six times for 10 seconds. This design permits the informative negative finding that joint attention was the least discriminative gaze feature (t = -0.79, non-significant) while unfamiliar own-race face viewing was the most discriminative - the opposite of what much of this corpus assumes. Resting EEG precedes the gaze battery rather than being recorded concurrently, which is why the cross-modal covariances the paper reports are between asynchronously acquired signals." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-057" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Essentially none. The paper has no limitations section and offers no caveats on sample size, generalisability or validation design. Identifiable in review: the reported accuracies are arithmetically incompatible with a participant-level 80/20 split of 42 children and correspond instead to 19 of 20, 14 of 19 and 15 of 19 samples, which combined with 4-second EEG epoching implies segment-level splitting and hence leakage across the train/test boundary; no diagnostic instrument or criteria are reported for either group; age and sex matching is asserted with no figures; the t-tests and covariance thresholds defining the graph structure were computed on the full dataset including test samples; validation is a single unrepeated split with no confidence intervals; and the reduction from 128 to 8 electrodes is justified only computationally." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-057" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No IQ, developmental, language, adaptive or severity measure, and no comorbidity information for either group." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-057" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "EEG recorded in a shielded room and eye tracking conducted subsequently; ethics approval indicates Beijing Normal University while the analysis was conducted at Wuhan University's School of Computer Science. No recruitment route, period or clinical setting is described." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-057" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "128-channel HydroCel Sensor Net (Electrical Geodesics) sampled at 256 Hz in a shielded room; eye tracking at 300 Hz with five-point calibration required to pass with small error vectors; EEGLAB for ICA-based artefact removal. Notably, only 8 of 128 electrodes (F3, F4, T3, C3, C4, T4, O1, O2) were retained, justified solely as being 'in order to reduce computational overhead' - a substantial discard of spatial information for a stated engineering rather than scientific reason." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-057" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Almost nothing. Two groups of 21 children aged between 3 and 6, with the single sentence 'No statistical differences in age and gender are observed between ASD and TD groups' offered in place of any figures - no means, standard deviations, sex counts or test statistics for either variable. For a study whose gaze features are cumulative fixation durations during face and social viewing, and whose EEG features include entropy measures that change substantially across early childhood, a three-year age band with no distributional information is a material gap. No IQ, language, severity, comorbidity, ethnicity or socioeconomic data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-057" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Frontiers in Human Neuroscience, 15, article 651349, in the Cognitive Neuroscience section - a neuroscience venue, though the work is primarily a machine learning contribution and the authors are all from a computer science school." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-057" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Gaze allocation across seven distinct social-viewing paradigms - own-race and other-race unfamiliar faces, joint attention, gaze following and social interaction - each reduced to a cumulative fixation duration measure within areas of interest. The behavioural coverage is broader than any other single study in the corpus, and the per-paradigm t-tests make it possible to see which constructs actually discriminate: unfamiliar own-race faces and social interaction do, joint attention and one of the two gaze-following tests do not." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-057" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with ASD', 'children with typically developing (TD)', 'ASD children', 'TD children', 'subjects'. Predominantly person-first with 'typically developing' rather than 'normal' or 'healthy'. The framing is neuroscience-oriented rather than deficit-focused, describing autism as 'an early-onset neurodevelopmental disorder characterized by the impairments in social communication and repetitive behaviors' and motivating the work by the claim that bio-signal analysis 'can provide more reliable evaluation of children ASD than traditional methods of behavior measurement relying on scales do'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-057" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The corpus's most architecturally ambitious fusion, and the ablation is properly constructed. Single-modality baselines using identical features and pipeline give 0.7368 for EEG and 0.7895 for gaze, against 0.9500 fused - a gain of over 15 points on the better single modality. Two comparators isolate the mechanism: a conventional pipeline concatenating features with mRMR selection and SVM reaches 0.8100, and a ResNet18 over concatenated features reaches only 0.7000 despite far greater capacity, which the authors read as showing that 'simply and rudely cascading EEG and eye-tracking recordings cannot make good use of multi-modality information'. The graph formulation itself is the contribution: features are nodes, thresholded covariances are edges, so convolution operates over discovered inter-feature structure rather than over a flat concatenation. The interpretive payoff is the cross-modal edge analysis, which identifies EEG synchronisation as related to exotic face, joint attention and gaze-following features, and entropy measures as related to face, joint attention and social interaction features - relationships that exist despite the two signals being recorded at different times. The caveat is that all of this rests on an accuracy figure whose validation unit is not stated and appears not to be the participant." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-057" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None stated beyond the general remark that the approach 'holds potentials in the applications when concerning fusing much more data sources'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-057" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Fourteen features total - seven EEG spanning time domain, time-frequency, synchronisation and four complexity measures, and seven gaze features corresponding one-to-one with the seven viewing tests. This is an extremely compact representation, and the one-feature-per-paradigm structure on the gaze side means each node carries the summary of an entire experimental condition, which is what makes the covariance edges interpretable as relationships between constructs rather than between arbitrary statistics. The corresponding cost is that all spatial detail within each gaze paradigm - which areas of interest were fixated - is collapsed, and 120 of 128 EEG channels are discarded." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-057" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: autism spectrum disorder, EEG, eye-tracking, multi-modality fusion, graph convolution network. Three of five concern the fusion method rather than the clinical problem, which accurately reflects a paper whose contribution is architectural." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-057" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Interpretability is pursued at two levels and is the paper's strongest non-architectural contribution. Univariate t-tests establish which individual features differ between groups, with results interpreted against prior literature - reduced theta increase and stronger beta in autism for the comparator's band features, and greater differences in eye and body fixation. More distinctively, the adjacency matrix is analysed edge by edge to describe which features relate to which, both within EEG (mean value related to all but STFT; approximate entropy unrelated to sample entropy; STFT unrelated to entropy measures because 'entropy based features are calculated to measure the complexity of time series while STFT is a localized process in time and frequency domains') and across modalities. This turns the model's structure into a substantive claim about EEG-gaze relationships rather than leaving it as an implementation detail." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-057" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The weakest element and the one that governs how the headline should be read. A single 8:2 split with no cross-validation, no repetition and no held-out tuning set. The unit of splitting is never stated, and the reported figures indicate it is not the participant: 42 children split 80/20 gives roughly 8 test children, on which 0.9500, 0.7368 and 0.7895 are unattainable, whereas they correspond precisely to 19/20, 14/19 and 15/19 samples. With EEG divided into 4-second epochs yielding roughly 90 segments per child, sample-level splitting would place segments from the same child on both sides of the boundary. Additionally, the t-tests and the covariance thresholding that determines the graph's edge structure were computed across the entire dataset, so the graph topology the network convolves over was constructed using test-sample information. There is no independent cohort, no external site and no replication." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-057" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; a single visit per child comprising sequential EEG and eye-tracking sessions, with no follow-up. Data are EEG traces and gaze fixation summaries. Governance is reported: conducted according to the Declaration of Helsinki, approved by the ethics committee of Beijing Normal University, with written informed consent provided." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-057" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2021. The framing is a two-stage critique. First, that single-modality analysis is inherently limited because 'the bio-marker information carried by the bio-signal of a single-modality is likely insufficient or distorted' - an argument for fusion on information-theoretic rather than merely empirical grounds. Second, and more specific, that existing fusion work 'mainly rely on feature-level fusion or decision-level fusion' and that 'these simple kinds of fusion strategies may not make full use of modality-cross information, thus the performances are largely limited'. The paper then demonstrates this empirically by implementing both a feature-concatenation baseline and a deep network over concatenated features, and showing the latter performs worst. It also grounds the cross-modal premise in developmental neuroscience, citing work linking frontolimbic connectivity at 6 months to responding-to-joint-attention at 9 months, and studies finding eye-tracking and EEG measures correlated during social stimulation - so the fusion argument rests on a claimed functional relationship rather than on an assumption that more data helps." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-057" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: fuse EEG and eye-tracking features through a graph convolutional network over a covariance-derived feature graph, improving on simple feature- or decision-level fusion. Main findings: 95% accuracy for the fused model against 79% for gaze alone and 74% for EEG alone; 81% for a conventional mRMR-plus-SVM pipeline and 70% for ResNet18 on concatenated features; gaze alone outperformed EEG alone; and covariance analysis revealed cross-modal relationships between EEG synchronisation and entropy features and face, joint attention and social interaction gaze paradigms despite asynchronous recording." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-057" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Mixed. Well specified: EEG preprocessing chain with filter band, sampling rate, referencing, ICA artefact removal, electrode selection and 4-second non-overlapping epochs; the seven eye-tracking tests with their purposes and presentation schedule; the 60 ms fixation threshold; the fourteen features by name; both comparator architectures including ResNet18 channel configuration; and complete t-statistics for every feature. Not specified: any diagnostic instrument; participant ages, sexes or matching statistics; the unit of the 8:2 split; the covariance threshold used to build the adjacency matrix; GCN layer configuration, training hyperparameters or random seed; and the random forest configuration used for gaze feature selection. No code, and a data availability statement that points to the article itself." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-057" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, precision, recall and F1 for the proposed model and all three comparators, plus t-statistics and p-values for every feature. Reporting all four metrics across a full ablation is good practice, and the single-modality baselines make the fusion gain directly readable. Missing: AUC, confusion matrices, confidence intervals and any statistical test between models - and with a test set of roughly 20 samples, the difference between 0.95 and 0.81 is about three samples. That all four metrics for the proposed method are exactly 0.9500 indicates a balanced test set of 20 with a single error." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-057" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A graph convolutional network over a feature graph, with random forest for upstream gaze feature selection, compared against an SVM-based feature-engineering pipeline and a ResNet18. The GCN choice is well matched to the stated problem - modelling relationships between features across modalities where no explicit correlation exists between the raw signals - and using features rather than subjects as graph nodes is an unusual and defensible formulation. That the much deeper ResNet18 performs worst supports the argument that architecture matched to the fusion problem matters more than capacity at this sample size." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-057" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups of 21 children each, aged 3-6, totalling 42. No other-diagnosis comparison group. Nothing further is reported about either group - no ages beyond the shared range, no sexes, no clinical characterisation - and because no provenance for the dataset is given, nothing can be recovered from a source publication either." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-057" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None reported. There is no diagnostic instrument, criteria, clinician, assessment date or severity measure anywhere in the paper for the autistic group, and no screening described for the comparison group. The dataset's apparatus (128-channel HydroCel), age range (3-6), ethics committee (Beijing Normal University) and gaze paradigms (own-race and other-race face viewing) match a separately published study, which suggests secondary analysis of an existing cohort - but no such provenance, citation or diagnostic inheritance is stated, so the labels have no traceable basis within this paper." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-057" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Six minutes of resting EEG per child, followed by approximately seven eye-tracking tests in which each photograph type appeared six times for 10 seconds" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional case-control classification with a modality ablation and comparison against two published or standard alternatives" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Preschool children with autism" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 21; comparison: 21" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Proposed GCN 0.9500 on all four metrics. Single-modality baselines using the same features and pipeline: eye-tracking alone 0.7895 accuracy, 0.8000 precision, 0.9230 recall, 0.8571 F1; EEG alone 0.7368, 0.7500, 0.9231, 0.8276. External comparators: the reimplemented feature-engineering pipeline 0.8100 on all metrics, and ResNet18 on concatenated features 0.7000 accuracy, 0.8050 precision, 0.7150 r",
                        "Representing EEG and eye-tracking features as nodes in a graph whose edges are inter-feature covariances, then fusing them with a graph convolutional network, gave 95% accuracy against 79% for gaze alone and 74% for EEG alone, and beat both a conventional feature-concatenation-plus-SVM pipeline (81%) and a ResNet18 over concatenated features (70%). The authors interpret the ResNet result as showin" ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy, precision, recall and F1 for the proposed model and each comparator, plus t-statistics and p-values for every feature. No AUC, confusion matrices, confidence intervals or statistical comparison between models" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Proposed GCN 0.9500 on all four metrics. Single-modality baselines using the same features and pipeline: eye-tracking alone 0.7895 accuracy, 0.8000 precision, 0.9230 recall, 0.8571 F1; EEG alone 0.7368, 0.7500, 0.9231, 0.8276. External comparators: the reimplemented feature-engineering pipeline 0.8100 on all metrics, and ResNet18 on concatenated features 0.7000 accuracy, 0.8050 precision, 0.7150 r; Representing EEG and eye-tracking features as nodes in a graph whose edges are inter-feature covariances, then fusing them with a graph convolutional network, gave 95% accuracy against 79% for gaze alone and 74% for EEG alone, and beat both a conventional feature-concatenation-plus-SVM pipeline (81%) and a ResNet18 over concatenated features (70%). The authors interpret the ResNet result as showin" ] ] ;
