<https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.635696> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "ORIGINAL RESEARCH published: 25 May 2021 doi: 10.3389/fpsyg.2021.635696 Edited by: Giovanni Ottoboni, University of Bologna, Italy Reviewed by: Gennaro Tartarisco, National Research Council (CNR), Italy Emilia I. Barakova, Eindhoven University of Technology, Netherlands *Correspondence: Roberta Simeoli roberta.simeoli@unina.it Specialty section: This article was submitted to Health Psychology, a section of the journal Frontiers in Psychology Received: 30 November 2020 Accepted: 19 April 2021 Published: 25 May 2021 Citation: Simeoli R, Milano N, Rega A and Marocco D (2021) Using Technology to Identify Children With Autism Through Motor Abnormalities. Front. Psychol. 12:635696. doi: 10.3389/fpsyg.2021.635696 Using Technology to Identify Children With Autism Through Motor Abnormalities Roberta Simeoli1*, Nicola Milano2, Angelo Rega1,3 and Davide Marocco1 1 Department of Humanistic Studies, University of Naples Federico II, Naples, Italy, 2 Institute of Cognitive Sciences and Technologies, National Research Council (CNR), Rome, Italy, 3 Neapolisanit S.R.L. Rehabilitation Center, Ottaviano, Italy Autism is a neurodevelopmental disorder typically assessed and diagnosed through observational analysis of behavior. Assessment exclusively based on behavioral observation sessions requires a lot of time for the diagnosis. In recent years, there is a growing need to make assessment processes more motivating and capable to provide objective measures of the disorder. New evidence showed that motor abnormalities may underpin the disorder and provide a computational marker to enhance assessment and diagnostic processes. Thus, a measure of motor patterns could provide a means to assess young children with autism and a new starting point for rehabilitation treatments. In this study, we propose to use a software tool that through a smart tablet device and touch screen sensor technologies could be able to capture detailed information about children’s motor patterns. We compared movement trajectories of autistic children and typically developing children, with the aim to identify autism motor signatures analyzing their coordinates of movements. We used a smart tablet device to record coordinates of dragging movements carried out by 60 children (30 autistic children and 30 typically developing children) during a cognitive task. Machine learning analysis of children’s motor patterns identiﬁed autism with 93% accuracy, demonstrating that autism can be computationally identiﬁed. The analysis of the features that most affect the prediction reveals and describes the differences between the groups, conﬁrming that motor abnormalities are a core feature of autism." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Simeoli R; Milano N; Rega A; Marocco D (2021). Using Technology to Identify Children With Autism Through Motor Abnormalities. *Frontiers in Psychology, 12, Article 635696*. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.635696" ;
    dcterms:creator "Marocco D",
        "Milano N",
        "Rega A",
        "Simeoli R" ;
    dcterms:title "Using Technology to Identify Children With Autism Through Motor Abnormalities" ;
    bibo:doi "10.3389/fpsyg.2021.635696" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_fa91fc18" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2021 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Recruitment sources, ages, inclusion and exclusion criteria are given; sex is entirely absent and IQ appears only as a range." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "Age is close but untested; IQ differs at the lower bound by 15 points with distributions unreported; sex cannot be assessed." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are cases and comparisons drawn from comparable settings?" ;
                            slr:justification "Cases from a rehabilitation centre with ongoing psychomotor and speech therapy, comparisons from a primary school." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "Sixty children aged 5-10, appropriate to the task but small, and the stated future target is younger children in whom the method is untested." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are motor confounds excluded?" ;
                            slr:justification "Normal vision and no sensory or motor deficit required of all participants - relevant given the outcome is a motor measure, though it narrows the autistic sample." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is cognitive load controlled?" ;
                            slr:justification "Only the first five items of each subtest were analysed because all participants completed those correctly, and the examiner-demonstrated item was excluded." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "DSM-5 plus ADOS-2 by clinicians stated to have no affiliation with the research team." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the features defined precisely?" ;
                            slr:justification "All twelve given with formal definitions, including the STH ratio and the DC/sdDC interpretation, computed with a cited R package." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sampling rate adequate for the dominant feature family?" ;
                            slr:justification "Acceleration measures carry most of the classification but are differenced from a 40 Hz coordinate stream, a constraint not discussed." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Tablet model, screen geometry, refresh and 40 Hz sampling; seating height and tablet placement; the full task battery and administration rule." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the unit of analysis the participant?" ;
                            slr:justification "1,500 task-level samples from 60 children, with folds assigned at random across subjects and tasks." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reported figure the cross-validated estimate?" ;
                            slr:justification "Cross-validation gave 88 +/- 3%; the 93%, its sensitivity, specificity, ROC and full feature ranking come from the best single fold model." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is feature robustness characterised?" ;
                            slr:justification "Full sequential-removal curve showing graceful degradation to 78% at five features and chance at one." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is model selection separated from performance estimation?" ;
                            slr:justification "Hidden-layer width, the reported operating point and the feature ranking were all determined on the same cross-validation." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the train/test split by participant?" ;
                            slr:justification "Explicitly 'regardless of the number of ASD or TD subjects in the test or training set'; no participant-held-out analysis anywhere." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is uncertainty quantified for the headline?" ;
                            slr:justification "No confidence interval on 93%, no confusion matrix, no per-fold spread, and no trivial baseline such as age or IQ." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No repository, but raw data are committed to be made available by the authors without undue reservation." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Approval by the Ethical Committee of Psychological Research, University of Naples Federico II, with written guardian consent." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Bespoke Unity/C# software and R analysis are described but not released." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are limitations stated candidly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Sample size, the IQ confound stated in terms, and the transfer question all raised in a dedicated passage." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Does the interpretation match the study's own importance ranking?" ;
                            slr:justification "The Discussion leads with STH, DC and MeanLength, which the ranking placed among the least impactful, while the acceleration measures it identified as most impactful are barely discussed." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is diagnostic specificity against other conditions addressed?" ;
                            slr:justification "The authors state the signature may overlap with ADHD, coordination disorder and intellectual disability, and that sensory integration dysfunction is not autism-specific." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The study's decisive weakness, and the reason its numbers are not comparable to participant-level results elsewhere in this corpus. The dataset is 1,500 samples formed as 60 subjects x 25 tasks, explicitly 'randomized between subjects and tasks', and the folds are drawn so that 'for each fold, we take the 10% of the samples for testing and the remaining 90% for training, regardless of the number of ASD or TD subjects in the test or training set'. Every child therefore has roughly 22 or 23 of their 25 vectors in training whenever the remaining two or three are tested. Because each vector is an average of that child's habitual movement dynamics within a task, the network can identify the individual from their kinematic signature and recover the diagnosis from that identification rather than from any group-general motor property - the same mechanism Liaqat et al. diagnose explicitly in their own eye-tracking data and that Cilia quantified as a fall from ~90% to ~71% when the unit was corrected. No participant-held-out analysis is reported anywhere, so the magnitude of the inflation here is unknown. A second, independent issue concerns which number is reported: ten-fold cross-validation with five repetitions returned 88 +/- 3%, and the paper then states that 'for the successive analysis, we used the best trained model across the 10-fold', from which the 93% accuracy, 0.87 sensitivity, 0.98 specificity, ROC curve and complete feature-importance ordering are all derived. The best of ten is a maximum, not an estimate, and it is the figure carried into the abstract, the Discussion and the Conclusion while the cross-validated 88 +/- 3% appears once. Hidden-layer width was additionally selected on the same cross-validation. The compounded effect is that the reported operating characteristics come from a model chosen for performing best on data structurally related to what it was trained on." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-073" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "One 17-dimensional vector per child per task: twelve kinematic features averaged over all finite trajectories in that task, plus five one-hot task indicators, yielding 60 x 25 = 1,500 rows. Two aspects of this construction determine what the results can mean. Averaging within task discards within-task variability and produces a stable per-child descriptor - which is exactly what makes each row informative about the individual as well as the group. And because every child contributes 25 such rows that share their individual movement characteristics, rows are strongly clustered within children; treating the 1,500 rows as exchangeable for the purpose of fold assignment is the analytic decision that undermines the headline figure. A representation of one vector per child would have given 60 rows and a much harder, but interpretable, problem." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-073" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with autism', 'autistic children', 'ASD children', 'individuals with autism', 'ASD individuals', 'ASD participants', 'ASD group', against 'typically developing children', 'TD group', 'their typically developing peers', 'neurotypical group'. Person-first and identity-first are used interchangeably, sometimes in the same sentence. The clinical vocabulary is dated in one respect: intellectual disability is described as 'mild mental retardation' with a 2016 WHO citation, a term already superseded in DSM-5 and ICD-11 at the time of writing. Autism itself is framed in deficit terms throughout - 'communication impairment, a lack of social interaction' - though the theoretical framing is comparatively sympathetic, drawing on embodied cognition and on Sinha's account in which stereotyped behaviour is a rational response to an unpredictable world rather than a symptom to be suppressed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-073" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Good on instrumentation and modelling, thin on data structure. Reported: tablet model, screen size, resolution, refresh rate and sampling frequency; the seating geometry; the task battery with its five domains, item structure and administration rule; the definition of a finite trajectory; all twelve features with formal definitions including the STH ratio and the DC/sdDC interpretation; the traj package with citations; feature standardisation; the full network specification (17-5-2, tanh, softmax, Adam, learning rate 0.01, Xavier initialisation, sparse categorical cross-entropy, 500 epochs); the grid-search range for hidden units and the criterion for choosing five; the cross-validation scheme; and the feature-removal procedure with its source. Missing or ambiguous: the number of trajectories per child, the aggregation from 25 tasks to 1,500 samples is stated but the fold-assignment rule is described only as random 'regardless' of subject composition, so the reader must infer that subjects span folds; no code release; no random seeds; and no participant-level performance breakdown that would allow the segment-versus-subject question to be resolved from the reported numbers. A data availability statement is present." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-073" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Single-modality: touchscreen kinematics only. The paper nonetheless performs a within-modality combination that is worth noting - the network receives twelve kinematic features together with five one-hot task-identity variables, so movement is interpreted conditional on cognitive context rather than pooled across it, and the task variables 'were always provided to the ANN' and were never subject to the removal analysis. This is a defensible design given that different Leiter-3 subtests impose different spatial demands, but it has an unexamined consequence: because task identity is always available, the model can learn task-specific kinematic norms, and since the feature vectors are per-child per-task averages, the combination of task identity plus habitual movement dynamics is close to a subject fingerprint. No cross-modality fusion is attempted, and none is claimed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-073" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups of 30, both explicitly sized: 30 children with a DSM-5 and ADOS-2 diagnosis of autism recruited from the Neapolisanit S.R.L. Rehabilitation Center, and 30 typically developing children recruited from a primary school. No other-diagnosis or developmentally-delayed comparison group, which the authors themselves identify as the key gap, noting that the motor signature 'may overlap with other disorders, such as attention deficit disorder, motor coordination disturbance, or general intellectual disabilities'. The two groups come from structurally different settings - a clinic where the autistic children receive ongoing psychomotor and speech therapy, versus a school - so group membership is confounded with familiarity with clinical testing, with the examiner's setting, and potentially with prior therapeutic work on exactly the fine-motor skills being measured. This is not discussed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-073" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Among the better ascertainments in the corpus for a study of this size. Diagnosis required both DSM-5 clinical criteria and the ADOS-2, and was made by 'qualified doctors and professionals in the sector' who 'have no affiliation with our laboratory or our research' - an explicit assertion of independence between the diagnosing clinicians and the research team that almost no other study here makes, and which forecloses the possibility of diagnostic classification being influenced by knowledge of the study hypothesis. Comparison children were excluded for suspected autism signs, developmental abnormalities, or current or past psychiatric or neurological disorder, though the screening was by clinician or teacher judgement rather than a standardised instrument, and any child about whose diagnosis or health a clinician or teacher was uncertain was excluded outright. The one waste is that the ADOS-2 was administered but its severity scores are never reported or used." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-073" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A digital reimplementation of the Leiter-3 nonverbal intelligence battery, built in Unity and C#, playing 'exactly the same tasks of the Leiter-3, presented in the same order' under the original administration rules, with the examiner advancing subtests at the standard error threshold. Five subtests span figure-ground, figure completion, classification analogies, sequential order and visual patterns, with two to eight draggable cards and placeholders per item and distractors present, ascending in complexity. Three features of the design deserve note. The paradigm is entirely dual-purpose: the child takes a cognitive assessment they would take anyway and the motor data are a by-product, so there is no added task burden and the approach is retrofittable to any clinic already using the Leiter-3. The nonverbal character of the instrument makes it usable across language levels, which matters for autism specifically. And the cognitive-load control is carefully reasoned - only the first five items of each subtest were analysed 'since all participants correctly performed at least the first five items', so kinematic differences cannot be attributed to some children struggling with the cognitive demand, and the demonstration item FG1 was excluded because the examiner may have moved cards during it. The five one-hot task-identity variables then give the network explicit information about which cognitive context each movement came from." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-073" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: autism spectrum disorder, sensory-motor impairment, motion analysis, machine learning, classification, assessment technologies. The pairing of 'sensory-motor impairment' with 'assessment technologies' captures the paper's dual identity - a theoretical claim about embodied cognition and a practical claim about instrumenting existing clinical tools - and 'assessment technologies' rather than 'diagnosis' signals the intended scope correctly." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-073" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Substantially candid on the scientific limitations and silent on the methodological one. Acknowledged, in a dedicated passage: the small sample, with replication needed 'to validate this method on a new not trained dataset' and an explicit question of whether the algorithm 'could remain predictive also for a greater sample or if it requires to be retrained'; the IQ confound, stated directly as 'we were not able to exclude intelligence as confounders', with the mitigation that most participants were high functioning and that analysing only universally-passed items limits cognitive interference; and - the most valuable admission in the paper - that the motor signature 'may overlap with other disorders, such as attention deficit disorder, motor coordination disturbance, or general intellectual disabilities', reinforced by the observation that sensory integration dysfunction, their explanatory mechanism, is also found in ADHD, so 'future extensions of this work should include other neurodevelopmental disabilities in order to verify the specificity of these motor signatures for ASD'. Not acknowledged: that the cross-validation folds split task-level samples rather than children, so the same child's data appears on both sides; that the headline 93% is the best of ten fold models while the cross-validated estimate is 88 +/- 3%; that hidden-layer width, feature ranking and the reported operating point were all selected on the same cross-validation used for reporting; and that cases and comparisons were recruited from systematically different settings." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-073" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: determine whether the kinematics of a simple dragging movement, recorded incidentally during a digital cognitive assessment, can classify autism, and describe the motor features that differentiate the groups. Main findings: 88 +/- 3% accuracy under ten-fold cross-validation with five repetitions, and 93% accuracy with sensitivity 0.87 and specificity 0.98 for the best fold model; classification degraded only gradually as features were removed, reaching 78% with five features and chance with one; speed and acceleration measures dominated the importance ranking over straightness and directional change; and autistic trajectories were shorter, less straight, more frequently redirected and far more variable in speed and acceleration." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-073" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Four directions, three of them named by the authors and one clearly implied. Replicate on a larger sample and determine whether the trained model transfers without retraining - a transfer question rather than a sample-size platitude. Control IQ explicitly as an independent variable rather than relying on task selection to suppress its influence. Include other neurodevelopmental conditions - ADHD, developmental coordination disorder, intellectual disability - to establish whether the signature is autism-specific rather than a general marker of atypical development; the authors give the mechanistic reason, that sensory integration dysfunction is not autism-specific. And extend the method to younger children, where earlier identification would matter most, which is where the motor-precursor literature they review predicts the signal should already be present." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-073" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, sensitivity, specificity and an ROC curve - an adequate panel, and the specificity/sensitivity asymmetry (0.98 versus 0.87) is reported rather than collapsed into accuracy. Two supplementary reporting choices are better than the corpus norm. Training-accuracy curves are shown with mean and 90% bootstrapped confidence intervals across all replications, so training stability is visible rather than asserted, and the authors state they 'checked that no overfitting is present in every fold'. And the feature-ablation curve reports accuracy, sensitivity and specificity jointly as a function of how many features are retained, which shows the graceful-then-sharp degradation pattern (78% at five features, 52% at one) and demonstrates that the result does not hinge on a single variable. What is missing bears directly on the headline: no confidence interval on the reported 93%, no confusion matrix, no per-fold table that would let a reader see the spread from which the best fold was drawn, and no comparison against a trivial baseline such as age or IQ - the latter especially relevant given that the authors concede IQ is an uncontrolled confound." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-073" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional, one session per child, no follow-up, though the authors state a future aim of applying the method to younger children. Ethics are reported: approval by the Ethical Committee of Psychological Research of the Department of Humanities, University of Naples Federico II, with written informed consent from legal guardians obtained before the study. The data are coordinate streams rather than video or audio, so re-identification risk is intrinsically low, and a data availability statement commits to sharing raw data 'without undue reservation'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-073" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The kinematics of goal-directed dragging - how a finger travels from a card to a placeholder - decomposed into how fast (mean, max, min and SD of speed), how the speed changes (the same four for acceleration), how directly (STH straightness), how consistently in direction (DC and sdDC) and in how many separate pieces (MeanLength). The behaviour is not social and not autism-specific in its surface form; the claim is that autism manifests in the microstructure of any goal-directed movement because prospective motor control is impaired, which is why an arbitrary cognitive task can serve as the probe. The theoretical grounding is more developed than in most of this corpus, running from Kanner's original identification of disrupted movement patterns through Leary and Hill on motor-symptom links, the enactive mind account, Friston's predictive coding and Sinha's proposal that stereotypies are a response to an unpredictably 'magical' world - and the motor findings are read back into that frame as over- and under-compensation from interrupted feedforward control." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-073" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Interpretability is pursued seriously and by an appropriate method. The stepwise-removal procedure of Olden et al. (2004) yields a complete importance ordering rather than a top-k list, and the accompanying curve shows how performance responds to progressive impoverishment of the input - more informative than a single importance ranking because it distinguishes redundant features from load-bearing ones. Boxplots of all twelve features by group then give the direction of every effect. The conclusions drawn are physically meaningful: speed and acceleration measures matter more than geometric straightness and directional coherence, and the movement picture that emerges - short, fragmented, non-straight trajectories with wide speed and acceleration excursions - is coherently linked to over- and under-compensation from disrupted prospective control, and cross-referenced to Anzulewicz's independent finding of greater impact force in autistic children's tablet interaction. There is one internal inconsistency: the Discussion's descriptive interpretation leads with STH, DC and MeanLength, but the study's own ranking removed STH second, sdDC fourth and DC sixth as the least impactful variables, while the acceleration measures the interpretation barely mentions were the last to be removed. The narrative therefore foregrounds the features the model found least useful. Both analyses were also run on the single best fold model rather than averaged across folds, so the ranking inherits that selection." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-073" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Deliberately commodity hardware: an Android 6.0 tablet with a 9.6-inch 1280x800 screen at 60 Hz refresh, dimensions given to the millimetre, with touch sampling at 40 Hz - so 40 coordinate pairs per second. No motion capture, markers, depth cameras or wearable sensors, which is the study's practical argument: the same measurement in the optical motion-tracking literature it cites requires a laboratory. Software is bespoke (Unity, C#), analysis uses RStudio with the traj package with its two source citations, and the Leiter-3 materials were supplied by Stoelting Co. The 40 Hz sampling rate is a meaningful constraint that goes unremarked: it bounds how finely acceleration - the feature family the importance analysis identifies as most discriminative - can be estimated from a differenced coordinate stream." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-073" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2021 (received November 2020, published May 2021). The framing is the standard critique of observational diagnosis - it 'requires a lot of time', relies on 'the interpretative skills of clinicians', and 'take a long time without being able to deliver an objective and shareable result' - but the paper adds a more specific diagnosis of the field's problem: 'one of the main gaps in the diagnosis of autism is related to the lack of a quantitative evaluation of the disorder; in fact, although the variability within the disorder is known, there is still no valid method to recognize and categorize these differences'. The target is therefore the absence of graded measurement rather than subjectivity as such. A second framing move is methodological and unusual for this corpus: the authors argue explicitly that classical group-difference statistics are the wrong instrument here, 'since several interactions of motion variables could, actually, affect the presumed typical autistic motor pattern, we cannot assume that there must necessarily be movement variables typical of autism', and cite Yarkoni and Westfall to justify a predictive rather than exploratory approach. That is a considered epistemic position rather than a default. The paper also positions itself in a specific lineage - Anzulewicz's iPad gameplay study with 262 features reaching 'up to 93% accuracy', Crippa's upper-limb kinematics at 96.7% - and its own 93% lands squarely on the number it cites, which the Discussion treats as convergent confirmation rather than as an occasion to ask whether the two studies share a validation design." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-073" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Partial. IQ was assessed with the Leiter-3, a nonverbal intelligence test appropriate for this population and administered in its original form for the assessment, but reported only as a range per group. Comorbidity was handled by exclusion rather than measurement: 'no existing comorbidities' was an inclusion criterion, no specific comorbidity was reported, and all participants were required to have normal vision and no sensory or motor deficit - the last a genuinely relevant control for a study whose outcome is a fine-motor measure, and one that also narrows the sample away from the substantial proportion of autistic children who do have motor impairment. No adaptive functioning measure, no language level, and no ADOS severity scores despite the instrument being used for diagnosis. Medication is not reported, though ongoing psychomotor and speech therapy is." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-073" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Frontiers in Psychology 12:635696 (2021), in the Health Psychology section, from the Department of Humanistic Studies at the University of Naples Federico II, the CNR Institute of Cognitive Sciences and Technologies, and the Neapolisanit rehabilitation centre. A psychology venue with a cognitive-science orientation rather than an engineering one, which is visible in the balance of the paper: the theoretical framing occupies more space than the model, the literature review traces motor abnormality in autism from Kanner forward, and the machine learning is deliberately modest. The clinical co-affiliation supplied the participants and the diagnostic pathway." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-073" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Naples, Italy, across two different settings by group: autistic children at the Neapolisanit S.R.L. Rehabilitation Center, which provided 'the clinical ward for the experimentation' and where those children also receive ongoing psychomotor and speech therapy; comparison children at a primary school. The physical arrangement is specified precisely and identically for both - child seated at a table 65-70 cm high adjusted for age, experimenter opposite, tablet within 20 cm of the table edge - which controls posture and reach geometry, the most obvious physical determinants of drag kinematics. What is not controlled is the setting asymmetry itself. Recruiting cases from a therapy centre and comparisons from a school means group membership is entangled with familiarity with clinical assessment and with prior therapeutic exposure to fine-motor practice, either of which could shift drag dynamics independently of diagnosis." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-073" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A single small feedforward network - 17 inputs, one hidden layer of five tanh units, two softmax outputs - chosen for the stated reason that neural networks handle 'complex and nonlinear relationships between variables', which matches the paper's explicit epistemic position that no individual movement variable need differ between groups and that what is categorisable is 'the dynamic interaction of these features'. That position is argued rather than assumed, with a citation to Yarkoni and Westfall on prediction versus explanation, and it justifies choosing a predictive framework over group-difference testing. Model selection was equally restrained: a descending grid search from 10 hidden units found comparable accuracy down to 5 and degradation below, and 5 was chosen on the explicit principle that fewer hidden units reduce overfitting. Deliberate simplicity of this kind is rare in this corpus and is the right instinct for a 60-participant dataset; the difficulty is that it was verified against a cross-validation that does not hold participants out." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-073" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Autistic group: 30 children, mean age 7 years (SD 1.4), IQ range 59-109 on the Leiter-3, with six children between 59 and 70. Comparison group: 30 children, mean age 6 years 8 months (SD 1), IQ range 74-110. Age is close but no test of the difference is reported and matching is not claimed as a procedure. Two reporting gaps are consequential. Sex is not reported for either group anywhere in the paper - no counts, no proportions, no statement that groups were balanced - which is a complete omission of a variable that affects both autism prevalence and fine motor development at these ages. And IQ is given only as a range, with no mean or standard deviation, so the extent of the cognitive difference between groups cannot be quantified even though the authors concede it is a confound: the autistic range extends 15 points lower at the bottom and the distributions within those ranges are unknown. The comparison group's lower bound of 74 is itself notable, sitting in the borderline range." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-073" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "One session per child; only the first five items of each of five Leiter-3 subtests were analysed" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional case-control classification, with autistic children recruited from a rehabilitation centre and comparison children from a primary school" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "School-age children with autism, predominantly without intellectual disability" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 30; comparison: 30" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Kinematic features of ordinary dragging movements, logged at 40 Hz while children completed a digital Leiter-3 battery, supported classification of autism at 88 +/- 3% under ten-fold cross-validation and 93% for the best fold. The motor picture is coherent and clinically interpretable: autistic children's drags were shorter, less straight, more frequently redirected, and far more variable in speed",
                        "Ten-fold cross-validation with five repetitions: 88 +/- 3% accuracy. Best single fold model: 93% accuracy, sensitivity 0.87, specificity 0.98. Feature-removal curve: performance degrades slowly to 78% by the seventh removal, then falls steeply to 52% - chance - with a single feature. The 93%/0.87/0.98 figures are the ones reported in the abstract, the Discussion and the Conclusion; the 88 +/- 3% c" ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy, sensitivity, specificity and an ROC curve; accuracy across training epochs with mean and 90% bootstrapped confidence intervals across replications; and accuracy, sensitivity and specificity as functions of the number of retained features" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Ten-fold cross-validation with five repetitions: 88 +/- 3% accuracy. Best single fold model: 93% accuracy, sensitivity 0.87, specificity 0.98. Feature-removal curve: performance degrades slowly to 78% by the seventh removal, then falls steeply to 52% - chance - with a single feature. The 93%/0.87/0.98 figures are the ones reported in the abstract, the Discussion and the Conclusion; the 88 +/- 3% c; Kinematic features of ordinary dragging movements, logged at 40 Hz while children completed a digital Leiter-3 battery, supported classification of autism at 88 +/- 3% under ten-fold cross-validation and 93% for the best fold. The motor picture is coherent and clinically interpretable: autistic children's drags were shorter, less straight, more frequently redirected, and far more variable in speed" ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "01b654cda351818fbc4b24004ebb0c0036e06c002fe85ad0665bbab54cb9cb9a" ;
