            slr:overall_judgment "some" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<https://doi.org/10.3390/info15030133> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Autism spectrum disorder (ASD) is a developmental disorder that encompasses difficulties in communication (both verbal and non-verbal), social skills, and repetitive behaviors. The diagnosis of autism spectrum disorder typically involves specialized procedures and techniques, which can be time-consuming and expensive. The accuracy and efficiency of the diagnosis depend on the expertise of the specialists and the diagnostic methods employed. To address the growing need for early, rapid, cost-effective, and accurate diagnosis of autism spectrum disorder, there has been a search for advanced smart methods that can automatically classify the disorder. Machine learning offers sophisticated techniques for building automated classifiers that can be utilized by users and clinicians to enhance accuracy and efficiency in diagnosis. Eye-tracking scan paths have emerged as a tool increasingly used in autism spectrum disorder clinics. This methodology examines attentional processes by quantitatively measuring eye movements. Its precision, ease of use, and cost-effectiveness make it a promising platform for developing biomarkers for use in clinical trials for autism spectrum disorder. The detection of autism spectrum disorder can be achieved by observing the atypical visual attention patterns of children with the disorder compared to typically developing children. This study proposes a deep learning model, known as T-CNN-Autism Spectrum Disorder (T-CNN-ASD), that utilizes eye-tracking scans to classify participants into ASD and typical development (TD) groups. The proposed model consists of two hidden layers with 300 and 150 neurons, respectively, and underwent 10 rounds of cross-validation with a dropout rate of 20%. In the testing phase, the model achieved an accuracy of 95.59%, surpassing the accuracy of other machine learning algorithms such as random forest (RF), decision tree (DT), K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN), and multi-layer perceptron (MLP). Furthermore, the proposed model demonstrated superior performance when compared to the findings reported in previous studies. The results demonstrate that the proposed model can accurately classify children with ASD from those with TD without human intervention." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Alsaidi M; Obeid N; Al-Madi N; Hiary H; Aljarah I (2024). A Convolutional Deep Neural Network Approach to Predict Autism Spectrum Disorder Based on Eye-Tracking Scan Paths. *Information, 15(3), 133*. https://doi.org/10.3390/info15030133" ;
    dcterms:creator "Al-Madi N",
        "Aljarah I",
        "Alsaidi M",
        "Hiary H",
        "Obeid N" ;
    dcterms:title "A Convolutional Deep Neural Network Approach to Predict Autism Spectrum Disorder Based on Eye-Tracking Scan Paths" ;
    bibo:doi "10.3390/info15030133" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_32da8ede" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2024 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participant selection and spectrum representativeness" ;
                            slr:justification "Twenty-nine autistic children inherited from a public dataset, with CARS mean 32.97 indicating a mild-to-moderate sample, but no group-level demographics, no inclusion or exclusion criteria and no IQ or language data. Case-control recruitment from specialist clinics against mainstream schools." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Confounder control" ;
                            slr:justification "No covariate is reported by group, so none can be controlled or even checked. The paper's own related-work section notes that removing age as a feature reduced accuracy across all classifiers in a prior study - an observation about gaze-based classifiers drawing on age information that it does not apply to its own work, despite age being available in the dataset." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Comparison group adequacy" ;
                            slr:justification "Thirty typically developing children from French schools with no reported screening for autism or developmental concerns, and no clinical comparison group." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Handling of missing data and attrition" ;
                            slr:justification "No attrition, tracking-quality or trial-rejection information is available, and no rendering specification is given, so what the images represent cannot be assessed. Preprocessing is limited to resizing, colour conversion and flattening." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome measurement blinding" ;
                            slr:justification "Preprocessing and classification are fully automated from image files; no human rating enters the pipeline." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Index test definition and prespecification" ;
                            slr:justification "Over a hundred hyperparameter configurations evaluated on the reporting cross-validation with the maximum taken as the result; no prespecified architecture, optimiser or operating point; no held-out confirmation." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reference standard quality" ;
                            slr:justification "Specialist clinic evaluation named but not described, CARS used for severity rather than diagnosis, no criteria or clinician detail, and no screening of the comparison group. Inherited rather than authored, but undefined nonetheless." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Measurement standardisation" ;
                            slr:justification "The inherited acquisition is well standardised for its time and better described here than in the other reuses - one eye tracker (SMI Red-m at 250 Hz), a quiet room with white barriers to reduce distraction, a fixed 60 cm viewing distance, and all interaction in the children's first language. But no calibration procedure, tracking criterion or scan-path rendering scheme is reported, and the presence of a live host directing attention in some videos means the elicited behaviour is not uniform across stimuli." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance estimation and chance level" ;
                            slr:justification "The reported accuracy is irreconcilable with the reported sensitivity and specificity at the stated class sizes - 95.59% claimed against approximately 79.0% implied - and the same holds for the multilayer perceptron comparator. The quantity labelled F1 is the harmonic mean of sensitivity and specificity, not F1. No confidence intervals, no AUC, no confusion matrix, and no majority-class baseline against an implied 60.0% floor." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data splitting and leakage control" ;
                            slr:justification "Ten-round cross-validation over 547 images from 59 children, roughly nine per child, with no indication that a child's images were kept within a fold and no participant-level result reported anywhere." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Preprocessing and feature selection nesting" ;
                            slr:justification "No feature selection, but hyperparameter selection across more than a hundred configurations was performed on the same cross-validation used to report the result. Notably, augmentation was deliberately avoided on stated and correct grounds, which removes one leakage route that other reuses of this dataset introduce." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Sample size adequacy for the modelling" ;
                            slr:justification "Fifty-nine children and 547 images for a model search spanning well over a hundred configurations. The authors identify no sample-size constraint, and indeed present the absence of augmentation as evidence that the dataset was adequate." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Completeness of methods reporting" ;
                            slr:justification "The computational pipeline is documented exhaustively - preprocessing chain with figures, twelve hyperparameter tables with accuracy for every cell, the winning configuration, software versions and hardware. Missing: code, cross-validation implementation and seed, the image-to-child mapping, the scan-path rendering scheme, and any derivation reconciling the reported accuracy with the reported class-conditional metrics." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reporting of results and uncertainty" ;
                            slr:justification "Sensitivity and specificity are reported alongside accuracy for all five models in two colour conditions, which is better practice than most reuses of this dataset - and is what makes the internal inconsistency detectable. But the three metrics cannot describe the same classifier, F1 is mislabelled, and there are no confidence intervals, no AUC and no confusion matrices." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Ethics, consent and conflicts" ;
                            slr:justification "The originating study's ethical approvals and Helsinki compliance are cited explicitly, which is appropriate practice for a secondary analysis and more than most dataset-reuse papers in this corpus provide. No funding or competing-interests statement appears in the retrieved text." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Acknowledgement of limitations" ;
                            slr:justification "No limitations section and no constraint acknowledged anywhere. The self-assessment is uniformly favourable, including the claim that the model 'can accurately classify children with ASD from those with TD without human intervention'." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data, code and protocol availability" ;
                            slr:justification "The source dataset is public and properly cited to its originators, so inputs are available. No code, no seed, no split specification, no preregistration." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, sensitivity, specificity and a mislabelled F1, reported for the proposed model and all four comparators in both greyscale and colour conditions - and this fuller metric set is what exposes the paper's central problem. Reporting sensitivity and specificity alongside accuracy is good practice, but here the three quantities cannot describe the same classifier: at 219 autistic and 328 non-autistic images, the reported 77.60% sensitivity and 79.91% specificity imply an accuracy of about 79.0%, not the 95.59% claimed, and the multilayer perceptron shows the same pattern (94.69% claimed against about 73.6% implied). The discrepancy is not small and is not reconciled anywhere. Separately, Equation (4) defines F1 as 2 x (Sensitivity x Specificity) / (Sensitivity + Specificity), which is the harmonic mean of sensitivity and specificity - a balanced-accuracy variant - rather than the F1 score, so the column labelled F1 does not mean what its name implies. Also missing: AUC, confusion matrices, confidence intervals, and any majority-class baseline against an implied 60.0% floor." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-110" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Inherited and described at second hand, but more informatively than by the other reuses. Children watched videos built to attract them - colourful balloons, animated characters, elements whose position changed during the study - and some videos included a live host who spoke to the child and attempted to direct attention towards both visible and hidden elements. That last detail matters for interpretation: a live host issuing verbal direction makes the task partly a joint-attention and instruction-following probe rather than pure free viewing, so the resulting scan-paths reflect responsiveness to social direction as well as spontaneous visual preference. What is not stated in this paper is how many videos each child saw, how long they lasted, how a session was segmented into the roughly nine scan-paths per child, or how gaze coordinates were rendered into images - the last being the determinant of what a convolutional network can extract from the drawing." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-110" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Acquisition equipment is inherited and specified: SMI Experiment Center Software with a Red-m remote eye tracker sampling at 250 Hz, producing images at 640 x 480. That is a high sampling rate and a named commercial system, which is more than several primary studies in this corpus provide about their own equipment. The analysis stack is given in full: Python 3.6.8 with Keras on TensorFlow, OpenCV, scikit-learn and Matplotlib, running on an Intel Core i7 tenth-generation machine with 16 GB RAM and an NVIDIA GeForce MX230. No calibration procedure, tracking-quality criterion or rendering specification is available." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-110" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Information 15(3), article 133, MDPI, open access under CC BY; received 22 January 2024, revised 21 February, accepted 26 February, published 28 February 2024 - a 37-day turnaround from submission to publication. The venue is a general information-science journal rather than a clinical, biomedical or specialist machine-learning one, which is consistent with the paper's shape: twelve tables of hyperparameter sweeps, extensive tutorial exposition of convolutional network components, and no limitations section, ethics statement of its own, funding declaration or competing-interests statement. The compressed review period is worth noting given that the paper's central results table contains an unreconciled arithmetic inconsistency between accuracy and the class-conditional metrics." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-110" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Published 2024 by the Department of Artificial Intelligence at the University of Jordan with Princess Sumaya University for Technology, Amman. The framing is the familiar cost-and-expertise argument, developed at unusual length: diagnosis requires specialised professionals using observation, interviews and questionnaires; current tools are time-consuming because of 'the extensive number of items that need to be checked by the specialist' and rely on 'static human-embedded rules'; and because 'each specialist relies on their own expertise and experience to diagnose autism spectrum disorder', the process suffers 'inconsistencies and a lack of reliability'. The introduction also engages with delayed diagnosis and its causes - limited resources in rural areas and low-income families, shortage of specialist referrals, parental awareness - which locates the work in the access literature. For this review the study's value is as the fifth reuse of the 547-image dataset and as the clearest instance of a specific reporting hazard. It is the only reuse to report sensitivity and specificity alongside accuracy, and those class-conditional metrics imply roughly 79% - close to the independent transfer-learning reuse at 81%, to the one-image-per-child leave-one-out figure of 72.88%, and to the source authors' own participant-level correction near 71%. The 95.59% headline is irreconcilable with them. Whichever number is correct, the paper illustrates why a review of this literature must recompute rather than accept reported accuracies, and why the near-ceiling figures that dominate this dataset's reuse literature should be treated as claims requiring arithmetic verification." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-110" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No dedicated future-work content in the retrieved text beyond the conclusion's framing that early-detection tools should be 'brief, easily understandable for parents, and easy to administer by healthcare professionals' and that eye tracking suits that purpose. Absent, and needed: reconciliation of the reported metrics, participant-level cross-validation, a held-out confirmation of the selected hyperparameter configuration, a majority-class baseline, and use of the available CARS scores to test whether classification tracks severity." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-110" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'Children with ASD' and 'typically developing (TD) children' predominate, person-first, alongside 'individuals with ASD' and 'people with ASD'. Autism is 'a developmental disorder that encompasses difficulties in communication (both verbal and non-verbal), social skills, and repetitive behaviors' and 'a neurodevelopmental disorder that impacts the behavioral and cognitive abilities of children'. The register is medical-model but not pejorative, and the introduction is unusually attentive to autistic people's communicative experience for an engineering paper - it discusses difficulty grasping 'deeper implications in dialogues, resulting in literal understandings and struggles in recognizing indirect messages like sarcasm', and notes that 'clear and specific instructions are necessary for their understanding'. It also engages seriously with heterogeneity, comorbidity (ADHD, anxiety, mood disorders) and the interpersonal-coordination framing of diagnostic conversation. There is no neurodiversity framing and no autistic-community involvement. One framing choice should be flagged: early detection is motivated partly by 'reduced costs for both individuals and society as a whole', a cost-benefit argument that recurs across this corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-110" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Visual attention during video viewing, encoded as a drawn trajectory. The behavioural rationale offered is the standard one - that autistic children show atypical visual attention, particularly towards human faces - and the introduction develops it further than most reuses of this dataset, discussing atypical preference for geometric images as a marker of a more severely affected subtype and noting that children with both autism and ADHD show shorter fixation durations on faces than children with autism alone. None of this is operationalised: no areas of interest, no fixation durations, no face-versus-object contrast is computed, because the input is a picture of a path and the model receives nothing else. The source protocol's inclusion of a live host directing the child's attention means the recorded behaviour also reflects responsiveness to verbal social direction, which the paper does not discuss. As with the other scan-path-image studies in this corpus, the representation makes behavioural properties (where the child looked) and incidental graphical properties (how much of the canvas the drawing covers, how dense the overplotting is) indistinguishable to the model, and no analysis separates them." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-110" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A network the paper calls T-CNN-ASD, described as a convolutional deep neural network but parameterised and reported entirely in terms of hidden-layer neuron counts, from a single layer of 50-600 neurons up to two layers of 500 and 250. The characterisation is internally confused: kernel size, stride and pooling are swept as hyperparameters, which implies convolution, yet the preprocessing explicitly flattens the 100 x 100 image 'from 3D to 1D' before the model, which removes the two-dimensional adjacency structure convolution operates on, and no layer diagram or channel specification is given. The comparison set is four classical classifiers - k-nearest neighbours, decision tree, random forest and multilayer perceptron - of which the multilayer perceptron reaches 94.69%, within one point of the proposed model, suggesting that whatever is driving performance is available to a plain feed-forward network on flattened pixels. The most informative modelling result is the optimiser sweep: Adam gives 95.59% and Nadam 95.07%, but SGD and Adadelta collapse to 56-62% across every architecture tried. A 35-point dependence on optimiser choice, on a fixed dataset with a fixed architecture family, indicates a result that is a property of the optimisation trajectory as much as of the data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-110" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Extensively reported on the computational side and unverifiable on the numerical side. Reproducible from the text: the public source dataset with participant statistics, stimulus description, eye tracker and native image size; the full preprocessing chain from 640 x 480 colour through 100 x 100 greyscale to a flattened one-dimensional vector, with figures; the complete hyperparameter grids for kernel size, stride, pooling, batch size, optimiser and architecture, each reporting accuracy for every cell; the winning configuration in full; and the software and hardware. Not reproducible: code, the cross-validation implementation and seed, the image-to-child mapping - the one fact needed to assess whether any evaluation is participant-disjoint - and, most seriously, the headline numbers themselves, since the reported accuracy cannot be derived from the reported sensitivity and specificity at the stated class sizes." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-110" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No fusion of any kind - a single modality rendered as a single image type, classified by a single network. The only representational variation tested is greyscale versus colour, with greyscale giving marginally better results (95.59% versus 94.69%). The paper's related-work section notes that others have combined eye tracking with kinematic data to gain 5-8 accuracy points, and that gaze features combined with demographic descriptors such as age improved classification with the observation that 'the average classification accuracy decreased when age was excluded as a feature in all tested classifiers' - a finding it reports without recognising its implication for its own work, namely that gaze-based classifiers in this literature draw part of their performance from age information. Since the present study's dataset carries age and CARS at the participant level and uses neither, both a fusion experiment and a confound check were available and not attempted." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-110" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional by inheritance, with no temporal analysis. Ethics is handled by reference to the source study: procedures 'were conducted following the ethical standards set by the institutional and/or national research committee, as well as in compliance with the 1964 Helsinki Declaration and its subsequent amendments'. Citing the originating study's ethical basis is appropriate practice for a secondary analysis and is more than most dataset-reuse papers in this corpus do. No funding statement and no competing-interests declaration appear in the retrieved text." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-110" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No study setting: a computational reanalysis at two universities in Amman, Jordan, of images collected in France. The source acquisition setting is described in more detail than in the other reuses of this dataset and is worth recording, because it bears on what the scan-paths represent: a quiet room at university premises, physical white barriers erected to reduce visual distraction, the child seated alone or on a parent's lap approximately 60 cm from the screen, and all interaction conducted in French, the participants' first language. Typically developing children came from schools in the Hauts-de-France region and autistic children from specialised clinics - the standard clinic-versus-school case-control geometry, inherited rather than chosen." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-110" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Fifty-nine children producing 547 scan-path images - the fifth independent reuse of this dataset encountered in the review. Unlike the other reuses charted here, this paper reports the source participant statistics in a dedicated table: 29 autistic and 30 typically developing children, 38 male (64%) and 21 female (36%), mean age 7.88 years and median 8.1, with CARS mean 32.97 and median 34.50. The image split is 219 autistic and 328 non-autistic, so roughly nine images per child. None of the demographic or clinical variables is broken down by diagnostic group, so it cannot be determined whether the groups were matched on age or sex - the sex ratio of 64% male across the whole sample is compatible with anything from a balanced split to a heavily male autistic group and a balanced comparison group. The 547 images, not the 59 children, are the unit of cross-validation throughout." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-110" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The stated aim is to build an enhanced convolutional deep network for autism identification from eye-tracking scan-path images, with an explicit methodological claim distinguishing it from prior work: 'we addressed the issue of working with a dataset containing a small number of images without utilizing augmentation. Whereas many earlier studies depended on augmenting image data to improve outcomes, we maintained the original image count and achieved superior performance.' That is a defensible and welcome position, since augmentation on a 547-image corpus split at image level compounds dependence between training and test data, as another study in this corpus demonstrates directly. Findings: 95.59% accuracy for greyscale images with two hidden layers of 300 and 150 neurons, kernel size 3, stride 1, Adam optimiser and 20% dropout; 94.69% with colour. Comparators on the same data reach 61.3% for k-nearest neighbours, 67.7% for decision tree, 69.60% for random forest and 94.69% for a multilayer perceptron. Optimiser choice dominates everything else: Adam and Nadam give 81-95%, RMSprop and AdaMax 85-94%, while SGD and Adadelta collapse to 56-62% - a 35-point spread that indicates the result is highly sensitive to optimisation rather than robustly present in the data. The decisive problem is that the same results table reports sensitivity 77.60% and specificity 79.91% alongside the 95.59% accuracy, and those three figures cannot describe the same classifier: with 219 autistic and 328 non-autistic images they imply an accuracy near 79%." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-110" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two problems compound, and a third makes the result difficult to interpret at all. First, cross-validation partitions the 547 images rather than the 59 children. With roughly nine images per child and no statement that a child's images were kept within a fold, each held-out image is accompanied by around eight siblings from the same child in training - the same route that has produced accuracies from 81% to over 99% across five reuses of this identical dataset, against the source authors' own participant-level figure near 71% and an independent one-image-per-child leave-one-out result of 72.88%. Second, the reported figure is the maximum of an extremely large hyperparameter search: separate sweeps over kernel size, stride, pooling, batch size, optimiser and thirteen architecture configurations, well over a hundred cells in total, every one scored on the same cross-validation with the best bolded and carried forward. No held-out confirmation of the selected configuration is performed, and the 35-point spread between optimisers on identical data shows how much room that search had to exploit. Third, and most consequentially, the headline accuracy cannot be reconciled with the sensitivity and specificity reported for the same model. At 219 autistic and 328 non-autistic images, 77.60% sensitivity and 79.91% specificity give approximately 79.0% accuracy. That implied figure is strikingly consistent with the other honest estimates on this dataset - 81.0% from an independent transfer-learning study, 72.88% from one-image-per-child leave-one-out, and roughly 71% from the source authors' own participant-level correction - which suggests the class-conditional metrics may be the trustworthy ones and the accuracy column an artefact. Either way, the paper reports two irreconcilable characterisations of its own classifier and reconciles neither, so no single number from it can be carried into a synthesis without that caveat." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-110" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: autism spectrum disorder (ASD); convolutional neural network (CNN); deep learning; eye-tracking scan path; multi-layer perceptron (MLP); machine learning (ML). Five of six are method labels and none names an application, although the body text speaks of automatic classification, early detection and biomarkers throughout. The coined model name T-CNN-ASD appears in the title and abstract and functions as branding for what is architecturally a small feed-forward network over flattened pixels." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-110" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "There is no limitations section and no constraint of any kind is acknowledged; the paper's self-assessment is uniformly favourable. The problems are correspondingly unaddressed and one is arithmetic rather than methodological. First, the reported metrics are mutually incompatible: Table 9 gives the proposed model 95.59% accuracy, 77.60% sensitivity and 79.91% specificity, but with 219 autistic and 328 non-autistic images those sensitivity and specificity values imply an accuracy of approximately 79.0%. The same holds for the multilayer perceptron comparator (94.69% reported against an implied 73.6%). Either the accuracy figures come from a different computation than the class-conditional ones, or one set is in error; the paper does not reconcile them, and a reader cannot determine which number describes the model. Second, the quantity labelled F1 is defined in Equation (4) as the harmonic mean of sensitivity and specificity, which is not F1. Third, cross-validation partitions the 547 images rather than the 59 children, so each held-out image has roughly eight siblings from the same child in training - the recurring leakage route in this dataset's reuse literature. Fourth, more than a hundred hyperparameter configurations were evaluated on that same cross-validation and the maximum reported as the result, with no independent confirmation; the 35-point spread between optimisers shows how much of the outcome depends on that search. Fifth, images are flattened to one dimension before entering a model described throughout as convolutional. Sixth, no majority-class baseline is computed against an implied 60.0% floor. And the CARS severity data the dataset carries, and which the introduction's discussion of severity-linked subtypes invites using, are reported and then ignored." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-110" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Better than the other reuses of this dataset, which typically report nothing, but still incomplete and entirely inherited. Table 1 gives, for the sample as a whole: 59 participants, 38 male and 21 female (64%/36%), 29 autistic and 30 typically developing, mean age 7.88 years and median 8.1, and CARS mean 32.97 and median 34.50. No variable is reported by group, so age matching, sex balance between groups and the severity distribution within the autistic group are all unavailable. The CARS figures nonetheless carry information the paper does not use: a mean of 32.97 with median 34.50 places the autistic sample in the mild-to-moderate band on that instrument, which characterises the spectrum position of the group better than most studies in this corpus manage - and the paper's own introduction notes that eye-tracking markers have been linked to a more severely affected subtype, so relating CARS to model output was both possible and thematically invited. Nothing else is available: no IQ, language level, comorbidity, medication or socioeconomic data, and no race or ethnicity beyond the participants being French-speaking children from the Hauts-de-France region." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-110" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Inherited and thinly reported, though better specified than in the other reuses of this dataset. The autistic children 'were evaluated in specialized clinics, and the level of autism in each child was gauged using the Childhood Autism Rating Scale (CARS)', while typically developing children were recruited from mainstream French schools. Two distinctions matter. CARS is described as gauging the level of autism rather than establishing the diagnosis, so it functions here as a severity measure applied after specialist clinic evaluation - and that evaluation is named but not described, with no diagnostic criteria, DSM or ICD version, clinician discipline or procedure given. And the comparison group's status rests on school recruitment with no screening instrument reported, so undetected autistic traits in that group cannot be excluded. For the present study these are inherited rather than authored deficiencies, but they define the target being predicted and the paper does not flag them as open." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-110" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A 100 x 100 greyscale image flattened to a 10,000-element vector, with a parallel colour variant. Three transformations separate the raw gaze data from the model input, each discarding information: rendering the coordinate time series as a static drawing removes temporal order, fixation duration and saccade velocity; downscaling from 640 x 480 to 100 x 100 means each output pixel spans roughly 6 x 5 source pixels, so fine spatial resolution of where on a face gaze landed is lost; and flattening removes spatial adjacency. What survives is the coarse geometry and density of the drawn path. The greyscale conversion is reported to work slightly better than colour, which is consistent with colour carrying no information in these renderings - but the rendering scheme is not described, so whether colour originally encoded anything (order, duration, which eye) cannot be determined." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-110" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "CARS scores are available and reported at the sample level - mean 32.97, median 34.50 - which makes this the only reuse of this dataset in the corpus to carry any severity information at all. That is a genuine asset and it is entirely unused: no analysis relates CARS to model output, no within-autism severity gradient is examined, and no check is made on whether misclassified images belong to less severely affected children. The omission is more conspicuous because the paper's own introduction sets up exactly this question, noting that 'an atypical preference for geometric images can serve as an early indicator of a specific subtype of autism spectrum disorder, which is linked to more severe symptoms', and that comorbid ADHD alters fixation durations on faces. Both observations invite a severity or subtype analysis on data the study already holds. No cognitive, language or adaptive measure is available for any participant." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-110" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No saliency map, class activation, filter visualisation, error analysis or example inspection is performed for any model. The discussion's account of why the proposed model outperforms the alternatives is entirely generic and borrowed from the image-processing literature - that convolutional networks are 'successfully used for image denoising' and that convolution 'reduces the high dimensionality of images without losing its information' - with no reference to anything observed in these scan-paths. The absence is compounded by the flattening step, which means the model is not exploiting spatial structure in the way that explanation assumes. For a study whose entire input is a picture of a behaviour, and where incidental graphical properties are as available to the network as behavioural ones, a single class activation map over correctly and incorrectly classified scan-paths would have been the most informative addition possible." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-110" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:study_design "Secondary computational analysis of a public eye-tracking scan-path dataset; a hyperparameter-optimisation and model-comparison study aimed at maximising classification accuracy on…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: ; comparison: 30 children, 328 images" ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "8f6734a1708f0b8032d251598cc4eca9bb56993ceb108a245497f65392cc7f29" ;
