    slr:journal "Information, 15(3), 133" ;
    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Participants are inherited from a public dataset with case-control geometry - autistic children from specialised clinics, comparison children from mainstream French schools. Demographics are reported only for the sample as a whole (38 male/21 female, mean age 7.88 years, CARS mean 32.97), never by group, so matching on age or sex cannot be assessed. No inclusion or exclusion criteria are stated and no clinical comparison group exists." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Cross-validation partitions the 547 images rather than the 59 children, with roughly nine images per child and no statement that a child's images were kept within a fold. No participant-level result is reported. Most seriously, the reported accuracy cannot be reconciled with the reported sensitivity and specificity at the stated class sizes, so the flow from data to reported figures cannot be verified." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Inherited and reported at second hand: specialist clinic evaluation, named but not described, with CARS used to gauge severity rather than to diagnose; no criteria, DSM or ICD version, or clinician description. Comparison-group status rests on school recruitment with no screening instrument reported." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "More than a hundred hyperparameter configurations were evaluated on the same cross-validation used to report performance, spanning kernel size, stride, pooling, batch size, optimiser and thirteen architectures, with the maximum reported and no held-out confirmation. The 35-point spread between optimisers on identical data indicates substantial sensitivity to that search. The model is described as convolutional while the input is explicitly flattened to one dimension." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "high" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<https://doi.org/10.3390/jcm9051260> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Autism spectrum disorder (ASD) is mostly diagnosed according to behavioral symptoms in sensory, social, and motor domains. Improper motor functioning, during diagnosis, involves the qualitative evaluation of stereotyped and repetitive behaviors, while quantitative methods that classify body movements’ frequencies of children with ASD are less addressed. Recent advances in neuroscience, technology, and data analysis techniques are improving the quantitative and ecological validity methods to measure speciﬁc functioning in ASD children. On one side, cutting-edge technologies, such as cameras, sensors, and virtual reality can accurately detect and classify behavioral biomarkers, as body movements in real-life simulations. On the other, machine-learning techniques are showing the potential for identifying and classifying patients’ subgroups. Starting from these premises, three real-simulated imitation tasks have been implemented in a virtual reality system whose aim is to investigate if machine-learning methods on movement features and frequency could be useful in discriminating ASD children from children with typical neurodevelopment. In this experiment, 24 children with ASD and 25 children with typical neurodevelopment participated in a multimodal virtual reality experience, and changes in their body movements were tracked by a depth sensor camera during the presentation of visual, auditive, and olfactive stimuli. The main results showed that ASD children presented larger body movements than TD children, and that head, trunk, and feet represent the maximum classiﬁcation with an accuracy of 82.98%. Regarding stimuli, visual condition showed the highest accuracy (89.36%), followed by the visual-auditive stimuli (74.47%), and visual-auditive-olfactory stimuli (70.21%). Finally, the head showed the most consistent performance along with the stimuli, from 80.85% in visual to 89.36% in visual-auditive-olfactory condition. The ﬁndings showed the feasibility of applying machine learning and virtual reality to identify body movements’ biomarkers that could contribute to improving ASD diagnosis." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Alcañiz Raya M; Marín-Morales J; Minissi ME; Teruel Garcia G; Abad L; Chicchi Giglioli IA (2020). Machine Learning and Virtual Reality on Body Movements' Behaviors to Classify Children with Autism Spectrum Disorder. *Journal of Clinical Medicine, 9(5), 1260*. https://doi.org/10.3390/jcm9051260" ;
    dcterms:creator "Abad L",
        "Alcañiz Raya M",
        "Chicchi Giglioli IA",
        "Marín-Morales J",
        "Minissi ME",
        "Teruel Garcia G" ;
    dcterms:title "Machine Learning and Virtual Reality on Body Movements' Behaviors to Classify Children with Autism Spectrum Disorder" ;
    bibo:doi "10.3390/jcm9051260" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_d3852378" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2020 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the comparison group properly assessed?" ;
                            slr:justification "Typically developing participants also presented ADOS-2 and ADI-R assessment reports." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "47-49 children with no held-out test set possible, as the authors acknowledge." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "Not matched on IQ, cognitive ability or motor comorbidity by the authors' own statement, and sex differs by more than 20 percentage points on a motor outcome." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Group sizes, ages with SDs, sex splits and both recruitment routes are stated." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "CAVE geometry, projectors, sound and scent hardware, camera, pose estimator, joint indices, confidence cutoff and displacement computation are all specified." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "ADOS-2 and ADI-R applied to both groups, but no scores reported and module 1 used for all participants including typically developing 4-7 year olds." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Does the measured quantity match the construct claimed?" ;
                            slr:justification "Mean inter-frame displacement is a magnitude, described throughout as movement 'frequency'; nothing in the pipeline measures rate, rhythm or repetitiveness, though repetitive behaviour is the target construct." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the stimulus design theoretically motivated?" ;
                            slr:justification "The cumulative sensory ladder follows the hyper/hypo-sensitivity literature and its rationale is stated explicitly." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are preprocessing and feature selection nested within folds?" ;
                            slr:justification "Normalisation statistics from training folds only; SVM-RFE performed per training fold with median ranks across folds." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is uncertainty quantified?" ;
                            slr:justification "No confidence intervals or between-model tests, on a sample where one child is worth 2.13 accuracy points." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the group comparison test appropriate?" ;
                            slr:justification "Wilcoxon signed-rank, a paired test, applied to two independent groups where Mann-Whitney U is required." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the validation split at the participant level?" ;
                            slr:justification "Leave-one-subject-out throughout." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are all model configurations reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "All 24 body-region by stimulus-condition models tabulated with accuracy, TPR, TNR, kappa and feature counts." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is model selection separated from performance estimation?" ;
                            slr:justification "Hyperparameters tuned to maximise Cohen's kappa on the same leave-one-out estimate, and the headline chosen from 24 configurations." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Toolboxes and versions named; no code." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is participant flow accounted for?" ;
                            slr:justification "Accuracy denominators imply 47 analysed children against 49 stated, with two autistic participants unexplained." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are limitations stated candidly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Sample size, unmatched IQ and motor comorbidity, absent other-disorder comparison groups and uncounterbalanced stimulus order are all named, with their consequences spelled out." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No release or availability statement." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Written caregiver consent, named ethics committee approval, and Helsinki Declaration conformity." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Acknowledged with unusual candour: the sample is small and precludes a genuine test set; autism's heterogeneity means larger training groups would be needed for generalisation; other neurodevelopmental comparison groups are absent, so the classifier may separate neurodevelopmental difference generally rather than autism specifically; the groups were not matched on IQ, cognitive ability or motor comorbidities, which the authors say directly limits attributing the differences to autism; and the stimulus order was not counterbalanced. Not acknowledged: the Wilcoxon signed-rank test is a paired test applied here to independent groups, where Mann-Whitney U is required, so the reported per-joint significances are unverified; 24 models were built and hyperparameters tuned to maximise kappa on the same leave-one-out estimate reported as the result, making 89.36% a best-of-many figure on 47 children where one child is worth 2.13 points; the sex distributions differ substantially on a motor outcome; the analysed sample appears to be 47 not 49; and the quantity called 'frequency' throughout is mean displacement, a magnitude." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-045" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A three-surface CAVE of 4 x 4 x 3 m at the Institute for Research and Innovation in Bioengineering, Polytechnic University of Valencia. Autistic participants came from Red Cenit, a Valencia cognitive development centre; controls were recruited by a management company through family mailings. Funded by the Spanish Ministry of Economy, Industry and Competitiveness ('Immersive virtual environment for the evaluation and training of children with autism spectrum disorder: T Room'), the Generalitat Valenciana REBRAND project, and ERDF Valencian Community funds." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-045" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Whole-body movement during imitation, decomposed anatomically into head, trunk, arms, legs and feet, and measured as mean inter-frame joint displacement. The behavioural target is repetitive and stereotyped motor behaviour - the DSM criterion domain the paper argues is under-studied relative to social communication - operationalised as movement magnitude under graded sensory provocation rather than as counts of identified stereotypies. Uniquely in the corpus, the behaviour is elicited by a designed sensory manipulation rather than merely observed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-045" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Journal of Clinical Medicine (MDPI), 9(5), 1260 - an open-access clinical journal, matching the paper's clinical framing and its detailed treatment of ADOS-2 and ADI-R." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-045" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Autistic group: n = 24, 21 male and 3 female (87.5% male), mean age 5.13 +/- 1.35 years. Typically developing group: n = 25, 16 male and 9 female (64% male), mean age 4.86 +/- 0.91 years. Overall range 4-7 years. The age difference is small but untested and the spreads differ. The sex imbalance is substantial and unaddressed, which matters because the dependent variable is body movement magnitude. Critically, the authors state plainly that 'the experimental groups were not matched on measures related to the intelligence quotient performance, cognitive abilities, and motor comorbidities (e.g., dyspraxia)' and that this limits confidence that the observed differences are attributable to autism. No ethnicity or socioeconomic reporting; single site in Valencia." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-045" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A conventional and appropriately conservative pipeline for 47 participants: PCA at 95% variance retention, then C-SVM with a Gaussian kernel, with nonlinear SVM-RFE including correlation bias reduction as a wrapper inside each training fold. No deep learning is used for classification - OpenPose supplies the deep component as a fixed, externally trained pose estimator, which is the right division of labour at this sample size. Hyperparameters were searched over seven logarithmically spaced values of cost and gamma between 0.1 and 1000." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-045" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2020. The framing is a critique of the gold standard on two grounds simultaneously - objectivity and ecological validity. On objectivity: 'since in ADOS examiner has to judge a child's performance giving scoring and rating, evaluation relies on the examiner's expertise and subjectivity; likewise, in ADI-R ASD diagnosis is based on caregivers' reports rather than on objective evaluation'. On ecology: the limitation is 'the absence of both objective assessment methods and the ecological validity of the setting'. Virtual reality is offered as addressing both at once - a controlled, repeatable environment that is nonetheless a simulated real-life scene, with sensory content that can be manipulated in ways a clinic room cannot. The paper positions itself as 'the first attempt to discriminate ASD children from children with typical neurodevelopment using body movements parameters of an imitation VR task and machine learning', and situates repetitive behaviour as an under-studied criterion domain relative to social communication." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-045" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Larger samples permitting a held-out test set and better coverage of autism's heterogeneity; comparison groups with other neurodevelopmental conditions involving movement impairment - ADHD and intellectual disability are named - specifically so that 'taking also into account different neurodevelopment conditions could improve the specificity of the classifiers for ASD rather than for neurodevelopment disorders in general'; matching on IQ, cognitive ability and motor comorbidity; and counterbalanced stimulus orders stratified by functioning level." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-045" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: determine whether body movement during multimodal VR imitation tasks discriminates autistic from typically developing children, which body regions carry the signal, and which sensory conditions best elicit it. Main findings: 15 joints differed significantly with autistic children moving more in 13 of them, concentrated in head and legs; best classification 89.36% (all joints, visual condition; and head joints, visual-auditory-olfactory condition); head is the most consistent region and improves with increasing sensory load; whole-body accuracy declines with increasing sensory load; the all-joints all-conditions model performs worst at 70.21%; and every model needed three or fewer features." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-045" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Avatar imitation in a projected virtual city street, under three cumulative sensory conditions - visual (waving), visual-auditory (dancing to a disco track), and visual-auditory-olfactory (biting a buttered muffin with a butter scent released by a wireless diffuser). The design is the most theoretically motivated stimulus escalation in the corpus: the sensory ladder follows the autism sensory literature directly, adding modalities to which autistic children are reported hypersensitive (visual, auditory) before one to which they are reported hyposensitive (olfactory), on the hypothesis that sensory load provokes repetitive behaviour. The imitation framing is also apt, since imitation deficits are independently documented. Order was fixed rather than counterbalanced, deliberately, to avoid sensory overload - a defensible clinical choice that the authors correctly flag as a methodological cost." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-045" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; a single 14-minute session with three 2-minute-40-second conditions each preceded by a 2-minute baseline, no follow-up. Data are depth video of identifiable children reduced to joint coordinates. Governance is properly reported: written information and written consent from family caregivers, approval by the Ethical Committee of the Polytechnic University of Valencia, and conformity with institutional and national committee standards and the 1964 Helsinki Declaration." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-045" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Mean inter-frame Euclidean displacement per joint, aggregated by body region and stimulus condition into a 24-cell factorial of datasets, each reduced by PCA then by RFE to three or fewer retained features. The representation is deliberately minimal - a single scalar per joint per condition - which is defensible at this sample size but discards everything about the temporal structure, rhythmicity or repetitiveness of the movement, despite repetitive behaviour being the construct the study is about. The paper's own terminology reflects the gap: it calls the measure 'frequency' throughout, but nothing in the pipeline computes a rate or a period." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-045" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Good on apparatus and pipeline, thin on results provenance. Reported: CAVE dimensions and projector specification, viewing distance, condition durations and baselines, camera model and SDK, OpenPose joint indices with their anatomical mapping, the 0.5 confidence cutoff, the displacement computation, PCA variance threshold, SVM kernel and search grid, the LOSO normalisation scheme, the nested RFE with correlation bias reduction, the optimisation criterion, and the software versions. Missing: any data or code release; ADOS-2 and ADI-R scores; frame rate of the depth camera; the proportion of joints discarded by the confidence threshold; an explanation for the two autistic children apparently absent from the analysis; and any account of how the 24 reported models relate to the number actually run." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-045" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The cross-validation architecture is correct: leave-one-subject-out folds, normalisation statistics computed on training folds only and applied to the held-out child, and SVM-RFE performed within each fold's training set with median ranks aggregated across folds. There is no participant-level leakage and no feature selection on the full dataset. What sits outside the loop is model selection - both the SVM hyperparameters ('the model was optimized to achieve best Cohen's kappa') and the choice of which of 24 configurations to headline were determined by the same leave-one-out estimate reported as the result. On 47 children this is a large source of optimism. The authors recognise the underlying constraint, noting that larger samples 'could allow validating the ML method, providing also the possibility to test the model' - an acknowledgement that no independent test was possible here." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-045" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: autism spectrum disorder; body movements; repetitive behaviors; virtual reality; machine learning. The inclusion of 'repetitive behaviors' locates the work in a specific DSM criterion domain rather than in autism generally, and the paper argues that this domain has been comparatively neglected - 'ASD studies primarily examine the weaknesses in social interaction abilities, and less on the stereotyped and repetitive motor behaviors'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-045" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No model-explanation method, but the factorial design itself is the interpretive instrument, and it yields anatomically specific conclusions: the head is the most consistently discriminative region and becomes more so under multisensory load, while arms and legs contribute less. This is supported independently by the joint-level statistical analysis showing more movement in autistic children at 13 of 15 significant joints, concentrated in head and legs, which the authors connect to documented stereotypies of head tilting and leg flapping. The interpretation is therefore anchored in observable anatomy rather than in abstract features - though the underlying group comparison uses an inappropriate paired test." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-045" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "CAVE with three ceiling ultra-short-throw projectors (100-degree image from 55 cm) and a Logitech Z906 5.1 sound system; Olorama Technology wireless scent diffuser with 12 UDP-triggered channels and programmable fan control of duration and intensity; Intel RealSense D435 depth camera with SDK 2.0; OpenPose for 25-joint pose estimation on an Nvidia GTX1060; MATLAB R2016a with LIBSVM. The scent delivery hardware is unique in this corpus and is what makes the olfactory condition possible." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-045" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups: 24 autistic children recruited through Red Cenit, a cognitive development centre in Valencia, and 25 typically developing children recruited by a management company through mailings to families. Both groups presented individual assessment reports including ADOS-2 and ADI-R, so the comparison group is instrument-assessed rather than merely unscreened. No other-diagnosis comparison group, and the authors identify this as a specificity limitation, proposing ADHD and intellectual disability comparisons for future work. Note that the results tables imply 22 rather than 24 autistic children were analysed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-045" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Both groups presented individual assessment reports including ADOS-2 and ADI-R, and both instruments are described in detail with their test-retest reliabilities (ADOS-2: 0.87 social affect, 0.64 repetitive behaviour, 0.88 global; ADI-R: 0.93-0.97). Applying the same instruments to both groups is a genuine strength. Two qualifications: no ADOS-2 or ADI-R scores, cut-offs or severity indices are reported for any participant, so the diagnostic evidence cannot be inspected; and ADOS-2 module 1 was used for all assessments, which is the module for children with little or no phrase speech - implying a minimally verbal autistic sample and making its application to typically developing 4-7 year olds difficult to interpret." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-045" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None reported, and their absence is explicitly acknowledged as a limitation. ADOS-2 and ADI-R were administered and yield severity indices in social affect and restricted/repetitive behaviour domains, but no scores appear and no severity-stratified analysis is performed. No IQ, cognitive, language or adaptive measure, and no motor comorbidity screening despite the outcome being motor." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-045" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, true positive rate, true negative rate, Cohen's kappa and retained feature count, tabulated for all 24 models. Reporting the full factorial rather than a selected best is good practice and is what allows an external reader to see both the multiplicity problem and the confusion-matrix imbalances - the pooled head model achieves 82.98% with 100% sensitivity but 68% specificity, while the pooled trunk model achieves identical accuracy with 63.64% sensitivity and 100% specificity, which the paper notes explicitly. Including Cohen's kappa is unusual in this corpus and appropriate given the near-balanced classes. Missing: confidence intervals, AUC, and any statistical comparison between models, which matters when adjacent accuracies differ by one or two children." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-045" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Multimodality here is in the stimulus rather than the sensing: a single depth camera captures movement, while visual, auditory and olfactory channels are added cumulatively to the environment. The results are informative and partly counterintuitive. For whole-body movement, discrimination is best under visual stimulation alone (89.36%) and degrades as modalities accumulate (74.47%, then 70.21%). For the head alone the pattern reverses, improving from 80.85% to 82.98% to 89.36% as sensory load rises. And pooling everything - all joints across all conditions - gives the worst result of any model at 70.21% with 45.45% sensitivity. The lesson transferable to the corpus's sensor-fusion studies is that indiscriminate aggregation across conditions and channels can destroy a localised signal." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-045" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with ASD', 'ASD children', 'children with typical development (TD)', 'children with typical neurodevelopment', 'ASD individuals', 'ASD population'. Mixed person-first and 'ASD children'. The framing is clinical but not burden-oriented; notably the paper's critique is directed at the assessment instruments rather than at the children - ADOS 'relies on the examiner's expertise and subjectivity' and ADI-R diagnosis 'is based on caregivers' reports rather than on objective evaluation'. The sensory-difference framing is also treated as explanatory rather than deficit-laden, with hyper- and hypo-sensitivity used to motivate the stimulus design." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-045" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "One session of 14 minutes total, comprising three stimulus conditions of 2 minutes 40 seconds each, with a 2-minute baseline recorded before each condition" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional case-control classification with a factorial comparison across five body regions and three cumulative sensory conditions" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Preschool and early school-age children with autism" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 24 stated in the participants section and abstract; the results tables imply 22 (true positive rates are multiples of 1/22 and accuracies multiples of 1/47), so two autistic children appear to have been dropped without explanation; comparison: 25" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Autistic children moved more than typically developing children during virtual imitation tasks, with significant differences at 15 joints and higher movement in 13 of them, concentrated in the head and legs. Machine learning over joint displacement classified the groups at up to 89.36% accuracy. Two structural findings emerge from the factorial design: the head is the most consistently discriminat",
                        "Best overall 89.36% (kappa 0.79) for all joints in the visual condition, with 100% TPR and 80% TNR, from a single retained feature out of 14; equal 89.36% (kappa 0.78) for head joints in the visual-auditory-olfactory condition with 77.27% TPR and 100% TNR. Pooled across conditions, head, trunk and feet each reach 82.98% (kappas 0.67, 0.65, 0.66), arms 74.47% and legs 72.34%. Accuracy declines as s" ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy, true positive rate, true negative rate, Cohen's kappa and the number of PCA features retained, tabulated for all 24 models - a full factorial report rather than a headline figure. Reporting TPR and TNR separately is what makes the confusion-matrix imbalance visible: the pooled head model r" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Best overall 89.36% (kappa 0.79) for all joints in the visual condition, with 100% TPR and 80% TNR, from a single retained feature out of 14; equal 89.36% (kappa 0.78) for head joints in the visual-auditory-olfactory condition with 77.27% TPR and 100% TNR. Pooled across conditions, head, trunk and feet each reach 82.98% (kappas 0.67, 0.65, 0.66), arms 74.47% and legs 72.34%. Accuracy declines as s; Autistic children moved more than typically developing children during virtual imitation tasks, with significant differences at 15 joints and higher movement in 13 of them, concentrated in the head and legs. Machine learning over joint displacement classified the groups at up to 89.36% accuracy. Two structural findings emerge from the factorial design: the head is the most consistently discriminat" ] ] ;
