    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Groups recruited through entirely different routes (a clinical development centre versus family mailings), sex distributions differ substantially (87.5% versus 64% male) on a motor outcome, and the authors state that groups were not matched on IQ, cognitive ability or motor comorbidities." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Both groups presented individual assessment reports including ADOS-2 and ADI-R, applied identically, though no scores are reported and module 1 was used throughout." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Leave-one-subject-out folds are correctly constructed with nested normalisation and RFE, but 24 model configurations were built and hyperparameters tuned to maximise Cohen's kappa on that same leave-one-out estimate, which is then reported as the result." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "unclear" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Session structure, condition durations and baselines are precisely specified, but the results denominators imply 47 rather than the 49 stated participants, with two autistic children unaccounted for, and no data-quality reporting accompanies the 0.5 joint-confidence cutoff." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "high" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<urn:slr:article:03e7999e2993> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "In recent years, research has been demonstrating that movement analysis, utilizing machine learning methods, can be a promising aid for clinicians in supporting autism diagnostic process. Within this field of research, we aim to explore new models and delve into the detailed observation of certain features that previous literature has identified as prominent in the classification process. Our study employs a game-based tablet application to collect motor data. We use artificial neural networks to analyze raw trajectories in a \"drag and drop\" task. We compare a two-features model (utilizing only raw coordinates) with a fourfeatures model (including velocities and accelerations). The aim is to assess the effectiveness of raw data analysis and determine the impact of acceleration on autism classification. Our results revealed that both models demonstrate promising accuracy in classifying motor trajectories. The four-features model consistently outperforms the two-features model, as evidenced by accuracy values (0.90 vs. 0.76). However, our findings support the potential of raw data analysis in objectively assessing motor behaviors related to autism. While the fourfeatures model excels, the two-features model still achieves reasonable accuracy. Addressing limitations related to sample size and noise is essential for future research. Our study emphasizes the importance of integrating intelligent solutions to enhance and assist autism traditional diagnostic process and intervention, paving the way for more effective tools in assessing motor skills." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Luongo M; Simeoli R; Marocco D; Milano N; Ponticorvo M (2024). Enhancing early autism diagnosis through machine learning: Exploring raw motion data for classification. *PLOS ONE, 19(4), Article e0302238. ht*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Luongo M",
        "Marocco D",
        "Milano N",
        "Ponticorvo M",
        "Simeoli R" ;
    dcterms:title "Enhancing early autism diagnosis through machine learning: Exploring raw motion data for classification" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_e7c16f2a" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2024 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Handling of missing data and attrition" ;
                            slr:justification "Three explicit and well-reasoned inclusion rules: recording begins only when the target image is dragged, incomplete drags are excluded, and only trajectories of 20-69 points are retained so that lengths are comparable. Generated trajectories under 20 points are also excluded in the simulation. No attrition is reported among the 20 children." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participant selection and spectrum representativeness" ;
                            slr:justification "Ten autistic children from a single rehabilitation centre, with comorbidity excluded by criterion and functioning inferred from tablet use rather than measured. The group is said to span a range of severity but no score is given, so representativeness cannot be assessed. Twenty participants in total is the smallest sample among included studies." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Comparison group adequacy" ;
                            slr:justification "Ten mainstream schoolchildren selected only on parent and teacher report of no neurodevelopmental disorder, recruited explicitly without assessment of pre-existing abilities, with no screening instrument and no clinical comparison group." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Confounder control" ;
                            slr:justification "No developmental or cognitive measure for either group in a fine-motor study of 3-6 year olds; an autistic age standard deviation nearly ten times the comparison group's; the autistic children receiving psychomotor therapy at the recruiting centre while the comparison children were not; and table height adjusted by age, introducing a postural difference correlated with a variable whose spread differs between groups." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Index test definition and prespecification" ;
                            slr:justification "Two prespecified feature sets compared directly, no feature selection, and preprocessing fixed a priori and specified in full. The unreported architecture is a reporting gap rather than a prespecification failure." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reference standard quality" ;
                            slr:justification "ADOS-2-confirmed clinical diagnosis administered by professionals explicitly independent of the research team - a strength - but with no score reported and with comparison-group status resting on unstructured parent and teacher report." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome measurement blinding" ;
                            slr:justification "Coordinates are logged automatically by the game software and modelling is fully automated; no human rating enters the pipeline, and the examiner gave no instructions that could differ by group." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Measurement standardisation" ;
                            slr:justification "Identical game settings for every participant, one tablet model at a fixed 60-degree tilt, a fixed experimenter position, a single 20 Hz logging rate, and a task designed to minimise verbal and social demand so that motor behaviour is elicited without social confound. The one deviation is table height adjusted by child age." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "unclear" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Preprocessing and feature selection nesting" ;
                            slr:justification "No feature selection occurs and the rotation normalisation is a deterministic geometric transform applied uniformly, so no fitted preprocessing component exists to leak." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Sample size adequacy for the modelling" ;
                            slr:justification "Twenty children, the smallest sample among included studies, with the 738-trajectory count giving an appearance of data volume that 20 independent units do not support. The authors name sample size as their first limitation." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data splitting and leakage control" ;
                            slr:justification "Four-fold cross-validation over 738 trajectories from 20 children, with the validation set quoted in samples and no indication that folds respected child boundaries. Each validation trajectory has roughly 36 siblings from the same child, session and hand in training, and after rotation normalisation those siblings differ only in execution noise. No participant-level result is reported." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance estimation and chance level" ;
                            slr:justification "Accuracy is reported as a fold-wise distribution rather than a point estimate, and ROC curves are given for both models - better than a bare number. But no sensitivity, specificity, precision, confusion matrix, AUC value, confidence interval or chance baseline appears anywhere." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Ethics, consent and conflicts" ;
                            slr:justification "Approval by the Ethical Committee of Psychological Research of the Department of Humanities at the University of Naples Federico II, written parental informed consent, two named funders with an explicit no-role statement covering design, collection, analysis, publication decision and manuscript preparation, and a declaration of no competing interests." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Completeness of methods reporting" ;
                            slr:justification "Exceptional on task, apparatus and preprocessing - exact recruitment dates, task geometry, tablet model and tilt, seating arrangement, sampling rate, all inclusion rules, the rotation normalisation in three numbered equations, the simulation range and the seven simplification rates. Silent on model architecture, training hyperparameters, seeds and whether folds were constructed by child." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Acknowledgement of limitations" ;
                            slr:justification "Four limitations named with concrete remedies - sample size, single-device tracking, absence of longitudinal observation, and absence of complementary measures with a specific proposal to add ocular movement data. Not acknowledged: the trajectory-level partitioning, the absence of developmental assessment in either group, the differing age spread, or the psychomotor-therapy confound." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data, code and protocol availability" ;
                            slr:justification "The trajectory data are published in a public GitHub repository at a stated URL - the only open data release among included studies in this review, and one that makes the central claim independently checkable. No code or preregistration." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reporting of results and uncertainty" ;
                            slr:justification "Fold-wise accuracy distributions and ROC curves, plus correlations with p-values for the simulation analysis. But no class-conditional metrics, no AUC value, no confidence intervals and no participant-level figure." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Binary accuracy, reported as a distribution across cross-validation folds rather than as a single point, which conveys stability better than a bare figure; ROC curves for both models; and Pearson correlations with p-values for the acceleration analysis. Reporting the fold-wise distribution is good practice and is rare in this corpus. But the metric set is otherwise thin for a diagnostic claim: no sensitivity, specificity, precision, F1 or confusion matrix is given, so the error profile is unknown and it cannot be determined whether the models fail preferentially on autistic or comparison trajectories; no AUC value is quoted despite ROC curves being plotted; and no confidence interval accompanies either headline accuracy. No majority-class baseline is stated. Since the modelled unit is the trajectory rather than the child, no participant-level metric of any kind is available - which is the figure a clinical claim would require." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-118" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No formal keyword list appears in the retrieved text. The organising terms are raw motion data, motor trajectories, drag and drop, artificial neural networks, embodied cognition, sensorimotor activity, serious game, simulated trajectories, and early autism diagnosis. The framing vocabulary is drawn from developmental psychology and cognitive science rather than from machine learning, and 'raw' does substantial work in the title and throughout - the study's question is precisely whether unprocessed coordinates suffice." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-118" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Among the strongest in this review, and uniquely so on data. Reproducible from the text: exact recruitment dates, inclusion criteria for both groups, the game platform and task geometry with eight targets at 45-degree spacing, the settings panel contents and the statement that identical settings were used for all participants, the tablet model and its 60-degree tilt, the table-height range and experimenter position, the 20 Hz sampling rate, all three data-inclusion rules including the 20-69 point trajectory-length window, the rotation normalisation written out across three numbered equations with the atan2 construction and the angle-range mapping, both feature sets, the four-fold cross-validation with its 110-sample validation size, the acceleration-variation range for the simulation, and the seven trajectory-simplification rates. The trajectory data are published on GitHub at a stated URL - the only open data release among included studies here, and one that makes the central claim independently checkable. Not reported: the network architectures, training hyperparameters, seeds, and, critically, whether cross-validation folds were constructed by child or by trajectory." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-118" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional, single session per child, with recruitment compressed into five weeks. No follow-up and no test-retest reliability for any kinematic measure - which the authors identify as a limitation and address directly, recommending that 'the sample should be expanded and observed longitudinally'. Governance is complete: approval by the Ethical Committee of Psychological Research of the Department of Humanities at the University of Naples Federico II, written informed consent from parents of all participants described as guaranteeing full understanding, funding from two named sources with an explicit statement that the funders had no role in design, data collection, analysis, publication decision or manuscript preparation, and a declaration of no competing interests. The data are deposited in a public GitHub repository, which is the only open data release among the included studies in this review." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-118" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A rotation-normalised sequence of 20-69 points per trajectory. The rotation step is the representational contribution and is worked out carefully: because targets sit at eight positions 45 degrees apart, raw trajectories to different targets are not comparable, so the angle between consecutive points relative to the origin is computed with atan2, mapped from the atan2 range into 0-360 degrees, and applied as a rotation - with all three steps written out as numbered equations and the resulting endpoint coordinates given. This makes trajectories to any target directly comparable while preserving their shape, and it is the kind of preprocessing that reflects thought about the construct rather than convention. The two-feature model then receives only these rotated coordinates and the four-feature model adds velocity and acceleration. Crucially, unlike the group's companion study on a different sample - which averaged twelve kinematic features across a task to give one vector per child - this study preserves the full temporal sequence, so the model sees the unfolding of the movement rather than its summary. The 20-69 point length window standardises comparability at the cost of discarding both very brief and very extended drags." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-118" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Published 2024 by the Natural and Artificial Cognition Lab in the Department of Humanistic Study at the University of Naples Federico II with the Neapolisanit S.R.L. Research and Rehabilitation Center, funded by a PNRR MUR project and a University of Naples internal line. Three of the five authors also appear on the variational-autoencoder study charted elsewhere in this review, and both studies recruit autistic participants from the same rehabilitation centre - though the samples, tasks and analyses differ, so this is a related programme rather than a reanalysis. The framing is a clinical-gap argument rather than an accuracy claim: motor differences are prevalent in autism and appear before language and social difficulties, yet they are treated as secondary symptoms and 'there are no adequate tools available to accurately assess motor function in children with autism' - so the contribution sought is an instrument for something currently unmeasured, not a replacement for something already measured. That is a more defensible position than most in this corpus and it is grounded in embodied-cognition theory rather than in appeals to objectivity. For the review the study's value is threefold. It is the only included study to publish its data openly, at a stated GitHub URL, which makes its central claim independently checkable and sets a standard the rest of the corpus does not meet. Its causal-manipulation interpretability method - generating synthetic trajectories with systematically varied acceleration and measuring how predictions respond - is a template other studies could adopt in place of post-hoc attribution. And its incidental finding that a model without acceleration as an input still correlates with acceleration at r = 0.69 is a useful counterweight to the assumption, common across this literature, that a feature-engineering gain means additional information rather than easier access to information already present. Set against these, its 738 trajectories come from 20 children and are partitioned by trajectory rather than by child, so the headline 0.90 is not an estimate of performance on an unseen child - and with only 20 participants, leave-one-child-out validation would have been trivial to perform." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-118" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'Children with autism', 'children with a diagnosis of autism' and 'individuals with ASD', person-first and consistent, alongside 'typically developing (TD) children'. Autism is 'a complex and heterogeneous neurodevelopmental disorder' with the standard two-domain description, and motor differences are called 'motor abnormalities' and 'motor disorders'. The framing is otherwise notably measured and contains one argument worth recording: the paper's opening critique is that autism diagnosis 'is based on subjective observations of behavior' focused on the two core domains, and that 'many other characteristics are overlooked in the diagnostic process, considered non-central to the disorder' - specifically motor characteristics, which 'have always been considered secondary symptoms and not closely related to the syndrome' despite evidence that they precede language and social difficulties. That is an argument for widening what is measured rather than for replacing clinicians, and it is grounded in embodied-cognition theory rather than in an appeal to objectivity alone. The paper also reports the literature on visuospatial ability in autism honestly as conflicting - some studies finding deficits, some no difference, some better performance - rather than selecting the supportive citations. There is no neurodiversity framing and no autistic-community involvement." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-118" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two artificial neural networks trained on sequential data, differing only in input dimensionality - two features (rotated x and y coordinates) versus four (adding velocity and acceleration). No architecture details, layer counts, activation functions, optimiser or training hyperparameters are given in the retrieved text, which is the study's main reporting gap and is conspicuous against the exceptional specification of the task, the preprocessing and the simulation procedure. No alternative model families are compared and no baseline classifier is reported. The modelling is deliberately minimal because the study's question is representational rather than architectural: whether raw trajectories suffice. Given 20 children, keeping the model simple is the right choice, but the absence of any specification means the comparison between the two models cannot be checked for confounds such as differing capacity or training duration." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-118" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No cross-sensor fusion. The study's ablation is between two representations of the same signal - raw coordinates alone versus coordinates plus velocity and acceleration - and its result is more interesting than a simple fusion gain. The four-feature model outperforms the two-feature model substantially (0.90 versus 0.76), which would ordinarily be read as the derived features adding information. But the simulation analysis shows that acceleration correlates with the two-feature model's predictions at r = 0.69 even though that model never receives acceleration as an input, so the information is already present in the raw coordinate sequence and is being recovered from it. The 14-point accuracy difference therefore reflects how easily a network of this size and training regime can extract a derivative from a 20 Hz position sequence, not what information the sequence contains. That is a genuinely useful distinction for the review's treatment of feature engineering: it shows a case where explicit derived features improve performance without adding information, which is the opposite of the usual assumption and is established by manipulation rather than asserted. The authors' own conclusion follows this reading, noting that raw data analysis has potential while the four-feature model excels." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-118" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two objectives, both methodological rather than clinical. First, to test whether raw motion trajectories alone - unprocessed x and y coordinates - carry enough information to classify autistic from typically developing children, or whether derived kinematic features are needed; the comparison is motivated by the group's own prior work, which had found that maximum and minimum acceleration and standard deviations of speed and acceleration were the leading contributors in a feature-based model. Second, to test the specific role of acceleration by generating synthetic trajectories with systematically varied acceleration and observing how the models respond. Findings: the four-feature model reached 0.90 accuracy against 0.76 for the two-feature model, with a consistently higher fold-wise distribution and a better ROC curve, so derived features help substantially. But the simulation analysis complicates the simple reading: acceleration correlated with the two-feature model's predictions at r = 0.69 despite that model never receiving acceleration as an input, and with the four-feature model's at r = 0.82, both at p < 0.001 over 932 samples, with higher acceleration increasing the probability of an autism prediction in both. So the information is present in the raw coordinate sequence and the four-feature representation makes it easier to use rather than supplying something new - a conclusion the authors reach and state, writing that their findings 'support the potential of raw data analysis in objectively assessing motor behaviors related to autism' while the four-feature model 'excels'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-118" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Four directions, all concrete and following from stated limitations. Expand the sample and observe it longitudinally, to establish predictive capacity over time. Move beyond a single tablet as the tracking device, on the reasoning that richer data collection would improve analysis even from raw coordinates. Coordinate complementary measures, specifically manual and ocular movement data together, to characterise hand-eye patterns - a well-targeted proposal given that the drag-and-drop task is inherently visuomotor and the study measures only the manual half. And cross-validate against other diagnostic tools. Not proposed, and needed: participant-level validation, developmental assessment of both groups, and relating model output to the severity range the autistic sample is said to span." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-118" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Twenty children aged 3-6, recruited between 13 February and 21 March 2023: 10 autistic (mean age 4 years, SD 0.94) from the Neapolisanit S.R.L. Rehabilitation Center where they were receiving psychomotor and speech therapy, and 10 typically developing (mean 3 years 9 months, SD 0.1) from a pre-primary and primary educational institute in the province of Naples. Inclusion for the autistic group required a clinical diagnosis, age 3-6 and absence of comorbid conditions; the only criterion for the comparison group was parent and teacher report of no neurodevelopmental disorder, with the paper stating explicitly that they were recruited 'without specific assessment of their pre-existing abilities'. The modelled unit is not the child but the trajectory: 738 trajectories were retained after filtering, roughly 37 per child, and a further set of computationally generated trajectories brought the correlation analysis to 932 samples. Twenty children is the smallest sample of any included study in this review, and the discrepancy between 20 participants and 738 modelling units is the central interpretive issue." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-118" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The stated limitations are four and are well chosen: small sample size, exclusive reliance on a tablet as the tracking device, the absence of longitudinal observation, and the absence of complementary measures - with the specific and useful suggestion that manual and ocular movement data be coordinated to characterise hand-eye patterns. The authors also call for cross-validation against other diagnostic tools. What is not addressed is the relationship between 20 children and 738 modelling units. Cross-validation partitions trajectories, and with roughly 37 trajectories per child each validation trajectory has around 36 siblings from the same child in training; movement kinematics are close to an individual motor signature at this granularity, so a model can separate children rather than groups and report high accuracy. No participant-level result appears anywhere, and the paper does not state whether folds respected child boundaries. Three further omissions matter. Neither group received any developmental or cognitive assessment, and the comparison children were recruited explicitly without assessment of pre-existing abilities, so a general developmental explanation cannot be excluded in a fine-motor task with 3-6 year olds. The autistic group's age standard deviation is nearly ten times the comparison group's, so the groups differ in developmental spread. And the autistic children were receiving psychomotor therapy at the recruiting centre while the comparison children were not, confounding motor training with diagnosis in a study of motor kinematics - the same confound this review recorded in the group's companion study on a different sample, and unaddressed in both." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-118" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Solid for the autistic group and absent for the comparison group. Autism required a clinical diagnosis confirmed with the ADOS-2, and the paper states something few in this review do: the diagnoses were made 'by healthcare professionals and industry experts who were independent of our research laboratory and study'. Explicit independence between the diagnosing clinicians and the research team removes one route by which knowledge of the study could influence the reference standard, and is worth crediting. Against this, no ADOS-2 score of any kind is reported, so the severity range the autistic group is said to span cannot be characterised; comorbidity was excluded by criterion, narrowing the sample relative to the clinical population; and typically developing status rests entirely on parent and teacher report of no neurodevelopmental disorder, with no screening instrument administered and no assessment of abilities - so undetected autistic traits or developmental difficulty in the comparison group cannot be ruled out." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-118" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Thin, and thinner for the comparison group than for the autistic group. Reported: group sizes, age means and standard deviations, recruitment source, and for the autistic group the requirement of an ADOS-2-confirmed diagnosis and absence of comorbidity. Not reported: sex, IQ, developmental level, language ability, socioeconomic status, race or ethnicity, or any severity score. Two features of what is reported deserve attention. The autistic group's age standard deviation is 0.94 years against 0.1 for the comparison group - nearly a tenfold difference in spread - so although the means differ by only three months the autistic group spans a much wider developmental range, which matters for a fine-motor outcome in children aged 3 to 6 where motor control changes rapidly. And no cognitive or developmental assessment was performed on either group; the comparison children were recruited without any assessment of pre-existing abilities, and the autistic group's functioning is inferred rather than measured, from the fact that all 'were able to engage with a tablet, suggesting a moderate level of functioning'. Since the outcome is the smoothness and dynamics of a dragging movement, and both groups were selected without reference to developmental level, a general developmental explanation for the difference cannot be separated from an autism-specific one. A further asymmetry is that the autistic children were undergoing psychomotor therapy at the recruiting centre - direct motor intervention - while the comparison children were not." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-118" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two recruitment sites in the Naples area with a familiar asymmetry: autistic children from the Neapolisanit S.R.L. Rehabilitation Center, which is also a co-author affiliation and where the children were receiving psychomotor and speech therapy, and comparison children from a mainstream pre-primary and primary school. The testing arrangement is described in unusual physical detail - table height 45-60 cm adjusted to the child's age, experimenter seated to the child's right at about 30 cm, a 12.3-inch tablet raised and tilted at approximately 60 degrees to give a full view of the game - and this level of specification is a genuine reproducibility asset. One element of it is also a small confound: adjusting table height by age introduces a systematic postural difference correlated with a variable whose spread differs sharply between groups. The wider recruitment geometry is the standard clinic-versus-school case-control arrangement, compounded here by the autistic group receiving active motor therapy at the recruiting site." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-118" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A purpose-built drag-and-drop game, and the design reasoning behind it is the paradigm's most valuable feature. The child drags an image from the screen centre to a target at one of eight positions spaced 45 degrees apart while avoiding distractors, with the settings panel allowing category, scene count and distractor number to be varied - all held constant here. Three design decisions address problems that recur throughout this review. No verbal instructions were given: the examiner deliberately withheld them so the child could work out the game's logic independently, and the two support mechanisms provided - a video tutorial triggered by the play button and a vibration cue on the image to be moved - were introduced explicitly 'to avoid stressful social situations and improve engagement and autonomy'. Designing the elicitation to minimise social demand, in a study measuring motor rather than social behaviour, removes a confound that several other studies here carry unexamined. The eight-target arrangement at fixed angular spacing then makes rotation normalisation possible, so trajectories to different targets become comparable - a design choice that pays off directly in the analysis. And recording is restricted to completed drags from image to target, with incomplete drags and stray touches excluded, so the measured behaviour is well defined. The costs are that session duration varied between children without being modelled, and that a single session per child leaves no within-child stability estimate." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-118" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A 12.3-inch Dell Latitude tablet running purpose-built software written in Unity 2D and C#, logging finger x and y coordinates at 20 Hz with asynchronous programming used for storage so that recording did not degrade game performance. No sensor, marker, camera or wearable is involved. The 20 Hz sampling rate is modest - one sample every 50 ms - and bounds what can be said about rapid acceleration changes, though it is adequate for the trajectory-shape features the study models. No analysis software or framework is named, and the neural network architectures are not specified in the retrieved text, which is the one substantive gap in an otherwise well-documented apparatus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-118" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The decisive weakness, and it is structural rather than procedural. The dataset is 738 trajectories from 20 children, roughly 37 each, and evaluation uses four-fold cross-validation with a 110-sample validation set described as 15% of the trajectory dataset. The unit is explicitly the trajectory: the validation size is quoted in samples out of 738, and nothing in the paper indicates that a child's trajectories were kept within a fold. Under a random trajectory-level split, each validation trajectory is accompanied by approximately 36 siblings produced by the same child in the same session with the same hand, differing only in which of eight targets was approached - and after rotation normalisation even that difference is removed. Fine motor kinematics at this granularity function close to an individual motor signature, so a model can separate children rather than groups and report high accuracy; the reported 0.90 is therefore not an estimate of performance on a child the model has not seen, and no participant-level figure appears anywhere. With only 20 children, a participant-disjoint scheme would have been straightforward - leave-one-child-out would give exactly 20 folds - and would have produced the number a clinical claim requires. Three secondary points compound this. No chance baseline, confidence interval or class-conditional metric accompanies either accuracy. The simulation and simplification analyses, which are the study's strongest contribution, are conducted on the same trained models and the same underlying trajectories. And the two feature sets were compared without model architecture being specified, so the 14-point difference cannot be checked against differences in capacity. The corrective is simple and the open data release makes it possible for others to perform: partition the 20 children, retrain, and report participant-level accuracy alongside sensitivity and specificity." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-118" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The strongest element of the study, and methodologically distinctive. Rather than applying a post-hoc attribution method, the authors manipulate the input: synthetic trajectories are generated with average acceleration systematically varied between -5 and +5 cm/s squared, and the models' predictions are measured as a function of that variation, yielding correlations of r = 0.69 and r = 0.82 with a stated direction - higher acceleration increases the probability of an autism prediction. This is causal rather than correlational evidence about the model's decision function, it is quantified, and it is directional. A second robustness analysis simplifies trajectories at seven rates from 35% to 90% and re-tests, with generated trajectories under 20 points excluded. And the analysis produces an informative incidental finding: the two-feature model, which has no acceleration input, still correlates with acceleration at 0.69, showing that the raw sequence carries the information. What is absent is any individual-level interpretation - no error analysis, no examination of which children were misclassified, and no relation between predictions and the severity range the autistic group reportedly spans." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-118" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None for either group. No IQ, developmental quotient, language, adaptive or severity measure was obtained, and the paper says so implicitly by describing the autistic group's functioning as inferred from their ability to use a tablet. The absence has two specific costs in this design. Because the outcome is fine motor kinematics in 3-6 year olds and neither group was assessed developmentally, the difference between groups cannot be attributed to autism rather than to general developmental level - and the autistic group's much wider age spread makes this more rather than less pressing. And because the autistic group is described as covering 'a range of symptom severity levels within the spectrum' without any score, the natural question of whether the model's confidence tracks severity cannot be asked, even though the study's simulation methodology is well suited to it." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-118" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "PLOS ONE 19(4), article e0302238, published 22 April 2024; received 9 January 2024, accepted 28 March 2024, open access under CC BY, with a named academic editor. The venue's requirements produce the data availability statement with its GitHub URL, the funder no-role statement and the competing-interests declaration - and the first of these is what makes this the only included study in the review with openly published data. Eighteen pages, with substantial space given to the theoretical framing, the task design and the preprocessing mathematics, and comparatively little to the model architecture, which is not specified." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-118" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Fine motor control during goal-directed touchscreen dragging, represented as a full temporal sequence rather than as summary statistics. The behavioural claim is that motor differences in autism are prevalent, early-appearing - present 'even before the onset of language and social interaction difficulties' - and clinically neglected, with the paper stating that despite their relevance 'currently there are no adequate tools available to accurately assess motor function in children with autism'. The theoretical grounding is embodied cognition: how a person moves shapes how they learn from and explore the environment, so motor patterns are informative about cognition rather than peripheral to it. Empirically the study localises the signal to acceleration: synthetic trajectories with higher average acceleration are more likely to be classified as autistic, with correlations of 0.69 and 0.82 for the two models. That is a specific, directional and testable behavioural claim - faster or more abruptly accelerating dragging movements are associated with autism in this sample - and it is arrived at by manipulation rather than by correlation with observed data. Two things the representation does not capture: the visual half of the visuomotor task, since only finger position is recorded and gaze is not, which the authors identify as the priority extension; and any measure of task success beyond the requirement that a drag reach its target." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-118" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional two-group classification study with a purpose-built serious game, combined with a simulation experiment in which trajectories are computationally modified to probe …" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: ; comparison: 10" ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "df27d08a10ee337b6dede5eafa74419f449d9ee17dd86ae58155a903de2ed95a" ;
