<urn:slr:article:0c3188260dff> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "​ Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) refers to a group of neurodevelopmental disorders, usually characterized by impairments in social interaction skills, struggle with verbal and nonverbal communication, and repetitive behaviors. ASD can not be diagnosed with any one medical test and presents a wide range of symptoms that make it hard to identify in patients of all ages and severities. This paper proposes a novel method of diagnosis to shorten time, resources, and uncertainty during this process. This predictive machine learning model uses eye tracking (ET) and follows saccadic eye movements in patients to identify patterns, irregularities, and fixation time as symptoms of ASD. The results of experimental testing show that the Two Class Boosted Decision Tree is the most accurate model to use, with an estimated accuracy score of 88-91%. As presented by the model, the tracking of sensorimotor activity such as saccades and application into machine learning has the capability to improve the diagnosis of patients across the spectrum. I. Introduction Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) is a spectrum disorder, meaning that symptoms range from completely debilitating to manageable. Most symptoms are identifiable from under 18 months old, making diagnoses in the early stages of life preferred and often leading to more positive outcomes. However, patients with less noticeable symptoms, often girls, who are found to be better at ‘copying’ stereotypical social patterns, are much harder to accurately diagnose, and often grow into adulthood without ever being diagnosed. ASD is a common disorder in the US, with CDC reporting 1 in 54 children diagnosed every year [1]. In addition, experts estimate 2 undiagnosed cases for every 3 accurately diagnosed [2]. The current method of diagnosis involves developmental screening, which includes numerous tests and questionnaires, and comprehensive development evaluation, which consists of structured tests and determines whether a child needs early intervention or special treatment [4]. The comprehensive development evaluation requires a multidisciplinary team consisting of a psychologist, neurologist, psychiatrist, speech therapist, and may include others. In order to diagnose the disorder, the team conducts in-depth neurological, cognitive, and language testing; no single medical test, like a blood test, can identify ASD [5]. Once identified, current treatments involve cognitive behavior therapy, speech-language therapy, behavioral management therapy, and much more to address the wide spectrum of symptoms [4]. Early intervention is one of the best practices, but diagnosis in children is not always guaranteed or accessible, and treatment becomes more uncertain as patients leave critical developing stages [4]. One aspect of ASD that has not been extensively researched is its effect on saccades, which are rapid, jerky eye movements between fixation points [9]. Using an eye" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Dinesh A (2020). Utilizing Artificial Intelligence to Diagnose Autism Spectrum Disorder Based on Eye Tracking Saccades. *2020 IEEE MIT Undergraduate Research Technology Conference (URTC)*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Dinesh A" ;
    dcterms:title "Utilizing Artificial Intelligence to Diagnose Autism Spectrum Disorder Based on Eye Tracking Saccades" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_9c4af7a3" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2020 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "Matching asserted in a single clause with no data; saccade latency, velocity and accuracy all vary strongly with age." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is class imbalance addressed?" ;
                            slr:justification "60:40 split unaddressed, and accuracy alone cannot distinguish a useful model from a majority-class predictor scoring 60%." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Group sizes only; no ages, sexes, recruitment route, site or period." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "108 participants, acknowledged by the author as a limitation, with no characterisation of who they are." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the paradigm's defining contrast exploited?" ;
                            slr:justification "Gap and overlap blocks are described but the gap-minus-overlap difference, the paradigm's analytic point, is never used as a feature or tested." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "No eye tracker, sampling rate, calibration, stimulus geometry or setting; the source dataset is never named or cited." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "Mutual-information selection is computed against the class labels across the whole dataset." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "'Confirmed to have ASD' with no instrument, criteria or diagnostician, and no traceable source publication." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparator models reported with performance figures?" ;
                            slr:justification "Six model families named as compared, none with a result, and three are not binary classifiers." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are reported figures internally consistent?" ;
                            slr:justification "Abstract gives 88-91%, text gives 88%, with no explanation of the range." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are metrics adequate for a diagnostic claim?" ;
                            slr:justification "Accuracy only; ROC curve plotted with no AUC value, and no sensitivity, specificity, precision, recall or confusion matrix." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is feature selection nested within validation?" ;
                            slr:justification "The methods and the reproduced Azure workflow both place filter-based selection before the split." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the validation scheme fully specified?" ;
                            slr:justification "k is never stated, nor is any hyperparameter, seed or per-fold result." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are descriptive group differences supported by statistics?" ;
                            slr:justification "No mean, variance, effect size, confidence interval or p-value appears anywhere in the paper." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "An Azure workflow screenshot is reproduced; no code, no configuration, no exportable pipeline." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the conclusions proportionate to the evidence?" ;
                            slr:justification "The paper proposes replacing multidisciplinary diagnostic evaluation on the basis of one accuracy figure from a leaked pipeline with no sensitivity or specificity." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No approval, consent or governance statement, and no provenance for the secondary data under which such approvals might be traced." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset identified or cited?" ;
                            slr:justification "The source dataset is never named, cited or linked, so nothing about it can be verified." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are tooling limitations disclosed?" ;
                            slr:justification "The author states that Azure ML Studio prevented adjustment of node count, leaf count and tree depth." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional secondary analysis. No ethics approval, consent procedure, anonymisation statement, data governance policy or data provenance is reported anywhere. Since the dataset is neither named nor cited, it cannot be established under what approvals the original collection took place or whether this reuse was authorised - a governance gap that is qualitatively different from simply failing to report approval for one's own collection." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-051" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: build a machine-learning classifier on saccade features to shorten and simplify autism diagnosis. Main findings: a Two-Class Boosted Decision Tree reached 88% accuracy (88-91% per the abstract); mutual-information filtering selected six of sixteen attributes, including mean primary saccade amplitude, absolute saccade error and latency; and descriptively, autistic participants showed comparable mean saccade latency but greater trial-to-trial variability, larger and more variable absolute saccade error amplified at higher target amplitudes, and lower peak velocity with longer time to reach it." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-051" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None reported. The entire participant description is 'a total of 65 participants with ASD and 43 with no autism present; these were closely related in terms of patients of similar ages and genders'. No ages, no age ranges, no sex counts, no ethnicity, no socioeconomic information, no country, no recruitment route and no statistical test of the asserted matching. This is the least participant characterisation of any included study in the corpus. It matters directly here because saccade latency, peak velocity and accuracy all change substantially across childhood and adolescence, so an unreported age difference would reproduce every group difference the paper describes." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-051" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'patients with ASD', 'ASD participants', 'affected patients', 'healthy patients', 'children with ASD'. Consistently medicalised - participants are 'patients' throughout and autism is repeatedly framed by severity, opening with the claim that 'symptoms range from completely debilitating to manageable'. The one nuanced observation concerns sex: the paper notes that 'patients with less noticeable symptoms, often girls, who are found to be better at copying stereotypical social patterns, are much harder to accurately diagnose, and often grow into adulthood without ever being diagnosed', which correctly identifies camouflaging as a diagnostic problem - though the study then reports no sex data at all." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-051" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No formal keyword list. Vocabulary: 'saccades', 'sensorimotor activity', 'predictive machine learning model', 'novel method of diagnosis', 'shorten time, resources, and uncertainty'. The register is throughput-oriented - the claim is about reducing the professionals, resources and waiting time required for diagnosis rather than about mechanism or accuracy per se." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-051" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Microsoft Azure Machine Learning Studio for the entire pipeline - data ingestion, column selection, missing-value cleaning, filter-based feature selection, train/validation splitting, model training and evaluation - with a screenshot of the workflow reproduced as a figure. No eye-tracking hardware is identified. The paper notes that the Studio limited its ability to tune the model, being unable to 'adjust the number of nodes, leaves, and depth', which is an unusual admission that the tooling constrained the science." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-051" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Retain more than six input features, since filtering may have discarded useful signal; move the model out of Azure ML Studio to permit tuning of node count, leaf count and tree depth; and expand beyond 108 participants for a more diverse and robust classifier better able to handle edge cases." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-051" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Nineteen tabular attributes reduced to sixteen by dropping ethnicity, blood type and eye colour, then to six by mutual-information filtering. The features arrive pre-computed, so no representation design was performed here. Two observations about the feature set are worth recording: the presence of blood type and eye colour in a saccade dataset is unexplained and suggests the source was not well understood; and dropping ethnicity as 'unrelated to tracking eye movement' removes the one variable that would have permitted any check on demographic confounding, which given the total absence of participant characteristics is a consequential deletion." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-051" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No IQ, developmental, language, adaptive or severity measure, and no comorbidity information. Given the paper's opening claim that autism symptoms 'range from completely debilitating to manageable', the absence of any severity characterisation means nothing can be said about where on that range the classified sample sits." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-051" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Poor. The workflow diagram and the named Azure modules make the pipeline order legible - which is how the feature-selection leakage becomes visible - but almost nothing else is reproducible. Not reported: the dataset's identity or citation, k for the k-fold cross-validation, any hyperparameters, the random seed, the identity of three of the six retained features, performance figures for any comparator algorithm, the AUC value behind the plotted ROC curve, and any statistic supporting any descriptive claim. No code and no data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-051" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The gap/overlap paradigm, which is a well-chosen task for this question even though the paper does not explain why: extinguishing the fixation point before target onset (gap) versus leaving it visible (overlap) separates attentional disengagement from saccade execution, and the paper correctly reports that overlap latencies were substantially longer than gap latencies, confirming the manipulation worked in the source data. Each participant completed one block of 30 gap trials and one block of overlap trials. The paradigm is described but not motivated, and the gap-minus-overlap difference that gives it its diagnostic interest is never analysed as such." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-051" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "k-fold cross-validation with k unstated, and with supervised feature selection performed on the complete dataset before the split. The paper sets out the sequence unambiguously - pass in data, select columns, apply filter-based feature selection, then split - and reproduces the Azure workflow confirming it. Because mutual information is computed against the class labels, every fold's feature set was chosen using information from that fold's test data, which biases the reported accuracy upward by an amount that cannot be estimated from what is reported. The paper's own description of cross-validation as a method that has 'been shown to lower bias and overfitting' sits directly alongside the step that defeats it. There is no held-out test set, no independent cohort, no external validation and no repetition." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-051" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Saccadic eye movement - specifically latency, accuracy (absolute error), amplitude, peak velocity, acceleration and the trial-to-trial variability of each, measured under gap and overlap conditions, together with fixation-proportion and pupil-area measures carried in the dataset. The rationale offered for targeting saccades rather than social gaze is worth noting and is not made elsewhere in this corpus in quite these terms: sensorimotor abnormalities are said to be detectable 'prior to the identification of social and cognitive deficits', so an oculomotor marker could in principle precede the social behaviours on which clinical diagnosis depends." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-051" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No multimodal fusion - a single modality throughout, and no within-modality condition comparison is exploited either. The gap and overlap blocks are described and their latency difference noted, but the paradigm's defining contrast is never used as a feature or analysed as a group-by-condition interaction, which is the main analytic opportunity the design offers and the paper leaves unused." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-051" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Participants are described as 'confirmed to have ASD' with no further detail - no instrument, no criteria, no diagnostician, no date. Non-autistic status is asserted as 'no autism present' with no screening described. Because the dataset is secondary and is never named or cited, the diagnostic basis cannot be traced to a source publication either. The paper's own introduction emphasises that diagnosis requires a multidisciplinary team conducting in-depth neurological, cognitive and language testing, which makes the absence of any account of how these 108 labels were obtained more conspicuous." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-051" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2020. The framing is access and throughput: CDC prevalence of 1 in 54, an estimate of 'two undiagnosed cases for every three accurately diagnosed', and a description of comprehensive developmental evaluation as requiring 'a multidisciplinary team consisting of a psychologist, neurologist, psychiatrist, speech therapist, and may include others' conducting in-depth neurological, cognitive and language testing, with no single medical test available. Two framing points are distinctive within this corpus. First, the sex argument - girls with subtler presentations who are 'better at copying stereotypical social patterns' go undiagnosed into adulthood - correctly identifies camouflaging as a diagnostic failure mode, although the study then reports no sex data. Second, the rationale for choosing saccades over social gaze is temporal rather than practical: sensorimotor abnormalities are said to be present 'prior to the identification of social and cognitive deficits', which would make an oculomotor marker earlier-detectable in principle than the social behaviours clinical diagnosis relies on. The paper also states that saccades in autism are an under-researched area, which is broadly consistent with this corpus, where gaze studies overwhelmingly examine where people look rather than how their eyes move." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-051" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The mutual-information filter is presented partly as an interpretability device - it 'communicates the attributes in the dataset which have the most predictive power in the model to drive future experimentation' - and three of the six retained features are named: mean amplitude of primary saccades, absolute saccade error and latency. The other three are never identified, and no model-derived importances, tree structure or decision rules are shown despite the classifier being a decision tree ensemble, which is among the more inspectable model families. The behavioural interpretation offered is that variability rather than central tendency distinguishes the groups, but the paper twice states that the implications are unclear - 'it is uncertain what further implications it has on sensorimotor activity' and 'it is still uncertain what the connections are between these dynamics and saccade error or latency'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-051" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A Two-Class Boosted Decision Tree, selected after comparing linear regression, logistic regression, multiclass neural networks, support vector algorithms and quantile regression models within Azure ML Studio. The stated justification - that ensemble boosting suits small tabular data better than the bagging of random forests or the hierarchical structure of neural networks - is reasonable in outline, and choosing a shallow tabular model over deep learning at n = 108 is the right instinct. But no performance figure is given for any comparator, so the selection cannot be assessed, and including linear and quantile regression in a binary classification comparison indicates the model set was not well matched to the task." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-051" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups totalling 108 participants: 65 with autism and 43 without. No other-diagnosis comparison group. Nothing further is reported about either group, and because the dataset is secondary and unidentified, no additional characteristics can be recovered. The class imbalance (60:40) is never addressed in the modelling, and with accuracy as the only reported metric a classifier predicting the majority class throughout would score 60%." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-051" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2020 IEEE MIT Undergraduate Research Technology Conference (URTC) - an undergraduate research venue, which is relevant context for the depth of methodological scrutiny the work received." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-051" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy alone, at 88% in the text and 88-91% in the abstract, with a plotted ROC curve whose AUC is never stated. For a paper proposing to replace multidisciplinary diagnostic assessment with a single test, the absence of sensitivity and specificity is the critical omission - these are the quantities that determine whether such a test would be usable, and with 60% of the sample autistic, accuracy alone cannot distinguish a useful classifier from one biased toward the majority class. No confusion matrix, no confidence interval, no per-fold variability and no statistical comparison against any baseline." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-051" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Acknowledged: that only 108 individuals were used and more would produce a more diverse and robust classifier with better performance on edge cases; that restricting to six features may have cost accuracy; and that Azure ML Studio prevented direct hyperparameter tuning. Not acknowledged, and decisive: mutual-information feature selection was applied to the full dataset before splitting, so supervised selection sits outside the cross-validation loop; no participant characteristics are reported so no confound can be excluded; the dataset's provenance, diagnostic basis and collection ethics are entirely unstated; only accuracy is reported for a proposed diagnostic test, with no sensitivity or specificity and an unlabelled ROC curve; the 60:40 class imbalance means a majority-class predictor scores 60%; the abstract's 88-91% range is unexplained against the text's single figure of 88%; the comparator set includes regression methods that are not binary classifiers; and every descriptive group difference is asserted without a statistic." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-051" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Not stated. No collection site, period, recruitment route or ethics approval appears, because the data are secondary and their origin is never identified. All modelling was performed in Microsoft Azure Machine Learning Studio." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-051" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Per the source protocol: one block of 30 gap trials and one block of overlap trials per participant" ;
                    slr:study_design "Secondary analysis of an existing saccade dataset with classifier comparison; no new data collection" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Not characterised" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 65; comparison: 43" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "88% accuracy for the Two-Class Boosted Decision Tree (abstract: 88-91%), with an unlabelled ROC curve. No performance figures are reported for any of the compared algorithms, so the claim that boosted trees were best cannot be checked",
                        "A boosted decision tree trained on six mutual-information-selected saccade features classified autism at a reported 88% accuracy. The descriptive observations - that mean saccade latency was similar between groups while trial-to-trial variability was elevated in autism, that absolute saccade error was larger and more variable especially at larger target amplitudes, and that maximum saccade velocit" ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy only, quoted as 88% in the text and as an 'estimated accuracy score of 88-91%' in the abstract. An ROC curve is displayed but no AUC value is given, and no sensitivity, specificity, precision, recall, F1, confusion matrix or confidence interval appears" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "88% accuracy for the Two-Class Boosted Decision Tree (abstract: 88-91%), with an unlabelled ROC curve. No performance figures are reported for any of the compared algorithms, so the claim that boosted trees were best cannot be checked; A boosted decision tree trained on six mutual-information-selected saccade features classified autism at a reported 88% accuracy. The descriptive observations - that mean saccade latency was similar between groups while trial-to-trial variability was elevated in autism, that absolute saccade error was larger and more variable especially at larger target amplitudes, and that maximum saccade velocit" ] ] ;
