                    slr:supporting_quote "Mutual-information feature selection was applied to the full dataset before cross-validation splitting, as stated in the methods and confirmed by the reproduced workflow, so label information from held-out folds informed feature choice; k is unstated and no hyperparameters are reported." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "high" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<urn:slr:article:0e322379d4f9> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Early screening and diagnosis of autism spectrum disorder (ASD) primarily rely on behavioral observations by qualiﬁed clinicians whose decision process can beneﬁt from the combination of machine learning algorithms and sensor data. We designed a computerized visual-orienting task with gaze-related or non-gaze-related directional cues, which triggered participants’ gaze-following behavior. Based on their eye-movement data registered by an eye tracker, we applied the machine learning algorithms to classify high-functioning children with ASD (HFA), low-functioning children with ASD (LFA), and typically developing children (TD). We found that TD children had higher success rates in obtaining rewards than HFA children, and HFA children had higher rates than LFA children. Based on raw eye-tracking data, our machine learning algorithm could classify the three groups with an accuracy of 81.1% and relatively high sensitivity and speciﬁcity. Classiﬁcation became worse if only data from the gaze or nongaze conditions were used, suggesting that “lesssocial” directional cues also carry useful information for distinguishing these groups. Our ﬁndings not only provide insights about visualorienting deﬁcits among children with ASD but also demonstrate the promise of combining classical behavioral paradigms with machine learning algorithms for aiding the screening for individuals with ASD." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "He Q; Wang Q; Wu Y; Yi L; Wei K (2021). Automatic classification of children with autism spectrum disorder by using a computerized visual‐orienting task. *PsyCh Journal, 10(4), 550-565*." ;
    dcterms:creator "He Q",
        "Wang Q",
        "Wei K",
        "Wu Y",
        "Yi L" ;
    dcterms:title "Automatic classification of children with autism spectrum disorder by using a computerized visual‐orienting task" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_991fe435" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2021 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Group sizes, sexes, ages, Raven, PPVT and SCQ scores tabulated with all pairwise tests, plus recruitment source and diagnostic route." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is sex representation adequate?" ;
                            slr:justification "75-88% male across groups, untested, and named by the authors as preventing any sex-specific analysis." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "74 children across three groups is reasonable for this design but small for a diagnostic claim." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "All three age-matched; high-functioning and typically developing groups additionally matched on verbal and nonverbal intelligence with reported test statistics." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is sample heterogeneity addressed structurally?" ;
                            slr:justification "The autistic sample is split by measured cognitive functioning and classified as three classes rather than pooled." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is assessor blinding maintained?" ;
                            slr:justification "The experimenter did not know which functioning subgroup an autistic child belonged to." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "Features are distributional statistics of raw gaze specified in advance without reference to group differences." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "Hospital DSM-5 diagnoses plus SCQ above threshold, with the unavailability of validated ADOS and ADI-R in Chinese disclosed." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Tracker, sampling rate, display geometry, viewing distance, calibration threshold, fixation definition, full stimulus inventory, instruction wording and trial timing with rationale." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Does the task measure the construct claimed?" ;
                            slr:justification "The authors note their attention-sustaining phase distinguishes the paradigm from conventional visual-orienting tasks and may itself drive group differences." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is uncertainty quantified?" ;
                            slr:justification "No confidence intervals on accuracy or AUC, and no statistical comparison between the condition-subset models." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is feature selection avoided or nested?" ;
                            slr:justification "All 68 features used without selection, with the rationale that selection on small samples inflates accuracy." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is cross-population generalisation tested?" ;
                            slr:justification "Two tests withholding an entire group from training, revealing a large transfer asymmetry." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are per-class metrics and confusion matrices reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Full matrices with per-class sensitivity, specificity and AUC for both three-class and binary problems." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the validation split at the participant level?" ;
                            slr:justification "Subject-wise leave-one-out, argued for explicitly against record-wise alternatives." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the hyperparameter set independently of the evaluation data?" ;
                            slr:justification "k fixed by piloting on a previously published external dataset." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Software named; no code released." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No release or availability statement." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the field's methodology critiqued?" ;
                            slr:justification "Manual behavioural coding, binary classification and feature-selection-driven accuracy inflation are each named as problems with specific remedies adopted." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Helsinki conformity, named Peking University committee approval, written parental consent, and separate consent for published photographs." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are limitations stated candidly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Static cues, the attention-sustaining confound, sex imbalance and base-rate constraints on comparison with clinical diagnosis all stated." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the diagnostic instrument gap explained?" ;
                            slr:justification "Absence of ADOS and ADI-R attributed to the lack of officially released and validated Chinese translations." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "k-nearest neighbours, deliberately simple, chosen for the low intrinsic dimensionality of gaze coordinates, for tolerance of overlapping class distributions and for small-sample performance. The single hyperparameter k was fixed by piloting on a different published dataset rather than tuned on the present data - so the entire model specification is set outside the evaluation, which removes the model-selection optimism that affects most of the corpus. No comparison against alternative classifiers is reported, which is a minor gap, but the choice is consistent with a paper whose stated aim is to avoid optimising anything on the evaluation data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-055" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "PsyCh Journal, 10(4), 550-565 - a psychology journal published by the Institute of Psychology, Chinese Academy of Sciences with Wiley. The venue explains the depth of the behavioural analysis relative to the modelling, and the two joint first authors span psychology and cognitive science." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-055" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Typically developing: 24 children, 18 male and 6 female, 5.24 +/- 0.81 years, Raven raw 22.17 +/- 9.03, PPVT raw 80.38 +/- 30.16, SCQ 8.50 +/- 5.13. High-functioning autistic: 26 children, 23 male and 3 female, 5.08 +/- 0.83 years, Raven 20.77 +/- 10.83, PPVT 77.44 +/- 22.39, SCQ 12.23 +/- 6.92. Low-functioning autistic: 24 children, 19 male and 5 female, 4.98 +/- 1.09 years, Raven 11.00 +/- 2.00, PPVT 22.88 +/- 12.30, SCQ 17.32 +/- 5.23. All three groups are age-matched with no significant pairwise differences, and the high-functioning and typically developing groups are additionally matched on both nonverbal intelligence (t(48) = -.49, p = .624) and verbal intelligence (t(46) = -.95, p = .356) - so the critical contrast is IQ-controlled, which almost no other study in this corpus achieves. Sex proportions differ (75%, 88%, 79% male) and are not tested; the authors name this as a limitation. No ethnicity or socioeconomic data; recruitment from a Beijing specialised preschool for the clinical groups." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-055" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Prior DSM-5 diagnoses made by professional pediatricians at licensed hospitals, corroborated by above-threshold scores on the Chinese SCQ for every autistic participant. The authors state plainly why the field standard was unavailable: 'We were unable to administer the Autism Diagnostic Observation Schedule or the Autism Diagnostic Interview-Revised to confirm the ASD diagnosis in our study since the Chinese translated versions of these two scales have not been officially released and validated.' This is a more informative disclosure than the silent omission found elsewhere in the corpus. Functioning subgroups were assigned by CRT-C2 and PPVT-R, with children failing to complete the Raven's test or scoring below the PPVT norm average classed as low-functioning - a criterion that partly conflates cognitive level with capacity to complete a structured test, which matters given that the outcome measure is also a structured task." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-055" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No cross-modal fusion - gaze only. The condition-level analysis serves the analogous purpose and yields a clear result: three-class accuracy falls from 81.08% to 78.4% using gaze cues alone and to 77.0% using non-gaze cues alone, so both cue families carry non-redundant information and the full set is better than either. This supports the paper's substantive claim that visual-orienting deficits in autism 'are not only tied to social cues but appear to be domain-general'. The behavioural analysis reinforces it: all three groups found face cues harder than arrow and finger cues, but the high-functioning group showed a cue-type effect on target dwell time that the low-functioning group did not, whose 'poor performance did not depend on the amount of social information contained in the cue picture'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-055" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three groups totalling 74 children: 26 high-functioning autistic, 24 low-functioning autistic and 24 typically developing, all age-matched. Splitting the autistic sample by cognitive functioning rather than treating it as homogeneous is the design's defining feature and is argued for explicitly, on the grounds that binary classification 'fails to take into account the heterogeneity in the ASD population' and that low-functioning autistic children may show behaviours confounded with developmental delay and even with 'normal people with autistic traits, such as stereotyped behaviors, aggression, and noncompliance'. No separate developmental-delay or other-diagnosis group." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-055" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A gamified visual-orienting task with a graded social-content manipulation - arrow, pointing finger, cartoon animal gaze, human gaze - crossed with four directions across 160 trials, using 160 distinct cue pictures and 640 distinct object pictures so that no stimulus repeats. The task requires not just an orienting saccade but sustained fixation on the target for 1000 ms to earn a reward, a design choice the authors justify from piloting: autistic children 'might occasionally choose one target, but then change their mind and fixate on another', so the long fixation window gives them the opportunity to revise. The 4000 ms trial limit was likewise set by piloting because 'some children with ASD would lose their attention during a trial'. The authors also flag that this attention-sustaining phase distinguishes their paradigm from conventional visual-orienting tasks and 'arguably contributes to the poor performance of children with ASD' - an unusually self-aware statement about what their own task measures." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-055" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; a single 15-minute session per child with no follow-up. Data are gaze coordinates over picture stimuli. Governance is fully reported: Helsinki conformity, named ethics committee approval at Peking University, written parental consent obtained before the experiment, and separate personal approval for publication of the photographs used as human-gaze cues. The experimenter was blind to functioning subgroup - 'the experimenter did not know a priori whether a child with ASD belonged to the LFA group or the HFA group' - which is a rare and worthwhile control." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-055" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2021. The framing is a systematic critique of three methodological patterns in the existing literature, each with a named remedy. First, that behavioural features in prior work 'often relied on manually labeled features such as the retrospective coding of children's behaviors in videotapes' which 'were essentially based on subjective ratings, which reduced the appeal of using machine learning algorithms' - remedied by using raw gaze samples. Second, that most studies performed binary classification, which 'fails to take into account the heterogeneity in the ASD population' - remedied by three-class classification including functioning level. Third, that brain-imaging classification 'is generally limited to adults or older children (over 7 years)' so that 'the application of machine learning to young children or infants, who are arguably in more need of automatic classification, is challenging' - remedied by a 15-minute child-friendly eye-tracking task. To these it adds the selection-inflation critique, singling out a named study reporting 96.7% after selecting an optimal feature set from 15 participants per group and noting that such practice 'is currently prevalent among medical studies with the machine learning techniques, and its overestimation of prediction accuracy should be cautioned'. That is the most precise methodological self-awareness in the corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-055" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with ASD', 'children with HFA', 'children with LFA', 'individuals with ASD', 'typically developing children'. Consistently person-first, including for the functioning subgroups. 'Low-functioning' and 'high-functioning' are used as technical designations tied to measured cognitive scores rather than as informal descriptors, and the paper defines them operationally. The framing is developmental and mechanism-oriented, discussing superior temporal sulcus activation and intention inference rather than burden or deficit." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-055" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Visual orienting - shifting and sustaining attention toward a target indicated by a directional cue - decomposed across four cue types spanning a social-content gradient from arrow through pointing finger and cartoon gaze to human gaze. Four behavioural quantities are measured: success rate, latency to disengage from the cue, and dwell time in the target, confound and uninformative regions. The social gradient is what makes the study theoretically informative rather than merely predictive: it permits the question of whether the deficit is social-specific, and the answer is that it is not." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-055" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Test dynamic directional cues; establish whether the method distinguishes autism from attention deficits, given that the attention-sustaining phase may be doing much of the work; recruit balanced numbers of girls and boys to test sex-specific performance; and treat base-rate constraints seriously when comparing automated classification against clinical diagnosis." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-055" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Sixty-eight distributional statistics computed from raw gaze samples - eight moments and order statistics for x and y in each of four regions, plus sample counts per region - used in full without selection. Two design details matter. Samples were stacked across all trials within a condition, justified by the verified absence of a learning effect between the first and last 10 trials in any group or condition. And because targets and confounds appeared at random positions, gaze samples in those regions were converted to local coordinates while cue and other regions retained global coordinates, so that the distributional statistics describe gaze relative to the object rather than relative to the screen. The stated principle - features 'arbitrarily specified without a prior assumption' and used without selection - is the direct methodological answer to the selection-inflation problem the paper identifies in the literature." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-055" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: autism diagnosis; autism screening; autism spectrum disorder; automatic classification; machine learning; visual orienting. 'Visual orienting' anchors the work in a specific and long-studied cognitive construct rather than in generic behavioural sensing, which is what allows the social-gradient manipulation to be theoretically interpretable." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-055" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The most complete cognitive characterisation in the corpus. Combined Raven's Test for nonverbal and PPVT-R for verbal mental intelligence, plus SCQ, administered to all three groups with means and standard deviations tabulated and all pairwise comparisons tested. These are not merely reported but used structurally - they define the functioning subgroups, they establish that the high-functioning and typically developing groups are matched on both intelligence measures, and they support the interpretation that visual-orienting performance tracks cognitive level. No adaptive functioning or comorbidity measures." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-055" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Excellent. Reported: full stimulus inventory with picture counts, dimensions, resolution and spatial separation; the exact instruction wording; trial timing with the empirical rationale for both the 1000 ms fixation requirement and the 4000 ms limit; calibration procedure and acceptance threshold; fixation definition (50-pixel radius, 100 ms); all four areas of interest; per-group trial exclusion rates; the local-versus-global coordinate handling for randomly positioned regions; all 68 features enumerated by type and count; the classifier with its k values and the external source used to fix them; the cross-validation scheme; and complete confusion matrices with per-class sensitivity, specificity and AUC for both the three-class and binary problems. Missing only: data or code release, and the authors' own choice not to report feature importances, which follows from their no-selection stance." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-055" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Acknowledged with four specific limitations: still rather than dynamic directional cues, whereas dynamic cues predominate in daily life; the attention-sustaining phase distinguishes this paradigm from conventional visual-orienting tasks and may itself drive the group differences, so future work should test whether the algorithms can separate autism from attention deficits; too few girls to test sex-specific performance, with the specific rationale that 'autistic girls exhibit more typical social attention than do autistic boys'; and that 'the predictive power of automatic classification techniques is constrained by the base rate of ASD in the population', so direct comparison against current diagnostic methods requires caution. The absence of ADOS and ADI-R is disclosed with its reason. Identifiable in review but not stated: the low-functioning group's 5.7% success rate means these children largely failed the task, so their classification may reflect general non-engagement rather than a visual-orienting deficit specifically; and the criterion for assigning children to that group included failure to complete the Raven's test, which partly conflates cognitive level with capacity to complete a structured assessment of the same general kind as the outcome task." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-055" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Tobii X120 at 120 Hz; a 25.5 x 34 cm display at 1024 x 768 viewed from approximately 60 cm; five-point calibration accepted only when mean visual angle error fell below 1 degree at all five points; Psychtoolbox on MATLAB for stimulus presentation; SPSS 20 for statistics. The calibration acceptance criterion is stated quantitatively, which few eye-tracking studies in this corpus do." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-055" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Laboratory testing at Peking University, with autistic participants recruited from a preschool specialised for autistic children in Beijing. Funded by multiple National Natural Science Foundation of China grants, an NSFC/DFG collaborative research centre, a Guangdong key project on autism diagnosis and treatment tools, and a Beijing municipal science and technology grant. No conflicts of interest declared." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-055" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Subject-wise leave-one-out cross-validation with no feature selection and a hyperparameter fixed from external data - about as clean an internal validation as this sample size permits, and argued for explicitly against record-wise alternatives with citation to the methodological literature on accuracy inflation. Beyond that, the two cross-group tests constitute a genuine generalisation experiment rarely attempted in this corpus: an entire group is withheld from training and used as an unseen population. The resulting asymmetry - 100% correct on unseen low-functioning children from a model trained on typically developing versus high-functioning, against 53.85% on unseen high-functioning children from a model trained on typically developing versus low-functioning - is interpreted correctly as showing that 'a model trained with more similar groups can perform well with test data from a disparate group, and a model trained with data from more different groups will underperform with test data from an intermediate group'. What remains absent is an independent cohort, an external site and any replication." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-055" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No model-explanation method, and none is possible given the no-selection design - but interpretability is provided instead by an extensive parallel behavioural analysis with mixed-design ANOVAs, effect sizes and Bonferroni-corrected post hoc tests across four measures and four cue conditions. This establishes what the classifier is likely responding to: a strict group ordering in success rate and latency, longer dwell in confound and uninformative regions for both autistic groups, and a cue-type interaction present in the high-functioning group but absent in the low-functioning group. Scan-path visualisations for representative participants from each group make the qualitative differences legible. The interpretation offered - that low-functioning children's scan paths 'appeared to be more random and less purposeful' - is honest about being descriptive." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-055" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: classify three groups defined by diagnosis and cognitive functioning from raw gaze during a visual-orienting task, and test whether less-social directional cues also discriminate. Main findings: 81.08% three-class accuracy rising to 93.24% for binary autism versus typical development; success rates ranked 74.7%, 29.1% and 5.7% across typically developing, high-functioning and low-functioning groups; deficits appeared for arrow and finger cues as well as gaze cues, with removing either cue family costing accuracy; and cross-group tests showed a model trained on typically developing versus high-functioning children identified 100% of unseen low-functioning children while the reverse transfer achieved only 53.85%." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-055" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Complete: overall accuracy, per-class sensitivity and specificity, full confusion matrices and per-class ROC AUC for the three-class problem, the same for the collapsed binary problem, and accuracy for both cross-group generalisation tests. The confusion matrix is interpreted substantively rather than merely reported - errors concentrate on mislabelling the other groups as high-functioning, which the authors relate to that group's intermediate behavioural position and which explains its lowest specificity. Reporting the binary result alongside the three-class result quantifies exactly what collapsing heterogeneity buys: 93.24% versus 81.08%." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-055" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "One session per child of approximately 15 minutes, comprising 5 practice trials and 160 test trials" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional three-group classification study with a graded social-content manipulation and explicit cross-group generalisation tests" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Preschool children with autism, split by cognitive functioning level" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 50 - 26 high-functioning and 24 low-functioning; comparison: 24" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Raw gaze distributions from a 15-minute gamified visual-orienting task classified three groups - typically developing, high-functioning autistic and low-functioning autistic children - at 81.08% accuracy under subject-wise leave-one-out cross-validation with no feature selection, rising to 93.24% when the two autistic groups were collapsed. Visual-orienting deficits were present for non-social cue",
                        "Three-class: 81.08% accuracy, with sensitivity and specificity of 73.1% and 85.4% for high-functioning, 79.2% and 90.0% for low-functioning, and 91.7% and 96.0% for typically developing; AUC 0.775, 0.908 and 0.927 respectively. Errors concentrated on mislabelling the other two groups as high-functioning, which the authors relate to that group's intermediate behavioural position. Collapsing to bina" ;
                    slr:outcome "Overall accuracy, per-class sensitivity and specificity, full confusion matrices and per-class ROC AUC for the three-class problem; the same for the collapsed binary problem; and accuracy for the two cross-group generalisation tests" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Three-class: 81.08% accuracy, with sensitivity and specificity of 73.1% and 85.4% for high-functioning, 79.2% and 90.0% for low-functioning, and 91.7% and 96.0% for typically developing; AUC 0.775, 0.908 and 0.927 respectively. Errors concentrated on mislabelling the other two groups as high-functioning, which the authors relate to that group's intermediate behavioural position. Collapsing to bina; Raw gaze distributions from a 15-minute gamified visual-orienting task classified three groups - typically developing, high-functioning autistic and low-functioning autistic children - at 81.08% accuracy under subject-wise leave-one-out cross-validation with no feature selection, rising to 93.24% when the two autistic groups were collapsed. Visual-orienting deficits were present for non-social cue" ] ] ;
