<urn:slr:article:218fb0c15541> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Identiﬁcation of autism spectrum disorder (ASD) in children is challenging due to the complexity and heterogeneity of ASD. Currently, most existing methods mainly rely on a single modality with limited information and often cannot achieve satisfactory performance. To address this issue, this paper investigates from internal neurophysiological and external behavior perspectives simultaneously and proposes a new multimodal diagnosis framework for identifying ASD in children with fusion of electroencephalogram (EEG) and eye-tracking (ET) data. Speciﬁcally, we designed a two-step multimodal feature learning and fusion model based on a typical deep learning algorithm, stacked denoising autoencoder (SDAE). In the ﬁrst step, two SDAE models are designed for feature learning for EEG and ET modality, respectively. Then, a third SDAE model in the second step is designed to perform multimodal fusion with learned EEG and ET features in a concatenated way. Our designed multimodal identiﬁcation model can automatically capture correlations and complementarity from behavior modality and neurophysiological modality in a latent feature space, and generate informative feature representationswith betterdiscriminabilityand generalizationfor enhanced identiﬁcation performance. We collected a multimodal dataset containing 40 ASD children and 50 typically developing (TD) children to evaluate our proposed method. Experimental results showed that our proposed method achieved superior performance compared with two unimodal methods and a simple feature-level fusion method, which has promising potential to provide an objective and accurate diagnosis to assist clinicians." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Han J; Jiang G; Ouyang G; Li X (2022). A Multimodal Approach for Identifying Autism Spectrum Disorders in Children. *IEEE Transactions on Neural Systems and Rehabilitation Engineering, 30, 2003-2011*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Han J",
        "Jiang G",
        "Li X",
        "Ouyang G" ;
    dcterms:title "A Multimodal Approach for Identifying Autism Spectrum Disorders in Children" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_115dfe8d" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2022 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are cases and comparisons drawn from comparable settings?" ;
                            slr:justification "Comparison children from a local kindergarten; autistic children from an unnamed school site." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Group sizes, age range and questionnaire instruments given with t-tested demographics in Table I, but the autistic group's recruitment site is never named." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "Demographics were formally tested with two-sample t-tests, but the values are not recoverable and no covariate adjustment is described." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "Ninety preschoolers is reasonable for the paradigm but small for a three-network architecture, and there is no external cohort." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is developmental or cognitive level measured?" ;
                            slr:justification "No IQ, developmental quotient, language or adaptive measure in a 3-6-year-old sample." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the comparison group actively screened?" ;
                            slr:justification "ABC, SRS and SCQ completed by teachers, with no child reaching any cut-off." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "Four parent-report questionnaires with no observational diagnostic instrument, on the authors' own account of limited site access." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Tracker model, sampling rate, calibration gate, viewing distance, stimulus source and duration, fixation filter, interpolation rule and binocular averaging all specified." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the neurophysiological acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Electrode count, sampling rate, reference, impedance threshold, all filter settings, epoch length, artifact pipeline, bad-channel rule and region grouping given." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is differential data quality between groups addressed?" ;
                            slr:justification "Clean EEG segments differ substantially by group (20.4 versus 27.2) and the >70% looking-time gate is applied without an exclusion count." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the feature sets theoretically motivated?" ;
                            slr:justification "EEG families chosen against specific published claims about power, complexity and network topology; AOIs designed to separate eyes, mouth, body, adult, child and joint attention." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are appropriate baselines compared on identical data?" ;
                            slr:justification "Two unimodal models and a naive-concatenation fusion baseline, all on the same dataset and folds." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are claimed differences tested statistically?" ;
                            slr:justification "'Significantly outperformed' is used repeatedly of differences for which no test is reported." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is normalisation fitted on training data only?" ;
                            slr:justification "Stated explicitly, with the test set rescaled by training minima and maxima." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is hyperparameter selection described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Three network geometries, noise level, two learning rates and two iteration counts are stated without any selection procedure." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the unit of analysis the participant?" ;
                            slr:justification "One feature vector per child, with EEG averaged across segments and gaze across trials." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is there external validation?" ;
                            slr:justification "Single dataset, two institutions, one city." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the cross-validation subject-independent?" ;
                            slr:justification "Explicitly stated, with stratified folds preserving the class ratio." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No availability statement of any kind." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Beijing Normal University School of Psychology Research Ethics Committee, with informed consent and parental permission before enrollment." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "The third-party DeepLearnToolbox is cited with its GitHub URL, but no study-specific code is released." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are feature-level findings reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No group means, effect sizes, directions or importance for any of the 221 features, despite a feature set designed to be interpretable." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are limitations stated candidly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Only two engineering constraints are named; the diagnostic weakness the Methods concede is never carried into the Discussion." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the fusion benefit mechanistically explained?" ;
                            slr:justification "Confusion matrices show EEG stronger on the autistic class and gaze stronger on the typical class, identifying the source of the gain." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional, single session, no follow-up. Ethics approval from the School of Psychology Research Ethics Committee of Beijing Normal University, with informed consent obtained from all children with parental permission before enrollment. Funding sources are itemised (NSFC grants 62003228 and 61761166003, a Beijing Municipal Education Commission project, and a National Key R&D programme grant). No data availability statement, no data sharing plan and no discussion of how the EEG and gaze recordings are stored or protected - though both modalities are lower-identifiability than the video and audio data used elsewhere in this corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-074" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The most carefully argued fusion analysis in the corpus, and one of very few where the mechanism of the fusion benefit is demonstrated rather than assumed. Four models are trained on identical data and folds: EEG alone, gaze alone, naive concatenation of raw features, and the proposed two-stage latent fusion. The ordering is informative at every step. Both fusion variants beat both unimodal models, so combining helps. Two-stage fusion beats naive concatenation, so the benefit comes from combining learned high-level representations rather than merely from having 221 features instead of 96 or 125 - the control that distinguishes a fusion architecture claim from a dimensionality claim. And the confusion matrices identify why: EEG classifies autistic children better (82.5% versus 77.7%) while gaze classifies typically developing children far better (94% versus 80%), so the modalities err in opposite directions and fusion recovers both strengths, reaching 92.5% and 98%. That asymmetry is a substantive finding in its own right and is the kind of evidence that most multimodal papers in this literature substitute an accuracy delta for. The t-SNE panels add a visual account, showing the fused representation separating classes where raw and concatenated features overlap. Two caveats: the paper does not test partial fusion (for instance gaze plus a single EEG family) so the minimum useful combination is unknown, and the behavioural modality alone at 86.67% already exceeds the EEG modality, which bears directly on cost-benefit given that eye tracking is far cheaper and faster to acquire than a 128-channel net in a preschooler." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-074" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The weakest analytic dimension. There is no feature-level interpretability of any kind: no importance ranking, no per-family ablation, no group means, no effect sizes, no directional statements for any of the 221 features. This is a substantial loss because the feature set was designed to be interpretable - the ten brain regions, five frequency bands, four entropy scale ranges and seven graph metrics each correspond to a specific published hypothesis, and the eight areas of interest were constructed precisely to distinguish attention to eyes from mouth, to adult from child, and to joint-attention regions. A study that reported which of those differed would have contributed to the autism literature independently of its classifier; as written it contributes only a number. What interpretability exists is at modality level and is done well: the confusion-matrix decomposition establishing the EEG/gaze complementarity, and t-SNE projections of six feature spaces (raw EEG, raw gaze, raw concatenation, learned EEG, learned gaze, fused) showing progressive class separation. The t-SNE panels also surface an honest negative observation the authors do not hide - learned EEG features separate less well than expected, attributed to information loss in dimension reduction - though these projections are computed over the full dataset rather than per fold, so they illustrate rather than validate." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-074" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Stacked denoising autoencoders used hierarchically - two for unimodal representation learning, a third for fusion - with greedy layer-wise unsupervised pre-training followed by supervised fine-tuning. The choice is well matched to the problem: 221 hand-engineered features from 90 children is a regime where supervised deep learning would overfit immediately, and unsupervised pre-training exploits the input data without consuming label information, while the denoising objective with zero-masking corruption prevents the identity mapping and encourages robustness. The paper justifies the choice by precedent in Alzheimer's diagnosis, ADHD classification and machinery fault diagnosis. The architecture is also deliberately shallow - two hidden layers per model, compressing 125 to 50 and 96 to 50 - which is appropriate for the sample size, though the t-SNE analysis reveals a cost the authors acknowledge: learned EEG features separate the classes less well than raw ones in the visualisation, which they attribute to dimension reduction discarding useful information." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-074" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Specified in detail on the acquisition side, which is a strength. EEG: Electrical Geodesics 128-channel Ag/AgCl passive electrode net, 1000 Hz sampling, at least five minutes, Cz online reference, impedances verified below 50 kOhm via Net Station, downsampling to 250 Hz, 50 Hz notch, 0.5-45 Hz band-pass, 4-second non-overlapping epochs, a cited automated artifact detector covering eye movements, blinks, line noise, breathing, muscle, abrupt slopes and outliers, supplementary visual inspection, a 200 microvolt bad-channel threshold with neighbour interpolation, and 62 of 128 electrodes retained across ten regions. Eye tracking: Tobii TX300 at 300 Hz, 1024x768 display, ~60 cm viewing distance, five-point calibration gated on small error vectors, Tobii fixation filter, linear interpolation of gaps up to 100 ms, binocular averaging, and a >70% screen-looking-time inclusion threshold. Modelling uses the publicly available DeepLearnToolbox, cited with its GitHub URL - so the learning machinery, at least, is reproducible off the shelf." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-074" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with ASD', 'ASD children', 'individuals with ASD', 'ASD subjects', 'the ASD population', against 'typically developing (TD) children', 'TD subjects'. Acronym-as-modifier throughout with no person-first construction. The clinical framing is notably medicalised: autism is repeatedly called a 'disease' ('ASD is a complex and heterogeneous disease'), classification errors are framed as 'misdiagnosis rate', the ROC axes are relabelled 'diagnosis rate' and 'misdiagnosis rate', and the stated purpose is to 'assist clinicians to make a precise diagnosis decision'. Describing a neurodevelopmental condition as a disease and a false positive as a misdiagnosis positions the work as diagnostic rather than screening, which is a stronger claim than the parent-report reference standard can support." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-074" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups, both explicitly sized: 40 autistic children and 50 typically developing children, 90 in total, aged 3-6. Typically developing children came from a local kindergarten; the autistic children's site is identified only obliquely as 'the school site' where reliable diagnostic information was limited, which suggests a special-education setting but is never stated. No other-diagnosis or developmental-delay comparison group, so the model is tested only against typical development. The class imbalance (40:50) is handled correctly at the validation level by stratified fold construction, and both classes are large enough that the confusion-matrix decomposition is meaningful." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-074" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The behavioural target is social visual attention: where a preschooler looks while watching a child and an adult interact, resolved to eight regions that separate the two people, separate eyes from mouth from body within each, and add an explicit joint-attention region. The six statistics per region capture both what was attended (fixation count, total and mean fixation duration, visit count) and the priority of attention (time to first fixation, number of fixations before first entering the region) - the latter pair encoding attentional precedence rather than mere allocation, which is the more theoretically pointed measure. The paired EEG modality is not behavioural at all, being eyes-open resting state with no task, so what the fusion combines is a behavioural response to social content with a task-free measure of intrinsic brain dynamics." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-074" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Beijing, China. Comparison children were recruited from a local kindergarten; the autistic children came from a school site whose identity is not given and where the authors report limited access to reliable diagnostic information. Both recordings took place under controlled conditions - EEG in a quiet room with the child in an armchair usually accompanied by a caregiver, eye tracking at a fixed ~60 cm viewing distance following a five-point calibration that had to pass with small error vectors before proceeding. Recruiting the two groups from different institutional settings is a structural feature the paper does not discuss, and it carries a specific risk for the eye-tracking modality here: the stimulus is Tiger Qiao Hu, a widely broadcast Chinese children's programme, so prior familiarity with the characters could differ systematically between children in a mainstream kindergarten and children in a special-education setting, and familiarity affects where a viewer looks." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-074" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Done correctly, and stated in terms that leave no ambiguity: 'we perform a 10-fold subject-independent cross-validation to evaluate the performance of the proposed model, where each subject is regarded as a sample', with stratified sampling to preserve the class ratio in every fold, each fold tested exactly once, and the average across folds reported with standard deviations. Because every child contributes exactly one feature vector - EEG features are averaged across that child's clean segments, gaze features aggregated across their two trials - there is no possibility of the segment-level leakage that inflates results throughout this corpus, and the 95.56% is a genuine participant-level figure. Feature normalisation is also fitted on training data only. Two gaps remain. The three network geometries, the noise level, both learning rates and both iteration counts are stated as given, with no description of how they were arrived at, so selection optimism cannot be excluded and may be substantial across four compared architectures. And there is no external validation - no second site, no held-out cohort, no replication sample - so the estimate, though internally sound, rests entirely on 90 children from two institutions in one city. The correct comparison for this study's 95.56% is therefore not the segment-level 90%-plus figures elsewhere in the corpus but the small set of participant-level results, against which it stands as one of the highest and, on validation design, one of the better supported." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-074" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: determine whether learned fusion of an internal neurophysiological modality and an external behavioural modality outperforms either alone, and whether hierarchical latent-space fusion beats naive feature concatenation. Main findings: MMSDAE reached 95.56% accuracy, 92.5% sensitivity, 98% specificity and AUC 0.984 under subject-independent stratified ten-fold cross-validation, improving on eye tracking alone (86.67%) by 8.89 points and EEG alone (81.11%) by 14.45; both fusion variants beat both unimodal models, and two-stage fusion beat naive concatenation; the modalities are complementary in a specific and demonstrated way, with EEG stronger on autistic children (82.5% versus 77.7%) and gaze much stronger on typically developing children (94% versus 80%); and the behavioural modality alone outperformed the neurophysiological one." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-074" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Index terms: autism spectrum disorders, multimodal fusion, electroencephalogram, eye-tracking, stacked denoising autoencoders, classification. Two modality terms, one architecture term, one fusion term - a keyword set entirely oriented to method, with 'multimodal fusion' rather than 'autism' functioning as the paper's actual subject." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-074" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2022 (submitted September 2021, published July 2022). The framing combines the standard prevalence appeal (1 in 54 per US CDC) with two more specific claims: that 'there is limited understanding of the neural patterns behind ASD and the severity of the disease', and that 'there is a lack of experienced experts for ASD diagnosis' - the second being a workforce argument that is more concretely relevant in the Chinese context the study is situated in than the objectivity arguments that dominate elsewhere in this corpus. The paper's distinctive positional claim is methodological: that 'most existing methods mainly rely on a single modality with limited information and often cannot achieve satisfactory performance', and that since autism has 'abnormal manifestations from the cellular level to the behavioral level' no single level of measurement can suffice. Its related-work section is unusual in citing single-modality accuracy figures from both literatures side by side - 92.34% and 96.44% for EEG, 88.51% and 85.1% for eye tracking - which sets up the fusion argument but also, unintentionally, shows that the highest reported unimodal figures already exceed this study's own unimodal results (81.11% and 86.67%), a discrepancy that goes unremarked and that most likely reflects differences in validation design rather than in signal quality." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-074" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No cognitive, language or adaptive measure of any kind for either group - a significant omission in 3-to-6-year-olds, where developmental level varies enormously and both resting EEG spectral power and social gaze allocation are strongly age- and ability-dependent. No comorbidity information and no exclusions described. Four symptom instruments (ABC, SCQ, SRS, CABS) were administered to the autistic group and three to the comparison group, and their scores are tabulated, so a severity gradient exists in the data; it is used only to characterise the sample and is never related to the model's continuous output, missing the opportunity to test whether the classifier's confidence tracks symptom level - the analysis that would have provided clinical anchoring." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-074" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two directions, both about model efficiency rather than about validity. Replace the three-model pipeline with convolutional or attention architectures to reduce computational cost; and, more interestingly, develop fusion that can operate 'even especially in the absence of one modality during the model training', which would address the practical barrier that the current design requires both an EEG net and an eye tracker for every child - a genuine obstacle to the scalability the paper claims. A third, briefer aim is to explore additional autism-related features via advanced signal processing. Absent from the agenda: external validation, direct diagnostic confirmation, developmental-level control, or any analysis of which features drive the result." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-074" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Strong on signal processing and network architecture, absent on selection procedure and data structure. Fully reported: every EEG acquisition and preprocessing parameter, every eye-tracking acquisition parameter, all three feature families with their band definitions, region groupings, MSE scale ranges and the seven graph metrics; the AOI scheme and the six per-AOI statistics with the arithmetic yielding 96 features; the normalisation formula together with the explicit statement that it is fitted on training data and applied to test; all three SDAE layer geometries; noise level, both learning rates, both iteration counts and the optimiser; the cross-validation scheme with its subject-independence and stratification; and the toolbox with its URL. Not reported: how the network geometries, noise level and learning rates were chosen, and whether that selection used the reporting folds; how many children were screened out by the >70% looking-time rule; whether any EEG recordings were excluded entirely; any group means or effect sizes for any feature; random seeds; and any data or code release beyond the third-party toolbox." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-074" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Group sizes (40 and 50) and the 3-6 age range appear in the running text; ages, sex distribution and the four questionnaire scores are tabulated in Table I with p values from two-independent-sample t-tests, but the table did not render in the extracted text so the values cannot be recovered. That the comparison was made formally and reported with p values is to the study's credit and distinguishes it from the several studies here that report no demographic testing at all. What is not addressed is whether any demographic difference that did emerge was subsequently controlled for - no covariate adjustment or matched-subsample analysis appears anywhere. No IQ, developmental quotient, language level or adaptive functioning measure was collected for either group, which for a 3-6-year-old sample leaves the most obvious alternative explanation - general developmental level - entirely unaddressed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-074" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two paradigms with complementary demands, both requiring nothing of the child. EEG is eyes-open resting state for at least five minutes, so there is no task at all and no compliance requirement beyond sitting - the caregiver may be present. Eye tracking uses two ~30-second clips of a dynamic social interaction between a child and an adult, presented in random order, with an attention-grabbing animated kitten between trials, and 'no response was required from the children'. This is a well-judged design for the 3-6 age band: neither component depends on language, instruction-following or motor response, which is precisely what limits most computational paradigms in preschoolers. The eight areas of interest are the analytically substantive choice, distinguishing adult from child and, within each, eyes from mouth from body, plus a dedicated joint-attention region - a scheme built to test specific published claims about social attention. That the resulting 96 features are then fed undifferentiated into an autoencoder, with no analysis of which AOIs mattered, wastes the design's discriminative structure." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-074" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Only two limitations are stated, and both are engineering constraints rather than threats to validity: that deployment requires EEG and eye-tracking data to be available simultaneously, and that training three SDAE models costs more computation than one. Unaddressed: the diagnostic reference standard rests on parent-report questionnaires, which the authors themselves flag as a data-access compromise in the Methods but never carry into the interpretation of their results; the two groups were recruited from different institutional settings; no cognitive or developmental measure was collected in a 3-6-year-old sample, leaving developmental level uncontrolled; the >70% screen-looking-time gate may exclude children differentially by group with no count reported; the EEG segment yield differs markedly between groups (20.4 versus 27.2 clean segments), which the paper states in passing and never discusses despite its implication that artifact burden - and hence measurement noise - is itself group-dependent; hyperparameter selection is undescribed; there is no external validation cohort; and 90 children is a small sample for a model with three stacked networks. The Conclusion's claim that the framework 'can automatically learn and fuse useful information... without the need of much more diagnostic expert experience' is also stronger than a study whose labels came from parent questionnaires can support." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-074" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The study's weakest element, and unusually, the authors say so themselves. Diagnosis is stated to have been confirmed under DSM-5, but the very next sentence concedes 'we had limited access to reliable information in the school site' and explains that the children were therefore assessed using parent report on four instruments - the Autism Behavior Checklist, Social Communication Questionnaire, Social Responsiveness Scale and Clancy Behavior Scale. None is an observational or clinician-administered diagnostic instrument; all four are screening or symptom-report scales. No ADOS, ADI-R, CARS or direct clinical observation is reported. The consequence for interpretation is specific: the label the classifier is trained to predict is grounded in parent report, so a demonstration that a model recovers it at 95.56% shows agreement with parent-reported symptomatology, not with clinically established diagnosis. The comparison group is better handled - ABC, SRS and SCQ completed by teachers, with no child reaching any cut-off - so negative cases are screened on three instruments even though positive cases lack observational confirmation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-074" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A three-level representation. Level one is hand-engineered and theory-driven: EEG features chosen to test published claims about spectral power, signal complexity and network topology in autism, and gaze features chosen to test claims about social attention allocation. Level two compresses each modality to a 50-dimensional latent code via its own autoencoder. Level three concatenates the two 50-dimensional codes into a 100-dimensional vector and compresses again to 20 before classification. Two design points matter. Fusion occurs after each modality has been separately abstracted, so the fusion network operates on comparable representations rather than on raw features of wildly different scale and character - which is the paper's explanation for why it beats naive concatenation. And normalisation is fitted on training data only and applied to test data, a discipline stated explicitly and observed by very few studies in this corpus. The unit throughout is one vector per child, which is why the cross-validation can be genuinely subject-independent." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-074" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "IEEE Transactions on Neural Systems and Rehabilitation Engineering 30:2003-2011 (2022), a biomedical engineering venue. Authorship spans the School of Psychology at Capital Normal University, the School of Electrical Engineering at Yanshan University, and the State Key Laboratory of Cognitive Neuroscience and Learning at Beijing Normal University, with two first authors marked as contributing equally - one psychological, one engineering. Funded by three national and municipal Chinese grants. The psychology-engineering pairing explains the study's shape: rigorous signal acquisition and a validation design that respects subject independence, alongside an almost complete absence of clinical characterisation and of feature-level interpretation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-074" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, sensitivity and specificity with fold-wise standard deviations, ROC curves with AUC for all four models, and complete confusion matrices for all four - a full panel, and reported comparatively rather than only for the winning model. Reporting standard deviations across folds supports one of the paper's substantive claims, that MMSDAE 'presents the best stability and robustness with the smallest standard deviation among all compared methods', which is a claim about variance that accuracy alone cannot carry. The confusion matrices are the most useful element, since they convert an accuracy comparison into a diagnosis of where each model fails and thereby ground the complementarity argument. Missing: no confidence intervals on the reported means, no statistical test of the difference between models despite the word 'significantly' being used repeatedly of differences that are never tested, no per-fold table, and no comparison against a trivial baseline such as age - which for 3-to-6-year-olds is a real omission given that both resting EEG spectra and gaze behaviour change rapidly across that band." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-074" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "One session per child: at least five minutes of resting-state EEG, then a break, then two eye-tracking trials of about 30 seconds each" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional case-control classification with a within-study ablation comparing two unimodal models, a feature-concatenation baseline and the proposed two-stage fusion model on …" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Preschool children with autism, aged 3 to 6" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 40; comparison: 50" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Fusing resting-state EEG with gaze during social video classified preschool children at 95.56% accuracy under subject-independent cross-validation, against 86.67% for eye tracking alone and 81.11% for EEG alone. The gain is explained rather than merely asserted: confusion matrices show the two modalities fail in different directions - EEG identifies autistic children better while gaze identifies t",
                        "MMSDAE (two-stage fusion): 95.56% accuracy, 92.5% sensitivity, 98% specificity, AUC 0.984, with the smallest standard deviation of any model. ET-SDAE: 86.67% accuracy. EEG-SDAE: 81.11%. CONCAT-SDAE (naive feature concatenation): intermediate, better than both unimodal models but worse than MMSDAE. Fusion improved overall accuracy by 14.45 points over EEG alone and 8.89 points over eye tracking alo" ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy, sensitivity and specificity with standard deviations across folds; ROC curves with AUC for all four models; and full confusion matrices for all four models" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "MMSDAE (two-stage fusion): 95.56% accuracy, 92.5% sensitivity, 98% specificity, AUC 0.984, with the smallest standard deviation of any model. ET-SDAE: 86.67% accuracy. EEG-SDAE: 81.11%. CONCAT-SDAE (naive feature concatenation): intermediate, better than both unimodal models but worse than MMSDAE. Fusion improved overall accuracy by 14.45 points over EEG alone and 8.89 points over eye tracking alo; Fusing resting-state EEG with gaze during social video classified preschool children at 95.56% accuracy under subject-independent cross-validation, against 86.67% for eye tracking alone and 81.11% for EEG alone. The gain is explained rather than merely asserted: confusion matrices show the two modalities fail in different directions - EEG identifies autistic children better while gaze identifies t" ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "84a863f8d5265ef10c0289f9ee0308d9b7d552d7640bfe627f94e305304ce700" ;
