<urn:slr:article:35c8bd7a6e13> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Background: Gelotophobia, the fear of being laughed at, is a social anxiety condition that affects approximately 6% of neurotypical individuals and up to 45% of those with autism spectrum disorder (ASD). This comorbidity can significantly impair the quality of life, particularly in adolescents with high-functioning ASD, where the prevalence reaches 41.98%. Accurate and automated detection tools could enhance early diagnosis and intervention. Objective: This study aimed to develop a deep learning–based diagnostic system that integrates facial emotion recognition with validated questionnaires to detect gelotophobia in individuals with or without ASD. Methods: The system was trained to identify ASD status using a balanced dataset of 2932 facial images (n=1466; 50% from individuals with ASD and n=1466; 50% from neurotypical individuals). The images were processed using the DeepFace library to extract facial features, which were then used as input for the deep learning classifier. After identifying ASD status, the same images were further analyzed using the pretrained DeepFace model to evaluate facial expressions for signs of gelotophobia. In cases where facial cues were ambiguous, the GELOPH<15> questionnaire, consisting of 15 items, was administered to confirm the diagnosis The system was fully implemented using the Python programming language. Deep learning models were developed using libraries such as PyTorch for training the multilayer perceptron classifier, while CUDA was used to accelerate computations on compatible graphics processing units. Additional libraries from the Python programming language, such as scikit-learn, NumPy, and Pandas, were used for preprocessing, model evaluation, and data manipulation. DeepFace was integrated using its Python application programming interface for facial recognition and emotion classification. Results: The dataset comprised 2932 facial images collected from platforms such as Kaggle and ASD-related websites, including 1466 (50%) images of children with ASD and 1466 (50%) images of neurotypical children. The dataset was split into 2653 (90.48%) training samples and 279 (9.51%) testing samples, with each image contributing 100,352 extracted features. We applied various machine learning models for ASD identification. The system achieved an overall prediction accuracy of 92% across both training and testing datasets, with the multilayer perceptron model demonstrating the highest testing accuracy. The system successfully classified gelotophobia in cases where facial expressions were clear. However, in cases of ambiguous facial cues, the DeepFace model alone was insufficient. Incorporating the GELOPH<15> questionnaire improved diagnostic reliability and consistency. Conclusions: This study demonstrates the effectiveness of combining deep learning techniques with validated diagnostic tools for detecting gelotophobia, particularly in individuals with ASD. The high accuracy achieved highlights the system’s potenti" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Eldawansy M; El Bakry H; M Shohieb S (2025). Data Mining–Based Model for Computer-Aided Diagnosis of. *JMIR Formative Research, 9, Article e72115*." ;
    dcterms:creator "El Bakry H",
        "Eldawansy M",
        "M Shohieb S" ;
    dcterms:title "Data Mining–Based Model for Computer-Aided Diagnosis of" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_84bcd4ed" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2025 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Comparison group adequacy" ;
                            slr:justification "Neurotypical images from the same public collection with no demographic characterisation and no screening described, against an autistic class drawn partly from autism-related websites." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Confounder control" ;
                            slr:justification "Age, sex and cultural background explicitly not controlled, as the authors state - in a study whose feature extractor is optimised to encode facial identity and therefore represents demographic attributes strongly." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participant selection and spectrum representativeness" ;
                            slr:justification "A public image assembly with no ascertainment, no spectrum description and an unreported number of distinct children. No sample exists at all for the gelotophobia target." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Handling of missing data and attrition" ;
                            slr:justification "Split counts are reported precisely (2,653 and 279) but no image-quality criteria, exclusions or face-detection failures are described." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reference standard quality" ;
                            slr:justification "None for autism; none at all for gelotophobia, with the ethics section and the abstract contradicting each other over whether the validating instrument was ever administered." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Measurement standardisation" ;
                            slr:justification "Uncontrolled internet photographs with unknown and potentially class-correlated acquisition conditions, processed by a pretrained library developed on adult neurotypical faces and applied without fine-tuning to children." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Index test definition and prespecification" ;
                            slr:justification "Several models compared with the best selected on the reported testing accuracy; no hyperparameters stated; 100,352 features retained without reduction; no prespecified operating point." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome measurement blinding" ;
                            slr:justification "Classification is fully automated with no human rater at inference." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Sample size adequacy for the modelling" ;
                            slr:justification "One hundred thousand three hundred and fifty-two features per image against 2,932 images - a ratio of about 34 to 1 - with no dimensionality reduction and an unknown number of distinct children behind the images." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data splitting and leakage control" ;
                            slr:justification "Image-level splitting with the number of distinct children unknown, using an identity-optimised face-recognition embedding - the configuration most exposed to within-subject leakage in this branch." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance estimation and chance level" ;
                            slr:justification "Accuracy alone, reported as spanning training and testing together, with no held-out figure, no sensitivity or specificity, no confusion matrix, no interval, and no chance baseline stated. No metric of any kind for the gelotophobia task." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Preprocessing and feature selection nesting" ;
                            slr:justification "Ten-fold cross-validation correctly confined to the training portion, and no feature selection performed - but model selection across several classifiers used the reported evaluation." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Acknowledgement of limitations" ;
                            slr:justification "Five limitations named, including uncontrolled demographics, the absence of an external validation cohort, reliance on static images, and the fact that the proposed architectures were not implemented - candid by the standards of this branch. The provenance confound, the absent reference standards and the unmeasured gelotophobia component are all unmentioned." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reporting of results and uncertainty" ;
                            slr:justification "A single accuracy figure conflating training and testing for autism, and no quantitative result at all for gelotophobia, in a paper whose stated objective is gelotophobia detection." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Completeness of methods reporting" ;
                            slr:justification "Corpus size and location, split counts, feature count, fully versioned software stack, hardware, classifier family and cross-validation scheme are all reported, and the GELOPH<15> is supplied as an appendix. Participant counts, demographics, splitting unit, ascertainment and hyperparameters are not." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Ethics, consent and conflicts" ;
                            slr:justification "A numbered ethics committee approval, Declaration of Helsinki conformity, and an explicit statement about data privacy and responsible AI use - more than most of this branch obtains. But the corpus of children's facial photographs is described as anonymised and free of personally identifiable information, which misstates what a face image is, particularly in a study that processes it with a face-recognition library. No funding; no competing interests." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data, code and protocol availability" ;
                            slr:justification "Datasets stated to be available in the Kaggle repository; the questionnaire is supplied as a supplementary file; no code released." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "unclear" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two constructs of very different tractability. For autism, static facial appearance and expression, with the usual consequence that the model receives everything an uncontrolled photograph contains - morphology, expression at the captured instant, pose, apparent age, ancestry, resolution and image provenance - and no analysis identifies which of these it uses. For gelotophobia, the paper proposes to read a dispositional social fear from a still facial image. This is the weakest construct mapping in the review and its weakness is structural rather than a matter of execution: gelotophobia is defined by how a person interprets laughter directed at them, it is measured by self-report for that reason, and no laughter, social interaction or ridicule appears anywhere in the data. The paper's own finding that DeepFace 'was insufficient' for ambiguous facial cues, and its observation that fear and disgust involve minimal facial muscle movement, both point toward the same conclusion without stating it - that the construct is not visible in a photograph. The fallback offered is the GELOPH<15> self-report, which is the appropriate instrument and which the study did not administer." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-137" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Thin, and thinner still for the second target. For autism, accuracy alone is reported at 92%, described as holding 'across both training and testing datasets' - a formulation that conflates the two and gives no separate held-out figure, on a test partition of 279 images where a 95% interval would span roughly plus or minus 3 points before any leakage consideration. No sensitivity, specificity, precision, recall, F1, AUC, confusion matrix or confidence interval appears. For gelotophobia, no quantitative metric of any kind is reported: the results state that the system 'successfully classified gelotophobia in cases where facial expressions were clear' and that DeepFace alone was insufficient otherwise, neither of which has a denominator, a comparison or a ground truth. A paper whose stated objective is 'to develop a deep learning-based diagnostic system ... to detect gelotophobia' reports no number for that detection." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-137" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional secondary image analysis with no longitudinal element. The ethics reporting is better than most of this branch and deserves credit: approval by the Faculty of Computers and Information Research Ethics Committee with a stated approval code (2024 01 004), conformity to the Declaration of Helsinki, and explicit reference to 'ethical standards regarding data privacy and the responsible use of AI in health-related research'. Very few studies in the facial-image branch obtain or report any approval at all. Two features of the statement are nonetheless questionable. It describes the images as coming from 'a publicly available, anonymized dataset on Kaggle, ensuring no personally identifiable information was used' - but a photograph of a face is among the most directly identifying data types that exists, and the same study processes those photographs with a face-recognition library whose purpose is to establish identity from them; calling such a corpus anonymised misstates what it is. And the claim that 'no direct contact with human participants occurred' is accurate and is precisely what makes the gelotophobia component unevaluable, since it means no one was ever assessed for the condition the system claims to detect. The study declares no funding and no competing interests, and supplies the GELOPH<15> as a supplementary appendix." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-137" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None empirically. The proposed architecture is a two-stage fusion of facial expression analysis with questionnaire response, with the questionnaire invoked when facial cues are ambiguous - a sensible design in principle, since it routes the hard cases to the instrument actually validated for the construct. But the questionnaire was never administered to any person, so no fusion occurred on any data, and the claim that 'incorporating the GELOPH<15> questionnaire improved diagnostic reliability and consistency' describes an expected property of the design rather than an observed result." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-137" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None, and its absence is more damaging for the gelotophobia component than for any other study in this review. Gelotophobia is a fear about how other people's laughter is intended - a dispositional appraisal, elicited only when laughter is present and directed at the person. The images here were taken in unknown contexts with no laughter, no social interaction and no ridicule, so the condition the model is asked to detect has no eliciting circumstance anywhere in the data. The GELOPH<15> exists precisely because the construct is an internal state accessible through self-report rather than through observation, and the paper cites it as validated while proposing to bypass it with a still photograph. For the autism component the paradigm absence carries the usual cost: expression, pose, gaze and emotional context all vary freely and none is held constant across the two classes." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-137" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The stated aim is a dual diagnostic framework detecting both autism and gelotophobia, motivated by evidence that gelotophobia affects around 6% of neurotypical people but up to 45% of autistic people, with prevalence reaching 41.98% in adolescents with high-functioning autism - a genuine and under-studied comorbidity, and the identification of it as a target is the paper's most interesting idea. The reported findings are 92% overall accuracy for autism identification using DeepFace features and a multilayer perceptron, with 10-fold cross-validation on the training portion, and qualitative statements for gelotophobia: the system classified it successfully where facial expressions were clear, DeepFace alone was insufficient where cues were ambiguous, and adding the questionnaire improved reliability. The evidential standing of the two halves is entirely different. The 92% is a real number from a real classification experiment, subject to the provenance and splitting problems that this review has recorded for six other studies on the same corpus. The gelotophobia statements are descriptions of an intended workflow, since no gelotophobia assessment was performed on any person and no metric of any kind is reported for that task." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-137" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Published 2025 by the Information Systems department at Mansoura University, Egypt, with no funding and no declared conflicts. The framing identifies a genuinely under-researched problem: gelotophobia, the fear of being laughed at, affects around 6% of neurotypical people but up to 45% of autistic people, reaching 41.98% in adolescents with high-functioning autism, and the paper's account of the condition - internalised belief, heightened rejection sensitivity, shame, social withdrawal - is written from the experiencing person's perspective in a way that little else in this corpus manages. Identifying a comorbidity that shapes autistic people's social participation, and proposing to route ambiguous cases to a validated self-report instrument rather than forcing a facial judgement, are both good instincts. For the review the study contributes three things, all cautionary. It is the seventh built on the Kaggle facial-image corpus family, and its own description - images 'collected from platforms such as Kaggle and ASD-related websites' - restates the provenance confound that makes every figure in that branch uninterpretable, while its choice of DeepFace, a face-recognition library whose embeddings encode individual identity, maximises the exposure that an image-level split with an unreported number of distinct children creates. It applies an emotion classifier trained on neurotypical faces to autistic children without fine-tuning, which a study charted elsewhere in this review shows empirically to degrade substantially. And it is the only paper in the corpus whose headline contribution rests on no measurement at all: the abstract states that the GELOPH<15> 'was administered to confirm the diagnosis' while the ethics section states it 'was not directly administered to participants in this study' and that 'no direct contact with human participants occurred', so the gelotophobia system was never evaluated against any ground truth, no metric for it appears anywhere, and the conclusion nevertheless claims its effectiveness has been demonstrated. That distinction matters for the synthesis: most of the problems documented in this review concern measurements that are confounded or inflated, whereas here a diagnostic claim is advanced for a construct on which nothing was measured. The paper's own limitations section, which names uncontrolled demographics, the absence of external validation and the fact that its proposed architectures were never implemented, is candid enough to make clear that the authors understood the work as preliminary; the abstract and conclusion do not." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-137" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "DeepFace supplies 100,352 features per image, which a multilayer perceptron then classifies, with several other machine learning models compared and the perceptron reported as best on testing accuracy. Ten-fold cross-validation was applied within the training portion, which is the correct arrangement when a separate test partition exists. Two modelling concerns. The feature count exceeds the image count by a factor of about 34, with no dimensionality reduction or feature selection described, which is an extreme ratio for a shallow classifier and is not commented on. And DeepFace's principal representation is a face-recognition embedding optimised to encode individual identity - so the features are, by design, maximally informative about which person a face belongs to, which is exactly the information an image-level split makes exploitable when multiple images of the same child are present." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-137" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No attribution, saliency, feature analysis or error analysis is reported for either component. The omission is more consequential here than in most of the branch because of what the features are: 100,352 DeepFace values, dominated by an identity-verification embedding, offer no interpretable handle at all, and nothing in the paper indicates whether the classifier responds to facial morphology, expression, image quality or the photographic character of images sourced from autism-related websites. For the gelotophobia component there is nothing to interpret, since there is no measured output to explain." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-137" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Standard clinical framing with an unusually sympathetic account of the second condition. Autism is 'a neurodevelopmental disorder' characterised by 'difficulties in social interactions, communication impairments, repetitive behaviors, and restricted interests', with the spectrum concept explained accurately as reflecting variation in severity and ability. The description of gelotophobia is the paper's best writing and is genuinely person-centred: individuals 'may perceive all laughter as negatively directed at them, interpreting it as a form of mockery or threat, which often leads to social withdrawal and isolation', and the account traces how the belief becomes internalised into 'heightened sensitivity to rejection, feelings of shame, and an increased tendency toward social avoidance'. Framing a condition through the person's experience of it, rather than through observable deficit, is rare in this corpus. The paper also refers to autistic people as 'populations considered socially vulnerable', which is an acknowledgement of social position rather than individual pathology. Against this, the study's method sits awkwardly with its own framing: a condition defined by an internal appraisal of others' intentions is to be detected from a photograph of a face. No autistic person or organisation is reported as involved." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-137" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Five directions, well matched to the acknowledged limitations: expand and diversify the dataset with a demographically varied sample of children with and without autism and gelotophobia; validate on independent cohorts from multiple institutions; try ResNet-50, EfficientNet-B4 and MobileNet-V3; integrate eye tracking, speech prosody and body language to capture dynamic signals; and build a real-time prototype. The first of these - actually collecting data from children assessed for both conditions - is what the study would have needed to support any of its gelotophobia claims, and its appearance as future work is an implicit acknowledgement that no such data underlie the present paper. Not proposed: obtaining a diagnostic reference standard for autism, participant-level splitting, or reporting per-class performance." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-137" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Mixed. Reported: corpus size, class balance and Kaggle location; the split proportions with absolute counts (2,653 training, 279 testing); 100,352 features per image; the library stack with version numbers; the hardware; the classifier family; the cross-validation scheme; a numbered ethics approval; and the GELOPH<15> instrument itself as a supplementary file. Not reported: the number of distinct children in the corpus, any demographic composition, whether splitting respected individuals, any diagnostic ascertainment for either target, hyperparameters for any model, per-class metrics or confusion matrices, and code. A reader could reconstruct the computational pipeline and could not evaluate what it was applied to." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-137" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No acquisition equipment. Analysis used an Intel Core i7-11800H with 16 GB RAM under Windows 10, with Python 3.9.7, scikit-learn 0.24.2, TensorFlow 2.9.2 and DeepFace 0.0.93, plus PyTorch for the multilayer perceptron and CUDA for acceleration. Versioning every library is good practice and makes the computational pipeline reproducible. The consequential detail is the choice of DeepFace as the feature extractor: it is a face-recognition and emotion-classification library trained on adult neurotypical face corpora for identity verification and basic-emotion classification, and it is applied here without fine-tuning to children's faces for a diagnostic task. A study charted elsewhere in this review showed directly that an affect model trained on neurotypical faces performs poorly on autistic children and improves markedly when fine-tuned on autistic data; that finding applies to the emotion half of the pipeline here and is not cited or addressed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-137" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A 100,352-dimensional DeepFace feature vector per image. Whole-image transfer through a pretrained face model retains every source-dependent property of the photograph, and the identity-encoding character of the embedding makes the representation particularly ill-suited to a corpus split at image level with an unreported number of distinct children. No landmark geometry, morphometric measurement or temporal information is used, so no specific facial construct is operationalised." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-137" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two separate problems, of different kinds. For the autism task, the evaluation is a 90.48/9.51 image-level split into 2,653 training and 279 testing images, with 10-fold cross-validation on the training portion. The number of distinct children behind 2,932 images is never stated and no participant-level partitioning is described, so in a web-assembled corpus where a single child may contribute several images, images of the same child can fall on both sides of the boundary - the failure this review has now recorded for six other studies on this corpus family. The exposure is greater here than usual because DeepFace's features are optimised for face identity, so the representation is specifically good at the thing an image-level split leaves exploitable. Compounding this, the reported 92% is described as spanning 'both training and testing datasets', so no held-out figure is given separately, and no per-class metric or interval accompanies it. The authors do identify the absence of an external validation cohort, correctly noting that 10-fold cross-validation addresses overfitting but not generalisability. None of this touches the more basic issue that the autistic images were harvested from autism-related websites, so the label encodes provenance and no splitting scheme could separate that from a facial phenotype. For the gelotophobia task, the problem is not validation but the absence of anything to validate. The ethics section states that the GELOPH<15> 'was not directly administered to participants in this study' and that 'no direct contact with human participants occurred', which contradicts the abstract's assertion that it 'was administered to confirm the diagnosis' and means that no image in the corpus carries a gelotophobia label of any kind. There is therefore no ground truth, no held-out set, no metric and no result - and the conclusion nonetheless states that the study 'demonstrates the effectiveness of combining deep learning techniques with validated diagnostic tools for detecting gelotophobia'. Across this whole review, this is the only instance of a primary claim for which no measurement of any kind was performed, and it is worth distinguishing in the synthesis from the many studies whose measurements are confounded or inflated." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-137" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None for autism and none for gelotophobia, with the second absence being the more remarkable. The autism label reflects folder membership in a public image collection assembled partly from autism-related websites; no diagnostic instrument, clinician judgement or medical record is cited for any image, and the paper does not identify this as a limitation. For gelotophobia the position is stronger still: the ethics section states that the GELOPH<15> questionnaire 'was not directly administered to participants in this study; it was used solely as a reference from previously validated research' and that 'no direct contact with human participants occurred'. This directly contradicts the abstract, which says that in ambiguous cases 'the GELOPH<15> questionnaire, consisting of 15 items, was administered to confirm the diagnosis'. Taking the ethics statement as authoritative - as its specificity and its consistency with the study's design require - no person in this study was ever assessed for gelotophobia, so the system's gelotophobia outputs were never compared with anything. The conclusion nonetheless states that the study 'demonstrates the effectiveness of combining deep learning techniques with validated diagnostic tools for detecting gelotophobia'. This is the clearest instance in the review of a headline claim resting on no measurement whatever, and it is distinct in kind from the label-quality problems recorded elsewhere: those studies have bad ground truth, this component has none." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-137" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No severity, cognitive, adaptive or language measure exists for any image. The GELOPH<15>, which would have supplied a continuous gelotophobia score, was not administered to anyone." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-137" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "There are no participants. The units are 2,932 facial images, balanced at 1,466 per class, described in the results as 'collected from platforms such as Kaggle and ASD-related websites'. The number of distinct children behind those images is never stated, and no age, sex, ancestry, cognitive, language or clinical information is available for either class - an absence the authors acknowledge directly, noting that 'demographic variables, such as age, gender, and cultural background, were not explicitly controlled'. This is the seventh study in this review built on the Kaggle facial-image corpus family, and the description of the autistic class as sourced partly from autism-related websites places it squarely in the same category as the others: the label records where an image was found rather than any assessment of the child in it. For the paper's second target, gelotophobia, there are not even images with labels - no person was assessed for gelotophobia by any means, so that half of the study has no sample at all." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-137" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: social anxiety; emotion recognition; machine learning; facial expression analysis; neurodevelopmental disorders; GELOPH<15>; residual network with 50 layers; ResNet-50; artificial intelligence. Two of the nine name ResNet-50, which the limitations section states was not implemented in this study and which appears only as future work - so the keyword list advertises a method the paper did not use. Terms carried through the body: DeepFace, multilayer perceptron, gelotophobia, comorbidity, high-functioning autism, data mining, computer-aided diagnosis." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-137" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No study setting. The corpus is a public assembly described as drawn 'from platforms such as Kaggle and ASD-related websites', which is the construction principle this review has documented across the facial-image branch: the autistic class harvested from autism-associated online content, the comparison class from elsewhere, with the result that collection route is the one documented difference between the two populations of images. Nothing in the paper's design addresses this, and no per-source analysis is possible from what is reported." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-137" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The limitations section is one of the better ones in this branch and names five issues: the corpus of public 2D images may not represent the diversity of facial expressions in the target groups because age, gender and cultural background were not controlled; ten-fold cross-validation mitigates overfitting but there is no external validation cohort; subtle emotional states such as fear and disgust are hard to distinguish because they involve minimal muscle movement; the approach uses only static images while real interactions are dynamic and multimodal; and the advanced architectures and multimodal inputs proposed as future work were not implemented. Naming uncontrolled demographics and the absence of external validation directly is creditable. What is not addressed: the autistic images come from autism-related websites, so the label records provenance rather than diagnosis, and no diagnostic reference standard exists for autism at all; the split is at image level with the number of distinct children unreported, opening the leakage route that this review has documented for six other studies on this corpus family, and DeepFace's identity-optimised embeddings make that exposure greater than for a generic feature extractor; the 92% is reported as spanning training and testing together with no separate held-out figure; no sensitivity, specificity, confusion matrix or interval is given for autism and no metric at all for gelotophobia; the feature-to-image ratio is about 34 to 1; and above all, the GELOPH<15> was never administered to anyone, so the gelotophobia component - the paper's headline contribution - has no ground truth, no evaluation and no measured result, while the conclusion claims its effectiveness has been demonstrated." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-137" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "JMIR Formative Research 9:e72115, 2025, Original Paper, with a companion article listed at formative.jmir.org/2026/1/e91833. The formative-research track is intended for early-stage and feasibility work, which fits a study proposing a diagnostic architecture ahead of collecting the data to evaluate it. The venue's structure produced the ethics, funding, data-availability and competing-interest declarations, and the ethics statement is more detailed than most in this branch - it is also the statement that reveals the contradiction with the abstract about whether the GELOPH<15> was administered. That a paper whose abstract, objective and conclusion all centre on gelotophobia detection was published while its own ethics section records that no gelotophobia assessment took place is a reviewing gap that the structured declarations made visible rather than concealed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-137" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Entirely absent, and acknowledged as such. No age, sex, ancestry, socioeconomic, cognitive or comorbidity information exists for either image class, and the authors state plainly that age, gender and cultural background 'were not explicitly controlled'. Two consequences follow that the acknowledgement does not address. Facial classifiers key strongly on apparent age and on sex-linked morphology, and autism is diagnosed roughly four times more often in boys, so an unmeasured sex imbalance between a class harvested from autism-related sources and a class harvested elsewhere is a live and untestable alternative explanation for the reported 92%. And DeepFace, the feature extractor, is a face-recognition library whose embeddings are optimised to encode individual identity - which makes demographic attributes among the properties most strongly represented in the 100,352 features fed to the classifier. Comorbidity is a further specific gap here, since the paper's own second target is a comorbid condition whose prevalence in autism it puts at up to 45%, yet no comorbidity is recorded for any image." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-137" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:study_design "Retrospective algorithm-development study on a public facial-image corpus, with a second proposed diagnostic component for which no data were collected" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: ; comparison: 1,466 images of neurotypical children" ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "ae6892c4c64bd91090e52080416a2e9ee747e3c99c5995227605e30d5aca418f" ;
