<urn:slr:article:4a5b35736368> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) is a severe condition related to brain development that impairs communication and interaction with others. It is a ”developmental disorder” because symptoms generally appear in the ﬁrst two years of life or later. Till that age, children behave pretty similar to typically developing individuals. Tracking the symptoms leading to autism is challenging because of the wide variation in the type and severity of the disorder. One of the pitfalls is that many countries, including Pakistan, do not track or report their autism cases. In this paper, we have shown how we performed predictive analysis after collecting data from the only government-run Child Psychiatry Department in Pakistan, Mayo Hospital, Lahore. We also collected data from a private school made explicitly for children with autism in Lahore. Data of a total of 100 autistic children under the age of 12 was collected. To avoid any class imbalance problem, we also collected data of 100 healthy children. After performing various machine learning algorithms on our data, our model managed to classify children with autism with an accuracy of 95%. To the best of our knowledge, no such work has been done regarding autism in Pakistan before. This research aims to create a supervised machine learning model that can classify ASD individuals in much less time and effort compared to currently practiced diagnostic procedures and help us visualize common causes leading up to it so that appropriate lifestyle changes can be made accordingly. In the future, the number of records can be increased, and accuracy can be maximized even further. Our model can be used by various physicians and parents of children with ASD for early prediction and for visualizing common causes leading up to it." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Farooqi N; Bukhari F; Iqbal W (2021). Predictive Analysis of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) using Machine Learning. *2021 International Conference on Frontiers of Information Technology (FIT)*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Bukhari F",
        "Farooqi N",
        "Iqbal W" ;
    dcterms:title "Predictive Analysis of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) using Machine Learning" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_02fa4215" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2021 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "No matching described or tested, while sex, age and birth order are model features." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "200 children cross-sectionally cannot support the stated aim of identifying causes for lifestyle modification." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Recruitment sites given for the autistic group only; no ages, sexes, or source for the comparison group." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the comparison group screened?" ;
                            slr:justification "Described only as 'healthy children' with no source or assessment." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "No instrument, criteria or clinician; the label is a self-reported questionnaire field." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is recall bias addressed?" ;
                            slr:justification "Most items are retrospective caregiver recollections about infancy and pregnancy, with no acknowledgement that recall differs systematically after a diagnosis." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Does the data type match the review's behavioural criterion?" ;
                            slr:justification "Only about six of 21 items concern child behaviour, and those are reported rather than measured." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the data collection described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "The 21-item questionnaire is fully documented with item wording and types; collection mode differences and response handling are not." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are sensitivity and specificity reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Stated to have been calculated but never tabulated, though derivable from the confusion matrices." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is multiplicity across the results grid addressed?" ;
                            slr:justification "Twenty-seven algorithm-by-selection cells with the maximum quoted and no correction or intervals." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the selected features disclosed?" ;
                            slr:justification "No selection method's retained set is reported, so the stated aim of identifying important factors is unmet." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is feature selection independent of the reported result?" ;
                            slr:justification "SelectKBest's k set to 17 explicitly because it gave the best results." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is uncertainty quantified?" ;
                            slr:justification "No confidence intervals; one test case is worth 2.5 accuracy points." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is any cross-validation or repeated splitting performed?" ;
                            slr:justification "A single 80/20 split giving 40 test children." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the scope of application stated honestly?" ;
                            slr:justification "The authors state the model needs professional supervision and that the local dataset should not be applied elsewhere without consultation." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No release or availability statement." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the data-infrastructure context documented?" ;
                            slr:justification "The absence of any autism case tracking in Pakistan and of stored records at the country's only government child psychiatry department is set out concretely." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No approval, consent or governance statement for data including maternal substance use, antidepressant use and miscarriage history." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "No code, hyperparameters or seed." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are descriptive claims supported by comparison?" ;
                            slr:justification "Conclusions list single-group frequencies about maternal vaccination and parental smoking without control rates, base rates or tests, in a paper promising to identify causes for lifestyle change." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The paper's stated aim includes helping 'visualize common causes leading up to' autism, and interpretability is therefore central to its purpose - but no interpretability analysis is performed. No feature importances, no selected feature lists, no coefficients, no decision rules. What is offered instead is a list of narrative observations in the conclusion, each a frequency within the autistic group presented without the corresponding control-group frequency. Two of these are actively hazardous as written: 'most of the mothers were vaccinated during pregnancies' and 'almost 50 percent of the parents smoked before giving birth to children with autism' both appear in a list introduced as observations about autism, in a paper whose abstract promises to identify causes so that lifestyle changes can be made. Without a comparison rate these statements are uninformative, and in the vaccination case the juxtaposition risks reinforcing a well-documented and thoroughly refuted misattribution." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-068" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The Child Psychiatry Department at Mayo Hospital Lahore, described as the only government-run child psychiatry department in Pakistan and seeing on average two patients daily, together with a private school for autistic children in Lahore. Questionnaires were administered on paper at the school and via Google Forms otherwise. No setting is described for the comparison group." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-068" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Essentially none. No ages, means, standard deviations, sex counts or matching statistics are reported for either group. The age eligibility is stated inconsistently - under 12 in the abstract and under 10 in the methods. Sex, age and birth order are model features while remaining undescribed at group level, so the classifier's reliance on demographic separation cannot be assessed. Histograms of the distributions are presented as figures but no counts are given in text or table. No socioeconomic data beyond parental education, which is itself a model feature." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-068" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The weakest element. A single 80/20 split of 200 children gives a 40-child test set, with no cross-validation, no repetition across splits and no separate tuning set. SelectKBest's k was chosen as 17 'as it gave the best results', which tunes a selection parameter against the reported evaluation. Twenty-seven algorithm-by-selection combinations were evaluated and the maximum quoted in the abstract. There is no independent cohort, no external validation and no confidence interval, so the reported 95% cannot be distinguished from the 90% cells by anything the paper provides." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-068" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No severity, cognitive, language or adaptive measure. Family history of cognitive disability is collected as a predictor rather than as a characterisation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-068" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Expand the dataset; build a mobile application for clinicians; maintain historical records and raise mental health awareness; and strengthen trait correlations with more data. The authors add two cautions that function as recommendations - that the model requires professional supervision in use, and that the dataset is local and should not be transferred to other populations without consultation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-068" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2021 International Conference on Frontiers of Information Technology (FIT), pages 305-309 - a general computing conference with no clinical remit." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-068" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups of 100 children each: 100 autistic children recruited from the Child Psychiatry Department at Mayo Hospital Lahore and a private autism school, and 100 children described as healthy whose recruitment source is never stated. The comparison group was collected specifically 'to ensure that our model is trained without any class imbalance problem', so its purpose is stated as statistical rather than clinical. No other-diagnosis comparison group." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-068" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Twenty-one questionnaire items binary- or one-hot-encoded, reduced by three alternative selection schemes. The representation is transparent and the questionnaire is fully documented, which makes it reproducible as an instrument even though the analysis is not. The critical gap is that the outcome of selection is never reported: SelectKBest retained 17 of the features, but which 17 is not stated, and the variance-threshold and model-based schemes likewise report no retained sets." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-068" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Acknowledged: the dataset should be enlarged for reliability; the model 'should be used with care and under supervision of some professional'; and 'this is a local dataset which should not be applied elsewhere in the world without consultation'. The last two are genuine scoping cautions that most of the corpus omits. Not acknowledged: no diagnostic instrument for either group; a single 80/20 split with a 40-child test set and no cross-validation; SelectKBest's k tuned to give the best results; the maximum reported across 27 cells; no group demographics despite sex, age and birth order being model features; no ethics approval; recall bias in retrospective caregiver report, which differs systematically between parents of diagnosed and undiagnosed children; and, most consequentially, the presentation of single-group frequencies about maternal vaccination and parental smoking as observations about autism without any control comparison or base rate." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-068" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Nine classifiers spanning probabilistic, instance-based, linear, tree, ensemble and kernel families, each described in a sentence with no rationale for inclusion and no hyperparameters. Breadth substitutes for justification. That six of the 27 cells reach 95% while the rest cluster between 90% and 92.5% - a spread of two test cases - indicates the comparison is not resolving real differences between algorithms." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-068" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; a single questionnaire per family with no follow-up, though the items ask retrospectively about the perinatal period and early childhood. Data include maternal substance use, antidepressant use and miscarriage history - sensitive material about identifiable families collected partly through a commercial web form. No ethics committee approval, consent procedure, anonymisation policy or data-handling statement appears anywhere in the paper." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-068" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Poor. Reported: the full 21-item questionnaire with each item's type and wording, the encoding scheme, the three feature-selection methods with their parameters, the nine algorithms with brief descriptions, the 80/20 split ratio, a complete 27-cell accuracy table, and confusion matrices for all nine algorithms under one selection scheme. Absent: any diagnostic instrument, ethics approval, group demographics, recruitment source for the comparison group, the identity of features retained by any selection method, all hyperparameters, the random seed, sensitivity and specificity values despite being said to have been computed, and any data or code. The questionnaire itself is the one element documented well enough to be reused." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-068" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No multimodal fusion. What the study does combine is heterogeneous variable types within one questionnaire - child behaviour, perinatal history, family history and parental lifestyle - but no analysis separates their contributions, no feature importances are reported, and the retained feature sets under each selection scheme are never disclosed. Since the design's stated purpose is to identify which factors matter, this omission removes the paper's principal claimed contribution: a reader cannot tell whether classification rests on the developmental items, on demographics such as sex and birth order, or on parental background variables." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-068" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Paper questionnaires and Google Forms for collection; Python with scikit-learn for encoding, feature selection and classification. No hardware, versions, seeds or hyperparameters are reported." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-068" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No behavioural task and no observation of any kind. All data are caregiver responses to a 21-item questionnaire, most of it retrospective - the age at which a child first walked or spoke, whether they rocked in a crib as a baby, whether they were ever thought deaf, and maternal behaviours during a pregnancy that occurred years earlier. Recall accuracy for such items is known to be limited and to differ systematically between parents of diagnosed and undiagnosed children, since a diagnosis reframes recollection of early development. The paper does not address recall bias." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-068" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Index terms: autism, autism spectrum disorder, machine learning, predictive analysis, ASD. Four of five are generic; 'predictive analysis' is the operative framing and signals an aetiological ambition the design does not support." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-068" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No behaviour is observed. Six of 21 items concern the child's behaviour as recalled by a caregiver - crib rocking, suspected deafness, insistence on particular music, reaction to bright lights, colours or unusual sounds, age of walking and age of first words - which together sample early sensory reactivity, repetitive movement and developmental milestone timing. The remaining fifteen items are perinatal, familial and parental variables that are not behavioural at all. This composition is the principal reason the record sits at the boundary of the review's scope and should be distinguished in the per-group analysis from directly instrumented behavioural studies." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-068" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: build the first autism prediction model for Pakistan from a purpose-collected questionnaire and identify commonly associated background factors. Main findings: 95% best accuracy across several algorithm-and-selection combinations, with 90% sensitivity and 100% specificity in the best confusion matrices on a 40-child test set; and a set of narrative single-group observations about birth order, sex, family history, sensory reactivity, maternal vaccination and parental smoking, none accompanied by control comparison or statistical test." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-068" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. The autistic group is defined by recruitment from a child psychiatry department or a specialist school, with no diagnostic instrument, criteria, clinician or date reported anywhere. The comparison group has no stated screening or source. The label is simply a questionnaire field, 'ASD diagnosed', answered yes or no. This is the weakest ascertainment among included records, and it is consequential because the entire study is a supervised classification of that label." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-068" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with autism', 'autistic children', 'ASD individuals', 'healthy children'. The comparison group is 'healthy', and autism is introduced as 'a severe condition related to brain development that impairs communication and interaction with others'. The framing is disease-oriented, and the stated purpose of identifying 'common causes leading up to it so that appropriate lifestyle changes can be made accordingly' implies modifiable aetiology in a way the design cannot support." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-068" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2021. The framing is about data absence rather than methodological gap, and on that point it is informative and specific. The authors note that 'no reliable epidemiological data on the prevalence of autism exists' for South Asia, that Pakistan does not track or report autism cases, that the country's only government-run child psychiatry department averages two patients a day, and that 'even in that department, no such reasonable measures have been taken to store data in any format', so data 'had to be collected from scratch'. That account of health-system data infrastructure is a genuine contribution to the corpus's picture of where this research happens and under what constraints, and it distinguishes the paper's problem from the throughput and objectivity arguments that dominate elsewhere. The scientific execution does not match the framing: the stated aim of identifying causes for lifestyle modification is beyond what a 200-child cross-sectional questionnaire without diagnostic ascertainment can address, and the conclusions drawn from it are single-group frequencies presented without comparison." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-068" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy for all 27 combinations, confusion matrices for nine algorithms under one scheme, and ROC curves. Providing the confusion matrices is what allows sensitivity and specificity to be derived - random forest and SVM both achieve 18 of 20 autistic children detected with all 20 comparison children correctly classified - even though the paper states these were calculated but does not tabulate them. Missing: confidence intervals, any statistical comparison, and any acknowledgement that a 40-child test set makes each case worth 2.5 accuracy points." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-068" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Single questionnaire completion per child" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional case-control classification on a purpose-collected questionnaire dataset" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Children with autism under 12 (stated elsewhere as under 10)" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 100; comparison: 100, collected 'to ensure that our model is trained without any class imbalance problem'" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "A 21-item caregiver questionnaire administered to 100 autistic and 100 non-autistic children in Lahore supported classification at 95% accuracy across several algorithms, with the best confusion matrices showing 90% sensitivity and 100% specificity on a 40-child test set. The paper's stated broader aim - identifying 'common causes leading up to' autism - is not met: no feature importances are repo",
                        "Best accuracy 95% - achieved by random forest and SVM under SelectKBest, random forest and XGBoost under variance thresholding, and SVM and gradient boosting under SelectFromModel. The remaining cells range from 90% to 92.5%. Confusion matrices under SelectKBest show test sets of 40: random forest and SVM each gave 18 true positives, 2 false negatives, 0 false positives and 20 true negatives, corr" ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy for all 27 combinations, plus a confusion matrix for each of the nine algorithms under SelectKBest and ROC curves. Sensitivity and specificity are said to have been calculated from the confusion matrices but their values are not tabulated" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Best accuracy 95% - achieved by random forest and SVM under SelectKBest, random forest and XGBoost under variance thresholding, and SVM and gradient boosting under SelectFromModel. The remaining cells range from 90% to 92.5%. Confusion matrices under SelectKBest show test sets of 40: random forest and SVM each gave 18 true positives, 2 false negatives, 0 false positives and 20 true negatives, corr; A 21-item caregiver questionnaire administered to 100 autistic and 100 non-autistic children in Lahore supported classification at 95% accuracy across several algorithms, with the best confusion matrices showing 90% sensitivity and 100% specificity on a 40-child test set. The paper's stated broader aim - identifying 'common causes leading up to' autism - is not met: no feature importances are repo" ] ] ;
