<urn:slr:article:63f4f1af3671> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Autism spectrum disorder (ASD) is a developmental disorder with a life-span disability. While diagnostic instruments have been developed and qualiﬁed based on the accuracy of the discrimination of children with ASD from typical development (TD) children, the stability of such procedures can be disrupted by limitations pertaining to time expenses and the subjectivity of clinicians. Consequently, automated diagnostic methods have been developed for acquiring objective measures of autism, and in various ﬁelds of research, vocal characteristics have not only been reported as distinctive characteristics by clinicians, but have also shown promising performance in several studies utilizing deep learning models based on the automated discrimination of children with ASD from children with TD. However, diﬃculties still exist in terms of the characteristics of the data, the complexity of the analysis, and the lack of arranged data caused by the low accessibility for diagnosis and the need to secure anonymity. In order to address these issues, we introduce a pre-trained feature extraction auto-encoder model and a joint optimization scheme, which can achieve robustness for widely distributed and unreﬁned data using a deep-learning-based method for the detection of autism that utilizes various models. By adopting this auto-encoder-based feature extraction and joint optimization in the extended version of the Geneva minimalistic acoustic parameter set (eGeMAPS) speech feature data set, we acquire improved performance in the detection of ASD in infants compared to the raw data set." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Lee JH; Lee GW; Bong G; Yoo HJ; Kim HK (2020). Deep-Learning-Based Detection of Infants with Autism Spectrum Disorder Using Auto-Encoder Feature Representation. *Sensors, 20(23), Article 6762. https*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Bong G",
        "Kim HK",
        "Lee GW",
        "Lee JH",
        "Yoo HJ" ;
    dcterms:title "Deep-Learning-Based Detection of Infants with Autism Spectrum Disorder Using Auto-Encoder Feature Representation" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_8be557e3" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2020 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "Age distributions barely overlap in a window where vocal development changes month to month." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "10 autistic infants against 29 typically developing, which the authors describe themselves as insufficient and unbalanced." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the comparison group clinically relevant?" ;
                            slr:justification "Controls are infants brought for diagnostic assessment who were assessed as typically developing, not a community convenience sample." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Site, period, per-infant age, sex and both diagnosis dates are tabulated for all 39 participants." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Room dimensions, ambient noise level, sampling rates, segmentation rules and the five-category vocalisation taxonomy are all specified." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "Seven instruments plus psychiatrist DSM-5 best-estimate, applied identically to both groups." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "eGeMAPS extraction is automatic and label-blind, but the 54-feature baseline was selected by Mann-Whitney tests against the labels, and the auto-encoder's auxiliary head is label-supervised by design." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the recording ecologically valid?" ;
                            slr:justification "Audio comes from the clinical diagnostic session itself rather than a contrived elicitation task." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is there any independent or external validation?" ;
                            slr:justification "Single site, single cohort, no held-out infants, no external replication." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is class imbalance accounted for in reporting?" ;
                            slr:justification "UAR reported alongside accuracy, adopted explicitly from the INTERSPEECH 2009 Emotion Challenge for imbalanced classes." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are confusion matrices reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Full matrices for all four models, which is what makes the low autistic-class sensitivity recoverable." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are performance metrics labelled correctly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Precision and recall are transposed in the results table; the reported UAR confirms the correct assignment." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the validation split at the participant level?" ;
                            slr:justification "Splitting is by utterance across 1,125 utterances from 39 infants; the same speaker appears on both sides of each fold." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are baselines comparable and fairly configured?" ;
                            slr:justification "BLSTM held at eight cells across conditions to match prior work, so the auto-encoder contribution is isolated." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Not released; the paper argues that anonymity requirements are why such data cannot circulate." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Architecture and optimiser named; no code, no hyperparameters for learning rate, batch size, dropout or loss weighting." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Two IRB approval numbers with explicit statement of fully anonymized retrospective use." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a negative result reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Mann-Whitney feature selection degraded performance relative to using all 88 features, reported and explained rather than omitted." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are limitations stated candidly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Results described as poor by deep-learning standards and the improvement as marginal; small, dispersed, unbalanced data named repeatedly." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Five-fold cross-validation with a separate randomly selected 10% test set balanced across vocalisation categories, early stopping on validation loss, and best-model checkpointing - properly structured as a protocol. The defect is the unit of division: splitting is by utterance, not by infant, so with 1,125 utterances from 39 children the same speaker appears on both sides of every fold, and the models can exploit speaker identity rather than autism-related acoustics. Given that the two classes are also near-disjoint in age, speaker-level information is doubly informative about the label. No independent test cohort, no external site, no per-infant aggregation, and no comparison against the seven clinical instruments the labels came from." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-040" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Funded by the Korean IITP grant No. 2019-0-00330, 'Development of AI Technology for Early Screening of Infant/Child Autism Spectrum Disorders based on Cognition of the Psychological Behavior and Response' - a national programme explicitly targeting AI-based early autism screening, which situates this paper within a funded state initiative rather than an isolated academic effort." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ_funding_note" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-040" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Autistic group: n = 10, 4 male / 6 female, mean age 19.20 +/- 2.52 months, all aged 12-24 months. Typically developing group: n = 29, 19 male / 10 female, mean age 14.72 +/- 2.45 months, all aged 6-18 months. Age separation is near-total: every infant in the 6-12 month band is TD and every infant in the 18-24 month band is ASD, with overlap confined to the 12-18 month band. The sex ratio is inverted relative to autism's usual epidemiology (40% male in the autistic group versus 66% in the TD group) and is not addressed. Country and site are single (South Korea, SNUBH); no socioeconomic or ethnicity data. Per-infant age, sex, initial diagnosis date and definite final diagnosis date are tabulated for all 39 participants, which is unusually transparent. Note that the running text states the ASD mean age as 14.72 months, contradicting the table - an apparent transposition with the TD figure." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-040" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Good on architecture and preprocessing, thin on training detail. Reported: layer dimensions, auxiliary output formulation with equations, frame length and overlap, five-frame grouping, normalisation fitted on training data only, batch normalisation, ReLU, dropout, ADAM, early stopping with 100-epoch patience, best-model checkpointing, and the utterance decision rule (mean frame-wise class index above 0.5). Not reported: learning rate, batch size, dropout rate, auto-encoder pretraining schedule, the relative weighting of reconstruction and auxiliary losses in the joint objective, and whether SVM hyperparameters were tuned. No code and, by design, no data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-040" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No experimental task at all - and this is a deliberate methodological position rather than an omission. Audio comes from the clinical diagnostic procedure itself, so what the models see is the same behaviour a clinician sees, recorded in situ with clinician speech, parent speech, toy noise and chair scraping all present. The cost is that infant-only segments must be isolated by hand and that the recording contains no controlled elicitation; the benefit is ecological validity and the possibility of retrofitting to sessions already being recorded." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-040" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional per infant, with a definite final diagnosis recorded about a month after the initial examination. Data are clinical audio of infants, and the governance reporting is exemplary by corpus standards: two IRB approval numbers, explicit statement that analysis used fully anonymized retrospective data, and - unusually - the paper treats infant anonymity as a first-order constraint shaping what data can ever exist in this field, which is the stated motivation for the small-data method it develops." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-040" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: determine whether auto-encoder-based learned features, jointly optimised with the classifier, improve automated autism detection from infant vocalisations under small, unbalanced, unrefined data conditions. Main findings: the jointly optimised AE+BLSTM outperformed vanilla BLSTM and SVM on accuracy (0.6818), F1 (0.5457) and UAR (0.6509); Mann-Whitney-based selection of 54 eGeMAPS features degraded rather than improved performance; t-SNE shows eGeMAPS-88 and eGeMAPS-54 spaces nearly identical and heavily overlapped while the auto-encoder space is visibly redistributed; and at the categorical level autistic infants produced proportionally fewer first words and more atypical vocalisation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-040" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Infant vocalisation captured passively during clinical assessment - one syllable utterances, canonical babbling, non-syllabic babbling, first words, and atypical voice including screaming and crying. Uniquely in the corpus, the vocalisations are categorised by developmental stage before modelling, so the acoustic analysis sits on top of an explicit linguistic-development taxonomy rather than treating all speech as homogeneous." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-040" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2020. The framing cites prevalence rising from 1 in 150 children in 2000 to 1 in 54 in 2016, and identifies the bottleneck as the gold-standard behavioural assessments themselves - time-consuming, requiring multidisciplinary teams, and unstable because of accessibility, subjectivity and 'interpretive bias between professions'. Its most distinctive contribution to the corpus's framing is a data-availability argument: it names infant anonymity requirements and parental non-recognition at early developmental stages as the structural reasons the field's data is small, dispersed by sex, age and vocalisation count, and unrefined, and it observes that 'these problems were typically overlooked by previous research with controlled and small amounts of data'. The method is then designed for those conditions rather than assuming they will be solved." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-040" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Full confusion matrices for all four models, plus accuracy, precision, recall, F1 and unweighted average recall - UAR explicitly adopted from the INTERSPEECH 2009 Emotion Challenge because the classes are imbalanced. Reporting UAR alongside accuracy is good practice and rare in this corpus. Two caveats: precision and recall are transposed in the table (the printed 0.4526 for the AE model is autistic-class recall, as the reported UAR confirms), and all metrics are utterance-level, so no per-infant performance can be derived. Derived from the matrices, autistic-class sensitivity is 45.3% (AE), 41.3% (eGeMAPS-88), 35.8% (eGeMAPS-54) and 13.1% (SVM), against specificities of 84.9%, 84.8%, 84.2% and 97.2%." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-040" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No multimodal fusion - audio only, and the paper notes that video recordings existed but only their audio was used. The structural comparison it does make is across feature representations of the same modality: raw eGeMAPS-88, statistically selected eGeMAPS-54, and learned auto-encoder bottleneck features. Extension to linguistic features alongside acoustic ones for older children is named as future work." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-040" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No feature attribution or post hoc explanation. Interpretability is pursued at the representation level through t-SNE projections of the three feature spaces, which support a specific and testable claim: eGeMAPS-88 and eGeMAPS-54 are almost indistinguishable and heavily overlapped between classes, while the auto-encoder produces a more separable distribution. The authors offer a mechanistic reading - the bottleneck weights significant parameters up and ambiguous ones down - and a diagnosis of why statistical selection failed, namely that eGeMAPS contains temporal parameters relevant to vocalisation generally which confuse the ASD/TD distinction. The clinically interpretable layer is the vocalisation taxonomy rather than the model." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-040" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "eGeMAPS - all 88 parameters, per 25 ms frame with 10 ms overlap, normalised using training-set statistics and grouped into blocks of five consecutive frames so the BLSTM sees local temporal context; the SVM receives the same five frames flattened into a 440-dimensional vector. The auto-encoder compresses 88 to 54 dimensions, matched deliberately to the 54-feature statistical subset so the two dimensionality-reduction strategies are directly comparable. That comparison is the paper's cleanest result: learned compression beat statistical selection, and statistical selection was worse than no reduction at all." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-040" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Explicitly acknowledged: the data set is insufficient, dispersed and unbalanced; performance is 'relatively poor in comparison to the general classification tasks based on deep learning'; the improvement from joint optimisation is 'only marginal'; the BLSTM was deliberately kept small (eight cells) to match prior work rather than to maximise performance; and comparison with the prior study is only indirect because feature selection did not reproduce. Not acknowledged: the near-disjoint age distributions between groups, on the variable that most strongly governs infant vocalisation; the inverted sex ratio; the utterance-level rather than infant-level splitting, which allows the same child's voice into both training and evaluation folds; and the transposed precision and recall labels in the results table. Reading the confusion matrices directly also shows that autistic-class sensitivity is only 45.3% even for the best model, a figure the accuracy and UAR headline numbers obscure." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-040" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: auto-encoder; bidirectional long short-term memory (BLSTM); joint optimization; acoustic feature extraction; autism spectrum disorder. Four of the five are machine-learning terms and autism appears last - the paper's identity is a representation-learning contribution demonstrated on an autism task, and its comparisons (INTERSPEECH challenge metrics, eGeMAPS, openSMILE) situate it in the computational paralinguistics community." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-040" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'infants with autism spectrum disorder', 'children with ASD', 'children with TD', 'typical development'. Person-first and consistently paired ASD/TD. The framing is clinical-disorder oriented - 'a developmental disorder with a life-span disability' - but without the burden-of-disease register found elsewhere in the corpus. Notably the paper describes existing diagnosis as compromised by 'subjectivity and interpretive bias between professions' rather than by clinician inadequacy." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-040" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Increase the volume of infant speech data; refine the acoustic features and the auto-encoder architecture; adopt deeper and more current model structures, since the authors expect the auto-encoder's redistributed feature map to pay off more in complex models than in the deliberately small BLSTM used here; extend the approach to children aged 3-4 who can produce several sentences, adding linguistic features to acoustic ones; and generalise beyond autism to the detection of developmental delay." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-040" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Retrospective secondary analysis of audio extracted from video recordings of clinical autism diagnostic sessions conducted at Seoul National University Bundang Hospital between 2016 and 2018, under IRB approval for use of fully anonymized data from an existing study. Recording conditions are reported: two clinical rooms of stated dimensions with ambient noise around 40 dB." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-040" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Four models compared on identical data: a linear-kernel SVM baseline, a vanilla eight-cell BLSTM on 88 features, the same BLSTM on 54 statistically selected features, and a stacked auto-encoder jointly optimised with the BLSTM. The auto-encoder is semi-supervised, carrying an auxiliary binary ASD/TD softmax head on its latent layer so that the representation is shaped by the diagnostic target rather than by reconstruction alone. Joint optimisation of representation and classifier is the paper's actual contribution, and holding the BLSTM fixed at eight cells across conditions is what isolates it." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-040" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Sensors (MDPI), published as a Letter - a short-format engineering venue rather than a clinical journal, consistent with the paper's framing as a proof of possibility rather than a diagnostic study." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-040" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "48 kHz stereo recording downsampled and downmixed to 16 kHz mono; openSMILE for the 88 eGeMAPS parameters at 25 ms frames with 10 ms overlap; TensorFlow; t-SNE. Model components: a 88-70-54-70-88 stacked auto-encoder with an auxiliary softmax head on the latent layer, and a BLSTM with eight cells matched to prior work for comparability." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-040" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "K-CARS, SRS and SCQ were administered and could have supported severity analysis, but no severity, cognitive, language or adaptive scores are reported and no severity-stratified result is given. The five-category vocalisation labelling (single syllable, canonical babbling, non-syllabic babbling, first word, atypical voice) functions as a developmental-stage descriptor and is the only developmental characterisation reported, given per group as proportions of total vocalisation time." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-040" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups, 39 infants: 10 diagnosed autistic and 29 typically developing, all recruited as consecutive clinical cases undergoing diagnostic assessment at one hospital between 2016 and 2018. The typically developing group is not a convenience sample - these are infants brought for assessment who were assessed as typical, which makes them a clinically relevant comparison (children of diagnostic concern who turn out not to be autistic) rather than a community control group. Analysis is at the utterance level: 1,125 utterances, 475 autistic and 650 typically developing." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-040" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The most thorough in the corpus. Every participant was assessed with seven instruments: ADOS-2, ADI-R, BeDevel-Interview, BeDevel-Play, K-CARS (refined from CARS-2), the Social Communication Questionnaire and the Social Responsiveness Scale. The final diagnosis was the best clinical estimate by a licensed child psychiatrist against DSM-5 criteria using all available information. Both groups received the identical procedure, so the typically developing label carries the same evidential weight as the autistic one - a property almost no other study in this corpus has. A definite final diagnosis date roughly one month after initial examination is recorded for each infant." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-040" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "One clinical diagnostic session per infant, recorded between 2016 and 2018; a definite final diagnosis followed roughly one month after the initial examination for each participant" ;
                    slr:study_design "Retrospective secondary analysis of clinical audio, with utterance-level binary classification and a four-way model comparison" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Infants assessed for autism" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 10; comparison: 29" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "The jointly optimised AE+BLSTM was best on every metric: accuracy 0.6818, F1 0.5457, UAR 0.6509, against 0.6640/0.5091/0.6302 for BLSTM on 88 eGeMAPS features, 0.6373/0.4545/0.5997 on the 54 selected features, and 0.6178/0.2234/0.5514 for the SVM. Deriving from the confusion matrices (475 autistic and 650 typically developing utterances), sensitivity for autism is 45.3% for the AE model, 41.3% for",
                        "Under genuinely difficult data conditions - 39 infants, unbalanced classes, unsegmented clinical audio - a semi-supervised auto-encoder jointly optimised with a BLSTM improved autism detection from infant vocalisations over both an SVM baseline and a vanilla BLSTM on raw eGeMAPS features, reaching 68.2% accuracy and 0.651 UAR. Statistical feature selection by Mann-Whitney U made things worse, not " ;
                    slr:outcome "Full confusion matrices for all four models plus accuracy, precision, recall, F1 and unweighted average recall (UAR), the last chosen from the INTERSPEECH 2009 Emotion Challenge specifically because it accounts for class imbalance. Classification is per utterance: frame-wise softmax outputs are conv" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "The jointly optimised AE+BLSTM was best on every metric: accuracy 0.6818, F1 0.5457, UAR 0.6509, against 0.6640/0.5091/0.6302 for BLSTM on 88 eGeMAPS features, 0.6373/0.4545/0.5997 on the 54 selected features, and 0.6178/0.2234/0.5514 for the SVM. Deriving from the confusion matrices (475 autistic and 650 typically developing utterances), sensitivity for autism is 45.3% for the AE model, 41.3% for; Under genuinely difficult data conditions - 39 infants, unbalanced classes, unsegmented clinical audio - a semi-supervised auto-encoder jointly optimised with a BLSTM improved autism detection from infant vocalisations over both an SVM baseline and a vanilla BLSTM on raw eGeMAPS features, reaching 68.2% accuracy and 0.651 UAR. Statistical feature selection by Mann-Whitney U made things worse, not " ] ] ;
