<urn:slr:article:7df517928741> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Autism spectrum disorder (ASD) is a comprehensive mental development disorder characterized by abnormal interpersonal communication and interaction patterns, narrow scope of interests, and limited content of activities. Due to the lack of biological diagnostic indicators, the current diagnosis of ASD mainly relies on experts’ comprehensive clinical analysis of children, which is usually subjective and highly dependent on doctors’ individual professional skills. In this study, we propose an auxiliary diagnostic method for early ASD, which is based on the eye movement data analysis of autistic children. The method involves biological motion visualization, eye tracking, machine learning, and other related techniques. More specifically, the visualized biological motion animation is divided into five stages according to different biological behaviors of human skeletal figures presented in the animation. At the same time, the screen is divided into six areas to represent different regions the children gaze at. Following these two temporal and spatial principles, features can be extracted from eye movement data. Based on those extracted features, machine learning methods, particularly KNN, Gaussian-NB, and Cubic-SVM, are trained to classify and diagnose autistic children, making future timely treatment possible." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Fang H; Fan L; Hwang JN (2021). Auxiliary Diagnostic Method for Early Autism Spectrum Disorder Based on Eye Movement Data Analysis. *2021 IEEE 7th International Conference on Cloud Computing and Intelligen*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Fan L",
        "Fang H",
        "Hwang JN" ;
    dcterms:title "Auxiliary Diagnostic Method for Early Autism Spectrum Disorder Based on Eye Movement Data Analysis" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_8692a64f" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2021 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are exclusions balanced between groups?" ;
                            slr:justification "Six of 39 autistic children excluded against three of 53 controls, a rate difference not commented on." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "83 analysed children with a 40-child test set, acknowledged by the authors as too limited for comparison against existing methods." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "Ages 4-10 versus 2-15 and sex 74% versus 51% male, both untested and unadjusted, on a task whose demands vary strongly with age." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Group sizes, sexes and age ranges only; no means, standard deviations, recruitment route for controls, or any clinical characterisation." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Does the strongest feature measure the construct claimed?" ;
                            slr:justification "Area0 is defined by the eye deviation angle reading zero because gaze 'cannot be tracked by the device', so tracking failure is treated as a measure of inattention." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the stimulus choice theoretically motivated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Point-light biological motion is grounded in the sensitivity literature and isolates motion perception from face processing." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Tracker, monitor, animation content, sampling period and stage structure given; calibration, viewing distance, lighting and data-quality thresholds are not." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "Features derive from a priori hypotheses, but the two hyperparameters governing their computation were set empirically with no stated procedure." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "No instrument, criteria or clinician reported for either group." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are diagnostic metrics reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Accuracy, sensitivity and specificity with confusion matrices for all three classifiers." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is uncertainty quantified?" ;
                            slr:justification "No confidence intervals; on 40 test children one participant is worth 2.5 accuracy points, so the top two models differ by one child." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a refuted hypothesis reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Head-eye co-movement correlated opposite to prediction, reported plainly with the interpretation revised." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is feature design validated independently of the test data?" ;
                            slr:justification "The Pearson correlation analysis justifying the three feature hypotheses was computed over all 83 samples including the test half." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is any cross-validation or repeated splitting performed?" ;
                            slr:justification "A single random 43/40 split with no repetition, folds or held-out tuning set." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are hyperparameters selected by a stated procedure?" ;
                            slr:justification "Epsilon and tau 'empirically set', with the authors noting other values could improve performance." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Algorithms derived mathematically; no code, no k value for KNN." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the feature set fully specified?" ;
                            slr:justification "All 43 features enumerated with a systematic naming convention and computation rules." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are limitations stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Sample size, hyperparameter arbitrariness and the weak clap feature acknowledged; confounding, ethics and validation design not." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No release or availability statement." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No approval, consent or governance statement, with participants as young as two." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Collect more samples to permit meaningful comparison against existing diagnostic methods, and establish whether the clap-prediction feature is genuinely diagnostic given its weak observed correlation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-056" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2021 IEEE 7th International Conference on Cloud Computing and Intelligent Systems (CCIS) - a computing conference. The first author is a high-school student, with university co-authors from Shanghai Jiao Tong University and the University of Washington, which is relevant context for the level of methodological review the work received." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-056" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Acknowledged: the dataset is limited so 'comparisons cannot be simply drawn with other existing diagnostic methods, and more samples are needed for further analysis'; the clap-prediction feature 'needs to be further justified' given its weak correlation; and the hyperparameters were empirically set with the explicit note that 'different values could be set to gain a better performance'. The refutation of hypothesis 2 is reported clearly and the interpretation revised. Not acknowledged: no diagnostic instrument or criteria are reported for either group; no ethics approval; groups differ substantially in age (4-10 versus 2-15) and sex (74% versus 51% male) with no test or adjustment; validation is a single random split with no cross-validation or repetition, so the 40-child result carries wide sampling uncertainty; the correlation analysis validating the feature hypotheses used the full dataset including the test half; exclusion rates differ between groups (15% versus 6%); and the most predictive feature family, area0, is defined by the tracker's failure to resolve gaze, conflating attentional signal with equipment dropout." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-056" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Autistic group: 39 children, 29 boys and 10 girls, aged 4-10. Comparison group: 53 children, 27 boys and 26 girls, aged 2-15. No means, standard deviations, matching statement or statistical test is reported for either variable. Both differences are substantial: the comparison group is 51% male against 74%, and its age range brackets the clinical group at both ends, including children five years younger and five years older than any autistic participant. For a six-minute passive-viewing task with an abstract point-light stimulus, where the strongest feature is failure to maintain trackable gaze, an age spread from 2 to 15 years in one group only is a first-order confound. No IQ, language, severity, comorbidity or socioeconomic data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-056" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A six-minute point-light biological motion animation constructed from human skeletal figures with body keypoints, showing three behaviours in sequence: figures walking forward from distant to near, walking upside-down, and clapping hands. The stimulus choice is well motivated from the literature - typical viewers have 'a strong visual sensitivity to biological movement', autistic children show 'abnormal gaze towards scattered light points or rotating aperture', and have 'a lower tendency to immobilize biological moving objects'. Using abstract point-light figures rather than faces or naturalistic video isolates biological motion perception from face processing, which is a cleaner manipulation than most gaze studies in this corpus attempt. The upside-down walking condition in particular is the standard inversion control for biological motion, though the paper does not analyse it as such." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-056" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: build an auxiliary autism diagnostic method from structured, interpretable eye-movement features rather than unstructured data. Main findings: cubic-kernel SVM achieved 0.93 accuracy, 0.80 sensitivity and 1.00 specificity; Gaussian naive Bayes 0.90/0.80/0.96 and KNN 0.80/0.60/0.92; gaze at untrackable regions and head-eye co-movement were the most label-correlated feature families; and the hypothesis that autistic children would show more head-eye co-movement was refuted, the correlation running in the opposite direction." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-056" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2021. The framing is the standard subjectivity argument - diagnosis 'mainly relies on experts' comprehensive clinical analysis of children, which is usually subjective and highly dependent on doctors' individual professional skills' - with an added emphasis on pre-verbal assessment, aiming to 'diagnose children without linguistic ability in an early stage'. Its distinctive contribution to the corpus's framing is a preference for structured over unstructured data: it observes that 'most of the studies focus on unstructured data, which has complicated correlations among numerous parameters' and argues that 'it is highly desired to develop an effective auxiliary diagnostic method for ASD with the utilization of structured data'. That is an argument for interpretability over raw predictive power, and it is one of the few places in this corpus where the trade-off is stated as a design principle rather than discovered after the fact. The biological motion rationale is also well grounded, citing the classic finding of strong visual sensitivity to biological movement in typical viewers against reports of abnormal gaze to point-light displays in autism." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-056" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Forty-three features, every one traceable to one of three stated behavioural hypotheses, with a systematic naming convention (area-n-N for region n in stage N, plus per-region means). The representation is genuinely interpretable - each feature is a rate with a plain-language meaning - which serves the paper's clinical-support framing. Two design decisions bear scrutiny. Area0 is defined as an eye deviation angle of exactly zero, taken to mean gaze that 'cannot be tracked by the device', so a measurement artefact is promoted to a behavioural feature; this may capture real inattention but cannot distinguish it from tracker dropout, off-axis head position or non-compliance. And the region assignment depends on a single angular tolerance epsilon set to 2.25 by unspecified means, so the entire spatial feature family rests on one tuned constant." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-056" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; a single six-minute session per child with no follow-up. Data are numeric eye and head deviation angles. No ethics committee approval, parental consent procedure, anonymisation or data governance statement appears anywhere, despite the sample including children as young as two recruited through a service centre for children with developmental needs." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-056" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three classical classifiers compared on identical features: KNN with Euclidean distance, Gaussian naive Bayes and a cubic-kernel SVM. The choice is appropriate for 83 samples and 43 features, and the paper's stated rationale for the whole approach - preferring structured interpretable features over unstructured deep learning inputs - is coherent and is followed through. The cubic kernel is an unusual choice, more prone to overfitting than the standard RBF at this sample size, and no justification for the polynomial degree is given." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-056" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Interpretability is the paper's organising principle rather than an afterthought. Every feature derives from a stated hypothesis, and the Pearson correlation heat map is used to test each hypothesis against the data and report the outcome - two supported, one refuted. The refutation is handled properly: head-eye co-movement correlated negatively with the label, so the conclusion states that autistic children 'show less head-eye co-movements than normal children (instead of positive correlation in the hypothesis 2)' while noting the feature remains diagnostically useful. The authors also draw a clinically directed conclusion, identifying which features 'doctors should pay attention to when diagnosing'. What is missing is any model-derived importance - the correlations are univariate and computed independently of the classifiers, so nothing establishes that the SVM actually uses the features the correlation analysis highlights." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-056" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No IQ, developmental, language, adaptive or severity measure, and no comorbidity information for either group." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-056" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The weakest element. A single random split of 83 children into 43 training and 40 testing, with no cross-validation, no repetition over alternative splits, no held-out tuning set and no independent cohort. The two feature hyperparameters were set empirically with no procedure stated and an explicit acknowledgement that other values could improve performance, and the correlation analysis used to justify the feature design was computed across the entire dataset including the test half - so the features whose relevance is demonstrated on all 83 children are the same features evaluated on 40 of them. With a 40-child test set, the reported accuracies carry sampling uncertainty of roughly plus or minus 8 percentage points that is never quantified." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-056" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'autistic children', 'children with ASD', 'ASD children', 'normal children', 'normal people'. The comparison group is consistently 'normal' rather than 'typically developing'. The framing is strongly deficit-oriented, opening with 'the mental retardation of ASD, which increases with age' and describing the aim as reducing 'the intellectual deficiency of autistic children'. This is among the more pathologising registers in the corpus and the developmental claim it opens with - that intellectual impairment in autism increases with age - is not a standard finding." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-056" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. The autistic group is identified only by recruitment from a named children's service centre, with no diagnostic instrument, criteria, clinician, date or severity information anywhere in the paper. The comparison group's status as 'normal' is asserted without any screening. This is the weakest ascertainment among the included studies in this corpus, and it is particularly striking given that the paper's own introduction criticises existing diagnosis for depending on 'doctors' level of experience, which makes the diagnostic results quite subjective and variable' - the labels its models learn from come from exactly that unexamined source." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-056" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Autistic participants were recruited from the Shanghai Putuo District Zhanyi Children Cultivation Service Center; no setting is stated for the comparison group and no laboratory conditions, lighting or viewing distance are described. Acknowledged support from two university research teams at Shanghai Jiao Tong University and Shanghai University of Engineering Science. The first author is affiliated with a Shanghai high school." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-056" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Visual attention to point-light biological motion, decomposed three ways: where on the screen gaze falls across six regions and five animation stages, whether head and eye move together, and whether the child anticipates a repeated action. The head-eye co-movement measure is distinctive in this corpus - most eye-tracking studies discard head movement as a nuisance to be minimised, whereas here it is treated as a behavioural signal in its own right, hypothesised to index stereotyped head-shaking. That the hypothesis was refuted does not diminish the value of measuring it." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-056" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No cross-modal fusion, but a within-session combination of eye and head channels from a single device, which is closer to fusion than most gaze studies here attempt. No ablation isolates the contribution of the head channel, though the correlation analysis identifies head-eye features among the most label-correlated, alongside the area0 gaze features. The temporal decomposition into five stages is a second axis of combination - features are computed per stage and pooled - but no analysis compares stages, so which phase of the animation carries the signal is not established despite the stage structure being built into the design." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-056" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Mixed. Well specified: the three behavioural hypotheses, the six-region screen division, the rule mapping eye deviation angle to gaze region, the head-eye co-movement criterion, the clap-prediction rule, the complete 43-feature inventory with naming convention, the stage and sub-stage structure, the sampling period, both hyperparameter values, and the three classifiers with their mathematical definitions. Not specified: any diagnostic instrument; ethics approval; calibration procedure, viewing distance or data-quality thresholds; the value of k for KNN; how epsilon and tau were chosen; participant ages beyond ranges; and any data or code release. The complete absence of a diagnostic basis alongside a full derivation of the SVM kernel function is a striking imbalance of reporting effort." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-056" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, sensitivity and specificity with confusion matrices for all three classifiers - adequate for a diagnostic framing and better than accuracy alone. The reported figures make the asymmetry visible: the best model detects 12 of 15 autistic children while correctly classifying 25 of 25 typically developing children, so it misses one autistic child in five. No AUC, precision, F1, confidence interval, per-split variability or statistical comparison between models, and with only 40 test children a single participant is worth 2.5 accuracy points, so the 0.93 versus 0.90 difference between the top two models is one child." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-056" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups: 39 autistic children recruited from the Shanghai Putuo District Zhanyi Children Cultivation Service Center and 53 children described as normal, totalling 92. Nine samples were excluded for partial data loss or non-cooperation, leaving 83 - 33 autistic and 50 typically developing, so 6 autistic and 3 comparison children were removed, a differential exclusion rate of 15% versus 6% that is not commented on. No other-diagnosis comparison group." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-056" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Tobii 4C eye tracker, which records head as well as eye movement - the feature that makes the head-eye co-movement hypothesis testable and distinguishes this setup from the gaze-only trackers used elsewhere in the corpus; a 29WK500-P monitor; and the biological motion animation from prior published work. Sampling at 0.02-second intervals (50 Hz). No calibration procedure, viewing distance or data-quality criteria are described." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-056" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: Auxiliary diagnosis, eye movement data, feature extraction, classification algorithms. 'Auxiliary diagnosis' is the operative framing - the tool is positioned as support for clinicians rather than as a replacement - and 'feature extraction' correctly identifies where the paper's effort went." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-056" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "One session per child viewing a six-minute animation, sampled every 0.02 seconds and divided into 5 stages of 7 sub-stages each, giving 35 data segments per child" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional case-control classification with a hypothesis-driven feature design and a single random train/test split" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "School-age children with autism" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 39 recruited; 33 retained after exclusions; comparison: 53 recruited; 50 retained after exclusions" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Forty-three interpretable features derived from three explicit behavioural hypotheses about gaze during biological motion viewing classified autism at 93% accuracy with a cubic-kernel SVM on a 40-child test set, though with sensitivity of only 0.80 against perfect specificity. The correlation analysis produced a genuine and honestly reported refutation: head-eye co-movement, hypothesised to be ele",
                        "On the 40-child test set (25 typically developing, 15 autistic): cubic-kernel SVM 0.93 accuracy, 0.80 sensitivity, 1.00 specificity; Gaussian naive Bayes 0.90, 0.80, 0.96; KNN 0.80, 0.60, 0.92. Sensitivity of 0.80 corresponds to 12 of 15 autistic children detected, and specificity of 1.00 to 25 of 25 typically developing children correctly classified. All three models show markedly higher specific" ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy, sensitivity and specificity with confusion matrices for each of the three classifiers. No AUC, precision, F1, confidence interval or statistical comparison between models" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "On the 40-child test set (25 typically developing, 15 autistic): cubic-kernel SVM 0.93 accuracy, 0.80 sensitivity, 1.00 specificity; Gaussian naive Bayes 0.90, 0.80, 0.96; KNN 0.80, 0.60, 0.92. Sensitivity of 0.80 corresponds to 12 of 15 autistic children detected, and specificity of 1.00 to 25 of 25 typically developing children correctly classified. All three models show markedly higher specific; Forty-three interpretable features derived from three explicit behavioural hypotheses about gaze during biological motion viewing classified autism at 93% accuracy with a cubic-kernel SVM on a 40-child test set, though with sensitivity of only 0.80 against perfect specificity. The correlation analysis produced a genuine and honestly reported refutation: head-eye co-movement, hypothesised to be ele" ] ] ;
