<urn:slr:article:9b39e593462a> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "This paper presents the pathological voice detection and classiﬁcation techniques using signal processing based methodologies and Feed Forward Neural Networks (FFNN). The important pathological voices such as Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) and Down Syndrome (DS) are considered for analysis. These pathological voices are known to manifest in different ways in the speech of children and adults. Therefore, it is possible to discriminate ASD and DS children from normal ones using the acoustic features extracted from the speech of these subjects. The important attributes hidden in the pathological voices are extracted by applying different signal processing techniques. In this work, three group of feature vectors such as perturbation measures, noise parameters and spectral-cepstral modeling are derived from the signals. The detection and classiﬁcation is done by means of Feed Forward Neural Network (FFNN) classiﬁer trained with Scaled Conjugate Gradient (SCG) algorithm. The performance of the network is evaluated by ﬁnding various performance metrics and the the experimental results clearly demonstrate that the proposed method gives better performance compared with other methods discussed in the literature." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Jayasree T; Shia S (2021). Combined Signal Processing Based Techniques and Feed Forward Neural Networks for Pathological Voice Detection and Classification. *Sound&Vibration, 55(2), 141-161*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Jayasree T",
        "Shia S" ;
    dcterms:title "Combined Signal Processing Based Techniques and Feed Forward Neural Networks for Pathological Voice Detection and Classification" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_25e34bbd" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2021 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "A single 5-14 year range for all groups with no group means or tests, on measures that change with maturation." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is an other-diagnosis comparison group included?" ;
                            slr:justification "A Down syndrome group enables differential classification rather than only condition-versus-typical." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Group sizes and sex counts only; no ages, recruitment route, clinical detail or collection setting." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample appropriate and adequate for the stated target?" ;
                            slr:justification "Six children per clinical group and seven controls, with no limitations section acknowledging this." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictors defined without knowledge of outcome?" ;
                            slr:justification "Acoustic features are computed automatically from the signal with no label-dependent selection." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Recording format, resolution, window length and preprocessing given; elicitation procedure, samples per child, and recording distance or equipment specification are not." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Can the task express the phenomenon claimed?" ;
                            slr:justification "A sustained vowel cannot exhibit the monotonic or sing-song prosody the paper itself identifies as characterising autistic speech." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "No diagnostic instrument or criteria for either clinical group." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are hyperparameters selected independently of the reported metric?" ;
                            slr:justification "Hidden neuron counts chosen by trial to maximise accuracy for each feature set and task." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are alternative classifiers compared on identical features?" ;
                            slr:justification "SVM and naive Bayes reported on MFCC and RASTA-PLP for all three tasks." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the partition at the participant level?" ;
                            slr:justification "255 samples from 19 children partitioned 70/15/15 with no statement that a child's samples stayed together." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a negative result reported honestly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Feature-family combination degraded performance in nearly every condition, stated plainly." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the reported figures plausible given the design?" ;
                            slr:justification "Perfect accuracy with AUC 1 in six of 33 conditions from a single sustained vowel, using features standard in speaker recognition." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is uncertainty quantified?" ;
                            slr:justification "No confidence intervals, confusion matrices or statistical tests anywhere." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "MATLAB named; no code, though the signal-processing steps are specified in enough detail to reimplement." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are group-level acoustic differences described?" ;
                            slr:justification "No means, distributions, effect sizes or tests for any feature, so nothing is established about how the voices differ." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No release or availability statement." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are limitations stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No limitations section and no caveats on sample size or validation." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the terminology appropriate to the population?" ;
                            slr:justification "Autism framed as a 'voice disability' and 'pathological voice'; Down syndrome phenotype described using 'mental retardation'." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No approval, consent or governance statement of any kind for voice recordings of clinically diagnosed children." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Almost none. Nineteen children aged 5-14, with sex counts by group but no ages, means, standard deviations or matching test for any group. The 5-14 range is the critical gap: fundamental frequency, jitter, shimmer and formant structure all change substantially across childhood and pubertal onset falls inside this window, so any group age difference would directly produce the acoustic differences the classifier exploits. Sex is likewise a primary determinant of these features and is balanced only by count across very small groups. No IQ, severity, comorbidity, language ability, socioeconomic or recruitment information." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-060" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2021. The framing is unusual and revealing: autism is introduced not as a neurodevelopmental condition but as one of two 'voice disabilities' within a paper about pathological voice detection, alongside Down syndrome, in a context where '1.2% of the world's total population is facing some type of voice disability'. Prevalence figures are given for both conditions in the United States and India (1 in 59 and 23 per 10,000 for autism; 1 in 700 and 1 in 1250 for Down syndrome). The critique of existing diagnosis is brief - ADOS-R and ADI-R 'require standardized structures for capturing the behaviors and found to be very difficult' - and the paper's real intellectual investment is in the acoustic literature, which it reviews carefully across five prior Down syndrome voice studies with their specific findings on fundamental frequency, jitter and shimmer. Its stated novelty is the differential task: prior work 'analyzed the variability of acoustic features in the voice produced by children with ADS, DS and normal children' but 'there has been no attempt to classify both'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-060" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. The paper describes ADOS-R and ADI-R in its introduction and criticises them for requiring 'standardized structures for capturing the behaviors and found to be very difficult', but never states how the six autistic children in its own sample were identified - no instrument, criteria, clinician or setting. The same applies to the Down syndrome group, where the paper notes that 'Down Syndrome Screening tests are used for diagnosing Down Syndrome. Nevertheless, they cannot give appropriate results', without saying what was used here. Control status is asserted without any screening." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-060" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No IQ, developmental, language, adaptive or severity measure, and no comorbidity information for any of the three groups." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-060" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three groups, but 19 children in total: 6 autistic (3 boys, 3 girls), 6 with Down syndrome (2 boys, 4 girls) and 7 controls (3 boys, 4 girls), contributing 79, 77 and 99 sustained vowel samples respectively. The inclusion of a Down syndrome group is the design's most valuable feature - it is one of only a handful of studies in the corpus with an other-diagnosis comparison rather than only typical development, enabling the differential question the paper poses. But with six children per clinical group the analysis rests on a very small number of individuals however many samples each provides." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-060" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Sustained phonation of the vowel /ah/, chosen explicitly against the alternative of connected or spontaneous speech. This is a defensible methodological position for voice-quality analysis - it holds linguistic content constant and isolates phonatory function - but it is a severe constraint for autism specifically, since the acoustic differences documented in autistic speech concern prosody, intonation contour and pragmatic use of voice, all of which are eliminated by a sustained vowel. The paper's own introduction describes autistic speech as 'machine-like monotonic or sing-song', properties that cannot manifest in an isolated sustained vowel, so the task may not be capable of capturing the phenomenon it is aimed at." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-060" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Voice quality during sustained phonation - specifically phonatory stability (jitter, shimmer), noise content of the glottal source (harmonics-to-noise, glottal-to-noise excitation and signal-to-noise ratios) and vocal tract spectral shape (MFCC, RASTA-PLP). This is the narrowest behavioural sample in the corpus: a single sustained vowel captures voice source and resonance properties but no prosody, no interaction, no linguistic content and no temporal structure of speech." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-060" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, sensitivity, specificity and AUC for all seven individual and four combined feature sets across all three tasks - 33 conditions in total - plus processing time comparisons and hidden-neuron sensitivity curves. The coverage is unusually complete, and the processing time analysis is a practical dimension few studies report, showing MFCC and RASTA-PLP to be cheapest because jitter and shimmer require fundamental frequency estimation and GNE requires Hilbert envelope extraction and cross-correlation per glottal cycle. Missing: confidence intervals, confusion matrices, and any statistical test - and with perfect scores reported in six of 33 conditions, the metrics carry no information about where the model fails." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-060" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None stated." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-060" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: Autism spectrum disorder; down syndrome; feed forward neural network; perturbation measures; noise parameters; cepstral features. Four of six name signal-processing constructs, accurately reflecting a paper whose contribution is a feature-family comparison with autism as one of two application labels." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-060" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; multiple phonations per child in a single session with no follow-up. Data are voice recordings of identifiable children with clinical diagnoses. No ethics committee approval, parental consent procedure, anonymisation statement or data-handling policy appears anywhere in the paper - a complete absence rather than a partial omission, and one that matters given the population." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-060" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No model-explanation method and, more consequentially, no descriptive analysis of any group difference - no acoustic means, distributions, effect sizes or tests. The paper therefore establishes only that a classifier separates the groups, not what distinguishes them acoustically, which is a substantial gap for a study whose introduction reviews directional acoustic findings at length. The interpretation offered for why spectral-cepstral features win is at the level of feature family rather than individual parameter: they are Fourier-transform-based while jitter, shimmer, HNR and GNE are time-domain, and the result is said to be 'consistent with previous studies that, spectral and cepstral based features are best suited for discriminating voice with hoarseness from normal voice'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-060" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The decisive weakness. Partitioning is 70/15/15 with six-fold cross-validation, but over 255 voice samples drawn from only 19 children - roughly 13 phonations each - with no statement that a child's samples were confined to one partition. Repeated sustained vowels from one speaker constitute a speaker-identification problem, and MFCC, RASTA-PLP, jitter, shimmer and HNR are the standard feature set for exactly that task, so a classifier can achieve near-perfect accuracy by recognising individuals and mapping them to their known group. Six of 33 reported conditions achieve exactly 100% accuracy with AUC 1, which is what speaker-level leakage produces and which is otherwise implausible for a clinical classification from a single vowel. Additionally, the hidden neuron count was chosen by trial to maximise accuracy separately for each feature set and task, so every figure is a maximum over that search. There is no independent cohort, no external validation and no held-out speaker test." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-060" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A single-hidden-layer feed-forward network trained by scaled conjugate gradient, chosen for its lack of user-dependent parameters and avoidance of line search, compared against RBF-kernel SVM and naive Bayes on identical features. The comparison is systematic and the network wins in every condition, though the margins - 100% against 78.5% for SVM and 91.2% for naive Bayes on the same MFCC features for autism detection - are large enough to be suspicious, since a shallow network and an RBF SVM on the same low-dimensional features would not normally diverge this far unless one is exploiting something the other is not." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-060" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The most pathologising language in the corpus, and conceptually as well as ethically problematic. Autism is repeatedly described as a 'pathological voice' and grouped under 'voice disability' alongside Down syndrome - a categorisation that misstates what autism is, since it is not a disorder of the voice. The Down syndrome phenotype is described as including 'mental retardation, general hypotonia... maxillary hypoplasia... with a relative macroglossia (unusually large tongue), short neck, and obesity'. Children are 'normal' versus 'affected'. Autistic speech is characterised as 'abnormal and is described as machine-like monotonic or sing-song'. Even allowing for a signal-processing venue's conventions, the register is markedly out of step with the rest of the corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-060" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three feature families with an explicitly argued progression. Perturbation measures are introduced first and their limitation stated: 'a high degree of jitter consequences in a voice with roughness... Besides, it is very difficult to measure fundamental frequency, in the case of a pathological voice'. Noise energy measures follow, with their limitation likewise stated: they 'depend on the voice recording environment'. Spectral-cepstral features are then introduced to overcome both, with MFCC justified because it 'analyze the vocal tract independently of the vocal folds that can be injured due to voice pathologies' and RASTA-PLP because its bandpass filtering removes channel effects. This is a coherent and well-referenced design rationale, and the empirical ordering of results matches it. Each family is summarised to a fixed-length vector by statistical moments across frames." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-060" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The fusion result is clearly negative and is reported without spin, which makes it useful. Combining feature families reduced performance in nearly every condition: for autism detection MFCC plus RASTA-PLP fell to 80.7% from 100% for either alone, and HNR plus SNR fell to 53.2%; for Down syndrome detection MFCC plus RASTA-PLP fell to 78.3%. The single exception is HNR plus GNE reaching 100% for autism-versus-Down-syndrome discrimination. The authors state the conclusion plainly: 'the combined feature set provides less performance when compared to the individual features, for most of the cases'. Given the sample size this is more likely a dimensionality effect than a substantive finding, but it stands in useful contrast to the widespread assumption elsewhere in this corpus that combining features or modalities helps." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-060" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: detect autism and Down syndrome from sustained vowel phonation and discriminate between them, comparing three acoustic feature families. Main findings: MFCC and RASTA-PLP achieved 100% accuracy for autism detection and Down syndrome detection; MFCC and harmonics-to-noise ratio achieved 100% for autism-versus-Down-syndrome discrimination; perturbation and noise measures performed worse throughout; combining feature families reduced performance in almost every condition; and the feed-forward network outperformed SVM and naive Bayes comparators on the same features." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-060" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Sound and Vibration, 55(2), 141-161 - an acoustics and signal-processing journal, not a clinical or machine-learning venue, published open access under CC BY 4.0. The venue explains both the depth of the signal-processing exposition and the absence of clinical reporting standards." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-060" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Audio Editor 2016 on a laptop recording PCM mono WAV at 16-bit resolution in a stated noise-free location; MATLAB on an i5 processor for all processing; LIBSVM for the SVM comparator. The recording chain is consumer-grade, which the paper does not treat as a limitation despite its own observation that 'the noise parameters depend on the voice recording environment. Hence the values of parameters also vary accordingly'. The stated sampling rate of '24,000 kHz' is a transcription error for 24 kHz." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-060" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Detailed on signal processing, absent on everything else. Fully specified: pre-emphasis, framing and windowing with 30 ms Hamming windows; the RASTA-PLP computation given as a seven-step procedure; MFCC parameters including window length, shift, coefficient count and dynamic features; RASTA-PLP order and window overlap; the network architecture, activation functions, training algorithm, learning rate, momentum, epoch count and target error; the hidden-neuron selection procedure; and the partition ratios. Absent: diagnostic instruments, group ages, ethics approval, recruitment, how samples were elicited or how many per child, whether the partition respected speaker boundaries, any group-level acoustic statistics, and any code or data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-060" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. There is no limitations section and no caveat anywhere on sample size, validation design, generalisability or the absence of clinical characterisation. The nearest approach is the acknowledgement that hidden neuron counts have no exact formula and were fixed by trial. Identifiable in review: 255 samples derive from only 19 children with roughly 13 samples each, and the 70/15/15 partition operates over samples with no statement that speakers were kept together, which alone would explain the perfect accuracies; hidden neuron counts were tuned to maximise the reported accuracy for each feature set and task; no diagnostic instrument is reported for either clinical group; ages are given only as a collective 5-14 range in a domain where voice changes with maturation; no ethics or consent statement exists; and the sustained vowel task cannot express the prosodic properties the paper itself identifies as characterising autistic speech." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-060" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Recording in an unspecified 'noise free location' using consumer audio editing software on a laptop, at two engineering colleges in Tamil Nadu. No recruitment route, clinical setting or collection period is described, and no funding or ethics information appears." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-060" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Multiple sustained phonations per child, yielding roughly 13 samples per participant" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional three-way acoustic classification with sample-level analysis, comprising two detection tasks and one differential-classification task" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Children with autism, alongside a separate Down syndrome cohort" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 6 autistic children (3 boys, 3 girls) providing 79 voice samples; comparison: 7 children, 99 samples" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Autism detection: MFCC and RASTA-PLP both 100% accuracy with AUC 1; SNR 89.4%, jitter 88.88%, shimmer and GNE 83.33%, HNR 77.8%. Down syndrome detection: MFCC and RASTA-PLP both 100%, HNR 94.73%, SNR 92.4%, GNE 89.47%, jitter and shimmer 81.25%. Autism versus Down syndrome: MFCC and HNR both 100%, SNR 90.4%, RASTA-PLP 85.7%, jitter and shimmer 81.25%, GNE 73.68%. Comparators on MFCC for autism det",
                        "Feed-forward networks on spectral-cepstral features of sustained /ah/ phonation reportedly achieved 100% accuracy for autism detection, Down syndrome detection and autism-versus-Down-syndrome discrimination, with MFCC and RASTA-PLP consistently outperforming jitter, shimmer and noise-energy measures. Two findings are informative independent of the inflated absolute values. Combining feature famili" ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy, sensitivity, specificity and AUC for every feature set on every task, plus processing time comparisons and hidden-neuron sensitivity curves. Reporting is extensive in coverage but contains no confidence intervals, no confusion matrices and no statistical comparison between conditions" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Autism detection: MFCC and RASTA-PLP both 100% accuracy with AUC 1; SNR 89.4%, jitter 88.88%, shimmer and GNE 83.33%, HNR 77.8%. Down syndrome detection: MFCC and RASTA-PLP both 100%, HNR 94.73%, SNR 92.4%, GNE 89.47%, jitter and shimmer 81.25%. Autism versus Down syndrome: MFCC and HNR both 100%, SNR 90.4%, RASTA-PLP 85.7%, jitter and shimmer 81.25%, GNE 73.68%. Comparators on MFCC for autism det; Feed-forward networks on spectral-cepstral features of sustained /ah/ phonation reportedly achieved 100% accuracy for autism detection, Down syndrome detection and autism-versus-Down-syndrome discrimination, with MFCC and RASTA-PLP consistently outperforming jitter, shimmer and noise-energy measures. Two findings are informative independent of the inflated absolute values. Combining feature famili" ] ] ;
