<urn:slr:article:a9c2bcedd414> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Artificial intelligence (AI) technology has recently shown promising results in detecting Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD), but it faces significant challenges because it is still in early stages of development. This study proposes to use linguistic features in the detection of ASD using artificial intelligence. In order to systematically understand different AI model performances, two types of AI models have been explored and evaluated. Five classical ML models were used: logistic regression, Gaussian naive Bayes, random forests, k-nearest neighbors (K-NN), and support vector machines (SVMs), and two deep learning models were used: multilayer perceptron (MLP) and a convolutional neural network (CNN). All model development work is based on analyzing speech transcripts of 64 children total aged 3 to 6 from two data banks, CHILDES and ASDBank, including 30 children with ASD and 34 typically developing (TD) controls. The first step was to examine the annotations of the transcriptions, then extract various linguistic features from the texts. AI models were trained to determine whether a child has ASD based on the characteristics of the transcripts analyzed. There are three major findings of this study. First, neural network models will outperform classical models in ASD detection by around 5%-10%. The models multilayer perceptron and convolutional neural networks achieved 83% and 84% accuracy, respectively. Second, among the classical machine learning models, logistic regression, SVMs, K-NN, and random forests all achieved accuracies in the range 77%-80%, while Gaussian naive Bayes performed the worst by around 10%. Third, the linguistic feature with the highest importance in ASD detection is MLU, and the features MLU, MLT, rUtts, and rTNW show significant distinctions between age and gender demographics in addition to having high importance in ASD detection. Overall, this research recommends using deep learning for clinical applications of ASD detection." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Li J (2025). Artificial Intelligence-Based Detection of Autism Spectrum Disorder Using Linguistic Features. *2024 IEEE 3rd International Conference on Computing and Machine Intelligence (ICMI)*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Li J" ;
    dcterms:title "Artificial Intelligence-Based Detection of Autism Spectrum Disorder Using Linguistic Features" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_786b6280" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2025 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Handling of missing data and attrition" ;
                            slr:justification "All 64 retained children contribute complete feature vectors; the one exclusion, of developmentally delayed children, is stated with its reason; token-level cleaning rules are documented in detail." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Comparison group adequacy" ;
                            slr:justification "Typically developing children drawn partly from corpora containing no autistic children and using different protocols, so corpus of origin is partly correlated with class. Developmentally delayed children available in a source corpus were deliberately excluded, removing the non-autistic clinical comparison." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participant selection and spectrum representativeness" ;
                            slr:justification "Sixty-four children from four archived corpora with no ascertainment described, no spectrum characterisation, and an acknowledged imbalance toward boys and toward the younger age band." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Confounder control" ;
                            slr:justification "No independent language or cognitive measure, in a study whose leading predictor is a standard index of expressive language development; no age or sex composition reported by diagnostic group, despite the paper's own finding that the top feature increases with age." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reference standard quality" ;
                            slr:justification "Genuine diagnoses inherited from established research archives, but no instrument, criteria or ascertainment procedure is reported in this paper." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Index test definition and prespecification" ;
                            slr:justification "Six features defined in advance with both child-alone and ratio forms and an explicit rationale for the ratios; but two versions were compared empirically and certain features removed before the headline result, using the same data." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Measurement standardisation" ;
                            slr:justification "Four source protocols differing in session length (30 minutes, 10 minutes, unspecified, 30 minutes), in structure (one with a cleanup period) and in investigator role - the last mattering because half the features are ratios to the investigator's output. Ratio normalisation addresses length but not protocol." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome measurement blinding" ;
                            slr:justification "Feature computation is fully automated from archived transcripts by documented rules; no human rater is involved at any stage of this study." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "unclear" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data splitting and leakage control" ;
                            slr:justification "One transcript and one feature vector per child, so the split unit is the person and no within-participant leakage is possible - among the minority in this review not exposed to that failure." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Preprocessing and feature selection nesting" ;
                            slr:justification "Feature versions compared and certain features removed on the full dataset before the reported headline accuracies, so those are post-selection estimates." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance estimation and chance level" ;
                            slr:justification "Five metrics for all seven models with ROC variability bands across 50 repeated splits - unusually honest uncertainty reporting - and results benchmarked against three prior studies. But test folds hold sixteen children, no confidence intervals are given, and no chance or majority-class baseline is stated." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Sample size adequacy for the modelling" ;
                            slr:justification "Sixty-four children with test folds of sixteen, supporting a claimed 5-to-10-point advantage for neural models and a 1-to-2-point margin between two of them. The author identifies limited data as the first limitation." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "unclear" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Acknowledgement of limitations" ;
                            slr:justification "Two limitations named - limited data and residual transcription noise across corpora, the latter correctly identifying cross-corpus heterogeneity from one side. The language-delay confound, the partial correlation between corpus and class, the protocol differences and the post-selection headline are all unaddressed." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Ethics, consent and conflicts" ;
                            slr:justification "Secondary analysis of a public research archive with consent obtained at source, stated explicitly - materially different from the web-scraped and institutionally collected material elsewhere in this review. Funding and competing interests are not stated." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data, code and protocol availability" ;
                            slr:justification "Source corpora are public and individually named through TalkBank; no code released; no preregistration." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reporting of results and uncertainty" ;
                            slr:justification "Five metrics for every model plus ROC mean curves with a one-standard-deviation band - better uncertainty communication than almost anything else in this review - but no confidence intervals, no confusion matrices, no baseline, and no statement of which features were removed before the headline figure." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Completeness of methods reporting" ;
                            slr:justification "All four corpora named with protocols where known, exclusion stated, feature definitions with rationale, NLP package, detailed token-cleaning rules with the annotation conventions handled, seven models with library provenance, the convolutional architecture layer by layer, split proportion, and the repeated-split count with its stability criterion." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: Autism Spectrum Disorder, Natural Language Processing, Artificial Intelligence, Machine Learning, Deep Learning, Linguistics. Balanced across population, modality and method, and 'Linguistics' in particular signals that the features are psycholinguistic constructs rather than learned embeddings. Terms carried through the body: mean length of utterance, mean length of turn, total number of words, number of different words, TalkBank, CHILDES, ASDBank, Stanza, feature importance, train-test split." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-132" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, precision, recall, F1 and AUC for all seven models, with ROC curves that plot the individual splits, the mean curve and a one-standard-deviation band. That variability band is worth singling out: on 64 children with 50 repeated splits it conveys the instability of the estimate more honestly than a point figure, and it is a form of uncertainty reporting that almost no other study in this review provides. Precision and recall are also interpreted correctly in clinical terms, with recall of 80-81% read as a low false-negative rate 'important for ensuring that children with ASD are diagnosed correctly' and precision of 84-86% as protecting children without autism from being misdiagnosed. Missing: confidence intervals, confusion matrices, calibration, and any positive predictive value at a realistic prevalence - the last mattering because the reported precision holds only at the near-balanced sample ratio." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-132" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Larger and more varied datasets; better preprocessing to reduce transcription noise; and, implied by the mean-word-length finding, lexical complexity measured in phonemes or morphemes rather than characters. Not proposed, and needed: an independent language measure to separate delay from autism; reporting of age and sex composition by diagnostic group; model evaluation stratified by sex, given the interaction the paper itself reports; and reinstatement of the developmentally delayed children as a third class, since the source corpus contains them and the resulting three-way discrimination is what clinical practice requires." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-132" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A systematic seven-model comparison spanning logistic regression, Gaussian naive Bayes, random forests, k-nearest neighbours, support vector machines, a multilayer perceptron and a small convolutional network. Comparing classical against neural models on the same features is the paper's stated purpose and is executed evenly - same features, same splits, same metrics. The scale is appropriate for 64 children and twelve features, and the classical models are the ones such data can support. The conclusion drawn from the comparison, however, exceeds it: a 5-to-10-point advantage for neural models on 64 children, with test folds of sixteen and no confidence intervals, is not a stable basis for the recommendation to use deep learning clinically, and the one-to-two-point margin between the multilayer perceptron and the convolutional network is certainly not." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-132" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No instrumentation. Stanford's Stanza NLP package parsed sentences and tokens; scikit-learn supplied five classical models and the multilayer perceptron; Keras supplied the convolutional network. The computational cost is trivial and the input is text that already exists in a public archive, so this is among the least resource-intensive designs in the review - which is also its limitation, since it inherits whatever the original recordings and transcriptions captured and can add nothing." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-132" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Sixty-four children aged 3 to 6 - 30 autistic and 34 typically developing - assembled from four archived TalkBank corpora. The autistic children come from ASDBank's Eigsti and Nadig collections; the typically developing children come partly from those same collections and partly from CHILDES's Warren and Kuczaj corpora, added deliberately 'to try to balance demographic (gender and age) representations'. The paper is honest about the residual imbalance, stating that 'most participants are male, and there are more children aged 3-4 than aged 5-6', and it explains the sex imbalance correctly as a consequence of autism being 'almost 4 times as common in boys than in girls'. Numerical age and sex compositions by diagnostic group are not reported, which matters because the paper's own analysis finds that mean length of utterance - the top-ranked predictor - is the only feature that consistently increases with age. Developmentally delayed children present in the Eigsti corpus were deliberately excluded to keep the task binary, which removes the non-autistic clinical comparison group the source data could have supported. The unit of analysis is the child, one transcript and one feature vector each." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-132" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Sound on the mechanics for a study of this size, and unresolved on the confounds. Each child contributes one transcript and one feature vector, so the split unit is the person and no within-participant leakage is possible - which places this study among the minority in the review not exposed to the failure that dominates the image, frame and thermal branches. The evaluation is a 75/25 split repeated over 50 random draws with results averaged, and the choice is both explained and empirically justified: cross-validation was rejected because of small sample size, and 50 repetitions were selected because metrics stabilised, varying by no more than 1% as further splits were added. Stating a stability criterion for the number of repetitions is a level of care that very few papers in this corpus show, and the ROC plots with a one-standard-deviation band around the mean curve carry that honesty into the presentation. Three qualifications bound what this buys. Test folds contain sixteen children, so each averaged estimate rests on very small samples and no confidence interval is given; the headline 83-84% is stated elsewhere in the paper to have been obtained 'after the removal of certain features', a selection made on the same data, so it is a post-selection figure; and repeated random splitting addresses sampling variance and not the three confounds that determine what is being separated. Those are, in order of importance: that the top-ranked feature is a developmental language index with no independent language measure available and no age composition reported by group; that part of the typically developing class comes from corpora containing no autistic children, so corpus of origin is partly correlated with class; and that the four source protocols differ in session length, structure and investigator role, while half the features are ratios to that investigator's output. None of these would be revealed by any splitting scheme, and none is addressed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-132" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None across modalities - text only, with no acoustic, prosodic or visual information. The ratio features constitute a form of dyadic fusion in that they combine the child's output with the investigator's within a session, which is conceptually similar to the interaction-level modelling in the pose-based studies charted elsewhere in this review and is more than most transcript-based work attempts. Its vulnerability is that the investigator's behaviour is not standardised across the four source corpora, so the denominator of every ratio feature varies by protocol." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-132" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional secondary analysis of archived transcripts, one session per child, with no follow-up. The ethics position is stated briefly and is defensible: 'The data used in this research is from a publicly available database. The information was gathered with informed consent. This research will not involve any personal information.' Unlike the web-scraping and institutional-imaging studies charted elsewhere in this review, the underlying material was collected for research purposes with consent obtained at source and archived under a governance framework designed for reuse, so a secondary analysis of it raises materially different questions from harvesting children's photographs from support pages or thermally imaging pupils at a special school. Funding and competing interests are not stated." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-132" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A vector of at most twelve interpretable linguistic values per child, reduced after a first round comparing the child-alone and ratio forms of each feature. This is the most parsimonious representation in the review and is well matched to the sample: every dimension is a quantity with a published psycholinguistic literature, computed by documented rules from a public archive, so the representation is auditable end to end. Its limitation is that transcript-level aggregation discards everything about how the speech unfolded - no sequence, no timing, no prosody - so constructs such as turn-taking latency or intonation, which the wider literature associates with autistic speech, are outside its reach." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-132" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The stated aim is to compare classical machine learning against deep learning for autism detection from linguistic features, using more than one data source and paying attention to how features differ across age and sex - three gaps the paper identifies in prior work, the second of which ('all previous studies based their research on a single data source') is a fair criticism of much of this literature. Findings: the convolutional network reached 84% accuracy and the multilayer perceptron 83%, against 77-80% for logistic regression, support vector machines, k-nearest neighbours and random forests, and roughly ten points lower for Gaussian naive Bayes; mean length of utterance was the top-ranked feature for both models that supply importances, confirming one prior study and contradicting another on total number of words; and the demographic analysis found MLU rising with age, a reversal of the sex gap between the 3-4 and 5-6 bands, and a sex-by-diagnosis interaction on mean length of turn. The performance figures are modest, honestly reported, and benchmarked against three prior studies at 70-80%, which places them in a credible range for this task and in sharp contrast to the 95-100% figures elsewhere in this review. What the study cannot establish is what the models detect: its leading feature is a developmental language index, no independent language measure exists, and the age composition of the two groups is unreported." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-132" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No severity, cognitive, adaptive or language measure exists independently of the modelled features - and this absence is the study's central interpretive problem rather than a routine gap. Mean length of utterance is the single most important predictor in both feature-importance analyses, and MLU is one of the oldest and best-established indices of expressive language development in child language research. Autistic children in this age range frequently have language delay, so a model whose leading feature is MLU may be detecting delayed expressive language rather than autism. Distinguishing the two requires an independent measure of language ability, which the archived transcripts do not supply here and which the paper does not seek. The point is sharpened by the paper's own demographic analysis: it reports that MLU is 'the only feature that consistently significantly increased with age', so the top predictor is simultaneously a developmental index and a diagnostic one, in a sample whose age composition by diagnostic group is not reported." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-132" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Structural and quantitative properties of spontaneous speech, operationalised as six standard psycholinguistic descriptors: mean length of utterance, mean length of turn, mean word length, number of utterances, total number of words and number of different words. Two design decisions distinguish this from a generic bag-of-features approach. Each feature was computed both for the child alone and as a ratio to the investigator's value in the same session, and the two versions were compared empirically to see which carried more information - a construct-level question about whether autistic children's speech differs absolutely or relative to their conversational partner's. And the count-based features were used only in ratio form to normalise for transcript length. The results locate the discriminative information in utterance and turn length and in the child's output relative to the adult's, with typically developing children higher on cMLU, rMLU, rMLT, rUtts and rTNW - a coherent picture of shorter utterances, shorter turns and proportionally less talk. Mean word length carried no signal, and the paper draws the right conclusion, that characters are a poor proxy for lexical complexity and that phonemes or morphemes would be the correct unit. The construct that this cannot separate from autism is expressive language level, of which MLU is a canonical index." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-132" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Play-based spontaneous speech, but not one paradigm - four. Eigsti recorded 30-minute play sessions, Nadig 10-minute free play followed by a cleanup period, Warren toy play of unspecified duration and Kuczaj 30-minute spontaneous speech samples. Spontaneous play speech is a good elicitation context for the constructs of interest: it produces enough utterances to estimate MLU stably, it imposes minimal demand, and it captures conversational turn-taking with an adult, which the ratio features exploit. The heterogeneity is handled only partly. The paper normalises the three count-based features as ratios to the investigator specifically 'to normalize for different transcript lengths', which is the right instinct and addresses the most obvious threat. It does not address that a 10-minute free-play session with a cleanup period and a 30-minute spontaneous speech sample differ in more than length - in topic structure, in how much the adult initiates, and in what kinds of utterance are elicited - or that one corpus's session duration is simply unknown." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-132" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Among the better-handled in the review, because the features are interpretable by construction and the interpretation is pursued rather than asserted. Feature-importance plots are given for logistic regression and random forests; mean length of utterance emerges as the top predictor in both; and the finding is compared explicitly against prior literature, confirming one study's MLU result and contradicting another's conclusion that total number of words does not differ. The stratified analysis goes beyond importance to describe how each feature behaves across diagnosis, age band and sex, producing the specific and testable claims recorded above. And the negative result on mean word length is interpreted correctly rather than ignored - characters are the wrong unit for lexical complexity, and phonemes or morphemes would be better. What interpretability cannot settle here is the identity of the underlying construct: MLU is a developmental language index and no independent measure of language ability exists in the data, so the models' leading signal is compatible with autism-specific pragmatic difference and with generic expressive language delay, and the paper does not raise the distinction." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-132" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2024 IEEE 3rd International Conference on Computing and Machine Intelligence (ICMI), doi 10.1109/ICMI60790.2024.10585946. A short computing-conference paper, single-authored by a student at The Harker School in San Jose - a secondary school - which is worth recording factually because it contextualises both the scale and the character of the work. The paper is careful where a beginner might not be: it splits by participant, justifies its repeated-split design with a stated stability criterion, documents its token-cleaning rules against TalkBank's transcription guide, plots ROC variability bands, compares its feature findings explicitly against three named prior studies including where it contradicts them, and reports modest accuracies without inflation. It is weaker on the clinical reasoning that a conference in computing and machine intelligence would not have prompted: no independent language measure, no age composition by group, a non-autistic clinical comparison deliberately discarded, and a recommendation about clinical model choice resting on a one-to-two-point margin. Set beside several journal papers in this review that report 98-100% from image-level splits on web-scraped corpora, its 84% from a participant-level split on a consented research archive is the more informative result." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-132" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Good for a short conference paper, and notably strong on the preprocessing that determines whether cross-corpus features are comparable. Reported: all four source corpora by name with recording protocols where known, the deliberate exclusion of developmentally delayed children, group sizes, the age range, all six features with both their child-alone and ratio definitions and the reason ratios were used, the NLP package, the token-cleaning rules in detail - symbols removed, bracketed and parenthesised tokens removed as unspoken, 'xxx' and '@<letters>' special-form markers stripped because they are used inconsistently across datasets, '&=' tokens removed as non-speech sounds - the seven models with their library provenance, the convolutional architecture layer by layer, the 75/25 split, the 50 repeated splits with the stability criterion that justified that number, and all five metrics with ROC variability bands. Not reported: age and sex composition by diagnostic group, the source corpora's diagnostic procedures, which features were removed before the headline accuracies, and code." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-132" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Presented at an IEEE computing conference in 2024 by a single secondary-school author in California. The framing is the standard early-detection argument, stated accurately: 1 in 36 children affected, no medical test, professional diagnosis reliable from age 2, and many children diagnosed late or never. For this review the study is valuable on four counts that have little to do with its accuracy figure. It is one of a small number of included studies whose split unit is the participant and whose evaluation design is both explained and empirically justified - 50 repeated 75/25 splits chosen because metrics stabilised within 1% - and it reports estimate variability through ROC standard-deviation bands rather than a bare point figure. Its features are named psycholinguistic constructs with an established literature, so its results are directly comparable with prior findings, and it makes those comparisons explicitly, confirming one study's mean-length-of-utterance result and contradicting another's on total word count. Its accuracy of 83-84%, benchmarked against three prior studies at 70-80%, sits in a credible range for a genuinely hard task and provides a useful calibration point against the 95-100% figures produced elsewhere in this review by leaked or confounded designs. And its sex-stratified feature analysis reports a diagnosis-by-sex interaction - mean length of turn lower in autistic boys than typically developing boys but higher in autistic girls than typically developing girls - which, if real, means a classifier trained predominantly on boys would misread girls, exactly the failure another study in this review demonstrated empirically with sensitivity falling from 94.8% pooled to 43.6% for girls. Three cautions travel with it. The top-ranked predictor is mean length of utterance, a canonical index of expressive language development that the paper's own analysis shows rising with age, and with no independent language measure and no age composition reported by group, language delay and age cannot be separated from autism as explanations. Part of the typically developing class was drawn from corpora containing no autistic children and using different recording protocols, so corpus of origin is partly correlated with class, and the four protocols differ in session length, structure and investigator role while half the features are ratios to the investigator's output. And the developmentally delayed children present in one source corpus were deliberately excluded to keep the task binary, discarding the non-autistic clinical comparison that would have made the study far more informative and that the review has found almost nowhere else." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-132" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No study setting; four archived corpora with different recording protocols. The heterogeneity is presented as a strength - two data banks used 'to better represent the population and make the detection model systems more reliable' - and it is a defensible position against the single-corpus practice the paper criticises. It also creates a partial provenance confound that goes unexamined. The autistic children come only from Eigsti and Nadig; the typically developing children come from those two plus Warren and Kuczaj, which contain no autistic children at all. For that supplemented fraction, corpus of origin is perfectly correlated with class, and the corpora differ in ways that bear directly on the features: session lengths of 30 minutes, 10 minutes, unspecified and 30 minutes respectively; a structured cleanup period in one and not the others; and different investigator roles, which matters because half the features are ratios of the child's output to the investigator's. The paper addresses the transcription-convention side of cross-corpus variation in detail and does not address the protocol side." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-132" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two limitations are stated: limited data, with a call for greater variety of participant backgrounds; and residual noise after feature computation, acknowledged as partly inevitable 'since speech is transcribed in different ways across datasets'. The second identifies cross-corpus heterogeneity at the transcription level, which is the right issue seen from one side. What is not addressed: mean length of utterance, the top predictor, is a developmental language index that the paper's own analysis shows increasing with age, and with no independent language measure and no reported age composition by group, language delay and age cannot be separated from autism; part of the typically developing class comes from corpora containing no autistic children and using different protocols, so corpus of origin is partly correlated with class; session lengths differ threefold where stated and are unknown for one corpus, and the investigator's role differs across protocols although half the features are ratios to the investigator; developmentally delayed children were deliberately excluded, removing the clinical comparison that would have made the result far more informative; the headline accuracies were obtained 'after the removal of certain features' selected on the same data; no confidence intervals accompany estimates from test folds of sixteen children; and no model performance is reported by age band or sex despite the feature analysis being stratified and despite that analysis finding a sex-by-diagnosis interaction." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-132" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Partially reported and, unusually, analytically used. The paper states the age range (3 to 6), acknowledges that boys predominate and that the 3-4 band is larger than the 5-6 band, and explains the sex imbalance by reference to the diagnostic ratio. It does not give numerical age or sex breakdowns by diagnostic group, and no cognitive, language, socioeconomic or ancestry information is available. What distinguishes it from the rest of this corpus is what it does with the demographics it has: rather than reporting them and moving on, it computes every feature separately for autistic and typically developing children, for the two age bands and for boys and girls, and reports the interactions. The results are substantive - mean length of utterance rises with age; boys aged 3-4 exceed girls of the same age on the key features while girls aged 5-6 exceed boys; and mean length of turn is lower in autistic boys than typically developing boys but higher in autistic girls than typically developing girls. That last sex-by-diagnosis interaction, if real, would mean a single classifier trained mostly on boys could systematically misread girls - which is exactly the failure mode a speech study charted elsewhere in this review demonstrated empirically, with sensitivity falling from 94.8% pooled to 43.6% for girls. The gap here is that the paper stops at feature-level description and never evaluates model performance by subgroup, so the implication of its own finding goes untested." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-132" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Standard clinical-deficit framing, brief and without the physiognomic or pathologising excesses found elsewhere in this corpus. Autistic children 'commonly have difficulty communicating and interacting with others, show repetitive and restricted behaviors, and have delayed cognitive and movement skills'; the phrase 'there is no permanent cure for ASD' appears, and children are once referred to as 'patients with ASD'. Person-first language predominates. The framing of the access problem is accurate and non-alarmist: diagnosis is difficult because 'there is no medical test', a professional diagnosis from age 2 is considered reliable, and 'some patients with ASD are not diagnosed until after their childhood, and many do not receive a diagnosis at all'. No neurodiversity framing appears and no autistic person is reported as involved. The paper's most careful language is about sex: rather than treating the male preponderance in its sample as a nuisance, it names the four-to-one diagnostic ratio as the cause and then analyses features separately for boys and girls - one of very few studies in this review to treat sex as a substantive variable rather than a sampling artefact." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-132" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Inherited from the source corpora and not transmitted. Children are designated autistic or typically developing by the original Eigsti and Nadig investigators, but this paper reports no diagnostic instrument, criteria, clinician qualification or assessment date. The labels are nonetheless in a different class from those in the web-scraped branches of this review: ASDBank and CHILDES are established research archives whose contributing studies performed documented clinical ascertainment, so the underlying labels are real diagnoses rather than folder membership or hashtag use. The specific weaknesses are that the paper does not tell the reader what those diagnoses rested on, and that the typically developing designation for the Warren and Kuczaj children means only that those corpora were collected from typically developing populations, without any screening described in this paper." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-132" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:study_design "Retrospective secondary analysis of archived speech transcripts, comparing five classical and two deep learning classifiers over repeated random train-test splits, with a descripti…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: ; comparison: 34 typically developing children, drawn from Eigsti and Nadig and supplemented from Warren and Kuczaj 'to try to balance demographic (gender and age) representations'" ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "27e1b0eeb0bdc45d52e0a78974fabb389783a117700e1a280e267f718df3807c" ;
