<https://doi.org/10.1111/1460-6984.12731> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Background: Deficits in the ability to use language in social contexts, including storytelling skills, are observed across the autism spectrum. Development in machine-learning approaches may contribute to clinical psychology and psychiatry, given its potential to support decisions concerning the diagnosis and treatment of psychiatric conditions and disorders. Aims: To evaluate the usefulness of deep neural networks for detecting autism spectrum disorder (ASD) from textual utterances, specifically from narrations produced by individuals with ASD. Methods & Procedures: We examined two text encoders: Embeddings from Language Models (ELMo) and Universal Sentence Encoder (USE), and three classification algorithms: XGBoost, support vector machines, and dense neural network layer. We aimed to classify 25 participants with ASD and 25 participants with typical development (TD) based on their narrations produced during the Autism Diagnostic Observation Schedule, Second Edition (ADOS-2) picture book task. The results of computational approaches were compared with the results of standardized testing and classifications made by two psychiatrists (raters). The raters were asked to read utterances produced by a participant (without an examiner’s statements and additional information) and assign a participant to one of the two groups: ASD or with typical development (TD). Outcomes & Results: The computer-based models had higher sensitivity, specificity, positive predictive values and negative predictive values than the raters, and lower than the two standardized instruments: ADOS-2 and Social Communication Questionnaire (SCQ). Conclusions & Implications: Our findings lay the groundwork for future studies involving deep neural network-based text representation models as tools for augmenting the ASD diagnosis or screening. Both ELMo and USE text encoders provided promising specificities, sensitivities, positive predictive values and This is an open access article under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivs License, which permits use and distribution in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited, the use is non-commercial and no modifications or adaptations are made. © 2022 The Authors. International Journal of Language & Communication Disorders published by John Wiley & Sons Ltd on behalf of Royal College of Speech and Language Therapists 948 wileyonlinelibrary.com/journal/jlcd Int J Lang Commun Disord. 2022;57:948–962. WAWER and CHOJNICKA 949 negative predictive values. Our results indicate the usefulness of page-level embeddings for utterance representation in ADOS-2 picture book task. K E Y WO R D S autism spectrum disorders (ASD), deep neural networks, deep learning, language processing, narrative WHAT THIS PAPER ADDS What is already known on this subject Deficits in the use of language in social contexts, and narrative ability in particular, are observed across the autism spectrum. Most re" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Wawer A; Chojnicka I (2022). Detecting autism from picture book narratives using deep neural utterance embeddings. *International Journal of Language & Communication Disorders, 57(5), 948-962*. https://doi.org/10.1111/1460-6984.12731" ;
    dcterms:creator "Chojnicka I",
        "Wawer A" ;
    dcterms:title "Detecting autism from picture book narratives using deep neural utterance embeddings" ;
    bibo:doi "10.1111/1460-6984.12731" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_81ecbc6f" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2022 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a non-spectrum clinical comparison group included?" ;
                            slr:justification "Identified by the authors as needed in future work." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "Age p = 0.92; verbal IQ p = 0.20; non-verbal IQ p = 0.13, though the authors describe the IQ match as imperfect." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sample representative of the target population?" ;
                            slr:justification "Fluent speakers with non-verbal IQ of 80 or above only, and the authors note the sample was selected for clear-cut cases." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Recruitment routes, inclusion and exclusion criteria, ages and both IQ measures given with tests; sex composition claimed as matched but never reported." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the comparison group screened stringently?" ;
                            slr:justification "Excluded for personal and family autism history, developmental disorder history, and neurological, psychiatric or suspected genetic conditions, plus fresh ADOS-2 and SCQ." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the page-segmentation procedure reproducible?" ;
                            slr:justification "Performed manually and acknowledged as 'sometimes ambiguous', resolved only by reference to video." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the elicitation task described and justified?" ;
                            slr:justification "ADOS-2 Telling a Story from a Book with the specific title, and a rationale grounded in the book's depiction of unreal situations and characters' mental states." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is transcription reliability quantified?" ;
                            slr:justification "Two independent blind transcribers, word-level reliability 91%, word-for-word comparison and video-based resolution of discrepancies - the only such figure in the corpus." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the language samples characterised?" ;
                            slr:justification "Mean 22 sentences, 323 tokens, 4:24 duration, 13.6 of 15 pages narrated, and a 15% rate of pages with no utterance." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "Prior multidisciplinary ICD-10 diagnosis, with ADOS-2 and SCQ administered fresh for the study by research-reliable clinicians." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is model selection separated from performance estimation?" ;
                            slr:justification "Best-classifier selection across three families and a 27-point grid performed on the same leave-one-out folds used for reporting." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are all model configurations reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "All six encoder-by-representation configurations appear, but only the best classifier within each." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is uncertainty quantified?" ;
                            slr:justification "No AUC, confidence interval or significance test on any of the ten reported rows." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are negative results reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Recurrent classifiers noted in a footnote as substantially worse; the weakest configuration (USE single, 0.56 sensitivity) reported alongside the best." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is performance benchmarked against meaningful comparators?" ;
                            slr:justification "Two standardised instruments and two human raters on the same 50 participants with identical metrics - the corpus's most informative comparative framing." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the unit of analysis the participant?" ;
                            slr:justification "One vector per participant in every configuration, with leave-one-out cross-validation over people." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Available on request, withheld from public deposit on an explicit privacy rationale appropriate to identifiable clinical transcripts." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a deployability argument allowed to override a performance argument?" ;
                            slr:justification "The single-vector approach is recommended for clinical use despite slightly lower scores, because page segmentation cannot be fully automated." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the ceiling comparison qualified?" ;
                            slr:justification "The authors note their instruments exceeded published validation figures because the sample was selected for clear cases while validation samples included non-spectrum participants." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the model's performance interpreted proportionately?" ;
                            slr:justification "The authors state the models are far below the instruments, that outperforming raters 'does not indicate that neural networks can replace clinical judgment', and that the role can only be complementary." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the absence of interpretability acknowledged?" ;
                            slr:justification "Named as a limitation with word-level attribution proposed as a route to linguistic discovery." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Not released, though package URLs and settings are given in footnotes." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "University of Warsaw Faculty of Psychology ethics approval with a three-tier consent structure covering parents of minors, parents of those 16 and over, and participants themselves." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional, one narration per participant, no follow-up. Ethics reporting is complete and unusually granular on consent, which was obtained separately from three parties as applicable: parents of participants under 16, parents of participants aged 16 and older, and the participants themselves - a structure that respects the assent of older adolescents and adults alongside parental consent. Approval came from the Faculty of Psychology, University of Warsaw Research Ethics Committee, with Declaration of Helsinki conformity stated. Data are withheld from public deposit on an explicit and appropriate privacy rationale - narrative transcripts of identifiable clinical assessments 'containing information that could compromise the privacy of the research participants' - with availability on request from the named authors. No competing interests, with Polish National Science Centre and University of Warsaw funding declared." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-083" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Poland, with participants contacted through a university clinic, diagnostic or therapeutic centres, or schools, and assessed as part of a research evaluation. The narratives themselves come from ADOS-2 administrations conducted by research-reliable clinicians, so the elicitation context is a standardised clinical assessment rather than a naturalistic setting. This has the same double edge noted in the OHSU studies: the standardisation makes narratives comparable across participants and the data are a by-product of an assessment already being conducted, but the assessment is specifically designed to elicit autism-related behaviour, so performance may not transfer to spontaneous narration. The authors raise the related point about narrative context, citing evidence that 'participants with ASD exhibit lower performance in less structured tasks (i.e., narratives of personal experiences, conversation) than more structured picture book tasks' - which implies, though the authors do not draw the inference explicitly, that a less structured elicitation might yield stronger rather than weaker classification." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-083" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Six directions, each following from a stated limitation. Larger samples permitting stratification by age, sex and IQ. Inclusion of non-spectrum and other psychiatric groups. Other narrative contexts, with the specific prediction that less structured elicitation may behave differently. Model explanation techniques applied to word-level contributions, with the goal that 'giving meaning to collections of important words may lead to discovering new dimensions of language relevant to ASD' - which frames explainability as a route to linguistic discovery rather than only to trust. Combination of text with speech and extra-linguistic features, citing Asgari and colleagues and Fusaroli and colleagues. And automatic speech-to-text to eliminate the transcription bottleneck. The authors also express a clear preference between their own representations for translational purposes: 'the single vector approach, rather than the pages approach, seems to have the greatest possible clinical utility', because page segmentation is ambiguous and manual whereas the single vector 'enables full automation and objectification of the measurement' - choosing the marginally worse-performing option on deployability grounds, which is the right kind of reasoning and rare in this corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-083" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2022 (submitted October 2020, accepted April 2022). The framing is unusual in this corpus for beginning from the strength of existing practice rather than its weakness: 'the validity and reliability of diagnosis of ASD improves considerably when diagnostic evaluation is carried out using standardized tools with good psychometric properties', and computational models are positioned as tools that 'may provide tools that, similarly to psychological testing, support clinicians in their decision-making'. The limiting statement follows immediately - 'due to the multidimensionality and complexity of the diagnosis problem, the role of such automatic analyses of narratives may only be complementary or supportive' - and the paper then proves its own point by measuring the gap between its models and the standardised instruments. The specific clinical gap it identifies is narrower and better argued than the usual appeals to cost or delay: hand-coding of narratives 'is time and labour intensive, and requires extensive training for coders to achieve reliability', which 'limit[s] sample sizes and may be difficult to implement across different research sites', while 'objective, valid and reliable measures of narration ability among individuals with ASD with no clear structural language deficits are limited' and 'standardized tests often miss evident-in-naturalistic-settings differences'. The target population is therefore explicitly the group for whom current instruments are least sensitive - 'participants with less severe autism spectrum-related symptoms, more advanced language, and no intellectual disability' - and the sample is constructed to match, with non-verbal IQ of 80 or above and fluent speech required." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-083" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The ADOS-2 Telling a Story from a Book task using Tuesday by David Wiesner, a 15-page wordless picture book in which frogs float on lily pads and fly over a town at night. The choice is substantively motivated: the book presents 'unreal and humorous situations, and several mental and emotional states of the characters', so producing a coherent narrative requires inference about characters' internal states, causal linking of unusual events, and management of a fantastical premise - exactly the pragmatic demands the narrative literature identifies as difficult in autism. Being wordless, it supplies no linguistic scaffolding, so the entire narrative is the participant's construction. The resulting samples are short: on average 22 sentences, 323 tokens and 4 minutes 24 seconds, with participants producing utterances for a mean of 13.6 of 15 pages. That brevity is the practical constraint on the whole study - a few hundred words per person is a thin basis for a 15,360-dimensional representation - and the paper's honest performance figures should be read against it." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-083" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: autism spectrum disorders (ASD), deep neural networks, deep learning, language processing, narrative. The inclusion of 'narrative' alongside three method terms signals the study's dual location in computational linguistics and communication disorders, and the journal's structured 'What this paper adds' box frames the contribution in clinical rather than technical terms." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-083" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two-stage and well structured. Entry required a prior clinical diagnosis of idiopathic autism made by a multidisciplinary team including a psychiatrist under ICD-10 criteria - so ascertainment is clinical and independent of the research team. For the study itself, ADOS-2 was administered by research-reliable clinicians under the supervision of an author who is an ADOS-2 trainer, and parents completed the SCQ. The critical design detail is stated explicitly: 'Prior to this study, participants were not evaluated using SCQ and ADOS-2 measures.' That independence is what makes the instruments usable as comparators rather than as circular confirmations - though not entirely, since group membership was determined by the prior clinical diagnosis and the instruments are then evaluated against that same clinical determination. The Polish versions of both instruments have published psychometric validation, and the SCQ's reported agreement with clinical diagnosis (kappa 0.84) and with ADI-R (kappa 0.81) is cited. The comparison group's negative status is established by three separate exclusions plus fresh ADOS-2 and SCQ administration." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-083" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: evaluate deep neural text encoders for detecting autism from narrative transcripts and compare them against standardised instruments and human expert judgement on the same participants. Main findings: best computational configurations reached sensitivity 0.72 and specificity 0.68; two psychiatrists reading identical transcripts achieved 0.56-0.60 sensitivity and 0.60-0.64 specificity, close to chance; ADOS-2 achieved 1.00 sensitivity and 0.92 specificity and SCQ 0.92 and 0.96 on the same sample; page-level representation with zero vectors for skipped pages outperformed whole-narration representation; and ELMo and USE reached identical top scores despite different architectures, suggesting representation granularity matters more than encoder choice." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-083" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Both verbal and non-verbal IQ measured in every participant using age-appropriate Wechsler scales - the WISC-R for those aged 6.0-16.11 and the WAIS for those older - with means, standard deviations, ranges and t-tests reported for both. Reporting verbal and non-verbal IQ separately is appropriate and uncommon, and matters directly for a narrative-language outcome. Non-verbal IQ of 80 or above was an inclusion criterion, so the sample is confined to participants without intellectual disability, which the authors identify as limiting generalisation: 'our study included only fluently speaking participants with ASD with no intellectual disability and might not apply to everyone on the autism spectrum'. ADOS-2 overall and SCQ overall scores characterise severity in both groups. No adaptive functioning or language-specific standardised measure was administered, and no comorbidity information is reported beyond the idiopathic-autism inclusion requirement." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-083" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'individuals with ASD', 'participants with ASD', 'people with ASD', 'individuals with typical development', 'participants with TD' - consistently person-first, with 'controls with TD' used for the comparison group. 'Patients' appears once in a general statement about clinical practice. The framing acknowledges heterogeneity explicitly at the outset - 'the clinical expression of the disorder is heterogeneous, and symptoms may vary widely from person to person. The cognitive abilities of people with ASD can range from gifted to severely challenged' - and describes autistic people as those who 'may communicate, interact, behave and learn differently from most other people', which is a difference rather than deficit construction. Narrative characteristics are nonetheless described in deficit terms throughout the literature review ('poor narrative skills', 'struggle with conveying the gist', 'deficits in pragmatic language'), reflecting the source literature." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-083" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Frozen pre-trained encoders feeding conventional classifiers, rather than end-to-end fine-tuning - the appropriate choice for 50 participants and roughly 300 words each, and justified on exactly those grounds: 'Thanks to the properties of embedding vectors, classification algorithms can successfully learn from relatively small labelled data sets.' Three classifier families were tried (XGBoost, SVM with two kernels, and a single dense layer with a 27-point hyperparameter grid), and a footnote records that recurrent classifiers were also tested and performed substantially worse. Reporting the failed recurrent experiments is good practice. The problem is the reporting rule for the successful ones: 'For each input combination we only report the results of the best performing classifier', so each of the six reported figures is the maximum over at least three classifier configurations and, for the masking rows, over a 27-point grid - all selected on the same leave-one-out folds used for reporting, with no held-out estimate of the selection cost." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-083" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Narrative discourse as linguistic content, deliberately abstracted from acoustics. What is modelled is the semantic and syntactic structure of what the participant said, encoded by language models pre-trained on general Polish text - so the target is pragmatic and structural narrative ability rather than prosody or voice quality. The literature review specifies the constructs at stake: conveying the gist of a story, appropriate use of referential devices, coherence and causal connection, avoidance of irrelevant components, lexical diversity, and internal state language describing characters' perceptions, emotions and cognitive states. None of these is measured directly; the embeddings are expected to capture them implicitly, which is the study's bet and also the source of its interpretability gap. The complement to this design is Asgari and colleagues elsewhere in this corpus, who model the acoustics of the same kind of ADOS-derived speech while discarding the content, and MacFarlane and colleagues, who show that the two channels detect different participants." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-083" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Six limitations are stated, all substantive. The sample is small with a wide age range and imperfect IQ matching, so 'we were not able to divide our sample into subgroups based on age, sex or IQ level'. The sample includes only fluently speaking participants without intellectual disability, so results 'might not apply to everyone on the autism spectrum' - followed by an unusually thoughtful observation that this may mean the figures 'show the upper limit of diagnostic possibilities from the closed text data only'. Only one narrative context was tested, with the authors noting prior evidence that less structured tasks are harder for autistic participants. No non-spectrum clinical comparison group was included. The approach requires high-quality transcription, which remains difficult to automate for spontaneous speech. And the models offer no interpretability. The authors additionally volunteer, when discussing why their instruments outperformed published validation figures, that 'participants in our sample were carefully selected with clear signs of ASD among individuals in the ASD group and lack of developmental problems among individuals in the TD group' - a candid acknowledgement that the sample is easier than a clinical population, which qualifies their own headline comparison. Not addressed: that only the best classifier per input combination is reported, over three families and a 27-point grid; the absence of any confidence interval on figures derived from 25 cases per class; and the unreported sex composition." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-083" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None, and the authors treat this as the study's principal scientific shortcoming rather than a minor gap: 'Neural network encoders followed by classifiers can take into account a wide variety of utterance characteristics but does not help to identify which particular aspects of language are crucial.' They frame the remedy as a route to discovery rather than to trust, proposing LIME-style word-level attribution so that 'giving meaning to collections of important words may lead to discovering new dimensions of language relevant to ASD', and connecting it to their own prior work identifying sentiment and language abstraction as relevant dimensions - while noting 'it is very likely that this list is not exhaustive'. That framing is correct: for a study whose motivating literature is entirely about specific narrative constructs (coherence, causal connection, referential devices, internal state language), a classifier that cannot say which of these it is using contributes no linguistic knowledge. The one indirect interpretive finding is that skipped pages carry signal, inferred from zero-vector representation outperforming masking." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-083" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Reported with tests for every variable. Autistic group: 25 participants, 14.55 +/- 5.46 years (7.1-24.6), non-verbal IQ 109.08 +/- 13.04 (80-140), verbal IQ 106.62 +/- 19.70 (72-131). Comparison group: 25 participants, 14.38 +/- 5.83 years (7.3-25.3), non-verbal IQ 114.64 +/- 12.50 (80-134), verbal IQ 113.12 +/- 14.33 (85-141). Age matches closely (p = 0.92) and both IQ measures are non-significant (p = 0.13 and p = 0.20), though the authors describe the match as 'nearly matched' and list imperfect IQ matching among their limitations - an appropriately cautious reading, since a five-to-six point gap in both verbal and non-verbal IQ at n = 25 per group is underpowered to detect. Verbal IQ matters particularly here because the outcome is narrative language. The one reporting gap is sex: the groups are stated to be matched on sex and ethnicity, but no counts, proportions or test are given for either, so the claim cannot be verified. The age range is wide (7 to 25 years), spanning most of narrative development, and the authors note they could not stratify by age, sex or IQ at this sample size." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-083" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Single modality - text only, with no acoustic or extra-linguistic input, and combination with speech features named as future work. Within the modality the study performs a systematic representation comparison that functions as its main experimental variable: two encoders crossed with three representation schemes, reported as six configurations. The pattern is informative. Page-level representation with zero vectors beat whole-narration representation for both encoders, which the authors attribute to information loss in aggregation - 'the aggregation that occurs in the single vector approach for the whole narration causes some key information to be lost' - and to the possibility that particular pages matter more than others. The zero-vector scheme also beat masking, which is the more interesting of the two comparisons: masking tells the model to ignore pages the participant skipped, while zero vectors leave a marked absence, and the better performance of the latter supports the authors' reading that 'even missing out certain pages may provide clues to identify ASD participants'. Silence is treated as signal rather than as missing data. And the two encoders reaching identical top scores despite one being a bidirectional LSTM and the other a transformer suggests the architecture is not the limiting factor at this sample size." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-083" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Good on the computational pipeline and the language sample, thinner on the participants. Reported: inclusion and exclusion criteria for both groups; the ADOS-2 task and the specific book; the transcription protocol with its convention, blinding, reliability figure and discrepancy resolution; descriptive statistics of the transcripts (sentences, tokens, duration, pages narrated); all three representation schemes with their vector dimensions and the 15% missing-page rate; every encoder with its version, pre-training corpus and settings; the classifier families with the dense layer's full hyperparameter grid; the leave-one-out scheme; and package URLs. A footnote even records that recurrent classifiers were tried and performed substantially worse. Not reported: sex composition despite matching being claimed; how the narrative was divided into page-level subsets, described only as done manually and acknowledged as 'sometimes ambiguous, especially when a participant skips one of the pages'; the number of trials or seeds; and any AUC, confidence interval or significance test. Data are available on request rather than deposited, for stated privacy reasons; no code release." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-083" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Sensitivity, specificity, positive predictive value and negative predictive value - clinically appropriate metrics reported identically for all ten rows of the comparison table. Reporting PPV and NPV alongside sensitivity and specificity is uncommon here and is meaningful in this design because the class balance is 25:25, so the predictive values are directly interpretable as performance at 50% prevalence. The table's real contribution is its scope: six model configurations, two human raters and two standardised instruments, all evaluated on the same 50 participants with the same four metrics, which allows the reader to locate the models between a human floor and an instrument ceiling rather than against an absent baseline. What is missing matters at this sample size. There is no AUC, no confidence interval and no significance test anywhere, so a sensitivity of 0.72 represents 18 of 25 correct and carries a binomial confidence interval running from roughly 0.51 to 0.88 - wide enough that the differences among the six configurations, and between the models and the raters, are not established. Nor is any chance or trivial baseline reported, though the balanced design makes 0.50 the natural reference and the human raters serve a similar function empirically." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-083" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Leave-one-out cross-validation with the participant as the held-out unit, chosen deliberately for small-sample efficiency, and correctly described. Because each participant is represented by a single vector there is no possibility of utterance-level leakage. What is not controlled is selection: only the best classifier per input combination is reported, across three families and, for the masking configurations, a 27-point hyperparameter grid, all evaluated on the same folds that produce the reported numbers. With 50 participants and six reported configurations each maximised over several classifiers, the optimism is unquantified and could plausibly account for a substantial part of the 12-point gap between the best models (0.72) and the human raters (0.60). No permutation test, confidence interval or AUC is reported to bound this. Set against that, the study's benchmarking design provides a form of validation that internal resampling cannot: the human raters constitute an empirical performance floor obtained on identical input, and the standardised instruments an empirical ceiling obtained on identical participants, both of which anchor the model figures in a way no single-model study can achieve. The authors themselves qualify the ceiling, noting that their instruments exceeded their own published validation figures because the sample was selected for clear-cut cases while the validation sample included non-spectrum clinical participants." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-083" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No specialised acquisition equipment - narratives were video recorded during ADOS-2 sessions already being conducted. The methodological weight sits on transcription, which is described more carefully than in any other study here: two experienced transcribers worked independently and blind to diagnosis, following the Codes for Human Analysis of Transcripts convention, achieving word-level reliability averaging 91%; one trained transcriber then compared the two transcripts word for word and resolved discrepancies by reference to the video. Reporting an actual reliability figure for transcription is unique in this corpus - MacFarlane and colleagues describe extensive transcriber training and consistency checks but state explicitly that they have no reliability results. Computational tools are named precisely: the Polish ELMo from ELMoForManyLangs pre-trained on 20 million words from Wikipedia and Common Crawl, at default settings; Universal Sentence Encoder large v3; XGBoost with default settings; scikit-learn NuSVC; and TensorFlow 2, with package URLs given in footnotes." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-083" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "International Journal of Language and Communication Disorders 57(5):948-962 (2022), published on behalf of the Royal College of Speech and Language Therapists - a speech and language therapy journal, so the readership is clinical rather than computational. Authorship pairs the Institute of Computer Science of the Polish Academy of Sciences with the Faculty of Psychology at the University of Warsaw, with equal contribution declared, and the psychology author is an ADOS-2 trainer who supervised the assessments. That composition explains the study's shape: the clinical measurement is handled to research standards (research-reliable ADOS-2 administration, blind dual transcription with reliability, three-tier consent) while the computational work is competent but modest, and the interpretation is oriented toward what a speech and language therapist could use. The journal's format requires an explicit statement of clinical implications, which the authors use to state the supportive rather than replacement role of the method." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-083" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups of 25, recruited through a university clinic, diagnostic or therapeutic centres and schools, and 'matched for age, sex and ethnicity, and nearly matched for intelligence quotients'. The autistic group required a prior clinical diagnosis of idiopathic autism from a multidisciplinary team including a psychiatrist under ICD-10 criteria; the comparison group was excluded for personal or family history of autism, history of developmental disorders, and neurological or psychiatric conditions or suspected genetic syndromes. No other-diagnosis comparison group, which the authors identify as a limitation and propose for future work: 'as we compared individuals with ASD and controls with TD, future studies should include individuals with non-spectrum disorders and other psychiatric conditions'. The design's distinguishing feature is that two further comparators operate on the same 50 participants - two standardised instruments and two human raters - so the study contains four independent classification approaches on one sample rather than one model evaluated in isolation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-083" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "One feature vector per participant in every configuration, so the unit is the person and leave-one-out cross-validation is genuinely participant-level. The representation question is the study's actual experiment, and it is framed as a trade-off rather than an optimisation. The page-level scheme carries far more information - 15,360 dimensions for ELMo against 1024 for the single vector - and performs marginally better, but requires manually segmenting the narrative by book page, which the authors describe as 'sometimes ambiguous, especially when a participant skips one of the pages during storytelling' and which they were only able to resolve because they had video recordings; with audio alone it 'may require arbitrary decisions whether the utterance should be assigned to one page or the next'. They then recommend the single-vector approach for clinical use despite its slightly lower scores, because it is fully automatable. That is a deployability argument overriding a performance argument, made explicitly, and it is the kind of reasoning almost absent from the rest of this corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-083" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "One narration per participant during a single ADOS-2 administration, averaging 4 minutes 24 seconds (range 2:28-7:22)" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional case-control classification with a within-study benchmarking design comparing computational models, human expert judgement on identical input, and standardised diag…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Fluently speaking autistic children, adolescents and young adults without intellectual disability" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 25; comparison: 25" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Best computational models: ELMo with page-level representation, zero vectors and SVM, and USE with page-level representation, zero vectors and XGBoost, both at sensitivity 0.72, specificity 0.68, PPV 0.69 and NPV 0.71. ELMo with a single whole-narration vector and SVM gave the highest sensitivity at 0.76 but specificity of only 0.60. USE with a single vector was weakest at 0.56 sensitivity. The tw",
                        "Pre-trained deep text encoders applied to picture-book narratives classified autism at sensitivity 0.72 and specificity 0.68 - modestly better than two experienced psychiatrists given the same transcripts (0.56-0.60 sensitivity, 0.60-0.64 specificity, near chance) and far worse than the standardised instruments administered to the same participants (ADOS-2 at 1.00 and 0.92; SCQ at 0.92 and 0.96). " ;
                    slr:outcome "Sensitivity, specificity, positive predictive value and negative predictive value, reported identically for six computational configurations, two human raters and two standardised instruments in a single table" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Best computational models: ELMo with page-level representation, zero vectors and SVM, and USE with page-level representation, zero vectors and XGBoost, both at sensitivity 0.72, specificity 0.68, PPV 0.69 and NPV 0.71. ELMo with a single whole-narration vector and SVM gave the highest sensitivity at 0.76 but specificity of only 0.60. USE with a single vector was weakest at 0.56 sensitivity. The tw; Pre-trained deep text encoders applied to picture-book narratives classified autism at sensitivity 0.72 and specificity 0.68 - modestly better than two experienced psychiatrists given the same transcripts (0.56-0.60 sensitivity, 0.60-0.64 specificity, near chance) and far worse than the standardised instruments administered to the same participants (ADOS-2 at 1.00 and 0.92; SCQ at 0.92 and 0.96). " ] ] ;
