    slr:journal "B" ;
    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "A matched case-control design with autistic children drawn from a hospital and intervention service centres and comparison children from a kindergarten, so recruitment route differs by group. Children with any other psychiatric diagnosis, major physical or neurological disease or prior psychotropic medication were excluded, and 23 clinically diagnosed children scoring below CARS-2 30 were removed - truncating the spectrum at precisely the point where screening is most difficult. The authors state this themselves and connect it to performance inflation. Age and sex are matched and formally tested, which is better than most of this corpus, and the 1:1 ratio is a design artefact rather than a prevalence." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Best-estimate clinical diagnosis by senior child and adolescent psychiatrists under DSM-5, corroborated by CARS-2 with the instrument's published psychometrics cited. This is genuine clinical ascertainment by qualified clinicians and among the strongest reference standards in the review. The severity restriction it was combined with is captured under patient selection rather than here." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "unclear" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "The task instruction is verbatim and tightly constrained, the toy set is fixed, and the coding manual is supplied in full, with three coders trained over a month and inter-coder consistency of 83.7-91.7% on 30 shared cases. But every one of the 81 indicators originates in a human judgement, coder blinding to group is not stated, and the recording contexts differ by recruitment route, so group membership may be inferable from a video independently of the behaviours scored. Five predictors were selected from 81 candidates and the operating cut-off chosen from the same ROC curve." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "All 246 children completed the task and contributed one indicator vector each; the 23 exclusions are accounted for with their reason and occurred before the analysis. Diagnosis preceded the play task and was independent of it. The unit of analysis is the child throughout, so no within-participant leakage is possible. No recordings are reported lost or excluded for quality." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "unclear" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2025.1274675> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "EDITED BY Ashad Kabir, Charles Sturt University, Australia REVIEWED BY Chiranjibi Sitaula, The University of Melbourne, Australia Delaram Sadeghi, Islamic Azad University of Mashhad, Iran *CORRESPONDENCE Najla D. Al Futaisi n.al-futaisi18@imperial.ac.uk RECEIVED 08 August 2023 ACCEPTED 28 May 2025 PUBLISHED 18 August 2025 CITATION Al Futaisi ND, Schuller BW, Ringeval F and Pantic M (2025) The Noor Project: fair transformer transfer learning for autism spectrum disorder recognition from speech. Front. Digit. Health 7:1274675. doi: 10.3389/fdgth.2025.1274675 COPYRIGHT © 2025 Al Futaisi, Schuller, Ringeval and Pantic. This is an open-access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (CC BY). The use, distribution or reproduction in other forums is permitted, provided the original author(s) and the copyright owner(s) are credited and that the original publication in this journal is cited, in accordance with accepted academic practice. No use, distribution or reproduction is permitted which does not comply with these terms. The Noor Project: fair transformer transfer learning for autism spectrum disorder recognition from speech Najla D. Al Futaisi 1*, Björn W. Schuller 1,2, Fabien Ringeval 3 and Maja Pantic 4 1GLAM – Group on Language, Audio & Music, Imperial College London, London, United Kingdom, 2Chair of Embedded Intelligence for Health Care and Wellbeing, University of Augsburg, Augsburg, Germany, 3Grenoble INP, LIG, University Grenoble Alpes, Inria, CNRS, Grenoble, France, 4iBUG – Intelligent Behaviour Understanding Group, Imperial College London, London, United Kingdom Early detection is crucial for managing incurable disorders, particularly autism spectrum disorder (ASD). Unfortunately, a considerable number of individuals with ASD receive a late diagnosis or remain undiagnosed. Speech holds a critical role in ASD, as a signiﬁcant number of affected individuals experience speech impairments or remain non-verbal. To address this, we use speech analysis for automatic ASD recognition in children by classifying their speech as either autistic or typically developing. However, due to the lack of large labelled datasets, we leverage two smaller datasets to explore deep transfer learning methods. We investigate two ﬁne-tuning approaches: (1) Discriminative Fine-Tuning (D-FT), which is pre-trained on a related dataset before being tuned on a similar task, and (2) Wav2Vec 2.0 Fine-Tuning (W2V2- FT), which leverages self-supervised speech representations pre-trained on a larger, unrelated dataset. We perform two distinct classiﬁcation tasks: (a) a binary task to determine typicality, classifying speech as either that of a typically developing (TD) child or an atypically developing (AD) child; and (b) a four-class diagnosis task, which further classiﬁes atypical cases into ASD, dysphasia (DYS), or pervasive developmental disorder-not otherwise speciﬁed (NOS), alongside TD. This research aims to improve early recognition strategies, particularly for individuals with ASD. The ﬁndings suggest that transfer learning methods can be a valuable tool for autism recognition from speech. For the typicality classiﬁcation task (TD vs. AD), the D-FT model achieved the highest test UAR (94.8%), outperforming W2V2-FT (91.5%). In the diagnosis task (TD, ASD, DYS, NOS), D-FT also demonstrated superior performance (60.9% UAR) compared to W2V2-FT (54.3%). These results highlight the potential of transfer learning for speech-based ASD recognition and underscore the challenges of multi-class classiﬁcation with limited labeled data." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Al Futaisi ND; Schuller BW; Ringeval F; Pantic M (2024). The Noor Project: fair transformer transfer learning for autism spectrum disorder recognition from speech. *Frontiers in Digital Health, 7, Article*. https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2025.1274675" ;
    dcterms:creator "Al Futaisi ND",
        "Pantic M",
        "Ringeval F",
        "Schuller BW" ;
    dcterms:title "The Noor Project: fair transformer transfer learning for autism spectrum disorder recognition from speech" ;
    bibo:doi "10.3389/fdgth.2025.1274675" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_3ecb92bf" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2024 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Handling of missing data and attrition" ;
                            slr:justification "Complete partition tables are given for every class and every evaluation set. But no per-class participant counts, no account of excluded or unusable recordings, and no sample-duration statistics." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Comparison group adequacy" ;
                            slr:justification "The four-class task includes dysphasia and PDD-NOS as non-autistic clinical comparison groups - a design almost absent from this review and its principal merit. But the binary comparison group was recorded in schools while the clinical groups were recorded in clinics, so the contrast confounds diagnosis with recording setting." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Confounder control" ;
                            slr:justification "No age data by group in a corpus spanning 6-18 years, across which voice changes fundamentally; no language measure although one class is a language disorder; no control or normalisation for recording environment, which differs systematically by class. Sex is the one confounder taken seriously, through fairness testing, and the models fail that test substantially." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participant selection and spectrum representativeness" ;
                            slr:justification "Convenience corpora collected for other purposes, one with no control group at all. Ages 4-18 with no distribution reported, no cognitive or language measure, and participant counts that cannot be reconciled between sections." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Measurement standardisation" ;
                            slr:justification "No recording equipment, sampling rate, acoustic condition or preprocessing chain reported for either corpus, and the two binary classes were recorded in different physical settings with no normalisation or channel compensation applied. No task or elicitation is described." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reference standard quality" ;
                            slr:justification "Diagnoses inherited from source corpora with no procedure, instrument or criteria described, using pre-DSM-5 categories. The typically developing group is defined by absence of reported difficulties rather than by assessment." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome measurement blinding" ;
                            slr:justification "Classification is fully automated from audio; no human rater is involved at inference." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Index test definition and prespecification" ;
                            slr:justification "Two architectures compared on identical folds with a separate development partition and a prespecified primary metric. Hyperparameters and the number of runs are not reported, and no uncertainty estimate accompanies any figure." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Sample size adequacy for the modelling" ;
                            slr:justification "Deep transformer models fine-tuned on 62 minutes of labelled audio, with the four-class test partition containing 26 samples per clinical class from an unstated and possibly very small number of children. The authors acknowledge that the test sets are small and 'not fully representative of the real-world'." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data splitting and leakage control" ;
                            slr:justification "Speaker-disjoint splitting with the rationale stated explicitly, and a separate development partition - among the best-articulated splitting practice in the review. But the number of speakers per class is unknown, and speaker disjointness cannot address the class-level recording-setting confound." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Preprocessing and feature selection nesting" ;
                            slr:justification "Representations are learned; minority-class upsampling is applied to training only, with development and test distributions deliberately left unchanged 'to be representative of real-world' - a correct and explicitly reasoned choice that very few papers in this corpus make." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance estimation and chance level" ;
                            slr:justification "Unweighted average recall as primary metric, full metric suite across all partitions, confusion matrices, subgroup sensitivity and specificity, and benchmarking against a stated baseline and prior work on the same corpus - including a table showing the study's own four-class result below both. No confidence intervals or run-to-run variance." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "unclear" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reporting of results and uncertainty" ;
                            slr:justification "Every model reported on every partition with four metrics plus confusion matrices, and benchmarked against baseline and prior work - including where the study loses. But no confidence intervals, no variance across runs, and no significance testing, on margins as small as 0.7 points and test sets as small as 26 samples per class." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Acknowledgement of limitations" ;
                            slr:justification "Six limitations stated in detail, including the abandonment of the study's original younger-age aim, domain shift between corpora, class and gender imbalance with the fairness consequence spelled out, small test sets, and an explicit caution that the headline UAR must be interpreted against the corpus's specific characteristics. The clinic-versus-school recording confound is the significant omission." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data, code and protocol availability" ;
                            slr:justification "Neither corpus is openly available and no code is released. Contributions are stated to be in the article and supplementary material with inquiries to the corresponding author." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Ethics, consent and conflicts" ;
                            slr:justification "Approval attributed to the dataset administrations rather than a named research ethics committee, and consent stated as not required for participants or guardians - in a study of recorded child speech, and in a paper whose own introduction reflects at length on ethics committees as safeguards for exactly this kind of data. No funding and no competing interests declared." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Completeness of methods reporting" ;
                            slr:justification "Complete partition tables, split proportions, splitting rule with rationale, both architectures with their pre-training corpora, the resampling policy, the metric, and the fairness procedure with formulae. Missing: code, hyperparameters, sample durations, recording conditions, per-class participant counts, age distributions, and any diagnostic procedure." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "unclear" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The weakest demographic reporting of any included study, and the gaps are consequential rather than cosmetic. No age distribution is given for any group in either corpus, though the combined range spans 4 to 18 years - the period across which voice undergoes its most profound changes, including the pubertal fall in male fundamental frequency, vocal-tract growth and the maturation of prosody and syntax. If the atypical and typical groups differ in age composition, an age classifier and a diagnosis classifier would be behaviourally identical, and nothing reported allows this to be tested. No IQ, language, adaptive or socioeconomic measure exists, which is particularly striking given that one of the four diagnostic classes is dysphasia - the task asks a model to separate autism from a specific language impairment with no independent measure of language ability in any group. Ancestry is not reported. Sex is reported but inconsistently, at 22% female in De-Enigma and 19% in CPSD in one section and at 5% and 10% in another. The one demographic variable that receives serious analytic attention is sex, through equalised-odds fairness testing - and that analysis returns the study's most important result, showing that performance measured on a mixed-gender partition conceals a substantial deficit for girls. That single finding does more for this review's understanding of demographic confounding than the descriptive tables of most other included studies." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-126" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Absent as an independent element and inherited without description. De-Enigma children are stated to be 'diagnosed with ASD' and CPSD children to carry diagnoses of autism, dysphasia or PDD-NOS, but neither corpus's diagnostic process is described - no instrument, no criteria, no clinician qualification, no date. The typically developing group is characterised only by absence: 'no prior history of difficulties with learning, speech, hearing or general learning', which is a screening statement rather than an assessment. Two features compound this. The CPSD categories are pre-DSM-5, since PDD-NOS was eliminated as a diagnosis in 2013, so the labels encode a superseded nosology whose boundaries with autism were themselves contested - and the four-class task therefore asks a model to reproduce a distinction that the diagnostic system has since abandoned. And the atypical and typical children were recorded in different physical settings, clinic and school respectively, so the label is confounded with the circumstance of recording independently of whatever the diagnoses mean. The paper does not treat the reference standard as a topic at all." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-126" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Vocalisation and speech, with the target construct stated as prosodic. The motivating account is specific: autistic speech is monotonous and 'lacks the typical variations in pitch, intonation, and expressive elements that are commonly observed in the speech of typically developing children', a pattern reported to persist into school age. Both speech and non-speech vocalisations are used from De-Enigma, and the authors defend that choice on communicative grounds - 'non-verbal vocalisations are a form of speech and hold communicative value' - and argue it extends the approach toward pre-linguistic assessment in younger children. What the study cannot establish is whether its models respond to that construct. The representation is a learned embedding of the raw waveform, no acoustic analysis or attribution is performed, and prosody is not measured directly at any point, so the models' 94.8% could rest on fundamental-frequency variability, on vocal-tract length, on speaking rate, on the language spoken, on the age of the speaker, or on the reverberation of the room. The four-class task is more informative about the construct than the binary one, because separating autism from dysphasia requires something more specific than general atypicality of speech - and it is precisely there that performance falls to 60.9%, below the baseline of 62.4%." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-126" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Frontiers in Digital Health 7:1274675, Original Research, open access under CC BY; received 8 August 2023, accepted 28 May 2025, published 18 August 2025, with editor and reviewers disclosed by name. The near two-year interval between submission and acceptance is unusual. The venue is a digital-health rather than a clinical journal, and the paper reads accordingly: the partitioning, metrics, benchmarking and fairness analysis are handled to a good computational standard while the clinical description of the corpora - diagnostic procedures, ages, per-class participant counts, recording conditions - is left almost entirely to the source datasets and is internally inconsistent where it is given. The supplied bibliographic metadata dates the article to 2024 while the article itself is a 2025 publication." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-126" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. A single audio modality throughout, with no fusion across sensors, channels or annotation types. The transfer in this study is across datasets and tasks rather than across modalities - discriminative fine-tuning transfers from one child-vocalisation corpus to another, and Wav2Vec 2.0 transfers from 960 hours of unlabelled adult speech to child clinical speech. The comparison between these two transfer sources is the study's clearest methodological result and is worth recording for the review: pre-training on a small, closely related corpus of child autistic vocalisations (D-FT) outperformed pre-training on a vastly larger but unrelated adult speech corpus (W2V2-FT) on both tasks, by 3.3 points on typicality and 6.6 points on diagnosis. Domain proximity beat scale. The caveat is that D-FT's advantage may partly reflect its exposure to De-Enigma's autistic vocalisations, which are class-informative in a way the unlabelled adult corpus is not." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-126" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two transfer-learning approaches compared head to head. Discriminative fine-tuning pre-trains on De-Enigma child vocalisations and fine-tunes on CPSD - the first application of this technique to autism recognition from speech, according to the authors. Wav2Vec 2.0 fine-tuning uses self-supervised representations pre-trained on 960 hours of unlabelled speech, with the quantisation step removed so that context representations derive solely from the input signal, feeding a BLSTM classifier. Both are appropriate to the data-scarcity problem the study sets out to address, and the comparison is fairly constructed - identical folds for both models and both tasks, a separate development partition, and evaluation on the same test sets. The finding that the smaller, domain-matched pre-training corpus outperforms the much larger unrelated one is the substantive modelling result. Minority-class upsampling was applied in training only, with development and test distributions left intact - a deliberate choice, correctly explained, and one that this review has found in strikingly few papers, since rebalancing the evaluation set is a common and usually silent source of inflation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-126" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Mixed, with the strongest and weakest language in this corpus sitting a few pages apart. The clinical framing in the introduction is dated and inaccurate: autism is 'a neurological disease correlated by imbalances in the brain', it is 'incurable' though it 'can be managed', and the population is characterised through what it lacks - 'a lack in verbal skills or continuing to be non-verbal'. 'Individuals with ASD' and 'children with ASD' are the standard usages, with 'atypically developing' as the analytic label. Against this, three passages show real care. The fairness section states that bias testing 'is crucial in our experiment, specially that we are adhering to a vulnerable group', and grounds the analysis in the observed male-to-female diagnostic ratio and in the 'female camouflage effect', which suggests females are better at masking autism - one of very few places in this review where under-identification of autistic women is treated as a methodological problem rather than a footnote. The limitations argue that 'non-verbal vocalisations are a form of speech and hold communicative value', which treats the sounds autistic children make as communication rather than as noise to be filtered - a substantive interpretive commitment. And the paper's reflection on research ethics committees as safeguards for child data, and on the difficulty of obtaining such data, shows awareness of the population's vulnerability even though the study's own ethics declaration is thin. No autistic person or community organisation is reported as involved." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-126" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two corpora combined, and the participant accounting is the weakest part of the description. De-Enigma contributes 55 autistic children aged 4 to 10, described as 25 from Serbia and 25 from the United Kingdom - a split that does not sum to 55 - with no control group at all, yielding 5,368 vocalisation samples over 115 minutes. The Child Pathological Speech Database contributes monolingual French-speaking children aged 6 to 18, recruited in Paris, carrying diagnoses of autism, dysphasia or PDD-NOS alongside a typically developing group, yielding 1,722 samples over 62 minutes. The CPSD participant counts cannot be reconciled: the control group is said to consist of 64 typically developing children (52 male, 12 female), while the corpus as a whole is said to have 13 female participants contributing 334 samples and 54 males contributing 1,388 - a total of 67, which would leave three children for the autism, dysphasia and PDD-NOS groups combined. The number of children in each atypical class is never stated anywhere in the paper. This is not a bookkeeping quibble. Speaker-disjoint splitting is the study's stated safeguard against leakage, and its protective value depends entirely on how many speakers each class contains: if the 26 autism, 26 dysphasia and 26 PDD-NOS samples in the diagnosis test partition come from one or two children each, then class membership and speaker identity are nearly the same variable, and disjointness across the split cannot separate them. The sex proportions are also stated inconsistently, as 78% and 81% male in the dataset section and as 95% and 90% male in the limitations. Age distributions per group are not reported for either corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-126" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The limitations section is long, specific and unusually self-critical, covering six issues. The study's original aim - autism recognition in children aged 1 to 3 - was abandoned because data on that population could not be obtained, and the authors say so plainly. The two corpora were collected in different settings, creating domain-shift risk and possible bias toward the more heavily represented one. Class imbalance limited the models, and the authors note that the resampling remedies themselves affect classification performance. Gender imbalance is severe and the fairness testing demonstrates its consequence. The test sets are small and 'not fully representative of the real-world'. And the headline is explicitly qualified: a development UAR of 78.0% against a test UAR of 94.8% is flagged as reflecting corpus-specific characteristics and the additional partitioning, with the conclusion that 'due to the limitations in the dataset, the results reported herein must be carefully interpreted' - reinforced by a citation to a discussion of 'potential over-expectation in speech analysis tasks for real-world application'. What is not addressed is the recording-setting confound within CPSD, where atypical children were recorded in clinics and typical children in schools, which the paper's own dataset description states and which makes acoustic environment a perfect proxy for the binary label. Nor is the participant-count inconsistency resolved, or the number of children per diagnostic class stated - facts that determine how much protection speaker-disjoint splitting actually provides. No age data are available in a corpus spanning 6 to 18 years. No diagnostic procedure is described for either corpus, and the CPSD categories are pre-DSM-5. No language measure exists although one class is a language disorder. Nothing is said about what the children were doing when recorded. And the specific direction of the development-to-test discrepancy - test scoring sixteen points above development - is noted but not explained, though a test score well above a development score usually indicates that the partitions differ in composition rather than that the model improved." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-126" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The aim is to test whether deep transfer learning can compensate for the scarcity of labelled child speech data in autism recognition, by comparing two fine-tuning strategies - discriminative fine-tuning pre-trained on a related child corpus, and Wav2Vec 2.0 fine-tuning pre-trained on 960 hours of unrelated adult speech - across a binary typicality task and a four-class diagnosis task, with fairness testing by gender. The findings are best read against the study's own comparison tables, which the authors supply. On binary typicality, D-FT reached 94.8% unweighted average recall and W2V2-FT 91.5%, against a stated baseline of 94.1% and prior published results of 90.7% and 93.6% on the same corpus - an improvement of 0.7 points over baseline. On four-class diagnosis, D-FT reached 60.9% and W2V2-FT 54.3%, against a baseline of 62.4% and prior results of 67.1% and 69.4% - so both proposed models fall below the baseline and below prior work on the task that actually requires separating autism from other conditions. And the fairness analysis shows that the binary headline does not survive subgroup evaluation: D-FT sensitivity falls from 94.8% on the mixed-gender test set to 43.6% on the female set and 61.5% on the male, with W2V2-FT falling from 92.3% to 24.4% and 53.8%. Publishing a benchmark table showing one's own model below baseline on the harder task, and a fairness table showing a fifty-point sensitivity collapse, is a level of candour this review has encountered in very few papers." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-126" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None attempted. No saliency, attribution, acoustic analysis or characterisation of the learned representations is presented, and none is claimed - the models are evaluated purely as classifiers. The only window onto behaviour is the confusion matrices, which the authors do interpret, observing that both models predict typically developing more successfully than atypically developing and attributing this to the class distribution. For a study whose motivating hypothesis is specific and acoustically measurable - reduced variation in pitch, intonation and expressive elements - the absence of any analysis connecting model decisions to prosody is the clearest missed opportunity in the paper, because it is the analysis that would distinguish a model responding to speech from one responding to the acoustics of a clinic room. The fairness analysis partially compensates by revealing something about the models that no saliency map would have: that their apparent competence does not extend to girls." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-126" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Reported: both corpora with their sources and project pages, sample and duration totals, complete partition tables giving class counts for training, development, test, female and male sets under both tasks, the 70/15/15 split proportions, the speaker-disjoint splitting rule with its stated rationale, the two architectures and their pre-training corpora, the decision to upsample the minority class in training while leaving development and test distributions intact, unweighted average recall as the primary metric, the equalised-odds procedure with its sensitivity and specificity formulae, confusion matrices for every model and partition, and comparison tables against a baseline and against published results on the same dataset. Not reported: code, hyperparameters, sample durations, recording equipment and conditions, the number of children per diagnostic class, age distributions by group, any diagnostic procedure for either corpus, and the number of runs behind each figure. Neither corpus is openly available. The partition tables are the strongest element - they let a reader compute the class balance of every evaluation set, which is what makes the fairness results interpretable and what reveals that the gender test sets differ in composition from the mixed-gender set." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-126" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Four directions are named: stratified sampling to distribute class imbalance evenly across folds; generative adversarial networks for minority-class synthesis, argued to be more representative of real data than SMOTE or ADASYN; extension to children younger than four and to pre-linguistic vocalisations, which the authors argue is supported by their use of non-speech vocalisations from De-Enigma; and continued fairness work given the gender disparities. Not proposed, and needed before the binary result can be interpreted: acoustic normalisation or channel compensation to address the clinic-versus-school recording split, reporting of the number of children per diagnostic class, age distributions by group, and evaluation on a corpus in which both classes were recorded under matched conditions." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-126" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Raw waveform segments per vocalisation, encoded by learned representations - either the discriminatively fine-tuned model's features or Wav2Vec 2.0 context representations. No hand-crafted acoustic descriptors are used in the study's own models, though the ComParE feature set is discussed as background. Sample durations are not stated but can be inferred at roughly 1.3 seconds for De-Enigma and 2.2 seconds for CPSD from the reported totals. Short segments are a real constraint on a prosodic claim: intonation contour, pitch range and expressive variation are properties of utterances and turns rather than of one- to two-second fragments, so the representation may not span the timescale on which the hypothesised difference lives." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-126" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: autism spectrum disorder, child speech, artificial intelligence, deep learning, transformer, transfer learning, fairness in AI. The inclusion of 'fairness in AI' is the notable element and is earned rather than decorative - the fairness analysis occupies a full section, is grounded in the diagnostic sex ratio and in the female camouflage effect, uses a defined criterion (equalised odds) with stated formulae, and produces the paper's most consequential result. This is the only keyword set in the review to name fairness, and one of very few papers anywhere in the corpus to evaluate subgroup performance at all." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-126" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Not described, for either corpus, and the omission is more damaging here than in most studies because the paper's substantive claim is prosodic. The motivating account is that autistic speech is monotonous, 'lacking the typical variations in pitch, intonation, and expressive elements' - but prosody is determined as much by what a speaker is doing as by who they are. Spontaneous conversation, responding to an adult's questions, reading aloud, repeating modelled utterances and playing all produce systematically different intonation contours. Nothing is stated about what the children in either corpus were doing when recorded, whether an adult or a robot was present, what was said to them, or whether the elicitation differed between the clinic-recorded atypical children and the school-recorded typical ones. De-Enigma was collected within a robot-assisted autism education project, so its vocalisations arise from a specific and unusual interactive context that is not characterised at all. Without a task description, a prosodic difference between groups cannot be attributed to speaker characteristics rather than to what each group was asked to do." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-126" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No severity, cognitive, language or adaptive measure is available in either corpus as used here. De-Enigma carries additional annotations for emotion, autistic behaviour and non-verbal vocalisation type, which the authors explicitly set aside as outside their focus - and among these, the autistic-behaviour annotation would have permitted exactly the severity-gradient analysis this review has repeatedly found missing. The four-class diagnosis task partially substitutes for a severity measure by requiring differentiation among clinical categories rather than only from typical development, which is more informative than any single-group severity correlation would have been." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-126" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None reported, which is itself a finding. For a speech study, no microphone, sampling rate, channel configuration, recording environment specification or preprocessing chain is given for either corpus. Sample durations are not stated; the reported totals imply mean sample lengths of roughly 1.3 seconds in De-Enigma and 2.2 seconds in CPSD, which are short for prosodic analysis. Computation used two pre-trained model families - a discriminative fine-tuning architecture and Wav2Vec 2.0 with quantisation removed feeding a BLSTM classifier - but no hardware, framework or hyperparameter detail is provided. The absence of acoustic condition reporting is directly connected to the study's principal vulnerability: with the two classes recorded in different physical settings, the properties of the recording chain are exactly what a reader would need to judge whether the signal is speech or room." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-126" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional secondary analysis with no longitudinal element and no repeat measurement. The ethics reporting is the thinnest of any included study that reports any at all: approval is attributed to 'The Child Pathological Speech Database and De-Engima Project administrations' - the dataset custodians rather than a named research ethics committee - and written informed consent was 'not required from the participants or the participants' legal guardians/next of kin in accordance with the national legislation and institutional requirements'. The contrast with the paper's own introduction is worth noting, since that section reflects specifically on research ethics committees and institutional review boards as safeguards for child data and presents transfer learning as a response to the difficulty of obtaining such data - the topic is raised seriously in the argument and handled formulaically in the declaration. The study received no funding and declares no competing interests, and author contributions are itemised." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-126" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No primary collection, and the setting structure of the evaluation corpus is the study's central unexamined problem. The CPSD description states that 'recordings of all subjects were captured in their typical environment, i.e clinic for the LIC subjects and elementary, secondary, and high school for the TD subjects'. The atypical classes were therefore recorded in clinical rooms and the typical class in schools, so reverberation, background noise, microphone type and placement, examiner presence and recording protocol all covary perfectly with the binary label - and the entire binary result is trained and tested on this corpus. No acoustic normalisation, channel compensation or noise handling is reported that would mitigate it. The paper does discuss setting as a limitation, but the setting difference it examines is the one between its two corpora (De-Enigma at home, CPSD in laboratory conditions), which affects pre-training rather than the labelled contrast; the within-CPSD clinic-versus-school structure, which is the confound that directly threatens the headline, is never mentioned. This is a clean instance of a pattern the review has documented across modalities - a class label that is inseparable from the circumstances under which the two classes were recorded - arising here in audio rather than in images." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-126" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Well chosen and honestly deployed. Unweighted average recall is the primary metric, which is the correct choice for imbalanced classes and sets chance at 50% and 25% for the two tasks. Accuracy, precision, recall and F1 are reported for every model and every partition - mixed-gender, female and male - for both tasks, alongside confusion matrices and sensitivity and specificity by gender for the equalised-odds analysis. Most valuably, two comparison tables place the results in context: one against a baseline and two published studies on the same corpus, the other against published Wav2Vec 2.0 implementations. Those tables show that the study's typicality result exceeds the baseline by 0.7 points, and that its diagnosis result of 60.9% falls below the baseline of 62.4% and below prior results of 67.1% and 69.4%. Reporting a table in which one's own method loses on the harder task is rare in this corpus and should be recorded as such. What is missing is any uncertainty estimate - no confidence intervals, no variance across runs, no significance testing - which matters when the typicality margin over baseline is 0.7 points and when the diagnosis test partition contains 26 samples per clinical class." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-126" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Published 2025 by speech and behaviour-analysis groups at Imperial College London, the University of Augsburg and University Grenoble Alpes - the GLAM and iBUG laboratories, whose senior authors are central figures in computational paralinguistics and automatic behaviour understanding - with no funding and no declared conflicts. The framing is data-scarcity-driven: obtaining child speech data is hard because ethics committees rightly restrict it, so transfer learning is offered as a way to build usable models from small corpora. For this review the study earns its place on four counts, none of which is its headline. It is one of only two included studies whose comparison groups include another clinical population, and the only one to attempt a four-way separation of autism from dysphasia and PDD-NOS - the discrimination that clinical practice actually requires. It names the leakage problem precisely and splits by speaker to address it, in language sharper than most papers in this corpus manage. It performs equalised-odds fairness testing on held-out gender partitions and publishes a result that undermines its own headline: D-FT sensitivity falls from 94.8% pooled to 43.6% for girls and 61.5% for boys, and W2V2-FT from 92.3% to 24.4% and 53.8% - which should be read as a general warning that pooled accuracies in this literature may conceal large subgroup deficits that nobody has looked for. And it benchmarks itself against a stated baseline and prior work on the same corpus, showing its four-class result at 60.9% against a baseline of 62.4% and prior results of 67.1% and 69.4%, so its own model loses on the harder task. Two cautions travel with it. The binary result rests on a corpus in which the atypical children were recorded in clinics and the typical children in schools - a recording-setting confound perfectly aligned with the class label, stated in the paper's own dataset description and never revisited - so 94.8% may be substantially a measure of room acoustics, and no normalisation is reported that would rule this out. And the participant accounting is internally contradictory, with the number of children in each diagnostic class never stated, which leaves the protective value of the speaker-disjoint splitting unquantifiable. The general lesson matches the one drawn from the multiscale-attention facial study charted nearby: careful validation methodology applied above a confounded data layer produces a well-validated uninterpretable number, and the review should treat the two together." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-126" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Genuinely mixed, with the best-stated splitting principle in this corpus sitting alongside an unexamined confound that undercuts the headline. On the credit side, the splitting is speaker-disjoint and the reasoning is articulated precisely: samples from each child belong to one fold only, 'to prevent the model from over-fitting by learning speaker or recording idiosyncrasies, and therefore deceptively excelling'. That is the leakage problem named accurately, and it is the failure mode this review has documented across the scanpath-image, video-frame and EEG branches where it went unaddressed. A separate development partition is maintained, the same folds serve both tasks for comparability, unweighted average recall is used throughout, minority-class upsampling is confined to training with development and test distributions deliberately left intact, and the results are benchmarked against a baseline and against published work on the same corpus. On the debit side, three problems bound what the 94.8% means. First, speaker disjointness cannot address a confound operating at the class level, and the corpus description states that atypical children were recorded in clinics while typically developing children were recorded in schools - so a model that has learned clinic acoustics transfers perfectly across any speaker boundary, and no acoustic normalisation is reported. Second, the number of children in each atypical class is never stated and the two participant counts given are mutually incompatible; if the diagnostic classes rest on very few speakers, the 26 test samples per class may effectively be one or two voices, and speaker-disjoint splitting offers little protection when class and speaker are nearly the same variable. Third, the development UAR of 78.0% against a test UAR of 94.8% is an inversion that the authors flag but do not explain, and a test score sixteen points above development usually indicates that the partitions differ in composition rather than that the model generalises unusually well - a reading supported by the fairness analysis, in which the same model's sensitivity falls to 43.6% and 61.5% on the female and male partitions. The fairness testing is the most valuable validation act in the paper and deserves emphasis: by evaluating on gender-specific held-out sets rather than only on a pooled test set, it exposes a fifty-point sensitivity gap that pooled reporting concealed entirely. That is a generalisation check almost no other study in this review performs, and its result is a warning about every pooled accuracy in this corpus. Finally, the four-class result is the study's most trustworthy number precisely because it is its worst: 60.9% against a 25% chance level, below the published baseline, on the task that requires distinguishing autism from dysphasia and PDD-NOS rather than from typical development." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-126" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:study_design "Retrospective algorithm-development study on two existing child speech corpora, with binary and four-class classification tasks, transfer-learning comparison, equalised-odds fairne…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: ; comparison: 1,109 typically developing samples in CPSD. The paper states the control group 'consists of 64 Typically Developing children (TD), 52 of which are males and 12 females'." ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "ab4b22c5ee32ea4d5d62e1af6c705c3275d66b3fcd5f8a55d7143d14990ccc20" ;
