<https://doi.org/10.3390/su15107790> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Early screening, which can lead to early diagnosis and intervention for children with autism (ASD), can signiﬁcantly improve their life quality. Consequently, the observational process of the ASD diagnosis and the lack of experts make the technology-based ASD screening methods more demanding. On the other hand, early ASD screening based on behavioral patterns is one of the most reliable methods that could be accomplished by analyzing children’s playing patterns. Thus, in this paper, we present an extension of our initial sensorized toy car functionalities by adding shaft encoders to detect attention to details and interest in rotating objects in children with ASD. Using the two modalities to detect different ASD symptoms improved our screening accuracy by more than 10%. Having such a sensorized toy car, which can be used at homes or clinics for screening ASD without high expertise, can improve the life of children with ASD and their families by providing an initial screening for further evaluation. This is essential for sustainable development, especially considering the increased rate of children with ASD around the world." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Mehralizadeh B; Baradaran B; Nikkhoo S; Soleiman P; Moradi H (2023). A Sensorized Toy Car for Autism Screening Using Multi-Modal Features. *Sustainability, 15(10), Article 7790*. https://doi.org/10.3390/su15107790" ;
    dcterms:creator "Baradaran B",
        "Mehralizadeh B",
        "Moradi H",
        "Nikkhoo S",
        "Soleiman P" ;
    dcterms:title "A Sensorized Toy Car for Autism Screening Using Multi-Modal Features" ;
    bibo:doi "10.3390/su15107790" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_70a30e2b" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2023 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participant selection and spectrum representativeness" ;
                            slr:justification "Convenience sample of 28 autistic and 18 typically developing children at one Tehran autism centre, with no stated inclusion or exclusion criteria for either group and no recruitment route given for the controls. Age 3-6 only. Spectrum position is unmeasured, so representativeness cannot be assessed at all." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Handling of missing data and attrition" ;
                            slr:justification "Fifty children tested, 46 modelled, with the four-child gap corresponding to the other-disorder group but never stated as an exclusion. The five non-engaging autistic children are described but their handling is not specified. No signal-quality or packet-loss accounting under UDP transport, and no sampling rate given." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Confounder control" ;
                            slr:justification "Age is well matched (4.63 versus 4.61 years) and that is the only matched variable. Sex is not reported, IQ and developmental level are not measured, and the authors' own observation that TD children 'played with more energy and moved the car in a wider area' points at a general developmental-level difference that no measured covariate can rule out. The engagement asymmetry - five autistic children ignoring the toy entirely versus universal TD engagement - is stated and then modelled rather than controlled." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Comparison group adequacy" ;
                            slr:justification "Typically developing children only in the modelled analysis. Four children with cerebral palsy and fragile X syndrome were recruited, played with the car, were described qualitatively as less enthusiastic, and were then excluded from every classification result without comment - discarding the study's own opportunity for a differential contrast." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Index test definition and prespecification" ;
                            slr:justification "Feature subset, algorithm and kernel were all chosen by search on the analysis sample; six configurations are tabulated and the maximum is the abstract's claim. No prespecified operating point." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome measurement blinding" ;
                            slr:justification "All measurement is sensor-derived and automated; no human rating enters the feature pipeline, so differential observer bias in measurement is not a concern." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reference standard quality" ;
                            slr:justification "None described. No criteria, instrument, clinician, process or date for any diagnosis, and no autism screening of the comparison group. This is the weakest reference-standard reporting encountered so far in this corpus." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Measurement standardisation" ;
                            slr:justification "The apparatus is identical for all children and well specified down to part numbers, with electronics concealed to avoid distraction, which eliminates the acquisition-chain confounds seen elsewhere in this corpus. But the session is unstandardised: 3-5 minutes of free play, with a parent or therapist present or absent unrecorded, no instruction protocol, and no stated sampling rate." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data splitting and leakage control" ;
                            slr:justification "The split unit is the child, with one aggregate vector per participant, so no within-child leakage is possible - a real strength relative to most of this corpus. But the cross-validation description is terse and partly self-contradictory ('the training set with 80% of samples and the test set with 20% of the remaining samples'), and the relationship between the single 80/20 split and the 5 folds is not made precise." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance estimation and chance level" ;
                            slr:justification "No majority-class baseline (which alone gives 60.9%), no permutation test, no confidence intervals or fold-level spread, no AUC. Test folds contain about nine children, so one child moves fold accuracy by 11 points. Reported accuracies also fail to reconcile with the stated sensitivity, specificity and class ratio in at least three of six rows." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Preprocessing and feature selection nesting" ;
                            slr:justification "Two consecutive rounds of backward elimination reduced 54 features to 10, described in a dedicated section preceding the results with the surviving features named as a fixed outcome and no indication of refitting inside folds. At 46 samples and 54 candidate features this is the regime in which unnested selection produces the largest optimism." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Sample size adequacy for the modelling" ;
                            slr:justification "46 children for a 54-feature selection problem plus a three-algorithm, three-kernel, four-configuration model search. The authors identify the feature-to-sample ratio as a problem and respond with feature reduction, but the reduction is itself performed on the same small sample." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Ethics, consent and conflicts" ;
                            slr:justification "Informed parental consent under the Declaration of Helsinki, anonymised participant IDs, and psychologist supervision are all reported and appropriate. But formal ethics review was not obtained: the IRB statement asserts that non-intrusive screening 'does not require ethics approval as long as the privacy of the participants is considered', substituting an engineering-school proposal acceptance and the autism centre's internal guidelines. For research on 3-6 year old children with developmental disabilities that is not adequate. Funding (none) and conflicts (none) are declared." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reporting of results and uncertainty" ;
                            slr:justification "Four metrics across six configurations is a good ablation table, and the SVM/random forest error-profile discussion is a genuine strength. But there is no uncertainty quantification of any kind, no AUC, and the accuracy column does not reconcile with sensitivity, specificity and the 28:18 ratio for several rows. The text also mis-references Table 5 as Table 4." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data, code and protocol availability" ;
                            slr:justification "Data available on request from the corresponding author; hardware and firmware stack described well enough to rebuild; an ROS package developed for integration. No code repository, no preregistration, no analysis plan." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Completeness of methods reporting" ;
                            slr:justification "Hardware reporting is excellent - board, sensor part numbers, encoder type and placement, transport protocol, firmware ecosystem, an ROS interface package - and both feature tables are complete with the final selected subset named. Analysis reporting is absent: no software, no hyperparameters for any of three algorithms, no augmentation scheme, no seeds, no sampling rate, and no diagnostic criteria." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Acknowledgement of limitations" ;
                            slr:justification "No limitations section. The only concessions are that the reimplemented baseline underperformed the original because data and setup differed, and that the system should currently serve as a referral warning rather than a diagnosis. Nothing is said about the missing reference standard, the unnested feature selection, the model search, the engagement confound or the dropped clinical group." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, sensitivity, specificity and precision, reported for all six classifier/feature configurations in a single table, plus a confusion matrix for the best model. Reporting four metrics across a full ablation grid rather than one number for one model is better practice than much of this corpus. What is missing: AUC or ROC, F1, any confidence interval or standard deviation across the five folds, calibration, and any translation of the operating point to a screening context - no PPV or NPV at a plausible population prevalence, despite the paper's entire argument being about population-level screening access. At the study's own 60.9% prevalence a precision of 87.67% is unremarkable; at a 1-2% community prevalence, sensitivity 81.7% with specificity 81.0% would give a positive predictive value in the low single digits, which is the number that matters for a device proposed for home and daycare use. There is also an internal consistency problem: for several rows the reported accuracy cannot be recovered from the reported sensitivity and specificity given the 28:18 class ratio - row 6 implies about 81.4% rather than 85.56%, row 4 about 72.3% rather than 78.61%, and row 1 about 77.3% rather than 71.11% - so the columns appear not to be computed on a common basis." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-096" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two behavioural constructs, both explicitly named as autism symptoms and both operationalised through the child's manipulation of a single object. First, interest in rotating objects and attention to parts rather than wholes, captured by the wheels-only play state: the encoder registers wheel rotation while the accelerometer shows the car body stationary, meaning the child is spinning the wheels and watching them rather than driving the car. The design was prompted by direct observation with version 1 that autistic children spent a great deal of time rotating the wheels and watching them while TD children did so much less. Second, movement and repetitive-play patterns, captured by the triaxial acceleration signature of the car being driven - its spectral content, energy, inter-axis correlation, jolt count and tilt dynamics. The four-state decomposition (not playing, wheels only, on the floor, in the air) is the behavioural core of the paper and yields an interpretable budget of how a child spends a play session. A third behaviour enters the model implicitly and without acknowledgement: engagement with the toy itself, via the not-playing ratio, which is dominated by the five autistic children who ignored the car completely against a TD group in which every child was 'very interested'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-096" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Not reported at all - this is the study's most serious gap. The paper never states how any child's autism diagnosis was established: no DSM or ICD criteria, no ADOS, ADI-R, CARS, GARS or M-CHAT, no named clinician or team, no date of diagnosis, no inclusion or exclusion criteria for either group. The only clue is the recruitment site, an autism centre, so the ASD label is presumably the child's existing service-eligibility status. TD status is equally unspecified: the typically developing children are not described as having been screened for autism or developmental delay by any instrument, and their recruitment route is not given. The four children in the other-disorder group are labelled with CP and fragile X, again with no source. The result is that the study's target variable - the thing the 85.56% accuracy is accurate about - is undefined. This places it at the weakest end of reference-standard reporting in this corpus, below even the studies that use a single unblinded clinician's DSM judgement, because here there is no statement of any process at all." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-096" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Better than most on the split unit, weaker on everything downstream. The unit of splitting is the child - one aggregate feature vector per participant - so the segment-level leakage that inflates results throughout this corpus is impossible here by construction, and the paper deserves credit for that even though it is a consequence of the aggregate representation rather than a deliberate safeguard. The reported scheme is an 80/20 division with 5-fold cross-validation rotating which 20% is held out, giving fold test sets of about 9 children and averaged metrics. The problems lie elsewhere. First, feature selection: two consecutive rounds of backward elimination were run over the feature set, described in a section that precedes the results and with the surviving 10 features named as a fixed outcome, with no indication that the procedure was repeated within each training fold. Selecting 10 of 54 features on all 46 children and then cross-validating the chosen subset is a well-characterised source of optimism, and it is most severe exactly in this regime of many features and few samples - the regime the authors themselves identify as the reason feature reduction was needed. Second, model selection: three algorithms, three SVM kernels and four feature configurations were evaluated on the same 46 children, and the maximum of that grid is the abstract's claim. Third, no chance baseline: the majority class alone gives 60.9%, and no permutation test or confidence interval is reported, so with roughly 9 children per test fold a single child's label moves fold accuracy by 11 percentage points and the difference between the 78.61% and 85.56% configurations is a handful of children. Fourth, prevalence: 61% autism in the modelled sample against a population rate near 1-2%, with no prevalence-adjusted metric. The corrective design is straightforward and worth stating for the synthesis - move both backward-elimination rounds and the kernel/algorithm choice inside the training folds, report the mean and spread across folds rather than the best cell of the grid, add a majority-class and a permutation baseline, and retain the CP and fragile X children as a differential-diagnosis check." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-096" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Mixed, with an unusual profile: this is one of the few studies in the corpus where the physical apparatus is more reproducible than the analysis. A reader could rebuild the car from the part numbers given and would have the ROS interface and Arduino firmware ecosystem described. Both feature tables are complete, listing all 44 baseline acceleration features with their symbols and all 10 new features with their definitions, and the final selected subset is named feature by feature. The four play states are defined operationally. Room size and session duration are given. Data are available on request. Against this: no code repository, no sampling rate, no model hyperparameters for any of the three algorithms, no random seed or fold assignment, no statement of whether feature selection was refit inside each fold, and no diagnostic criteria - so the labels themselves could not be reproduced. There is also an internal reference error, with the text pointing to 'Table 4' for the results that appear in Table 5." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-096" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Sustainability 15(10), article 7790, MDPI, in a section handled by an academic editor from the image-processing field. This is an open-access multidisciplinary sustainability journal rather than a clinical, autism-specialist or biomedical-engineering venue, and the fit is achieved through an explicit framing device: the paper argues that low-cost widespread screening advances the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals by extending access to under-served and rural populations, and the argument recurs in the abstract, discussion and conclusion. That framing explains several features of the report - the emphasis on device cost and expert-independence, the comparison table of competing modalities by accessibility and cost, and the near-absence of clinical detail such as diagnostic criteria. Received 28 December 2022, revised 13 March 2023, accepted 25 April 2023, published 10 May 2023, CC BY." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-096" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Classical machine learning throughout, with three algorithms compared: a linear-kernel SVM as the primary model, a random forest, and a multilayer perceptron trained with unspecified data augmentation. The SVM was retained because it outperformed the others on overall accuracy, and the linear kernel was chosen after testing three kernel types. No deep learning, no sequence model, no probabilistic or Bayesian approach; the whole session is collapsed to one fixed-length feature vector per child before any model sees it. The choice is defensible at n = 46 and the authors implicitly acknowledge the sample-size constraint by noting that 'the size of the training set is small compared to the size of the feature vector' as the motivation for aggressive feature reduction. The most interesting modelling observation in the paper is the discussion of the SVM/random forest trade-off: the random forest is 4.7 points worse on accuracy but better on both sensitivity and specificity, and the authors state plainly that a user may prefer it because it would miss fewer autistic children and mislabel fewer typically developing ones - an error-profile argument that most studies in this corpus never make." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-096" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Well documented at the hardware level, undocumented at the software level. Hardware: an ESP8266 NodeMCU IoT board streaming sensor data over Wi-Fi by UDP; an ADXL345 MEMS triaxial accelerometer; two Hall-effect magnetic shaft encoders mounted on the front and rear wheels; battery power; all electronics concealed inside the car. Firmware is built on the Arduino ecosystem explicitly to simplify future development, and an ROS package was written so the car can be integrated into other screening systems - a genuine and unusual contribution to reusability. Software for analysis: nothing is named - no Python, MATLAB, scikit-learn or equivalent, no SVM implementation or regularisation parameter, no MLP architecture or augmentation method, no random forest settings. Two acquisition parameters that a replicator would need are also missing: the sampling rate of the accelerometer and encoders, and any accounting of packet loss, which is a real concern because UDP was chosen precisely for throughput over reliability and offers no delivery guarantee." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-096" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Good, and grounded in physics rather than post-hoc attribution. Every feature is a named, humanly meaningful quantity, and the four play states are defined by an explicit and checkable sensor logic: encoder moving with accelerometer static means wheel spinning; accelerometer moving with encoder static means the car is airborne; both moving means floor play; neither means no play. The selected 10 features are listed individually so a reader can see exactly what the model uses, and the three encoder features that survived - wheels-only ratio, not-playing ratio, total wheel turn rate - map directly onto the behavioural hypothesis that motivated the hardware change. The mechanism itself came from observation of children playing with version 1, not from a literature-driven guess, which is a legitimate and transparent route to a feature. What is absent is any quantitative feature-level analysis: no group means or effect sizes for the wheels-only ratio or any other feature, no statistical test that autistic children actually spin the wheels more, no feature importances or coefficients, and no error analysis. So the paper can say which features the classifier used but cannot say by how much the groups differ on them, which is why the not-playing-ratio confound cannot be assessed from the reported results." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-096" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional, single session, no follow-up, no test-retest reliability for any feature - which matters for a device intended for repeated home use. Ethics reporting is unusual and problematic. Consent is handled well: parents were informed of the study, its goals, the procedure and their rights, informed consent was obtained in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki, a child psychologist supervised, and data were stored under anonymous participant IDs without names or personal information. But formal ethics review was not obtained and the Institutional Review Board Statement argues that it was not needed: the proposal was accepted by the University of Tehran's School of ECE 'considering the privacy of participants and non-intrusiveness of the screening nature of the work', and the authors assert that 'this is a non-intrusive screening that does not require ethics approval as long as the privacy of the participants is considered', supplemented by the autism centre's own guidelines. A departmental engineering-school sign-off plus site guidelines is not equivalent to ethics-committee review of research on 3-6 year old children with developmental disabilities, and the non-intrusiveness of the measurement does not remove the need for review of recruitment, consent and data handling in a vulnerable population." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-096" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The paper has no limitations section, and the omission is felt. What it does concede is narrow but honest: that the reimplemented baseline underperforms the original because the data and collection setup differed and the optimisation may not have matched; and, in the future-work paragraph, that at this stage the system should serve as a warning to prompt expert referral rather than as a diagnostic tool. The unacknowledged limitations are substantial. (i) No reference standard is described for any group, so the classification target is undefined. (ii) Six model/feature configurations are reported in Table 5 and the maximum is presented as the result, with no penalty for selection; the honest reading of an SVM chosen among three kernels, three algorithms and four feature sets on 46 children is that 85.56% is the top of a search distribution whose other members run 71-81%. (iii) Feature selection by two rounds of backward elimination over all 46 children's data, described before and separately from the evaluation, appears unnested, which on a 54-feature vector and 46 samples is exactly the regime where selection bias is largest. (iv) The engagement confound is stated in the text and then never revisited: five autistic children ignored the car entirely, all TD children were 'very interested', and the not-playing ratio is one of the three encoder features selected into the final model - so part of the classifier's discriminative power is a measure of whether the child engaged with the toy at all, which is a real behavioural difference but not the attention-to-rotating-objects mechanism the paper claims to have captured. (v) No developmental or motor measure exists to rule out general developmental level as the driver, despite the authors' own observation that TD children 'played with more energy and moved the car in a wider area'. (vi) The four CP/fragile X children were recruited and dropped. (vii) The reported metrics do not fully reconcile: at 28 ASD and 18 TD, row 6's sensitivity of 81.67% and specificity of 81.00% imply an accuracy near 81.4%, not 85.56%, and rows 1 and 4 show similar discrepancies, so either the class balance within folds differs from the overall sample or the columns are not all computed on the same basis." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-096" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Genuine two-stream fusion within a single physical device, and one of the cleaner ablation designs in the corpus. Streams: triaxial acceleration of the car body (movement pattern) and dual wheel shaft encoders (rotation). Fusion is early - after two rounds of backward elimination, first within each modality using inter-feature correlation and then across modalities, the selected features from both streams are concatenated into one vector for training. The ablation is fully reported and interpretable: 71.11% for acceleration alone, 78.61% for acceleration plus encoders, 75.83% for acceleration plus the new roll/pitch features, and 85.56% for all three sets, showing that each addition contributes and that the two contributions are partly complementary rather than redundant. The complementarity has a physical basis rather than a statistical one: the two sensors disambiguate states that either alone would confuse, since a stationary car with spinning wheels and a car carried through the air with static wheels are distinguishable only by looking at both signals together, and the new roll/pitch features are themselves derived by short-time Fourier transform of the accelerometer specifically to characterise what the body is doing during wheel play. The caution is that the ablation shares the sample and the feature-selection procedure with the final model, so the gains are measured under the same optimistic conditions as the headline accuracy; and that the largest single-feature contributor from the encoder side may be the not-playing ratio, which indexes engagement rather than rotation interest." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-096" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Published 2023 from the University of Tehran (Mechanical Engineering and ECE) with co-authors then at the University of Minnesota and UC Riverside, with no external funding. The framing is health-equity engineering rather than diagnostics: the opening premise is that many cases remain undiagnosed 'due to the limited access to experts, especially in low and middle-income societies', and the proposed answer is a device cheap enough to deploy in homes and daycares. Version 2 of an existing platform, so the framing is explicitly incremental - the contributions are stated as introducing a new modality and suggesting new features, and the authors state that they do not wish to compare their accuracy against other modalities in the literature but only against their own version 1. That restraint is unusual and appropriate. The paper is also of its moment in treating multimodality as an answer to within-spectrum heterogeneity - different autistic children show different symptoms, so a comprehensive screen needs several sensing channels - an argument that recurs across the 2022-2023 papers in this corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-096" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Single site: an autism centre in Tehran, Iran. Children played in a 3 x 4 m room at the centre for 3-5 minutes, alone or accompanied by a parent or therapist, with a child psychologist from the centre supervising. This creates a gap between the study as conducted and the study as framed: the paper's central argument is for a home-based IoT device usable 'at homes, daycares, or clinics' in natural settings without expert involvement, and it repeatedly criticises fMRI, EEG and robot-based approaches for requiring specialised centres - yet all the reported data were collected at a specialised centre under psychologist supervision. The recruitment geometry is also the standard case-control one: an already-identified autistic group from a service setting against a typically developing comparison group, at 61% autism prevalence, which is roughly 60 times the population base rate the device is proposed to screen at." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-096" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No cognitive, developmental, adaptive, language or motor assessment was administered to any participant, and no autism severity measure was obtained. This is a substantive omission for two reasons specific to this design. First, the task is a fine and gross motor task performed by 3-6 year olds, and the extracted features - jolt counts, acceleration energy, tilt rates, spatial extent of movement - are all motor-competence-sensitive; without a developmental or motor measure it is impossible to separate an autism-specific play signature from a general developmental-level difference, particularly since the authors themselves observe that 'the TD children played with more energy and they moved the car in a wider area compared to the other two groups'. Second, the discussion rests its case for multimodality on within-spectrum heterogeneity - 'children with ASD may be at different parts of the ASD spectrum' - which is precisely the variable the study does not measure, so the claim cannot be tested against the data and the model cannot be checked for whether it succeeds only on more severely affected children." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-096" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three groups recruited, two modelled. Recruited: 28 children with ASD, 18 typically developing children, and 4 children with other disorders (cerebral palsy and fragile X syndrome), total 50, all aged 3-6 years and tested at an autism centre in Tehran. Modelled: 46 subjects, which is exactly 28 + 18, so the four-child other-disorder group is excluded from every classification result without the exclusion being stated. A further subgroup is described but not removed: five of the 28 autistic children 'did not seem interested in playing with the sensorized toy car and neglected it completely', so roughly 18% of the autism group contributed a session of near-zero play. Whether their data were retained is not stated explicitly, but the 46-subject count implies they were, and one of the three selected encoder features is the not-playing ratio." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-096" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Author keywords: autism spectrum disorder (ASD); sensorized toy; machine learning; IoT. The inclusion of 'IoT' rather than a clinical term signals where the paper locates itself - in connected-device engineering, not diagnostics - and 'sensorized toy' is the contribution rather than a topic. Neither 'screening', 'diagnosis' nor any behavioural term appears, despite screening being the stated purpose. Terms carried through the body: playing patterns, attention to details, interest in rotating objects, repetitive behaviors, shaft encoders, multi-modal features, early screening, sustainable development." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-096" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'Children with autism (ASD)', 'children with ASD' and the abbreviation ASD throughout; 'Typically Developed (TD) children' - an idiosyncratic expansion of the usual 'typically developing'. Person-first construction is used consistently ('children with ASD', 'people with ASD'). The clinical framing is medical-model and unhedged: autism is 'a neurodevelopmental disorder that causes problems in social communication and interaction', the targeted behaviours are named as 'symptoms' including 'the obsessive attention to detail' and 'repetitive behaviors', and the conclusion refers to the system as screening 'cognitive deficits'. There is no engagement with neurodiversity perspectives and no autistic-community involvement statement, although the framing of purpose - equitable access to screening in low- and middle-income and rural settings, tied to the UN Sustainable Development Goals - is unusual in this corpus and reflects a genuine health-equity motivation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-096" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A single 54-dimensional hand-crafted feature vector per child, reduced to 10 by feature selection. The 44 inherited acceleration features are per-axis means, variances, the five dominant frequencies and their amplitudes, signal energies, pairwise inter-axis correlations, a jolt count and the playing-time duration - i.e. a mix of time-domain statistics and coarse spectral descriptors computed over the whole session. The 10 new features are of two kinds: seven state-occupancy ratios derived from the joint encoder/accelerometer signature (fractions of total and of interactive playtime spent not playing, on wheels only, on the ground and in the air) plus the total wheel turn rate, and two tilt-count features (about X and about Y) obtained by short-time Fourier transform of the acceleration signal to recover the car's roll and pitch during wheel play. The representation is therefore entirely aggregate: no temporal ordering, no sequence, no windowing, and no per-episode analysis, so a child who spins the wheels once for ninety seconds and one who spins them thirty times for three seconds each are indistinguishable. The final selected set was seven acceleration features (Y-direction jolt, tilt about Y, X-direction energy, X-Y acceleration correlation, the fourth-highest X frequency, the fifth-highest Z frequency, and the relative amplitude of the highest Y frequency) and three encoder features (wheels-only play ratio, not-playing ratio, total wheel turn rate)." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-096" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three concrete directions, the first two of which address real gaps. Test on a wider population and on a variety of cognitive deficits to determine whether the device can differentiate autism from other conditions rather than only from typical development - which is precisely what the four excluded CP and fragile X children could have begun to address. Investigate how the toy car's size, shape and colour affect children's engagement with it and therefore the extracted features - a design-sensitivity question rarely raised in this literature and directly relevant to whether a play-based screen generalises across devices. And position the device as one component of a larger multimodal screening system rather than standalone, on the argument that different autistic children present different symptoms so a single modality will always miss some." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-096" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: to extend the group's existing accelerometer-only sensorized toy car by adding wheel shaft encoders, testing whether autistic children's documented interest in rotating objects can be captured as a second sensing modality and whether fusing it with movement features improves screening accuracy. The comparison is deliberately internal - the authors state they do not wish to compare their accuracy against other modalities in the literature, only against their own version 1. Findings: the reimplemented accelerometer-only baseline achieved 71.11% accuracy on the new data (lower than the 85% reported for version 1, attributed to different data, different collection setup and a from-scratch reimplementation); adding encoder features gave 78.61%; adding new roll/pitch acceleration features instead gave 75.83%; adding both gave 85.56% with sensitivity 81.67%, specificity 81.00% and precision 87.67% - the claimed improvement of more than 10 percentage points over baseline. Of the ten features surviving two rounds of backward elimination, three came from the encoders: the ratio of wheels-only play to total playtime, the not-playing ratio, and the total wheel turn rate. A secondary and arguably more informative finding is that random forest on the full feature set gave lower accuracy (80.83%) but the best sensitivity and specificity of any configuration (85.48%, 83.67%), which the authors flag as a reason a deployer might prefer it - a rare instance in this corpus of explicitly preferring a model on error-profile grounds rather than on headline accuracy." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-096" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Extremely thin. Table 2 reports only group size, mean age and median age: ASD n = 28, mean 4.63, median 4.0; TD n = 18, mean 4.61, median 4.0; others n = 4, mean and median 5.5. Age is therefore well matched between the two modelled groups, which matters for a motor-play task in 3-6 year olds. Nothing else is reported: no sex distribution for any group, no IQ or developmental quotient, no language level, no socioeconomic status, no race or ethnicity, no comorbidities, no medication. The absence of sex counts is a deliberate, argued choice rather than an oversight - the authors state they did not balance sex because play complexity and toy engagement with car-like toys have been shown to be similar across sexes in autistic children - but declining to balance a variable is not a reason to decline to report it, and without the counts a reader cannot check whether the two groups happened to differ. Given that all children were recruited at a single autism centre in Tehran, the sample is also culturally and geographically homogeneous, which is worth noting for a device whose selling point is deployment in homes worldwide." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-096" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three to five minutes of unstructured free play with a toy car in a small room. This is one of the least constrained paradigms in the corpus and that is largely a virtue - there is no script, no examiner prompt, no screen, no wearable, and the electronics are hidden inside the car body specifically so the child is not distracted by them, so what is measured is spontaneous object play. The cost of that freedom is that nothing is standardised: session length varies by up to 67% between children, the presence of a parent or therapist in the room varies and is not recorded, whether anyone interacted with or encouraged the child is not stated, and the floor surface, lighting and available space beyond the room dimensions are not described. Because several features are ratios normalised by total or interactive playtime, some of this variability is absorbed, but the absolute features (jolt count, wheel turn rate, energy) are not obviously duration-invariant. The four-state decomposition is the paradigm's real contribution: by defining not-playing, wheels-only, floor and air play through the joint accelerometer/encoder signature, the authors turn an unstructured session into an interpretable behavioural budget." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-096" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional two-group diagnostic-classification study; an incremental engineering extension of the same group's earlier sensorized toy car (Moradi et al., reference [13]), whos…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: ; comparison: 18" ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "9bfd5255be50fc828e6e49f42f3175848d196b09e174dbca039569284524d279" ;
