    slr:journal "Sustainability, 15(10), Article 7790" ;
    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "The interval between diagnosis and testing is unreported. Fifty children were tested and 46 modelled, with the four-child discrepancy unexplained in the analysis sections. All modelled children received the index test in a single 3-5 minute session of variable length, with a parent or therapist present or absent unrecorded. No sampling rate or packet-loss accounting is given despite UDP transport, so it is not possible to confirm that all children's signals were of comparable completeness." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "The index test was not prespecified. Two rounds of backward elimination selected 10 features from 54 on the full sample, three algorithms and three SVM kernels were compared, four feature configurations were evaluated, and the maximum of that grid is reported as the result. No threshold or operating point was fixed in advance. Interpretation is automated and label-free at inference, but the entire pipeline that produced the reported configuration had access to all 46 children's labels." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Case-control sampling at a single autism centre: 28 already-identified autistic children against 18 typically developing children, at 61% prevalence. No inclusion or exclusion criteria are stated for either group, no recruitment route is given for the TD children, and no consecutive or random enrolment is described. Four children with cerebral palsy and fragile X syndrome were recruited and then omitted from all analyses without explanation, removing the only available differential-diagnosis contrast. Five autistic children who ignored the toy entirely appear to have been retained, and the resulting engagement difference feeds a selected model feature." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "No reference standard is described. The paper names no diagnostic criteria, instrument, clinician, procedure or date for the autism diagnoses, and does not state how typically developing status was established or whether those children were screened for autism or developmental delay. The ASD label is presumably service-eligibility status at the recruiting centre. Without any specification of the target condition's ascertainment, the reported accuracy cannot be interpreted." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "high" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<urn:slr:article:0a8191e6e542> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Affect recognition is currently an active research area for machine learning researchers. Researchers are paying more and more attention to the assessment and the classification of emotions in people with mental disorders in order to propose monitoring healthcare systems and then to better assist patients. In this paper, a supervised learning method to classify Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) and to evaluate affect levels among autistic children is proposed. The use of methodologies for clinical patient monitoring and management is the focus of our research. YouTube video frames acquired in unconstrained environments and conditions of autistic children demonstrating typical autistic behaviors, as well as images of neurotypical people are used to evaluate the performance of the proposed approach. This paper also proposes an extended version of a dataset, including the additional affect labels corresponding to the affect levels of autistic children. Finally, experiments using different models were conducted to determine which architecture performed best. These experiments have shown very promising results for continuous affect recognition of children with ASD. Results show also that using a model that has only been trained on neurotypical subjects does not perform as great when the subjects are ASD children." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Dia M; Khodabandelou G; Sabri AQM; Othmani A (2024). Video-based continuous affect recognition of children with Autism Spectrum Disorder using deep learning. *Biomedical Signal Processing and Control, 89*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Dia M",
        "Khodabandelou G",
        "Othmani A",
        "Sabri AQM" ;
    dcterms:title "Video-based continuous affect recognition of children with Autism Spectrum Disorder using deep learning" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_5a331ffe" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2024 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Confounder control" ;
                            slr:justification "No age, sex or ancestry information for either class in a study that classifies faces, where apparent age and sex-linked morphology are known strong drivers and autism's four-to-one diagnostic sex ratio makes imbalance likely." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participant selection and spectrum representativeness" ;
                            slr:justification "Children whose families chose to film and publish their stereotypies - a self-selected group biased toward visible presentation, with no demographic or clinical characterisation of any kind." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Comparison group adequacy" ;
                            slr:justification "The non-autistic class is never described: no size, no source, no individual count. The data-distribution table lists SSBD as the sole database for all splits, contradicting the existence of a second class." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Handling of missing data and attrition" ;
                            slr:justification "Two of 75 videos excluded for quality with the reason stated, and frames retained only where the face is observable - reported clearly, though the retention criterion could select differently across classes and total image counts are never given." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reference standard quality" ;
                            slr:justification "Autism inferred from parental upload behaviour with an explicit statement of trust in the uploaders; no clinical assessment for any child. The non-autistic label has no stated basis." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Measurement standardisation" ;
                            slr:justification "Home videos on heterogeneous devices in uncontrolled conditions, with the authors themselves citing poor image and video quality. No normalisation applied, and the comparison images' acquisition conditions are entirely unknown." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome measurement blinding" ;
                            slr:justification "Classification is automated from image data. The affect annotations were produced by five raters trained on a protocol under supervision, with inter-annotator agreement reported." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Index test definition and prespecification" ;
                            slr:justification "Architectures and hyperparameters fully specified, with negative tuning results reported honestly and a separate validation partition maintained. But the input class composition is undefined and no operating threshold is prespecified." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data splitting and leakage control" ;
                            slr:justification "Subject-disjoint splitting implemented with the rationale stated and partitions reported by child and duration - materially better than the frame-level branch. But the guarantee covers only the SSBD half, and no splitting scheme can address a confound between image corpora." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Preprocessing and feature selection nesting" ;
                            slr:justification "Representations are learned; a separate validation partition is maintained; hyperparameters were taken from the original architecture paper with alternatives tried and reported as offering no gain." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Sample size adequacy for the modelling" ;
                            slr:justification "A transformer classifier trained on 44 children and tested on 14, with the image counts never stated. The authors list dataset scarcity and small size as their first limitation." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance estimation and chance level" ;
                            slr:justification "No specificity, confusion matrix or confidence interval for the classification, and the chance baseline is undeterminable because class balance is described as balanced in one section and 'very imbalanced' in another. The 5-fold figure is a best-of-folds validation accuracy rather than a mean held-out estimate." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "unclear" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Completeness of methods reporting" ;
                            slr:justification "Datasets, exclusions, annotation protocol, split proportions with per-child counts and durations, full architectures and hyperparameters, and metric definitions are all reported. The composition and source of the non-autistic class - the single fact needed to interpret the result - is not." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reporting of results and uncertainty" ;
                            slr:justification "Accuracy, F1 and recall across three evaluation configurations, plus MSE and CCC for regression with a well-argued case for reporting both and a demonstrated instance of the two metrics ranking models oppositely. No intervals, no specificity, no confusion matrices." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data, code and protocol availability" ;
                            slr:justification "'The authors do not have permission to share data.' The new arousal and valence annotations presented as a dataset contribution are therefore unavailable, and no code is released." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Acknowledgement of limitations" ;
                            slr:justification "Genuinely searching about the annotation layer - non-expert raters, reaction-time contamination of averaged labels, and no handling of disagreement, all volunteered - and the introduction cites a failed-replication warning about machine learning for autism diagnosis. Silent on the undocumented comparison class, the contradictory class balance, and the ethics of the source data." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Ethics, consent and conflicts" ;
                            slr:justification "No ethics statement, no institutional approval and no consent statement anywhere, for work that crops faces from identifiable children's home videos and has five raters annotate their emotional states frame by frame. Funding and competing interests are declared." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Absent for both classes, and more consequential than the usual absence because the study classifies faces. No age, sex, ancestry, socioeconomic, cognitive or language information is reported for the 73 autistic children or for the undocumented comparison images. Three specific risks follow. Facial classifiers key strongly on apparent age and on sex-linked morphology, and autism is diagnosed roughly four times more often in boys, so an uncontrolled sex imbalance between classes could produce the entire result. The SSBD children were filmed by families who chose to post the footage publicly, which is a self-selected population by geography, language, internet access and disposition. And the comparison images, wherever they came from, will carry their own demographic profile, which cannot be matched because neither profile is known. The paper does note that the borrowed CK+ corpus contains people 'of various genders as well as ethnic backgrounds', so the relevance of demographic composition is recognised for other people's datasets and not applied to its own." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-129" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Seventy-three children in the autism class, one per video, from 73 of the 75 videos in the Self-Stimulatory Behaviours Dataset - home recordings posted to YouTube by parents and caregivers showing children spinning, head banging or arm flapping, two videos having been dropped for quality. The split is reported by child and by duration: 44 children and 3,214 seconds in training, 15 children and 1,582 seconds in validation, 14 children and 1,496 seconds in test. That per-child reporting is better than the frame-count reporting typical of this branch. What is entirely absent is the other half of the sample. The methods state only that 'in order to have a complete and balanced database to train this model, we extracted the frames featuring the faces of ASD children and gathered facial images of other ASD children and facial images of neurotypical youngsters' - so a non-autistic class exists, but its size, its source, the number of children in it and their characteristics are nowhere given, and the data-distribution table lists SSBD as the sole database for all three splits, which cannot be true if a second class is present. No age, sex, ancestry, cognitive or language information is available for anyone in either class. For a two-class facial image classifier, the composition of both classes is what determines the meaning of the result, and here only one is described." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-129" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The stated limitations are unusually honest about measurement and silent about the study's central weakness. Three are listed formally - the scarcity and small size of public autism datasets, that 'existing datasets are not necessarily trustworthy', and the uncontrolled acquisition conditions and poor image quality - and the discussion volunteers three more concerning the annotation layer, each of them a real threat: the five raters 'are not experts in affect and emotion estimation. Therefore, it is impossible to confirm that the information gathered is accurate'; averaging their ratings 'is subject to the annotators' reaction times', so inattention produces lag that deviates from the ground truth; and simple averaging 'does not deal with disagreements either'. The introduction also cites Bone and colleagues' finding that on a larger and more balanced dataset they 'were unable to reproduce the results of several works that claim to drastically reduce the time to diagnose autism using machine learning', and the authors state that their own approach 'will not replace clinicians in terms of their expertise'. What is never addressed: the non-autistic class is undescribed, so the classification result is uninterpretable and may reflect a difference between image corpora rather than between children; the class balance is called balanced in the methods and 'very imbalanced' in the results, leaving no determinable chance baseline; the autism label rests on trusting YouTube uploads, which is presented as a warrant rather than examined as a limitation, and sits awkwardly beside the authors' own statement that existing datasets are not necessarily trustworthy; the subject-disjointness guarantee covers only the SSBD half of the data; no demographic information exists for either class in a study classifying faces; and there is no ethics or consent statement for identifiable children's home videos annotated for emotional state." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-129" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No acquisition equipment; the videos were 'shot using various devices in unrestricted natural settings' by families. Analysis used a Haar cascade for frame selection and face cropping, and three architectures whose configurations are reported in useful detail - a Perceiver with the LAMB optimiser at learning rate 0.001 updated every three epochs, weight decay 0.0001, dropout 0.0001, eight transformer blocks of twelve heads with four cross-attention repetitions, patch size 4 and a 256x256 latent array; a CNN-WGAN trained with Wasserstein loss; and an ImageNet-pretrained Inception v3 taken from the Keras applications with top layers excluded. Continuous annotation used dedicated rating software accepting mouse, keyboard or joystick input. The heterogeneity of the source recording devices is acknowledged as a limitation - 'the unconstrained environment and conditions of acquisition and the bad images and videos quality' - but no normalisation, resolution criterion or channel compensation is described that would address it." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-129" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None for the models. No saliency, attribution or feature analysis is reported for the classifier or the regressor, so what distinguishes the two image classes is never examined - which matters especially here, since the undocumented composition of the negative class means an attribution analysis is the only way a reader could tell whether the model responds to children or to corpora. The one transparency contribution is at the measurement layer: an inter-annotator correlation matrix showing each rater's agreement with the mean annotation, accompanied by the authors' own critique of the averaging procedure. That is honest reporting of label quality rather than model interpretability, but in a study whose labels are the weakest link it is arguably the more useful of the two." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-129" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No study setting exists. The autism data are home videos filmed by families on unspecified devices in unconstrained conditions and uploaded to YouTube; the comparison images have no stated origin at all. The paper is unusually thoughtful about setting as a variable, opening its dataset section with an explicit taxonomy - a controlled environment is one where 'researchers have a lot of control over factors that can affect the data being collected' and adjust them 'to reduce potential sources of error or bias', while an uncontrolled environment is one where 'data are obtained under natural or actual settings' without manipulation - and it categorises its four corpora accordingly, arguing that spanning both 'helps to understand a model's behavior'. That analysis is carried through rigorously for the affect task, where the drop from 98.4% on CK+ to 62.2% on AffectNet is precisely the controlled-versus-uncontrolled contrast made visible. It is not carried through for the autism task, where the same reasoning would ask whether the positive and negative images were acquired under comparable conditions - and where the paper's silence about the negative class means the question cannot even be posed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-129" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None implemented. Audio-visual fusion is stated as an aim in the introduction - 'audio-visual cues will also be used to extract emotional features' - and appears in the conclusion as future work, so the paper announces a fusion approach it does not deliver. The related-work section reports a prior audio-visual study reaching about 72%, with the observation that 'the facial expression part shows less accuracy because the features are less discriminative in comparison to the features used for the audio part (screaming and shouting)'. That observation is worth recording alongside the Geneva multimodal study charted elsewhere in this review, which found by a different route that its audio channel's predictive power came largely from non-vocal sound rather than speech: two independent studies pointing to acoustic events accompanying behaviour, rather than facial or vocal expression as such, as the carrier of the signal in in-the-wild recordings." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-129" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three architectures compared across four datasets and two task types, which is a broader and more systematic comparison than most of this branch attempts. A Perceiver transformer was chosen for autism classification on the grounds of image-classification performance and cross-instance weight sharing; an ImageNet-pretrained Inception v3 emerged as the best affect regressor; a CNN-WGAN was included for its discriminator's sensitivity to image authenticity. The authors report negative tuning results honestly - varying the Perceiver's latent dimension, head count and learning rate produced no improvement over the original paper's configuration - and they explain the Perceiver's poor regression performance mechanistically, attributing it to 'the absence of temporal information'. Comparison against published methods is included for every experiment, and the study's own models lose in several of them, which is reported without softening." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-129" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two constructs, only one of which the pipeline is well matched to. The classifier targets facial appearance in crops taken from videos whose defining content is self-stimulatory movement - spinning, head banging, arm flapping - so the behaviour that identifies the corpus is cropped away before modelling, leaving whatever differs in the face. The authors recognise this obliquely by proposing gesture analysis as future work. The affect regressor targets emotional expression in the continuous arousal-valence plane, and here the construct choice is well argued: the paper notes that affective computing has concentrated on discrete emotion categories, that 'conventional methods of estimating or quantifying affect in neurotypical individuals are not effective for individuals with mental health disorders', and that no dataset of continuous affect labels for autistic children existed - which the authors then set out to remedy. The resulting demonstration that a neurotypical-trained affect model transfers poorly to autistic faces is a substantive statement about the construct itself: emotional expression in autistic children is not the same measurement problem as in neurotypical adults, and models built on the latter do not simply generalise." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-129" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Published 2023-2024 by the LISSI laboratory at Université Paris-Est Créteil with the Faculty of Computer Science and Information Technology at the University of Malaya, funded by a Malaysia-France bilateral research grant. The framing is affective computing rather than diagnosis: the goal is a platform for longitudinal monitoring that could alert clinicians and parents to deterioration, and the motivating claim is that affect measures developed on neurotypical people do not work for autistic people. For this review the study's value is concentrated in one finding and one contrast. The finding is that a model trained on AffectNet - a large in-the-wild corpus of neurotypical faces - performs poorly at estimating arousal and valence in autistic children, and that fine-tuning it on autistic data produces noticeably better results, from which the authors conclude that data relating to autistic children rather than to neurotypical subjects is what such a task requires. That is a clean empirical demonstration, in a corpus otherwise short of them, that emotion-recognition technology built on general populations does not transfer to autistic faces - which bears on the several studies in this review that apply generic affect models to autistic children, and on any deployed system that would do so. The contrast is between this study's modest, stable, subject-disjoint 74.5% on SSBD face crops and the 94.6% that the authors themselves cite from a study using the Kaggle facial-image corpus, noting only that it was 'on another dataset' whose 'data distribution' the comparator exploited. This review has charted five studies on that Kaggle corpus reporting 81.0% to 99.8% under image-level splitting, against the present study's mid-seventies under subject-level splitting on a different in-the-wild corpus - and the pattern is the one the synthesis has documented throughout: figures in this branch track the splitting and labelling scheme rather than any property of the children. Three cautions travel with the study. The autism label rests on the authors' stated confidence in parents who posted videos of their children stimming, with no clinical ascertainment of any kind. The composition of the non-autistic class is never disclosed, so the classification result cannot be interpreted, and the paper's own data table contradicts its existence. And there is no ethics or consent statement anywhere, in work that crops faces from identifiable children's home videos and has five non-expert raters annotate their emotional states frame by frame - a gap made more conspicuous by the fact that the paper does discuss the trustworthiness of its data, but only as a question about label accuracy." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-129" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Mixed within a single paper, and the internal tension is worth recording. Some passages are attentive: the term neurotypical is defined explicitly as 'an individual whose neurological development and functioning are considered typical regarding the society's norms and expectations' - a definition that locates typicality in social expectation rather than in biology, which is a neurodiversity-adjacent formulation; the authors relay approvingly that 'ASD is not a disease'; and they observe that differences in emotional perception 'may be the cause for them to experience social and emotional isolation', locating the difficulty in the interaction rather than solely in the person. Against this, the clinical description reproduces the harshest vocabulary in the review: individuals with autism 'typically demonstrate a lack of social interaction and emotional intelligence as well as having repetitive, abhorrent, stigmatized, and fixated behavior'. Calling autistic behaviour abhorrent, in a paper whose data consist of home videos that families posted of their own children, is a serious lapse - and it sits two paragraphs from the statement that autism is not a disease. Elsewhere the register is standard medical ('individuals with ASD', 'ASD children', 'patients'). No autistic person or community is reported as involved in the annotation protocol, in defining what counts as an affective state, or in reviewing the use of family-uploaded videos." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-129" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None, and the paper's account of why is the most candid statement of provenance-based labelling in this review. SSBD videos were 'posted by parents/caregivers on YouTube', collected and annotated for stimming behaviour, and the authors reason: 'Therefore, and in the way the dataset is described, the data only concerns ASD children ... We are also confident in the reliability of the parents who uploaded these videos, and we, therefore, consider that the youngsters presented as the main subject of these videos are all autistic.' The autism label thus rests on two inferences - that a family posting a video of their child stimming implies a diagnosis, and that families are reliable reporters of that diagnosis - and on an explicitly stated act of trust. No clinical assessment, diagnostic instrument, medical record or clinician judgement exists for any child in the corpus. The comparison class is worse still: no basis whatever is offered for the non-autistic label, because the class itself is never described. Two structural consequences follow. Because the positive class comprises children whose families filmed and published their stereotypies, it is drawn from the visibly and severely affected end of the phenotype rather than from any representative spectrum. And because the paper elsewhere lists 'existing datasets are not necessarily trustworthy' as a limitation while simultaneously declaring confidence in these particular uploads, the two statements sit uneasily together." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-129" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two aims are pursued in one pipeline: classify autism from face images, then estimate continuous affect in the identified autistic children, with a view to a longitudinal monitoring platform. The autism classification result is modest and stable - 74.5% accuracy with F1 73.7% and recall 73.2% at a 10% test split, 74.8% at 20%, and 75.2% under 5-fold cross-validation. The affect results are where the substance lies and they are structured as a genuine comparison across conditions: 93.7-98.4% for discrete affect on laboratory-controlled CK+, falling to 57.3-62.2% on in-the-wild AffectNet, with continuous-affect concordance correlations on AffectNet of 0.342-0.513 for arousal and 0.373-0.661 for valence. The finding the paper is built around, and the one worth carrying into the synthesis, is the transfer result: a model trained solely on AffectNet - neurotypical faces - performs poorly on autistic children, while fine-tuning it on the newly annotated autistic data produces 'noticeably superior outcomes', leading the authors to conclude that 'it is preferable to have a dataset where the data obtained relates to ASD children and not neurotypical subjects, specifically, when the task is to predict the values for valence and arousal'. That is an empirical demonstration that affect-recognition tools built on neurotypical populations do not transfer to autistic faces, which has direct implications for every deployed emotion-recognition system and for the several studies in this corpus that apply generic affect models to autistic children." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-129" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A study with better splitting practice than most of its branch and an unexaminable result, which is an instructive combination. The splitting is genuinely careful for the autism experiment: subjects are disjoint across partitions, the authors state why - 'in order not to distort the results or create overfitting' - and explain how it was achievable, since one child appears in one video. The partition is reported by child and by seconds of video rather than by frame count, which is precisely the disclosure the frame-level studies elsewhere in this review omit. Robustness is probed twice more, by varying the test proportion from 10% to 20% (74.5% to 74.8%) and by 5-fold cross-validation (75.2%), and the authors correctly note that a 5-fold arrangement approximates a 20% test split, so the agreement is expected rather than independent confirmation. All of this is sound. What defeats it is that the classifier's negative class is never described. For a two-class image model, the reported figure is a discrimination between two populations of images, and only one of those populations is characterised; if the non-autistic faces come from a curated corpus such as CK+ or AffectNet - both of which the study uses elsewhere - then the model may be separating home-video frames from studio or scraped photographs, and no splitting scheme could reveal it. The paper's own data-distribution table naming SSBD as the sole database across all three splits is incompatible with a second class existing, so the reporting is not merely incomplete but internally inconsistent. The class balance is likewise contradictory, called balanced in the methods and 'very imbalanced' in the results, which leaves the chance baseline for 74.5% undetermined. Two further points. The subject-disjointness argument rests on a property of SSBD alone, so whatever comprises the negative class has no stated leakage control - and the same corpus, under conventional splitting, was found by another study in this review to contain training-test pairs with cosine similarity up to 0.9714, indicating substantial near-duplication that subject-level splitting is designed to but may not fully address. And the 5-fold figure is reported as 'top-5 validation accuracy', a best-of-folds validation quantity rather than a mean held-out accuracy, so it is weaker evidence than it appears. Set against these, one part of the study's validation design is exemplary and belongs in the synthesis: the affect experiments constitute a true cross-population transfer test - train on neurotypical faces, evaluate on autistic children, then fine-tune - and the result, that the neurotypical-trained model transfers poorly and fine-tuning materially improves it, is both methodologically clean and substantively important." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-129" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Strong on procedure and empty at the one point that matters most. Well reported: all four datasets with their properties and their categorisation as controlled or uncontrolled, the two SSBD exclusions and the face-observability criterion for frame retention, the annotation protocol with the rater count, training, scale, software modality and supervision, the normalisation equation and the inter-annotator correlation matrix, the split proportions for every dataset, the subject-disjointness rule with per-split child counts and video durations, the complete Perceiver hyperparameter configuration and the evidence that alternatives were tried without gain, the Inception v3 provenance and layer handling, and formal definitions of every metric. Absent: the composition, source and size of the non-autistic image class; the total number of images in any split; code; and the new annotations, which cannot be shared. The first of these is disabling - a reader cannot determine what the classifier's 74.5% is a discrimination between, and the paper's own data-distribution table, which lists SSBD as the sole database across all three splits, is inconsistent with a second class existing at all." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-129" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three directions, each tied to an identified weakness. Move from visual-only to audio-visual models. Replace the simple averaging of annotator ratings with a neural multi-label learning approach capable of handling disagreement and reaction-time lag - a direct response to the authors' own critique of their ground truth. And incorporate the subjects' gestures into both tasks, which is the most interesting of the three: the corpus is defined by gestural stereotypy, and cropping to the face discards exactly the behaviour that identifies the children. Not proposed, and prerequisite to interpreting the classification result: documenting the composition and source of the non-autistic class, and evaluating whether the classifier separates children or corpora." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-129" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, F1 and recall for classification; mean squared error and concordance correlation coefficient for regression. The regression metric choice is well argued and the argument is worth recording: the authors note that a comparator 'did not report the performances of their approach according to the CCC despite it being the most popular metric for audio and visual affect recognition' and observe that 'it is possible that their approach is very accurate according to MSE but much less accurate according to CCC' - then demonstrate exactly that inversion in their own results, where Inception v3 beats the Perceiver on MSE while losing on CCC. Recognising that two metrics can rank models oppositely, and showing it in one's own data, is a level of measurement care rare in this corpus. The classification reporting is correspondingly weaker: no specificity, no confusion matrix, no confidence intervals, no positive predictive value, and - because the class balance is stated inconsistently - no determinable chance baseline for the reported 74.5%." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-129" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: Autism Spectrum Disorder, deep learning, discrete affect recognition, continuous affect assessment, SSBD dataset. The set is method- and dataset-facing, with the corpus named explicitly - which is helpful for tracing the SSBD subliterature, of which this review now contains three members. Terms carried through the body: Perceiver, cross-attention, WGAN, Wasserstein loss, Inception v3, arousal, valence, concordance correlation coefficient, self-stimulatory behaviour, in-the-wild, lab-controlled, longitudinal monitoring." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-129" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Biomedical Signal Processing and Control 89:105712, Elsevier; received 24 February 2023, revised 18 October 2023, accepted 5 November 2023, available online 8 November 2023, with a 2024 issue date. A signal-processing venue, and the fingerprints are visible in both directions: the architectural detail, the metric definitions, the four-dataset comparison and the careful argument about MSE versus CCC are all to a good engineering standard, while the absence of any ethics or consent statement for identifiable children's home videos, the undocumented comparison class and the absent demographics passed review without comment. The journal's structure elicited funding, competing-interests, author-contribution and data-availability declarations but has no field that would have prompted a human-subjects statement." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-129" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "RGB face crops produced by a Haar cascade, with only frames in which the child's face is observable retained and the number of frames per video varying accordingly. No landmark geometry, no morphometric derivation, no body pose and no temporal sequence for the classifier. Two consequences. Cropping to the face discards the stereotypies that define the corpus, so the classifier cannot use the behaviour that identified these children as autistic in the first place - which the authors implicitly acknowledge by proposing gesture analysis as future work. And retaining only frames with a visible face introduces a selection that could differ systematically between classes, since a child spinning or flapping presents their face to a home camera less consistently than a child photographed for a curated corpus would." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-129" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No severity, cognitive, adaptive or language measure exists. The study's own contribution on this axis is a new continuous affect layer - arousal and valence values for SSBD frames, produced by five trained annotators using continuous rating software with a mouse, keyboard or joystick, on a 0 to 5 scale, normalised to the -1 to 1 range for comparability with published work, with the per-frame values averaged across raters and an inter-annotator correlation matrix reported. This is a genuine measurement contribution and the authors subject it to the sharpest self-criticism in the paper: the annotators 'are not experts in affect and emotion estimation. Therefore, it is impossible to confirm that the information gathered is accurate'; averaging 'is subject to the annotators' reaction times', so that an inattentive rater's lag propagates into the label; and simple averaging 'does not deal with disagreements either'. The stated ambition of automated severity-level assessment, which would 'alert clinicians and parents when aggravation occurs', is not realised - affect level is not severity, and no severity data exist to validate against. The annotations themselves are not released, since the authors do not have permission to share data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-129" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional secondary analysis with no longitudinal element, despite the stated goal being a longitudinal monitoring platform. There is no ethics statement, no institutional review board reference and no consent statement anywhere in the paper - a full-text search returns no occurrence of the word. The data are identifiable home videos of children, published by their families for reasons of their own, from which faces were cropped and then annotated frame by frame for emotional state by five raters in order to build an autism classifier. Whether families posting a video of their child's stimming for a community of other parents thereby consent to its use as autism training data, and whether a child depicted at two or three years old has any interest in the matter, are questions the paper does not raise. The one place the authors do discuss the trustworthiness of their data concerns label accuracy rather than the ethics of use. Funding under the Malaysia-France Bilateral Research Grant IF040-2021 is declared, competing interests are declared as none, and the data availability statement reads in full: 'The authors do not have permission to share data' - which means the annotations offered as the paper's dataset contribution cannot be obtained." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-129" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None for the autism data; the children were filmed by their families for their own purposes. For the affect layer the paper does define a paradigm, and describes it carefully: five annotators, all trained on the software and the protocol beforehand, watched the videos and gave instantaneous ratings of arousal and valence between 0 and 5 using a mouse, keyboard or joystick, with the authors present throughout to ensure the protocol was followed and with worked examples supplied. Ratings were averaged across annotators per frame and normalised to the -1 to 1 range used in the affect literature. The absence of a paradigm on the data side has a specific consequence the study half-recognises: what a child is doing when filmed determines their expression, and in SSBD every autistic child is by construction engaged in a stereotypy while the comparison children are doing something unknown - so the classifier and the affect model are both operating across an uncontrolled difference in activity as well as any difference in the children." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-129" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:study_design "Retrospective algorithm-development study on public corpora, with a new annotation layer contributed by the authors, comparing three deep architectures across four datasets and two…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: ; comparison: Never stated. The methods say only that 'in order to have a complete and balanced database to train this model, we extracted the frames featuring the faces of ASD children and gathered facial images of other ASD children and facial images of neurotypical youngsters' - so a non-autistic class exists, but its size, its source and the number of individuals it represents are nowhere given. The data-distribution table lists SSBD as the sole database for all three splits, which cannot be reconciled with the presence of a second class." ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "c85da067258bc6ff471186879eca7c220a4d11b6c388d3a3995657e82b7d527e" ;
