    slr:journal "Displa" ;
    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "The comparator for the reported agreement is a single experienced clinician's 0-5 response-to-name score, assigned in real time by the same person conducting the assessment and calling the child's name, with no blinding, no second rater and no reliability statistic. The scoring rule itself is explicit and well specified, which is a strength, but the item requires a live judgement about whether a head turn included eye contact, made from behind the child, and no evidence is offered that a second clinician would score the same sessions the same way. The autism diagnoses underlying the sample rest on ADOS-2 administered at the same department, with no scores reported." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "All 30 children received both the index test and the reference standard within the same session, which eliminates any interval concern. The problems are structural: the 109 test events are analysed as independent units although they cluster within 30 children and are strongly serially dependent, since a child who does not respond to the first call generally does not respond to the remaining four; no chance-agreement baseline is computed against a sample in which 17 of 30 children scored 5; and no accounting is given of detection, tracking or landmark failures, two of which are known to have occurred and are described only anecdotally in the discussion." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "A consecutive or convenience clinical series of 30 children at one tertiary hospital, 29 of them already autistic and 1 typically developing, selected by ADOS-2 module (TM, M1 or M2). No inclusion or exclusion criteria beyond module are stated, no recruitment period is given, and the demographic description is limited to a single overall mean age of 3.65 years with no sex, IQ, language or severity data. Because the sample contains essentially no non-autistic children, the study cannot address discrimination even in principle, yet is framed throughout as supporting early screening." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "The index test is not prespecified and is not validated on data separate from that used to construct it. All five decision constants (90 degree and 20 degree shoulder thresholds, 10-frame and 8-frame durations, 3 s window) were set by hand and evaluated on the same 30 children, and the retrained YOLOv3 detector was fitted on more than 1,700 images of those same children. The judgement is automated and does not consult the clinician's score at inference, but no part of the pipeline was ever exercised on an unseen participant." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "high" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<urn:slr:article:44d2304739ef> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Background: Autism spectrum disorder (ASD) is a neurodevelopmental disorder that results in altered behavior, social development, and communication patterns. In recent years, autism prevalence has tripled, with 1 in 44 children now affected. Given that traditional diagnosis is a lengthy, labor-intensive process that requires the work of trained physicians, significant attention has been given to developing systems that automatically detect autism. We work toward this goal by analyzing audio data, as prosody abnormalities are a signal of autism, with affected children displaying speech idiosyncrasies such as echolalia, monotonous intonation, atypical pitch, and irregular linguistic stress patterns. Objective: We aimed to test the ability for machine learning approaches to aid in detection of autism in self-recorded speech audio captured from children with ASD and neurotypical (NT) children in their home environments. Methods: We considered three methods to detect autism in child speech: (1) random forests trained on extracted audio features (including Mel-frequency cepstral coefficients); (2) convolutional neural networks trained on spectrograms; and (3) fine-tuned wav2vec 2.0—a state-of-the-art transformer-based speech recognition model. We trained our classifiers on our novel data set of cellphone-recorded child speech audio curated from the Guess What? mobile game, an app designed to crowdsource videos of children with ASD and NT children in a natural home environment. Results: The random forest classifier achieved 70% accuracy, the fine-tuned wav2vec 2.0 model achieved 77% accuracy, and the convolutional neural network achieved 79% accuracy when classifying children’s audio as either ASD or NT. We used 5-fold cross-validation to evaluate model performance. Conclusions: Our models were able to predict autism status when trained on a varied selection of home audio clips with inconsistent recording qualities, which may be more representative of real-world conditions. The results demonstrate that machine learning methods offer promise in detecting autism automatically from speech without specialized equipment. (JMIR Pediatr Parent 2022;5(2):e35406) doi: 10.2196/35406" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Chi NA; Washington P; Kline A; Husic A; Hou C; He C; Dunlap K; Wall DP (2022). Classifying Autism From Crowdsourced Semistructured Speech. *JMIR*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Chi NA",
        "Dunlap K",
        "He C",
        "Hou C",
        "Husic A",
        "Kline A",
        "Wall DP",
        "Washington P" ;
    dcterms:title "Classifying Autism From Crowdsourced Semistructured Speech" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_2b766683" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2022 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on age?" ;
                            slr:justification "Median 5 years versus 9.5 - a 4.5-year gap never mentioned outside the demographics paragraph." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Group sizes, sexes, median ages, video and clip counts and collection period given; no age means, ranges by group, or exclusion criteria." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are clip contributions balanced across children?" ;
                            slr:justification "425 clips from 20 autistic children against 425 from 38 comparison children - roughly 21 versus 11 clips each." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on sex?" ;
                            slr:justification "95% versus 39% male, acknowledged and benchmarked against the 4:1 to 3:1 population ratio." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is developmental or language level measured?" ;
                            slr:justification "No cognitive, language, adaptive or severity measure for either group, in a study whose outcome is speech." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the acoustic conditions characterised?" ;
                            slr:justification "No distribution of devices, sampling rates, signal-to-noise ratios or recording distances, so no check that acoustic properties do not covary with group." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "Unverified parent report with no instrument or clinician contact, as the authors state." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the audio preprocessing reproducible?" ;
                            slr:justification "The splicing criterion is stated but performed manually with no reliability check, no clip duration and no per-child sampling rule." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the elicitation context described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "The app, both game types, the 90-second session, the recording arrangement and the parent's mediating role are all specified." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Do the features match the stated construct?" ;
                            slr:justification "The motivation is prosodic - pitch, intonation, stress - yet no pitch, intonation or stress feature appears in the feature set." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is hyperparameter selection described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Random forest hyperparameters are stated as 'manually chosen' with no procedure; deep model selection is undocumented." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are multiple model families compared on identical folds?" ;
                            slr:justification "Random forest, CNN and wav2vec 2.0 on the same tabulated fold composition." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are cross-validation folds participant-disjoint?" ;
                            slr:justification "Explicitly constrained so all of a child's clips fall in one fold, with the recording-idiosyncrasy rationale stated." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a trivial baseline reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No age-only or sex-only comparator despite both differing markedly between groups." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are negative results reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Logistic regression, naive Bayes, AdaBoost and a fine-tuned Inception v3 all reported as underperforming." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the unit of reported metrics the participant?" ;
                            slr:justification "Metrics are averaged over 850 clips from 58 children; no per-child accuracy is reported." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is variability across folds reported and interpreted?" ;
                            slr:justification "Standard deviations on every metric, per-fold ROC curves, and the observation that all models covary across folds, correctly attributed to fold size." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Described as novel; no availability statement." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Not mentioned, though all architectures and hyperparameters are specified." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is ethics approval reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No IRB statement in the extracted text, though per-session parental consent to share is built into the app flow." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are competing interests disclosed?" ;
                            slr:justification "The senior author declares founding Cognoa, a company developing digital paediatric diagnostics." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the principal confound acknowledged?" ;
                            slr:justification "The sex imbalance is quantified and discussed; the larger age gap is never mentioned after the demographics paragraph." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is privacy addressed substantively?" ;
                            slr:justification "A dedicated subsection proposing on-device inference and federated learning, motivated by autism's stigmatisation and parents' reluctance to share video." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are any acoustic or behavioural findings reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No group means, effect sizes or feature importances - the study yields accuracy figures and no characterisation of how the groups' speech differed." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Correct on the dimension that matters most in this corpus and incomplete on a secondary one. The fold constraint is stated explicitly and reasoned exactly right: 'all clips spliced from a given child's video had to be included in the same fold to prevent models from learning from child-specific recording idiosyncrasies, including environmental background noise and audio quality'. That names both the mechanism and its two concrete forms, and it is the discipline missing from Cilia's image-level folds, Simeoli's task-level folds, Liaqat's image-wise splits and Gaspar's augmented copies. Fold composition is tabulated so the balance can be checked, all three models are evaluated on the same folds, and metrics are averaged with standard deviations. Three gaps remain. Metrics are clip-level rather than child-level, so 850 correlated observations from 58 children are treated as the effective sample and no per-child accuracy is available. There is no held-out test set beyond cross-validation, and the random forest's hyperparameters were 'manually chosen' with no procedure described, so some selection optimism is unquantified. And most importantly, no analysis addresses whether the classifier is separating diagnosis or separating age and sex - there is no age-matched subsample, no covariate adjustment, and no age-only or sex-only baseline, despite both variables differing markedly between groups and both directly determining child vocal acoustics. The validation protocol is therefore sound against leakage and silent on confounding." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-079" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No cognitive, language, adaptive or severity measure for either group, and no comorbidity or medication information. This matters more than usual for a speech study, since expressive language level directly determines what a child produces in a 90-second game and would be expected to differ between a median-5-year-old autistic group and a median-9.5-year-old comparison group on both developmental and diagnostic grounds. Nothing in the data allows that to be examined, and no exclusion criteria of any kind are described." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-079" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups from a crowdsourced convenience sample: 20 children with a parent-reported autism diagnosis and 38 neurotypical children, 58 in total, contributing 77 videos over four years from 2018 to 2021. Clip counts were deliberately balanced at 425 per class, which means the 20 autistic children contributed roughly 21 clips each against roughly 11 each for the 38 comparison children - so the autistic class is built from fewer children sampled more densely, and each autistic child exerts about twice the influence on the class representation. No other-diagnosis comparison group. The recruitment mechanism is itself distinctive: families downloaded a mobile game, played it at home, and then chose whether to share the recording, so the sample is self-selected among app users willing to disclose a diagnosis and share video of their child." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-079" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Deliberately none beyond what families already own: participants' own smartphones, with no model, microphone or sampling-rate constraint, held against the parent's forehead during play. This is the study's contribution and its cost. The benefit is that the recordings carry the acoustic heterogeneity of real deployment - varying room acoustics, distances, device microphones, background noise - so a model that works here has already survived conditions that laboratory studies control away. The cost is that none of that variation is characterised: no distribution of device types, recording levels, signal-to-noise ratios or sampling rates is reported, so it cannot be determined whether any acoustic property covaries with group. Given that the two groups were recruited through the same app but differ in age and sex, a systematic difference in, say, microphone distance for younger children is plausible and unexamined. Analysis tools are named precisely: Librosa, scikit-learn, Keras with Visualkeras, and the facebook/wav2vec2-base checkpoint." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-079" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three limitations are stated, one of them thoroughly. The sex imbalance is quantified precisely (95% versus 39% male), benchmarked against the expected 4:1 to 3:1 population ratio, and the conclusion drawn that the dataset 'may still not be completely representative of real-world conditions' - among the more careful demographic self-assessments in the corpus. The unverified nature of parent-reported diagnoses is conceded directly. The small dataset and the non-scalability of manual splicing are both raised, with automatic blind source separation named as the necessary but difficult alternative. What is absent is the study's largest problem: the 4.5-year median age gap between groups appears once, in a single sentence of the demographics paragraph, and is never mentioned again - not in the limitations, not in the discussion, not as a caveat on the accuracy figures. No age-matched analysis, age covariate or age-only baseline is attempted. Also unaddressed: that the autistic class is built from 20 children sampled at roughly 21 clips each while the comparison class draws on 38 children at roughly 11 each; that metrics are clip-level rather than child-level despite the folds being child-disjoint; that no acoustic characterisation of any kind is reported; and that the prosodic rationale in the introduction is not reflected in any feature actually used." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-079" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with ASD', 'children with autism', 'affected children', 'individuals affected', against 'neurotypical (NT) children'. Person-first throughout, and the use of 'neurotypical' rather than 'typically developing' or 'normal' is the more neurodiversity-aligned choice, though it sits alongside conventional deficit framing of autism as 'delayed linguistic development, social interaction deficits, and behavioral impairments' and speech features described as 'peculiarities' and 'abnormalities'. The paper is unusual in explicitly naming stigma as a design consideration, noting that privacy 'is particularly important for studies involving commonly stigmatized disorders like autism' and motivating on-device inference for parents who 'hesitate to share videos with strangers'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-079" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three representations of the same 850 clips. Hand-engineered acoustic descriptors for the random forest; Librosa-generated mel spectrograms treated as images for the CNN; and raw waveforms for wav2vec 2.0. The unit throughout is the clip, not the child, and this is the study's one residual validation gap: the folds are child-disjoint, which correctly prevents a child's clips appearing on both sides, but the metrics are averaged over clips, so the reported figures aggregate 850 correlated observations from 58 people. A child who contributes 21 clips influences the accuracy twenty-one times over, and no per-child prediction or per-child accuracy is reported. Since clip counts differ systematically by group - roughly 21 per autistic child against 11 per comparison child - the clip-level metrics also weight the two classes' children unequally." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-079" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: test whether autism can be detected from self-recorded home speech captured on ordinary smartphones, removing the specialised equipment and centralised recording locations that the authors argue make prior work undeployable. Main findings: a purpose-built 8.7-million-parameter CNN over mel spectrograms reached 79.3% accuracy and 0.822 AUROC; a fine-tuned wav2vec 2.0 reached 76.9% and 0.815; a random forest over conventional acoustic features reached 69.7% and 0.740; folds were constructed child-disjoint to block recording-idiosyncrasy leakage; all three models covaried across folds, suggesting the folds are too small; an ImageNet-pretrained Inception v3 underperformed the smaller purpose-built CNN; and the whole result rests on 115.5 minutes of audio from 58 children." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-079" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three families compared on identical folds - an ensemble tree model on hand-engineered features, a convolutional network on spectrograms, and a fine-tuned self-supervised transformer on raw waveforms - which spans the realistic design space for a small audio classification problem. The comparison is conducted honestly: alternatives that lost are named rather than omitted (logistic regression, Gaussian naive Bayes, AdaBoost for the shallow family; Inception v3 for the deep one), and the winning CNN is the smallest deep model tried. Full hyperparameters are given for the random forest and described as 'manually chosen', with no tuning procedure stated - which leaves open whether the shallow model was tuned as carefully as the deep ones and hence whether the ten-point gap partly reflects unequal effort. The fold-to-fold correlation across all three models is the most thoughtful analytic observation in the paper: 'each model appears to perform well on fold 2 while performing relatively poorly on fold 3. This suggests that the data in each fold may be too limited', which correctly attributes the variance to the data partition rather than to model instability." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-079" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "JMIR Pediatrics and Parenting 5(2):e35406 (2022), an open-access digital-health journal for paediatric readers. Authorship spans Stanford's Division of Systems Medicine in Pediatrics, Bioengineering, Computer Science, Biomedical Data Science and Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences, with the senior author's group having produced a substantial body of prior work on mobile autism screening, wearable social-cue delivery and crowdsourced annotation - much of it cited here. Funding is extensive, listing four NIH grants, an NSF award and numerous foundations. The senior author declares founding Cognoa, a company developing digital paediatric diagnostics, disclosed appropriately; the same company's FDA-authorised screening product is critiqued by name in the Ko et al. paper elsewhere in this corpus for feeding human ratings rather than raw video into its model - a critique that this study's raw-audio approach implicitly answers." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-079" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Reported, and the reporting reveals the study's central problem. Autistic group: 20 children, 19 male (95%) and 1 female, median age 5 years. Comparison group: 38 children, 15 male (39%), 22 female (58%) and 1 unspecified, median age 9.5 years. Overall age range 3-12. Two large imbalances follow. The 4.5-year median age gap is the more serious and is never mentioned anywhere in the paper - not in the limitations, not in the discussion - despite being the single variable that most determines the acoustics of a child's voice across this range: fundamental frequency falls, formants shift with vocal-tract growth, articulation stabilises, disfluency declines and utterance length increases substantially between 5 and 9.5 years. The sex imbalance is the reverse - 95% versus 39% male - and this one is acknowledged, quantified and benchmarked, with the authors noting that even the naturally skewed 4:1 to 3:1 population ratio would predict only 75-80% male, so their split exceeds expectation, and concluding that the dataset 'may still not be completely representative of real-world conditions'. Combining the two, the autistic group is younger and almost entirely male while the comparison group is older and majority female, so the groups differ systematically on both determinants of child vocal acoustics before diagnosis is considered. No IQ, language level, developmental measure or matching procedure is reported." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-079" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Single modality - audio only - with no fusion attempted, though multimodal extension is proposed as future work. The paper does run a genuine three-way representation comparison on identical child-disjoint folds, which is more informative than most single-modality studies here and yields two transferable observations. First, learned representations beat hand-engineered ones by roughly ten points (79.3% and 76.9% against 69.7%), and the gap is largest in AUROC (0.822 and 0.815 against 0.740), suggesting that whatever separates the classes is not well captured by the conventional descriptor set. Second, the two transfer-learning results point in opposite directions and the authors interpret both correctly: an ImageNet-pretrained Inception v3 with 33 million parameters lost to a purpose-built 8.7-million-parameter CNN, attributed to 'the irrelevance of ImageNet features to spectrograms', while a self-supervised audio transformer transferred usefully, justified by prior evidence of wav2vec's utility 'for suprasegmental tasks such as emotion prediction'. Domain-matched pretraining helped; domain-mismatched pretraining hurt, even at four times the parameter count." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-079" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Five metrics per model, each as mean and standard deviation across five folds, plus per-fold and mean ROC curves and confusion matrices for all three - complete and comparative reporting. The standard deviations do real work here and are worth reading closely: the random forest's recall SD is 0.247, larger than the entire performance gap between models, indicating that its per-fold recall swings wildly, while its accuracy SD is only 0.013 - a pattern signalling that it trades sensitivity against specificity differently in each fold. Wav2vec's AUROC SD of 0.077 against the CNN's 0.010 similarly shows the CNN is the more stable model despite the authors' expectation that the 95-million-parameter transformer would be 'more robust'. The confusion matrices add a real finding: both deep models produce few false positives while the random forest produces many, with all three having similar false negative rates. What is missing is any comparison against a trivial baseline - given a 4.5-year median age difference between groups, an age-only classifier is the obvious comparator and would establish how much of the 79.3% is diagnostic at all." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-079" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2022. The framing combines the usual epidemiological appeal with two access statistics that are more concrete than the corpus norm: 83.86% of American counties have no autism diagnostic resources at all, and the delay from first consultation to diagnosis averages over two years, pushing typical diagnosis past age four with documented downstream consequences including higher psychotropic medication use, lower IQ and reduced language aptitude. The distinctive positioning, though, is a critique of the immediately preceding speech literature on deployability rather than on accuracy. The authors name two studies achieving comparable or better results - one using 'a specialized biosensor device with 4 directional microphones', another 'multiple wireless microphones, providing high purity recordings at a central recording location (a hospital)' - and argue that such work 'does not accelerate the process of autism detection' precisely because the conditions that produce clean data are the conditions that prevent scaling. They then add a validity argument alongside the logistical one: recording in unfamiliar settings with unfamiliar adults may itself alter the behaviour being measured. That pairing - that laboratory control buys signal quality at the cost of both reach and ecological validity - is the sharpest statement of the deployment-versus-fidelity tradeoff in this review, and it is the axis along which this study should be compared with Asgari and colleagues, who took the opposite position and gained interpretability, clinical characterisation and confound control in exchange for requiring a clinical ADOS session." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-079" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional, with data accumulated opportunistically over four years from 2018 to 2021 rather than in a defined recruitment window; no follow-up. Parental consent to share each recording is built into the app flow - families view the video after each session and choose whether to send it - which is a stronger form of ongoing consent than a single upfront authorisation, though no IRB approval statement appears in the extracted text. Privacy is treated substantively rather than as boilerplate, with a dedicated Discussion subsection arguing that the models' small size permits on-device inference and privacy-preserving federated learning, motivated explicitly by autism's stigmatisation and by parents who want a diagnostic signal but are unwilling to share video. A senior author declares founding Cognoa, a company developing digital paediatric health solutions." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-079" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: autism, mHealth, machine learning, artificial intelligence, speech, audio, child, digital data, mobile app, diagnosis. The presence of 'mHealth' and 'mobile app' alongside 'diagnosis' locates the work in digital health delivery rather than in speech science, which matches the paper's actual contribution - a deployment architecture rather than an acoustic finding." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-079" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Good on modelling, thin on data. Reported: the app, the two game types, the 90-second session length, the recording arrangement, the manual splicing criterion (segments containing no voice but the child's), clip counts by class, the mean of 1.32 videos and 14.7 clips per child, the fold composition table, the child-disjoint fold constraint with its rationale, the complete random forest feature list and all six hyperparameters, the CNN's layer structure, regularisation penalty, epoch count and exact parameter count, the wav2vec checkpoint and fine-tuning step count, the alternative models tried and rejected, and the total 115.5 minutes of audio. Not reported: any acoustic property of the recordings, the group-specific age ranges or means, any splicing reliability check or inter-splicer agreement, clip durations, the criterion for how many clips were taken per child, any data or code release, and random seeds. The manual splicing step is the least reproducible element - it is a human judgement applied to every clip, performed by unspecified people to an unspecified standard, and the authors identify its non-scalability as a limitation without addressing its consistency." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-079" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No feature importance, attribution, saliency, spectral analysis or group comparison of any acoustic quantity is reported, and no interpretation is offered of what distinguishes the classes. This is a substantial gap given that the paper's entire motivation is a specific set of prosodic claims - echolalia, monotonous intonation, atypical pitch, irregular stress - none of which is tested, measured or discussed after the introduction. The random forest's feature set would have supported at least a permutation-importance analysis at no cost, and mel spectrograms admit standard saliency methods. The consequence is that the study yields three accuracy figures and no knowledge about autistic children's speech, so it cannot be evaluated for construct validity: there is no way to tell whether the models are keying on prosody, on vocal-tract size, on recording distance, or on utterance length. The authors do gesture at interpretability as a future goal, proposing that combining audio features with computer-vision measures 'could possibly result in interpretable models', which acknowledges the present absence." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-079" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Nominally prosody. The introduction rests its case entirely on prosodic abnormality, citing 'echolalia, monotonous intonation, and atypical pitch and linguistic stress patterns' as 'among the most notable signs of autism', and this is the paper's justification for looking at speech at all. What is actually modelled is broader and less specific: the random forest uses a generic speech-recognition feature set - MFCCs, chroma, RMS energy, spectral centroid, bandwidth and rolloff, zero-crossing rate - containing no pitch, intonation contour, stress or rhythm measure whatsoever, while the CNN and wav2vec models learn unconstrained representations from spectrograms and waveforms. The behavioural target is therefore 'whatever in the acoustic signal separates these two groups of children', which given the sample composition includes vocal-tract size, fundamental frequency range and articulatory maturity - all direct functions of the age and sex on which the groups differ. Because no acoustic finding is reported, the paper cannot say whether anything prosodic was involved. The contrast with Asgari and colleagues elsewhere in this corpus is instructive: that study modelled pitch, jitter, shimmer and harmonics-to-noise ratio explicitly, reduced to two interpretable features, and correlated them with independently rated severity." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-079" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Five directions, with the most substantive being technical. Collect more data across a wider selection of both groups, argued from the observation that 115.5 minutes already sufficed. Replace manual splicing with automatic blind source separation - the authors are realistic about this, calling it 'an exceptionally challenging task' while noting it is 'possibly a necessary hurdle to overcome to develop widely available and consistently effective autism machine learning diagnosis resources', which correctly identifies the bottleneck for any home-recording pipeline where parents and children speak simultaneously. Explore wearable collection through devices such as Google Glass, building on the group's prior work delivering social cues through wearables. Add crowdsourced human annotation of audio features. And combine audio with computer-vision quantification of hand stimming, upper-limb movement and eye contact toward an interpretable multimodal system. Notably absent: any proposal to verify diagnoses, to match on age, or to characterise what acoustically distinguishes the groups." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-079" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Participants' own homes, with no researcher present, no controlled acoustics and no equipment standardisation - the design's defining property and its explicit purpose. The authors' argument is twofold and both parts are worth noting. Logistically, prior speech studies used directional microphone arrays or multiple wireless microphones at a hospital, which 'pos[es] significant barriers to the widespread availability of automatic diagnosis tools', reinforced by the observation that 83.86% of American counties have no autism diagnostic resources at all and that the delay from first consultation to diagnosis averages over two years. Behaviourally, the authors argue that 'interacting with unknown adults in foreign environments could be stressful and possibly affect the behavior of children with autism, thus leading to observations that are not generalizable to the real world' - a validity argument for home recording rather than merely a convenience one. The consequence embraced rather than lamented is heterogeneous audio quality, which the paper treats as fidelity to deployment conditions." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-079" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Ninety-second sessions of the Guess What? mobile game, structured as either charades - acting out emotions, characters, sports, chores or objects - or a quiz on colours, shapes, numbers and word spellings. The child follows the gameplay while the parent mediates, holding the smartphone against their own forehead with the front-facing camera on the child. Three properties matter. The interaction partner is the child's own parent rather than a clinician or a stranger, which is what makes the elicited speech naturalistic. The session is extremely short, and the total corpus is only 115.5 minutes across 58 children, which the authors turn into a positive by noting that so little data still yielded workable accuracy. And the task structure is only semi-standardised: families choose between two game types and the resulting speech differs accordingly, so the linguistic and prosodic demands are not equated across children - a source of variation the paper does not examine, and one that could interact with age given that a median-5-year-old and a median-9.5-year-old will engage very differently with a spelling quiz." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-079" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Parent report alone, and the authors state the limitation directly: 'while we require parents to disclose their child's clinical diagnosis by choosing from options not widely known to those who have not received a clinical evaluation, these labels are self-reported and thus unverified'. No standardised instrument, no clinician involvement, no medical record check. The safeguard offered - that the diagnostic labels presented in the app are unfamiliar to families who have not been through an evaluation - is a reasonable heuristic against casual mislabelling but does nothing to establish which diagnosis a child actually received, when, by whom, or against what criteria. The comparison group's neurotypical status likewise rests on parental assertion with no screening instrument. This is the weakest reference standard in the corpus alongside the benchmark-reuse studies, and it is a consequence of the crowdsourcing design rather than an oversight: a fully remote app-based collection cannot verify diagnoses without a separate clinical pathway." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-079" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "90-second gameplay sessions; each child contributed a mean of 1.32 videos and 14.7 clips, totalling 115.5 minutes of audio across the whole dataset" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional case-control classification on a crowdsourced convenience sample, structured as a three-way model comparison under identical child-disjoint folds" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Children with a parent-reported autism diagnosis" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 20 children (19 male, 1 female) contributing 425 clips; comparison: 38 children (15 male, 22 female, 1 unspecified) contributing 425 clips" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "CNN: 79.3% accuracy (SD 0.013), 80.4% precision, 79.3% recall, 79.0% F1, AUROC 0.822 (SD 0.010). Wav2vec 2.0: 76.9% accuracy (SD 0.005), 78.2% precision, 74.6% recall, 76.8% F1, AUROC 0.815 (SD 0.077). Random forest: 69.7% accuracy (SD 0.013), 68.7% precision, 74.4% recall, 69.4% F1, AUROC 0.740 (SD 0.09). Notably the random forest's recall standard deviation is 0.247 - larger than the differences",
                        "Speech recorded on ordinary parental smartphones during 90-second home gameplay sessions supported autism classification at 79.3% accuracy and 0.822 AUROC using a small CNN over mel spectrograms, with a fine-tuned wav2vec 2.0 close behind at 76.9% and a random forest on conventional acoustic features clearly worse at 69.7%. The result rests on only 115.5 minutes of audio from 58 children, and the " ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy, precision, recall, F1 and AUROC, each as mean and standard deviation across the five folds; per-fold and mean ROC curves for each model; and confusion matrices for all three models" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "CNN: 79.3% accuracy (SD 0.013), 80.4% precision, 79.3% recall, 79.0% F1, AUROC 0.822 (SD 0.010). Wav2vec 2.0: 76.9% accuracy (SD 0.005), 78.2% precision, 74.6% recall, 76.8% F1, AUROC 0.815 (SD 0.077). Random forest: 69.7% accuracy (SD 0.013), 68.7% precision, 74.4% recall, 69.4% F1, AUROC 0.740 (SD 0.09). Notably the random forest's recall standard deviation is 0.247 - larger than the differences; Speech recorded on ordinary parental smartphones during 90-second home gameplay sessions supported autism classification at 79.3% accuracy and 0.822 AUROC using a small CNN over mel spectrograms, with a fine-tuned wav2vec 2.0 close behind at 76.9% and a random forest on conventional acoustic features clearly worse at 69.7%. The result rests on only 115.5 minutes of audio from 58 children, and the " ] ] ;
