<urn:slr:article:6de639c98328> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Language and communication impairments are considered one of the core features of autism spectrum disorder (ASD). Quantifying the language atypicalities in autism is a challenging task. Prior works have explored acoustic, and text-based features to assess children’s language and communicative behaviours and have shown their relevance in the diagnosis of autism. In this work, we explore the semantic and pragmatic language features in children with autism (CwA) to understand their significance in the diagnosis of autism. We use natural language processing (NLP) and machine learning (ML) techniques to automatically extract relevant features and detect the existence of speech behaviours such as echolalia, semantic coherence, repetitive language, etc. We further analyse their correlation with the clinical diagnosis of autism. We conducted validation experiments on the transcripts of 76 children (35 ASD and 41 TD) extracted from the CHILDES databank. Our analysis shows that the semantic and pragmatic language features are representative candidates for autism diagnosis and are found to complement the syntactic and lexical features in the classification of CwA with an accuracy of 94%. Further, these features being more coherent and relatable to the standard diagnostic tools improves the interpretability of the diagnostic predictions made using speech signals." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Narayan V; Shukla J (2023). SPASHT: Semantic and Pragmatic Speech Features for Automatic Assessment of Autism. *ICASSP 2023 - 2023 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processin*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Narayan V",
        "Shukla J" ;
    dcterms:title "SPASHT: Semantic and Pragmatic Speech Features for Automatic Assessment of Autism" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_c6a4ea7b" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2023 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participant selection and spectrum representativeness" ;
                            slr:justification "Thirty-five autistic children inherited from three corpora, with no sex, IQ, language level or severity data and no selection criteria applied by these authors. The age span from 12 months to 8 years is not a developmental design but a pooling artefact, since the youngest band contains no comparison children." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Confounder control" ;
                            slr:justification "None attempted. Age, conversation partner, session length, transcription convention and corpus identity all co-vary with diagnosis to some degree, and every feature is a language measure in children, where developmental stage dominates. Count features are length-normalised, which handles one component; nothing addresses the others." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Handling of missing data and attrition" ;
                            slr:justification "Speaker information was separated and transcripts cleaned; unintelligible units were retained as a feature rather than discarded, which is behaviourally defensible though it lets transcription-quality differences between corpora enter the model. No attrition or completeness accounting is reported, and no per-corpus data-quality comparison is made." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Comparison group adequacy" ;
                            slr:justification "Typically developing children from two of the three corpora only, so the comparison group is absent from one entire source and from the youngest age band. The Eigsti corpus contains a developmental delay group - the material for the differential comparison the paper's own conclusion identifies as needed - and it is not used." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Measurement standardisation" ;
                            slr:justification "Three different elicitation paradigms pooled - 30-minute free play, 10-minute parent-child play, and natural mother-child interaction with infants - with different partners and transcription conventions. The paper's own terminology for the partner drifts between therapist, trainer and parent, while child-partner discourse coherence is among the top-contributing features." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Index test definition and prespecification" ;
                            slr:justification "Feature elimination by SHAP from 77 to 63 appears to precede the split, and six classifier families were compared with the best retained. No prespecified operating point. Hyperparameter search is correctly nested within the training data." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reference standard quality" ;
                            slr:justification "None described. Labels are inherited binary clinical status from three independently assembled corpora with no instrument, criteria, clinician or era reported, and no acknowledgement that ascertainment must differ across an age span of 12 months to 8 years." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome measurement blinding" ;
                            slr:justification "Feature extraction and classification are fully automated from transcripts; no human rating enters the pipeline, and the transcripts themselves were produced independently of this study." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Preprocessing and feature selection nesting" ;
                            slr:justification "SHAP-driven elimination from 77 to 63 features described on the initially extracted set with no nesting statement - the regime, at 76 samples and 77 candidate features, in which unnested selection produces the largest optimism." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance estimation and chance level" ;
                            slr:justification "A majority-classifier baseline is computed and reported as the first row of both results tables with improvements quantified against it - better practice than most of this corpus - and four metrics are given for all six classifiers and all seven feature configurations. But no confidence intervals, no AUC, no confusion matrix, and a test set of 17 items in which one child is 5.9 percentage points, so the headline margin over the next configuration is a single child." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data splitting and leakage control" ;
                            slr:justification "A single fixed split with no cross-validation of the reported results. The unit is most plausibly the child, which avoids within-child leakage, but the split is random across three heterogeneous pooled corpora one of which contains autistic children only, so corpus identity is available as a shortcut and no leave-one-corpus-out check is performed." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Sample size adequacy for the modelling" ;
                            slr:justification "Seventy-six children, 77 candidate features, six classifier families compared, and a 17-item test set. The authors do not identify sample size as a limitation, though it constrains every comparison the paper makes." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reporting of results and uncertainty" ;
                            slr:justification "Twenty-eight complete metric rows across two tables with a majority baseline - thorough ablation reporting - but no uncertainty quantification of any kind on a 17-item test set, and no confusion matrix or AUC." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Completeness of methods reporting" ;
                            slr:justification "Every feature is named and defined in a table, including the BERT-cosine coherence construction and the TF-IDF unexpected-word measure; the tools, the feature counts before and after elimination, the classifier family and kernel, and the grid-search-with-5-fold-CV procedure are all stated. Missing: code, the split and seed, the identity of the 63 retained features, the per-corpus composition of the split, and the BERT variant. The stated 90:10 split is inconsistent with the reported accuracies." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Ethics, consent and conflicts" ;
                            slr:justification "No ethics statement, no reference to the source studies' approvals or to CHILDES conditions of use, and no competing-interests declaration. Funding is acknowledged in full. Defensible in substance for secondary analysis of a public corpus, but all are absent as reporting elements." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data, code and protocol availability" ;
                            slr:justification "The source corpora are public through CHILDES and are cited to their originators, so the inputs are available. No code, no split specification, no preregistration." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Acknowledgement of limitations" ;
                            slr:justification "No limitations section. The only acknowledged constraint is inconsistency in datasets used across the literature, which motivates the majority-classifier comparator. Nothing about the corpus-label confound, the age imbalance, the unnested feature selection, the classifier search, or the 17-item test set." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No cross-modal fusion, and the absence is conspicuous for a paper at a speech-signal-processing venue: the introduction cites acoustic findings on pitch range and variability in autistic children, the abstract refers to 'diagnostic predictions made using speech signals', and the title names speech features - yet the study operates entirely on transcripts and computes nothing acoustic. What it does instead is a careful within-modality fusion across three linguistic levels, and reports the ablation in full: syntactic complexity alone 70%, part-of-speech frequencies alone 82%, semantic-pragmatic features alone 82.3%, the last two combined 88.2%, and all three 94.1%. That progression is the study's real contribution and is more informative than a single accuracy figure, because it establishes that the semantic-pragmatic family carries as much signal alone as the lexical family and that the three are complementary rather than overlapping - the specific question the authors identified as unanswered in the prior literature. The caution is that the ablation shares its data, its unnested feature selection and its 17-item test set with the headline result, so the ordering is more reliable than the individual numbers." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-108" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "An entirely software-based pipeline, well specified for its scale. CLAN, the standard CHILDES analysis toolkit, supplied the morphosyntactic baseline features. L2SCA, an established second-language syntactic complexity analyser, supplied the 23 syntactic complexity measures. BERT embeddings with cosine similarity supplied the two discourse-coherence measures. TF-IDF vectors supplied the unexpected-word count. An AutoML tool was used to compare classifier families, and SHAP for attribution and for feature elimination. Using off-the-shelf, community-standard tools throughout is appropriate and makes the pipeline reconstructible in outline, though the specific BERT variant is not named and no code is released. Notably absent, given publication at ICASSP and repeated reference to 'speech signals' and 'speech features', is any acoustic analysis: the study operates entirely on transcripts, and the prosodic properties the introduction cites from prior work - increased pitch range and variability in autistic children - are not measured." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-108" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The study's strongest dimension, and interpretability is treated as the objective rather than as a diagnostic afterthought. Three layers support it. The feature set is specified against ADOS, so every measure has a clinical referent and the model's inputs are quantities a clinician would recognise - echolalia, discourse coherence, initiative in asking questions, vocabulary extent - rather than embeddings or spectral coefficients. The feature-family ablation quantifies what each level of linguistic description contributes, which is itself an interpretive result: semantic-pragmatic features alone reach 82.3%, matching part-of-speech features, and the families combine additively to 94.1%. And SHAP attribution identifies which individual features drive predictions, with the findings then checked against independent literature rather than asserted - the number of distinct word roots emerges as the largest contributor and is linked to prior work on vocabulary extent in autistic children, while the prominence of unexpected words and of both discourse-coherence measures is connected to evidence that autistic children show reduced global coherence in conversation. The authors' claim that these features are 'coherent and understandable to non-experts as well' is defensible. Two limits: no error analysis or per-child examination is performed, and no check is made on whether the top features differ systematically across the three source corpora - which, given that one corpus supplies only autistic children, is the analysis that would distinguish a linguistic finding from a corpus artefact." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-108" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Conversational language, targeted at the pragmatic and semantic level rather than the acoustic or syntactic one, and specified against a clinical instrument rather than by exploration. The design principle is stated plainly: features were extracted 'following the standard diagnostic tool for ASD - Autism Diagnostic Observation Schedule', and the resulting measures map onto behaviours ADOS actually rates - echolalia, the ability to sustain back-and-forth interchange, asking questions, building upon statements, use of pronouns. Operationally this yields nine semantic-pragmatic measures: mean length of utterance in morphemes, number of distinct word roots, questions initiated by the child, immediate phrase repetition, coherence between the child's own consecutive utterances, coherence between the partner's and the child's utterances, echolalia as immediate repetition of the partner's bigrams, proportion of unintelligible units, and unexpected word use. Two design decisions are worth noting. Echolalia and child-partner coherence are inherently dyadic, defined by the relationship between what the partner said and what the child said next - so unlike almost every other behavioural measure in this corpus, these features encode interaction rather than individual output. And unintelligibility is retained as a feature rather than discarded as noise, which is behaviourally defensible since articulation and intelligibility are clinically relevant, though it also means transcription-quality differences across corpora enter the model." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-108" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, macro precision, macro recall and macro F1, reported for every one of six classifiers and every one of seven feature-set configurations, with a majority classifier as the first row of both tables. Macro averaging suits the near-balanced 35:41 split, and including the majority baseline as a named comparator - with improvements quantified against it - is better practice than most studies charted here, particularly given the authors' explicit rationale that inconsistency in the datasets used across the literature makes cross-study comparison unreliable. The gaps are nonetheless serious at this sample size. No confidence interval is reported anywhere, and since every accuracy is an exact multiple of 1/17 the test set contains 17 items, so one child is 5.9 percentage points and the difference between the headline 94.1% and the next-best 88.2% is a single child. No confusion matrix, no AUC, and no sensitivity or specificity named as such. The majority-classifier figure of 56.4% also does not correspond to the 53.9% implied by the overall class ratio, so it appears to be computed on a particular split, which is not stated." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-108" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two directions, both apt: identify autism severity from speech, and explore differential diagnosis using speech-based analysis. The second is the more important and is unusually actionable, because the Eigsti corpus the study already uses contains a developmental delay group alongside autism and typical development. Distinguishing autistic children from children with developmental delay on pragmatic features - rather than from typically developing children on measures dominated by general language level - is the clinically meaningful version of this study's claim, and the material for it was in hand. Not proposed, and needed: leave-one-corpus-out validation, which the three-corpus structure makes available and which would test whether the model generalises across settings rather than learning corpus identity; adjustment for age and language level; and nesting of feature selection." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-108" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Almost nothing at the individual level. Table 1 reports, per corpus, the number of autistic and typically developing participants and an age range: Eigsti 16/16 aged 3-6, Nadig 13/25 aged 3-7, Flusberg 6/0 aged 12-26 months. No sex, IQ, language level, socioeconomic status, race or ethnicity, comorbidity or severity is given for any child, and no group means or matching statistics of any kind are reported. Three consequences follow, all unaddressed. Because the pooled age range spans 12 months to 8 years while the comparison group is confined to 3-7 years, the two groups differ in age distribution by construction - and every feature in the study is a language measure, which in children is dominated by developmental stage. Mean length of utterance and number of distinct word roots, the latter identified by SHAP as the single largest contributor to the model's predictions, are canonical expressive-language milestones that change dramatically between 12 months and 8 years, so an age difference between groups is not a nuisance covariate here but a direct alternative explanation for the headline result. And without IQ or language-level data there is no way to separate an autism-specific pragmatic signature from a general language-delay one, which is precisely what the unused developmental delay group in the Eigsti corpus could have tested." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-108" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'Children with autism (CwA)' is used throughout as the working abbreviation, alongside 'typically developing (TD)' and 'neurotypical', which is a more current pairing than the 'normal' contrast used elsewhere in this corpus. The clinical framing is nonetheless firmly deficit-based and in one respect dated: autism is characterised by 'repetitive and stereotypic behaviours and impaired social and communicative skills collectively called the triads of impairments', a formulation superseded by DSM-5's two-domain structure a decade before this paper. Language differences are described as 'language and communication impairments', 'language atypicalities' and 'aberrant receptive and expressive language development', and the targeted behaviours are 'stereotypic language behaviours'. There is no neurodiversity framing and no autistic-community involvement. Against this, the paper's motivating concern is a reasonable one about assessment rather than about people - that quantifying behavioural traits 'is cumbersome, and the efficacy of such methods primarily depends on the evaluators' expertise' - and its stated goal is interpretable measurement that a non-expert could follow, which is a service orientation rather than a replacement one." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-108" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three different paradigms pooled as though equivalent. Eigsti recorded 30-minute free play sessions; Nadig recorded approximately 10 minutes of parent-child free play; Flusberg recorded natural mother-child interaction in infants and toddlers. The conversation partner is not held constant and the paper's own terminology drifts - the echolalia feature is defined by repetition of bigrams 'originally spoken by the therapist', the discourse-coherence feature is described as similarity 'between BERT embeddings of consecutive sentences of therapist and child', SHAP results refer to 'trainer-child (t-c) discourse', and the corpus descriptions say the partner was a parent or the mother. Whoever the partner was, they are a live participant whose own language shapes every child-partner feature, and pragmatic behaviour in a child differs systematically with whether the interlocutor is a parent or a clinician. Since child-partner discourse coherence ranks among the top contributing features, this is not a peripheral concern. The countervailing strength is the paradigm's ecological character: these are unstructured natural conversations rather than elicited tasks, so the pragmatic behaviours the features target - taking initiative, building on statements, sustaining a topic - had a genuine opportunity to occur." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-108" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Absent as a described procedure. The paper's entire statement on this point is that it 'considered the binary classification problem of classifying a child as having ASD or not, as all three datasets had binary labelling of the clinical status' - so the labels are inherited from three separately assembled research corpora with no account of how autism was diagnosed in any of them, by whom, against which criteria or in which era. Given that the Flusberg corpus records children aged 12-26 months, its labels must derive from a very different ascertainment process from corpora recruiting 3-7 year olds, and pooling them without comment means the target variable is heterogeneous in an unexamined way. ADOS is invoked repeatedly, but as the source of the behavioural constructs the features are designed to mirror - echolalia, back-and-forth interchange, building on statements - rather than as an instrument administered to these children. That is a legitimate and rather elegant use of a diagnostic instrument as a specification for feature engineering, and it should not be confused with having a reference standard." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-108" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The stated objectives are threefold: to automatically extract semantic and pragmatic language features including echolalia, semantic coherence and repetitive language usage; to compare them systematically and identify the most significant; and to build an interpretable assessment system on that basis. The gap the paper identifies in the literature is specific and fair - prior work on semantic-pragmatic features for autism has examined individual measures such as echolalia or pronoun usage in isolation, and 'the cumulative effect of the features and how they complement the lexical, syntactic and other text-based features in the diagnosis of autism has not been analysed'. Findings: the best classifier, an RBF SVM on all 63 retained features, reached 94.1% accuracy with 94% macro F1 against a majority-classifier baseline of 56.4% accuracy and 32% macro F1. The feature-set ablation is the substantive result and is reported in full: morphosyntactic baseline 70.59%, syntactic complexity alone 70%, part-of-speech frequencies alone 82%, semantic-pragmatic features alone 82.3%, part-of-speech plus semantic-pragmatic 88.2%, and all three families 94.1%. So the semantic-pragmatic set matches the lexical set on its own and the three families are complementary rather than redundant, which is exactly the claim the study set out to test. SHAP attribution places the number of distinct word roots first, with unexpected words and both child-child and child-partner discourse coherence in the top ten, and the authors relate each to prior findings on vocabulary extent and on global coherence in autistic conversation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-108" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Published 2023 by the Human-Machine Interaction Lab at IIIT Delhi, funded by an Indian government Science and Engineering Research Board start-up grant with partial support from a TCS Foundation centre and the Infosys Centre for Artificial Intelligence. The framing is the standard objectivity argument - autism diagnosis depends on behavioural assessment because 'the unavailability of genetic tests or biological markers' leaves no alternative, and 'quantifying these behavioural traits is cumbersome, and the efficacy of such methods primarily depends on the evaluators' expertise'. What distinguishes the paper's positioning is the specificity of the gap it claims: prior computational work on semantic-pragmatic language in autism has examined individual features such as echolalia or pronoun use in isolation and 'focused only on smaller feature sets', so 'the cumulative effect of the features and how they complement the lexical, syntactic and other text-based features in the diagnosis of autism has not been analysed'. That is a well-identified and genuinely open question, and the feature-family ablation is a direct answer to it. The paper's other distinctive commitment is to interpretability as a design constraint rather than a reporting nicety - features are specified against ADOS so that predictions can be explained in clinically recognisable terms - which puts it in the same small group as the joint-attention and facial-complexity studies charted here. For this review its main value is as the corpus's clearest demonstration that pragmatic language measures carry as much discriminative signal as lexical ones and combine with them additively; its main caution is that this was established on three pooled corpora, one of which contains autistic children exclusively." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-108" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No study setting: this is a computational reanalysis conducted at IIIT Delhi on transcripts collected years earlier in North American research laboratories. The three source settings are described only briefly and differ substantially - 30-minute free play sessions in one corpus, roughly 10 minutes of parent-child play in another, and natural mother-child interaction with 12-26 month olds in the third. That heterogeneity is the study's central unexamined problem, because it is not random with respect to the label: one corpus supplies autistic children exclusively. Any systematic difference between the corpora - transcription conventions, how disfluency and unintelligibility were marked, who the partner was, how long the session ran, what era the recording belongs to - is therefore partially aligned with diagnosis. Length-dependent features are normalised over conversation length, which handles the most obvious component, but transcription and partner effects are not addressable by normalisation and are not discussed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-108" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "One fixed-length vector per child summarising an entire conversational session, comprising 45 part-of-speech tag frequencies, 23 syntactic complexity measures and 9 semantic-pragmatic measures, reduced from 77 to 63 by SHAP-guided elimination that removed non-informative tags such as SYM and MD and an um-propagation artefact. Count-based features - echolalia, unintelligible units, unexpected words - are normalised over conversation length, which is a necessary and correctly applied step given that source sessions range from about 10 to 30 minutes. Two of the features are computed with contextual embeddings rather than surface statistics: child-child and child-partner discourse coherence are cosine similarities between BERT representations of consecutive sentences, which is a reasonable operationalisation of topic maintenance and is more sensitive than lexical overlap would be. The representation discards temporal structure beyond that local coherence, so trajectory across a session - whether a child warms up, disengages, or shifts topic progressively - is not represented." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-108" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The weakest dimension, and the problems compound. The reported scheme is a single 90:10 train-test split with 5-fold cross-validation used inside the training data only for SVM hyperparameter selection - which is the correct placement for hyperparameter tuning and is stated clearly. But three issues sit above it. First, the arithmetic does not match the description: every reported accuracy is an exact multiple of 1/17 (64.7, 70.59, 76.5, 82.3, 88.2, 94.1 per cent), which fixes the test set at 17 items, roughly 22% of 76 rather than the stated 10%, and the discrepancy is unexplained. Whatever the intended design, a 17-item test set means one child is worth 5.9 percentage points, so the headline 94.1% is 16 of 17 correct and its margin over the next-best configuration is one child - a difference no confidence interval is offered for and none would support. Second, SHAP-based elimination from 77 to 63 features is described as performed on the initially extracted set with no indication of nesting inside the split, so the retained feature set was chosen with sight of the evaluation data; on 76 samples and 77 candidate features this is exactly the regime in which unnested selection produces substantial optimism. Third, six classifier families were compared and the best retained, with a 30-point spread between the worst and best, so the reported figure is the maximum of a search. Beneath all of this lies the pooling problem, which no amount of correct splitting would fix: because the Flusberg corpus contributes autistic children only and from a much younger age band, corpus identity partially predicts diagnosis, and a random split across pooled corpora cannot separate a linguistic signature from a corpus signature. The corrective design is available in the data and would be far more informative - leave-one-corpus-out validation, which would show directly whether features learned on Eigsti and Flusberg transfer to Nadig and vice versa, together with nesting the SHAP elimination and reporting the ablation ordering rather than point accuracies." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-108" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Seventy-six children assembled from three independent corpora in the CHILDES databank: Eigsti (16 autistic, 16 typically developing, aged 3-6, 30-minute free play), Nadig (13 autistic, 25 typically developing, aged 3-7, approximately 10 minutes of parent-child free play) and Flusberg (6 autistic, no comparison children, aged 12-26 months, natural mother-child interaction), giving 35 autistic and 41 typically developing overall. The composition matters more than the totals. Flusberg contributes autistic children only, so corpus membership is partially informative about diagnosis by construction, and it contributes them from an age band - infancy and early toddlerhood - that no comparison child occupies. The three corpora also differ in session length by a factor of three, in conversation partner, and in transcription era and convention. The paper notes that the Eigsti corpus contains a developmental delay group alongside autism and typical development, but that group is not used. No child-level information beyond corpus, group and corpus-level age range is reported anywhere." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-108" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "There is no limitations section, and the omissions matter more than in most studies of this type because several are specific to the pooling strategy. The three corpora differ in age band, session length, conversation partner and transcription convention, and - decisively - the Flusberg corpus contributes 6 autistic children and no comparison children, so corpus membership is partially predictive of diagnosis and corpus-specific artefacts are available to the classifier as diagnostic cues. The autistic group therefore extends to 12 months while the comparison group starts at 3 years, in a study whose features are language measures and whose top SHAP contributor is the number of distinct word roots - a canonical developmental index. Feature elimination by SHAP from 77 to 63 features is described before the train-test split with no statement of nesting, so the retained set saw the evaluation data. Six classifiers were compared and the best reported. All accuracies are multiples of 1/17, implying a 17-item test set rather than the 7 or 8 that a 90:10 split of 76 children would give, and no confidence interval accompanies a comparison in which one child moves accuracy by 5.9 points - so the 94.1% versus 88.2% difference between the best model and the next is one child. No demographic or cognitive data exist for any participant. And the Eigsti corpus's developmental delay group, which would have supported the differential comparison the conclusion identifies as needed, was present in the source material and not used. What the paper does do well on the evaluation side is report a majority-classifier baseline as a named comparator in both results tables, which is better practice than most of this corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-108" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "ICASSP 2023, the IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing - the principal venue for speech signal processing, and a slightly incongruous home for a study that performs no acoustic analysis at all. The paper operates entirely on transcripts, computing lexical, syntactic and semantic-pragmatic measures, while the abstract refers to 'diagnostic predictions made using speech signals' and the introduction reviews acoustic findings on pitch range and variability that the study does not build on. The four-page format accounts for the absence of a limitations section, an ethics statement and a competing-interests declaration, though not for the unexplained mismatch between the stated 90:10 split and the 17-item test set implied by the reported accuracies." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-108" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Index terms: speech analysis, autism spectrum disorder, human-computer interaction. All three are broad, and the third is a venue-fit choice rather than a description of the work. The acronym SPASHT is glossed in the abstract as meaning 'unambiguous', a deliberate reference to the paper's interpretability claim. Terms carried through the body: semantic and pragmatic features, echolalia, discourse coherence, repetitive language, stereotypic language behaviours, morphosyntactic features, syntactic complexity, CHILDES, CLAN, L2SCA, SHAP, majority classifier, interpretability." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-108" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Six classifier families compared by an AutoML tool - majority baseline, k-nearest neighbours, logistic regression, random forest, gradient boosting and support vector machine - with an RBF-kernel SVM retained on the basis of best accuracy and its C and gamma tuned by grid search with 5-fold cross-validation. The choice of simple, interpretable models is appropriate to 76 samples and 63 features and is consistent with the paper's stated priority of interpretability over raw performance. Two aspects of the comparison need stating. Selecting the best of six families on the same small evaluation set means the reported 94.1% is the maximum of a search, and the spread across families is wide (64.7% to 94.1%), so the choice is doing real work. And SHAP serves a dual role - as the feature-elimination criterion reducing 77 features to 63, and as the post-hoc attribution method interpreting the final model - which is efficient but means the interpretation is being read off a feature set that SHAP itself selected on the full data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-108" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Better than most conference papers of this length. Reproducible: the three source corpora are public, named and cited with their participant counts and age ranges; every one of the 77 features is named with a definition in a two-column table, including the exact construction of the BERT-cosine coherence measures and the TF-IDF basis of unexpected words; the normalisation of count features over conversation length is stated; the tools are named; the reduction from 77 to 63 features and its SHAP basis is described; the classifier family, kernel and the grid-search-with-5-fold-CV procedure for C and gamma are given; and both the six-classifier comparison and the seven-configuration feature ablation are reported with four metrics each. Not available: code, the split or seed, the identity of the 63 retained features, the per-corpus composition of the training and test sets, the BERT variant, and the AutoML configuration. The stated 90:10 split is also inconsistent with the reported accuracies, all of which are exact multiples of 1/17." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-108" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No external cognitive, language or severity measure exists, and the absence is more consequential here than in most studies because language is simultaneously the modality, the feature set and the plausible confound. The study computes mean length of utterance in morphemes and number of distinct word roots - both standard expressive-language indices used clinically to characterise developmental level - and uses them as classifier inputs, with the second identified by SHAP as the dominant predictor. In a sample where the autistic group extends down to 12 months and the comparison group does not go below 3 years, that finding is compatible with the model having learned a developmental-language gradient rather than an autism-specific one. Nothing in the analysis distinguishes the two, because no independent measure of language level exists to adjust for. Severity is likewise absent and is named as future work. The gap also blocks the more interesting question the feature set was built to answer: whether pragmatic atypicality - echolalia, poor discourse coherence, failure to initiate questions - is present over and above general language level, which is the clinically meaningful version of the claim." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-108" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional by inheritance, with one session per child and no temporal analysis. The pooling of corpora spanning 12 months to 8 years creates something that looks like developmental range but is not a developmental design, since age is confounded with corpus and with label. No ethics statement appears, which is defensible for a secondary analysis of a public research corpus but is still an omission - the CHILDES databank operates under its own conditions of use and no reference to the source studies' approvals is made. Funding from an Indian government start-up research grant with partial industry-linked centre support is acknowledged; no competing-interests statement is provided." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-108" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:study_design "Secondary computational analysis of pooled public child-language corpora; a feature-engineering and ablation study testing whether automatically extracted semantic and pragmatic la…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: ; comparison: 41 typically developing (Eigsti 16, Nadig 25; Flusberg contributes none)" ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "08c10ffc5a5eea7150f131c11f9cfd359f076073a414b1e833cad3f30d4cf075" ;
