    slr:journal "ICASSP 2023 - 2023 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processin" ;
    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "A single fixed split with no cross-validation of the reported results and no repeated splits. All reported accuracies are exact multiples of 1/17, implying a 17-item test set at odds with the stated 90:10 division of 76 children, and the discrepancy is unexplained. No confidence intervals accompany comparisons in which one child is worth 5.9 percentage points. The per-corpus composition of training and test sets is not reported, so it cannot be determined whether the split is balanced across the three heterogeneous sources." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "None described. Labels are inherited from three separately assembled corpora with no diagnostic instrument, criteria, clinician, date or procedure reported for any child, and no acknowledgement that ascertainment must differ between a corpus of 12-26 month olds and corpora of 3-7 year olds. ADOS is used as a specification for feature design, not as an administered instrument." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Seventy-six children pooled from three independently collected corpora with no selection criteria of the authors' own and no participant information beyond corpus, group and corpus-level age range. The pooling is not label-neutral: the Flusberg corpus contributes 6 autistic children and no comparison children, and does so from an age band (12-26 months) that no comparison child occupies, so corpus membership is partially predictive of diagnosis. No sex, IQ, language level, severity or comorbidity is reported for anyone, and the developmental delay group present in one source corpus is not used." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "SHAP-based feature elimination from 77 to 63 features is described before the train-test split with no statement of nesting, so the retained feature set saw the evaluation data. Six classifier families were compared on the same data with a 30-point performance spread and the best retained. Hyperparameter tuning by grid search with 5-fold cross-validation is correctly placed inside the training data, which prevents a worse rating on that specific point." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "high" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<urn:slr:article:7d280d7f3a95> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Detecting individuals with autism spectrum disorder (ASD) remains a challenge due to the resources and specialized professionals needed for accurate diagnosis, particularly for children where time is a critical factor. Early diagnosis of ASD is crucial for improving the quality of life for affected individuals, as it allows for timely intervention and support. In this study, we underscore the importance of artiﬁcial intelligence (AI) in developing innovative diagnostic methods, with the primary objective of creating AI models that assist in identifying users who may have ASD. Although several studies have utilized traditional machine learning (ML) and deep learning (DL) techniques to detect various illnesses, few have focused on detecting ASD using text as input. We employ natural language processing (NLP) techniques combined with AI models, speciﬁcally decision trees, extreme gradient boosting (XGB), k-nearest neighbors algorithm (KNN) as ML models, and bidirectional encoder representations from transformers (BERT) as DL models. The core idea involves extracting tweets from Twitter users through the platform’s API, classifying the texts as written by individuals who claim to have ASD (ASD users) or by those without ASD (non-ASD users). We generated a dataset of 404,627 tweets and used a subset of 90,000 tweets, comprising 45,000 from each classiﬁcation group, for training and testing the models. The results demonstrate a predictive model with an accuracy of over 84% when classifying texts potentially originated from ASD users. This research paves the way for using DL models to enhance the accuracy of detecting and diagnosing ASD in individuals effectively, emphasizing the critical role of AI in advancing early diagnostic methods for better patient outcomes." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Rubio-Martín S; García-Ordás MT; Bayón-Gutiérrez M; Prieto-Fernández N; Benítez-Andrades JA (2023). Early Detection of Autism Spectrum Disorder through AI-Powered Analysis of Social Media Texts. **." ;
    dcterms:creator "Bayón-Gutiérrez M",
        "Benítez-Andrades JA",
        "García-Ordás MT",
        "Prieto-Fernández N",
        "Rubio-Martín S" ;
    dcterms:title "Early Detection of Autism Spectrum Disorder through AI-Powered Analysis of Social Media Texts" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_0e476686" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2023 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participant selection and spectrum representativeness" ;
                            slr:justification "Two hundred and twenty-one accounts found by keyword search of public biographies, with no demographic, cognitive or severity information for anyone. Self-disclosure of autism on social media selects a narrow and self-organised slice of the population - verbally fluent, digitally engaged, frequently diagnosed in adulthood - and nothing characterises which slice." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Confounder control" ;
                            slr:justification "No covariate is known so none can be controlled. The specific uncontrolled confound is topic: the positive class was assembled by searching for autism keywords, autism-related vocabulary was not removed from the tweets, and no topic analysis was run, so subject matter is fully confounded with the label. Author identity is a second uncontrolled factor, since the split does not separate writers." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Comparison group adequacy" ;
                            slr:justification "Thirty-one users whose selection is never described, unmatched on any attribute and unscreened for undisclosed autism, supplying the entire 45,000-tweet negative class at roughly 1,450 tweets each. The negative label means 'did not state it publicly', not 'is not autistic'." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Handling of missing data and attrition" ;
                            slr:justification "Four cleaning steps are stated clearly and removed 60% of the raw corpus (1,014,723 to 404,627 tweets) - duplicates, retweets, incompletely extracted tweets and cross-platform auto-posts. The reduction is appropriately reported in aggregate but is not broken down by user or group, and how the 90,000-tweet balanced subsample was drawn from the 404,627 is not described." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reference standard quality" ;
                            slr:justification "Unverified self-disclosure in a public biography, with no diagnosis, instrument or verification, and an undescribed, unscreened negative class. The manual exclusion of organisations, advocates and family members is careful and well documented, but it improves the precision of a keyword search rather than establishing a diagnosis." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Index test definition and prespecification" ;
                            slr:justification "Best of four models compared on the same split, with grid-searched hyperparameters and no separate validation set. The text representation given to the three classical models is never stated, so the model-family comparison cannot be interpreted." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome measurement blinding" ;
                            slr:justification "Classification is automated and does not consult labels at inference; labels were assigned from profile text before any modelling." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Measurement standardisation" ;
                            slr:justification "There is no elicitation protocol at all: the behavioural sample is whatever each user chose to post over five years, unconstrained in topic, audience, length, purpose or timing, spanning both the 140- and 280-character limits. Nothing about the measurement is standardised across users or classes." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Preprocessing and feature selection nesting" ;
                            slr:justification "No feature selection is described, but no text representation is described either, and the grid search over classical-model hyperparameters had no validation set separate from the reported test data. No autism-related vocabulary was masked, so the most obvious confound was left in the input." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Sample size adequacy for the modelling" ;
                            slr:justification "Ninety thousand tweets is ample for the models used, but the effective sample is 252 writers and, for the negative class, 31 - which is the number that governs generalisation. The apparent statistical power of a 22,500-tweet test set is illusory given that it contains no new authors." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data splitting and leakage control" ;
                            slr:justification "A single random 75/25 split over tweets, not authors. Each of the 31 comparison users has roughly 1,090 of their own tweets in training and 360 in test; the model can succeed by recognising individual writing styles rather than any autism-related property. No performance estimate exists for an unseen writer." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance estimation and chance level" ;
                            slr:justification "One random split with no cross-validation, no repetition, no confidence interval and no chance baseline (50% at the tweet level). Full confusion matrices are reported, which is a real strength and exposes the decision tree's near-degenerate behaviour, but sensitivity and specificity are never computed and no user-level metric is given." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reporting of results and uncertainty" ;
                            slr:justification "Full confusion matrices for all four models are published, which is better practice than accuracy alone and is what makes the decision tree's degenerate behaviour visible. But no confidence intervals, no chance baseline, no sensitivity or specificity computed from the counts, no AUC, and no user-level result." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Completeness of methods reporting" ;
                            slr:justification "Data collection is well described - six keywords, three manual exclusion rules with examples, four cleaning steps, date range, counts before and after - and model hyperparameters and the BERT checkpoint are given. But the selection of the 31 comparison users, the text representation for the classical models, BERT's fine-tuning regime and any random seed are all absent." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Acknowledgement of limitations" ;
                            slr:justification "Two limitations stated, both about label truthfulness, and both valid. Nothing about the tweet-level split, the 31-user negative class, topic confounding, the absent demographics, the missing user-level metric, or the mismatch between a paper titled 'Early Detection' and a dataset of adult social-media users." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Ethics, consent and conflicts" ;
                            slr:justification "No ethics approval, no ethics committee, no consent, and no discussion of the ethics of training an autism classifier on identifiable people's public health disclosures harvested without their knowledge. The only privacy measure is redacting username, location and date of birth from two example biographies 'in compliance with Twitter's policies' - a platform-terms consideration, not a research-ethics one. No competing-interests statement. Funding is acknowledged." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data, code and protocol availability" ;
                            slr:justification "No code, no data, no preregistration. The dataset consists of identifiable individuals' posts and could not be shared straightforwardly, which is a reason to have addressed data governance rather than to omit it." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The stated objective is to build models 'capable of diagnosing ASD through the analysis of texts posted by users who disclose their condition in their biographies', motivated by the scarcity of specialists and the value of early diagnosis. Three contributions are claimed: a new Twitter dataset of 404,627 tweets from 252 users of whom 221 disclose autism; application of several machine-learning techniques to build predictive models; and comparison of classical machine learning against deep learning. Findings: BERT-base-multilingual-cased, used untuned, reached 84.3% accuracy and F1 0.84, outperforming XGBoost at 71.6% (F1 0.74), decision trees at 61.2% (F1 0.72) and k-nearest neighbours at 60.8% (F1 0.67), all three of which were hyperparameter-tuned by grid search. The authors present this as the paper's headline - that a default deep model beats optimised classical ones - and describe the result as 'quite astonishing'. The confusion matrices they report, to their credit, reveal more than the accuracies do: the decision tree produced 11,229 true positives against just 41 false negatives, meaning it labelled nearly every tweet autistic and achieved 99.6% sensitivity at 22.6% specificity - close to a degenerate classifier, which its 61.2% accuracy and 0.72 F1 conceal. BERT's counts correspond to sensitivity 82.1% and specificity 86.5%. No user-level result is reported for any model." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-102" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Data collection used a Python script against the Twitter developer API, exporting to CSV. Analysis ran in two Jupyter notebooks under Python 3.9 on a single workstation (Intel Core i7-9700K at 3.60 GHz, 32 GB RAM, NVIDIA GeForce RTX 2080). The models are named with their selected hyperparameters - decision tree (max depth 9, min samples leaf 2, min samples split 4), XGBoost (colsample_bytree 0.8, gamma 5, max depth 5, min child weight 1, subsample 0.8), k-nearest neighbours with k = 1 - and the deep model is identified precisely as BERT-base-multilingual-cased, used without tuning by deliberate design. GridSearchCV is named as the search tool. What is conspicuously missing is the representation layer for the three classical models: the paper states that natural language processing techniques were combined with the models but never says how text became numbers - no tokenisation, no bag of words, no TF-IDF, no n-gram range, no embedding - so it is not possible to say what the decision tree, XGBoost and k-nearest neighbours were actually fitted to, which makes their comparison against BERT uninterpretable as a claim about model families." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-102" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Partial, and unusually asymmetric. The data-collection procedure is described well enough to repeat in principle: the six search keywords are listed, the three manual exclusion rules (organisations, advocates, family members) are stated with examples, the four cleaning steps are enumerated, the date range is given, and the tweet counts before and after cleaning are reported. Model configuration is also specified, with selected hyperparameters for all three classical models, the exact BERT checkpoint, the split ratio and the compute hardware. Against this: how the 31 comparison users were chosen is never stated, which alone prevents reconstruction of the dataset; the text representation for the classical models is undescribed; BERT's fine-tuning regime (epochs, learning rate, batch size, maximum sequence length) is not given; no random seed is reported for a study whose single result rests on one random split; and neither code nor data is available. The data could not straightforwardly be shared in any case, since they consist of identifiable individuals' posts, which is a reason to have addressed data governance rather than a reason to omit it." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-102" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Self-disclosure in a public social-media biography, with no clinical component of any kind. Users were located by keyword search over biographies for 'Autism', 'ASD', 'Asperger', 'Aspie', 'Autistic' and 'ActuallyAutistic', and each candidate profile was then reviewed by hand and discarded if it belonged to an organisation or society, to someone describing themselves as an 'ASD advocate', or to a family member using phrasing such as 'father of an ASD kid' or 'mother of an ASD kid'. That filtering is thoughtful, is described in enough detail to repeat, and removes the most obvious sources of false positives - it is the best-executed part of the study. What it cannot do is establish that anyone is autistic. There is no diagnosis, no instrument, no criterion, no date and no verification, and the authors themselves note that biographies may contain false information and that tweets may be written by someone other than the account holder. The comparison label is weaker still and is not described at all: the paper never states how the 31 non-autistic users were found or chosen, whether they were sampled at random or selected for any property, or whether any effort was made to exclude autistic people who simply do not disclose - which, given that undiagnosed and undisclosed autism is common, means the negative class is defined by the absence of a public statement rather than by the absence of the condition. What the models therefore predict is whether a tweet came from an account whose biography contains an autism keyword." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-102" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No cognitive, language, adaptive or severity measure exists, and none could exist given the data source. This matters more than in most studies here because language is the entire modality: a paper classifying people by their writing reports no lexical, syntactic, readability or pragmatic measure of that writing, and no characterisation of how the two corpora differ. Severity appears only in the introduction, where Asperger syndrome is described as 'the one that is labelled as severity 1' and is included among the search keywords, but no severity information is extracted, used or reported. The absence has a specific analytical cost: because self-disclosure of autism on social media is likely to be associated with particular parts of the spectrum - verbally fluent, self-aware, digitally engaged, often diagnosed later in life - the sample is drawn from a narrow and self-selected slice of the autistic population, and nothing in the study characterises which slice." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-102" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2023 IEEE 36th International Symposium on Computer-Based Medical Systems (CBMS), pages 235-238 - a four-page conference paper at a computer-science venue oriented to medical systems. The format explains some of the compression, though not the absence of an ethics statement, which conference length limits do not excuse. The venue also explains the paper's evaluative frame: the result is presented as a model comparison with a winner, in the idiom of a benchmark leaderboard, rather than as a clinical or linguistic finding. Two DOIs appear in the retrieved text (10.1109/CBMS58004.2023.00051 and .00223), an artefact of the proceedings metadata." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-102" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Four models compared: a decision tree, XGBoost, k-nearest neighbours with k = 1, and BERT-base-multilingual-cased. The comparison is framed as a deliberate test - the three classical models were hyperparameter-tuned by grid search while BERT was left entirely at defaults, 'to see if the DL model just with the basic model gives more accurate results than ML models that have been optimised' - and BERT won by 13 accuracy points over the best classical model. Setting up an asymmetric comparison and reporting it transparently is a reasonable framing, but the comparison is not interpretable as stated, because the text representation given to the classical models is never described. BERT builds its own contextual representation from subword tokens; the decision tree, XGBoost and k-nearest neighbours must have been given something, and what that was is unstated. If it was a sparse bag-of-words vector, then the finding is about representation rather than model family, and the paper cannot tell the two apart. Two of the classical results also look near-degenerate rather than merely weak: k = 1 nearest neighbours over a high-dimensional text representation is close to a memorisation baseline, and the decision tree assigned nearly everything to the positive class. Neither observation is drawn out." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-102" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The terminology is the most internally contradictory in this corpus, and the contradiction is instructive. The search keywords include 'ActuallyAutistic' and 'Aspie' - terms coined and used by autistic people themselves, the first being an identity hashtag that exists precisely to distinguish autistic voices from those speaking about autism, and one that carries an explicit rejection of pathologising language. The paper harvests those self-identifications and then describes the people who wrote them as 'ASD patients', as 'individuals who suffer from ASD', and as 'people that suffer from ASD'; the comparison group is 'people that do not suffer from ASD'. Autism is 'a developmental disability' that 'affects the social and interactive skills of those who have it', which 'appears before the age of three and can last a lifetime'. The framing of value is entirely deficit-and-outcome: 'people who are not diagnosed in childhood have a poorer quality of life'. So the study takes its ground truth from a community that names itself in one register and reports its findings in the opposite one, without noticing. There is no neurodiversity framing, no autistic-community involvement, and no acknowledgement that the data subjects are a self-organised community with stated views about how they are described - or about being classified by algorithms trained on their own posts." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-102" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The two stated limitations are correctly identified and concern label validity: users may give false information in their biographies, and tweets may be written by someone other than the account owner. Everything else is unaddressed, and several of the omissions are decisive. The split is random over 90,000 tweets, not over the 252 users, so every author in the test set has the great majority of their own tweets in training; for the comparison class this is extreme, since 31 people supply all 45,000 negative tweets, meaning roughly 1,090 of each comparison user's tweets are available to help classify their remaining 360. A model that learned to recognise 31 individual writing styles would score very well and would have learned nothing about autism. There is no control for topic, in a design where the positive class was assembled by searching for autism keywords and no autism vocabulary was removed from the text. The 31 comparison users are undescribed, unmatched and unscreened, so undisclosed autistic people may sit in the negative class and any group difference in age, nationality, profession or interests is confounded with the label. No demographic data exist. Hyperparameters were grid-searched with no separate validation set. No user-level metric is reported although per-user aggregation is the only form in which such a system could be used. And the framing gap is never acknowledged: a paper titled 'Early Detection', motivated by diagnosing children before age three, is trained entirely on adult social-media users." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-102" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The weakest validation design in the corpus, and the failure is structural rather than marginal. A single random 75/25 split was applied to 90,000 tweets, with the tweet as the unit. Those tweets come from 252 users, so every user is represented on both sides of the split, and the imbalance makes this acute for the negative class: 31 comparison users supply all 45,000 non-autistic tweets, roughly 1,450 each, of which about 1,090 land in training and 360 in test. A model needs only to recognise those 31 writing styles - vocabulary, topics, habitual phrasings, recurring names, professional jargon, regional idiom - to classify the negative test set almost perfectly, and BERT is exactly the kind of model that will do so. The positive class is less concentrated at roughly 200 tweets per user but is leaky by the same mechanism. Nothing in the paper estimates performance on a writer the model has never seen, which is the only quantity relevant to the stated application. Compounding this: there is no cross-validation and no repeated split, so the single reported figure carries no stability estimate; hyperparameters were grid-searched with no validation set distinct from the test set; no chance baseline or confidence interval is given; and no user-level aggregation is performed, so the study never reports the metric its own use case requires. The corrective is simple and would be a genuinely informative experiment: hold out entire users, expand the comparison group well beyond 31 people, mask autism-related vocabulary, and report user-level sensitivity and specificity. On the current design, the 84.3% cannot be interpreted as evidence about autism at all." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-102" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No feature importance for the tree-based models, despite decision trees and XGBoost both supporting it natively and both having been fitted; no attention or probing analysis for BERT; no error analysis; no inspection of misclassified examples; no lexical comparison between the two corpora; no topic modelling. The paper's entire interpretive content is the observation that BERT 'utilizes neural networks to recognize intricate patterns within the input data' and that this 'demonstrates the potential of deep learning models to extract relevant patterns'. The absence is not merely a missed opportunity but the reason the study's central claim cannot be assessed: the difference between a classifier that has learned something about autistic communication and one that has learned that certain accounts discuss autism, or that has memorised 31 people's writing styles, would be visible in even a cursory look at the most predictive tokens, and no such look is reported." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-102" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "There is no setting in the usual sense. No participants were recruited, contacted, assessed or informed; the study consists of harvesting five years of public posts from 252 identifiable Twitter accounts through the developer API and classifying them offline. The research group is at Universidad de Leon in Spain, and the compute is a single desktop workstation. Two features of this design deserve comment for the review. It is the only study in the corpus with no acquisition protocol at all, which means it is free of every acquisition confound that troubles the video and audio studies here - no equipment differences, no examiner effects, no laboratory artificiality - and simultaneously free of every control those protocols provide, since nothing about who wrote what, when, why, or to whom is known or standardised. And it is the only study here whose data subjects are neither aware of nor consenting to their participation, on a topic they disclosed publicly about themselves, which raises governance questions the paper does not engage with at all." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-102" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups of Twitter accounts rather than of assessed people: 221 users whose public biography states that they are autistic and 31 users who do not, totalling 252. The imbalance is severe and consequential. Because the modelled dataset was balanced at the tweet level - 45,000 per class from a cleaned pool of 404,627 - each of the 31 comparison users contributes on average about 1,450 tweets while each of the 221 autistic users contributes about 200, a sevenfold difference in per-user concentration. The entire negative class is therefore the writing of 31 individuals. No user was recruited, contacted or aware of the study; the data are historical public posts from 1 January 2017 to 31 January 2022. Cleaning removed 60% of the raw corpus (1,014,723 to 404,627 tweets) by deleting duplicates, retweets, incompletely extracted tweets and cross-platform auto-posts, which is a substantial and appropriately reported reduction, though it is not broken down by user or by group." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-102" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional in analysis though the data span five years, with no temporal modelling: tweets from 2017 and 2022 are pooled and classified independently, so nothing is learned about stability or change over time, and no test-retest or within-user consistency is examined. Ethics is the study's most serious deficiency and is entirely absent. There is no institutional review board approval, no ethics committee, no research-ethics statement, no consent, and no discussion of the ethics of the enterprise. The data are health-related disclosures made by identifiable individuals about themselves, harvested without their knowledge and used to train a classifier whose stated purpose is to identify people who may be autistic. The only privacy measure described is the redaction of username, location and date of birth from two example biographies reproduced as a figure, done 'in compliance with Twitter's policies' - a platform-terms consideration rather than a research-ethics one. The paper does not consider whether the autistic Twitter community, which is organised and vocal, would regard such a classifier as acceptable; whether a model that infers autism from public text could be used against the people it identifies; or whether public availability implies availability for this purpose. Given that several of the search keywords are terms this community coined for itself, the omission is conspicuous." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-102" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None whatsoever. No age, sex, race or ethnicity, socioeconomic status, education, nationality, language background, comorbidity or medication is available for any of the 252 users in either group, and none is estimated or discussed. Three consequences follow. First, the sample is necessarily adolescents and adults, since Twitter requires account holders to be at least 13 - which sits at odds with a paper titled 'Early Detection' and motivated in its opening pages entirely by the value of diagnosing children before the age of three, a mismatch the text never addresses. Second, no matching of any kind was possible between the 221 autistic and 31 comparison users, and none was attempted, so any demographic difference between the groups - in age, sex, nationality, education, topic interests, or how much they tweet - is fully confounded with the label. Third, the only English-language requirement is stated for the tweets, but the chosen model is BERT-base-multilingual-cased, a multilingual checkpoint, which suggests the corpus may not be uniformly English. The one participant characteristic that is described in detail is not demographic but procedural: the manual exclusion of organisational accounts, self-described advocates and family members from the autistic group, which is careful work and is the paper's strongest methodological passage." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-102" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two directions, both narrow: tune BERT's hyperparameters, since it was deliberately left at defaults and might improve further; and train additional deep and classical models 'to ensure that no high-performing models are overlooked'. Both extend the leaderboard rather than test the finding. The steps that would matter are absent from the list: splitting by user rather than by tweet, masking autism-related vocabulary to separate topic from style, describing and expanding the comparison group, reporting user-level performance, and obtaining any form of clinical validation for the label. So is any consideration of research ethics or of engagement with the community whose posts constitute the dataset." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-102" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy and F1 for each model, together with full confusion matrices giving true positives, false positives, true negatives and false negatives - and the confusion matrices are the most valuable thing in the results, because they expose behaviour the summary metrics hide. The decision tree's counts (tp 11,229, fn 41, tn 2,540, fp 8,690) show a model that assigned almost every tweet to the autistic class: 99.6% sensitivity against 22.6% specificity, reported as 61.2% accuracy and F1 0.72, both of which make it look like a mediocre classifier rather than a near-degenerate one. BERT's counts give sensitivity 82.1% and specificity 86.5%, reasonably balanced. Publishing the raw counts for all four models is good practice and lets a reader recover what the headline numbers conceal. What is missing: sensitivity, specificity, precision and recall are never named or computed even though the counts are present; there is no AUC; there are no confidence intervals; there is no majority-class baseline, though the balanced tweet-level design makes it 50%; and there is no user-level metric, which is the only level at which the proposed application would operate. F1 is also computed with the autistic class as positive, which given the balanced tweet-level design is defensible but is never stated." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-102" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No fusion, and no possibility of it in this design - text alone, one tweet at a time. Two structural forms of integration that the data support were also not used. The first is user-level aggregation: 252 users each contribute hundreds or thousands of tweets, and pooling per-tweet predictions into a per-user decision would be the natural analogue of the multi-stimulus aggregation used to good effect by several eye-tracking studies in this corpus, and is the only form in which a screening application could work, since no one would be assessed on a single tweet. It is neither performed nor discussed, and no user-level metric appears. The second is metadata: the Twitter API returns posting time, reply and thread structure, follower and following counts, engagement figures and interaction partners, all of which are behavioural signals about social communication and all of which are discarded in favour of raw text. The paper's own framing - that social media offers insight into 'the social experiences and challenges faced by individuals with ASD' - points directly at interaction structure, which is precisely what is thrown away." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-102" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Index terms: Machine Learning, Deep Learning, ASD, tweets, NLP. Four of the five are method or data-source labels and the fifth is the condition; no term describes a behaviour, a linguistic construct or a clinical application, which accurately reflects a paper with no behavioural hypothesis. Terms carried through the body: early detection, early diagnosis, social media texts, biographies, ActuallyAutistic, Aspie, hyperparameters search, BERT-base-multilingual-cased, GridSearchCV. The title's 'AI-Powered' is doing rhetorical rather than descriptive work." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-102" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. This is the only included study with no elicitation paradigm: the behavioural sample is whatever 252 people chose to post on Twitter over five years, unconstrained in topic, audience, purpose, length or timing. The consequence that matters most is topic confounding, and it goes unaddressed throughout. The autistic group was assembled by searching biographies for autism-related keywords including 'ActuallyAutistic', a hashtag whose function is to mark autistic people's own commentary on autism; people who identify that way in their profile will frequently tweet about autism, disability, sensory experience, diagnosis, accommodation and advocacy. No preprocessing step removed autism-related vocabulary from the tweets, no topic model or lexical analysis was run, and no ablation tested whether the classifier still works with such terms masked. So the most parsimonious explanation of the 84.3% figure - that BERT is detecting subject matter rather than communication style - is neither tested nor mentioned. The paper's own framing invites this reading, since its introduction speaks of identifying 'linguistic patterns, emotional expressions, and communication styles', which is a style hypothesis, while its method offers nothing that could separate style from topic." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-102" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Published 2023 by the SALBIS and SECOMUCI research groups in the Department of Electric, Systems and Automatics Engineering at Universidad de Leon, Spain, partially supported by a university internal research programme. The framing is the familiar access-and-scarcity argument - diagnosis 'remains a challenge due to the resources and specialized professionals needed', existing methods rest on interviews and questionnaires that 'take too much time' - combined with a strongly stated case for early diagnosis occupying most of the introduction. The positioning within the field is explicitly as a gap-filler: the authors note that prior autism machine-learning work has used images and eye tracking, that 'few have focused on detecting ASD using text as input', and that there is 'a notable gap in research utilizing the information found in Twitter users' biographies'. That gap is real, and social-media language is a legitimate and under-explored modality. What makes the paper a cautionary case for this review is the distance between its framing and its evidence: a study motivated in its opening pages by identifying autistic children before the age of three, using adult self-disclosure on a platform with a minimum age of 13, claiming to 'pave the way' for diagnosis on the basis of a tweet-level classification whose validation design cannot distinguish autism from authorship. It is also the only study in the corpus that builds a health classifier from identifiable individuals' public disclosures with no ethics approval, no consent and no discussion of either." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-102" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Written language produced spontaneously on social media - the only study in this corpus whose behavioural sample is entirely self-initiated public communication rather than behaviour elicited under a protocol. That is genuinely interesting as a modality: it is ecologically valid in a way no laboratory task can be, it is longitudinal by default (five years per user), it costs nothing to collect at scale, and it captures communication in its natural social context with real audiences and real stakes. But the study does nothing to characterise the behaviour it collects. No linguistic construct is named or measured - not lexical diversity, sentence length, pronoun use, sentiment, pragmatic markers, disfluency, figurative language or any of the features the autism-language literature has examined - and no analysis reports how the two corpora differ. The introduction gestures at 'linguistic patterns, emotional expressions, and communication styles' but nothing in the method operationalises any of these. The result is a study in which the behavioural target is left entirely to the classifier to define, with no account of what it found. Given that the positive class was assembled by searching for autism keywords and that no autism vocabulary was removed from the tweets, the behaviour actually being detected may be talking about autism rather than any property of autistic communication." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-102" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "For BERT, subword tokens and contextual embeddings from the multilingual cased checkpoint, applied to one tweet at a time. For the three classical models, unspecified - the paper says natural language processing techniques were used but names no tokenisation, vectorisation or feature scheme, so the representation those models consumed is unknown. In every case the unit is a single tweet treated independently: no thread context, no reply structure, no temporal ordering, no user-level aggregation, and no metadata. The maximum sequence length is not stated, which matters because tweets in the 2017-2022 window span both the 140- and 280-character limits. Nothing was masked or normalised beyond the four corpus-cleaning steps, so hashtags, mentions, URLs, emoji and autism-related vocabulary all remain in the text the models see - the last of these being the reason the results cannot be read as evidence about communication style." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-102" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:study_design "Retrospective classification study on scraped social-media text; a cross-sectional comparison of language produced by self-identified autistic Twitter users against language produc…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: ; comparison: 31" ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "e2c8551a400b3123029973d5a8ba9ae244c9adfec989200b29e1d51242e7c039" ;
