    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "unclear" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "ADOS-2 Module 4 administered by research-certified psychiatrists following a published protocol with published coding criteria - the most standardised behavioural measurement in the review, and the published algorithms were applied at their published cut-offs without modification. But administrator blinding to the established diagnosis is not stated and is unlikely given the setting, every item code is a clinician judgement, and no within-study inter-rater reliability was computed at the item level on which the analysis depends. The six-item model was selected from 32 candidates on the full sample." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "unclear" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Both groups recruited consecutively from the same hospital's outpatient and inpatient services with matched age, sex and education - removing the recruitment asymmetry that affects nearly every other case-control study in this review. But eligibility required IQ above 85 in both groups and excluded comorbidity from both, plus severe positive symptoms from the schizophrenia group, so the sample is the diagnostically clean end of a problem defined by diagnostic ambiguity. The two groups were also recruited in non-overlapping windows about two years apart." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "DSM-5 consensus diagnosis by several psychiatrists with extensive experience in both conditions, drawing on assessment interactions and full psychiatric and medical history, with the explicit statement that neither ADOS-2 nor ADI-R contributed to it. The deliberate independence of reference standard from index test is what makes the study's central finding interpretable." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "All 90 participants received both the reference standard and the index test, with diagnosis preceding ADOS-2 administration in every case. No attrition or indeterminate results are reported. The unit of analysis is the participant throughout and the holdout split partitions participants, so no leakage is possible." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "unclear" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<urn:slr:article:b4ca4bcef771> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Background: The increasing use of social media platforms has given rise to an unprecedented surge in user-generated content, with millions of individuals publicly sharing their thoughts, experiences, and health-related information. Social media can serve as a useful means to study and understand public health. Twitter (subsequently rebranded as “X”) is one such social media platform that has proven to be a valuable source of rich information for both the general public and health officials. We conducted the first study applying Twitter data mining to autism screening. Objective: We aimed to study the feasibility of autism screening from Twitter data and discuss the ethical implications of such models. Methods: We developed a machine learning model to attempt to distinguish individuals with autism from their neurotypical peers based on the textual patterns from their public communications on Twitter. We collected 6,515,470 tweets from users’ self-identification with autism using “#ActuallyAutistic” and a separate control group. To construct the data set, we targeted English-language tweets using the search query “#ActuallyAutistic” posted from January 1, 2014 to December 31, 2022. We encrypted all user IDs and stripped the tweets of identifiable information such as the associated email address prior to analysis. From these tweets, we identified unique users who used keywords such as “autism” OR “autistic” OR “neurodiverse” in their profile description and collected all the tweets from their timelines. To build the control group data set, we formulated a search query excluding the hashtag “#ActuallyAutistic” and collected 1000 tweets per day during the same time period. We trained a word2vec model and an attention-based, bidirectional long short-term memory model to validate the performance of per-tweet and per-profile classification models. We deleted the data set and the models after our analysis. Results: Our tweet classifier reached a 73% accuracy, a 0.728 area under the receiver operating characteristic curve score, and an 0.71 F1-score using word2vec representations fed into a logistic regression model, while the user profile classifier achieved an 0.78 area under the receiver operating characteristic curve score and an F1-score of 0.805 using an attention-based, bidirectional long short-term memory model. Conclusions: We have shown that it is feasible to train machine learning models using social media data to predict use of the #ActuallyAutistic hashtag, an imperfect proxy for self-reported autism. While analyzing textual differences in naturalistic text has the potential to help clinicians screen for autism, there remain ethical questions that must be addressed for such research to move forward and to translate into the real world. While machine learning has the potential to improve behavioral research, there are still a plethora of ethical issues in digital phenotyping studies using social media with respect to user consent of marginalize" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Jaiswal A; Washington P (2024). Using #ActuallyAutistic on Twitter for Precision Diagnosis of Autism. *JMIR Formative Research, 8, Article e52660*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Jaiswal A",
        "Washington P" ;
    dcterms:title "Using #ActuallyAutistic on Twitter for Precision Diagnosis of Autism" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_4b02df7c" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2024 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Handling of missing data and attrition" ;
                            slr:justification "Corpus counts reported completely by group; the location field excluded with the reason given; a minimum-tweet threshold applied for the profile task and stated. Preprocessing is fully specified." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participant selection and spectrum representativeness" ;
                            slr:justification "A self-selected online advocacy community, English-only, on a platform skewed toward younger and higher-socioeconomic users, with no demographic information and no clinical ascertainment. The authors state the representativeness problem in these terms themselves." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Confounder control" ;
                            slr:justification "No age, sex, ancestry or socioeconomic data for either group, and the #ActuallyAutistic community plausibly differs from a general platform sample on all of these. The one confound actively controlled is label-defining vocabulary, removed deliberately." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Comparison group adequacy" ;
                            slr:justification "A daily sample of general English-language Twitter, differing from the positive class in topic, community membership, posting motivation and sampling procedure as well as in putative autism status. The authors address only one contamination direction, bounding it at population prevalence." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reference standard quality" ;
                            slr:justification "Hashtag use plus profile keywords with no clinical validation, and an entirely unscreened control group. Stated as the first limitation and reflected accurately in the paper's own conclusion." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Index test definition and prespecification" ;
                            slr:justification "The preprocessing chain, keyword-removal list, representations, model families, splitting rules and selection procedure are all specified in advance and reported in full. Algorithm selection used cross-validation with a separately held test partition." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Measurement standardisation" ;
                            slr:justification "Text is naturally occurring so there is no elicitation to standardise, and preprocessing is applied uniformly. But the two classes' material was gathered by different sampling procedures, and heavy normalisation removes paralinguistic features that may carry signal." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome measurement blinding" ;
                            slr:justification "Classification is fully automated from text; no human rater is involved at any stage of labelling or inference." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance estimation and chance level" ;
                            slr:justification "Accuracy, AUC and F1 reported for both tasks on near-balanced classes, with no inflation in how they are described. But no confidence intervals despite a corpus that would support tight ones, no calibration, and no positive predictive value at population prevalence - the figure that would demonstrate why the authors' own restriction to cross-sectional use is correct." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data splitting and leakage control" ;
                            slr:justification "User-level splitting for both tasks with the rationale articulated - among the clearest statements of the principle in this review - plus separate validation and test partitions and a minimum-tweet threshold for the aggregated task." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Preprocessing and feature selection nesting" ;
                            slr:justification "The sixteen autism-related tokens that define the label were removed a priori on substantive grounds, closing the target-leakage route that would otherwise have made the task trivial. Algorithm selection used 5-fold cross-validation with the test partition held out." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Sample size adequacy for the modelling" ;
                            slr:justification "6.5 million tweets from 188,596 users, far exceeding what the model families require. Sample size is the one dimension on which this study exceeds every other in the review." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "unclear" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data, code and protocol availability" ;
                            slr:justification "Dataset and models deliberately destroyed after analysis and never shared outside the author team, for stated ethical reasons; code not mentioned. Unavailability is a considered decision rather than an omission, and is explained." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Ethics, consent and conflicts" ;
                            slr:justification "IRB approval 2023-00248 under expedited review, a separate data-governance determination, and measures taken beyond regulatory requirement including encrypted user IDs and anonymised mentions and email addresses. Consent is treated as the primary limitation rather than as satisfied by the public-data exemption, engaged with substantively through the literature, and acted upon by destroying the data and models. Funding and competing interests are not stated in the retrieved text." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reporting of results and uncertainty" ;
                            slr:justification "Accuracy, AUC and F1 for both classifiers, reported consistently across abstract, results and conclusion with no inflation. Missing: confidence intervals, calibration, operating-point sensitivity and specificity, and predictive value at realistic prevalence." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Acknowledgement of limitations" ;
                            slr:justification "Five limitations with developed reasoning, a self-imposed restriction of legitimate use to population level, explicit enumeration of misuse scenarios, and a closing caution against building comparable models without consent and participatory design. The group-definition confound is the substantive omission." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Completeness of methods reporting" ;
                            slr:justification "Scraping tool, both search queries with date range, group-definition criteria, control sampling rate, the full preprocessing chain, the complete keyword-removal list, sentiment tool, representations, model families, splitting rules and proportions for both tasks, and all corpus counts. The pipeline is reconstructible even though the corpus deliberately is not." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None available, and the authors are candid about what follows. There is no age, sex, ancestry, socioeconomic, cognitive or comorbidity information for any of the 188,596 users, and location was dropped as unusable. The limitations section states the resulting representativeness problem directly: restricting to English 'raises concerns about the lack of diversity in the data, where only English-speaking users from higher socioeconomic groups or younger adults are represented in the data set, as they comprise a larger portion of Twitter users'. Two consequences go further than the paper takes them. The #ActuallyAutistic community is widely observed to include a high proportion of late-diagnosed and self-identifying autistic adults, and a higher proportion of women, than clinically ascertained autism samples - so the demographic distance between the two corpora is likely to be substantial and is entirely unmeasurable, meaning that a sex- or age-linked difference in writing style would be indistinguishable from an autism-linked one. And the two groups differ in a way that no demographic variable captures at all: one is defined by participation in a topic-organised community and the other is a background sample of the platform, so their tweets differ in subject matter, audience, posting motivation and network position before any question of authorship arises." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-127" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No instrumentation. snscrape for collection, better-profanity for masking swear words, VADER for lexicon-based sentiment, word2vec and TF-IDF for representation, and an attention-based bidirectional LSTM for user-level classification. The equipment cost of this study is zero and its collection cost is a scraping script - which is precisely why the ethical analysis matters more here than anywhere else in this review. Every other included study is bounded by what a research team can afford to collect from consenting participants; this one is bounded only by what a platform will serve, and the authors' response is to build the model, measure it, describe what it would enable, and destroy it." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-127" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None modelled, though the opportunity was substantial and deliberately declined. The curated corpus retains account creation date, friend count, follower count, tweet timestamp, reply count, retweet count, like count, hashtag lists and posting source, and the authors observe that 'other supporting metadata could be used in the future for network analysis or statistical studies'. Fusing behavioural text with network position and engagement metrics would be a natural extension and would plausibly improve discrimination - which is precisely why not doing it, in a paper that concludes such models should not be built without consent, is consistent rather than an oversight." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-127" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional in analysis but drawing on nine years of longitudinal record, since users' full timelines from 2014 to 2022 were collected - a temporal depth no other included study approaches. That depth is used only descriptively, to show a rising trend in autism-related discussion, and no within-user temporal modelling is attempted. Ethics reporting is by a distance the most thorough in this review. The study holds University of Hawaii Institutional Review Board approval 2023-00248 under expedited review and passed a separate institutional data governance review that deemed it exempt 'due to the inherently public nature of the study data'. The authors then describe measures 'not required by the Institutional Review Board': encrypting user IDs to reduce reidentification risk, and anonymising user mentions and personal information such as email addresses within tweet text. They engage with the consent question substantively rather than resting on the public-data exemption, citing survey evidence that 84% of users were not or only slightly concerned about university research use while observing that 'this leaves a considerable portion of the population who remains concerned', and noting that privacy policies are 'long, with complex legal terms that the users may fail to understand or authorize'. They call for large-scale consent procedures and a regulatory framework for social media data mining. And they act: 'the data set and the model that we built using those data have been deleted. When the data set did exist, it was never shared outside of the original authors.' Funding and competing interests are not stated in the retrieved text." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-127" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None, and this is the study's chief methodological attraction. There is no elicitation, no examiner, no instruction and no observation effect - people wrote what they wanted to write, at their own pace, over nine years. Against the 90-second free-play sample, the 3-minute video and the 40-minute standardised assessment charted elsewhere in this review, a user's full Twitter timeline is an enormous and ecologically valid behavioural record. What the absence of a paradigm removes is any control over what the two groups were writing about, and here the comparison is unfavourable: because the positive class was selected by participation in a hashtag community, its members were writing about autism, diagnosis, accommodation, sensory experience and ableism far more than a background sample would. Removing the explicit autism keyword list attenuates but cannot eliminate this, since topic pervades vocabulary, syntax and stance well beyond a list of sixteen terms. Naturalistic data therefore buys freedom from task effects at the price of losing any guarantee that the two samples are doing the same thing." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-127" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Deliberately partial, with the incompleteness explained rather than concealed. Fully reported: the scraping library, both search queries with their date range, the profile-keyword criterion defining the autism group, the 1,000-per-day sampling rate for controls, the complete preprocessing chain from profanity masking through tokenisation, stop-word removal, stemming and lemmatisation to padding, the full sixteen-item list of removed autism-related keywords, the sentiment tool and the censored-versus-uncensored comparison, the representation methods and model families, the 5-fold cross-validation used for algorithm selection, the 85:15 user-level split for tweet classification with a further 85:15 training-validation division, the 80:20 user split and five-tweet minimum for profile classification, and all corpus counts by group. Not available: the dataset and the trained models, both destroyed, and the code, which is not mentioned. The pipeline is therefore reconstructible and the corpus is not - which is the outcome the authors intend, since re-creating the corpus is the act they argue should not be undertaken without consent." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-127" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The stated objective was 'to study the feasibility of autism screening from Twitter data and discuss the ethical implications of such models' - the ethics being a co-equal aim from the outset rather than an appended caveat, which is unique in this corpus. The technical finding is modest and honestly reported: a tweet-level classifier reached 73% accuracy, AUC 0.728 and F1 0.71 using word2vec representations with logistic regression, and a user-profile classifier reached AUC 0.78 and F1 0.805 using an attention-based bidirectional LSTM. Aggregating a user's full timeline improved discrimination over single tweets, as expected if the signal is a property of a writer rather than of a post. Exploratory analysis found that sentiment polarity was nearly unchanged between the raw and profanity-censored corpora, and that autism-related discussion rose across 2014-2022. The conclusion the authors actually draw is not about accuracy: having demonstrated feasibility, they restrict the legitimate use of such models to 'a broader cross-sectional level' rather than individual prediction, enumerate the misuse scenarios the artefact would enable, delete the data and the models, and caution researchers against building comparable systems without explicit consent and participatory community-centred design. That is the study's contribution and it is a real one." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-127" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: autism, autism spectrum disorder, machine learning, natural language processing, public health, sentiment analysis, social media analysis, Twitter. The set spans population, method and application domain and includes 'public health', which locates the work in population monitoring rather than individual diagnosis - consistent with the conclusion's restriction of legitimate use to the cross-sectional level. Terms carried through the body: digital phenotyping, #ActuallyAutistic, neurodiversity, weak supervision, VADER, word2vec, attention-based BiLSTM, data governance, participatory research, informed consent." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-127" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A sensible staged progression rather than a single architecture. TF-IDF features were first passed through four classical algorithms - support vector machines, naive Bayes, logistic regression and XGBoost - with 5-fold cross-validation on accuracy used to select the best; word2vec representations were then trained with the selected algorithm and evaluated on a fuller metric set; and an attention-based bidirectional LSTM over a self-trained embedding layer was used for the user-level task, with padding and an unknown token to handle variable tweet lengths. The final tweet-level result comes from word2vec with logistic regression, and the profile-level result from the BiLSTM. The modelling is proportionate: a corpus of 6.5 million tweets can support a deep sequence model, and the authors escalate to one only for the aggregated user-document task where sequence structure and attention have a plausible role." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-127" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The most careful terminology in this review, and the only paper here that treats the politics of autism language as part of its subject matter rather than as background. Identity-first language is used throughout - 'millions of individuals are autistic', 'autistic community', 'autistic peers' - alongside 'individuals with autism' in places, and 'neurotypical' rather than 'normal' or 'healthy' for the comparison group. More substantively, the paper explains the origin of its own inclusion criterion in community terms: hashtags such as #AutismMom and #AutismParent 'have represented the perspectives of neurotypical parents, significantly influencing research and policies in this domain', but 'these advocacy groups often overshadow adults with autism, creating a gap in their representation within decision-making processes', and #ActuallyAutistic emerged as 'a paradigm shift ... in the autism rights movement', redirecting attention toward autistic people 'rather than solely focusing on caregivers'. That passage does something no other included study attempts: it recognises that the autism research literature has been shaped disproportionately by parents and professionals rather than by autistic people, and it selects its sample specifically from autistic voices for that reason. The conclusion follows through, calling for 'a more inclusive approach during the model development process that involves the autistic community directly in the ideation and consent processes' and referring to autistic people as a 'marginalized population' whose consent is at issue. The framing is not uniformly neurodiversity-aligned - the introduction still describes autism through misdiagnosis, delayed services and 'negative outcomes later in life' - but the paper is the only one in this corpus to treat the people it studies as a constituency with standing in how they are researched. The published record also shows that the article attracted a formal comment (JMIR Formative Research 2024;8:e59794) and was issued as a corrected version, which indicates that its approach was contested in the literature." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-127" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No study setting exists in the ordinary sense - the data are public posts scraped from Twitter using snscrape, a library chosen because it avoids the need for platform API keys. The absence of a setting is the point: the data are naturally occurring, produced without observation effects, at a scale no laboratory could approach, at essentially zero collection cost. The corresponding costs are equally structural. Nobody knew they were being studied. The two classes were assembled by different procedures - full timeline harvesting for the autism group, daily sampling for controls - so the corpora differ in composition independently of authorship. The platform's user base is skewed by age, language, geography and socioeconomic status in ways the authors acknowledge. And the positive class is defined by membership in a community organised around a topic, so the contrast is between a self-advocacy discourse and general platform conversation rather than between two comparably situated populations." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-127" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Five limitations are stated and the reasoning behind each is unusually developed. There is no clinical validation for any diagnosis, and expert or crowdsourced annotation would help. Hashtag users may be family members, parents, caregivers or advocacy organisations rather than autistic people, though the authors judge this rare given the negative social connotations of using #ActuallyAutistic without a diagnosis. Autistic users who do not self-identify will contaminate the control group, bounded at roughly the population prevalence of under 3%. VADER sentiment may be less accurate than human labelling. And the English-only restriction limits diversity, over-representing younger and higher-socioeconomic users. The authors add a scope restriction that functions as a finding - 'the predictive power of social media is not to be used at an individual level but at a broader cross-sectional level' - and name the consent failure as 'the primary limitation of this study', enumerating misuse scenarios including university admissions, hiring, and government surveillance. What is not addressed is the group-definition confound: the positive class is a community organised around a topic and the negative class is a platform sample, so a classifier could reach 73% by recognising the register of autism self-advocacy rather than any property of autistic language production, and removing sixteen keywords does not close that route. Nor is the structural asymmetry addressed by which the autism group's full timelines were harvested while control posts were sampled daily, so that 17,323 users contribute nearly as many tweets as 171,273 - a difference in how material was selected that is confounded with the label. No demographic data exist for either group in a comparison where the communities plausibly differ in age and sex. No feature analysis is reported for the classifiers, so the interpretive question is left open where it could have been partly answered. And the contamination bound assumes that population prevalence transfers to Twitter's user base and that non-self-identifying autistic users write like non-autistic ones." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-127" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None, and the paper's handling of this is the most precise in the review. The positive label is use of #ActuallyAutistic plus autism-related keywords in the profile description - self-identification within an online community. The authors do not claim otherwise at any point: their own conclusion states that the models predict 'use of the #ActuallyAutistic hashtag, an imperfect proxy for self-reported autism', and their first limitation is that 'while we focused on individuals who self-identified as autistic, there is no clinical validation for their diagnosis'. They then reason about contamination in both directions. Users of the hashtag might be parents, caregivers or advocacy organisations rather than autistic people, which they judge rare 'due to the negative social connotations of using #ActuallyAutistic without a diagnosis' - an appeal to community norms rather than evidence, but an informed one, since the hashtag's function within the autism rights movement is precisely to mark first-person testimony. And autistic users who do not self-identify will contaminate the control group, which they bound at roughly the population prevalence, arguing that 'with autism having a prevalence rate of <3%, the model performance should not degrade by more than 3%' - a bound that assumes population prevalence transfers to Twitter's user base and that non-self-identifying autistic users write like non-autistic ones, neither of which is established. What the paper does not consider is that its label might be a design choice rather than a defect: undiagnosed self-identifying autistic adults are precisely the population an access-oriented screening tool would most want to reach, and treating their self-identification as noise assumes that clinical ascertainment is the only valid criterion in a study whose framing elsewhere questions exactly that assumption." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-127" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The largest sample in this review by an order of magnitude, and the least clinically characterised. The autism corpus comprises 3,137,952 tweets from 17,323 users, identified by their use of #ActuallyAutistic in English-language posts between 1 January 2014 and 31 December 2022 combined with 'autism', 'autistic' or 'neurodiverse' appearing in their profile description, after which all tweets from those users' timelines were collected. The control corpus comprises 3,377,518 tweets from 171,273 users, obtained by a search query excluding the hashtag and sampling 1,000 tweets per day over the same window. No demographic information of any kind exists for either group - no age, sex, ancestry, socioeconomic status, cognitive level or comorbidity - and the location field was discarded as largely missing or uninformative, though the authors note that most of the top 20 stated locations were variants of the United Kingdom. Two structural features of the sample deserve emphasis. The tweet counts are near-balanced while the user counts differ tenfold, because the autism group's entire timelines were harvested while control posts were sampled from the platform's daily stream - so the two classes differ in how their material was selected as well as in who produced it, and this is not discussed. And the autism group is not a clinical population but a self-selected online community organised around a hashtag with an explicit advocacy stance, which the paper describes accurately and which bears on everything downstream." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-127" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No severity, cognitive, adaptive or language measure exists for any user, and none could without linking to clinical records. The corpus retains rich behavioural metadata - account age, friend and follower counts, reply, retweet and like counts, posting source, timestamps - which the authors note could support network or statistical analysis but deliberately do not use. Some of these would function as crude proxies for social engagement and could have supported a gradient analysis of the kind this review has repeatedly found missing; the paper's decision not to pursue them is consistent with its overall restraint about extracting more from the data than it collected consent for." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-127" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "JMIR Formative Research 2024;8:e52660, Original Paper, open access. The record carries two notable annotations: the version retrieved is a corrected version, with the correction statement published at JMIR Formative Research 2024;8:e59349, and a formal comment on the article appears at JMIR Formative Research 2024;8:e59794. A published comment on a paper of this kind indicates that its approach was contested in the literature, which is itself part of the paper's significance for this review - it is the only included study whose ethics generated a documented scholarly exchange. JMIR's formative-research track is intended for feasibility and early-stage work, which fits a study whose conclusion is that the thing is possible and should not be done without consent. The venue's structure produced the ethical-considerations section with its IRB number, data-governance determination and description of measures taken beyond regulatory requirement - a section that has no counterpart anywhere else in this corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-127" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Published 2024 by the Department of Information and Computer Sciences at the University of Hawaii at Manoa. The framing is the standard access argument - limited availability of standard tests, misdiagnosis, delayed services, negative outcomes later in life - applied to a data source of unprecedented scale and zero collection cost, with the stated aim of 'enabling support for communities with limited access to diagnostic resources'. For this review the study matters on four counts, only one of which is its performance. It is the only included study whose behavioural data are naturally occurring and unelicited, at a scale - 6.5 million posts from 188,596 people over nine years - that no laboratory design can approach, which makes it the clearest available test of whether ecological validity and scale can substitute for measurement control. The answer it returns is instructive: 73% accuracy and AUC 0.728, modest against the 90-plus percent figures obtained elsewhere in this corpus under laboratory conditions with clinical ascertainment, and obtained despite the vastly larger sample. It implements two deliberate leakage controls - user-level splitting, with the reasoning stated, and removal of the sixteen autism-related tokens that define its own label - which places it among the more careful studies here on mechanics. It is the only paper in this review to discuss its own label honestly enough to state in its conclusion that the model predicts hashtag use rather than autism. And it is the only paper anywhere in the corpus that argues against the deployment of what it built: having demonstrated feasibility, the authors restrict legitimate use to population-level inference, enumerate the misuse scenarios an individual-level classifier would enable - insurance profiling, university admissions, hiring, government surveillance of citizens - delete the dataset and the models, note that the data were never shared outside the author team, and caution researchers against building comparable systems without explicit consent and participatory community-centred design. Set beside the studies charted nearby that scrape identifiable photographs of children from support pages with no ethics statement at all, or that ship an 'AI-driven assistive diagnostic tool' for at-home use on the strength of a model that recomputes a questionnaire's own scoring rule, this paper is the corpus's clearest demonstration that the ethical questions raised by this literature are tractable when researchers choose to engage with them. The substantive caution that travels with it is that its positive class is a self-selected advocacy community and its negative class a background platform sample, so the 73% has several sufficient explanations and the study does not distinguish them - a limitation the authors partially anticipate by declining to treat the result as a finding about autistic language at all." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-127" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The technical directions named are clinical or crowdsourced annotation to replace hashtag labels, extension beyond English, and use of the retained network metadata. But the direction the paper actually advocates is procedural: 'a more inclusive approach during the model development process that involves the autistic community directly in the ideation and consent processes', supported by research-community work to 'find ways to support large-scale consent procedures' and by 'a regulatory framework for social media data mining'. The paper closes by keeping itself 'as a case study of what is currently possible with publicly available' data rather than proposing a successor system. No other study in this review proposes participatory governance as its principal future work, and none argues against the further development of its own method." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-127" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Careful on the mechanics and unresolved on the substance, which is the reverse of the pattern in most of this corpus. The mechanics are good. Splitting is at user level with the reasoning stated explicitly - tweets from one user must not scatter across training and test, 'potentially leading to overfitting by the model due to learning user-specific patterns' - and applied to both tasks, 85:15 for tweet classification with a further 85:15 training-validation division, 80:20 for profile classification. Algorithm selection used 5-fold cross-validation with a held-out test partition maintained separately. And the sixteen-token removal list closes the most obvious inflation route, since the label is defined by a hashtag whose presence in the text would otherwise make the task a lookup. Together these are more deliberate leakage controls than most studies in this review implement, and the modest performance obtained is consistent with the controls having worked. What remains unresolved is the construct. The positive class consists of users who chose to participate in a hashtag community organised around autism advocacy; the negative class is a daily sample of general platform conversation. These populations differ in topic, audience, community membership, posting motivation, network position and - plausibly, though unmeasurably - in age, sex and geography. A model reaching 73% accuracy on that contrast may be detecting autistic language production, or the discourse register of self-advocacy, or the difference between a topically coherent community and a background sample, and the study provides no evidence separating them. The corpus construction adds a further asymmetry that is not discussed: the autism group's complete timelines were harvested while control tweets were drawn at 1,000 per day, so 17,323 users supply almost as many tweets as 171,273, and the two classes differ in how their material was sampled as well as in who wrote it. The authors reason carefully about label contamination in both directions and bound the control-group error at the population prevalence, but that bound assumes prevalence transfers to Twitter's user base and that non-self-identifying autistic users write like non-autistic ones. The honest reading is that the study demonstrates a detectable difference between two corpora whose separation has several sufficient explanations, and that its authors' decision to treat the result as a feasibility demonstration rather than a finding about autistic language is the correct one." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-127" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "TF-IDF vectors and word2vec embeddings at tweet level; a self-trained embedding layer feeding an attention-based BiLSTM over a concatenation of all of a user's preprocessed tweets at profile level, restricted to users with at least five tweets. The representation is the product of aggressive normalisation - profanity masking, removal of non-alphanumerics, links, mentions and HTML, lowercasing, stop-word removal, stemming and lemmatisation - which strips much of the paralinguistic texture of social media writing (capitalisation, punctuation, emoji, elongation) that might itself carry the signal of interest. The most consequential representational decision is the removal of sixteen autism-related tokens including the hashtag that defines the label, on the explicit ground that they 'could introduce bias and lead to model overfitting'. That is a target-leakage control, correctly reasoned, and it is the same failure mode this review documents in the questionnaire-item studies where the label is an arithmetic function of the features - handled here rather than left in place." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-127" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, AUC and F1 for both classifiers, with 5-fold cross-validation accuracy for algorithm selection: 73% accuracy, AUC 0.728 and F1 0.71 at tweet level; AUC 0.78 and F1 0.805 at profile level. The figures are modest by this corpus's standards and are presented without inflation - the abstract, results and conclusion all carry the same numbers and none is described as high. What is absent is uncertainty and operating-point reporting: no confidence intervals despite a corpus that would support tight ones, no calibration, and no sensitivity or specificity at any threshold. For a paper that raises screening, the absent quantity is the positive predictive value at population prevalence - at roughly 1-2% base rate, a classifier with AUC 0.78 would place the overwhelming majority of its positive calls on non-autistic people. The authors reach a compatible conclusion by a different route, restricting legitimate use to cross-sectional rather than individual inference, but they do not compute the number that demonstrates why." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-127" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Written language produced in unconstrained public communication - the only such behaviour in the review, and the largest behavioural record it contains. Three analytic layers were applied: sentiment polarity via VADER, computed on both the raw corpus and a profanity-censored version with the two found to be almost identical; topic analysis for qualitative insight; and lexical-distributional modelling through TF-IDF and word2vec for classification. What the study cannot say is which linguistic properties distinguish the groups, because no feature analysis or attribution is reported for the classifiers. The behaviour targeted is therefore best described as 'written register' rather than any specific construct, and the ambiguity between two candidate constructs - autistic language production on the one hand, and participation in an advocacy discourse on the other - is the study's central unresolved question. It is worth noting against this that the authors' preprocessing removes a sixteen-item vocabulary specifically to prevent the second construct from being trivially detectable, which is more than most studies in this corpus do about their own confounds even when they name them." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-127" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Limited at the model level and thoughtful at the corpus level. Sentiment and topic analyses were run before classification to develop qualitative understanding of the data, and the comparison of sentiment polarity between raw and profanity-censored versions is a genuine methodological check - it establishes that any polarity difference between groups is not simply a difference in swearing. But no attribution, coefficient inspection, attention visualisation or error analysis is reported for either classifier, so what the models responded to is unknown. This is the study's clearest missed opportunity, because inspecting the highest-weighted terms in the word2vec logistic regression would have spoken directly to the interpretive question the paper leaves open - whether 73% reflects something about autistic language or about the vocabulary of a self-advocacy community - and because the prior removal of sixteen autism-related tokens means the answer would have been informative rather than circular." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-127" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:study_design "Retrospective observational digital-phenotyping study with a purpose-built social media corpus, comprising exploratory sentiment and topic analysis followed by per-tweet and per-us…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: ; comparison: 3,377,518 tweets from 171,273 users, obtained by a search query excluding #ActuallyAutistic and sampling 1,000 tweets per day across the same period" ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "19a792be83247bcb575d17664d167e75fc0e735e66293cf7ddb8227ba35a9fb0" ;
