    slr:journal "JMIR Formative Research, 8, Article e52660" ;
    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Splitting is at user level with the rationale stated, applied to both tasks, with separate validation and test partitions and a minimum-tweet threshold for the profile task. Corpus counts are reported completely by group, and the preprocessing chain and exclusion of the location field are described. No within-user leakage is possible." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "unclear" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Text is naturally occurring, so there is no observation effect or task artefact, and preprocessing is fully specified including a deliberate removal of the sixteen autism-related tokens that would otherwise leak the label. But heavy normalisation strips paralinguistic features, no feature analysis is reported, and the model may be detecting the register of an advocacy discourse rather than any property of autistic language." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Hashtag use plus profile keywords, with no clinical validation of any kind - stated by the authors as their first limitation and reflected in their own description of the target as 'use of the #ActuallyAutistic hashtag, an imperfect proxy for self-reported autism'. The negative label is unscreened membership of a background sample." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "The positive class is a self-selected online community defined by use of a specific advocacy hashtag plus profile keywords; the negative class is a daily sample of general English-language Twitter. No demographic information exists for either group, the two classes were assembled by different sampling procedures (full timeline harvesting against daily sampling), and the platform's user base is skewed by age, language, geography and socioeconomic status - all acknowledged by the authors." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "high" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<urn:slr:article:c846224c5959> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) is a developmental impairment with brain abnormalities, characterized by social communication deficits and repetitive behavior patterns per recent diagnostic criteria. The Aberrant Behavior Checklist (ABC) is commonly used to analyze these behaviors and guide medication dosage. However, the short duration of ABC measurements may not accurately capture the full spectrum of ASD severity, such as mild, moderate, or severe, potentially leading to adverse drug effects. Therefore, continuous monitoring is necessary for accurate ASD classification to provide appropriate dosages. To address this challenge, a novel thermal imaging-based framework for ASD classification has been proposed, integrating continuous behavioral analysis to optimize medication dosage. The approach utilizes skin temperature and facial mood (FM) features extracted from thermal images. The Deep Network-based Facial Mood Extraction (DN-FME) model efficiently extracts facial mood features while mitigating high memory usage, computation speed issues, and overfitting. The DN-FME model demonstrated a performance accuracy of 96.39%, precision of 96.60%, recall of 96.53%, and an F1 score of 96.54%. For classification, features from skin temperature and facial moods were analyzed using machine learning classifiers: Random Forest (RF), Support Vector Machine (SVM), and K-Nearest Neighbor (KNN). The RF and SVM classifiers achieved an accuracy of 98.33%, while the KNN classifier achieved an accuracy of 90.00%. Unlike existing methods focusing mainly on severe ASD stages, this framework evaluates mild, moderate, and severe stages, demonstrating superior accuracy compared to traditional clinical assessments and observational techniques. This allows for more precise medication dosage adjustments for ASD children." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Muniraja Manjunath K; Veeramani V (2025). A novel thermal imaging-based framework for continuous ASD classification and behavior analysis using facial mood and skin temperature features. *Biomedical Signal Proces*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Muniraja Manjunath K",
        "Veeramani V" ;
    dcterms:title "A novel thermal imaging-based framework for continuous ASD classification and behavior analysis using facial mood and skin temperature features" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_45ef7022" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2025 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Handling of missing data and attrition" ;
                            slr:justification "Image counts are reported by capture setting with the number of students in each, but no exclusions, detection failures or unusable frames are described, and the 20 collected videos are never accounted for." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participant selection and spectrum representativeness" ;
                            slr:justification "Thirty male children at one residential special school, a setting selecting for higher support needs, with no age distribution or severity-group composition reported and no girls at all." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Comparison group adequacy" ;
                            slr:justification "No comparison group exists; all analyses are between severity levels within an autistic sample whose class sizes are never stated." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Confounder control" ;
                            slr:justification "No control for ambient temperature, exertion or capture setting in a study whose primary feature is skin temperature; no recording of current medication, which alters thermoregulation and correlates with severity by construction; no age-by-severity breakdown across a 5-to-15 range spanning major developmental change." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Measurement standardisation" ;
                            slr:justification "Emissivity is set and reported, which is good practice, but images span outdoor and indoor environments with no ambient compensation, no distance or angle control, and no environmental logging - so absolute skin-temperature readings are not comparable across settings." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reference standard quality" ;
                            slr:justification "No diagnostic ascertainment described, and the severity labels being predicted have no stated origin. The ABC is discussed as background but never reported as administered to these children." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Index test definition and prespecification" ;
                            slr:justification "The denoising pipeline and mood network are fully specified with hyperparameters and ablations; the three severity classifiers carrying the headline result have no reported hyperparameters or tuning procedure." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome measurement blinding" ;
                            slr:justification "Classification is automated, but the facial-mood ground truth for these thermal images must have been assigned by human raters whose identity, training and blinding are nowhere described." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data splitting and leakage control" ;
                            slr:justification "An 80-20 image-level split over 1,500 images from 30 children with roughly 50 images each and no participant-level partitioning described. Severity is a fixed per-child attribute, so identifying the child recovers the label." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Sample size adequacy for the modelling" ;
                            slr:justification "A three-class severity model with an effective sample of 30 children, inflated fiftyfold by treating each image as an independent observation." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Preprocessing and feature selection nesting" ;
                            slr:justification "Mood-network dropout and batch normalisation were selected on validation accuracy and reported transparently with numeric tables. No feature selection or tuning procedure is described for the severity classifiers." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance estimation and chance level" ;
                            slr:justification "Class distribution across the three severity levels is never reported, so neither chance nor a majority-class baseline is determinable. No confidence intervals, no confusion matrices, no per-class breakdown and no specificity, and the ROC description conflates specificity with the false positive rate." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data, code and protocol availability" ;
                            slr:justification "No code. Data - identifiable thermal facial images of 30 disabled children from a named, addressed institution - offered to anyone on request, with no stated access controls or governance." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Ethics, consent and conflicts" ;
                            slr:justification "'Ethical Approval. Not required.' No institutional review board, no ethics committee, no consent and no assent statement, for prospective thermal imaging of 30 disabled children at a named residential special school in a study proposing to automate their psychiatric medication dosing. The clearest ethics gap in this review." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Acknowledgement of limitations" ;
                            slr:justification "No limitations section exists. The future-work paragraph concedes by implication that the sample is neither diverse nor representative and that no clinical evaluation was performed, but no conclusion is qualified." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reporting of results and uncertainty" ;
                            slr:justification "Point estimates only across every stage, with no confidence intervals, no confusion matrices, no per-class results and no baseline, on an effective sample of 30 children." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Completeness of methods reporting" ;
                            slr:justification "Collection site, camera and emissivity, image counts by setting, denoising pipeline, full network architecture and hyperparameters, two ablations and all metric formulae are reported. The origin of both the severity labels and the facial-mood labels, the class distribution, the splitting unit and the classifier hyperparameters are not." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Almost nothing, and the omissions are unusually consequential because the output is a drug recommendation. Reported: age range 5 to 15, sex (all male), and school. Not reported: age distribution, any breakdown by severity level, cognitive or adaptive level, language, comorbidity, current medication, or socioeconomic background. Four specific problems follow. Thermoregulation and facial thermal patterns change substantially between ages 5 and 15, so if severity levels are unevenly distributed by age the classifier may be reading development rather than severity, and nothing reported allows this to be checked. Current medication is itself a determinant of skin temperature - antipsychotics including risperidone impair thermoregulation, which is a documented and clinically monitored effect - so children already on higher doses because they are more severely affected may be thermally distinguishable for pharmacological rather than behavioural reasons, a circularity that would produce exactly the reported result. Comorbid epilepsy, intellectual disability and sensory conditions are common in a special-school population and affect both behaviour and autonomic function. And the all-male sample means the framework has no evidence base for half the population it would be applied to. The one demographic issue the paper does engage is skin tone, and it engages it well: thermal imaging is argued to be preferable to visible-spectrum facial analysis precisely because it 'is not affected by lighting conditions or skin tone', which is a genuine sensor-level answer to a fairness problem that most face-based studies in this review leave unexamined." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-130" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional despite being framed as continuous monitoring - no child is followed over time, no repeat measurement is analysed, and the 20 videos collected 'for real-time testing to assess the algorithm's performance in dynamic settings' are mentioned once and never reported on. Longitudinal study appears only as future work. The ethics position is the most serious in this review and is stated in five words: 'Ethical Approval. Not required.' There is no institutional review board reference, no ethics committee, no consent statement and no assent statement anywhere in the paper. The participants are 30 disabled children aged 5 to 15, resident at a named special school whose full postal address is printed in the article, thermally photographed during play, lessons and meals, in a study whose declared purpose is to automate adjustment of their psychiatric medication. Who authorised the imaging, whether guardians consented, and whether the children were told what was happening are all unaddressed. This exceeds the ethics gaps recorded elsewhere in this review for the web-scraping studies, because the data here were collected prospectively and in person from a dependent institutionalised population rather than harvested from material families had already made public. The study declares no funding; competing interests are not stated; and the data - identifiable thermal images of these children - are offered to anyone who asks." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-130" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Sharply divided between the signal-processing work, which is well documented, and the clinical work, which is not. Reported: the collection site by name and postal address, the camera and its emissivity setting, image counts by capture context with the number of students in each, age range and sex, the denoising pipeline with PSNR and SSIM evaluation against alternatives, the DN-FME architecture with its eleven convolutional and three dense layers, kernel sizes, parameter count and input resolution, the complete hyperparameter table, two ablation tables with numeric results, the three severity classifiers, the AUC values, all metric formulae and the computation-time comparison. Not reported: the origin of the severity labels; the origin of the facial-mood labels, which someone must have assigned to thermal images of these children and which the paper never mentions; the class distribution across severity levels; whether the 80-20 split respected participants; hyperparameters for the three classifiers; ambient conditions; results from the 20 videos; code; and any ethics or consent arrangements. A reader could reimplement the denoising and the mood network and could not reconstruct or evaluate the severity result at all." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-130" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The three-level severity grade is the study's only clinical variable, it is the classification target, and its derivation is unstated - so the study has a severity measure in the sense that it predicts one, and none in the sense that it validates against anything documented. No cognitive, adaptive, language or behavioural instrument is reported as administered, and no continuous score is available against which the predicted probability could be correlated. This is the more striking because the study's own rationale is a severity-measurement argument, and a good one: the ABC takes about 15 minutes and 'does not capture the full range of daily behavioral variations in children', so a measure that samples continuously across a school day would in principle capture something a single sitting cannot. That argument is the strongest case for continuous behavioural monitoring anywhere in this review. But the study cannot demonstrate it, because it trains against labels that must themselves have come from a brief assessment of exactly the kind it criticises, and it never examines whether its predictions vary within a child across the day - which is the analysis its own thesis calls for and which its four-setting, multi-image-per-child data would support." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-130" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "There is none for diagnosis and none stated for severity, and the second gap is the more damaging. All 30 children are described as autistic on the basis of attending a special school; no diagnostic instrument, clinician judgement or record is cited for any of them. The classification target is severity level 1, 2 or 3, and the origin of those labels is nowhere given - not in the dataset description, not in the methodology, not in the results. The Aberrant Behavior Checklist is discussed at length in the introduction as the 58-item instrument clinicians use to grade severity and guide dosage, and it is the obvious candidate, but the paper never states that it was administered to these children, by whom, when, or with what training. Two consequences follow. First, what the classifiers predict is simply unknown, so the 98.33% cannot be interpreted even setting aside the splitting problem. Second, the paper's central comparative claim becomes incoherent: it reports that the machine learning methods 'outperform the clinical ABC method' in classifying severity levels and presents a table comparing their accuracies. If the ABC supplied the labels, a model can at best reproduce it and cannot exceed it, and any apparent excess is measurement of the model's agreement with its own training signal; if some independent criterion existed against which both were scored, the paper does not say what it was. A claim to beat the clinical standard, in a paper proposing to adjust medication, requires the standard to be specified, and it is not." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-130" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Biomedical Signal Processing and Control 100:107009, Elsevier; received 25 November 2023, revised 8 August 2024, accepted 30 September 2024, online 10 October 2024, with a 2025 issue date. The same journal published another study charted in this review, and the pattern is consistent across both: signal-processing components are held to a reasonable standard - denoising evaluated against alternatives on PSNR and SSIM, architectural choices supported by ablation, hyperparameters tabulated - while the clinical apparatus passes without scrutiny. Here that meant an article proposing automated psychiatric medication dosing for children was published with the declaration 'Ethical Approval. Not required.', with no stated source for its outcome labels, with no participant-level validation, and with no limitations section. The journal's declaration structure elicited statements on ethics, funding, data availability and authorship, so the ethics field was completed - with a denial of applicability that went unqueried." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-130" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Medical and deficit-framed throughout, with several formulations that are outdated or inaccurate. Autism is 'a developmental impairment with brain abnormalities', 'a pervasive neurodevelopmental disability', and the introduction speaks of the need for treatments that 'target the underlying abnormalities in the autistic brain'. Prevalence is presented in alarm terms - a rise 'from 1 in 5000 in 1975 to 1 in 45 by 2018' described as 'alarming' and as underscoring urgency - without acknowledging that changing diagnostic criteria and ascertainment account for much of that trend, which makes the framing a claim about incidence that the cited figures do not support. The children themselves appear almost entirely as objects of measurement and dosing: they are 'ASD children' and 'autistic children' whose 'behaviors' are to be monitored continuously so that medication can be adjusted, and no autistic person, family member or advocacy organisation is reported as involved in designing the framework or in judging whether continuous thermal surveillance of children at school is acceptable. One passage is more careful and deserves credit: the discussion of medication notes that 'autistic children may be more sensitive to medication effects and more prone to experiencing adverse drug effects compared to non-ASD children', and the introduction states plainly that the two approved medications 'do not effectively address the core symptoms of ASD and are associated with significant side effects'. That is an accurate and clinically important observation - but it is used to justify more precise dosing rather than to raise any question about the framework's premise that a camera should inform the dose." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-130" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: autism spectrum disorder, facial moods, skin temperature, ASD classification, deep learning, machine learning. The set names the population, both feature families, the task and the methods, and is more balanced than the method-only lists typical of engineering venues in this corpus. Terms carried through the body: thermal imaging, emissivity, Aberrant Behavior Checklist, medication dosage, Non-Decimated Wavelet Transform, DnCNN, PSNR, SSIM, Viola-Jones, DN-FME, continuous monitoring, severity levels." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-130" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None, by design. Images were captured during ordinary school activities with no instruction, elicitation or standardisation, which is the correct choice for a framework whose entire argument is that brief structured assessments miss daily variation. The cost is that nothing is held constant: a child running outdoors, sitting in a classroom and eating a meal differ in exertion, posture, distance from the camera, head angle and thermal environment, and all of these affect both the measured skin temperature and the appearance of the face to a mood classifier. The paper does not report distance to subject, viewing angle, or whether images within a setting were taken in a single session. What is missing is the analysis that this data structure uniquely enables and that the study's own thesis demands: whether a child's predicted state varies across the day and across contexts, which would be evidence of continuous behavioural measurement rather than of static classification. Instead the design collapses multiple images per child into a single per-child severity label, which discards precisely the temporal variation the framework exists to capture." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-130" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A reasonable metric set applied at every stage - PSNR and SSIM for denoising, accuracy, precision, recall and F1 for both classification stages, ROC curves with AUC for severity (RF 0.9878, SVM 0.9865, KNN 0.9268), and computation time - with all formulae defined explicitly. What is missing determines whether any of it can be read. Class sizes across the three severity levels are never given, so neither the chance level nor the majority-class baseline is known and 98.33% has no reference point. There are no confidence intervals, on 1,500 images from 30 children where the effective sample is 30. There are no confusion matrices and no per-class breakdown, so whether the mild and moderate classes are distinguished at all - the clinically important discrimination for dosing - cannot be seen. There is no specificity, and the ROC description conflates specificity with the false positive rate. And the comparison table reporting that the classifiers exceed 'the clinical ABC method' has no stated criterion for either, so the numbers in it cannot be interpreted." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-130" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Published 2024-2025 by the Department of Electronics and Communication Engineering at Vignan's Foundation for Science, Technology and Research in Andhra Pradesh, with data collected at a special school in Chennai and no funding. The framing is pharmacological rather than diagnostic, and this makes the study unique in the review: its output is not a diagnosis or a screening flag but a psychiatric medication dosage level, mapped from a predicted severity grade across a named list of agents including metformin, arbaclofen, cannabidiol, oxytocin, bumetanide, lovastatin and trofinetide. Two things about it deserve to be carried into the synthesis, one positive and one cautionary. The positive: the modality argument is the best-reasoned sensor-level answer in this corpus to a problem the review has documented repeatedly in visible-spectrum facial analysis, since thermal imaging requires no illumination and, as the authors correctly note, 'is not affected by lighting conditions or skin tone' - so a face-based method is proposed that is structurally immune to the demographic fragility that undermines the photograph studies charted here. The clinical motivation is also the sharpest statement of the continuous-monitoring case anywhere in the review: a 15-minute checklist 'does not capture the full range of daily behavioral variations in children', and the children were accordingly imaged during play, lessons and meals rather than in a clinic. The cautionary point is that neither strength is realised. The 80-20 split is over images rather than children, with about 50 images per child, so the classifiers can identify individuals and thereby their fixed severity labels, and 98.33% is what that arrangement produces; the severity labels themselves have no stated source, making the predicted quantity undefined; the claim to outperform the clinical ABC is incoherent if the ABC supplied those labels; class sizes are unreported so no baseline exists; skin temperature was measured across indoor and outdoor settings with no ambient compensation, confounding environment with any physiological signal; no feature importance distinguishes temperature from mood; the 20 videos reserved for real-time testing are never analysed; the sample is entirely male; current medication, which itself alters thermoregulation and correlates with severity by construction, is unrecorded; and there is no limitations section. Above all, the study was conducted on 30 disabled children at a named residential special school under the declaration 'Ethical Approval. Not required.', with no consent or assent statement of any kind, and offers its identifiable thermal images to anyone on request. For a review assessing what this literature does with its findings, this is the clearest case of a leaked accuracy attached to the highest-stakes recommendation in the corpus - a paper that lists cardiac arrest and kidney failure among the consequences of imprecise dosing, and then proposes that an image-level-split classifier of unstated provenance should inform the dose." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-130" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A residential special school in Chennai, with images captured across four naturalistic contexts during the school day - outdoor play, classroom, dining area and veranda. This is genuinely ecological data collection, and multi-context sampling within an ordinary day is exactly what a continuous-monitoring argument requires; very few studies in this review collect behavioural data in the settings where children actually spend their time. The same feature creates the study's central measurement hazard, and it is specific to this modality. Skin temperature is determined substantially by ambient temperature, solar radiation, airflow and physical exertion, all of which differ sharply between an outdoor playground in Tamil Nadu and an indoor dining hall. No ambient compensation, environmental logging or per-setting normalisation is described, and the image counts by setting (500, 600, 200, 200) come from different and overlapping groups of children (10, 12, 4, 4) whose severity levels are not reported. If severity is unevenly distributed across capture settings - which with 4 children in two of them is likely - then the thermal environment is confounded with the label, and the confound would survive any splitting scheme. A per-setting stratified analysis would have addressed this directly and is not performed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-130" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A FLIR USB thermal imaging camera calibrated at an emissivity of 0.98, with analysis in MATLAB 2023a on a Dual-Core E5700. Reporting the emissivity value is good practice and is more acquisition detail than most studies in this review provide. The choice of modality is also well argued on grounds that matter for this corpus: thermal imaging needs no external illumination, is unaffected by lighting conditions, and - the point worth carrying into the synthesis - is 'not affected by ... skin tone', which is a sensor-level answer to the demographic fragility that this review has documented repeatedly in visible-spectrum facial analysis. What is missing is everything needed to interpret an absolute temperature reading: camera model, spatial resolution, noise-equivalent temperature difference, distance to subject, and ambient temperature and humidity at each capture. For a study whose primary feature is skin temperature and whose images span outdoor and indoor settings, the absence of ambient logging is not a minor reporting gap but a limit on what the measurement means." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-130" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Denoised thermal face images at 160x160 for the mood network, and a concatenation of learned mood output with measured skin temperature for the severity classifiers, whose dimensionality is never stated. Two representational points matter. Absolute skin temperature is only meaningful relative to ambient conditions, and no normalisation or ambient reference is applied, so the same child at the same physiological state would yield different feature values outdoors and indoors. And each image is treated as an independent observation despite 50 coming from each child, so the representation carries no notion of within-child variation - which is exactly what a continuous-monitoring framework should model and what the paper's own critique of the 15-minute ABC assessment implies it should measure." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-130" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Five directions are listed: larger and more diverse samples, additional modalities including speech and genetic information, ensemble models, longitudinal designs to track change over time, and evaluation in real-world clinical settings. The fourth and fifth are the ones the framework actually requires, and their inclusion is a tacit admission that a system proposed for continuous longitudinal monitoring and medication adjustment has been evaluated neither longitudinally nor clinically. Not proposed, and prerequisite to any of the above: participant-disjoint evaluation, disclosure of how severity labels were obtained, per-setting analysis to separate thermal environment from physiological signal, recording of current medication, inclusion of girls, and ethics review." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-130" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The aim is a framework for continuous thermal monitoring of autistic children that classifies severity and thereby informs psychiatric medication dosing, motivated by the argument that the ABC's brief administration cannot capture daily behavioural variation and that imprecise dosing risks adverse effects the paper lists as including cardiac arrest, kidney failure, irregular heartbeats and high blood pressure. Three results are reported. The denoising pipeline, combining a non-decimated wavelet transform with a DnCNN, outperformed comparison filters on PSNR and SSIM. The DN-FME facial-mood network reached 96.39% accuracy across five categories - neutral, anger, sadness, fear, happiness - with two properly conducted ablations showing dropout at 0.2 outperforming 0.5 (0.9639 against 0.9521) and batch normalisation adding four points (0.9639 against 0.9378). And the severity classifiers reached 98.33% for Random Forest and SVM with AUCs of 0.9878 and 0.9865, against 90.00% for KNN. The paper then reports that its classifiers outperform the clinical ABC method and presents a severity-to-dosage mapping. The engineering components are competently executed and reported; the clinical components rest on labels of unstated origin, an image-level split that allows the classifiers to identify individual children, and a comparison against a clinical standard that is very likely the source of the labels themselves." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-130" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The single sentence describing evaluation is the study's undoing: '80% of the dataset was allocated for training purposes while the remaining 20% was used for performance validation'. The dataset is 1,500 images from 30 children, approximately 50 images each, and no participant-level partitioning is mentioned anywhere - the word 'split' appears nowhere else in the paper, and no cross-validation is performed for the severity task. On the natural reading, about 40 images of every child are in training and 10 in test. Severity is a fixed attribute of a child, so recognising the child suffices to recover the label, and thermal facial images of the same child in the same setting are near-duplicates of one another. A 98.33% three-class accuracy is therefore the expected arithmetic of the arrangement rather than evidence that thermal features encode severity, and the same is true of the AUC of 0.9878. This is the same failure the review has documented in the scanpath-image, video-frame and EEG branches, appearing here in a fourth modality, and it is compounded by four further problems. The severity labels have no stated source, so the quantity being predicted is undefined. The class distribution is unreported, so no baseline exists against which 98.33% could be judged even if the split were sound. The claim that the classifiers 'outperform the clinical ABC method' is incoherent if the ABC supplied the labels, and no alternative criterion is described. And skin temperature, the primary feature, was captured across outdoor play, classroom, dining and veranda settings with no ambient compensation, from overlapping groups of 10, 12, 4 and 4 children whose severity levels are not reported - so if severity is unevenly distributed across settings, thermal environment is confounded with the label in a way that no splitting scheme could remove. The remedy was available and unused: 20 videos were collected 'for real-time testing to assess the algorithm's performance in dynamic settings' and would have provided a genuinely held-out evaluation, but no result from them is reported. What makes this record worth stating at length is the destination of the number. A leaked accuracy in a diagnostic paper produces an overstated claim; here it produces a proposal to set the dose of psychiatric medication for disabled children, in a paper that itself lists cardiac arrest and kidney failure among the consequences of getting the dose wrong." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-130" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "There is no limitations section. The future-work paragraph names five extensions - more diverse and representative samples, additional modalities such as speech or genetic information, ensemble models, longitudinal designs, and evaluation in real-world clinical settings - which concede by implication that the present sample is neither diverse nor representative and that nothing has been clinically evaluated, but none of these is allowed to qualify any conclusion. What should have been stated: the 80-20 split is over 1,500 images from 30 children with roughly 50 images each and no participant-level partitioning, so the classifiers can identify the child and thereby the label, and a 98.33% three-class accuracy is the expected arithmetic of that arrangement rather than evidence about thermal signatures of severity. The severity labels have no stated source, so the target is undefined. The comparison against 'the clinical ABC method' is incoherent if the ABC produced those labels. Class sizes are unreported, so no baseline exists. Skin temperature was measured across outdoor and indoor settings without ambient compensation, and if severity is unevenly distributed across settings the environment is confounded with the label. All participants are male. Current medication - itself a determinant of thermoregulation, and correlated with severity by construction - is unrecorded. No feature importance is given, so it is unknown whether temperature or mood drives the result. The 20 held-out videos are never analysed. And there is no ethics approval or consent for prospectively imaging disabled children in an institution." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-130" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Thirty male autistic children aged 5 to 15, all attending Don Guanella Special School in Poonamallee, Chennai, contributing 1,500 thermal images distributed across four settings within the school: 500 images from 10 students during outdoor play, 600 from 12 students in the classroom, 200 from 4 students in the dining area and 200 from 4 students on the veranda, plus 20 videos collected for real-time testing that are never analysed. Every image is of an autistic child; the study contains no non-autistic participants and no diagnostic task, since the classification target is a three-level severity grade among autistic children only. Four features of the sample bound what can be learned from it. It is entirely male, so nothing reported can be assumed to apply to autistic girls, and the paper does not remark on this. It is small in the way that matters - 30 children, 50 images each - so image counts overstate the effective sample fiftyfold, and the reported 98.33% must be read against that. The children are drawn from a single residential special school, a setting that selects for higher support needs and makes the sample unrepresentative of the autistic population the framework's medication claim addresses. And the four capture settings differ physically in ways that directly affect the primary measurement: outdoor play in Chennai, a classroom, a dining area and an open veranda produce systematically different skin temperatures for reasons unrelated to autism, and the distribution of severity levels across those settings is never reported." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-130" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Present for the mood model at the level of its outputs - five named, clinically legible emotion categories, with two ablations documenting the architectural choices and their numeric effects - and entirely absent for the severity classifiers, which is where a system recommending medication dosages most needs it. No feature importance is reported despite Random Forest providing it without additional work, so it is unknown whether the model responds to skin temperature or to facial mood, to which mood categories, or to which facial regions. Nothing links a severity prediction to any observable property of a child that a clinician could inspect, challenge or corroborate. The paper's own framing makes this gap sharper than usual: it proposes replacing a 58-item instrument, whose items are explicit and auditable, with a classifier whose basis is not reported, and it presents the substitution as an improvement in precision." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-130" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A three-stage pipeline, competently constructed at each stage. Viola-Jones face detection, then denoising by a non-decimated wavelet transform combined with a DnCNN, then the DN-FME network - eleven convolutional layers and three dense layers, 5x5 and 3x3 kernels, batch normalisation in blocks conv3 to conv5, 14 million parameters, 160x160 input - and finally Random Forest, SVM and KNN for three-class severity. Model sizing is reasoned rather than assumed: the authors explain that a smaller input resolution and limited data made a dropout of 0.5 counterproductive because it 'resulted in dropping useful information necessary for learning features', and they demonstrate the point with a monotonic table of validation accuracy across seven dropout values. Choosing classical classifiers for the final stage is also appropriate to 30 children. The gap is at that final stage: no hyperparameters are reported for any of the three classifiers, and no tuning procedure is described - so the stage carrying the paper's headline result is the least documented." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-130" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two constructs from one sensor, and the choice is better motivated than most in this review. Facial mood is classified into five categories - neutral, anger, sadness, fear and happiness - and skin temperature is measured directly, on the physiological rationale that pulsating blood flow drives the largest facial temperature variations and that emotional change produces detectable thermal change in facial skin. The target is therefore emotional state and autonomic arousal rather than social communication, gaze or motor stereotypy, which distinguishes this study from nearly all of the corpus and connects it plausibly to the clinical problem it addresses: the medications at issue are prescribed largely for irritability and agitation, so a continuous measure of affective state and arousal is the right construct for titrating them. Three problems attach to the execution. The mood labels for thermal images of these particular children must have been assigned by someone, and the paper never says who or how - so a five-category emotional ground truth exists with no stated provenance, in a population whose facial expression is itself atypical. Skin temperature is confounded with ambient conditions and exertion across four physically different capture settings with no compensation. And no feature importance is reported for the severity classifiers, so whether the discriminative information lies in mood or in temperature - a question with entirely different clinical implications - is unknown." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-130" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two feature families from a single thermal sensor - learned mood categories and measured temperature - concatenated for severity classification. This is within-modality fusion rather than multi-sensor fusion, and no ablation separates the two contributions, which is the analysis that would give the result whatever interpretive content it might have. The omission is notable because it was free: Random Forest supplies feature importances by default and the paper reports Random Forest results without them. Without that decomposition, an accuracy of 98.33% is consistent with the model reading affective state, with it reading ambient temperature at the moment of capture, and with it reading individual identity across an image-level split - and the study provides no means of distinguishing them." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-130" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:study_design "Retrospective algorithm-development study on originally collected thermal imagery, comprising a denoising stage, a facial-mood classification stage and a three-class severity class…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: ; comparison: None. The classification task is three-level severity (level 1 mild, level 2 moderate, level 3 severe) among autistic children only, so the study does not address diagnosis and is not a case-control design." ] ] ;
