    slr:journal "PLOS ONE, 19(10), Article e0308388" ;
    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "unclear" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "DSM-5 clinical criteria supported by gold-standard diagnostic assessment within an established clinical cohort - genuine ascertainment, and among the better standards in this review. But the specific instruments are deferred to supplementary material, and since the model input is the ADOS recording, whether ADOS also informed the diagnosis is the question that determines independence and is not answered." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "All 160 children contributed recordings; the 50-50 split is participant-level and matched on age, sex and ADOS module; segments are nested strictly within participants so no within-participant leakage is possible; and all tuning was confined to an 80-20 division of the training half. No attrition or excluded recordings are reported." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Both groups enrolled in the same longitudinal cohort, matched exactly on sex (48 male, 32 female each) and closely on age, with the comparison group actively screened for neurological and psychiatric conditions and for first-degree family history of autism. The 1:1 ratio is a design choice rather than a prevalence, and the sample skews young (1.1-5.1 years) and has no non-autistic clinical comparison group - but the selection itself is sound and the 40% female representation is the highest in the review." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Both groups recorded during the same ADOS protocol in the same setting, so acquisition context is matched across classes. The video representation strips appearance to skeletal keypoints on a black background, architecturally excluding identity and setting confounds. Audio was normalised across microphones to a common format. The 90% segment-confidence filter is a selection step whose effect is unquantified, and the undiarised audio band includes the examiner." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "low" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<urn:slr:article:e15a1551c489> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "This study explores the challenge of differentiating autism spectrum (AS) from non-AS conditions in adolescents and adults, particularly considering the heterogeneity of AS and the limitations of diagnostic tools like the ADOS-2. In response, we advocate a multidimensional approach and highlight lexicogrammatical analysis as a key component to improve diagnostic accuracy. From a corpus of spoken language we developed, interviews and storyrecounting texts were extracted for 64 individuals diagnosed with AS and 71 non-AS individuals, all aged 14 and above. Utilizing machine learning techniques, we analyzed the lexicogrammatical choices in both interviews and story-recounting tasks. Our approach led to the formulation of two diagnostic models: the first based on annotated linguistic tags, and the second combining these tags with textual analysis. The combined model demonstrated high diagnostic effectiveness, achieving an accuracy of 80%, precision of 82%, sensitivity of 73%, and specificity of 87%. Notably, our analysis revealed that interview-based texts were more diagnostically effective than story-recounting texts. This underscores the altered social language use in individuals with AS, a crucial aspect in distinguishing AS from non-AS conditions. Our findings demonstrate that lexicogrammatical analysis is a promising addition to traditional AS diagnostic methods. This approach suggests the possibility of using natural language processing to detect distinctive linguistic patterns in AS, aiming to enhance diagnostic accuracy for differentiating AS from non-AS in adolescents and adults." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Kato S; Hanawa K; Saito M; Nakamura K (2024). Creating a diagnostic assessment model for autism spectrum disorder by differentiating lexicogrammatical choices through machine learning. *PLOS ONE, 19(9), Artic*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Hanawa K",
        "Kato S",
        "Nakamura K",
        "Saito M" ;
    dcterms:title "Creating a diagnostic assessment model for autism spectrum disorder by differentiating lexicogrammatical choices through machine learning" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_13426811" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2024 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Confounder control" ;
                            slr:justification "Age differs significantly but by about a year; sex is not significantly different. The decisive uncontrolled factor is cognitive and educational level: measured at full-scale IQ 81-91 in the autistic group and not measured at all in the comparison group, where college grade point average was substituted - in a study whose outcome is grammatical complexity and variety of lexicogrammatical choice." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Comparison group adequacy" ;
                            slr:justification "Every comparison participant received the ADOS-2 and was confirmed non-spectrum against quoted cut-offs, which is stronger screening than any other study here provides. But three-quarters are college students selected for average grade point average, so the group is not a clinical comparison in the differential sense, and the 17 who came through the clinical pathway were selected precisely for having no psychiatric diagnosis." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participant selection and spectrum representativeness" ;
                            slr:justification "Sixty-four autistic participants with full clinical characterisation - full-scale IQ 81-91, SRS-2 85.53, Vineland-II composite 64.83, AQ 36.64 - so the sample is well described and spans a broader ability range than the high-functioning cohorts typical of this corpus. Co-occurring conditions were deliberately retained on explicit reasoning about clinical utility. The 62.5% female composition is unusual and unremarked." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Handling of missing data and attrition" ;
                            slr:justification "All 135 participants appear in the analysis with no attrition reported, and the entire spoken output was used without length or duration criteria, so no differential exclusion by verbosity occurred. But no transcript-quality or exclusion accounting is given, and the seven-year recruitment window is not broken down by group." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Index test definition and prespecification" ;
                            slr:justification "Hyperparameters adopted from standard values and explicitly not searched; no feature selection; four configurations reported in full across two tasks and two annotation methods rather than the best being selected; model comparisons tested formally." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reference standard quality" ;
                            slr:justification "DSM-5 by neurodevelopmental specialists with ADOS-2 confirmation by a Western Psychological Services research-reliable administrator, plus SRS-2, Wechsler IQ, Vineland-II, AQ and PARS-TR; and ADOS-2 applied to the comparison group with published non-spectrum cut-offs quoted and group means reported. The only study in this review to instrument both groups with the gold standard." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Measurement standardisation" ;
                            slr:justification "All language samples elicited by a certified ADOS-2 administrator following the Module 3 and 4 protocols, so the elicitation is as standardised as anything in this corpus. The entire spoken output was analysed without length requirements, avoiding a verbosity-based selection artefact. Annotation quality was quantified against manual annotation at F = 0.88 rather than assumed." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome measurement blinding" ;
                            slr:justification "Classification is automated from transcripts. But manual annotation was performed by people who may have known participants' group membership, and the transcripts themselves come from sessions in which the examiner was assessing the participant for autism - so the elicitation was not blind even if the modelling was." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance estimation and chance level" ;
                            slr:justification "Accuracy with 95% confidence intervals for all sixteen conditions, plus precision, sensitivity, specificity and AUC, and McNemar tests for pairwise model comparison with non-significance explicitly not read as equivalence. No majority-class baseline is stated, but the confidence intervals serve the same interpretive function against a 52.6% floor." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Preprocessing and feature selection nesting" ;
                            slr:justification "No feature selection occurs, and the one fitted preprocessing component - the Bi-LSTM annotator - is explicitly nested within the cross-validation loop." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data splitting and leakage control" ;
                            slr:justification "Participant-level leave-one-out cross-validation formally specified, with the automatic tag annotation model retrained inside each fold so that a fitted preprocessing component cannot leak information from held-out participants into their own annotations." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Sample size adequacy for the modelling" ;
                            slr:justification "One hundred and thirty-five participants is at the upper end for this corpus and the confidence intervals are correspondingly informative, but the authors name sample size as their principal limitation, describe the work as exploratory, and interpret their statistical comparisons with explicit reference to limited power." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Acknowledgement of limitations" ;
                            slr:justification "A dedicated limitations section naming sample size repeatedly, describing the study as exploratory, identifying the weakest annotation category with concrete remedies, and interpreting non-significant statistical comparisons with reference to power. Not acknowledged: the unmeasured comparison-group IQ and the substitution of grade point average for cognitive testing, and the fact that the language samples come from the diagnostic sessions themselves." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Ethics, consent and conflicts" ;
                            slr:justification "Hirosaki University Committee on Medical Ethics approval with IRB numbers and their update history, information security guidelines followed, age-appropriate written consent including guardian consent for those under 20, and anonymisation with removal of identifiable utterances from transcripts - a privacy measure specific to spoken personal narrative. Funding itemised and competing interests declared as none." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Completeness of methods reporting" ;
                            slr:justification "Participant selection rationale, full diagnostic battery, ADOS-2 cut-offs applied to both groups, both elicitation tasks with their provenance, the annotation scheme with headings, functions and tag types, the transfer-comparison method, input construction for each model, all training hyperparameters with the statement that they were not searched, the fold-nested annotation procedure, and the full evaluation scheme are all specified. Software packages and seeds are not named." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data, code and protocol availability" ;
                            slr:justification "A data availability statement is provided and supplementary files carry the tag frequency comparisons, but the corpus itself is described in a prior publication rather than deposited, and no code is released." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reporting of results and uncertainty" ;
                            slr:justification "Sixteen conditions with accuracy, 95% confidence intervals, precision, sensitivity, specificity and AUC, plus a McNemar comparison table with test statistics and p-values, and explicit power-aware interpretation of non-significant results. The most disciplined uncertainty reporting in the review after the population EHR study." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The best-calibrated metric reporting in the review after the population EHR study. Accuracy is reported with a 95% confidence interval for every one of sixteen conditions - four models by two tasks by two annotation methods - alongside precision, sensitivity, specificity and AUC. Reporting confidence intervals is what makes the results interpretable at this sample size: the best interview model's interval of [0.75, 0.89] sits clearly above the 52.6% majority-class floor, while the weakest picture-book configuration at 0.60 [0.51, 0.69] does not. Model comparisons are then tested formally with McNemar rather than being asserted from point differences, and the results are tabulated with test statistics and p-values. Most importantly, non-significant comparisons are interpreted correctly: the authors state that non-significance 'does not definitively indicate the absence of a performance difference between models, particularly in the context of small sample sizes', citing the literature on statistical power. Across this corpus, model rankings resting on differences of one or two participants are routinely presented as findings; this is the only study that tests such comparisons and then declines to over-read them. Absent: confusion matrices, and any relation between predictions and the clinical severity variables collected." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-115" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three directions, all concrete. Comprehensive verification with a substantially larger and more diverse participant pool, with the logistical difficulty of achieving this acknowledged rather than waved away. Improvement of the automatic annotation system, particularly for te-clauses, either by sharpening category definitions or by expanding training data - a specific and testable target derived from the study's own error analysis. And extension to other languages through transfer comparison, the same method used to build the Japanese system network, which the authors argue makes the annotation scheme 'applicable to any language'. That last is an unusually ambitious but well-grounded claim, since it rests on a documented linguistic methodology rather than on an assumption that features transfer. Not proposed, and needed: cognitive assessment of the comparison group, and analysis relating model output to the SRS-2, AQ, Vineland-II and ADOS-2 data already collected." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-115" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional in analysis, though the corpus was assembled over seven years, with prospective recruitment and retrospective record examination running concurrently. No follow-up, no repeat sampling and no test-retest assessment of the linguistic measures - which matters for an instrument proposed as a diagnostic aid, since stability across occasions is unestablished. Ethics reporting is complete and specific: approval by the Hirosaki University Committee on Medical Ethics under IRB 2013-142, subsequently updated to 2018-168 with the previous number 2015-055 also given; the committee's information security guidelines followed; written consent from participants aged 20 and above and from both participants and guardians for those 19 and under; and anonymisation by alphanumeric identifier with identifiable utterances removed from transcripts. The last of these is a specific privacy measure appropriate to a corpus of personal spoken narratives and is not standard practice in this corpus. Funding from two JSPS KAKENHI grants is itemised and competing interests are declared as none." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-115" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Hirosaki University's Department of Neuropsychiatry, with natural language processing collaboration at Tohoku University. Language samples were drawn from ADOS-2 administrations conducted by a research-reliable administrator, so the recording setting is the standardised diagnostic assessment itself. Recruitment ran from September 2013 to October 2020, combining prospective enrolment with retrospective examination of diagnostic records under the same ethical framework. The recruitment geometry is the study's weak point relative to its reference standard: autistic participants come through a university neuropsychiatry clinic while three-quarters of the comparison group are college students recruited for the study, so clinical status, educational setting and probably cognitive level all co-vary with group. The 17 clinically assessed comparison participants partially mitigate this by coming through the same clinical pathway, but they are under a quarter of that group." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-115" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Strong on conceptual and procedural specification, weaker on artefacts. Reproducible from the text: the participant selection rationale with its critical-period justification, the full diagnostic battery, the ADOS-2 non-spectrum cut-offs applied to the comparison group with observed means, the two elicitation tasks and their ADOS-2 provenance, the Systemic Functional Linguistics annotation scheme with its lexicogrammatical headings, linguistic functions and tag types tabulated at category level, the transfer-comparison method by which the Japanese system network was built, the input construction for each of the four models, all training hyperparameters with the statement that they were not searched, the leave-one-out procedure with the annotation model retrained within each fold, and the evaluation metrics with confidence intervals and McNemar comparisons. Supplementary files carry the tag frequency comparisons. Not available: code, the corpus itself (described in a prior publication rather than deposited), the specific software stack, and any random seed. The annotation scheme's full tag inventory is summarised rather than exhaustively listed in the main text." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-115" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "PLOS ONE 19(9), article e0311209, published 27 September 2024; received 19 March 2024, accepted 15 September 2024, open access under CC BY, with a named academic editor. Twenty-nine pages, of which a substantial proportion is devoted to the linguistic theory, the annotation scheme and the interpretation of what the tags mean - so the balance is unusual for this corpus, with the machine learning occupying perhaps a quarter of the paper and the linguistic framework the rest. The venue's reporting requirements produce the data availability, funding and competing-interests statements, and the length permits the annotation scheme to be documented at a level that a conference paper could not accommodate. Two supplementary files carry the tag frequency comparisons between groups." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-115" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "One hundred and thirty-five participants aged 14 and above, primarily 14 to 20: 64 diagnosed with autism spectrum and 71 non-autistic. The age floor is a reasoned choice rather than a convenience, selected as post-critical-period for language acquisition with reference to Lenneberg's and Newport's hypotheses, so that the linguistic measures reflect settled grammatical competence rather than ongoing acquisition. The comparison group is deliberately compound: 17 individuals who went through the same comprehensive clinical diagnostic process and were determined to have no psychiatric disorder, and 54 recruited as non-autistic, predominantly college students. Every comparison participant received the ADOS-2 and was confirmed non-spectrum against published cut-offs, with group means reported (Module 3 3.17 and Module 4 4.00 for the clinical subgroup; 2.01 and 4.27 for the recruited subgroup). The autistic cohort deliberately includes co-occurring conditions, displayed in a dedicated figure, on reasoning the authors set out explicitly. Recruitment and retrospective record analysis ran concurrently from September 2013 to October 2020." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-115" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Published 2024 by the Department of Neuropsychiatry at Hirosaki University with Aomori Chuo Gakuin University and the Natural Language Processing Lab at Tohoku University, funded by two JSPS KAKENHI grants. The framing is diagnostic supplementation grounded in a specific, well-evidenced critique of the gold standard rather than in generic complaints about subjectivity: ADOS-2 does not clearly differentiate autism from ADHD or from the negative symptoms of schizophrenia and other psychiatric comorbidities; masking, compensation and camouflaging can conceal impairment; and adult diagnosis is further hampered because caregiver developmental reports are usually unavailable while self-insight is unreliable. That is a more precise diagnosis of what automated assessment is for than any other paper in this corpus offers, and it leads to a correspondingly bounded claim - language analysis as one input among several, not a replacement. Four features make the study particularly valuable to this review. It is the only one that applies a gold-standard instrument to both groups, so its comparison group is screened rather than assumed. It is the only one that nests a fitted preprocessing component inside its cross-validation. It is the only one that tests model comparisons statistically and then declines to over-read non-significant differences. And it is the only one that refuses to rank its models by accuracy, retaining an interpretable linear model on the explicit ground that interpretability is not commensurable with accuracy in clinical use. Set against these, its unmeasured comparison-group IQ is a real limitation, and its reliance on ADOS-2 transcripts means the speech analysed was produced inside the diagnostic encounter itself - a form of dependence between sample and label that no other language study here faces and that the paper does not discuss." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-115" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Lexicogrammatical choice - which options a speaker selects from the grammatical resources their language offers - as an operationalisation of pragmatic impairment. The theoretical grounding is Systemic Functional Linguistics, in which language is a system network of choices and meaning is made by selecting among them, and the clinical construct is defined with care as difficulty in 'adapting language formality based on the situation, interpreting non-literal language (such as idioms, metaphors, irony, and sarcasm), and understanding the nuances of language that affect interpersonal interactions... rather than with the basic structural or grammatical components'. The annotation scheme operationalises this across multiple lexicogrammatical systems, of which the paper details several: modality, where prior work by the first author found autistic individuals less likely to use probability expressions (must, will, may) and evidentiality expressions (seems, looks like, likely, is said, according to) and connected this to inferential reasoning; te-clauses with eight functional subtypes; negotiating particles, comprising twelve Japanese sentence-final and medial particles such as kana, ne, yo and yone that mark attitudinal stance toward a proposition and signal the speaker's claim on the information; and explanative mood. Two features distinguish this target from the rest of the corpus. It is language-specific in a principled way - the particles carrying much of the signal have no English equivalent and were identified through a system network built for Japanese - which is the opposite of the usual practice of applying an English-derived feature set across languages. And the empirical result localises the signal to socially interactive language: interview transcripts discriminated better than narrative recounting in every configuration tested, which is what a pragmatic account predicts and a purely structural account does not." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-115" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two tasks, both taken from ADOS-2 Modules 3 and 4 as administered rather than constructed for the study: interview responses and picture-book story recounting. The choice has one clear advantage and one clear cost. The advantage is standardisation - the same certified administrator elicited both from every participant under a protocol designed to sample social communication, so the elicitation is as comparable across participants as anything in this review. The cost is that the speech analysed was produced during the diagnostic session itself, so the examiner's behaviour toward each participant is part of the context and is unobserved, and the language sample is not independent of the encounter that produced the label. The paper is explicit about what it does with the material: the entire spoken output is evaluated 'without setting specific requirements for text length, speaking duration, or word count', and the analysis addresses 'patterns of lexicogrammar selection from participants' responses' rather than the dynamics of interaction - so turn-taking, latency and interactional contingency are outside scope by design. The contrast between the two tasks is the most valuable feature of the paradigm and is exploited properly: interview is dialogic and socially demanding, picture-book recounting is monologic and scaffolded by the stimulus, and the consistent superiority of interview transcripts across all four models and both annotation methods is a within-study replication of the paper's theoretical claim." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-115" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The limitations section is substantive and correctly prioritised. Sample size is named repeatedly as the principal constraint, with the conclusion that the study 'should be viewed as exploratory, aimed at providing initial insights rather than definitive conclusions' - a self-assessment few papers in this corpus offer and fewer still act on. The infeasibility of manual annotation in clinical settings is acknowledged as the reason the automatic annotator exists, and its weakest category (te-clauses, subdivided into eight classifications) is identified with two concrete remedies. Most notably, the McNemar results are interpreted with explicit reference to statistical power: 'the absence of statistically significant differences in our McNemar test results does not definitively indicate the absence of a performance difference between models, particularly in the context of small sample sizes'. Correctly refusing to read non-significance as equivalence is a discipline absent everywhere else in this review. What is not addressed matters nonetheless. IQ was measured in the autistic group at 81-91 and not measured at all in the comparison group, which was selected for average college grade point average - so an unquantified cognitive and educational gap separates the groups in a study of grammatical complexity, and the substitution of grade point average for IQ testing is presented as a selection criterion rather than acknowledged as a limitation. The language samples come from the ADOS-2 sessions that produced the labels, so the elicitation context is the diagnostic encounter itself. The comparison group is three-quarters university students against an autistic group whose educational status is unreported. And the 62.5% female composition of the autistic sample goes unremarked despite the paper's own emphasis on camouflaging." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-115" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'Autism spectrum (AS)' and 'non-AS' throughout, with 'individuals with AS' as the standard construction - and the choice of 'autism spectrum' rather than 'autism spectrum disorder' is deliberate, appearing even in the running head where the journal's own metadata uses ASD. Clinical description centres on 'pragmatic impairment', defined precisely and non-pejoratively as difficulty with 'the effective use of language in social contexts... rather than with the basic structural or grammatical components' - a distinction most papers in this corpus do not draw. The register is that of clinical linguistics rather than of deficit cataloguing, and the introduction engages seriously with autistic experience in ways rare here: it discusses masking behaviour, compensation strategies and learned camouflaging as phenomena that 'can conceal critical information about impairment, potentially leading to misdiagnosis', and notes that adult diagnosis is hampered because caregiver developmental reports are usually unavailable while 'patient self-insight is unreliable'. That framing treats diagnostic difficulty as a property of the encounter rather than of the person. Deficit vocabulary does appear - 'impairment', 'dysfunction', 'core deficits' - and there is no neurodiversity framing or autistic-community involvement, but the paper's characterisation of what it is measuring is more precise and less totalising than the corpus norm." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-115" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Extensive for the autistic group and absent for the comparison group. Available and reported: ADOS-2 Module 3 (6.93 +/- 1.38) and Module 4 (11.42 +/- 3.55) totals; SRS-2 total 85.53 +/- 9.00, indicating substantial social-communication difficulty; AQ 36.64 +/- 8.04, above the usual screening threshold; Vineland-II adaptive composite 64.83 +/- 22.53, well below the normative mean and with a very wide spread; PARS-TR 13.54 +/- 6.27 for preschool items and 24.30 +/- 11.49 for adolescent and adult items; and full-scale IQ from WISC-IV (81.22 +/- 14.42) or WAIS-III (91.33 +/- 20.12). Taken together these characterise a sample with average-to-borderline cognitive ability, marked adaptive-functioning impairment and clear social-communication difficulty - a genuinely informative clinical picture, and one that positions this cohort quite differently from the high-functioning verbal samples that dominate the rest of the review. Two things are not done with this material. None of it is related to model output, so the natural questions - whether linguistic distinctiveness scales with SRS-2 or AQ score, whether misclassified participants have lower ADOS-2 totals - are unasked despite the data being in hand. And no equivalent measure exists for the comparison group, so the severity information cannot be used to characterise the contrast, only one side of it." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-115" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Four configurations spanning two model families, with the trade-off between them argued rather than resolved by accuracy. Logistic regression on normalised tag frequencies serves as the interpretable model, retained explicitly because 'in the medical context, interpretability is crucial because we are dealing with human lives' and because it 'allowed us to identify tags that impacted the outputs and quantify these effects'. Bi-LSTM models serve as the performance-oriented alternative, in three variants: tags only, text only, and tags plus text concatenated at each time step. A fifth model, a separate Bi-LSTM sequence labeller, performs automatic annotation and is retrained within each cross-validation fold. Two aspects of the modelling deserve particular credit. Hyperparameters were adopted from standard values and deliberately not searched, on the stated basis that preliminary work showed the search had little impact - which removes a large source of optimism on a 135-participant dataset. And the authors decline to declare a winner: after observing that the linear model was less precise, they write that 'it is not possible to make an unconditional judgment of which model is better', because interpretability and accuracy are not commensurable in a clinical setting. No other study in this corpus refuses to rank its models by accuracy." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-115" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No instrumentation - the data are transcribed ADOS-2 sessions. The apparatus is analytical and is specified adequately: logistic regression on normalised tag frequencies; Bi-LSTM models with 300-dimensional word and hidden vectors trained with Adam at learning rate 0.001, batch size 32, for 50 epochs; and a separate Bi-LSTM sequence labeller for automatic annotation. The most consequential methodological statement concerns what was not done: the hyperparameters 'were adopted from commonly used values and not explored, as preliminary experiments determined that the impact of the search for hyperparameters' was limited. Declining to search hyperparameters on a 135-participant dataset removes a source of optimism that most studies in this corpus exploit heavily, and doing so deliberately and saying so is exceptional. No software package or version is named, which is the one specification gap." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-115" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Among the two or three soundest validation designs in the review, and sound in ways that require deliberate effort rather than good fortune. The split unit is the participant, with leave-one-out cross-validation formally specified: each of the 135 samples serves once as the test case with the remaining 134 as training data. The step that distinguishes it is the handling of the annotation model. Automatic tag annotation is itself a fitted component, and fitting it once on the whole corpus would leak information from held-out participants into their own annotations; instead the procedure trains the annotator on the training partition of each fold and then uses that fold-specific annotator to label the held-out sample. Very few studies in this corpus recognise that a preprocessing model is a fitted component at all, and none other nests one this carefully. Hyperparameters were adopted from standard values and deliberately not searched, removing the model-selection optimism that inflates most results here. Confidence intervals accompany every accuracy, and model comparisons are tested with McNemar rather than read from point differences, with non-significance correctly not interpreted as equivalence. Two residual concerns are not validation failures but bound what the result means. The comparison group's cognitive and educational level is unmeasured while the autistic group's full-scale IQ is 81-91, so an unquantified ability gap may contribute to the separation. And the transcripts come from the ADOS-2 sessions that produced the labels, so the language sample and the diagnostic judgement arise from the same encounter - which does not leak the label into the features but does mean the model is trained on speech elicited by an examiner who was assessing the participant for autism at that moment." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-115" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No formal keyword list appears in the retrieved text. The organising terms are lexicogrammatical choices, pragmatic impairment, Systemic Functional Linguistics, system network, annotation scheme, corpus of spoken language, Bi-LSTM, leave-one-out cross validation, transfer comparison, differential diagnosis, masking and camouflaging. The vocabulary is drawn from clinical linguistics rather than from machine learning, and the title's framing - 'creating a diagnostic assessment model... by differentiating lexicogrammatical choices' - names a linguistic mechanism rather than an architecture, which is the reverse of the corpus norm." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-115" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The study begins from a specific and well-evidenced clinical problem rather than from a technical opportunity: ADOS-2, the gold standard, has documented limitations for adolescents and adults, because it does not clearly separate autism from ADHD or from the negative symptoms of schizophrenia and other psychiatric comorbidities, and because masking, compensation and camouflaging can conceal impairment. The proposed remedy is a comprehensive mapping of pragmatic impairment through lexicogrammatical analysis, which the authors note has not previously been attempted - prior linguistic work examined single grammatical areas such as modality, relative clauses or syntax, leaving 'the overall picture of the impairment... uncharted'. Findings: on interview transcripts the combined text-plus-tag Bi-LSTM reached 0.82 accuracy (95% CI 0.75-0.89) with AUC 0.90 under automatic annotation, and 0.80 (0.73-0.87) with sensitivity 0.73 and specificity 0.87 under manual annotation. The tag-only linear model reached 0.78 and the text-only model 0.80, and McNemar tests found no significant differences between them on the interview task. The substantive result is the task contrast: every model performed better on interviews than on picture-book recounting, where the linear model fell to 0.60-0.64 and the best model reached 0.78. The authors interpret this as evidence that the discriminative signal lies in socially interactive language use rather than in narrative production generally, which is exactly what a pragmatic-impairment account predicts and is a stronger form of evidence than a single accuracy figure." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-115" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The most thorough reference standard in the review alongside the joint-attention study, and the only one that applies a gold-standard instrument to both groups. Autism diagnosis followed DSM-5 criteria applied by experienced clinicians specialising in neurodevelopmental disorders, with ADOS-2 as the primary confirming instrument administered by someone holding research reliability certified through Western Psychological Services, and supported by a full battery: SRS-2, age-appropriate Wechsler IQ testing, Vineland-II, AQ and PARS-TR caregiver interview. What distinguishes it further is the comparison group. Every non-autistic participant received the ADOS-2 and was confirmed non-spectrum against the published cut-offs, which are quoted (6 or below on Module 3; combined communication and social interaction of 7 or below on Module 4), with group means reported separately for the clinically assessed and the recruited subgroups. Almost every other study in this corpus establishes comparison status by absence of a diagnosis or by parent report; this one administers the same instrument to both sides. The inclusion of co-occurring conditions in the autistic cohort is also a deliberate and well-argued choice rather than an oversight. Two qualifications remain: the diagnosing clinicians were not blind to anything and no inter-rater reliability is reported beyond the administrator's certification, and the language samples analysed come from the same ADOS-2 sessions that generated the labels." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-115" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Interpretability is a design constraint, an argument and a reported finding rather than a post-hoc gloss. The features are categories from an explicit linguistic theory, so a model contribution attaches to a nameable grammatical choice - a modality expression, a particular sentence-final particle, a te-clause subtype - rather than to an embedding dimension, and the linear model is retained specifically so those contributions can be identified and quantified. The authors' refusal to declare the deep model superior on accuracy grounds, on the reasoning that interpretability matters differently when 'we are dealing with human lives', is the clearest statement of that principle in the corpus. Group differences in tag frequencies are tabulated in supplementary material. The annotation system's own errors are analysed rather than assumed away, with an overall F of 0.88 and the te-clause identified as the dominant error source together with an explanation of why - it subdivides into eight classifications whose boundaries are hard to define. What is missing is the individual level: no error analysis identifies which participants were misclassified, and no analysis relates model output to the SRS-2, AQ, Vineland-II or ADOS-2 scores the study holds, which would have tested whether the linguistic signal tracks clinical severity." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-115" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Reported asymmetrically, and the asymmetry is the study's principal weakness. For the autistic group: age 18 +/- 3.48, sex 24 male to 40 female, ADOS-2 Module 3 and 4 totals, SRS-2 total 85.53 +/- 9.00, WISC-IV full-scale IQ 81.22 +/- 14.42 and WAIS-III full-scale IQ 91.33 +/- 20.12, Vineland-II adaptive composite 64.83 +/- 22.53, AQ 36.64 +/- 8.04, and PARS-TR scores for preschool and for adolescent/adult items. This is the most complete clinical characterisation of an autistic sample anywhere in the review. For the comparison group: age 19 +/- 2.77, sex 39 male to 32 female, ADOS-2 totals - and then nothing except a college grade point average range of 2.4 to 2.8, with the authors stating plainly that 'IQ testing was not part of our methodology'. The consequence is a confound that cannot be assessed: the autistic group has measured full-scale IQ between 81 and 91, while the comparison group is three-quarters university students selected for average academic standing, so the groups almost certainly differ substantially in cognitive and educational level, in a study whose entire outcome is the range and complexity of grammatical choices a speaker makes. Two further features deserve note. Age differs significantly (p = 0.00) but by only about a year, so the significance reflects sample size rather than a meaningful gap. And the autistic sample is 62.5% female - the only female-majority autistic cohort in this review, and by a very wide margin - which is unremarked despite the paper's own extended discussion of camouflaging, a phenomenon reported disproportionately in autistic women and directly relevant to whether linguistic markers would be detectable in this sample." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-115" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two representations of the same speech are fused and their contributions separately established. The tag stream encodes the linguistic-theoretical description - which lexicogrammatical options were selected - while the text stream encodes the words themselves, and the combined model concatenates tag and word embeddings at each time step so the Bi-LSTM sees both simultaneously. The ablation is reported across four configurations, two tasks and two annotation methods, and its result is instructive precisely because it is modest: on interviews the tag-only linear model reached 0.78, the tag-only Bi-LSTM 0.80, the text-only Bi-LSTM 0.80 and the combined model 0.80-0.82, with McNemar tests finding no significant differences between them. So the theoretically motivated tag representation and the raw word sequence carry largely overlapping information, and combining them adds at most a point or two. The authors report this honestly rather than claiming a fusion benefit, and note that 'the text-DNN and text+tag-DNN models performed almost the same, with the text+tag-DNN model being marginally higher'. That is a more useful contribution to the review's treatment of multimodality than a claimed gain would have been: it shows that an expensive, theory-driven annotation layer can be approximately recovered from surface text by a sequence model, while remaining far more interpretable - which is why the authors retain it." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-115" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two representations, both derived from the same transcripts. Tags come from a Systemic Functional Linguistics annotation scheme built for Japanese through transfer comparison, comprising four system networks and eleven or more lexicogrammatical headings each with multiple tag types - modality, te-clauses with eight subtypes, twelve negotiating particles, explanative mood and others. For the linear model the input is a tag-frequency vector normalised by total word count, which discards order. For the Bi-LSTM variants the input is the ordered sequence of tag sets, with each word represented by the sum of the embedding vectors of its tags, giving 300-dimensional tag embeddings; the text model uses 300-dimensional word embeddings; and the combined model concatenates the two at each time step. Annotation was performed both manually and automatically, with both evaluated in parallel throughout, and the automatic annotator achieving a mean F of 0.88 against manual - so the study reports both the ceiling achievable with perfect annotation and the performance obtainable in a setting where manual annotation is impossible." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-115" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional diagnostic-classification study built on a purpose-developed corpus of spoken Japanese, combining prospective recruitment with retrospective analysis of diagnostic …" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: ; comparison: 71, comprising 17 who underwent the same comprehensive diagnostic process and met criteria for no psychiatric disorder, and 54 recruited as non-autistic (primarily college students) with ADOS-2 confirming non-spectrum status" ] ] ;
