    slr:journal "PLOS ONE, 19(9), Artic" ;
    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "unclear" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Every participant contributed transcripts from both tasks and all 135 appear in the analysis, with leave-one-out cross-validation partitioning participants. Against this, the language samples were produced during the ADOS-2 administrations that generated the diagnostic labels, so sample and label arise from the same encounter; no attrition or transcript-exclusion accounting is reported; and the seven-year recruitment window is not broken down, so it is unclear whether the two comparison subgroups were recruited contemporaneously with the autistic cohort." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Case-control sampling with autistic participants recruited through a university neuropsychiatry clinic and three-quarters of the comparison group recruited as college students, so clinical status and educational setting co-vary with group. The comparison group's cognitive level was not measured at all - grade point average was substituted for IQ testing - while the autistic group's full-scale IQ is 81-91, so a substantial ability gap is likely and unquantified in a study of grammatical complexity. Mitigating features that do not lift the judgement: the autistic cohort deliberately retains co-occurring conditions on well-argued grounds, the age floor is chosen on a principled linguistic rationale, and 17 comparison participants came through the same clinical pathway." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "DSM-5 diagnosis by experienced neurodevelopmental specialists with ADOS-2 confirmation by a research-reliability-certified administrator, supported by SRS-2, Wechsler IQ testing, Vineland-II, AQ and PARS-TR. Uniquely in this review, the comparison group also received the ADOS-2 and was confirmed non-spectrum against quoted published cut-offs, with means reported for both comparison subgroups. Residual gaps insufficient to raise the rating: no blinding statement and no inter-rater reliability beyond the administrator's certification." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Hyperparameters were adopted from standard values and explicitly not searched; no feature selection is performed; four model configurations are reported in full rather than the best being selected; and model comparisons are tested with McNemar rather than read from point differences. The automatic annotation model, the one fitted preprocessing component, is retrained within each cross-validation fold. Classification is automated and blind to labels at inference." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "unclear" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<https://doi.org/10.1002/aur.2733> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Variability in expressive and receptive language, difficulty with pragmatic language, and prosodic difficulties are all features of autism spectrum disorder (ASD). Quantifying language and voice characteristics is an important step for measuring outcomes for autistic people, yet clinical measurement is cumbersome and costly. Using natural language processing (NLP) methods and a harmonic model of speech, we analyzed language transcripts and audio recordings to automatically classify individuals as ASD or non-ASD. One-hundred fifty-eight participants (88 ASD, 70 non-ASD) ages 7 to 17 were evaluated with the autism diagnostic observation schedule (ADOS-2), module 3. The ADOS-2 was transcribed following modified SALT guidelines. Seven automated language measures (ALMs) and 10 automated voice measures (AVMs) for each participant were generated from the transcripts and audio of one ADOS-2 task. The measures were analyzed using support vector machine (SVM; a binary classifier) and receiver operating characteristic (ROC). The AVM model resulted in an ROC area under the curve (AUC) of 0.7800, the ALM model an AUC of 0.8748, and the combined model a significantly improved AUC of 0.9205. The ALM model better detected ASD participants who were younger and had lower language skills and shorter activity time. ASD participants detected by the AVM model had better language profiles than those detected by the language model. In combination, automated measurement of language and voice characteristics successfully differentiated children with and without autism. This methodology could help design robust outcome measures for future research. Lay Summary: People with autism often struggle with communication differences which traditional clinical measures and language tests cannot fully capture. Using language transcripts and audio recordings from 158 children ages 7 to 17, we showed that automated, objective language and voice measurements successfully predict the child’s diagnosis. This methodology could help design improved outcome measures for research. K E Y W O R D S autism, automated measures, communication, disfluency, natural language processing, pragmatic language, prosody, voice" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "MacFarlane H; Salem AC; Chen L; Asgari M; Fombonne E (2022). Combining voice and language features improves automated autism detection. *Autism Research, 15(7), 1288-1300*. https://doi.org/10.1002/aur.2733" ;
    dcterms:creator "Asgari M",
        "Chen L",
        "Fombonne E",
        "MacFarlane H",
        "Salem AC" ;
    dcterms:title "Combining voice and language features improves automated autism detection" ;
    bibo:doi "10.1002/aur.2733" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_2895bdf0" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2022 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a non-autistic clinical comparison group included?" ;
                            slr:justification "ADHD participants merged with typically developing children explicitly 'to more adequately test for the specificity of the results'." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "Age matched (p = 0.60); sex (p = 0.00065) and IQ (p < 0.001) are not, and the authors list loose matching as a limitation." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are voice-relevant confounds excluded?" ;
                            slr:justification "Current psychoactive medication, thyroid disorder, head injury and several psychiatric conditions all excluded." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are analysis exclusions balanced across groups?" ;
                            slr:justification "14 of 17 analysis exclusions fell on the autistic group, including 6 who produced neither um nor uh." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Full flow from 289 screened to 158 analysed, with sex, age, IQ, race, ethnicity, ADOS and CCC-2 scores tabulated and tested." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the unmatched variables tested for their effect?" ;
                            slr:justification "Sex-stratified AUCs (0.8901 boys, 0.8067 girls) do not differ from the full sample; IQ and ADOS severity never predicted misclassification in any model." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the effect of preprocessing on the acoustic features known?" ;
                            slr:justification "The authors state denoising is essential - without it 'the acoustic features mainly represent the noise' - and that its full impact is unknown." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is transcription reliability quantified?" ;
                            slr:justification "Extensive training, consensus resolution and biannual consistency checks are described, but the authors state 'we do not have transcription reliability results'." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the features individually interpretable?" ;
                            slr:justification "All seventeen are named quantities with stated definitions and clinical rationale." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the measures computed blind to diagnosis?" ;
                            slr:justification "Transcribers were blind to both diagnostic status and intellectual ability; acoustic features are computed automatically." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "Best-estimate DSM-IV-TR by a two-discipline team reviewing videos and scored protocols, with ADOS-2 by research-reliable administrators." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Microphone type and placement with rationale; the task, its selection criteria, and its position within the ADOS all specified." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the validation scheme justified against the methodological literature?" ;
                            slr:justification "Leave-one-subject-out adopted with citation to Saeb et al. (2017) on subject-wise versus record-wise validation." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a chance or trivial baseline reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No permutation null or demographic-only baseline is provided." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the unit of analysis the participant?" ;
                            slr:justification "One averaged feature vector per participant, with leave-one-subject-out cross-validation." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are model comparisons statistically tested?" ;
                            slr:justification "DeLong tests for correlated ROC curves on every pairwise comparison, with 95% confidence intervals on all AUCs." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the cross-validation result checked for robustness?" ;
                            slr:justification "10-fold gave 0.9235 against leave-one-out 0.9205, p = 0.9219." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is feature selection reported honestly?" ;
                            slr:justification "The optimal eight-feature subset (0.9344) did not significantly beat the full model (0.9235, p = 0.1592), and the full model remains the headline." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is there an independent replication cohort?" ;
                            slr:justification "Single site, and the sample overlaps with the two prior papers whose models are being replicated." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are limitations stated candidly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Ten distinct limitations including possible overestimation from the ADOS setting, unknown test-retest reliability, absent transcription reliability statistics and unknown denoising effects." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the intended scope of the work stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Explicitly not for diagnostic or screening use; diagnosis serves as an external criterion for validating outcome measures." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the fusion benefit mechanistically established?" ;
                            slr:justification "Participant-level profiling shows the language model detects lower-language children and the voice model detects higher-language children, with no language measure predicting the voice model's successes." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "OHSU IRB approval with informed written consent or assent from all participants and their parents at the initial visit." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Analysis code released at a stated GitHub URL - one of very few studies in the corpus to do so." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "De-identified data available on request from the authors; no repository." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Interpretability is built into the design rather than added afterwards, and it operates at three levels. The features are individually meaningful by construction - mean length of utterance, filled-pause ratio, disfluency proportion, F0, shimmer, loudness - so any model weight is directly interpretable, in contrast to the learned representations used by the deep-learning speech studies elsewhere in this corpus. mSVM-RFE ranks them and identifies an optimal eight, evenly split between modalities, though the selection is honestly reported as producing no significant gain. And the misclassification analysis functions as the deepest interpretive layer, answering not 'which features matter' but 'which children does this measure see', which is the question that determines clinical utility. Its findings are specific and consequential: the language model is biased toward younger children with poorer language and less output; the voice model is unbiased with respect to language level; the residual misses have the highest IQ and most language output; and neither ADOS severity nor IQ predicts misclassification in any model - from which the authors draw the appropriately hedged conclusion that 'if replicated, this result could confirm that our voice and language measurement methodology specifically taps aspects of communication skills that are not confounded by autism severity or developmental delay'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-080" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Leave-one-subject-out cross-validation, and uniquely in this corpus the choice is justified by citation to the methodological literature on exactly this issue - Saeb et al. (2017) on subject-wise versus record-wise validation - with the rationale that it approximates 'the clinically relevant use-case of diagnosis in unseen participants'. Because each participant contributes a single averaged feature vector, there is no possibility of the within-subject leakage that inflates results throughout this review. Robustness was checked against 10-fold cross-validation, giving 0.9235 against 0.9205 with p = 0.9219, confirming the estimate is not an artefact of the resampling scheme. Comparisons between models use DeLong's test rather than informal inspection. Sex-stratified models were fitted to test whether the sex imbalance drove the result. The confounding variables were tested rather than acknowledged. What remains: the soft-margin constants were chosen per model on performance and the linear configuration was selected for reporting over tuned non-linear alternatives, so some selection optimism is present though bounded by the small hyperparameter space; feature selection was performed under 10-fold rather than leave-one-out for software reasons; and there is no independent replication cohort - the sample is a single site and overlaps with the two prior papers whose findings are being replicated, so the 'replication' is of models rather than of data. The authors' own framing is appropriately cautious about all of this, describing the results as 'preliminary but very encouraging'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-080" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'autistic people', 'autistic children', 'autistic individuals', 'people with and without autism', 'children with ASD', 'individuals with autism' - identity-first and person-first used interchangeably and comfortably, with identity-first predominating in general statements about the population. 'Patients' never appears. The framing is explicitly respectful and says so: 'this growing detection and awareness of autism requires thoughtful and respectful research to help facilitate communication between people with and without autism' - which frames the goal as bidirectional communication rather than as remediating a deficit. Communication is described as involving 'differences' and 'variability' at least as often as 'difficulties', and the lay summary opens with 'People with autism often struggle with communication differences which traditional clinical measures and language tests cannot fully capture' - locating the inadequacy in the measures rather than in the people. This is among the most careful terminology in the corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-080" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Acquisition is minimal and reusable - a single suspended microphone three feet from the child during a routine ADOS administration - which is what makes the measures retrofittable to existing clinical archives. The analytic pipeline is where the equipment complexity sits: a harmonic model of speech with model-based jitter and shimmer estimation, adopted because 'standard methods for calculating jitter and shimmer are sensitive to the pitch estimation'; a pretrained fully automated deep-neural-network denoiser applied before feature extraction; and R for all statistical analysis with code released. The denoising step warrants attention because its necessity is stated unusually starkly: 'Recordings from both groups contain similar, ubiquitous noise not caused by participants. If denoising is not performed, the acoustic features mainly represent the noise instead of participants and the classifier cannot distinguish between groups.' Applying the denoiser identically to both groups is the correct control, and stating that raw acoustics were noise-dominated is candid, but it means the entire voice result is conditional on a black-box preprocessing step whose interaction with group-specific vocal characteristics is, as the authors concede, unknown." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-080" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, sensitivity, specificity and AUC with 95% confidence intervals for all three models, with DeLong tests for every pairwise AUC comparison - the appropriate test for correlated ROC curves derived from the same participants, and one that almost no other study in this corpus uses. The confidence intervals do real work: the voice model's interval (0.7076-0.8525) and the combined model's (0.8768-0.9641) do not overlap, so that comparison is visibly supported, whereas the language and combined intervals do overlap and the DeLong test is correspondingly marginal at p = 0.033. Reporting sensitivity and specificity separately is what makes the complementarity finding legible - the voice model's specificity of 0.6857 is the weakness that the language model repairs, and the combined model's unchanged specificity alongside raised sensitivity is the signature of the two modalities catching different people. The misclassification analysis adds a class of evidence absent elsewhere: Mann-Whitney comparisons of age, IQ, two CCC-2 scales, two ADOS domains and three task indices between detected and missed participants, for each of the three models separately, with the counterpart analysis for non-autistic participants in supplementary material. No chance or trivial baseline is reported, which is the one gap." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-080" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two analytic groups from three clinical ones: 88 autistic participants and 70 non-autistic participants, the latter formed by merging children with ADHD and typically developing children. The merge is justified on two stated grounds and both are substantive - preliminary analyses showed no significant difference between the ADHD and typically developing subgroups on any automated language measure, and 'including a non-autistic clinical group alongside TD participants provided an opportunity to more adequately test for the specificity of the results with respect to the presence or absence of autism'. This is the corpus's clearest instance of a study building the specificity test into its comparison group rather than naming it as future work. Flow accounting is complete: 289 screened, 104 excluded for not meeting strict diagnostic criteria, 10 for failing to complete initial assessment, leaving 175 in the parent neuroimaging study (102 ASD, 45 ADHD, 28 TD); 6 autistic participants excluded for untranscribable ADOS sessions due to poor sound quality or non-compliance; and 11 more excluded for analysis - 6 with undefined um proportion because neither um nor uh occurred in the task, 4 without IQ results, and 1 with an acoustically corrupted file. Note that the exclusions fall disproportionately on the autistic group (14 of 17), and that the 6 excluded for producing no filled pauses at all are plausibly among the least verbally fluent." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-080" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Oregon Health & Science University, with participants recruited through community outreach and referral from OHSU specialty clinics into an fMRI study - so the sample is a research cohort rather than a consecutive clinical series. Recording arrangements are described precisely and with their rationale: 'The microphone was suspended in a cage and placed on a table approximately 3 feet from where the child sat. This allowed the recording to accurately capture the child's language, even if, as is common in ADOS Module 3 administrations, the child sometimes moved around the room before being reminded to sit down.' That sentence does two things few papers here manage - it specifies the acquisition geometry and it explains the specific behaviour the geometry was designed to accommodate. The authors nonetheless flag microphone quality and placement as insufficiently controlled and recommend tightening it in future work to reduce reliance on denoising." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-080" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "One 17-dimensional vector per participant, produced by averaging frame-level acoustic measures across the task and computing transcript-level language measures over the same interval, then normalising all features to [0,1]. The unit is therefore the participant throughout, which is what makes leave-one-subject-out cross-validation meaningful. One representational property is worth noting because the authors use it defensively and correctly: almost every measure is length-normalised, being either an average over frames or c-units or a proportion computable on any sample length, the sole exception being number of distinct word roots. This lets them rebut the objection that the nine missed autistic participants escaped because their unusually long transcripts broke the measures - 'it is highly unlikely that the measures did not perform well for high language output, and more likely that high IQ and older children had fewer communication difficulties, making them harder to detect'. That is an argument about measurement invariance made from the construction of the features rather than from post hoc testing." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-080" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Autism Research 15(7):1288-1300 (2022), the journal of the International Society for Autism Research - a specialist autism venue with clinical and behavioural-science readership rather than an engineering one. Authorship spans OHSU's Department of Psychiatry, the Institute on Development and Disability and the Department of Pediatrics, with the senior author being a prominent autism epidemiologist and the two first authors marked as contributing equally, one from the language side and one from computation. Funding is from NIH and specifically the National Institute on Deafness and Other Communication Disorders. The venue and authorship explain the paper's balance: full clinical characterisation, careful terminology, statistical inference appropriate to a clinical audience (DeLong tests, confidence intervals, non-parametric group comparisons), and modelling that is deliberately conservative." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-080" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The most complete limitations section in the corpus, running to ten distinct items, several of which the authors were under no obligation to raise. Sample scope: ages 7-17 only; all participants had IQ in the normal range; all were fluent English speakers assessable with Module 3, so 'it is important for future studies to include individuals with lower IQ and less verbal participants'. Design: 'Participants were not tightly matched between groups which could result in reduced fit for analysis', notwithstanding the confound tests. Features: the set was deliberately limited to previously validated measures and should be expanded and tested component by component. Preprocessing: 'We do not know the full impact of denoising on voice features and results', with a recommendation to control microphone quality and placement to reduce the need for it. Setting: the samples come from ADOS administrations in a clinical research setting and cannot be generalised to other contexts, and because 'ADOS administration focuses on eliciting autistic features... it is possible that our results are overestimated compared to what would be obtained with more naturalistic conversational samples'. Reliability: test-retest and short-term stability are unknown. Transcription: manual transcription is costly and labour-intensive, and 'though the transcribers went through extensive training and regular consistency reviews, we do not have transcription reliability results'. In the discussion the authors additionally raise, unprompted, that examiners may have facilitated and maximised the language output of the more able participants, citing Bone et al., and that parents' CCC-2 scores 'could have been negatively biased in this subset due to their child's ASD diagnosis'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-080" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A single ADOS-2 Module 3 conversational task, chosen on three stated criteria that jointly amount to a principled sampling argument: it occurs in the second half of the assessment after the warm-up period, so the child is acclimatised; it 'consistently yields the most c-units of all ADOS tasks', so the sample is maximally dense; and it 'has a high standardization of conversational questions, leading to good comparability between participants', using pre-established open-ended interview questions. Two independent prior analyses from this group had converged on this task as the most discriminative. The paradigm's practical strength is that it requires no new protocol - the recording already exists in any videotaped ADOS - and its analytic strength is that all 158 participants completed the identical task, so between-participant differences are not confounded with differences in what was asked. The corresponding limitation is stated by the authors themselves: 'ADOS administration focuses on eliciting autistic features, thus it is possible that our results are overestimated compared to what would be obtained with more naturalistic conversational samples'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-080" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Linear-kernel SVMs throughout, chosen deliberately over more flexible alternatives and justified by prior use in speech-and-autism work. Non-linear SVMs and formal hyperparameter tuning were run and relegated to supplementary material with the linear configuration reported in the main text - which is the correct ordering when the goal is validating interpretable measures rather than maximising accuracy, though it does mean the reported soft-margin constants (C = 10, 1 and 100 for the three models) were selected on performance. The restraint is consistent with the study's stated purpose: 17 interpretable features, a linear classifier, and an emphasis on which participants are detected rather than on how high the number can be pushed. The mSVM-RFE analysis then delivers a genuinely useful negative result - the optimal eight-feature subset (0.9344) did not significantly outperform the full 17-feature model (0.9235, p = 0.1592), so feature selection bought nothing, and the authors report this rather than promoting the higher figure as their headline." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-080" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: autism, automated measures, communication, disfluency, natural language processing, pragmatic language, prosody, voice. Six of eight are linguistic or communicative constructs rather than computational methods - 'disfluency', 'pragmatic language' and 'prosody' name specific behavioural phenomena - which accurately signals a paper whose subject is communication measurement with machine learning as instrument, rather than the reverse." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-080" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Seven directions, each traceable to a stated limitation. Extend to adults and younger children, and to lower-IQ and minimally verbal participants who cannot complete Module 3. Test each component feature systematically to establish individual predictive power. Determine which voice features drive the voice model's false positives, with a specific and testable hypothesis - 'it could be that loudness is a general non-specific feature of young children interviewed on sensitive topics (as those covered by the Friends task) in an unfamiliar setting; if so, removal of these non-contributory features might improve the classification performance of voice-only models'. Control microphone quality and placement to reduce dependence on denoising. Establish test-retest reliability and short-term stability, which are prerequisites for the intended outcome-measure application. Sample naturalistic conversation outside the clinic, 'aiming at minimizing interference by strangers and capturing naturally occurring conversations in the ecological niche of the participants'. And replace manual transcription with automatic speech recognition, with the explicit caveat that 'future studies based on ASR transcription will need to re-evaluate performance of the models relying on ALMs' - recognising that ASR errors would propagate into disfluency and maze measures that depend on precise transcription." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-080" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Among the strongest ascertainments in the corpus and comparable to its companion paper. Diagnosis was confirmed by experienced child psychiatrists and clinical psychologists who directly assessed every participant in the autistic and ADHD groups under DSM-IV-TR criteria, followed by a research diagnostic team reviewing 'results of the standardized diagnostic assessments (both videos and scored protocols)' and applying best-estimate procedures. ADOS-2 Module 3 was administered to all participants by research assistants or a senior clinical psychologist trained to research reliability level. Autism was actively ruled out in both comparison subgroups using the ADOS, clinical interview and parent-completed autism questionnaires - so negative cases are excluded on three independent sources rather than assumed. The screening list is extensive and clinically targeted, excluding seizure disorder, cerebral palsy, paediatric stroke, chemotherapy history, sensorimotor handicaps, closed head injury, thyroid disorder, schizophrenia, bipolar disorder, current major depressive episode, fetal alcohol syndrome, Tourette's disorder, severe vision impairment, Rett syndrome, current psychoactive medication, non-English speakers and IQ below 70. That 104 of 289 screened participants were excluded 'for not meeting strict diagnostic criteria' indicates the criteria were applied stringently rather than nominally." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-080" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three explicit aims, each addressed. First, replicate the voice model of Asgari et al. (2021) and the language model of Salem et al. (2021) and compare their discriminant validity using the same methodology in the same sample - achieved, with language (AUC 0.8748) significantly better than voice (0.7800, DeLong p = 0.036), the difference lying in specificity (0.83 versus 0.69). Second, quantify the gain from combining them - the combined model reached AUC 0.9205, significantly better than voice (p < 0.001) and language (p = 0.033), with accuracy 0.8671, sensitivity 0.8977 and specificity 0.8286; the entire gain is in sensitivity while specificity is unchanged. Third, characterise which participants each model detects - the language model caught children with poorer structural and pragmatic language, less output and one year younger; the voice model caught children with better language profiles and showed no association between language measures and classification success; only nine autistic participants (10.2%) escaped the combined model, and these had the highest IQ and most language output of any subgroup." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-080" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Fully reported with formal testing on every variable. Autistic group: 88 participants, 73 male (83.0%), 11.4 +/- 2.1 years, IQ 99.5 +/- 19.9. Non-autistic group: 70 participants, 40 male (57.1%), 11.5 +/- 1.7 years, IQ 112.1 +/- 12.9. Race and ethnicity are tabulated for both groups (predominantly white, 13 and 8 Hispanic). Age matches (Mann-Whitney p = 0.6044); sex does not (Fisher's exact p = 0.00065); IQ does not (p < 0.001). What distinguishes this study is that both mismatches are tested rather than merely conceded. For sex, the combined model was refitted separately in boys (n = 113, AUC 0.8901, 95% CI 0.8261-0.9541) and girls (n = 45, AUC 0.8067, 95% CI 0.6493-0.9640), neither differing significantly from the full-sample AUC (p = 0.4428 and p = 0.178). For IQ, the misclassification analysis found that IQ was never significantly associated with correct or incorrect classification in any of the three models. The authors nonetheless list loose matching among their limitations - 'Participants were not tightly matched between groups which could result in reduced fit for analysis' - rather than treating the confound tests as closing the question." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-080" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two distinct behavioural constructs measured from the same recording. Language targets expressive complexity and pragmatic function through mean length of utterance, lexical diversity, disfluency structure, rate of production, intelligibility and echoic repetition of the examiner's immediately preceding words - the last of these operationalising a specifically autism-relevant behaviour. The um-versus-uh proportion is the subtlest measure and reflects a well-established finding that the two filled pauses signal different planning difficulties. Voice targets paralinguistic production: pitch, cycle-to-cycle frequency and amplitude perturbation, harmonics-to-noise ratio as a hoarseness index, H1H2 as a breathiness index, spectral envelope and entropy, and RMS loudness. The paper's central empirical claim about these constructs is that they are separable in the participants they identify - the language measures track language ability, the voice measures do not, and the voice measures therefore capture 'paralinguistic features (e.g., prosody, loudness) that are truly independent of language level and output'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-080" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Among the best in the corpus. Reported: the complete participant flow from 289 screened to 158 analysed with a reason and count at every step; the full exclusion criteria list; the diagnostic procedure at each stage; the ADOS module, administrator training level and task selection with rationale; microphone type, placement distance and its justification; the transcription protocol, unit definition, disfluency notation, blinding of transcribers to diagnosis and IQ, consensus resolution procedure and biannual consistency checks; the three-module audio pipeline; the precise definition of all seven language measures and all ten voice measures; the SVM kernel, the soft-margin constant for each of the three models, feature normalisation, and the fact that non-linear SVMs and formal tuning were also run and reported in supplementary material; the cross-validation scheme with its citation-backed justification and its 10-fold robustness check; the DeLong test for AUC comparison; the mSVM-RFE procedure with an explicit note that 10-fold was used because the software could not run under leave-one-out; and the statistical tests for every comparison. Analysis code is publicly released and de-identified data offered on request. What is missing, and acknowledged: transcription reliability statistics, despite the training and consistency checks; and the effect of the denoiser." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-080" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional; one assessment per participant, with the intended application being longitudinal outcome measurement rather than cross-sectional classification. The authors are explicit that the reliability evidence needed for that application does not yet exist: 'we do not yet know the reliability (test-retest) and short-term stability of the measures we generated from transcripts and audio recordings', though they state they are currently undertaking those studies. This is an important self-limitation, since an outcome measure without demonstrated test-retest stability cannot distinguish treatment effect from measurement noise. Ethics reporting is complete: IRB review and approval at OHSU, with informed written consent or assent obtained from all participants and their parents at the initial visit. A data availability statement offers de-identified data on request and gives a public GitHub URL for the analysis code." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-080" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The best-evidenced fusion result in the corpus, on three counts. The improvement is statistically tested rather than asserted: DeLong's test for correlated ROC curves gives p < 0.001 against the voice model and p = 0.033 against the language model, with 95% confidence intervals on all three AUCs (0.7800, 0.8748, 0.9205). The improvement's structure is diagnosed: the combined model's specificity is identical to the language model's (0.8286) while sensitivity rises from 0.7727 to 0.8977, so the voice features recovered autistic participants the language model missed without importing the voice model's false-positive tendency - which is exactly the asymmetric gain one would predict if the modalities are complementary rather than redundant, and the authors note it explicitly. And the mechanism is established at participant level rather than inferred from the numbers: children detected by the language model had significantly lower CCC-2 structural and pragmatic scores and less output, while for the voice model 'the CCC-2 scores and the Friends task indices did not differentiate correctly or incorrectly classified participants', which is the direct evidence that the acoustic channel indexes something orthogonal to language level. The clinical implication the authors draw follows properly from that evidence: voice analysis is needed 'to detect communication differences among subjects with ASD who have achieved high language skills', concurring with the observation that voice atypicalities persist in individuals with otherwise typical outcomes. Compared with Han et al. and Liao et al., who also demonstrate complementary failure directions via confusion matrices, this study goes further by characterising the complementarity in terms of participant clinical profiles rather than class labels." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-080" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Complete and used analytically throughout. IQ was estimated from a three-subtest WISC-IV short form (Information, Block Design, Vocabulary) with the Sattler and Dumont formula, and the authors note honestly that separate verbal and non-verbal estimates cannot be derived from this short form. The Children's Communication Checklist-2 was completed by parents, with its structure described in detail - ten subscales, the first four averaged into a Structural Language scale and four more into a Pragmatic scale, with the last two measuring behaviours characteristic of autism. ADOS-2 Social Affect and Restricted and Repetitive Behavior scores were computed under the Gotham et al. algorithm. Three objective language-output indices were additionally computed from the task itself: total c-units, total words and activity time. All of these then serve as predictors in the misclassification analysis, which is the paper's most original contribution - they are not merely descriptive but are the variables through which the complementarity of the two modalities is established." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-080" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2022. The framing is unusual for this corpus in three respects. First, it declines the diagnostic frame outright: 'This work (past and current) is not currently intended to be employed for diagnostic or screening purposes. Rather, our methodology uses already-established diagnosis as an external criterion against which we can validate (in the sense of discriminant validity) these voice and language measures.' The target is outcome measures for trials and longitudinal studies, and the paper closes on that application. Second, the critique of existing instruments is specific rather than generic - conventional tests like the CELF-4 and PPVT 'evaluate structural aspects of language' and must be augmented by natural language sampling; parent-report instruments such as the CCC-2 have validity limits 'as illustrated in treatment studies that have shown caregiver reports to be susceptible to the placebo effect'; and, echoing the group's companion paper, the ADOS and SRS 'include only one item each on voice quality' despite prosodic difference being a cited characteristic of autism. Third, the epidemiological framing is calibrated rather than alarmist, citing a worldwide prevalence range of 0.043% to 2.68% and noting that 'reasons for the increasing prevalence rate are uncertain' - written by an author who has published extensively on exactly that question. The comparison against prior work is likewise careful, noting that Tanaka et al.'s sample of four autistic and two typically developing participants made its findings 'unreliable', and that the sampling contexts of the compared studies differed from this one so that 'examination of contextual effects should be vigorously pursued'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-080" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "One ADOS-2 Module 3 administration per participant, with analysis confined to a single conversational task averaging 6.5 to 7.9 minutes" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional classification with three specific aims - replicate two prior single-modality models within one sample using one methodology, quantify the gain from combining them,…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "School-age children and adolescents with autism and fluent speech" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 88; comparison: 70, comprising typically developing children and children with ADHD combined into a single non-ASD group" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Language measures and voice measures extracted automatically from a single seven-minute ADOS-2 conversation classified autism at AUC 0.8748 and 0.7800 respectively, and their combination at 0.9205 - a statistically significant gain over both, established by DeLong tests on correlated ROC curves rather than by comparing point estimates. The mechanism of the gain is identified rather than assumed: t",
                        "Voice-only: accuracy 0.7215, sensitivity 0.7500, specificity 0.6857, AUC 0.7800 (95% CI 0.7076-0.8525). Language-only: accuracy 0.7975, sensitivity 0.7727, specificity 0.8286, AUC 0.8748 (0.8199-0.9297) - significantly better than voice (DeLong p = 0.036), the advantage lying entirely in specificity. Combined: accuracy 0.8671, sensitivity 0.8977, specificity 0.8286, AUC 0.9205 (0.8768-0.9641), sig" ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy, sensitivity, specificity and ROC AUC with 95% confidence intervals for all three models; DeLong tests for all pairwise AUC comparisons; sex-stratified AUCs; and Mann-Whitney comparisons of age, IQ, four clinical scores and three task indices between correctly and incorrectly classified par" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Voice-only: accuracy 0.7215, sensitivity 0.7500, specificity 0.6857, AUC 0.7800 (95% CI 0.7076-0.8525). Language-only: accuracy 0.7975, sensitivity 0.7727, specificity 0.8286, AUC 0.8748 (0.8199-0.9297) - significantly better than voice (DeLong p = 0.036), the advantage lying entirely in specificity. Combined: accuracy 0.8671, sensitivity 0.8977, specificity 0.8286, AUC 0.9205 (0.8768-0.9641), sig; Language measures and voice measures extracted automatically from a single seven-minute ADOS-2 conversation classified autism at AUC 0.8748 and 0.7800 respectively, and their combination at 0.9205 - a statistically significant gain over both, established by DeLong tests on correlated ROC curves rather than by comparing point estimates. The mechanism of the gain is identified rather than assumed: t" ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "8f35e1ff983f78a6cefaec858be5eccfcb12adcf44a9b56d60124dfc1b3dd032" ;
