    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Splitting is by scan-path image rather than by participant, with roughly sixteen images per child, and no subject-disjoint evaluation appears anywhere. Augmented image counts are quoted for whole datasets rather than training partitions, implying rotated duplicates on both sides of the split - and test accuracy rises monotonically with augmentation level in the sex-by-age experiment (85.07%, 85.81%, 98.84%), the characteristic signature of that leakage. Training accuracy is 100% in essentially every table. Exposure to individual video clips ranged from 1 to 92 viewings, so participants did not see the same material." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "None described. The retrieved text contains no diagnostic instrument, criteria, clinician, procedure or date for either group, and no cognitive or severity measure - despite an abstract that grounds its motivation in looking patterns changing with 'intellectual and social impairments'." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Eighty-six of 99 enrolled children with valid data, with the 13 excluded uncharacterised and the autistic total reported inconsistently as 54 or 55. No inclusion or exclusion criteria, recruitment route or setting is described. The groups differ markedly on both variables that are reported: 81.5% male in the autistic group against 46.9% in the comparison group, and 31.5% versus 15.6% aged 10 or over, with all four participants aged 18+ being autistic. The paper acknowledges that the data are 'unbalanced with respect to diagnosis, age, and gender' and takes no account of it in the diagnosis analysis." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Dropout and epoch settings were swept with the better reported in each table and no separate development set described. Rotation-based augmentation was applied at three escalating levels in one experiment with the highest-augmentation result reported as the finding. Per-clip ablation, temporal windowing and velocity encoding were all evaluated on the same data. No threshold or operating point is prespecified, and no chance baseline is computed." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "high" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<https://doi.org/10.1186/s13229-022-00517-2> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Background: Across behavioral studies, autistic individuals show greater variability than typically developing individuals. However, it remains unknown to what extent this variability arises from heterogeneity across individuals, or from unreliability within individuals. Here, we focus on eye tracking, which provides rich dependent measures that have been used extensively in studies of autism. Autistic individuals have an atypical gaze onto both static visual images and dynamic videos that could be leveraged for diagnostic purposes if the above open question could be addressed. Methods: We tested three competing hypotheses: (1) that gaze patterns of autistic individuals are less reliable or noisier than those of controls, (2) that atypical gaze patterns are individually reliable but heterogeneous across autistic individuals, or (3) that atypical gaze patterns are individually reliable and also homogeneous among autistic individuals. We collected desktop-based eye tracking data from two different full-length television sitcom episodes, at two independent sites (Caltech and Indiana University), in a total of over 150 adult participants (N = 48 autistic individuals with IQ in the normal range, 105 controls) and quantified gaze onto features of the videos using automated computer vision-based feature extraction. Results: We found support for the second of these hypotheses. Autistic people and controls showed equivalently reliable gaze onto specific features of videos, such as faces, so much so that individuals could be identified significantly above chance using a fingerprinting approach from video epochs as short as 2 min. However, classification of participants into diagnostic groups based on their eye tracking data failed to produce clear group classifications, due to heterogeneity in the autistic group. Limitations: Three limitations are the relatively small sample size, assessment across only two videos (from the same television series), and the absence of other dependent measures (e.g., neuroimaging or genetics) that might have revealed individual-level variability that was not evident with eye tracking. Future studies should expand to larger samples across longer longitudinal epochs, an aim that is now becoming feasible with Internet- and phone-based eye tracking. © The Author(s) 2022. Open Access This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License, which permits use, sharing, adaptation, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if changes were made. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article’s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitte" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Keles U; Kliemann D; Byrge L; Saarimäki H; Paul LK; Kennedy DP; Adolphs R (2022). Atypical gaze patterns in autistic adults are heterogeneous across but reliable within individuals. *Molecular Auti*. https://doi.org/10.1186/s13229-022-00517-2" ;
    dcterms:creator "Adolphs R",
        "Byrge L",
        "Keles U",
        "Kennedy DP",
        "Kliemann D",
        "Paul LK",
        "Saarimäki H" ;
    dcterms:title "Atypical gaze patterns in autistic adults are heterogeneous across but reliable within individuals" ;
    bibo:doi "10.1186/s13229-022-00517-2" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_dffe4127" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2022 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is generalisation scope stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Restriction to adults with IQ in the normal range is justified and then listed as a limitation on generalising to children and lower-IQ individuals." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the comparison group screened with an instrument?" ;
                            slr:justification "Autism Spectrum Quotient administered to both groups (15.70 versus 28.06)." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is differential exclusion examined?" ;
                            slr:justification "17% of autistic participants versus 3% of controls excluded for gaze data quality, reported precisely but not discussed." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is severity characterised across its range?" ;
                            slr:justification "ADOS CSS-Overall 7.04 +/- 1.98 with a full range of 3-10, and AQ in both groups." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "Age, sex and full-scale IQ all formally tested and non-significant; the only corpus study to equate IQ in a well-powered sample." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Recruitment channels, both sites' criteria, and complete flow from 183 recruited to 153 analysed with reasons and counts." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are areas of interest defined without experimenter judgement?" ;
                            slr:justification "Automated DensePose and RetinaFace segmentation with a stated geometric rule, detection rates over 61,724 frames, and manual verification of reconstructed overlays." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is tracking quality prevented from contaminating the behavioural measures?" ;
                            slr:justification "Region gaze normalised by frames with successful on-screen gaze, with a partial correlation control confirming reliability survives partialling out on-screen time." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Tracker, sampling rate, resolution, monitor, viewing distance, and a nine-point calibration plus validation with a 1.5-degree redo threshold repeated before each stimulus part." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is stimulus familiarity controlled?" ;
                            slr:justification "Measured on a nine-point scale, participants split at the midpoint, and every reported result verified not to differ." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are acquisition errors disclosed?" ;
                            slr:justification "Loss of validation data to a computer error and 120 Hz recording for 12 participants due to user error are both reported against the authors' interest." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "ADOS-2 Module 4 with the Hus and Lord algorithm and stated tolerances, plus independent clinical confirmation at one site." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the correct hypothesis tested for reliability?" ;
                            slr:justification "Between-group difference in reliability is bootstrap-tested, not merely whether each group is individually reliable." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a complementary unsupervised analysis run?" ;
                            slr:justification "Variational Bayesian mixture model learning cluster number from data, robust to initialisation at 5, 10 and 20." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is per-participant classification behaviour reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "95 correctly classified and 32 misclassified in more than 75% of iterations, revealing the bimodal structure behind the aggregate accuracy." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are effect sizes reported with uncertainty?" ;
                            slr:justification "Cohen's d with bootstrap confidence intervals from 10,000 resamples for every area of interest in both episodes." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the unit of analysis the participant?" ;
                            slr:justification "One feature vector per participant per epoch, with a held-out third of each group over 10,000 cross-validation iterations." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the chance level established empirically?" ;
                            slr:justification "0.607 from a permutation null rather than an assumed 0.500 - the distinction that determines the paper's conclusion." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are results replicated across stimuli and data quantities?" ;
                            slr:justification "Every analysis repeated across two distinct episodes and across epoch durations from 10 minutes down to seconds." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are multiple comparisons corrected?" ;
                            slr:justification "False discovery rate correction applied throughout." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are negative severity correlations reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No gaze feature correlated with ADOS calibrated severity, reported explicitly." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Not mentioned, though every tool is named and every procedure specified." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No availability statement in the extracted text; stimulus copyright is noted as constraining figure reproduction." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a null result reported and interpreted rather than buried?" ;
                            slr:justification "Classification at 0.618 against 0.607 chance is reported as the central finding and used to draw the paper's principal conclusion." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No statement in the extracted text." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are limitations stated candidly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Three substantive limitations, each with a remedy, including an alternative interpretation the authors construct and then argue against on mechanistic grounds." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Interpretability is pursued through study design rather than through model attribution, and is stronger for it. Four analyses jointly characterise what the gaze data contain. The effect-size profile across areas of interest, with bootstrap confidence intervals in both episodes, shows where the group differences lie and that they replicate. The reference-heatmap comparison establishes that the autistic and control group averages are nearly identical (r = 0.966) while individual autistic participants depart from both - which localises the phenomenon at the individual rather than group level. The fingerprinting analysis quantifies how much individually distinctive information the same features carry, and the four-versus-eight-feature comparison shows the information is distributed rather than concentrated. And the per-participant classification and clustering frequencies show which individuals the group-level model can and cannot place. The authors summarise the feature-level picture honestly as 'a rather distributed effect over heatmaps and facial features, with no single feature driving the effect', and note the caveat that their features were intercorrelated and their videos always contained people. The absence of a correlation between any gaze feature and ADOS severity is also reported, and is an important negative: the measures separate groups on average without indexing individual severity." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-078" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Thorough and site-specific. Caltech required three conditions jointly: a prior diagnosis by a qualified clinician holding an MD or PhD; confirmation via clinical interview with the participant, and a parent where available, conducted by a named licensed clinical psychologist among the authors; and ADOS-2 Module 4 scores meeting a precisely stated criterion - at or above cutoff on at least two components of the original algorithm and within one point on the third, or within one point of the total cutoff under the Hus and Lord revised algorithm with Social Affect plus Restricted Repetitive Behaviors >= 7. Indiana University required a prior community diagnosis of ASD, autism, Asperger's syndrome or PDD-NOS, plus ADOS-2 Module 4 administered by research-reliable administrators meeting the same revised-algorithm criterion. Naming the exact scoring algorithm and the tolerance around cutoffs, and stating that the two sites differed in whether independent clinical confirmation was additionally required, is more precise ascertainment reporting than any other study in this review provides. The comparison group was characterised with the AQ rather than by exclusion alone." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-078" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional with two measurement blocks on the same day separated by a break, drawn from a larger study that also included extensive behavioural assessment and neuroimaging. The authors identify the temporal limitation themselves as their second main limitation: 'we collected only two [blocks], both close in time and for similar (but distinct) video episodes', and argue that longer-term longitudinal data with dense state measures such as sleepiness and mood would be needed to distinguish measurement error from genuine state-dependent within-individual change. No ethics or consent statement appears in the extracted text, though the study is embedded in an established multi-site research programme. Copyright constraints on the sitcom prevented reproducing stimulus frames, and a royalty-free substitute image was used for the segmentation figure." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-078" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Free viewing of two episodes of a commercial television sitcom, chosen on an argued rationale rather than by convenience. The authors state that naturalistic video was selected over 'much more impoverished and artificial stimuli typically employed in earlier studies (such as cropped photographs of grayscale faces)' because movies deliver 'high participant engagement, and reducing confounds to data quality including sleepiness and motion artifacts', and because the same stimuli had proved effective in the group's prior neuroimaging work. Two distinct episodes were used specifically to test whether within-individual reliability holds across stimuli rather than merely across time, which is the stronger test. Three design controls attach to the stimulus. Prior familiarity with each episode was measured on a nine-point scale, participants split into familiar and unfamiliar, and every reported result verified not to differ between them. Gaze during the social-comprehension question pauses in Episode B was excluded. And the analysis showed that gaze to faces differed substantially between the two episodes (Cohen's d 1.15 in the autistic group, 1.70 in controls) while the between-group differences remained stable - establishing that the group effect is a property of the participants rather than of the particular video, which is precisely the confound that stimulus-specific studies elsewhere in this corpus cannot exclude." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-078" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups, with complete flow accounting. 183 individuals were initially recruited (67 autistic, 20 female, 22 at Caltech; 116 typically developing, 33 female, 34 at Caltech). 176 watched Episode A (62 autistic) and 167 watched Episode B (59 autistic); analyses were restricted to the 166 who watched both (58 autistic). Thirteen were then excluded for eye-tracking data quality - specifically, missing or off-screen gaze for more than half of one or both episodes - leaving 48 autistic and 105 typically developing participants. The exclusion was strongly differential: 10 of 58 autistic participants (17.2%) against 3 of 108 controls (2.8%), a figure reported precisely but not discussed. Its direction matters for interpretation: the analysed autistic sample is the subset whose gaze could be tracked reliably throughout 43 minutes of video, which if anything strengthens rather than weakens the paper's conclusion that classification fails, since the participants most likely to produce noisy data were removed. Participants were split across two independent sites, with 17 autistic and 33 control participants at Caltech and the remainder at Indiana University. No other-diagnosis comparison group." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-078" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Tobii TX300 at 300 Hz with 0.4-degree spatial resolution on a movable 23-inch 1920x1080 monitor at approximately 65 cm. Calibration discipline is the most rigorous in the corpus and is reported with its failure handling: a nine-point calibration followed by a nine-point validation quantifying error at all nine locations spanning the full screen, with results displayed immediately to the experimenter, who would adjust the screen or participant and repeat if any point exceeded 1.5 degrees of error - and the procedure repeated before each of the three parts of Episode A. The authors also report, against their own interest, that 'a computer error resulted in the loss of the validation data, so further quantitative assessment of validation accuracy is not possible', and that 12 participants were recorded at 120 Hz rather than 300 Hz 'because of a user error', noting that downsampling to the 24 Hz video frame rate compensated for the difference. Reporting both errors is a form of candour absent elsewhere in this corpus. Feature extraction used Detectron2 with DensePose and InsightFace with RetinaFace, and clustering used scikit-learn." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-078" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Fully reported with formal testing on every variable, in a table with test statistics and p values. Autistic group: 48 adults, 79.2% male, 27.69 +/- 5.37 years (18-41), full-scale IQ 111.38 +/- 14.98 (84-150), ADOS CSS-Overall 7.04 +/- 1.98 (3-10), AQ 28.06 +/- 7.67. Comparison group: 105 adults, 70.5% male, 27.09 +/- 7.29 years (19-55), full-scale IQ 110.01 +/- 10.20 (85-136), AQ 15.70 +/- 6.41. None of age (t(151) = -0.501, p = 0.617), sex (chi-squared = 0.864, p = 0.353) or IQ (t(140) = -0.637, p = 0.525) differed; only AQ did (t(151) = -10.314, p < 0.001), as it should. Eleven participants with missing IQ scores were explicitly excluded from that comparison and the exclusion is footnoted. This is the only study in the corpus to equate IQ between groups in a well-powered sample rather than acknowledging an IQ gap as a limitation, and the equating was achieved by restricting recruitment to adults with IQ in the normal range - a design choice the authors justify on the grounds of reducing 'variation related to general inattention, difficulty following instructions, or difficulty understanding the video stimuli', while acknowledging that it limits generalisation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-078" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2022. The framing is a deliberate inversion of the rest of this literature. Rather than arguing that clinical diagnosis is subjective and that automated classification would be objective, the paper starts from the premise that autism 'has so far defied any simple diagnostic markers or cognitive mechanisms' and asks what property of the data would have to hold for classification to be possible at all. It then names the field's characteristic omission precisely: 'Across ASD eye tracking studies, this is a major unanswered question: what portion of the variance in findings can be assigned to between-individual variability, and what portion can be assigned to within-individual variability?' - and observes that 'individual-level reliability is a prerequisite for successful classification and prediction'. That reframing converts a methodological question into a substantive one and produces a result the accuracy-maximising literature could not have produced: a demonstration that the measurement is reliable, the group difference is real and replicable, and the classifier nonetheless fails, because reliability and classifiability are different properties. The paper's positioning of eye tracking is correspondingly measured - it emphasises the modality's practical advantages, its applicability from infancy to adulthood, its resistance to volitional control and its emerging feasibility on phones and over the internet - while directing those advantages toward subtype discovery, longitudinal monitoring and intervention assessment rather than toward diagnosis." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-078" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two independent sites - Caltech and Indiana University - using 'otherwise identical eye trackers and video stimuli', with the two-site design adopted explicitly 'to optimize generalizability'. Recruitment was through flyers distributed at outpatient treatment programmes, community support groups and community locations including college campuses and YMCAs, so the sample is community-recruited rather than clinic-referred - which matters for representativeness, though the authors note the sample 'is not representative of ASD in general'. Site membership is carried through the analysis and displayed in every figure by marker shape, so site effects remain visible rather than being pooled away. This is the only multi-site study in the corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-078" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Eight, then four, interpretable scalar features per participant per epoch - each a percentage of gaze time to an automatically detected region, or a correlation with a reference heatmap. Two representational decisions carry substantial methodological weight. Areas of interest were generated frame by frame by pretrained computer vision models rather than hand-drawn, removing experimenter judgement about what counts as a face or a hand and making the approach applicable to arbitrary naturalistic video; detection rates are reported (96.2% of 61,724 frames containing a person; 94.9% with face parts) and the automatic segmentations were manually verified by watching reconstructed overlay videos. And gaze-time percentages to each region were normalised by the number of frames with successful on-screen gaze rather than by total frames, explicitly so that individual differences in tracking success could not bias the region-specific measures - the direct remedy for the data-quality-as-signal pattern that recurs throughout this corpus, where tracking failure rates differ by group and can masquerade as behaviour. A partial correlation analysis then verified that the reliability findings were not explained by on-screen gaze time alone." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-078" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The most statistically careful reporting in the corpus, and the one that determines the paper's central conclusion. Group differences are reported as Cohen's d with bootstrap confidence intervals from 10,000 resamples rather than as p values alone, with the authors stating they 'preferred Cohen's d to provide a practical estimate for the magnitude of an effect of interest... rather than just reporting the results of statistical tests'. Reliability is reported as Spearman's rho with a bootstrap test of the between-group difference in reliability - testing not whether each group is reliable but whether they differ in reliability, which is the actual hypothesis. Fingerprinting accuracy is tested against a permutation null with 10,000 label shuffles. Most importantly, classification accuracy is compared to an empirically derived chance level of 0.607 rather than to an assumed 0.500 - a distinction that entirely changes the reading of the 0.618 result, since against a nominal 50% baseline it would have appeared as a modest success. FDR correction is applied throughout. And per-participant classification frequencies across 10,000 iterations are reported, revealing that the aggregate accuracy conceals a bimodal structure: 95 participants classified correctly more than 75% of the time and 32 misclassified more than 75% of the time, 22 of those autistic. Reporting who is misclassified, rather than only how often, is what converts a null accuracy into a substantive finding about heterogeneity." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-078" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The agenda follows directly from the findings and is unusually specific. Scale up via internet-based (WebGazer) and smartphone-based eye tracking to obtain the sample sizes needed to identify subtypes, which the authors argue is now feasible and is made more so by their own finding that 2-minute epochs suffice. Collect longitudinal data with dense state-dependent measures - sleepiness, mood - to separate measurement error from genuine within-individual variation across weeks or years, and to assess intervention efficacy while revealing what an intervention changes ('gaze to faces but not to other features might change throughout training'). Design stimuli with specifically chosen visual features to maximise within-individual reliability and between-individual differences, which 'possibly... could also improve the classification analyses'. And integrate with neuroimaging and genetics, on the reasoning that systematic gaze differences 'would be expected to translate to differences also in evoked BOLD-fMRI activations' and 'may be an endophenotype reflecting substantial genetic effects'. The through-line is that establishing individual-level reliability was a prerequisite for all of these, which is the paper's framing of its own contribution." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-078" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Comprehensive and used analytically rather than descriptively. Full-scale IQ was measured in both groups and reported with range and test statistic; the AQ was administered to both groups; and ADOS-2 Overall Calibrated Severity Scores derived by the Hus and Lord algorithm were obtained for the autistic group with a full range of 3 to 10, meaning the sample spans the severity spectrum rather than clustering at one end. Severity was then correlated against all four gaze features, with the finding that none reached significance - a negative result that matters, since it means the gaze measures that do differentiate groups on average do not index how autistic an individual is. Comorbidity handling is deliberate and unusual: depression, anxiety and ADHD were permitted rather than excluded on the grounds that they are common in autism, with only moderate-to-severe depression (BDI-II >= 25) and antipsychotic medication ruled out. The authors then list this inclusiveness among their own limitations, noting it leaves open how much comorbidity contributed to the findings - a rare instance of a design decision being defended and then flagged as a threat in the same paper." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-078" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: determine whether the excess behavioural variability of autistic participants reflects within-individual unreliability or between-individual heterogeneity, by testing three mutually exclusive hypotheses. Main findings, in the order they eliminate hypotheses: within-individual reliability was high and statistically indistinguishable between groups at every epoch duration (Spearman's rho > 0.635, between-group difference p > 0.333), and individuals were identifiable from their own gaze at 35% against a 3-6% chance level from as little as 2 minutes of video, ruling out hypothesis 1; the autistic and typically developing reference heatmaps were correlated at r = 0.966 and autistic individuals matched the autistic reference less well than controls did, and unsupervised clustering split the autistic group nearly in half rather than separating it from controls, ruling out hypothesis 3; supervised classification reached 0.618 against an empirical chance level of 0.607 with 22 of 48 autistic adults reliably misclassified, confirming hypothesis 2. Group differences themselves were moderate and robust: Cohen's d 0.60-0.82, replicated across two episodes and detectable from 2-minute epochs." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-078" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Identity-first throughout: 'autistic individuals', 'autistic adults', 'autistic people', 'autistic participants', used consistently in the title, abstract and body, against 'typically developing (TD) controls'. 'ASD group' appears as a technical label for the analytic group but 'patients' never does, and no individual is described as 'an ASD'. The framing is notably non-deficit even where deficits are reported: autism is characterised as 'a complex and heterogeneous disorder that has so far defied any simple diagnostic markers or cognitive mechanisms', gaze differences are described as 'atypical' rather than impaired, and heterogeneity is treated as a scientific finding to be explained rather than as noise to be controlled. This is the most careful terminology in the corpus and is consistent with the paper's substantive argument, which is that treating autistic people as an interchangeable group is what makes classification fail." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-078" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Among the most reproducibly reported studies in the corpus. Specified: recruitment channels; both sites' diagnostic criteria including the exact ADOS-2 algorithm, cutoff and tolerance; the complete flow from 183 recruited to 153 analysed with reasons and counts at each step; eye tracker model, sampling rate, resolution, monitor size, viewing distance, calibration and validation protocol with its error threshold and repetition rule; both recording errors; stimulus identity, duration and part structure; the familiarity questionnaire and its cut-point; the complete area-of-interest construction including the specific models used, the merging of 24 DensePose parts into three, and the geometric rule for deriving eye and mouth regions from five keypoints; detection rates across all 61,724 frames; the manual verification of automatic segmentation; the downsampling to video frame rate, the 1-degree gaze disk and the maximum-overlap assignment rule; the normalisation of area-specific gaze by successful on-screen frames with its stated rationale; heatmap construction with Gaussian width, bin duration and the leave-one-out reference rule; the bootstrap, permutation and sampling procedures with their 10,000 iteration counts; classifier, held-out sizes and cross-validation repetitions; the mixture-model initialisation and its robustness checks; and the FDR correction. What is missing from the extracted text is an ethics statement, a data availability statement and any mention of code release." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-078" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The reference standard for this corpus, and the source of its most transferable methodological lesson. The classification procedure holds out a third of each group, samples a 10-minute epoch at random from the combined 43-minute dataset, trains on the remainder and repeats 10,000 times - so the estimate is not a single split and every participant is held out repeatedly. Decisively, the chance level is derived empirically from a null distribution and comes to 0.607 rather than the 0.500 that an imbalanced 48/105 design might naively suggest; the observed 0.618 therefore exceeds chance by about one percentage point at p = 0.039, which the authors correctly describe as 'only slightly greater than would be expected by chance' and as confirming that heterogeneity 'precludes diagnostically robust classification'. Had the same number been compared against 50%, it would have been reported as a 12-point improvement. Beyond classification, the validation apparatus includes: leave-one-participant-out construction of every reference heatmap 'to eliminate bias'; normalisation of area-specific gaze by successful on-screen frames so that tracking quality cannot leak into the behavioural measures; a partial correlation control confirming reliability is not driven by on-screen time; measurement of stimulus familiarity and verification that it affects no result; replication of every analysis across two distinct stimuli and across epoch durations from 10 minutes down to seconds; bootstrap confidence intervals on all effect sizes; permutation testing for fingerprinting; FDR correction throughout; and two independent sites with site membership displayed rather than pooled. The single most important output for this review is the fingerprinting result itself: gaze identifies an individual out of 153 at five to ten times chance from 2 minutes of data. That is a direct measurement of the individual-identity signal that any train/test split failing to separate participants will allow a classifier to exploit, and it explains quantitatively why studies here that split by image, segment, task or augmented copy report accuracies far above what participant-level designs achieve." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-078" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The modelling is deliberately simple and the choice is argued: a Gaussian naive Bayes classifier on four features, because the study's question is whether diagnostic information exists in the gaze data at all, not how much a flexible model can extract from 153 participants. The complementary unsupervised analysis uses a variational Bayesian Gaussian mixture model, which learns the number of clusters from the data rather than being told it - an important property here, since forcing two clusters would have guaranteed a partition that could be compared against diagnosis, whereas learning the number allows the data to indicate no meaningful split. It returned predominantly two clusters (25 of 33 windows) and was robust to initialisation at 5, 10 or 20 components. Pairing a supervised classifier with an unsupervised clustering that asks the same question from the opposite direction, and finding that both fail to recover diagnosis, is stronger evidence than either alone." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-078" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Single modality by design, and the paper is explicit that this is a limitation of its interpretation rather than a methodological choice to be defended: other measures 'would have revealed higher variability in ASD' is entertained as a live possibility. No fusion is attempted. The paper does perform an internal combination that is informative for the fusion literature elsewhere in this corpus: fingerprinting with all eight gaze features and with a reduced set of four gave nearly identical identification accuracy (32.6% versus 35.4% for the autistic group), because the removed features were highly correlated with the retained ones - non-social, non-head-body and hand gaze with face gaze, and mouth with eyes. That redundancy check, performed and reported rather than assumed, is the discipline missing from studies that report improvements from adding correlated features." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-078" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Social visual attention during naturalistic viewing, resolved into a hierarchy of automatically detected regions: faces, eyes within faces, mouth, hands, non-head body parts, and non-social content, plus two global measures - total on-screen gaze time and the correlation of a participant's moment-by-moment gaze heatmap with a group reference. The heatmap measure is the conceptually distinctive one, since it captures moment-to-moment conformity with what other viewers looked at rather than aggregate time in a region, and it produced the largest group effects (d = 0.820 and 0.692). Its use also generates the paper's most consequential single analysis: constructing a reference heatmap from the autistic group and finding that it correlates at r = 0.966 with the control reference, while autistic individuals nonetheless match it less well than controls do. That result establishes directly that there is no shared autistic pattern of looking - the average autistic viewer looks where the average control viewer looks, and individual autistic viewers depart from that average in individually different directions." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-078" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: eye tracking, autism, heterogeneity, individual differences, videos. Three of five name the study's actual subject - heterogeneity and individual differences - rather than a method or a performance target, which is unique in this corpus and accurately signals a paper whose contribution is conceptual rather than algorithmic." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-078" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Molecular Autism 13:39 (2022), an autism-specific journal with a mechanistic and clinical readership, published open access. Authorship spans Caltech's Division of the Humanities and Social Sciences with computation and neural systems affiliations, the University of Iowa, Indiana University, Tampere University and a joint senior authorship shared between the two site leads. The group's prior work is cited as motivation and critiqued in the process - the paper explicitly identifies the limitation of its own earlier study as the gap it now addresses. Publishing a null classification result in an autism journal, framed as informative rather than as a failed experiment, is itself notable given that the corpus's positive-accuracy studies appear predominantly in engineering venues." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-078" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three limitations are stated, each substantive and each with a proposed remedy - the most serious limitations section in the corpus. First, the sample is relatively small and 'not representative of ASD in general', and consists of adults with IQ in the normal range, so 'it remains unclear if our findings would generalize to children and individuals with lower IQ'; the inclusion of comorbid depression, anxiety and ADHD is folded into this as a further open question. Second, only two temporal blocks were collected, both on the same day, so within-individual precision is limited both by measurement error and potentially by state-dependent change, and longer-term longitudinal data across more diverse stimuli would be needed. Third, and most carefully argued, the interpretation rests on having measured only eye movements: 'It remains possible that other dependent measures - performance tasks or questionnaires based on interpreting the videos, or neuroimaging - would have revealed higher variability in ASD', including the specific alternative that autistic people might have unreliable cognition that they compensate for. The authors then argue against their own alternative rather than merely listing it, noting that 'eye movements are not under strong volitional control' and the stimuli are highly engaging, so 'a top-down compensatory mechanism thus seems implausible'. Two issues go unremarked: the differential exclusion rate for gaze data quality (17% of autistic participants versus 3% of controls), and the fact that the two sites used different diagnostic confirmation procedures." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-078" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Two full television episodes per participant on the same day separated by a break - approximately 22 and 21 minutes, combined into a 43-minute dataset for the sampling analyses" ;
                    slr:study_design "Two-site cross-sectional case-control study with repeated measurement across two stimuli in the same session, combining group-difference estimation, within-individual test-retest r…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autistic adults with IQ in the normal range" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 48 analysed, from 67 initially recruited; comparison: 105 analysed, from 116 initially recruited" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Classification accuracy was 0.618 +/- 0.058 against an empirical chance level of 0.607 - a difference of about one percentage point, p = 0.039. Of 153 participants, 95 (15 of them autistic) were correctly classified in more than 75% of iterations, while 32 (22 of them autistic) were misclassified in more than 75% of iterations - so 22 of 48 autistic adults, 46%, were reliably assigned to the wrong",
                        "The same dataset that yields moderate, replicable group differences in gaze to faces and eyes (Cohen's d 0.60-0.82, stable across two episodes and detectable in 2 minutes of video) yields classification performance at essentially chance: 0.618 accuracy against an empirically derived chance level of 0.607, with 46% of autistic adults reliably misclassified. The reason is established rather than spe" ;
                    slr:outcome "Cohen's d with bootstrap confidence intervals for group differences; Spearman's rho for within-individual reliability with bootstrap tests of between-group differences in reliability; fingerprinting identification accuracy against a permutation-derived chance level; classification accuracy against a" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Classification accuracy was 0.618 +/- 0.058 against an empirical chance level of 0.607 - a difference of about one percentage point, p = 0.039. Of 153 participants, 95 (15 of them autistic) were correctly classified in more than 75% of iterations, while 32 (22 of them autistic) were misclassified in more than 75% of iterations - so 22 of 48 autistic adults, 46%, were reliably assigned to the wrong; The same dataset that yields moderate, replicable group differences in gaze to faces and eyes (Cohen's d 0.60-0.82, stable across two episodes and detectable in 2 minutes of video) yields classification performance at essentially chance: 0.618 accuracy against an empirically derived chance level of 0.607, with 46% of autistic adults reliably misclassified. The reason is established rather than spe" ] ] ;
