            slr:overall_judgment "low" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2023.952433> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "EDITED BY Lauren Franz, Duke University, United States REVIEWED BY Barbara Le Driant, EA7273 Centre de Recherche en Psychologie Cognition, Psychisme et Organisations, Université de Picardie Jules Verne, France, Xiaoli Li, Beijing Normal University, China *CORRESPONDENCE Kota Iwauchi iwauchi.kota.ig9@is.naist.jp SPECIALTY SECTION This article was submitted to Digital Mental Health, a section of the journal Frontiers in Digital Health RECEIVED 25 May 2022 ACCEPTED 30 January 2023 PUBLISHED 16 February 2023 CITATION Iwauchi K, Tanaka H, Okazaki K, Matsuda Y, Uratani M, Morimoto T and Nakamura S (2023) Eye-movement analysis on facial expression for identifying children and adults with neurodevelopmental disorders. Front. Digit. Health 5:952433. doi: 10.3389/fdgth.2023.952433 COPYRIGHT © 2023 Iwauchi, Tanaka, Okazaki, Matsuda, Uratani, Morimoto and Nakamura. This is an open-access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (CC BY). The use, distribution or reproduction in other forums is permitted, provided the original author(s) and the copyright owner(s) are credited and that the original publication in this journal is cited, in accordance with accepted academic practice. No use, distribution or reproduction is permitted which does not comply with these terms. Eye-movement analysis on facial expression for identifying children and adults with neurodevelopmental disorders Kota Iwauchi 1*, Hiroki Tanaka 1, Kosuke Okazaki 2, Yasuhiro Matsuda 2,3, Mitsuhiro Uratani 2, Tsubasa Morimoto 2 and Satoshi Nakamura 1 1Augmented Human Communication Laboratory, Nara Institute of Science and Technology, Ikoma, Nara, Japan, 2Department of Psychiatry, Nara Medical University School of Medicine, Kashihara, Nara, Japan, 3Osaka Psychiatric Medical Center, Osaka, Japan Experienced psychiatrists identify people with autism spectrum disorder (ASD) and schizophrenia (Sz) through interviews based on diagnostic criteria, their responses, and various neuropsychological tests. To improve the clinical diagnosis of neurodevelopmental disorders such as ASD and Sz, the discovery of disorder- speciﬁc biomarkers and behavioral indicators with sufﬁcient sensitivity is important. In recent years, studies have been conducted using machine learning to make more accurate predictions. Among various indicators, eye movement, which can be easily obtained, has attracted much attention and various studies have been conducted for ASD and Sz. Eye movement speciﬁcity during facial expression recognition has been studied extensively in the past, but modeling taking into account differences in speciﬁcity among facial expressions has not been conducted. In this paper, we propose a method to detect ASD or Sz from eye movement during the Facial Emotion Identiﬁcation Test (FEIT) while considering differences in eye movement due to the facial expressions presented. We also conﬁrm that weighting using the differences improves classiﬁcation accuracy. Our data set sample consisted of 15 adults with ASD and Sz, 16 controls, and 15 children with ASD and 17 controls. Random forest was used to weight each test and classify the participants as control, ASD, or Sz. The most successful approach used heat maps and convolutional neural networks (CNN) for eye retention. This method classiﬁed Sz in adults with 64.5% accuracy, ASD in adults with up to 71.0% accuracy, and ASD in children with 66.7% accuracy. Classifying of ASD result was signiﬁcantly different (p<.05) by the binomial test with chance rate. The results show a 10% and 16.7% improvement in accuracy, respectively, compared to a model that does not take facial expressions into account. In ASD, this indicates that modeling is effective, which weights the output of each image." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Iwauchi K; Tanaka H; Okazaki K; Matsuda Y; Uratani M; Morimoto T; Nakamura S (2023). Eye-movement analysis on facial expression for identifying children and adults with neurodevelopmental disorders. *Fr*. https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2023.952433" ;
    dcterms:creator "Iwauchi K",
        "Matsuda Y",
        "Morimoto T",
        "Nakamura S",
        "Okazaki K",
        "Tanaka H",
        "Uratani M" ;
    dcterms:title "Eye-movement analysis on facial expression for identifying children and adults with neurodevelopmental disorders" ;
    bibo:doi "10.3389/fdgth.2023.952433" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_74416ba7" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2023 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participant selection and spectrum representativeness" ;
                            slr:justification "Fifteen autistic adults and fifteen autistic children recruited through psychiatric services, all without intellectual difficulties but with no IQ measured. The authors state the sample comprises 'only a small number of mild (high-functioning) cases' and that it remains unclear whether findings extend across the spectrum - an accurate self-assessment." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Comparison group adequacy" ;
                            slr:justification "The strongest comparison structure in this review after the human-benchmark study: neurotypical controls actively screened by physicians and psychologists, plus a psychiatric comparison group of 15 adults with schizophrenia assessed with PANSS. The schizophrenia group is not used to build a differential classifier, which is a missed opportunity, but its presence supports the parallel analysis that yields the paper's most valuable finding." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Confounder control" ;
                            slr:justification "Group sizes were equalised, but by ranking controls on SRS-2 - a variable related to the classified construct. Adult groups differ by up to six years in mean age with very different variances; children's groups are 87% versus 47% male; autistic children score 17.6 against 6.88 on the ADHD Rating Scale, an unaddressed attentional confound in a gaze task. None of these is adjusted. Against this, FEIT recognition scores were equivalent across all groups, so differential task success is excluded as an explanation for the gaze differences." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Handling of missing data and attrition" ;
                            slr:justification "Two explicit exclusion rules - scan-path length of zero, and a minimum 40% gaze-sample retention - with the resulting per-emotion trial counts listed exactly so attrition is visible. But the eight participants whose gaze never left the centre were retained and misclassified by every model; the error analysis identifies them precisely and diagnoses the cause, which is more than any other study here does, though no handling procedure follows." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reference standard quality" ;
                            slr:justification "DSM-5 research criteria with ADOS-2 re-evaluation for autism (adult mean total 46.5 +/- 11.9) and PANSS for schizophrenia (67.1 +/- 13.0); controls screened by clinician interview with stated exclusions for psychiatric history, drug abuse, epilepsy, learning delay and communication problems. Every group has an explicit ascertainment procedure." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome measurement blinding" ;
                            slr:justification "The measurement chain from gaze sampling through event classification to modelling is automated and label-free. Verbal emotion responses were scored against an objective key." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Measurement standardisation" ;
                            slr:justification "Identical eye tracker, 120 Hz sampling, 65 cm viewing distance, display resolution, stimulus set and trial timing for all participants, with a one-second fixation cross standardising the starting gaze position. The I-VT threshold is stated at 100 degrees per second and areas of interest are redrawn per image so that mouth-region measurement is not biased by expression-dependent mouth size." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Index test definition and prespecification" ;
                            slr:justification "Three gaze features fixed a priori from cited literature, no feature selection, architectures fixed in advance with a stated small-data rationale, and the learning rate as the only tuned quantity, optimised inside the nested inner loop." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Sample size adequacy for the modelling" ;
                            slr:justification "Thirty to thirty-one participants per classification problem, so one person is roughly three accuracy points and the 10-point and 16.7-point weighting gains correspond to about three and five participants. The authors identify small sample size as their first limitation. Per-fold training sets of 630-651 trials give an appearance of adequacy that 30 independent participants do not support." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance estimation and chance level" ;
                            slr:justification "Group sizes equalised so chance is 50%, every accuracy tested against it with a binomial test, and the failing result (schizophrenia, p = 0.052) reported as marginal rather than rounded into significance - the clearest chance handling in this corpus. Missing: confidence intervals, AUC, and any adjustment for the fact that four model variants were compared per problem." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data splitting and leakage control" ;
                            slr:justification "Nested leave-one-participant-out cross-validation with all of a held-out person's 21 or 32 trials excluded from training, and the learning rate tuned in the inner loop. Trial-level leakage is structurally impossible, which matters because the per-trial classifier operates on stimulus images shown identically to everyone." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Preprocessing and feature selection nesting" ;
                            slr:justification "No feature selection occurs. The random-forest weighting layer is trained within folds and its importances averaged across them rather than fitted once on the full sample." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "unclear" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reporting of results and uncertainty" ;
                            slr:justification "Accuracy, sensitivity and specificity for all four variants across all three problems, plus binomial tests against chance with the failing p-value shown. Per-emotion means and standard deviations tabulated for every feature. But no confidence intervals anywhere, no AUC, and no correction for comparing four model variants per problem." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Ethics, consent and conflicts" ;
                            slr:justification "Approved by ethics committees at both Nara Medical University and Nara Institute of Science and Technology; the procedure explained and informed consent obtained at the start of recording, with written consent from legal guardians recorded in the formal statement. Funding from a single CREST grant stated; competing interests declared as none." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Acknowledgement of limitations" ;
                            slr:justification "Three limitations, all substantive: small sample, absence of IQ data with an explicit statement that the sample is mild and high-functioning, and - unusually - a documented failure mode found by inspecting the participants that every model misclassified, showing that some people complete the task using peripheral vision without moving their eyes, quantified at 6.5% of adults and 17% of children. Not acknowledged: the trial-as-unit statistical analysis, the SRS-2-based control selection, and the age and sex imbalances." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Completeness of methods reporting" ;
                            slr:justification "Instrument battery, FEIT protocol and timing, eye tracker and sampling rate, viewing distance, I-VT threshold, area-of-interest definitions and per-image redrawing, both exclusion rules with resulting trial counts, scan-path pseudocode, both architectures with layer and kernel sizes, optimiser, loss, epoch cap, early-stopping patience, heat-map construction with Gaussian sigma and downscaling, and the nested cross-validation with its inner split - all specified. Statistical procedures named including the Benjamini-Hochberg correction." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data, code and protocol availability" ;
                            slr:justification "Raw data offered by the authors without undue reservation; model architectures fully tabulated and the scan-path algorithm published as pseudocode. No code repository and no preregistration. The FEIT stimuli are under copyright and could not be reproduced even as figures." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The Facial Emotion Identification Test, a validated instrument that uses morphing to generate expressions at varying intensity, including deliberately weak ones - which is central to the paper's argument, since the finding it converges on concerns subtle rather than prototypical expressions. Each trial: a one-second central fixation cross, then a face for 5 seconds, then a verbal response naming the emotion. Adults saw 21 images (three each of happiness, sadness, fear, anger, surprise, disgust, neutral) and children 32 (eight each of happiness, sadness, anger, surprise), all in random order. Three properties make this among the better paradigms in the corpus. It has an objectively correct answer on every trial, so task performance and gaze behaviour are separately measurable - and the result is striking: FEIT scores were essentially identical across groups (adults 15.5, 14.5 and 14.3 out of 21 for controls, autism and schizophrenia; children 22.1 and 22.2 out of 32), meaning the groups recognised the emotions equally well while looking at the faces differently. Any gaze difference therefore cannot be attributed to differential task success. The stimulus set is systematically structured by emotion, which is what makes per-image weighting meaningful and interpretable rather than an arbitrary ensemble. And the one-second fixation cross standardises the starting gaze position across trials and participants, so scan-path measures begin from a common origin. The paradigm's limitations are the short trial (5 seconds, giving at most 600 gaze samples), the static stimuli, and the copyright restriction that prevents the images being reproduced or shared." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-104" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The stated aim is to test whether modelling the specificity of gaze to particular facial expressions improves classification, on the premise - supported by prior basic research but not previously operationalised - that groups differ in gaze for some emotions and not others. The proposal is to classify each of the 21 or 32 FEIT images separately and then aggregate the per-image decisions with a random forest that learns which images matter, rather than by unweighted voting. Findings, all reported with binomial tests against chance and none oversold. For autistic adults versus controls the weighted CNN reached 0.710 accuracy (sensitivity 0.733, specificity 0.688), significantly above chance and 10 points above the unweighted CNN. For autistic children the weighted models reached 0.667 (weighted CNN sensitivity 0.600, specificity 0.733), significant and 16.7 points above the unweighted CNN, with the weighting raising sensitivity from 0.286 to 0.600. For schizophrenia the best models reached 0.645, only marginally above chance (p = 0.052), and weighting produced no improvement at all. The statistical analysis explains why the method behaves differently across conditions and is the more interesting result: in adults, reduced fixation at the eyes distinguished the schizophrenia group from controls for every one of the seven emotions, but the autistic group from controls for only two (anger and neutral), while scan-path length distinguished autism from controls for six of seven emotions. So the classic reduced-eye-looking signature was present uniformly in schizophrenia and only selectively in autism - which is exactly the condition under which per-image weighting helps for one and not the other. In children, almost no gaze feature reached significance at all, yet the weighted models still exceeded chance." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-104" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Reported per group in two tables and more completely than most in this corpus, though with gaps. Adults: n and sex ratio, age, Kikuchi's Scale of Social Skills 18-item, SRS-2, FEIT score, ADOS-2 and PANSS as applicable. Children: n and sex ratio, age, SRS-2, FEIT score, ADHD-RS and CBCL. Three imbalances are visible and none is adjusted. The adult groups span six years of mean age (Sz 26.0 +/- 5.85, control 29.3 +/- 3.72, ASD 32.1 +/- 9.13) with very different variances, and age affects both gaze behaviour and face processing. The children's groups differ sharply in sex - 13:2 male:female in the autistic group against 7:8 in controls, so 87% versus 47% male - which is the largest sex imbalance in a matched-design study charted here. And autistic children scored more than twice as high on the ADHD Rating Scale as controls (17.6 +/- 10.2 versus 6.88 +/- 9.40), so attentional difficulty is confounded with diagnosis in a task whose outcome is where the eyes go. Not measured at all: IQ, which the authors identify as a limitation and which matters because they characterise the sample as high-functioning without being able to quantify it; and race or ethnicity, relevant here because Japanese participants viewed the FEIT's Western facial stimuli, an other-race configuration that goes unremarked. What is unusually well reported is the phenotypic battery: SRS-2 in both datasets, ADOS-2 totals for autistic adults, PANSS for schizophrenia, plus social-skills and behaviour-checklist measures, with a clear explanation of why SRS-2 was not administered to the schizophrenia group." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-104" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Visual attention during facial emotion recognition, measured in three ways and analysed separately for each emotion presented. Fixation counts within anatomically defined areas of interest - the eyes region extending up to the eyebrows, the nose and the mouth - index where attention is allocated on the face. Saccade counts index how often gaze shifts. Scan-path length, defined as the mean displacement between consecutive gaze samples, indexes how far and how restlessly the eyes travel. The paper's behavioural hypothesis is the one the field takes for granted, that autistic people look less at the eyes; its finding is that this holds much more clearly for schizophrenia than for autism in these data. In adults, fixation at the eyes separated the schizophrenia group from controls for all seven emotions with p values from .0001 to .01, but separated the autistic group from controls for only two of seven (anger and neutral). What did separate autistic adults from controls, across six of seven emotions, was scan-path length - they moved their gaze further per sample. Fixation at the mouth separated autism from controls for five of seven emotions but never separated schizophrenia from controls. So the two conditions produce distinguishable gaze signatures: schizophrenia is characterised by uniformly reduced eye-region fixation, autism by increased mouth fixation and longer scan paths that vary with the expression shown. In children, almost nothing reached significance - only eye fixation for anger. A secondary behaviour is measured and turns out to be null: emotion recognition accuracy, which was essentially identical across all groups in both datasets, so the gaze differences occur without any difference in task success." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-104" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "One of the two or three soundest validation designs in this review. The scheme is nested leave-one-participant-out cross-validation: in each outer fold a single participant is held out entirely, with all 21 or 32 of their trials excluded from training, and the inner loop with an 8:2 training/validation split is used to optimise the learning rate. Placing hyperparameter selection inside the outer loop is stated explicitly and is the correct construction; the great majority of studies in this corpus either tune on the test set or do not say. Because the split unit is the participant, the trial-level leakage that inflates results throughout this literature - where a person's own segments appear on both sides of the split - is structurally impossible here, and this matters more than usual because the per-trial classifier operates on individual FEIT images that would otherwise be trivially matched across folds. Class balance was engineered in advance by equalising group sizes, so chance is approximately 50% and is not an artefact of prevalence. And every reported accuracy is tested against that chance level with a binomial test, with the failing result reported as failing. The consequences are visible in the numbers: accuracies of 0.645 to 0.710 rather than the 90-plus figures common in this corpus, which is what a properly participant-disjoint, chance-tested evaluation of gaze features in 30 people looks like. Three residual weaknesses. No confidence intervals accompany any figure, and with 30-31 participants one person is roughly 3 accuracy points, so the 10-point gain from weighting in adults corresponds to about three people. The controls were selected from a larger pool by SRS-2 ranking, so sampling is conditioned on a construct-related variable. And the statistical analysis that motivates the whole approach - and that is used to validate the feature importances - treats trials as independent units when they cluster within participants, without any mixed-effects adjustment, so those p-values are anticonservative even after Benjamini-Hochberg correction." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-104" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Frontiers in Digital Health 5:952433, Digital Mental Health section, Original Research, CC BY. Received 25 May 2022, accepted 30 January 2023, published 16 February 2023 - an eight-month review, longer than the Frontiers norm, with two named reviewers and a handling editor from Duke University disclosed as the journal requires. The venue is a digital-health rather than an engineering or clinical-psychiatry journal, and the reporting reflects that mixed audience: clinical instruments and diagnostic procedure are described to psychiatric standards while model architectures, optimiser settings and cross-validation structure are tabulated to engineering standards, and both the statistical analysis and the machine learning are given roughly equal space. Supplementary materials hold the nose-fixation and saccade analyses." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-104" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, sensitivity and specificity for four model variants across three classification problems - twelve complete metric triples, reported in a single table with the best in bold. Reporting all three metrics for every ablation condition rather than accuracy alone lets a reader see what the weighting actually does: for children it raises the baseline's sensitivity from 0.333 to 0.667 and the CNN's from 0.286 to 0.600, which is where the accuracy gain comes from, and the paper says so explicitly. The chance-level handling is the most straightforward in the corpus: group sizes were equalised so chance is approximately 50%, and each headline accuracy is tested against it with a binomial test, with all three p-values reported including the one that fails - schizophrenia at p = 0.052, described as marginally significant rather than rounded into significance. Feature importances from the random forest are averaged across cross-validation folds rather than fitted once. Missing: AUC or ROC, confidence intervals, and any precision or predictive-value figure. Given accuracies of 0.645 to 0.710 on samples of 30-31 participants, confidence intervals would be wide and their absence is the main reporting gap - a single participant moves accuracy by about 3 percentage points." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-104" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two parallel representations, compared directly. The baseline uses three interpretable scalars per trial: number of fixations, number of saccades and scan-path length. The CNN uses a spatial representation: gaze sample positions are set to 1 in an otherwise zero image, blurred with a Gaussian kernel of sigma 10, centre-cropped and downscaled to 34 x 34 greyscale, then paired with the stimulus image so the model sees both where the person looked and what was there. Two observations about this representation. It discards temporal order entirely - a heat map is a spatial density, so the sequence in which regions were visited, which the paper's own scan-path measure partly captures, is unavailable to the CNN. And the aggressive downscaling to 34 x 34 from a 1920 x 1080 display means each pixel spans roughly 56 x 32 display pixels, so the representation can distinguish eyes from mouth but not, say, one eye from the other or the pupil from the eyelid; this is a reasonable trade for a dataset of this size but bounds what the model can learn. One reporting inconsistency: fixation counts of 200-300 in a five-second trial exceed the number of possible fixation events at 120 Hz, so these are gaze samples classified as fixation rather than discrete fixation events, and the label is used loosely throughout." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-104" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Among the strongest in this review, and the only one applying condition-specific re-evaluation to two different diagnoses. Diagnoses were determined according to DSM-5 research criteria and then re-evaluated with the appropriate instrument: ADOS-2 for the autistic participants, with the adult group's mean total raw score reported (46.5 +/- 11.9), and the Positive and Negative Syndrome Scale for the schizophrenia group (67.1 +/- 13.0). Controls were not merely assumed neurotypical but were actively screened - 'physicians and psychologists interviewed them and excluded' anyone with learning delays or communication problems, and no control had a history of psychiatric disorder, drug abuse or epilepsy. So all three groups have an explicit ascertainment procedure, the two clinical groups have quantified symptom measures, and the control group has a stated exclusion protocol applied by clinicians. The residual gaps are minor by comparison: no inter-rater reliability is reported for the ADOS-2 or PANSS administrations, no interval between diagnosis and testing is given, and the ADOS-2 figures are total raw scores rather than calibrated severity scores, which limits comparability with other studies." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-104" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Rich on symptom severity and empty on cognition - an unusual combination in this corpus, where the reverse is more common. Severity and phenotype are measured with four or five instruments per dataset. Adults: SRS-2 total raw score (control 55.2 +/- 20.9, ASD 73.3 +/- 36.0 - note the very large SD in the autistic group, indicating wide variation in social-communication severity), ADOS-2 (46.5 +/- 11.9), PANSS for the schizophrenia group (67.1 +/- 13.0), and Kikuchi's Scale of Social Skills 18-item (control 64.7 +/- 11.0, ASD 45.3 +/- 9.46, Sz 59.1 +/- 9.14, so the autistic group scores lowest on self-reported social skill). Children: SRS-2 (control 19.2 +/- 11.5, ASD 74.7 +/- 26.9 - a very wide separation), ADHD-RS and CBCL. SRS-2 was deliberately not administered to the schizophrenia group because it has not been validated in that population, an omission explained rather than glossed. Against this, no IQ or developmental measure was obtained for anyone. The authors state the limitation squarely: participants were recruited without intellectual difficulties but 'we did not obtain any actual IQ values', so the study 'does not adequately take into account the influence of intellectual level', and the sample comprises 'only a small number of mild (high-functioning) cases'. Two consequences follow. Because the FEIT is a cognitive task with a correct answer, intellectual level plausibly affects both recognition accuracy and viewing strategy. And because SRS-2 was used to select which controls were retained, a severity-related variable enters the sampling as well as the description. Notably, no analysis relates any of these severity scores to model output, so the rich phenotyping is used descriptively rather than analytically." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-104" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No cross-sensor fusion, but two forms of within-study integration are central to the design. The first is the CNN's dual input: the gaze heat map is presented alongside the stimulus image, so the network can 'explicitly learn the facial regions and expressions' rather than treating gaze positions as coordinates divorced from what was at those coordinates. Since the same 21 or 32 images are shown to every participant, the image channel functions as a conditioning signal identifying which trial the heat map came from. The second, and the paper's actual contribution, is the aggregation layer: each of the 21 or 32 trials is classified independently and the per-trial decisions are then combined either by unweighted hard voting or by a random forest that learns which stimuli to trust. That is a form of stimulus-level fusion, and unlike most fusion claims in this corpus it is tested against its own ablation across three separate problems with informative results - weighting adds 10 points for autistic adults and 16.7 for autistic children but nothing for schizophrenia. The differential outcome is explained mechanistically rather than left as a number: weighting helps precisely when group differences are selective across stimuli, and is useless when they are uniform. That is a more transferable finding about when multi-stimulus aggregation should be weighted than any of the accuracy comparisons in this review." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-104" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Single-country, clinic-linked laboratory testing in Nara and Osaka, Japan, with participants recruited through the psychiatry department of Nara Medical University School of Medicine and Osaka Psychiatric Medical Center and tested on equipment at the Nara Institute of Science and Technology. The setting is a controlled screen-based assessment at a fixed 65 cm viewing distance rather than a naturalistic or point-of-care one, and no deployment scenario is proposed. Recruitment geometry is the standard clinical case-control arrangement, with the important qualification that both clinical groups come from psychiatric services and both are contrasted against the same actively screened control group, which is what makes the parallel comparison meaningful. One setting-related feature is unaddressed: the FEIT is a Western instrument and the participants are Japanese, so every trial involves other-race face processing, in a task whose entire outcome is where on a face the participant looks. Cross-cultural differences in face-scanning are well documented and the paper does not mention them." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-104" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Among the best-specified studies in the review. Reproducible from the text: the full instrument battery per group, the FEIT protocol with image counts per emotion and exact trial timing, the eye tracker and sampling rate, the viewing distance, the I-VT algorithm with its velocity threshold, the area-of-interest definitions and the per-image redrawing rule, both trial-exclusion rules with the resulting per-emotion trial counts listed exactly, the scan-path computation as pseudocode, both model architectures in a table giving neuron counts and kernel sizes and channel counts, the optimiser, loss function, epoch cap and early-stopping patience, the heat-map construction including the Gaussian sigma and the 34 x 34 centre-cropped downscaling, the three-step strategy adopted specifically because the dataset is small, and the nested leave-one-participant-out scheme with its 8:2 inner split. Statistical procedures are equally explicit, including Welch's t-test with Benjamini-Hochberg correction. Not available: code, and the FEIT images, which are under copyright and could not even be reproduced as figures. Raw data are offered on request without undue reservation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-104" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: autism spectrum disorder, schizophrenia, convolutional neural networks, eye movement, facial emotion recognition. The inclusion of schizophrenia alongside autism is the distinguishing feature and signals the paper's differential-psychiatric framing rather than an autism-screening one; neither 'diagnosis', 'screening' nor 'classification' appears, and the application terms in the body are 'biomarkers' and 'behavioral indicators'. Terms carried through the text: Facial Emotion Identification Test, scan-path length, heat map, hard voting, random forest weighting, nested leave-one-participant-out cross-validation, areas of interest, I-VT, feature importance, peripheral vision." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-104" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The three stated limitations are well chosen and one is a genuine contribution. Small sample size is acknowledged first. The IQ limitation is stated with unusual candour - participants were recruited without intellectual difficulties but no actual values were obtained, so the study 'does not adequately take into account the influence of intellectual level', and the sample is 'only a small number of mild (high-functioning) cases'. Most valuable is the error analysis, which is the kind of work almost no study in this corpus performs: the authors identified the eight participants misclassified by all four models, inspected their raw data, found that their gaze did not move from the centre at all, checked whether they had simply not engaged with the task, and found that their FEIT recognition scores were normal (18 and 19 of 21 for two autistic adults; 19, 25, 22 and 17 of 32 for four autistic children). The conclusion - that some people complete the task using peripheral vision without moving their eyes - identifies a failure mode intrinsic to gaze-based assessment, quantifies it at 6.5% of adults and 17% of children, and names it as a target for future work. Unstated limitations remain. The statistical analysis treats trials as independent when they cluster within participants at roughly two or three per person per emotion, so Welch's t-tests on 101-172 trials overstate the effective sample size. Control selection by SRS-2 ranking introduces a construct-related variable into the sampling, and the direction of the selection is ambiguous from the text. The adult groups differ by up to six years in mean age and the children's groups by 40 percentage points in sex ratio, neither adjusted. Autistic children scored more than twice as high on the ADHD Rating Scale as controls, an unaddressed comorbidity confound in a gaze task. And Japanese participants viewed Western faces without comment." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-104" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Interpretability is built into the aggregation design and is then cross-checked against independent evidence, which is the part that distinguishes it. The random forest that weights the 21 or 32 per-image decisions yields an importance per stimulus, averaged over cross-validation folds, and a table maps each face number to its emotion so the importances can be read semantically. The authors then test each importance ranking against the separate statistical analysis. For autistic adults the two highest-weighted images are neutral faces, and the statistics independently show all three gaze features differing significantly on neutral trials - a convergence the authors relate to documented difficulty with subtle expressions, since neutral is the least prototypical stimulus in the set. For autistic children the highest weights fall on surprise and two anger images, and anger is the single emotion showing a significant gaze difference in that dataset. For schizophrenia the top-weighted images are disgust and anger, and here the authors decline to interpret: 'we do not think that feature importance has any particular significance', because the statistics show eye-region fixation differing for every emotion, so no image is distinctive and the ranking is noise. Refusing to build a story around an importance ranking that the underlying statistics do not support is a discipline absent elsewhere in this corpus, and it makes the two interpretations that are offered more credible. The error analysis adds a second layer of interpretability at the individual level, identifying the specific participants the models could not classify and diagnosing why." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-104" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Published 2023 by the Augmented Human Communication Laboratory at Nara Institute of Science and Technology with the Department of Psychiatry at Nara Medical University School of Medicine and Osaka Psychiatric Medical Center, funded by a single JST CREST grant (JPMJCR19A5). The framing is diagnostic refinement within psychiatry rather than population screening: the opening premise is that experienced psychiatrists identify autism and schizophrenia through interviews, responses and neuropsychological tests, and that 'the discovery of disorder-specific biomarkers and behavioral indicators with sufficient sensitivity is important' to improve that process. Two aspects of the positioning are distinctive for this corpus. The paper's central concern is differentiation between two conditions rather than detection of one - 'since ASD and Sz are often difficult to differentiate, we propose to examine eye movement differences during facial expression recognition for the distinction' - which puts it among the very few studies here that treat the clinically realistic problem. And its contribution is framed as a modelling gap rather than a performance gap: prior basic research had established that gaze differences vary by presented emotion, but 'machine learning models that take this into account have not been examined', so the paper's claim is that a known behavioural fact should be built into the model architecture. The resulting accuracies are modest and are reported without inflation, with binomial tests against chance and a failing result left visible - a tone that stands out against the corpus's prevailing register." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-104" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Deliberately small models with a classical aggregation layer, and the modesty is justified rather than incidental. The per-trial classifiers are a three-hidden-layer multilayer perceptron with five neurons per layer, taking three scalar gaze features, and a two-convolutional-layer CNN with 3 x 3 kernels and 8 and 16 channels plus one fully connected layer, taking a 34 x 34 greyscale heat map and the stimulus image. The authors describe a three-step strategy adopted explicitly because the dataset is small: downscale the images to 34 x 34 greyscale after centre-cropping, reduce the CNN to two layers plus one fully connected layer, and use leave-one-participant-out cross-validation to maximise training data. Above the per-trial classifier sits the paper's contribution, an aggregation over the 21 or 32 per-trial decisions by either hard voting or a random forest, the latter learning which stimuli should count. Training used Adam with cross-entropy loss, a 300-epoch cap and early stopping after 15 epochs without validation improvement, with the learning rate optimised inside the nested cross-validation. The authors also justify not using a support vector machine, on the grounds that the per-fold sample size of 630 or 651 trials is adequate relative to a cited prior study - an argument about sample adequacy rather than an unexamined preference for deep learning." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-104" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A Tobii Pro Fusion eye tracker sampling at 120 Hz, with participants seated approximately 65 cm from a 1920 x 1080 display. Fixation and saccade classification used Tobii Pro Lab version 1.145 with its attention filter and the I-VT velocity-threshold algorithm, with the threshold stated explicitly at 100 degrees per second - a level of specificity about event detection that most eye-tracking papers in this corpus omit entirely, and one that matters because fixation counts are meaningless without it. Areas of interest were drawn on the eyes (extending up to the eyebrows), nose and mouth, and were redrawn for each image because mouth size varies with expression, which prevents a systematic bias in mouth-region measurement across emotions. Scan-path length was computed in Python with an algorithm published as pseudocode that segments rather than bridges invalid samples. Modelling used PyTorch with fully tabulated architectures. Clinical instruments: ADOS-2, PANSS, SRS-2, Kikuchi's Scale of Social Skills 18-item, ADHD-RS and CBCL. No calibration procedure is described." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-104" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three directions, each tied to a specific finding. Collect more data to establish validity, the standard response to a small sample. Develop a method that works for participants whose gaze barely moves - which follows directly from the error analysis and is a real methodological problem rather than a generic wish, since a gaze-based classifier that fails on 17% of children because they use peripheral vision has a systematic blind spot that more data alone will not close. And obtain actual IQ values and other individual characteristics so that the relationship between the model and the heterogeneity of the conditions can be examined - an acknowledgement that a high-functioning sample of 15 cannot speak for a spectrum. Not proposed, and worth noting: a direct autism-versus-schizophrenia classifier, which the dataset already supports and which would be the natural differential-diagnosis test; and validation on an independent cohort." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-104" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'People with autism spectrum disorder (ASD)' and 'people with schizophrenia (Sz)' predominate, person-first, with 'the ASDs' and 'the Szs' appearing as shorthand in the participants section and 'disorder groups' used throughout as the collective term for the clinical samples. The register is clinical-psychiatric and uses 'abnormalities' freely - 'ASD and Sz have abnormalities in cognitive functions, particularly social cognitive functions', 'abnormalities in eye movement during facial expression recognition have also been reported' - and 'atypical gaze patterns' and 'social cognitive deficits' elsewhere. Autism is described as 'a neurodevelopmental disorder, meaning that it does not appear in adulthood but is an innate trait', which is an accurate developmental statement rather than a deficit claim. One passage is notably person-centred in a way the corpus rarely manages: the introduction explains that some people 'have spent their childhood without being diagnosed with ASD' and that in adulthood 'difficulties in real social life may become emerged', sometimes with secondary depressive states leading to a first psychiatric presentation - a description of late diagnosis framed around the person's experience rather than around missed screening opportunities. There is no neurodiversity framing and no autistic-community involvement, but there is also no burden or cost rhetoric and no overstated claim." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-104" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two independent datasets and three diagnostic groups. Adults: 15 with autism (9 male, 6 female), 15 with schizophrenia (7 male, 8 female) and 16 controls (8 male, 8 female), the latter selected from 26 available controls in order to equalise group sizes, with the selection made by ranking on SRS-2 score in descending order. Children: 15 with autism (13 male, 2 female) and 15 controls (7 male, 8 female), again reduced from 17 by SRS-2 ranking. The presence of a schizophrenia group is what distinguishes this sample from almost every other in the corpus: it supplies a psychiatric rather than merely a neurotypical contrast, and although no autism-versus-schizophrenia classifier is built, the parallel analyses against a common control group produce the study's most useful finding. Note also that the modelled unit is the trial rather than the participant - each adult contributes 21 trials and each child 32 - but the cross-validation splits by participant, so the two levels are handled correctly in the modelling even though the statistical analysis treats trials as independent." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-104" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional, single session per participant, no follow-up and no test-retest reliability for any gaze feature. The two datasets are age-stratified rather than longitudinal - adults and children are separate samples, not the same people at two ages - although the introduction motivates the design partly by an interest in 'the developmental trajectory in processing emotional stimuli in neurodevelopmental conditions', and the parallel analysis does permit a cautious comparison across age groups, which the discussion makes: anger is highly weighted in both the adult and child autism models, suggesting a possible common specificity. Ethics reporting is complete for approval and consent: the data collection was approved by ethics committees at both Nara Medical University and Nara Institute of Science and Technology, the procedure was explained at the start of recording and informed consent obtained, and the formal ethics statement records written informed consent from participants' legal guardian or next of kin. Funding from a single CREST grant is stated, and competing interests are declared as none." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-104" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional multi-group classification study with two independent datasets (adults, children) and three diagnostic groups, combined with a per-emotion statistical analysis of g…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: ; comparison: 16 control adults and 15 control children" ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "9cc2ebcd203f7c818f2a5085f2c4d5c9f071b4534ceef6be5acbdc86174b5112" ;
