    slr:journal "Fr" ;
    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "DSM-5 research criteria with condition-specific re-evaluation - ADOS-2 for autism with total scores reported, PANSS for schizophrenia - and controls actively screened by physicians and psychologists who excluded anyone with psychiatric history, drug abuse, epilepsy, learning delay or communication problems. All three groups have an explicit ascertainment procedure and the two clinical groups have quantified symptom measures. Minor gaps that do not raise the rating: no inter-rater reliability, no diagnosis-to-testing interval, and ADOS-2 reported as total raw rather than calibrated severity score." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "The index test is prespecified in its essentials. Three gaze features fixed a priori from cited prior work, no feature selection, model architectures fixed in advance and justified by dataset size, and the single tuned hyperparameter - the learning rate - optimised inside the inner loop of a nested cross-validation. Classification is automated and blind to labels at inference. The random-forest weighting layer is trained within folds and its importances averaged across them rather than fitted once on all data." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "unclear" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "All participants received both the index test and the reference standard, and cross-validation partitions by participant so each person's trials stay together. Trial exclusion rules are stated (scan-path length of zero excluded; at least 40% of gaze samples retained) and the resulting per-emotion trial counts are listed exactly, so attrition is visible. Against this: the statistical analysis treats those trials as independent units in Welch's t-tests despite clustering within participants at two or three trials per person per emotion, no mixed-effects adjustment is made, and eight participants whose gaze did not move were retained and misclassified by every model - correctly identified in the error analysis but not excluded or handled." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Clinical case-control sampling with two psychiatric groups and screened controls, at engineered 50% prevalence. Control groups were reduced from 26 to 16 (adults) and 17 to 15 (children) by ranking on SRS-2 score, so sampling is conditioned on a variable related to the construct being classified and the direction of that selection is ambiguous from the text. The adult groups differ by up to six years in mean age with markedly different variances, the children's groups differ by 40 percentage points in sex ratio, autistic children have more than twice the ADHD Rating Scale score of controls, and no IQ was obtained for anyone - the authors state the sample comprises only mild, high-functioning cases." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "unclear" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<https://doi.org/10.3390/diagnostics12020518> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Autism spectrum disorder is a group of disorders marked by difﬁculties with social skills, repetitive activities, speech, and nonverbal communication. Deﬁcits in paying attention to, and processing, social stimuli are common for children with autism spectrum disorders. It is uncertain whether eye-tracking technologies can assist in establishing an early biomarker of autism based on the children’s atypical visual preference patterns. In this study, we used machine learning methods to test the applicability of eye-tracking data in children to aid in the early screening of autism. We looked into the effectiveness of various machine learning techniques to discover the best model for predicting autism using visualized eye-tracking scan path images. We adopted three traditional machine learning models and a deep neural network classiﬁer to run experimental trials. This study employed a publicly available dataset of 547 graphical eye-tracking scan paths from 328 typically developing and 219 autistic children. We used image augmentation to populate the dataset to prevent the model from overﬁtting. The deep neural network model outperformed typical machine learning approaches on the populated dataset, with 97% AUC, 93.28% sensitivity, 91.38% speciﬁcity, 94.46% NPV, and 90.06% PPV (ﬁvefold cross-validated). The ﬁndings strongly suggest that eye-tracking data help clinicians for a quick and reliable autism screening." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Kanhirakadavath MR; Chandran MSM (2022). Investigation of Eye-Tracking Scan Path as a Biomarker for Autism Screening Using Machine Learning Algorithms. *Diagnostics, 12(2), 518*. https://doi.org/10.3390/diagnostics12020518" ;
    dcterms:creator "Chandran MSM",
        "Kanhirakadavath MR" ;
    dcterms:title "Investigation of Eye-Tracking Scan Path as a Biomarker for Autism Screening Using Machine Learning Algorithms" ;
    bibo:doi "10.3390/diagnostics12020518" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_8b5fb23b" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2022 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the participant structure of the dataset acknowledged?" ;
                            slr:justification "The 59-participant structure is stated and, uniquely among the reuses of this dataset, used analytically." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched?" ;
                            slr:justification "No demographic information on which matching could be assessed." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is available severity information used?" ;
                            slr:justification "CARS-based severity is known to accompany the source dataset and is referenced in the literature review, but not used." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Participant and image counts only; no ages, sexes or clinical information." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Does the representation preserve the source encoding?" ;
                            slr:justification "Equal-weighted greyscale conversion discards the colour channel encoding velocity, acceleration and jerk." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioral data acquisition described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "No stimulus, tracker, duration or protocol in the methods; only a literature-review mention of the source equipment." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the image processing pipeline described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Greyscale conversion, resizing, flattening, PCA component counts, full network architecture and all hyperparameters specified." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the augmentation transforms physically plausible?" ;
                            slr:justification "Rotation by 45 and 60 degrees produces gaze trajectories no eye could generate on a rectangular screen." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "No diagnostic procedure reported for the analysed participants." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is uncertainty quantified?" ;
                            slr:justification "No confidence intervals anywhere, including on the 59-sample participant-level result." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the unit of analysis the participant in the main analyses?" ;
                            slr:justification "Image-wise 70:30 split with roughly 9.3 images per child." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the leakage mechanism identified?" ;
                            slr:justification "Attributed to 'similar replicates ... entered into the training set, as seen in the testing dataset'." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is augmentation confined to training data?" ;
                            slr:justification "Rotation applied to all 547 images before partitioning, so rotated copies span training and test." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is dimensionality reduction nested within validation?" ;
                            slr:justification "PCA fitted on the complete array before splitting in both the original and augmented conditions." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is a participant-level analysis performed?" ;
                            slr:justification "A one-image-per-participant subset of 59 evaluated by leave-one-out cross-validation, constructed explicitly to address contamination." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are conventional baselines reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Deep SVM at AUC 51.50% and decision jungle at 60.40% on the original images provide an informative near-chance control." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the contaminated figure kept out of the headline?" ;
                            slr:justification "The abstract, keywords and conclusion all lead with the 97.00% AUC that the discussion attributes to contamination." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible?" ;
                            slr:justification "Described as publicly available throughout." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the scope of the claim bounded?" ;
                            slr:justification "A dedicated section states the model is for screening rather than diagnosis." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the deployment proposal proportionate?" ;
                            slr:justification "A parent-facing mobile screening app for 6-to-24-month-olds is proposed on the basis of a participant-level sensitivity of 58.62% in an untested age band." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are metrics beyond accuracy reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "AUC, sensitivity, specificity, PPV and NPV for every model in every condition, argued as a departure from the field's convention." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Not mentioned, though platforms and hyperparameters are specified." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the data infrastructure problem identified?" ;
                            slr:justification "Open datasets combining multiple images from few participants are called 'unsuitable for reliable ASD screening'." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No statement anywhere." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Four directions. The first follows directly from the stated limitation and is the right one: obtain a dataset of single trials from a large group rather than multiple images from few participants. The second concerns collection quality - ensuring attention to the tracker and a distraction-free environment. The third proposes electrooculography as an alternative signal. The fourth is a deployment proposal that outruns the evidence considerably: 'a mobile app with an incorporated DNN-based ASD classifier and an extensive ET dataset of children aged 6 to 24 months may assist parents in independently screening their toddlers for ASD using a mobile device' - proposed for an age band entirely outside the analysed data, using a modality that requires an eye tracker, on the basis of a participant-level sensitivity of 58.62%." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-090" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No feature-level interpretability of any kind, and none possible given that the features are principal components of pixel arrays or convolutional activations over a rendered plot. No group means, effect sizes, directions or descriptive statistics about gaze appear anywhere. What the paper offers instead is interpretability at the level of experimental design: by running the same model across three data conditions it makes visible what each condition contributes, which is a more useful form of insight for this review than a saliency map would be. The discussion also contains speculation not supported by anything done here - that 'by leveraging the capabilities of complex CNN-based algorithms, ML models could quantify heterogeneities in ASD symptoms, allowing for accurate ASD severity grading using ET data' - which is notable because the source dataset contains CARS-based severity labels that would have permitted a direct test." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-090" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: ASD screening, autism spectrum disorder, machine learning, convolutional neural network (CNN), eye-tracking scan path images. The use of 'screening' rather than 'diagnosis' in the first keyword matches the paper's explicit positioning that the model 'is meant to be used for ASD screening' rather than as a diagnostic tool - a scope statement that several higher-profile studies in this corpus do not make." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-090" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Single modality with no fusion. The paper's comparative structure is instead across model families and, more importantly, across data conditions, and the second of these is the study's real contribution. Running the same convolutional network across three data conditions - original, rotation-augmented, and one image per participant - constitutes an ablation over validation design rather than over features, and it is the only such experiment in the corpus. The model-family comparison is also informative as an internal control: deep SVM on principal components of the same images reached AUC 51.50% on the original data, indistinguishable from chance, and decision jungle 60.40%, so the conventional pipeline extracts essentially nothing. That places whatever signal exists in the convolutional representation and simultaneously indicates that the pixel statistics captured by principal components carry no class information - a useful negative result that the authors note but do not pursue." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-090" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'autistic children', 'children with ASD', 'individuals with ASD', against 'typically developing (TD)' and 'TDs'. Person-first and identity-first are used interchangeably, and the comparison group is frequently reduced to the bare plural acronym 'TDs'. Autism is described as 'a chronic illness that lasts a lifetime' and elsewhere as a 'disease' and a 'condition' one may 'have', a framing that misdescribes a neurodevelopmental condition as a progressive illness. The introduction does include one observation that is clinically apt and rarely made in this corpus, noting that autism 'is diagnosed four times more frequently in boys than in girls; compared to boys, many girls with ASD show fewer visible signs' - though the study collects no sex data and cannot examine this." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-090" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two representations of the same images, both stripped of behavioural content. For the conventional models: greyscale conversion by equal channel weighting, resizing to 100x100, flattening to a 10,000-element vector, then principal component analysis retaining 500 components (1,750 after augmentation). For the network: the same greyscale 100x100 image passed through four convolutional stages producing 4,608 features. The greyscale step is the most consequential and passes without comment: the source dataset's distinguishing property is that gaze velocity, acceleration and jerk are encoded as colour gradients, and averaging the three channels equally discards precisely that information, leaving a monochrome trace of position only. Both this study and Gaspar and colleagues make the same conversion on the same images, so the two independent reuses of this dataset both destroy the derivative encoding it was constructed to carry." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-090" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional secondary analysis of a public dataset; no follow-up. No ethics statement, consent statement or IRB approval appears anywhere, and no pointer is given to the source dataset's approvals - an omission that is common for benchmark reuse in this corpus but is not acknowledged. Funding is not declared in the extracted text. The dataset is described throughout as publicly available but no URL or accession is given in the extracted portion." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-090" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Four algorithms across two families. The three conventional models were run in a graphical cloud environment with 'minimal settings, making them lightweight and suited for low-power computers', which is a deployment argument rather than a methodological one. The convolutional network was arrived at by iterative search - 'we continued modifying configurations of the above model, such as various combinations of convolutional, pooling, and dense layers until we found an optimal model' - and its hyperparameters likewise tuned 'until the model produced the highest score', so both architecture and hyperparameters were selected on performance with no held-out confirmation of that selection. The authors do report an honest interim observation, that 'we found that all models were overfitting, which could be due to two factors: model complexity or the limited size of the dataset', and their response - augmenting the data - is a reasonable inference that turns out to have created a worse problem, which they then diagnose correctly." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-090" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Not reported for the data collection. The literature review states, summarising the source publication, that recordings used 'an SMI RED series mobile eye tracker' while participants 'watched a series of videos to stimulate eye movement across the screen', but nothing about the room, country, recruitment or protocol is restated in the methods. The only settings named are the two analysing institutions and the compute environments - Microsoft Azure Machine Learning Studio Classic, Google Colaboratory, and a specified Dell laptop." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-090" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Nothing. No ages, no sexes, no group sizes beyond the participant and image counts, no IQ, no language level and no matching of any kind for either group. The literature review mentions in passing that a different Carette study involved children aged 8 to 10, but no demographic information is given for the participants whose images are analysed here. This is a lesser omission than in the two companion reuses of the same data only in that participant counts are stated and, uniquely, used; on every other demographic variable the study is silent." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-090" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two limitations are stated and the first is the most substantive limitation statement in this part of the corpus. It identifies the structural problem with the available data infrastructure: 'A few open-access ET datasets are available; however, they combine multiple ETSP images from a small set of participants rather than a single trial from a large group, making them unsuitable for reliable ASD screening.' That is a correct and generalisable diagnosis, and it is reinforced in the discussion where the authors attribute their own augmented result to 'possible data contamination as similar replicates are entered into the training set, as seen in the testing dataset'. The second limitation concerns collection quality, recommending that clinicians ensure the child attends to the tracker in a distraction-free environment. What is not acknowledged: that the abstract, keywords and conclusion all lead with the 97.00% figure the discussion attributes to contamination; that images from the same child span the training and test partitions even without augmentation; that principal component analysis was fitted on the full dataset before splitting; that rotation by 45 and 60 degrees produces physically impossible gaze traces; that the participant-level result rests on 59 samples with no confidence interval; and that greyscale conversion discards the colour-encoded derivative information the source images carry." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-090" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "This is simultaneously the study's weakest and its most valuable dimension, because it fails and then measures its own failure. Three separate contaminations affect the headline analyses. Rotation augmentation was applied to all 547 images before partitioning, so rotated copies of the same source image appear in training and test. Principal component analysis was fitted on the complete 547x10,001 array (and later the 2,566x10,000 array) before splitting, so the components for the conventional models are informed by test images. And images from the same child span the partition regardless of augmentation, at roughly 9.3 per participant. The authors identify the first of these explicitly - 'possible data contamination as similar replicates are entered into the training set, as seen in the testing dataset' - and respond by constructing a one-image-per-participant subset evaluated by leave-one-out cross-validation, which removes both the augmentation and the within-child duplication. The result is 72.88% accuracy with 58.62% sensitivity, against 97.00% AUC in the contaminated condition. That within-study drop of roughly 24 points, produced by the same architecture on the same source images, is the cleanest quantification of validation-induced inflation available in this review, and its close agreement with the approximately 71% Cilia and colleagues obtained by the same correction on the same data amounts to an independent replication of the corrected figure. The remaining weakness is that the participant-level analysis itself is thin - 59 samples, one arbitrarily selected image per child, leave-one-out, no confidence interval - so 72.88% is 43 correct classifications and carries a wide interval." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-090" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: identify an accurate model for autism screening from scan path images and determine whether performance survives restriction to one image per child. Main findings: conventional classifiers performed at or near chance on the original images (deep SVM AUC 51.50%, decision jungle 60.40%, boosted decision tree 67.60%); the convolutional network reached AUC 78.00% on the same images; rotation augmentation raised the network to AUC 97.00%, which the authors attribute to data contamination; and the participant-level subset of 59 images evaluated by leave-one-out cross-validation yielded 72.88% accuracy with sensitivity 58.62% and specificity 86.67%." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-090" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2022. The framing is the standard screening argument with the usual claim that current procedures 'are highly subjective, leading to misdiagnoses or missed diagnoses' and that an objective biomarker 'would substantially impact current diagnosis practices'. Two features of the positioning are better than that suggests. The scope is explicitly bounded - a dedicated Clinical Implications subsection states that 'rather than being a diagnostic tool, the research model we proposed in this work is meant to be used for ASD screening using ET data' - which is the correct claim for what the evidence supports and is stated before any results are discussed. And the literature review engages with prior figures specifically rather than generically, comparing its own augmented AUC of 97.00% against 'the performance (AUC: 90%) reported in the research work on the same dataset with augmentation' - that is, against the source authors' own result on the same images. What the paper does not do is carry its own contamination diagnosis into that comparison: having established that the augmented condition is contaminated, it compares its contaminated 97% favourably against another group's similarly obtained 90%, when the informative comparison is between its own participant-level 72.88% and the source group's participant-level result of approximately 71%." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-090" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Not described. The methods section begins from the images and never states what the children viewed. The literature review's one-line summary of the source study is the only information available, and it is not carried into the analysis or used to interpret any result. The limitations section does return to the acquisition question with a substantive recommendation - 'the clinician must ensure that the child is staring at the eye tracker throughout the trials and that the recording environment is free from any distractions that may impact the child's attention' - which correctly identifies attention and environmental control as determinants of data quality, but this is offered as guidance for future collection rather than as a characterisation of the data analysed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-090" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The metric panel is a deliberate improvement on the field's convention and is argued as such: 'In contrast to the previously reported studies, we evaluate the model performance using the area under the ROC curve (AUC), specificity, sensitivity, positive predictive value (PPV), and negative prediction value (NPV).' Reporting PPV and NPV alongside sensitivity and specificity is uncommon here and is what makes the participant-level result legible - sensitivity 58.62% against specificity 86.67% shows immediately that the model detects comparison children far better than autistic ones, which a single accuracy figure would conceal. All four models are reported in every data condition with all five metrics, so the deterioration of the conventional models and the improvement of the network under augmentation are both fully visible. Two gaps: no confidence intervals accompany any figure, which matters most for the 59-sample participant-level result; and Table 6 labels 72.88 as AUC while the discussion describes it as 'an overall accuracy of 72.88%', with the arithmetic (17 + 26 correct of 59) confirming it is accuracy, so the participant-level table header is mislabelled." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-090" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None used. CARS-based severity exists in the source data and is referenced in the literature review, but no cognitive, language, adaptive, severity or comorbidity measure enters the analysis, and no exclusions are described. Consequently nothing relates model output to any clinical dimension, and the paper's speculation that 'ML models could quantify heterogeneities in ASD symptoms, allowing for accurate ASD severity grading using ET data' is untested despite the severity data being available in the source." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-090" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Not reported for the analysis. The literature review does note that the source group 'developed a four-level severity-based classifier model for ASD, with classes such as TD, low, medium, and severe based on Childhood Autism Rating Scale (CARS) score', which establishes that CARS scores accompany the source dataset - so this paper is aware that a severity instrument exists and does not use it. No diagnostic instrument, criterion, clinician role or setting is stated as applying to the participants analysed, and the class labels are treated as image attributes. Under this review's classification this is dataset-label-only ascertainment, though better documented in its literature review than in its methods." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-090" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Diagnostics 12(2):518 (2022), an MDPI open-access clinical diagnostics journal. Authorship is from the Schools of Electronics and Electrical Engineering at Vellore Institute of Technology in India and the Department of Biomedical Engineering at Ajman University in the UAE, with no clinical or autism-research affiliation. The review timeline is compressed - received 27 January 2022, accepted 12 February, published 17 February, twenty-one days from submission to publication - and no ethics statement appears. The literature review is nonetheless the most substantive of the three studies reusing this dataset, summarising six prior works with their samples, equipment, stimuli and reported figures, and correctly identifying which prior study reported what." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-090" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups inherited from the Carette dataset: 29 autistic children contributing 219 images and 30 typically developing children contributing 328 images, 547 images from 59 children. The participant structure is not merely restated but actually used, which distinguishes this paper from the other two reuses of the same data in this corpus: a 'mini batch dataset that encapsulated only single trials from each of 59 participants' is constructed and evaluated separately. That analysis is the study's most valuable output. The main analyses nonetheless proceed at image level, so in the 70:30 split of 547 images and in the augmented analysis of 2,566 images, images from the same child appear in both training and test at roughly 9.3 images per participant. No other-diagnosis comparison group. The class imbalance of 219 to 328 is not corrected in the original analysis and is partly amplified by uniform augmentation, which preserves the ratio at 1,047 to 1,519." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-090" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Nominally atypical visual preference, with the introduction grounding this in the claim that autistic individuals 'pay less attention to faces and other social stimuli such as the human voice and hand gestures when compared to nonsocial stimuli' and that 'this unique pattern of eye movements appears in autistic children well before behavioral symptoms of ASD start showing'. Nothing of this construct survives into the analysis. The images are converted to greyscale - discarding the colour channel through which the source dataset encodes velocity, acceleration and jerk - resized to 100x100, and either flattened into pixel vectors for principal component analysis or passed through convolutional layers. No fixation, saccade, dwell time or area-of-interest quantity is computed at any stage, and no group difference in looking behaviour is reported anywhere. The construct discussed in the introduction and the quantity modelled in the methods are unconnected." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-090" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Reproducible as a pipeline, silent on the data. Fully reported: greyscale conversion with its channel weighting; resizing to 100x100 and flattening to 10,000 elements; the resulting array dimensions; the number of principal components retained in each condition; the complete convolutional architecture with all layer dimensions; the hyperparameters and the tuning procedure; the 70:30 split with exact image counts; the five-fold cross-validation; the augmentation rotations and the resulting class counts; the mini-batch construction and its leave-one-out protocol; the compute platforms and hardware; and full performance tables for every model in every condition. Not reported: participant demographics, diagnostic procedure, stimulus, acquisition protocol, the criterion by which one image per child was selected for the mini batch, random seeds, ethics approval, or any code." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-090" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No acquisition equipment is described beyond a literature-review mention of the source study's SMI RED tracker. The analytic environment is specified in unusual detail for a computational paper: Microsoft Azure Machine Learning Studio Classic for the conventional models, Google Colaboratory for the neural network, and a Dell XPS 15 9500 with an Intel i7-10750H at 2.60 GHz with six cores. Model hyperparameters are stated - binary cross-entropy loss, 50 epochs, batch size 16, learning rate 0.001 with Adam - along with the full convolutional architecture layer by layer with filter counts, kernel sizes, pooling dimensions and output feature dimensions." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-090" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "Not reported; the recordings belong to the source dataset" ;
                    slr:study_design "Secondary analysis of a public image dataset, structured as a staged experiment across three data conditions - original images, rotation-augmented images, and a one-image-per-parti…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Children from the Carette et al. gaze-path image dataset" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 29 children contributing 219 images; comparison: 30 children contributing 328 images" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "The same convolutional network applied to the same 547 public gaze-path images produced AUC 97.00% on a rotation-augmented set, AUC 78.00% on the unaugmented set, and 72.88% accuracy with 58.62% sensitivity when restricted to one image per child under leave-one-out cross-validation. The authors themselves attribute the augmented result to leakage - 'the discrepancy between the results using origin",
                        "Three data conditions produce three very different pictures. On the original 547 images, conventional models performed near chance - deep SVM AUC 51.50%, decision jungle 60.40%, boosted decision tree 67.60% - while the neural network reached AUC 78.00% with sensitivity 78.57% and specificity 75.47%. On the rotation-augmented 2,566 images, conventional models improved modestly (boosted decision tre" ;
                    slr:outcome "AUC, sensitivity, specificity, positive predictive value and negative predictive value for every model in every data condition. The choice is argued explicitly against the field's convention: 'In contrast to the previously reported studies, we evaluate the model performance using the area under the " ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Three data conditions produce three very different pictures. On the original 547 images, conventional models performed near chance - deep SVM AUC 51.50%, decision jungle 60.40%, boosted decision tree 67.60% - while the neural network reached AUC 78.00% with sensitivity 78.57% and specificity 75.47%. On the rotation-augmented 2,566 images, conventional models improved modestly (boosted decision tre; The same convolutional network applied to the same 547 public gaze-path images produced AUC 97.00% on a rotation-augmented set, AUC 78.00% on the unaugmented set, and 72.88% accuracy with 58.62% sensitivity when restricted to one image per child under leave-one-out cross-validation. The authors themselves attribute the augmented result to leakage - 'the discrepancy between the results using origin" ] ] ;
