    slr:journal "International Journal of Psychophysiology, 173, 69-8" ;
    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "some" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Clinical DSM-5 diagnosis by licensed child psychiatrists with M-CHAT-R/F distributions reported and stringent comparison-group family-history exclusions, but no observational diagnostic instrument and no description of the diagnostic procedure." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Groups differ by 20 Bayley cognitive points and 34 language points (both p < 0.001) with no adjustment and no acknowledgement, and by sex composition (p = 0.00); the number of participants entering the classification analyses cannot be reconciled with the 133 recruited and no exclusion or subsampling account is given." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "No flow accounting of any kind; the balanced classification set is acknowledged without stating its size or how it was formed, and no tracking-failure exclusions are reported." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "The reported 87.5% is the maximum across 24 classifier-by-feature-selection-by-stimulus-set configurations; feature selection including a Wrapper method precedes classification with nesting asserted but not described; no hyperparameters, confidence intervals or significance tests are reported." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "high" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<urn:slr:article:5e6c6c482e7c> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Objective: Autism spectrum disorder (ASD) affects nearly 1 in 44 children younger than 8 years old in the United States, and the situation may be even worse in remote areas of the world. However, it is difﬁcult to utilize existing approaches to screen patients with ASD in remote areas due to the lack of professionals and high-tech instruments. Therefore, we develop a fast and accurate scalable method for screening children with ASD. Methods: A deep weakly supervised artiﬁcial intelligence model is proposed for ASD screening based on the dynamic viewing patterns (DVP) over viewing time and visual stimuli. In training, we utilized a long short-term memory (LSTM) network to learn the mapping between the autoencoder-based encoded dynamic patterns and the labels. In testing, we fed the encoded DVP of each undiagnosed child into the trained network and predicted the diagnosis category based on the score on all stimuli. Results: Based on the multi-center evaluation on 165 subjects (95 typically developing children and 70 children with ASD) aged 3-6 years from different areas of China, our method achieves an average recognition accuracy of 96.73% (sensitivity 96.85% and speciﬁcity 96.63%). Conclusion: The DVP is a discriminating attribute to identify the atypical performance of ASD. The DVP-based model is an effective platform for enhancing auxiliary ASD screening accuracy. Signiﬁcance: We validated the importance of dynamic information on between-group differences and classiﬁcation. Additionally, the evaluation results suggest Manuscript received 17 June 2022; revised 15 October 2022; accepted 14 November 2022. Date of publication 21 November 2022; date of current version 21 April 2023. This work was supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grants 62172334, 62027813, and 62036005. (Corresponding author: Junwei Han.) Chen Xia is with the School of Automation, Northwestern Polytechnical University, China. Dingwen Zhang is with the School of Automation, Northwestern Polytechnical University, China, and also with the Institute of Artiﬁcial Intelligence, Hefei Comprehensive National Science Center, China. Kuan Li is with the Children Healthcare Department, The Afﬁliated Hospital of Northwest University, China. Hongxia Li is with the Children Rehabilitation Center, Shaanxi Rehabilitation Hospital, China. Jianxin Chen is with the School of Traditional Chinese Medicine, Beijing University of Chinese Medicine, China. Weidong Min is with the School of Mathematics and Computer Science, Nanchang University, China. Junwei Han is with the School of Automation, Northwestern Polytechnical University, Xi’an 710072, China, and also with the Institute of Artiﬁcial Intelligence, Hefei Comprehensive National Science Center, Hefei 230088, China (e-mail: junweihan2010@gmail.com). This article has supplementary downloadable material available at https://doi.org/10.1109/TBME.2022.3223736, provided by the authors. Digital" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Xia C; Zhang D; Li K; Li H; Chen J; Min W; Han J (2023). Dynamic Viewing Pattern Analysis: Towards Large-Scale Screening of Children With ASD in Remote Areas. *IEEE Transactions on Biomedical Engineerin*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Chen J",
        "Han J",
        "Li H",
        "Li K",
        "Min W",
        "Xia C",
        "Zhang D" ;
    dcterms:title "Dynamic Viewing Pattern Analysis: Towards Large-Scale Screening of Children With ASD in Remote Areas" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_8b9e0e8a" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2023 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Handling of missing data and attrition" ;
                            slr:justification "Device-level noise handling is specified numerically (merge points below 0.02, delete merged points under 0.1 s) and the minLength filter is analysed rather than assumed, with the authors demonstrating that a stricter setting would preferentially discard autistic children's data - a genuinely careful piece of work. But no attrition, exclusion or data-quality accounting is reported for the 333 children, and no calibration procedure or tracking-ratio statistic is given." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participant selection and spectrum representativeness" ;
                            slr:justification "One hundred and forty autistic children recruited through hospitals and rehabilitation services with no stated inclusion or exclusion criteria beyond normal or corrected vision, no diagnostic characterisation and no severity or cognitive measure. Representativeness cannot be assessed. The sample is nonetheless large and drawn from multiple sites in two provinces, which is more than most studies here offer." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Confounder control" ;
                            slr:justification "Age is well matched (4.66 +/- 1.00 versus 4.77 +/- 0.92). Sex is not: 80.0% male in the autistic group against 63.2% in the comparison group, unmatched, unadjusted and unmentioned, in a paper that separately reports higher error rates in boys. No cognitive or developmental measure exists to separate an autism-specific viewing pattern from a general attentional or developmental one, and the authors' own account of grey-mask fixations in 3-4 year olds shows the two are confusable." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Comparison group adequacy" ;
                            slr:justification "School-recruited typically developing children with no reported screening for autism or developmental concerns. No clinical comparison group, so the study cannot address whether the viewing signature is autism-specific or a general marker of atypical development or of reduced task engagement." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome measurement blinding" ;
                            slr:justification "The entire pipeline from fixation detection through scoring is automated and label-free at inference; no human rating enters the measurement chain." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Measurement standardisation" ;
                            slr:justification "Identical eye tracker, sampling rate, screen, image geometry, viewing distance, display and mask timing and stimulus set for every child, in a consistent therapy-room arrangement with at most two accompanying adults. Acquisition does not differ by group. The absence of a described calibration procedure is the one gap." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Index test definition and prespecification" ;
                            slr:justification "The Otsu threshold is fitted across training and test subjects together; network size, epochs, batch size, learning rate and minLength were compared on the evaluation data with no development set; and the 60-stimulus shortened protocol is selected in-sample from the same data on which accuracy is reported." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reference standard quality" ;
                            slr:justification "Not described. No instrument, criteria, clinician or process for either group in the main text, and no screening of the comparison group. Table IV lists comparator studies' instruments while leaving the study's own blank." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance estimation and chance level" ;
                            slr:justification "Accuracy at two aggregation levels, sensitivity, specificity, AUC, ablations, subgroup breakdowns by sex and age, and a five-permutation stability range - a fuller reporting set than almost anything else in this corpus. Missing: confidence intervals, a majority-class baseline (57.6%), and any prevalence-adjusted metric such as PPV, which is the number that matters for the proposed community-screening use." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Preprocessing and feature selection nesting" ;
                            slr:justification "No feature selection occurs and the full vector enters the model. But the Otsu threshold is fitted transductively across training and test scores, and the stimulus-discriminability ranking that motivates a shortened protocol is computed on the evaluation data." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data splitting and leakage control" ;
                            slr:justification "Subject-level 50% split repeated over five random permutations with the minimum reported, so all 82 patterns from a child stay together - the correct design for a model that scores individual stimuli. The semantic autoencoder is trained on MS COCO rather than participant data, closing a second potential leakage path." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Sample size adequacy for the modelling" ;
                            slr:justification "Three hundred and thirty-three children, 165 in the test set, with 82 stimulus patterns each - by a wide margin the largest sample charted here, and the paper argues explicitly and with supporting visualisation that samples under 100 subjects cannot characterise model performance." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "unclear" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data, code and protocol availability" ;
                            slr:justification "No code, no data, no stimulus set, no preregistration. Supplementary figures and tables are available through the publisher." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Acknowledgement of limitations" ;
                            slr:justification "Failure modes are analysed concretely (natural scenes, small objects, edge-placed objects) and the age restriction is stated. The subgroup findings on rehabilitation training and on younger children's inattention are reported honestly and are the paper's most important caveats, though their implications for what the marker measures are not drawn out. Nothing is said about the undescribed reference standard, the sex imbalance, the transductive threshold, or the in-sample parameter and stimulus selection." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Ethics, consent and conflicts" ;
                            slr:justification "Approval by the Medical Ethics Committee of Northwestern Polytechnical University under protocol 202002032 with Declaration of Helsinki conformity - a named committee with a protocol number, better than most reports here. But no parental consent or child assent statement appears for a study of 333 children aged 3-6, and no competing-interests statement is given. Funding is itemised." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Completeness of methods reporting" ;
                            slr:justification "The computational pipeline is specified to reimplementation standard - stimulus protocol, device and its noise rule, superpixel step, autoencoder corpus and loss, full LSTM equations, feature concatenation, weak-supervision labelling, aggregation, thresholding, and a parameter sensitivity table including a reported negative result. The clinical protocol that produced the labels is entirely absent, as are the final architecture dimensions and any calibration procedure." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reporting of results and uncertainty" ;
                            slr:justification "Two aggregation levels, sensitivity and specificity, AUC, ablations, five-permutation robustness with the minimum reported, and honest subgroup breakdowns that surface unflattering findings. But no confidence intervals, no chance baseline, and no prevalence-adjusted metric for a tool proposed for universal screening." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups, with a corpus considerably larger than most in this review. Total recruited: 333 children aged 3-6, comprising 140 with autism (112 boys, 28 girls; mean age 4.66 +/- 1.00 years) and 193 typically developing (122 boys, 71 girls; mean age 4.77 +/- 0.92 years), all with normal or corrected-to-normal vision. Recruitment was multi-centre, from several hospitals and schools across Shaanxi and Fujian provinces. The evaluation set is 165 subjects - 70 autistic and 95 typically developing - formed by a 50% subject-level random split, with robustness checked over five permutations. The paper explicitly frames sample size as a methodological argument rather than a logistical fact: Fig. 14(a) visualises how group separability degrades as more subjects are added, and the discussion states that 'when the number of subjects is less than 100, the test cannot accurately reflect the performance of the model' - a direct criticism of the small-sample studies that dominate this corpus, and one this study is entitled to make. A second unit of analysis exists and is handled correctly: each child contributes 82 stimulus-based viewing patterns, and these are scored individually before being aggregated to a subject-level decision, but the split is by child so the 82 patterns never straddle training and test." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-101" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The goal is explicitly infrastructural rather than merely methodological: to build a screening method that works where the constraints are affordability, child-friendliness and the absence of professionals, which the authors identify as the three barriers to autism screening in remote areas. The technical contribution is the dynamic viewing pattern - gaze treated as an ordered trajectory over both viewing time and the stimulus set, rather than as a static distribution of fixations - together with a pipeline that encodes each fixation semantically via an autoencoder, scores each stimulus sequence with an LSTM, and aggregates 82 stimulus scores into a subject decision. Findings: subject-level accuracy 96.73% with sensitivity 96.85% and specificity 96.63% on 165 test children, stable above 95.76% across five random subject-level splits. The supporting analyses are more informative than the headline. Conventional hand-crafted features reproduce the expected group differences - autistic children make fewer fixations with longer saccades, look less at faces and other salient objects, take longer to notice them, and dwell more on background and on the grey mask - but the authors state plainly that these differences are 'not sufficiently significant to directly use these hand-crafted features for classification'. A time-delay embedding analysis then shows that between-group distance grows with the length of the pattern section used and with the number of stimuli included, and that for sections shorter than five fixations the within-autism variability exceeds the between-group difference - a quantitative argument that separability is manufactured by using more temporal and more stimulus context, not merely asserted. Ablation confirms both feature families contribute (semantic 94.85%, low-level 93.33%, combined 96.73%) and that sequence modelling beats fixation-histogram and stacked-fixation baselines." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-101" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A three-stage deep pipeline with an unsupervised front end, a weakly supervised middle and an unsupervised decision rule. Stage one is a deep autoencoder used purely as a nonlinear dimensionality reducer, trained layer-wise then fine-tuned globally with cross-entropy reconstruction loss on 10,000 MS COCO images - so the semantic encoder never sees participant data, which is a clean separation and removes one potential leakage path. Stage two is an LSTM over the per-fixation feature sequence for each stimulus, chosen for its capacity to model sequential dependence and to avoid the vanishing-gradient problems of plain recurrent networks, ending in a fully connected layer and a single-neuron regression head that scores the pattern. Stage three aggregates the 82 stimulus scores into a subject average and binarises it with Otsu's maximum between-class variance threshold. The weak supervision is explicit and correctly reasoned: the authors note that an autistic child's viewing will be atypical on only some stimuli but that labels for which specific stimuli are unavailable, so each child's diagnostic label is propagated to all 82 of their patterns and the aggregation step is relied upon to recover the subject-level decision from noisy per-stimulus targets. This is the appropriate formulation for the problem and is stated as such rather than adopted implicitly. Two baselines are reimplemented for comparison - a fixation-count histogram with SVM and stacked deep features of up to 10 fixations with SVM - and both underperform, which is the study's evidence that sequence structure, not merely deep features, carries the signal." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-101" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Strong on method, absent on artefacts, with an unusual amount of parameter transparency. Reproducible from the text: the complete stimulus protocol including image count, categories, display and mask durations, session structure and viewing distance; the eye tracker, its sampling rate and its exact noise-suppression rule; the superpixel step; the autoencoder's external training corpus, loss function and two-stage training strategy; the full LSTM equations; the feature concatenation; the weak-supervision label assignment; the aggregation and Otsu thresholding; and a sensitivity table spanning hidden units, fully connected size, epochs, mini-batch size, learning rate and the minLength lower bound. The finding that the resized superpixel bounding box outperforms a centred patch is reported as a negative result, which is rare and useful. Not available: code, data, the 82-image stimulus set, the specific final architecture dimensions, and - most importantly - the diagnostic procedure that generated the labels. Ten supplementary figures and three supplementary tables exist but were not retrieved. As an IEEE TBME paper the reporting standard is engineering-first, which shows in the asymmetry between an exhaustively specified pipeline and an unspecified clinical protocol." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-101" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The stated limitations are concrete and unusually specific about failure modes: the model is trained only on 3-6 year olds and applies only there; natural scenes with small between-group differences and few training examples are hard; small objects defeat the semantic encoder; and images with objects near the edge cause false positives because typically developing children's exploratory fixations also land on the grey mask. Two further constraints appear as subgroup findings rather than caveats and are more interesting than either: sensitivity is lowest in 5-6 year olds because some had undergone rehabilitation training teaching them to attend to social information, prompting the statement that 'the model is more suitable for the initial screening of children who have not undergone rehabilitation training'; and specificity is lowest in 3-4 year olds because they cannot sustain attention to the screen. Taken together these say that the measured signal is erased by intervention at one end of the age range and mimicked by ordinary inattention at the other, which bears directly on what the proposed 'AI-marker' represents - a point the paper reports but does not draw out. The unstated limitations are led by the absence of any described diagnostic reference standard, without which the target of a 96.73% accuracy is undefined. Others: sex is imbalanced between groups by 17 percentage points and never mentioned, despite the paper's own finding that error rates differ by sex; the Otsu decision threshold is fitted on training and test scores together, so the operating point is chosen with sight of the test distribution; architecture, learning parameters and the minLength value were compared on the evaluation data with no separate development set; the stimulus-discriminability ranking used to justify a shortened protocol is derived from the same data on which accuracy is reported; there is no majority-class baseline (57.6%) or confidence interval; and no prevalence-adjusted metric is offered despite the paper proposing population-scale screening." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-101" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional, single session, no follow-up, and no test-retest reliability - which is a real gap for a tool proposed for repeated community screening. The nearest thing to a temporal analysis is Fig. 8, which tracks how the sample and the classification results evolved across the four quarters of 2021, showing 2-4 misclassified cases in an initial 40-child phase and stable results thereafter; this is a development log rather than a validation, but it is unusually transparent about how the model matured alongside the dataset. Ethics reporting is complete for approval and thin for consent: the study was approved by the Medical Ethics Committee of Northwestern Polytechnical University under protocol 202002032, with all procedures conducted in accordance with the latest revision of the Declaration of Helsinki - a named committee and a protocol number, better than most reports here - but no parental consent or child assent statement appears, despite the participants being 3-6 year olds. Funding from three National Natural Science Foundation of China grants is itemised. No competing-interests statement appears." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-101" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None. No cognitive, developmental, language, adaptive or severity measure was administered to any of the 333 children, and no autism severity score of any kind is reported. Three consequences follow that the paper does not address. First, the autistic sample cannot be positioned on the spectrum, so it is unknown whether the model's performance would hold in a less clearly affected group - a particular concern given that the discussion itself observes that adding subjects makes 'more unclear and atypical cases' appear. Second, the paradigm demands six minutes of sustained attention to a screen from 3-6 year olds, and the ability to comply is developmentally graded; without a cognitive measure it is impossible to separate an autism-specific viewing signature from a general attentional or developmental one, and the authors' own explanation of why specificity is lowest in 3-4 year olds - younger children 'find it difficult to focus on the screen the entire time, resulting in meaningless fixations that fall on the gray mask' - shows that this is not hypothetical. Third, the conclusion proposes that dynamic viewing patterns 'can also be employed to quantitatively assess subjects' ASD risk', but with no severity instrument in the study there is nothing against which such a continuous risk score could be calibrated or validated." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-101" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Visual attention during passive free viewing, reconceived as a temporally ordered process rather than a spatial distribution. The paper's construct - the dynamic viewing pattern - encompasses where each fixation lands, in what order, for how long, and with what saccade amplitude and direction connecting it to the next, across all 82 stimuli. The descriptive analysis identifies the component behaviours: autistic children make fewer fixations with larger saccade amplitudes; they fixate faces less than typically developing children, with the largest between-group difference of any region appearing in facial areas; they fixate other salient objects less across food, animals, toys, gazed objects and touched objects; they are slower to notice most object types; and they spend more time on background regions and on the inter-stimulus grey mask. The mechanistic account offered is information-theoretic rather than social-motivational: typically developing children are described as fixating regions of continuous salient information under an information-maximisation principle, while autistic children are 'passive in the information exploration process'. The temporal analysis locates the difference at the extremes of each trial - autistic children's first fixation is often random or outside the image while typically developing children's lands near salient objects, the middle of the trial shows convergence, and the final stage diverges again as autistic children drift to non-salient background. One measured behaviour deserves separate flagging because the authors identify it as a top-three driver of model scores: whether the fixation pattern stays on the image or moves to the grey mask. This is as much a measure of task engagement as of visual preference, and the paper's own age analysis shows the same behaviour arising in typically developing 3-4 year olds from ordinary inattention." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-101" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'Children with ASD' and 'typically developing (TD) children' are the working terms, used consistently and person-first. But the surrounding framing is the most burden-oriented in this corpus. The introduction states that 'most patients diagnosed with ASD cannot independently live, study and work when they grow up, which places a heavy burden on their families and society', and autistic children are referred to throughout as 'patients' - including in the abstract, where the aim is 'to screen patients with ASD'. Early intervention is motivated by 'reducing the harm caused by the disorder'. Elsewhere the vocabulary is that of deviation from a norm: 'atypical performance', 'abnormalities in ASD', 'abnormal distribution values', and children whose viewing is 'passive in the information exploration process'. Set against this, the study's practical orientation is genuinely equity-driven and its design is respectful of children in a way several others here are not - six minutes of passive viewing, no instructions, no restraint, mother present, stimuli chosen partly to hold attention. There is no neurodiversity framing and no autistic-community involvement. One passage complicates the picture in an interesting way: the finding that 5-6 year olds who had undergone rehabilitation training scored lower and were more often missed is reported neutrally as a limitation of the model, without remarking that it implies the measured signal is modifiable by intervention - which is a substantive point about what the 'AI-marker' is actually marking." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-101" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Index terms: Autism spectrum disorder, deep autoencoder, deep learning, dynamic viewing patterns, long short-term memory (LSTM) network. Four of the five are architectural or methodological, and the one novel construct - dynamic viewing patterns - is the paper's own coinage, which the abstract, title and body use consistently as a named attribute (abbreviated DVP throughout). Terms carried through the body: weakly supervised learning, between-group and intra-class difference, time-delay embedding, Hausdorff distance, superpixel, semantic hashing code, maximum between-class variance, AI-marker, scalable screening, medical cloud service centers. Neither 'diagnosis' nor 'classification' appears among the index terms despite both being performed, and 'screening' appears only in the title." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-101" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Per fixation: a c-bit semantic code obtained by locating the fixation's SLIC superpixel, resizing that superpixel's bounding box to d x d and passing it through the pretrained autoencoder, concatenated with four low-level descriptors - normalised position, duration, saccade amplitude and saccade orientation. A viewing pattern of m+1 fixations on one stimulus becomes a length-m sequence of these vectors, and each child yields 82 such sequences. The defining property is that no aggregation precedes the model: sequences enter the LSTM in their original order and length, in deliberate contrast to the histogram baseline (which discards order entirely) and the stacked-feature baseline (which fixes the fixation count at 10 by zero-padding or truncation). The authors' analysis of the minLength parameter is a good illustration of how carefully this was handled - raising the minimum sequence length from 2 to 4 fixations improves nothing and causes 'significant data loss in the ASD group', because autistic children produce fewer fixations, so filtering by pattern length would systematically discard the very participants the model must detect. Recognising that a preprocessing threshold interacts with the group difference it is meant to measure is a subtlety absent from most of this corpus. One reported negative result adds to the representation's credibility: using a fixed patch at each superpixel's centre performed worse than the resized full bounding box." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-101" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The paper does substantial interpretive work, but almost none of it is attribution on the trained model. Three strands. First, an extensive pre-modelling descriptive analysis with low-level and semantic gaze features, region-of-interest labelling, first-fixation timing and object-category breakdowns, which establishes the expected group differences and then concludes candidly that they are 'not sufficiently significant to directly use these hand-crafted features for classification' - an honest admission that the interpretable features do not do the work. Second, and most distinctive, a quantitative analysis of separability itself using time-delay embedding to compute Hausdorff and mean-minimal distances, showing how between-group distance and within-group variability change as a function of how much temporal context and how many stimuli are used. The finding that with pattern sections shorter than five fixations the within-autism variability exceeds the between-group difference is a genuinely useful result about the structure of the problem, and it turns the paper's architectural choices into consequences of measurement rather than assertions. Third, qualitative inspection of model scores, from which three drivers are named: whether the pattern stays on the image or drifts to the grey mask, whether fixations on salient objects are missing, and whether fixation order and duration are reasonable - each illustrated with worked successful and failed cases against the two baselines. The gap is that no attribution method is applied to the LSTM: there is no saliency analysis, no gate inspection, no per-fixation contribution, and no way to say for an individual child which of their 82 patterns or which fixations produced their score. For a system proposed as an aid to non-specialists in remote clinics, per-child explanation is exactly what would be needed, and the paper's own emphasis on an 'AI-marker' makes its absence more noticeable." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-101" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No cross-sensor fusion - a single eye tracker - but two feature families are combined within the modality and their contributions are separately quantified, which is more than most multimodal papers here achieve. The semantic stream encodes what the child is looking at, by locating each fixation in a SLIC superpixel and passing that region through an autoencoder pretrained on MS COCO to yield a compressed code; the low-level stream encodes the mechanics of looking - position, duration, saccade amplitude and saccade orientation. The ablation is clean and reported in the discussion: semantic features alone give 94.85% accuracy with 97.14% sensitivity and 93.16% specificity, low-level features alone 93.33% with 90.71% and 95.26%, and the combination 96.73% with 96.85% and 96.63%. The two are therefore complementary in a specific and interpretable way - semantic content drives sensitivity while movement mechanics drive specificity - and combining them raises both rather than trading one against the other. That is a more informative fusion result than the usual report of a single accuracy gain. A second, structural form of integration operates at the stimulus level: 82 per-stimulus scores are aggregated into one subject decision, and the paper shows both that individual stimulus scores are unreliable (Fig. 9(a) shows typically developing children scoring high on particular images) and that between-group separation grows with the number of stimuli pooled, so multi-scene aggregation is doing work analogous to multimodal fusion. On genuine cross-modality, the authors list fMRI, EEG, facial expression and observation-scale scores as future work and describe the problem of relating data across modalities as important and understudied." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-101" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Not described. This is the study's most serious reporting gap and it sits oddly alongside the sophistication of everything else. The Data Acquisition section states only that 140 children with autism and 193 typically developing children were recruited and that all had normal or corrected vision; no diagnostic instrument, no DSM or ICD criteria, no clinician, no assessment procedure and no diagnostic date appears anywhere in the main text for either group. Two clinical co-authors are affiliated with the Children Healthcare Department of the Affiliated Hospital of Northwest University and the Children Rehabilitation Center of Shaanxi Rehabilitation Hospital, so clinical diagnosis is clearly implied, and the multi-centre recruitment from hospitals suggests service-identified children - but none of this is stated. The irony is sharpened by Table IV, in which the authors tabulate the diagnostic instruments used by comparator studies in the literature - Autism Behaviour Checklist, M-CHAT, ADOS, ADI-R - as a characteristic worth reporting, while leaving their own blank. Typically developing status rests on school recruitment with no screening instrument reported. Supplementary material was not retrieved and may contain more; on the main text alone, the quantity the model predicts with 96.73% accuracy is not defined." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-101" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "IEEE Transactions on Biomedical Engineering 70(5):1622-1632, May 2023 - a high-profile biomedical engineering journal, submitted 17 June 2022, revised 15 October 2022, accepted 14 November 2022, published online 21 November 2022. The venue accounts for the paper's characteristic asymmetry: the signal-processing and machine-learning pipeline is specified to a standard that would permit reimplementation, complete with LSTM gate equations, a parameter sensitivity table, reimplemented baselines, ablations and a reported negative result, while the clinical apparatus that generated the labels is not described at all. Supplementary material comprising ten figures and three tables is hosted by the publisher. The paper adopts the journal's structured abstract format (Objective, Methods, Results, Conclusion, Significance), which is unusual in this corpus and enforces an explicit statement of clinical significance." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-101" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Multi-centre, and deliberately so: children were recruited from multiple hospitals and schools in Shaanxi and Fujian provinces, chosen because 'medical conditions are relatively poor' there. This is one of only a few studies in this corpus with genuine site diversity, and the framing is consistent throughout - the paper's entire motivation is that screening in remote and low-income areas is understudied, and the design constraints follow from that premise rather than being retrofitted to it. Recording took place in a therapy room with the child 0.5 m from the monitor and at most two other people present, the mother and one researcher, which is a realistic community-clinic arrangement rather than a laboratory. The counterweight is the usual one: recruitment pairs hospital- or rehabilitation-identified autistic children against school-recruited comparison children at 42% prevalence, so the geometry remains case-control and the accuracy does not transfer to a community screening population. The authors do not compute what their operating point would mean at population prevalence, which for a proposed universal screening tool is the omission that matters most." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-101" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A SciEye 2.0 eye tracker sampling at 140 Hz, using infrared illumination with corneal and retinal reflection, feeding a 1920 x 1080 monitor on which 1040 x 780 images were centred; the child sat 0.5 m away. The device's own preprocessing is specified numerically and is a genuine reporting strength: gaze points separated by less than 0.02 in normalised coordinates were merged, and merged points shorter than 0.1 s deleted, to suppress noise. No calibration procedure is described, which is the notable gap in an otherwise well-specified acquisition chain. On the analysis side: SLIC superpixel segmentation, a deep autoencoder trained layer-wise then fine-tuned globally with a cross-entropy reconstruction loss on 10,000 MS COCO images, and an LSTM with fully connected and regression heads trained by backpropagation through time, with the full gate equations reproduced. Otsu's maximum between-class variance method supplies the decision thresholds. Two baselines were reimplemented for comparison rather than merely cited - a fixation-count histogram with SVM, and stacked deep features of up to 10 fixations with SVM - which is more effort than most papers here expend on their comparators. Software frameworks and hardware for training are not named." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-101" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Reported: group sizes, sex counts and mean and standard deviation of age for both groups, plus normal or corrected vision as the only participant criterion. Age is well matched (4.66 +/- 1.00 versus 4.77 +/- 0.92). Sex is not: the autistic group is 80.0% male (112 of 140) and the comparison group 63.2% male (122 of 193), a difference of nearly 17 percentage points that is neither tested, matched, adjusted nor mentioned. This omission is more consequential here than in most studies because the paper's own subgroup analysis reports that 'the groups of boys presented higher error rates than the groups of girls' - so sex is demonstrably related to model performance and is also unequally distributed between the classes. Not reported at all: IQ or developmental level, language ability, socioeconomic status, comorbidity, medication, and any autism severity measure. Recruitment sites are described qualitatively as areas 'where medical conditions are relatively poor', which is the closest the paper comes to a socioeconomic characterisation and is offered as a strength of external validity rather than as a demographic variable. The absence of any cognitive measure is a real limitation for a passive-viewing task in 3-6 year olds, since the capacity to sit still and attend to a screen for six minutes varies sharply with developmental level - and the paper's own analysis of why specificity is lowest in the youngest children turns on exactly that capacity." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-101" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy at two levels of aggregation (A1 for stimulus-based patterns, A2 for subjects), sensitivity, specificity, ROC analysis and AUC, with results additionally broken down by sex and by age band and by ablation condition. Reporting sensitivity and specificity alongside accuracy is the minimum this literature should meet and is met here; adding subgroup breakdowns, and reporting them honestly when they are unflattering, goes beyond it. Robustness is addressed by repeating the subject-level split five times and reporting the minimum (95.76%) rather than only the mean. What is missing: confidence intervals; a majority-class baseline, which at 42.4% autism in the test set would be 57.6%; and any prevalence-adjusted metric such as positive predictive value, which is the number that matters for the community-screening application the paper proposes. At a population prevalence near 1-2%, sensitivity 96.85% with specificity 96.63% would yield a positive predictive value in the range of roughly 22-37% - far better than most operating points in this corpus, and a calculation the paper would have benefited from making explicit given how strongly it frames itself as a universal screening tool. The 82 stimulus-level scores are also not independent within a child, which affects the interpretation of A1 though not of A2." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-101" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Four directions, all substantive. Collect data from other age bands both to extend the model beyond 3-6 years and to characterise how dynamic viewing patterns change developmentally. Investigate diversity and individuality within the autistic group through subcategory classification and, notably, through explicit comparison of children who have and have not undergone rehabilitation training - which would convert the study's incidental finding about trained attention into a proper analysis. Predict stimulus discriminability directly with deep models, so that screening image sets can be designed rather than curated, building on the study's own demonstration that 60 well-chosen images match the full 82 while 60 random ones do not. And fuse gaze with fMRI, EEG, facial expression and observation-scale scores, described as an important and understudied challenge. What is missing from the list is the step that would matter most for the stated purpose: prospective evaluation in an unselected community population at realistic prevalence, with a described diagnostic reference standard." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-101" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Among the stronger validation designs in this corpus at the level of splitting, with two specific weaknesses further down the pipeline. The split unit is the subject: 50% of the 333 children are held out, so all 82 stimulus-based patterns from a child stay together and within-child leakage is structurally impossible - which matters here more than usual, because the model scores individual stimuli and a naive pattern-level split would have placed 41 of each test child's own patterns in training. The procedure is repeated over five random permutations with the minimum reported (95.76%), addressing split-assignment variance. The semantic autoencoder is trained on MS COCO rather than on participant data, so the representation learning introduces no leakage. Two ablation baselines are implemented on the same data rather than cited from other papers. Against this: first, the decision threshold is fitted transductively. The paper states that subject average scores were computed 'for each subject in training and testing' and the Otsu maximum between-class variance threshold selected on that combined distribution, and the same is described for the stimulus-level threshold. Otsu does not use labels, so this is not label leakage, but the operating point is nonetheless chosen with sight of the test subjects' score distribution, and in a deployment where children arrive one at a time no such distribution exists - the threshold would have to be fixed in advance from training data alone, and no result is reported under that constraint. Second, several choices were compared on the evaluation data with no separate development set: LSTM hidden units and fully connected size, epoch count, mini-batch size, learning rate, and the minLength lower bound, all reported in a single results table with the best configuration carried forward. The stimulus-discriminability ranking used to argue that 60 images suffice was likewise computed on the same data, so the proposed shortened protocol is selected in-sample. Third, no majority-class floor (57.6%) or confidence interval is given. None of these undermine the subject-level split, which is the hard part and is done correctly; they mean the 96.73% is a mildly optimistic estimate under a transductively chosen threshold rather than a fully prospective one." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-101" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Published 2023 by Northwestern Polytechnical University's School of Automation with the Institute of Artificial Intelligence at the Hefei Comprehensive National Science Center, in collaboration with the Children Healthcare Department of the Affiliated Hospital of Northwest University, the Children Rehabilitation Center of Shaanxi Rehabilitation Hospital, Beijing University of Chinese Medicine and Nanchang University - a broad engineering-clinical consortium, funded by three National Natural Science Foundation of China grants. The framing is health-equity infrastructure and is stated with more precision than most: the introduction identifies three specific barriers to screening in remote areas - affordability, child-friendliness and the availability of professionals - and every subsequent design choice is justified against one of them, including the explicit contrast with fMRI as an unaffordable alternative and the proposal that storage and analysis run on a cloud computing centre. The prevalence trajectory is cited precisely (1/68 in 2014, 1/59 in 2018, 1/44 in 2021 per US CDC), with the claim that developing-country prevalence is comparable. Two aspects of the positioning are notable for the synthesis. The paper explicitly criticises the small-sample norm in this literature, arguing from its own visualisation that 'when the number of subjects is less than 100, the test cannot accurately reflect the performance of the model' - a methodological argument that a 333-child multi-site study is entitled to make and that indicts much of the surrounding corpus. And the burden framing in the opening paragraph ('most patients diagnosed with ASD cannot independently live, study and work when they grow up, which places a heavy burden on their families and society') is the starkest deficit language encountered in this review, sitting uncomfortably beside an otherwise child-centred and access-oriented design." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-101" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Passive free viewing of 82 static images, presented in two sessions of roughly three minutes each with each image shown for 3 s and a 0.5 s grey mask between images, with the only instruction being to observe. Four stimulus categories were chosen 'according to the behavioural characteristics of children with ASD': facial images, social scenarios, animated characters and synthetic patterns. Three properties make this among the better-designed paradigms in the corpus. It is short and undemanding by explicit design, because the authors treat children's limited sustained attention as a constraint on the method rather than as a nuisance. It varies stimulus type systematically, which permits the study's most practically useful analysis - a ranking of all 82 images by discriminative power, showing facial images strongest (499.59) and animated characters weakest (356.27), and demonstrating that dropping to the best 60 images costs no accuracy while dropping to a random 60 costs a great deal. That result is a concrete contribution to protocol design that most eye-tracking studies here are not positioned to make. And the grey mask, introduced as a simple inter-stimulus interval, turns out to carry signal, since fixations landing on it are one of the three factors the authors identify as driving model scores. That last point is also the paradigm's weakness: dwelling on a blank grey screen is at least as much a measure of disengagement from the task as of atypical social attention, and the paper's own observation that 3-4 year olds in the comparison group generate 'meaningless fixations that fall on the gray mask' concedes that the same behaviour arises from ordinary inattention." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-101" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional two-group diagnostic-classification study with multi-centre recruitment, combined with a substantial descriptive analysis of gaze features and a methodological inve…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: ; comparison: 193" ] ] ;
    slr:content_sha256 "7ad56c01ae859302404d01d80f56591d6e15e183f88730cb3491bb374b414601" ;
