    slr:journal "IEEE Transactions on Biomedical Engineerin" ;
    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "unclear" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "The split is by subject, so each child's 82 stimulus patterns stay together and the evaluation units are properly independent at subject level; five random permutations are reported with the minimum given. The minLength preprocessing rule is analysed rather than applied blindly, with the authors showing that a stricter threshold would preferentially discard autistic children's data. Against this: the interval between diagnosis and testing is unreported, no attrition or data-exclusion accounting is given for the 333 children, the composition of the 165-subject test set relative to the five permutations is not fully clear, and no calibration procedure is described for the eye tracker." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "The index test is not fully prespecified. The Otsu decision threshold is fitted on subject average scores computed across training and test subjects together, so the operating point is chosen with sight of the test distribution; and network size, epoch count, mini-batch size, learning rate and the minLength lower bound are compared in a single results table on the evaluation data with no separate development set. The stimulus-discriminability ranking used to argue for a shortened 60-image protocol is likewise computed in-sample. Classification is otherwise automated and blind to labels at inference, and the semantic encoder is trained on an external corpus." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Case-control sampling at 42% autism prevalence, with autistic children recruited through hospitals and rehabilitation services and comparison children through schools. No inclusion or exclusion criteria are stated beyond normal or corrected vision, no recruitment process is described, and sex is markedly imbalanced between groups (80.0% versus 63.2% male) without matching, adjustment or comment. Mitigating features that do not lift the judgement: recruitment spans multiple hospitals and schools across two provinces, giving real site diversity; ages are well matched; and the sample of 333 is among the largest in this review." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "No reference standard is described in the main text for either group. The paper states that 140 autistic and 193 typically developing children were recruited, without naming any diagnostic instrument, criteria, clinician or procedure, and without reporting any screening of the comparison group. Table IV tabulates the diagnostic instruments used by comparator studies while leaving the study's own unstated. Clinical co-authorship at a children's healthcare department and a rehabilitation centre implies clinical diagnosis but does not substitute for a description." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "high" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<urn:slr:article:602daaeb6643> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Autism Spectrum Disorders (ASD) are a group of complex developmental conditions whose effects and severity show high intraindividual variability. However, one of the main symptoms shared along the spectrum is social interaction impairments that can be explored through acoustic analysis of speech production. In this paper, we compare 14 Italian-speaking children with ASD and 14 typically developing peers. Accordingly, we extracted and selected the acoustic features related to prosody, quality of voice, loudness, and spectral distribution using the parameter set eGeMAPS provided by the openSMILE feature extraction toolkit. We implemented four supervised machine learning methods to evaluate the extraction performances. Our findings show that Decision Trees (DTs) and Support Vector Machines (SVMs) are the best-performing methods. The overall DT models reach a 100% recall on all the trials, meaning they correctly recognise autistic features. However, half of its models overfit, while SVMs are more consistent. One of the results of the work is the creation of a speech pipeline to extract Italian speech biomarkers typical of ASD by comparing our results with studies based on other languages. A better understanding of this topic can support clinicians in diagnosing the disorder." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Beccaria F; Gagliardi G; Kokkinakis D (2022). Extraction and Classification of Acoustic Features from Italian Speaking Children with Autism Spectrum Disorders.. *Proceedings of the RaPID Workshop - Resources*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Beccaria F",
        "Gagliardi G",
        "Kokkinakis D" ;
    dcterms:title "Extraction and Classification of Acoustic Features from Italian Speaking Children with Autism Spectrum Disorders." ;
    bibo:pmid "local_afdb603d" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2022 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Recruitment sources, region, group sizes, age range and sex ratio given; no age means or tests, no clinical characterisation." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is developmental or language level measured?" ;
                            slr:justification "No cognitive, language, adaptive or severity measure in either group, in a study of narrative speech." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the comparison group screened?" ;
                            slr:justification "Typical development asserted without instrument or exclusion criteria." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched?" ;
                            slr:justification "Sex ratio matched exactly and age range shared, but no means, standard deviations or tests reported." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Were the groups recruited and recorded under comparable conditions?" ;
                            slr:justification "Autistic recordings from a prior project in a clinical setting; comparison recordings collected later at home by parents." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "One clause: diagnosis by a neuropsychiatrist under DSM-5 criteria, with no instrument or procedure." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the recording chain held constant across groups?" ;
                            slr:justification "Tripod-mounted video camera in a therapy playroom versus parents' phones at home, with no distances, devices or sampling rates specified." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the elicitation task held constant across groups?" ;
                            slr:justification "The same six-picture birthday-party narrative was used for both groups." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the feature set principled and standard?" ;
                            slr:justification "eGeMAPS chosen over ComParE and GeMAPS with explicit arguments about dimensionality and psychological grounding, and fully tabulated." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the acquisition confound applied consistently in interpretation?" ;
                            slr:justification "Loudness results are discarded on this basis while voice-quality and spectral results are retained." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the acoustic measures robust to the acquisition differences?" ;
                            slr:justification "Jitter, shimmer, harmonics-to-noise ratio, loudness and spectral measures are all functions of microphone type, distance and room acoustics." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the selection bias acknowledged?" ;
                            slr:justification "Stated in advance with a supporting citation to comparable work in dementia speech." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is any baseline reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No chance level, no unselected-feature comparator, no demographic baseline." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is feature selection nested within validation?" ;
                            slr:justification "Mann-Whitney selection performed across all 28 samples before the train-test split." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the test set adequate?" ;
                            slr:justification "Six children, so 83% accuracy is five of six and no confidence interval is possible." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is variability across runs reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Ten runs per method with means and standard deviations, showing SVM at 79.8% (SD 6.75) against a best of 83%." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is model selection based on stability rather than peak performance?" ;
                            slr:justification "SVM preferred over the decision tree for consistency despite equal peak accuracy, with the overfitting explicitly diagnosed." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Publicly released on GitHub with the URL given in the text." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are feature-level findings reported in full?" ;
                            slr:justification "All 16 selected features with group means, standard deviations, outlier counts and exact p-values." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is ethics approval reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No committee approval for either group; caregiver consent stated only for the autistic group." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the scope of the claim appropriately narrowed?" ;
                            slr:justification "Stated as a feature-extraction validity study, explicitly not a classifier for new speech data." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the recording confound treated as a limitation?" ;
                            slr:justification "Disclosed and defended, applied to loudness only, and correctly diagnosed in the future-work recommendation for head-mounted microphones." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible?" ;
                            slr:justification "Recordings not shared and no availability statement." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the literature engaged with critically?" ;
                            slr:justification "Contradictory findings reported throughout, including studies that disprove cited interpretations and one family of results the authors decline to treat as meaningful." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Uneven. Fully reported: the elicitation stimulus with its source; both recording arrangements including their divergence and its cause; the feature set with its version, the rationale for choosing it over two alternatives, and a complete table of all extracted feature families with definitions; the selection test; all 16 selected features with group means, standard deviations, outlier counts and exact p-values; the four classifiers; the split proportions and resulting sample sizes; the cross-validation scheme; the number of runs; both best-model and ten-run mean performance; and public code. Not reported: participant age means or tests; any cognitive, language or severity measure; the diagnostic procedure beyond one clause; recording durations, distances, sampling rates or device models; any classifier hyperparameters; comparison-group consent or ethics approval for either group; and any characterisation of speech quantity per child." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-087" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Partially, and in a revealing pattern. The recording divergence is disclosed in full and then defended: 'Even though we are aware that this might eschew our results, we consider that it will not have that significant impact because the storytelling task remained the same, and the quality of the recordings was high (i.e., there was no background noise).' The defence addresses task comparability and the absence of noise in the new recordings, neither of which is the issue - the issue is a systematic difference between two recording chains, which shifts every spectral and voice-quality measure regardless of how clean each chain is individually. The authors nonetheless demonstrate that they grasp the mechanism, because they apply it correctly to their own loudness findings: 'This difference in the results could be caused by the most reported problems: the recording environment and the microphone's position... For these reasons, we decided not to consider the results obtained as relevant for the present.' Having discarded loudness on exactly this reasoning, they retain jitter, shimmer, harmonics-to-noise ratio and the spectral measures, which depend on the same variables. The feature-selection bias is likewise acknowledged in advance with a citation to comparable work in dementia speech, and the scope is explicitly narrowed to feature-extraction validity rather than classification. Not acknowledged: the six-sample test set; that the headline figures are best-of-ten against much lower means; the absence of any diagnostic instrument, severity measure or comparison-group screening; the absence of cognitive and language measures; and the missing ethics approval." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-087" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The acquisition equipment is the study's decisive variable and is reported with enough candour to be evaluated. Autistic group: a video camera on a three-legged support, at an unspecified distance, in a therapy playroom. Comparison group: parents' own phones, model unspecified, at home. No sampling rates, microphone specifications, distances or room characteristics are given for either. The analysis chain is by contrast well specified - openSMILE 2.1 with eGeMAPS, chosen over ComParE because 6,376 features 'vastly outnumbers the sample size', and over GeMAPS for its psychological grounding - with Python, scikit-learn and Jupyter, and public code. The authors' own recommendation for future work is the correct diagnosis of what went wrong: 'it would be necessary that all the participants wear [a microphone] simultaneously from their mouths to ensure that all the variations are due to the actual speech production'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-087" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Accuracy, recall, precision, F1 and AUC, reported on both training and test sets for the best model of each method, plus means and standard deviations across ten runs. Reporting training alongside test performance is what allows overfitting to be diagnosed and the authors use it that way. Reporting the ten-run distribution rather than only the maximum is better practice still, and it substantially deflates the headline: SVM's mean accuracy is 79.8% against a best of 83%, the decision tree's mean is 68.2% against a best of 83%, and KNN's mean is 58.5%. What no metric can repair is the denominator. The test set contains six children, so 83% accuracy is five of six and 100% recall is three of three; a single reclassified child moves accuracy by 17 percentage points, and no confidence interval accompanies any figure. With 22 training and 6 test samples, and a further split into three cross-validation folds within the training set, the effective sample at every stage is too small for the metrics to discriminate between methods." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-087" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Five directions, of which two are unusually well targeted. Redesign the recording process so that intensity and pitch can be analysed at all, with the concrete specification that all participants should wear a microphone at a fixed mouth distance - the authors note this must be balanced against 'the necessity of maintaining some environmental comforts for the patients in a clinical condition', which correctly identifies the tension between naturalistic elicitation and acoustic control. Adopt common feature sets across studies so results are comparable across languages, citing De La Fuente and colleagues - a standardisation argument that this review's cross-linguistic study (Lau and colleagues) makes in a different form. The remaining three are larger datasets with feature-subset comparison to build a standard acoustic set for autism; investigation of how voice features relate to speaker age, sex and height; and testing whether the jitter and shimmer pattern seen here in children and reported by Kissine and Geelhand in adults persists across the lifespan, which the authors suggest would be 'a turning point in the early diagnosis'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-087" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Semi-spontaneous narrative from a six-picture sequence depicting a birthday party, from a commercially available storytelling toy, chosen as 'easily interpretable by neurotypical children of different ages'. This is a reasonable elicitation - picture-sequence narration is the standard paradigm in the narrative literature, it produces connected spontaneous speech rather than repetition or reading, and holding the task constant across groups is what the authors correctly identify as the one thing they did preserve when the recording arrangements diverged. Two limitations follow from the design's reuse. The autistic recordings were collected for a different study, on discourse and storytelling, so the elicitation was optimised for qualitative narrative analysis rather than acoustic measurement - which is precisely why the original comparison recordings from that project were unusable. And recording durations, utterance counts and speech quantity are not reported for either group, so it is not known whether the two groups produced comparable amounts of speech, which bears on the stability of every averaged acoustic functional." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-087" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Proceedings of the RaPID-4 workshop at LREC 2022 (Resources and Processing of linguistic, para-linguistic and extra-linguistic Data from people with various forms of cognitive or psychiatric impairment), published by ELRA under CC-BY-NC. Authorship spans the Department of Classical Philology and Italian Studies at the University of Bologna and the Department of Swedish, Multilingualism, Language and Technology at the University of Gothenburg, with the first author a student. This is a computational linguistics workshop rather than a clinical or engineering venue, which explains the paper's profile precisely: the linguistic and acoustic scholarship is of a high standard, the engagement with the prosody literature is extensive and critical, and the clinical characterisation, ethics reporting and validation design are correspondingly thin. The clinical data were collected by a third party for a prior thesis, acknowledged by name." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-087" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "One feature vector per child, so the unit is the participant and there is no within-subject leakage. The representational contribution is the argued choice of eGeMAPS: ComParE was rejected on an explicit dimensionality argument relative to sample size, and eGeMAPS chosen over GeMAPS on grounds of psychological interpretability, with the whole parameter set tabulated and defined. Using a published standard parameter set rather than a bespoke feature collection is also what makes the study's cross-language comparison possible - the authors compare their selected features directly against those reported by Pokorny and colleagues using the same set and find agreement on unvoiced spectral slope in the 0-500 Hz range - and it is the basis of their recommendation that the field standardise on common feature sets. That is a genuine methodological contribution independent of this study's data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-087" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Paralinguistic voice production during narrative, decomposed into four theoretically motivated families rather than treated as an undifferentiated feature vector. Frequency-related parameters index intonation and vocal-tract configuration; voice quality parameters (jitter, shimmer, harmonics-to-noise ratio) index the regularity of vocal fold vibration and are framed as measuring 'the difficulty in controlling the vocal fold vibrations, transforming the production into hoarseness, breathiness, and creaky voice'; loudness indexes intensity and, via its variability, 'the dynamic intonation of autistic speech production'; and spectral parameters index energy distribution. The theoretical grounding distinguishes pragmatic or linguistic prosody, carrying illocutionary force and sentence type, from affective prosody, carrying emotional state - the same distinction Lau and colleagues make - and locates the autism-relevant impairment in the affective channel, with consequences for both production and comprehension. The construct is coherent and the discussion of each family is genuinely scholarly; what it cannot survive is that all four families are equally functions of the recording apparatus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-087" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2022. The framing is linguistic rather than translational: the motivating gap is that 'there are already many English contributions showing abnormalities of autistic speech at the prosodic level' but 'few studies on this promising field in Italian', so the question is whether findings established in one language transfer to another - the same question Lau and colleagues address between English and Cantonese, approached here for a language typologically closer to English but still untested. The historical framing is distinctive and well handled, quoting Crystal's characterisation of prosody research as 'the Cinderella of speech' that remains 'in the cellar, with few visitors', and tracing the neglect back to Kanner's original 1943 description, which described a child for whom 'it made no difference whether one spoke to him in a friendly or a harsh way' - an observation the authors note 'the researchers ignored... in the decades that followed'. The clinical ambition is stated modestly and consistently: the aim is a tool that 'may evolve, through further studies' and could 'support clinicians in diagnosing the disorder', with the classification component explicitly disclaimed as not intended for use on new speech data. That restraint distinguishes the paper from most in this corpus and makes its data problems more regrettable rather than less, since the scholarship would have supported a stronger study." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-087" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "None whatsoever - no cognitive, language, adaptive, severity or comorbidity measure for either group. The absence is particularly consequential here because the outcome is narrative speech elicited from a picture sequence: expressive language level determines how much a child says, how fluently, and with what syntactic complexity, all of which shape the acoustic signal, and it would be expected to differ between a group recruited from speech and language therapy services and a community comparison group. Nothing in the data permits this to be examined, and the paper does not raise it." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-087" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Single modality with no fusion. The paper's comparative work is instead across classifiers, and the reporting of it is better than the headline suggests. Four methods are run ten times each and both the best model and the ten-run mean with standard deviation are given, which makes the instability visible: the decision tree's best model reaches 83% accuracy and 100% recall on the test set but its ten-run mean is 68.2% with a standard deviation of 16.40, and the authors read this correctly as overfitting - 'half of the ten best models of all the ones trained for DT are good in the classification task, but the others tend to decrease their performances drastically from the train to the test sets'. SVM is identified as most consistent (mean 79.8%, SD 6.75) and preferred on that basis rather than on peak performance, with the reasoning that linear SVM 'is the best to use when dealing with small datasets'. Choosing a model for stability over peak accuracy, and reporting the distribution that justifies it, is the right instinct and is rare in this corpus." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-087" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional, one recording per child, with the two groups recorded at different times separated by the onset of the pandemic. Ethical reporting is incomplete: written consent from caregivers is stated for the autistic group, but no consent statement appears for the comparison group and no ethics committee approval is reported for either. Data are not shared. The analysis code, however, is publicly released on GitHub, which is rare in this corpus and materially improves verifiability of the computational steps even though the recordings themselves are unavailable." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-087" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The weakest dimension, and the authors say so more clearly than most. Feature selection by Mann-Whitney test was performed across all 28 samples before any split, so the 16 retained features are those that best separate the very children on whom the classifiers are then evaluated. The consequence is stated in advance and with a supporting citation: 'the feature selection operated on clinical recorded data can lead to high performances of the classifier, which are not reproducible on new data considering all the speech production. This aspect produces a bias in the results obtained by the classifier when used to create a tool able to distinguish the speech productions of the disease. For instance, while performing the same task on people with Alzheimer Dementia's speech, Luz et al. (2020) report that the performance evaluation metrics drop consistently if applied to the same dataset without performing the feature selection.' The scope is then narrowed accordingly - 'this work aims to test the feature extraction effectiveness and not automatically classify ASD from new speech data' - which is the right response to a known bias, though it does not remove it from the reported numbers. Three further limits compound it: an 80/20 split leaves six test samples; ten runs per method were performed with the best reported alongside the means; and there is no independent validation, no permutation test, no confidence interval and no baseline. The single most important validity threat, however, is not statistical at all: features selected for their ability to separate two groups recorded on different equipment will select for the equipment difference, and no amount of validation discipline within the dataset can detect that." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-087" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two entirely different settings, and this is the study's defining problem. Autistic children were recorded in June 2019 by a designated speech therapist, in the room where they usually play, during a session that 'started with activities generally done during the treatment session so the child would act in the most spontaneous way', with 'a video camera placed on a tree-legged support'. Comparison children were recorded later by their own parents at home on the parents' phones, because 'due to the COVID-19 pandemic, it was impossible to collect the data in situ'. The clinical setting was designed carefully for the autistic children's comfort, which is good practice for eliciting natural speech and simultaneously guarantees a fixed, distant, uncontrolled microphone position in a room with unknown reverberation. A parent holding a phone produces a close-miked, low-noise, high-signal recording. Since the paper's entire yield is acoustic, the recording chain is not a nuisance variable but a direct competitor for every reported effect." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-087" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Keywords: Autism Spectrum Disorders, acoustic analysis, machine learning, openSMILE, eGeMAPS. Naming both the toolkit and the parameter set is deliberate and consistent with the paper's argument that the field should standardise on shared feature sets so that findings become comparable across studies and languages." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-087" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with ASD', 'people with autism', 'autistic people', 'the ASD group', with person-first and identity-first used interchangeably. The register is inconsistent in one respect that stands out: autism is repeatedly referred to as 'the disease' and 'the disorder' - 'to be able to use it during the diagnosis', 'distinguish the speech productions of the disease', 'a turning point in the early diagnosis of the disease' - and participants are twice called 'patients'. Against this, the paper is unusually attentive to correcting a stereotype, arguing from its own pitch data that 'the increasing pitch could suggest chasing away the idea that the speech of people with ASD is robotic, monotonous and without melodic variation, as reported in the past literature', and returning to the point again in the loudness discussion. The historical framing is careful too, quoting Kanner's original observation and noting that 'the researchers ignored this part of Kanner's study in the decades that followed'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-087" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The strongest part of the paper, and the reason it is worth charting despite its data problems. Every one of the 16 selected features is reported with group means, standard deviations, outlier counts and exact p-values, and then discussed individually against named prior work - with contradictory findings reported rather than suppressed. The pitch literature is described as containing 'opposite findings'; a proposed link between pitch excursions and 'motherese' speech is cited and then noted to have been 'disprove[n]' by later work; the loudness literature is enumerated as containing higher values, lower values, null results, and studies that avoided intensity entirely 'to avoid the risk of obtaining unclear results'; and the spectral findings are flagged as lacking comparators, with the authors stating plainly that 'we did not find any other relevant studies to confirm the importance of spectral features to detect ASD'. The jitter-shimmer-HNR discussion is the most substantive, correctly identifying the expected inverse relationship between jitter and harmonics-to-noise ratio, verifying it in their own data, and connecting it to the voice-pathology literature on breathiness and hoarseness. The interpretive irony is that this same literature supplies the reason to doubt the finding: harmonics-to-noise ratio is a signal-to-noise measure, and the two groups' signals were acquired through different chains." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-087" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Matched by design and reported only at that level. Both groups comprise 14 children aged 6 to 10 with 11 males and 3 females. No means, standard deviations or statistical tests are given for age, so the groups are known to share a range rather than shown to be equivalent - and across ages 6 to 10 children's vocal tracts, fundamental frequencies and narrative abilities change substantially, so a within-range imbalance would matter. The sex ratio was chosen deliberately to reflect the DSM-5 epidemiological figure of four males per female, which is a defensible design choice for representativeness though it means only three girls contribute to each group. No IQ, language, adaptive or severity measure was obtained for either group, so cognitive and linguistic level are entirely uncontrolled in a study whose outcome is narrative speech production. The authors identify a related gap themselves, noting that formant and fundamental frequency features are 'sensitive to the speaker's age, gender, and height' and recommending that future work investigate 'the correlation of voice features to the personal characteristics of the speakers'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-087" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: establish whether acoustic markers of autism reported for English and other languages also appear in Italian, using a standard clinical parameter set, with classification as a check on whether the selected features carry signal. Main findings: 16 of 88 eGeMAPS parameters differed significantly, spanning voice quality (jitter, shimmer, harmonics-to-noise ratio), loudness, pitch dynamics and spectral distribution; best classifiers reached 83% accuracy on a six-sample test set with SVM most consistent across ten runs at a mean of 79.8%; jitter and shimmer were higher on average but less variable in the autistic group, matching a pattern reported in Italian-language-independent adult work; and mean pitch was higher though not significantly, which the authors use to argue against the monotone-speech stereotype." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-087" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two groups of 14, drawn from a homogeneous Tuscan area between Florence, Pistoia and Prato, and matched by construction on group size, age range and sex ratio. The autistic children came from the speech and language therapy service of AUSL Toscana Centro and the Opera Santa Rita foundation, and their recordings were reused from a prior thesis project on discourse and storytelling in autism. The comparison children were recruited separately for this study 'from the same geographical area' with 'the same demographic characteristics'. No other-diagnosis comparison group. The critical structural fact is that the two groups were not merely recruited separately but recorded separately, at different times, by different people, using different equipment, in different environments - a distinction that matters far more for acoustic analysis than for the behavioural comparisons elsewhere in this corpus, and one that the paper discloses fully while underweighting its consequences." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-087" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "One sentence: 'The diagnosis was made by a neuropsychiatrist according to DSM-5 criteria.' No standardised observational instrument such as ADOS, no interview such as ADI-R, no rating scale, no statement of when the diagnosis was made, whether it was made for this study or taken from clinical records, or what the diagnostic procedure comprised. Recruitment through a speech and language therapy service and a disability foundation indicates that all participants were receiving services, but nothing further is established. The comparison group's status is asserted rather than screened - no instrument, no exclusion criteria, and no statement about family history or developmental concerns. This is among the weakest ascertainments in the corpus and is not acknowledged as a limitation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-087" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Four conventional classifiers - decision tree, K-nearest neighbours, random forest and SVM - run ten times each on 16 preselected features. The classification is explicitly instrumental rather than the contribution: 'the final aim of these implementations is to test whether these acoustic features are typical in the speech production of Italian-speaking children with ASD. Good results in the performances of the classifiers would confirm this assumption.' That framing is coherent but it does not escape the underlying problem, since features selected for significance on the same 28 samples will by construction classify those samples well regardless of what produced the difference. No hyperparameters are reported for any method, so the comparison across classifiers cannot be assessed for equal tuning effort, and no baseline of any kind is provided - not a chance level, not a demographic comparator, not the unselected 88-feature set, which would have been the most informative control given the authors' own citation of Luz and colleagues showing that performance 'drop[s] consistently if applied to the same dataset without performing the feature selection'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-087" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "One storytelling interview per child; recording durations are not reported" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional case-control acoustic comparison with a supervised classification step used as a validity check on feature selection, drawing on a previously collected autistic cor…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Italian-speaking children with autism aged 6 to 10" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 14; comparison: 14" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Best models on the six-sample test set: decision tree 83% accuracy, 100% recall, 96% precision, 80% F1, 88% AUC; SVM 83%, 100%, 67%, 80%, 88%; random forest 83%, 50%, 100%, 67%, 75%; KNN 67%, 100%, 50%, 67%, 75%. Means across ten runs are substantially lower and more informative: SVM 79.8% accuracy (SD 6.75) with AUC 84.9 (SD 5.44); random forest 70.1% (SD 10.33); decision tree 68.2% (SD 16.40) wi",
                        "Sixteen of 88 eGeMAPS acoustic parameters differed significantly between 14 Italian autistic and 14 typically developing children narrating a picture story, spanning voice quality (higher mean jitter and shimmer, lower harmonics-to-noise ratio), loudness (higher on all functionals), pitch dynamics (greater variability in falling slope) and spectral distribution (higher spectral flux, steeper unvoi" ;
                    slr:outcome "Accuracy, recall, precision, F1 and AUC, reported separately for the training and test sets for the best model of each method, plus means and standard deviations across ten runs per method" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Best models on the six-sample test set: decision tree 83% accuracy, 100% recall, 96% precision, 80% F1, 88% AUC; SVM 83%, 100%, 67%, 80%, 88%; random forest 83%, 50%, 100%, 67%, 75%; KNN 67%, 100%, 50%, 67%, 75%. Means across ten runs are substantially lower and more informative: SVM 79.8% accuracy (SD 6.75) with AUC 84.9 (SD 5.44); random forest 70.1% (SD 10.33); decision tree 68.2% (SD 16.40) wi; Sixteen of 88 eGeMAPS acoustic parameters differed significantly between 14 Italian autistic and 14 typically developing children narrating a picture story, spanning voice quality (higher mean jitter and shimmer, lower harmonics-to-noise ratio), loudness (higher on all functionals), pitch dynamics (greater variability in falling slope) and spectral distribution (higher spectral flux, steeper unvoi" ] ] ;
