    slr:journal "Children, 11(4), 429" ;
    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Feature reduction from 100 to 59 was made a priori on clinical-relevance grounds rather than by data-driven selection; the milestone binarisation rule is stated with its rationale and its cost; the Youden threshold is an objective, prespecified criterion rather than a tuned operating point; no hyperparameter search is reported; and four modelling methods are compared with full confusion counts rather than the best being selected and reported alone." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "The interval between predictor and outcome is imposed by design: predictors capped at 24 months, outcomes restricted to diagnoses recorded after 24 months, adopted explicitly to prevent leakage from diagnosis to predictors, with mean age at diagnosis 37.5 months. Cross-validation partitions children. Training and test AUC are both reported with overfitting acknowledged. Variable follow-up by birth cohort is addressed by a sensitivity model excluding 2019 births, and an external validity check using a variable deliberately excluded from the model confirms that flagged children were independently referred for developmental assessment at 3.29 times the rate." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "A whole national population cohort of 780,610 children born 2014-2019 with at least two years of computerised records, drawn from a universal Ministry of Health surveillance programme covering 75% of the country with the remainder under identical guidelines. There is no recruitment, no case-control sampling and no spectrum truncation: the comparison group is every other child in the cohort. Exclusion logic is stated and reasoned, including the deliberate retention of Fragile X, tuberous sclerosis and other autism-associated aetiologies on the grounds that autism has multiple causes. The one caveat, insufficient to raise the rating, is that the 25% of the population followed by health maintenance organisations are absent." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Routine clinical diagnosis recorded in health records, with no research instrument and no autism-specific screening tool in the surveillance protocol. Ascertainment is split between ICD-9 coding (56.6%) and verified free-text keyword search (43.4%), with provider recording practice varying. Decisively, the authors state that 'based on the national incidence of ASD, the true positive ASD group is at least 10 times higher in the examined population', so the great majority of autistic children in the cohort are labelled as comparison cases - substantial and acknowledged outcome misclassification, described by the authors as adding 'a lot of noise to the outcome label'." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "unclear" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<urn:slr:article:894b14e604e6> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "This paper presents a speech-based system for autism severity estimation combined with automatic speaker diarization. Speaker diarization was performed by two different methods. The first used acoustic features, which included MelFrequency Cepstral Coefficients (MFCC) and pitch, and the second used x-vectors – embeddings extracted from Deep Neural Networks (DNN). The speaker diarization was trained using a Fully Connected Deep Neural Network (FCDNN) in both methods. We then trained a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) to estimate the severity of autism based on 48 acoustic and prosodic features of speech. One hundred thirty-two young children were recorded in the Autism Diagnostic Observation Schedule (ADOS) examination room, using a distant microphone. Between the two diarization methods, the MFCC and Pitch achieved a better Diarization Error Rate (DER) of 26.91%. Using this diarization method, the severity estimation system achieved a correlation of 0.606 (Pearson) between the predicted and the actual autism severity scores (i.e., ADOS scores). Clinical Relevance—The presented system identifies children's speech segments and estimates their autism severity score. I. INTRODUCTION Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) is a neurodevelopmental disorder diagnosed by social communication impairments, repetitive behaviors, and confined interests [1]. A common clinical tool for assessing ASD severity is the Autism Diagnostic Observation Schedule (ADOS) [2], a 45min assessment where a clinician interacts with the child using a variety of tasks/games and scores the child’s behavior using a standardized scale. Raw ADOS scores range between 0 and 30, with higher values indicating more severe ASD symptoms. The vast majority of ASD children exhibit speech and expressive language abnormalities, which range from a total lack of speech (i.e., non-verbal children) to those who develop normal vocabulary and syntax, but exhibit difficulties with the use of appropriate prosody and pragmatics [3]. Previous studies have demonstrated that verbal children with ASD speak in abnormal ways, including strange prosody [4], increased pitch variability and pitch range [5], slower speech rate, and prolonged word production. These studies used manual annotation and inspection of the audio recordings. To perform automatic analysis of ADOS recordings, there is a need for a speaker diarization algorithm that divides the recording into segments of child and of other speakers. *Research supported by Israel Ministry of Science and Technology (Grant no. 3-17422). Speaker diarization is affected by the quality of the audio recording, the number of recording microphones, the distance between microphones and speaking subjects. This task is especially challenging in real-life recordings, like sessions performed in a clinic during the ADOS diagnosis, including" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Eni M; Gorodetski A; Dinstein I; Zigel Y (2022). The Impact of Speaker Diarization on DNN-based Autism Severity Estimation. *2022 44th Annual International Conference of the IEEE Engineering in Medicine &*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Dinstein I",
        "Eni M",
        "Gorodetski A",
        "Zigel Y" ;
    dcterms:title "The Impact of Speaker Diarization on DNN-based Autism Severity Estimation" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_988c462e" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2022 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the comparison group clinically ascertained?" ;
                            slr:justification "Comprises children with language or developmental delays alongside typically developing children, with ADOS scores reported." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Group sizes, ages, sex proportions and ADOS scores given; no cognitive, language or adaptive measures and no ethnicity or socioeconomic data." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is expressive language level measured?" ;
                            slr:justification "None, in a cohort described as mostly not verbally fluent, where language ability plausibly mediates both predictor and outcome." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are groups comparable on age and sex?" ;
                            slr:justification "3.7 versus 3.1 years and 80.0% versus 77.8% male, though neither is formally tested." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the effect of the minimum-speech requirement quantified?" ;
                            slr:justification "At least 20 seconds and 50 vocalisations required; the number of children excluded is not reported." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is manual annotation quality assessed?" ;
                            slr:justification "No inter-rater reliability, but the authors infer its imperfection from automatic diarization outperforming it and explain the mechanism." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "ADOS-2 by a clinician with research reliability, with raw scores as the continuous target." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the diarization procedure described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Both feature sets with dimensionality, the shared network structure, Viterbi post-processing, and the evaluation tool and collar." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the features specified?" ;
                            slr:justification "Forty-eight features named only by four examples, with the full set deferred to a prior publication." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the recording setup described reproducibly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Microphone model, 1-2 m distance, 44.1 kHz downsampled to 16 kHz, mean session duration and 12 +/- 5 dB signal-to-noise ratio all reported." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are splits participant-level and repeated?" ;
                            slr:justification "80:20 by child, executed 50 times, with means and standard deviations reported." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are competing configurations compared fairly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Three segmentation conditions with the same downstream architecture and per-condition tuned hyperparameters, plus total detected duration reported for each." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is hyperparameter tuning nested within the outer splits?" ;
                            slr:justification "Random search under five-fold cross-validation is described but its position relative to the 50 outer splits is not stated." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the unit of analysis the participant?" ;
                            slr:justification "One 100x48 matrix and one prediction per child, so the internal resampling of vocalisations does not create multiple test cases." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is severity grading distinguished from group separation?" ;
                            slr:justification "The correlation spans two clusters averaging ADOS 14.6 and 3.2, with no within-autism correlation reported." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the outcome distribution controlled across splits?" ;
                            slr:justification "Mean, standard deviation, kurtosis and skewness of ADOS scores matched between partitions - the right control for a bimodal target." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Not mentioned; several method components are deferred to prior publications." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "No availability statement." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is severity predicted rather than only classified?" ;
                            slr:justification "ADOS raw score is the regression target, addressing a gap the authors identify in the classification literature." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No approval, consent or institutional review statement, despite analysing recorded clinical assessments of young children." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the result positioned honestly against prior work?" ;
                            slr:justification "Described as 'similar' to a prior 0.634 in 33 children rather than as an improvement, with acquisition and sample differences acknowledged." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are limitations stated candidly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Audio quality and the minimum-speech requirement are named; the between-group component of the correlation is not." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two networks in sequence, each matched to its task. Diarization uses a fully connected network with two stacked softmax layers, the second added on cited grounds that it improves final classification, followed by Viterbi decoding to impose temporal coherence on the frame-level outputs - a sensible structure for a sequence-labelling problem. Severity estimation uses a convolutional network with a linear output for regression, trained with RMSprop and hyperparameters selected by random search under five-fold cross-validation, with the selected values reported separately for each segmentation condition. Reporting per-condition hyperparameters is a small but meaningful detail: it shows the comparison between segmentation methods was made with each given its own tuned configuration rather than one set imposed across all three. Neither architecture is described in enough depth to reimplement, with details deferred to a prior publication." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-093" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Reported compactly and adequately for both groups: 105 autistic children aged 3.7 +/- 1.3 years, 80.0% male, ADOS 14.6 +/- 6.5; and 27 non-autistic children aged 3.1 +/- 1.5 years, 77.8% male, ADOS 3.2 +/- 3.7. The groups are close on sex (a 2.2-point difference) and reasonably close on age (0.6 years), though neither is formally tested and matching is not claimed. The overall age range of 1 to 7 years is given in the discussion. What is absent is any cognitive, language or adaptive measure, which is a real gap for a study whose predictor is speech and whose sample is described as 'mostly do not speak fluently or use little or no phrase speech' - expressive language level would be the obvious covariate, and it plausibly mediates both the acoustic features and the ADOS score. Ethnicity and socioeconomic information are not reported." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-093" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No keyword list appears in the extracted text. The operative vocabulary is speaker diarization, severity estimation, diarization error rate, ADOS score and prosodic features - a speech-engineering register applied to a clinical regression target, which accurately describes a paper contributed to a biomedical engineering venue by a signal-processing group working with a clinical centre." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-093" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2022. The framing is unusually precise about what the field has and has not done. Prior speech studies 'reported high classification performance' but 'did not estimate the severity of autism symptoms in individual children', and the two exceptions are named and characterised - one on Japanese-speaking adolescents using support vector regression, one on English-speaking children reaching a correlation of 0.634 in 33 participants. The paper also identifies the specific practical dependency it is removing: the earlier acoustic findings on prosody, pitch variability and speech rate all 'used manual annotation and inspection of the audio recordings', which is what makes them non-scalable. Its comparative positioning is likewise concrete rather than promotional: it acknowledges achieving worse diarization error than a four-microphone beamforming study, explains why, notes that a prior 499-hour corpus comprised verbally fluent children whereas this cohort 'mostly do not speak fluently or use little or no phrase speech', and describes its own correlation as 'similar' to the 0.634 of a smaller study rather than as an improvement. Adding a Hebrew-language cohort also extends the linguistic coverage of this corpus, which is otherwise dominated by English with single studies in Italian, Polish, Cantonese and Chinese." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-093" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Fully specified and deliberately minimal: a single AKG CHM99 microphone at 1-2 metres, 44.1 kHz downsampled to 16 kHz, in the ADOS room. Reporting the microphone model, its distance, the sampling and downsampling rates, the mean session duration and the achieved signal-to-noise ratio is more complete acquisition reporting than most speech studies in this corpus provide, and it matters here because the paper's central claim - that automatic diarization matches manual annotation - depends on the audio being genuinely difficult. Analysis tools are named specifically: the Brno University of Technology x-vector system with its window size and rate, the dscore tool with a 0.25-second collar, and RMSprop with random-search tuning under five-fold cross-validation, with the selected batch size, epoch count and learning rate tabulated separately for each of the three segmentation conditions." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-093" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Appropriate to a regression problem and reported with variability. Severity estimation uses Pearson correlation and normalised RMSE, each as a mean and standard deviation across 50 repeated splits - so the reader sees not only that R is 0.606 but that its standard deviation is 0.099, which places the manual baseline of 0.567 +/- 0.120 well within the automatic method's variation and correctly supports the paper's cautious claim of 'equivalent or slightly better' rather than a claim of superiority. Diarization is evaluated with the standard diarization error rate, decomposed into substitution, false-accept and missed-detection components with the collar specified, plus recall, precision and accuracy on child segments, and with a stated and reasonable justification for excluding silence errors. The most useful supplementary figure is the total detected vocalisation duration under each condition - 10.85, 11.11 and 10.72 hours - which demonstrates that the three segmentation methods recovered comparable amounts of speech and so that the downstream differences are not simply a matter of one method finding more data. No confidence intervals on the correlations beyond the across-split standard deviations, and no significance test between conditions." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-093" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No purpose-built task - the speech analysed is whatever the child produced during a standard 45-minute ADOS-2 administration. This is the design's principal translational strength: any clinic already recording ADOS sessions holds the raw material, no additional child burden is imposed, and the sampling context is by construction the one in which the reference score is generated. The corresponding limitation is the one the review has noted for other ADOS-derived studies: the assessment is designed to elicit autism-related behaviour, so acoustic differences observed within it may not generalise to spontaneous conversation. The paper does not raise this. A second, more specific constraint is imposed downstream: the severity model requires at least 20 seconds of child speech comprising at least 50 vocalisations, so children who spoke less than this could not be analysed - and in a cohort described as mostly not verbally fluent, that threshold plausibly excludes a meaningful proportion, though the number is not given." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-093" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Among the better-designed validations in the corpus for a regression problem, with one important limitation that is interpretive rather than procedural. The split is by child and repeated 50 times, and - distinctively - each split is constructed so that the ADOS score distribution matches the full sample on four moments: 'we divided the children into training and testing datasets while balancing the distribution of ADOS scores (mean, standard deviation, kurtosis, and skewness) in each dataset'. Matching skewness and kurtosis as well as mean and variance is the right control for a bimodally distributed target, since an unlucky split could otherwise place most of the low-scoring comparison children on one side and inflate or deflate the correlation. Reporting means and standard deviations across the 50 repetitions rather than a single split follows properly. Hyperparameters were tuned by random search under five-fold cross-validation, though whether that tuning sat inside the 50 outer splits or was performed once beforehand is not stated - which leaves some selection optimism unquantified. The substantive limitation is what the correlation measures rather than how it was estimated: computed across a sample where the autistic group averages ADOS 14.6 and the comparison group 3.2, R = 0.606 conflates between-group separation with within-group severity grading, and no within-autism correlation is reported. That is the analysis that would distinguish a severity estimator from a diagnostic classifier expressed on a continuous scale, and it is the same conflation this review has flagged in Kojovic and colleagues." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-093" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "One cohort of 132 children recorded during routine ADOS-2 assessments, partitioned by diagnosis into 105 autistic and 27 non-autistic. The comparison group is the design feature worth noting: it comprises children 'diagnosed with language or developmental delays or were defined as typically developing', so it is a clinically referred population rather than a screened-typical one. That means the model is fitted across the range of children who actually present for autism assessment, and the regression target spans a continuum from ADOS 0 to 30 rather than two discrete classes. The proportion of the 27 who were developmentally delayed rather than typically developing is not reported, which limits how far the specificity claim can be pushed. No exclusion criteria are stated beyond the downstream requirement of at least 20 seconds and 50 vocalisations of child speech, and how many children that requirement removed is not reported." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-093" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Proceedings of the 2022 44th Annual International Conference of the IEEE Engineering in Medicine and Biology Society (EMBC), DOI 10.1109/EMBC48229.2022.9871523. All authors are at Ben-Gurion University of the Negev, spanning biomedical engineering and the Azrieli National Centre for Autism and Neurodevelopment Research, so signal-processing and clinical autism expertise are co-located in a single institution - which shows in the study's access to research-reliable ADOS administration and in the realism of its recording setup. Funded by the Israel Ministry of Science and Technology with a grant number. The work builds explicitly on the same group's prior publications for the vocalisation segmentation method, the 48-feature set and the CNN architecture, so this paper reports an increment - the substitution of automatic diarization for manual labelling - rather than a standalone system." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-093" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No cross-modal fusion, but the study performs a well-designed three-way comparison of segmentation inputs feeding an identical downstream model - manual annotation, MFCC-plus-pitch diarization, and x-vector diarization - which isolates the contribution of the segmentation stage cleanly. The result is the paper's most interesting finding and runs against expectation in two ways. Automatic diarization matched or slightly beat manual annotation (R 0.606 and 0.581 against 0.567), which the authors explain rather than merely report: 'the manual segmentation is not perfectly accurate, especially for the child segments. The child speech is often quiet and is sometimes masked by noises, especially when using a distant microphone in a reverberant environment.' And the more sophisticated representation lost: 512-dimensional x-vectors achieved better child-segment accuracy (89.84%) than MFCC plus pitch, yet produced worse downstream severity estimation - so segment-classification accuracy and downstream utility diverge, and optimising the intermediate metric would have selected the worse system. That dissociation between an intermediate objective and the end-to-end outcome is a transferable lesson for any pipeline architecture." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-093" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No feature-level interpretability: the 48 features are not individually reported, no importance analysis is performed, and no acoustic characterisation of severity is offered, so the study contributes a prediction system rather than a finding about how autistic children's speech varies with symptom level. The interpretability that is present operates at the level of the pipeline. The three-way segmentation comparison establishes which stage contributes what, and the finding that automatic diarization outperforms manual annotation is given a mechanistic account grounded in the recording conditions. The dissociation between x-vectors' better segment accuracy and their worse severity estimate is reported without explanation, but it is reported, and it is the kind of intermediate-versus-final metric divergence that a paper optimising only for diarization performance would never have surfaced." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-093" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No dedicated future-work section, but both stated limitations imply directions and one is developed comparatively. Improving acquisition - the authors' contrast with a four-microphone beamforming setup indicates that better signal-to-noise ratio is the obvious lever, though it would trade against the deployability that a single-microphone setup buys. And relaxing the 20-second, 50-vocalisation minimum, which is framed as a constraint of the deep-learning architecture and which currently excludes the least verbal children. The paper also positions its result against two prior severity-estimation studies - one on Japanese-speaking adolescents using support vector regression and one on English-speaking children achieving a correlation of 0.634 in 33 children - and notes that it achieves 'a similar correlation value (0.606) while using a larger dataset of 132 children', which implicitly frames sample size as the axis along which the field should progress." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-093" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The representation choice is the study's most considered technical decision. Rather than reducing each recording to a single averaged feature vector - which is what most speech studies in this corpus do - the system builds a 100x48 matrix per child, where each row summarises 48 features over a randomly drawn run of 10 sequential vocalisations. This preserves within-session variability, which matters for a construct like prosodic atypicality that is expressed as inconsistency rather than as a shifted mean, and it supplies a two-dimensional input that a convolutional architecture can exploit. Because the matrix is the input and one prediction is produced per recording, the unit of analysis remains the child despite the internal resampling, so no leakage arises from the 100 correlated rows. The design also implicitly handles variable session length: a child with more vocalisations contributes more distinct draws, but every child yields the same fixed-size matrix." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-093" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with ASD', 'ASD children', 'verbal children with ASD', against 'non-ASD' and 'typically developed (TD)'. Person-first and acronym-adjectival usage alternate. The clinical description is conventional and largely accurate, and one passage is notably careful about heterogeneity: autistic children 'exhibit speech and expressive language abnormalities, which range from a total lack of speech (i.e., non-verbal children) to those who develop normal vocabulary and syntax, but exhibit difficulties with the use of appropriate prosody and pragmatics'. Speech is described as 'abnormal' and 'strange prosody' is quoted from prior work, so the register is deficit-oriented, but the paper is precise about the range rather than treating autistic speech as a single phenotype - and that precision matters here, because the sample is drawn from the less verbal end of that range, which the authors state explicitly when comparing their corpus against prior work on 'verbally fluent children'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-093" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The ADOS examination room at a national autism research centre, with recording conditions described honestly rather than idealised: a single microphone 1-2 metres from the child, in an environment with 'background noise, reverberation, and overlapping speech', and with both child and therapist free to move during the session. The resulting average signal-to-noise ratio of child speech is reported as 12 +/- 5 dB and named as a limitation. The authors position this deliberately against better-equipped prior work, noting that another group 'applied a diarization method for speech signals using four microphones, including a beamforming algorithm that improved their recordings' SNR' and that 'even though our proposed diarization methods achieved higher DER values, we used only one distant microphone recorded in a noisy environment'. Accepting worse acquisition in exchange for deployability in an ordinary clinic room is a defensible trade and is argued as such." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-093" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The reference standard is the ADOS-2 itself, administered by a clinician with ADOS-2 research reliability, and it functions here as a continuous outcome rather than a categorical label - raw scores on the 0-30 scale are the regression target. That is a defensible use of the instrument and is more demanding than binary classification, since it requires the model to track a graded clinical judgement rather than a dichotomy. Two caveats. The categorical diagnoses used to describe the groups - autistic versus language delay, developmental delay or typical development - are stated without describing who assigned them or under what criteria, so the group composition rests on unspecified clinical judgement even though the regression target does not. And using a single instrument as both the sole clinical characterisation and the prediction target means the system is validated against one clinician's scoring on one occasion, with no second rater, no test-retest interval and no independent confirmation of the score's stability." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-093" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "ADOS raw scores are reported for both groups and are the dependent variable, so severity is characterised more centrally here than anywhere else in the corpus. Nothing else is measured: no IQ, developmental quotient, language level, adaptive functioning or comorbidity data for either group. The omission is consequential for interpretation because the sample is explicitly a young and largely non-fluent one, so expressive language ability varies widely and is not quantified. Since the predictors are acoustic properties of speech and the outcome is a clinical score that itself weights communication heavily, an unmeasured language-ability variable sits between the two and could account for a substantial part of the reported correlation without any autism-specific acoustic signal being involved." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-093" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional, one recorded session per child, with no follow-up and no test-retest assessment of the severity estimate's stability. The absence of any ethics, consent or institutional review statement is the most conspicuous reporting gap: the study analyses audio recordings of clinical diagnostic assessments involving young children, their parents and clinicians, manually labelled by researchers, and no approval is mentioned in the extracted text. Funding from the Israel Ministry of Science and Technology is acknowledged with a grant number. No data availability statement and no code release." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-093" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Good within the constraints of a four-page conference paper, with one systematic deferral. Reported: microphone model, distance, sampling and downsampling rates, session duration, signal-to-noise ratio; the six manual labelling categories; both diarization feature sets with dimensionality and, for x-vectors, window size and rate; the shared network structure with its dual softmax and Viterbi post-processing; the vocalisation splitting including the treatment of segments over 3 seconds; the 100x48 matrix construction with its 10-vocalisation sampling rule; the optimiser and per-condition hyperparameters; the diarization error decomposition with its collar and the rationale for ignoring silence errors; the split construction with its four matched distributional moments and 50 repetitions; and the total detected duration under each segmentation condition. Deferred to a prior publication: the identity of the 48 features beyond four examples, the vocalisation separation method, and the CNN architecture details. Absent: ethics approval, data or code release, and the number of children excluded by the 20-second threshold." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-093" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: build an end-to-end system that locates child speech within a full ADOS recording and estimates autism severity from it, and determine whether automatic diarization can replace manual annotation. Main findings: MFCC-plus-pitch diarization achieved a 26.91% diarization error rate against x-vectors' 89.84% child-segment accuracy; severity estimation reached Pearson R 0.606 +/- 0.099 with NRMSE 0.248 with automatic diarization, against 0.567 +/- 0.120 and 0.261 with manual segmentation; and automatic diarization therefore matched or slightly exceeded manual annotation, which the authors attribute to imperfect manual labelling of quiet child speech in a reverberant room." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-093" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two limitations are stated, both technical and both substantive. The audio quality achieved with a single distant microphone in a reverberant room, quantified as 12 +/- 5 dB signal-to-noise ratio for child speech, is named and contextualised against prior work using four microphones with beamforming. And the requirement for a minimum of 20 seconds of child speech comprising at least 50 vocalisations is acknowledged as a constraint of the deep-learning approach. What is not acknowledged is the more consequential interpretive issue: the correlation of 0.606 is computed across a sample containing two well-separated clusters, with the autistic group averaging ADOS 14.6 and the comparison group 3.2, so a large part of the association reflects group membership rather than graded severity within either group. No within-autism correlation is reported, and without it the claim to estimate severity rather than to detect diagnosis is not established. Also unaddressed: the absence of any language or cognitive measure in a cohort defined by limited verbal fluency; the use of a single ADOS administration as both the only clinical characterisation and the prediction target; and the absence of an ethics statement." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-093" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Prosodic and acoustic properties of spontaneous child speech during clinical assessment, grounded in a specific prior literature that the introduction summarises accurately: autistic children have been reported to show 'strange prosody', 'increased pitch variability and pitch range', 'slower speech rate, and prolonged word production', with the observation that 'these studies used manual annotation and inspection of the audio recordings' - which is precisely the dependency the present work is designed to remove. The 48 features are named only in part (pitch, formants, zero-crossing rate, duration), so the operationalisation cannot be fully evaluated from this paper. The construct that the model actually targets, though, is not an acoustic property but a clinical score: the system predicts ADOS raw scores, which aggregate social affect and restricted repetitive behaviour ratings across a 45-minute observation, most of which concerns behaviour the microphone cannot capture. That the model recovers 37% of the variance in such a score from speech acoustics alone is the substantive finding, and it is also the reason the result is hard to interpret mechanistically." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-093" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "One ADOS-2 session per child, mean duration 41.96 +/- 11.51 minutes, yielding approximately 11 hours of child vocalisation across the cohort" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional regression study on consecutively recorded clinical assessments, with a within-study comparison of two diarization methods against manual annotation as baseline" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Hebrew-speaking young children, mostly not verbally fluent" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 105; comparison: 27" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "A convolutional network regressing ADOS raw scores from 48 prosodic and acoustic features of child speech, extracted from full-length clinical assessment recordings, achieved a correlation of 0.606 with clinician-assigned severity across 132 children and 50 repeated participant-level splits. The system's distinguishing property is that it estimates a continuous severity score rather than a binary ",
                        "Diarization: MFCC plus pitch achieved the lowest diarization error rate at 26.91%; x-vectors achieved recall 73.42%, precision 74.06% and accuracy 89.84% on child segments. Total detected child vocalisation duration was closely comparable across conditions (10.85 hours for manual labelling, 11.11 for MFCC plus pitch, 10.72 for x-vectors). Severity estimation: manual labelling baseline gave Pearson" ;
                    slr:outcome "For diarization: diarization error rate decomposed into substitution, false accept and missed detection components, plus recall, precision and accuracy on child segments, with silence classification errors excluded on the stated ground that silence frames are removed downstream anyway. For severity " ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Diarization: MFCC plus pitch achieved the lowest diarization error rate at 26.91%; x-vectors achieved recall 73.42%, precision 74.06% and accuracy 89.84% on child segments. Total detected child vocalisation duration was closely comparable across conditions (10.85 hours for manual labelling, 11.11 for MFCC plus pitch, 10.72 for x-vectors). Severity estimation: manual labelling baseline gave Pearson; A convolutional network regressing ADOS raw scores from 48 prosodic and acoustic features of child speech, extracted from full-length clinical assessment recordings, achieved a correlation of 0.606 with clinician-assigned severity across 132 children and 50 repeated participant-level splits. The system's distinguishing property is that it estimates a continuous severity score rather than a binary " ] ] ;
