<urn:slr:article:c6e9187abd76> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Differences in speech prosody are a widely observed feature of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD). However, it is unclear how prosodic differences in ASD manifest across different languages that demonstrate cross-linguistic variability in prosody. Using a supervised machine-learning analytic approach, we examined acoustic features relevant to rhythmic and intonational aspects of prosody derived from narrative samples elicited in English and Cantonese, two typologically and prosodically distinct languages. Our models revealed successful classification of ASD diagnosis using rhythm-relative features within and across both languages. Classification with intonation-relevant features was significant for English but not Cantonese. Results highlight differences in rhythm as a key prosodic feature impacted in ASD, and also demonstrate important variability in other prosodic properties that appear to be modulated by language-specific differences, such as intonation." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Lau JCY; Patel S; Kang X; Nayar K; Martin GE; Choy J; Wong PCM; Losh M (2022). Cross-linguistic patterns of speech prosodic differences in autism: A machine learning study. *PLOS ONE, 17(6)*." ;
    dcterms:creator "Choy J",
        "Kang X",
        "Lau JCY",
        "Losh M",
        "Martin GE",
        "Nayar K",
        "Patel S",
        "Wong PCM" ;
    dcterms:title "Cross-linguistic patterns of speech prosodic differences in autism: A machine learning study" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_ffa35101" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2022 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and recruitment adequately described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Recruitment routes and demographics are given, but recruited and analysed counts differ without explanation and the demographic table conflicts with the analysis text for one group." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is cognitive level comparable across the two language samples?" ;
                            slr:justification "Wechsler scales in English versus TONI-4 in Cantonese; both show a within-language gap favouring comparison participants." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the comparison group screened stringently?" ;
                            slr:justification "Personal and family history of autism and related genetic disorders such as fragile X excluded in both language groups." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the demographic imbalance addressed?" ;
                            slr:justification "A post-hoc analysis in supplementary material addresses age and gender for the English intonation classification, but the imbalance falls on the one f0 model that reached significance." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are comparison groups matched on the variables that matter?" ;
                            slr:justification "Cantonese groups are matched on age and sex; English groups differ by 42 percentage points in proportion male and by three years in mean age." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the features theoretically motivated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Envelope rhythmicity linked to 2-8 Hz cortical entrainment; f0 contours to the intonational phrase in intonational phonology." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the elicitation task appropriate for cross-linguistic comparison?" ;
                            slr:justification "A wordless picture book requiring no translation, with an established history in narrative and cross-linguistic autism research." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the recording setup described?" ;
                            slr:justification "No microphone, device, sampling rate, room or distance for either site - a substantive gap for envelope-based features collected at two sites." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the analytic unit justified linguistically?" ;
                            slr:justification "The utterance is chosen because the intonational phrase is argued to be universal across languages, unlike the syllable-level units used in the group's prior single-language work." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the utterance selection rule stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Twenty utterances per participant were selected, with no criterion given." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "ADOS-2 with research-reliable administration, but no ADI-R, DSM application or best-estimate procedure." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the unit of analysis the participant?" ;
                            slr:justification "Features concatenated across 20 utterances into one vector per participant." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is significance established empirically?" ;
                            slr:justification "Permutation testing with 5,001 iterations, which is what licenses the paper's central negative findings." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the model held constant across comparisons?" ;
                            slr:justification "The same linear SVM across all six configurations, so differences are attributable to features and language rather than to tuning." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the cross-language result checked against language as a confound?" ;
                            slr:justification "A supplementary analysis demonstrates systematic cross-linguistic differences in the rhythm features, but the check is reported outside the main text." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is dimensionality reduction nested within cross-validation?" ;
                            slr:justification "PCA and hyperparameter tuning both performed inside folds - the correct handling with 8,640 features and 66 participants." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is performance variability reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Median AUC with full distributions shown as boxplots, plus accuracy, sensitivity, specificity and confusion matrices." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the feature classes formally compared?" ;
                            slr:justification "The rhythm-versus-intonation contrast rests on one result being significant and the other not, with no test of the difference and no confidence intervals." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is terminology choice explained?" ;
                            slr:justification "The person-first choice is justified explicitly on grounds of parallelism across groups, with an acknowledgement that preferences vary - unique in this corpus." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Analytic scripts deposited alongside the dataset, one of very few studies in the corpus to do so." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the interpretation proportionate to the evidence?" ;
                            slr:justification "The phonological account of the Cantonese null is well supported; the ASPM allele speculation rests on population genetics with no genetic data collected." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Informed assent and consent from participants and guardians, institutional ethics approval at both sites, Declaration of Helsinki compliance." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Minimal dataset deposited on OSF with a working URL - appropriate handling for identifiable speech." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are competing interests disclosed?" ;
                            slr:justification "One author's ownership of a government-supported startup declared with a statement of non-association." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are limitations stated candidly?" ;
                            slr:justification "Age range, sex composition, language-ability matching and language coverage are raised; the unexplained attrition, the recording gap and the non-comparable IQ instruments are not." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three limitations are stated, each substantive. The wide age range across groups and the small number of females are raised together, with the authors arguing that robust classification despite these may indicate 'aspects of prosodic profiles of ASD which are relatively age and gender invariant', supported by the post-hoc analysis, while conceding that 'future studies with larger samples are needed to confirm whether findings reported here extend to both males and females and different age ranges where prosodic abilities may vary'. The absence of language ability measures for group matching is identified with a precise mechanism - baseline language ability covaries with age and autism severity and influences pitch in particular. And the restriction to two languages is named, with specific proposals for extension. What goes unaddressed: the unexplained loss of 40% of the English autistic sample between recruitment and analysis; the discrepancy between the demographic table and the analysis counts for the Cantonese autistic group; the absence of any recording specification; the use of different IQ instruments across language groups, which makes the two samples non-comparable on cognitive level; and the fact that the sex imbalance falls precisely on the English intonation model, the one f0 classification that reached significance." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-082" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No feature-level interpretability, and none is possible given principal components of 8,640-dimensional envelope representations - the paper reports no group means, no effect sizes and no directional statements. What it provides instead is interpretability at the level of theoretical construct, which is the level its question operates at. The rhythm-versus-intonation contrast is itself the interpretive result, and the authors connect it to the existing descriptive literature ('the ML algorithms identified in this study appeared to capture patterns from acoustic features that were representative of these prosodic characteristics, paralleling such descriptive observations by clinicians and researchers') and to prior objective measures of speech rate, lexical stress and cross-syllabic durational variability. The Cantonese intonation failure is then given a mechanistic account grounded in phonology - pitch carries lexical meaning in tone languages, so f0 is under phonological constraint - and used to reconcile conflicting prior findings across English, Thai and Japanese. That is a stronger form of interpretation than a feature-importance ranking would have provided, because it generates a testable prediction: intonational differences in autism should be attenuated in tone-language speakers generally. The one interpretive overreach is the ASPM allele speculation, which substitutes a population-genetic explanation for a linguistic one that the paper has already established." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-082" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Tabulated with within-language significance testing, and the picture differs sharply between the two language groups. Cantonese: autistic 19 male : 9 female, 17.83 +/- 9.24 years, IQ 108.54 +/- 10.92; comparison 17 male : 7 female, 18.88 +/- 8.70 years, IQ 115.08 +/- 10.13 - well matched on sex and age, with an IQ gap. English: autistic 29 male : 4 female, 15.96 +/- 7.38 years, IQ 104.13 +/- 14.34; comparison 15 male : 18 female, 19.31 +/- 5.35 years, IQ 111.03 +/- 13.79 - a 42-percentage-point sex imbalance in opposite directions, a three-year age gap, and an IQ gap. The authors identify this asymmetry themselves, referring to 'differences in gender and age observed between the English (but not Cantonese) groups', and run a post-hoc analysis in supplementary material to rule them out as confounds in the English f0 classification. That the confounds are concentrated in the English sample matters because the English intonation model is the only intonation classification that reached significance, and f0 is strongly sex-dependent: an autistic group that is 88% male compared against a group that is 55% female differs in mean pitch for reasons unrelated to diagnosis. Age ranges are wide in every group (6-35 years overall), spanning pre- and post-pubertal voice change. IQ was assessed with Wechsler scales in English and the TONI-4 in Cantonese, so cognitive level is not comparable across languages even though both language groups show a difference favouring comparison participants." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-082" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Narration of Frog, Where Are You?, a 24-page wordless picture book, presented one page at a time on a monitor with no examples given. The choice is the study's enabling methodological decision. Because the book contains no words, the identical stimulus can be administered in any language without translation, and it elicits connected spontaneous narrative rather than repetition, reading or single-word production - so the speech is naturalistic while the content is controlled across participants and across languages. The task has a long history in narrative discourse research in autism and in cross-linguistic work, which the authors cite. Twenty utterances per participant, segmented at natural pauses, were selected for analysis. The analytic level is deliberately the utterance rather than the syllable, on the grounds that 'the utterance is associated with the intonational phrase, the most fundamental unit of prosody argued to be universal across all languages' - the linguistic argument that makes English and Cantonese commensurable, since syllable-level units (final-syllable excursion in English, sentence-final particles in Cantonese) are language-specific and had been the focus of the group's prior work in each language separately." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-082" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "One feature vector per participant, formed by concatenating measures across the 20 selected utterances - so the unit of analysis is the participant and there is no possibility of the utterance-level leakage that would arise if each utterance were treated as an independent sample. Given 8,640 rhythm features against 66 English or 48 Cantonese participants, dimensionality reduction is unavoidable, and the handling is the correct one: principal component analysis is performed inside each cross-validation training fold rather than once on the full dataset, so the components are not informed by test-fold participants. The representational commitment that distinguishes the study is retaining trajectories rather than summaries - a full f0 contour instead of mean and range, an envelope spectrum instead of a speech rate. The cost is interpretability, since no directional statement about any acoustic quantity can be recovered from a principal component of an envelope spectrum, and the paper accordingly reports no group means or effect sizes for any feature. That is a defensible trade for a study whose question is about generalisation across languages rather than about characterising a difference." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-082" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Five directions, four of them well grounded. Larger samples covering both sexes and wider age ranges. Comprehensive language-ability assessment for group matching, with the mechanism stated. Extension to further typological classes, with named candidates chosen for their position relative to the two languages studied - German and Dutch as rhythmically similar to English, Japanese as distant from both. Neurophysiological investigation of prosody production across language groups, building on existing findings of increased right inferior frontal gyrus and right caudate activation during prosody comprehension in autism, with the observation that such findings 'would be particularly compelling if replicated in individuals with ASD who are speakers of languages other than English'. The fifth is a proposal to examine the ASPM gene as a marker for prosodic differences, on the basis that most Han Chinese people carry a T allele favouring linguistic pitch processing which 'may implicate a role of genetic factors in contributing to cross-linguistic differences of ASD phenotypes'. This last is speculation built on a population-genetic association applied to a null classification result, with no genetic data collected in the study, and it is the one place where the paper's interpretive discipline lapses - it offers an ancestry-based genetic explanation for a language-based finding when the language explanation the authors themselves give (that lexical tone constrains f0) is sufficient." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-082" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Two sites in different countries with different recruitment routes - a research setting in the United States, where participants came from an established family-genetic study of autism, and community recruitment in Hong Kong through social media, schools, autism organisations and employment programmes. The recording setting itself is described minimally: pages presented on a computer monitor with narration audio recorded, and no information about room, microphone, distance or recording equipment in either site. For a study whose primary features are the amplitude envelope and its spectral structure, the absence of any acoustic-recording specification is a genuine reproducibility gap, and it is compounded by the two-site design: if recording conditions differed systematically between the United States and Hong Kong, that difference would be absorbed into the envelope features. The combined-language model would be sensitive to this, though the fact that rhythm classification succeeds within each language separately - where recording conditions are presumably homogeneous - substantially mitigates the concern." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-082" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Median AUC across 5,001 iterations with permutation-derived p values, reported alongside accuracy, sensitivity and specificity for every one of the six configurations, with the full AUC distributions displayed as boxplots and aggregated confusion matrices provided. Reporting the median across iterations with its distribution rather than a single point estimate is the correct summary for a repeated cross-validation, and it makes the stability of each result visible. The permutation testing is what carries the paper's central negative claims - without it, a Cantonese intonation AUC of 0.620 and a combined intonation AUC of 0.559 might have been reported as weak but positive results, whereas the permutation p values of 0.507 and 0.509 establish them as indistinguishable from chance. The sensitivity and specificity breakdown adds a further observation the authors draw out: the significant English intonation model has good sensitivity (0.758) but poor specificity (0.606), so it tends to call typically developing participants autistic, while the rhythm models are balanced across classes. No confidence intervals accompany the AUCs, and no formal test compares one model against another - the rhythm-versus-intonation contrast rests on one being significant and the other not, rather than on a test of their difference." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-082" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Split sharply between the computational and the empirical. Fully specified or deposited: the two feature classes with their theoretical motivation, the counts (8,640 rhythm features, 400 intonation features), the utterance selection rule and count, the analytic level with its linguistic justification, the classifier and kernel with an argument for the choice, the nested structure of dimensionality reduction and hyperparameter tuning within cross-validation, the permutation testing with 5,001 iterations, and the analysis scripts on OSF. Not specified: any recording equipment or acoustic condition; the reason 22 English autistic and 6 English comparison participants were dropped between recruitment and analysis; how the 20 utterances per participant were selected from the full narrative; who performed transcription and segmentation, with what training or reliability; the number of principal components retained; and the SVM hyperparameter ranges searched. The supplementary file carries the post-hoc confound analysis, the technical feature-extraction detail and a supplementary demonstration that the rhythm features do contain systematic cross-linguistic differences - the last being an important internal check that the combined-language rhythm result is not simply detecting language." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-082" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2022. The framing is unusual for this corpus in being about scientific generalisation rather than about diagnostic deployment. The stated problem is not that clinicians are subjective or that diagnosis is slow, but that 'most studies have concentrated on studying prosody in English-speaking groups' while 'language is fundamentally cultural, and how specific aspects of prosody are used can vary substantially across languages' - so the field's evidence base may describe an English-specific phenotype without knowing it. The paper documents the concrete consequence: higher f0 and larger f0 range are reported repeatedly in English-speaking autistic groups, yet a Thai study found lower f0 range and a Japanese study likewise, and the existing cross-linguistic comparisons have all been between closely related languages (English and Danish, both Germanic; Cantonese and Mandarin, both Sinitic). The clinical implications drawn are correspondingly framed around intervention and cultural sensitivity rather than screening - that cross-linguistic differences 'may also help to optimize culturally sensitive diagnostic and intervention practices', and that if extensive pitch experience ameliorates intonational differences then pitch is 'a fruitful target for speech interventions in ASD for those who do not speak a tone language'. Diagnostic application is mentioned only in the conclusion and hedged: 'although further study with larger samples, and with additional language comparisons, is needed to support adaptation of the current models into clinical use'. The methodological argument against scalar summaries - that averaging or taking extreme values discards the dynamics that constitute prosody - is also a positional claim about how the field measures, and it is the study's most transferable methodological contribution." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-082" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Four groups across two languages, with recruitment and analysis counts that do not match. Recruited: 55 English-speaking autistic, 39 English-speaking comparison, 28 Cantonese-speaking autistic, 24 Cantonese-speaking comparison. Analysed: 33, 33, 24 and 24 respectively, stated in a parenthesis within the narrative-elicitation section - so 22 of 55 English autistic participants (40%) and 6 of 39 English comparison participants (15%) do not appear in the analysis, with no reason given, no flow diagram, and no comparison between retained and dropped participants. The demographic table reports 28 for the Cantonese autistic group where the analysis text says 24, a further inconsistency. The English participants come from a larger family-genetic study of autism that also enrolled parents and controls; the Cantonese participants were recruited via social media, schools and organisations serving autistic people, and employment programmes for adults previously diagnosed with Asperger syndrome or high-functioning autism. No other-diagnosis comparison group in either language." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-082" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "IQ was measured in every participant, but with different instruments by language group: the WASI, WAIS-III/IV or WISC-IV for English participants and the TONI-4 for Cantonese participants. The TONI-4 is a nonverbal reasoning measure while the Wechsler scales yield full-scale IQ incorporating verbal subtests, so the two sets of scores are not interchangeable and cross-language comparison of cognitive level is not possible. Within each language group the comparison participants score higher (111.03 versus 104.13 in English; 115.08 versus 108.54 in Cantonese). ADOS-2 calibrated severity scores are reported for both autistic groups (7.02 English, 6.00 Cantonese) with full ranges spanning 3-9.5 and 3-10, so both samples cover the severity spectrum. No language ability measure was administered, and the authors identify this as a limitation that matters specifically for their outcome: 'future studies should employ more comprehensive assessments of language abilities for group matching, since baseline language ability may covary with age and autism severity, influencing prosody and pitch in particular'. No adaptive functioning or comorbidity data." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-082" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The strongest aspect of the study, and stated in the terms that matter: 'Several procedures were performed in the classification pipeline to further avoid overfitting and optimistic bias due to limited sample size and the high-dimensionality of data'. Three properties follow. Repeated 10-fold cross-validation is used rather than a single split. Dimensionality reduction into principal components and hyperparameter tuning are both performed 'in a nested fashion' inside the cross-validation, so neither the components nor the hyperparameters are informed by held-out data - the single most common source of optimistic bias in high-dimensional small-sample classification, and one that Liao and colleagues elsewhere in this corpus violate directly by selecting features on the full sample before cross-validating. Significance is established by permutation rather than assumed from a nominal chance level, with 5,001 iterations, and the resulting p values are what license the paper's negative claims. The unit is the participant throughout. What remains: no independent replication cohort, though the two-language design provides a form of internal generalisation test that is arguably stronger for the question at hand; no confidence intervals on the AUCs; no formal test comparing feature classes; and the unexplained 40% attrition from the English autistic sample, which is a selection concern that no amount of validation discipline can address. The combined-language model's validity also rests on the supplementary demonstration that language differences in the rhythm features do not account for the classification, a check that is performed but reported outside the main text." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-082" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No keyword list appears in the extracted text. The operative vocabulary is cross-linguistic, prosody, rhythm, intonation, tone language, machine learning classification, and autism phenotype - a register that places the work in linguistics and communication science with machine learning as instrument, which matches its actual contribution." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-082" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: identify prosodic differences in autism that span typologically distinct languages and those shaped by language-specific properties, by comparing rhythm-relevant and intonation-relevant acoustic feature classes within English, within Cantonese and across both combined. Main findings: rhythm classified successfully in English (median AUC 0.900), Cantonese (0.962) and combined (0.886), all p < 0.001; intonation classified significantly only in English (0.695, p = 0.007), failing in Cantonese (0.620, p = 0.507) and falling near chance when combined (0.559, p = 0.509). The dissociation is attributed to the phonological role of pitch in tone languages and is used to reconcile the conflicting f0 literature, where higher f0 and wider range are reported in English but lower f0 range in Thai and Japanese." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-082" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional, one narration per participant, no follow-up. Ethics reporting is adequate but generic: informed assent and consent from all participants and guardians as applicable, approval by each institution's ethics committee, and conformity with the Declaration of Helsinki, without naming the committees or protocol numbers. Data sharing is a genuine strength and rare in this corpus - 'The minimal dataset and analytic scripts are available from the OSF database' with a working URL, so the analysis is independently verifiable even though raw audio is not shared, which is the appropriate handling of identifiable speech. A competing interest is declared: one author owns a startup company supported by a Hong Kong government technology scheme, with a statement that the reported research is not associated with it." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-082" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'individuals with autism spectrum disorder (ASD)' and 'individuals with typical development (TD)' throughout, and uniquely in this corpus the choice is explained rather than defaulted: 'We acknowledge the variability in preferences related to person-first and identity-first terminology (i.e., individuals with ASD versus autistic individuals). We do not endorse one style over the other. For the purpose of this manuscript, to ensure that language is parallel when referencing both participant groups, we remain consistent with the following terminology.' The stated reason - parallelism across the two groups so that neither is described in a grammatically different form from the other - is a considered linguistic argument rather than a convention, and no other paper in the corpus addresses the question at all. Substantively, prosodic variation is framed as 'differences' rather than deficits throughout, including in the title, though the paper does report that such differences 'can contribute to an impression of oddness reported by listeners' when reviewing prior work." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-082" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Prosody decomposed into its two canonical components and operationalised as multivariate structure rather than summary statistics. Rhythm is captured through the amplitude envelope - its spectrum, its intrinsic mode functions and its temporal modulation spectrum - with a neurobiological rationale that envelope rhythmicity in the 2-8 Hz band 'is crucial in the neural processing of intelligible speech as it aligns with brain areas in the spoken language processing pathway that oscillate at a similar rate'. Intonation is captured as the full time-normalised f0 contour across each utterance, on the argument that the contour 'encompasses the dynamicity of speech intonation which varies across time within the most fundamental utterance-sized linguistic unit known as the intonational phrase'. The theoretical framing distinguishes affective prosody, which expresses emotion and intent and is broadly recognisable across cultures with possible evolutionary homologies in other mammals, from linguistic prosody, which encodes lexical and grammatical structure and varies drastically across languages - and this distinction predicts the study's result, since rhythm carries more affective and less linguistic load than pitch does in a tone language." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-082" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Not multimodal, but the study performs the corpus's most informative feature-class comparison, which serves an analogous function. Two feature classes representing the two components of prosody are tested on identical participants and identical utterances, within each language and combined, giving six models whose pattern is interpretable as a whole. The result is a dissociation rather than a fusion gain: rhythm classifies in both languages and survives pooling, intonation classifies in one language and collapses on pooling. Crucially the authors do not attempt to combine the two feature classes into a single model - which would have been the obvious accuracy-maximising move and would have obscured the dissociation. That restraint is what makes the study informative: the purpose is to determine which prosodic dimension is cross-linguistically robust, and combining them would have answered a different and less useful question. A supplementary analysis reported in the paper demonstrates that the rhythm features do contain systematic cross-linguistic differences, which is the necessary check that the successful combined-language model is separating diagnosis rather than exploiting language as a proxy - though with the two languages recruited at different sites through different routes, that check carries real weight and would ideally have appeared in the main text." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-082" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "PLOS ONE 17(6):e0269637 (2022), open access. Authorship spans the Roxelyn and Richard Pepper Department of Communication Sciences and Disorders at Northwestern University, the Department of Linguistics and Modern Languages and Brain and Mind Institute at the Chinese University of Hong Kong, Chongqing University, and St. John's University - a genuinely bi-national collaboration, which is what the design requires and which is unique in this corpus. Funding comes from NIH grants in the United States and the Health and Medical Research Fund and two private foundations in Hong Kong. The communication-sciences provenance is visible throughout: the theoretical framing of prosody, the choice of a standard narrative elicitation task, the distinction between affective and linguistic prosody, and the interpretation of results in phonological terms all reflect a speech-language rather than an engineering orientation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-082" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A single linear-kernel SVM applied consistently across all six configurations, with the kernel choice argued from the structure of the problem rather than selected empirically: linear kernels are 'preferable to non-linear kernels because theoretically it is always possible to find a linear decision boundary that separates data, in spite of high data dimensionality and small sample size', with precedent cited in neuroimaging and gene expression, where features routinely outnumber samples. Holding the model fixed across all six configurations is the right design for a study whose question is about features rather than about learners - any performance difference between rhythm and intonation, or between English and Cantonese, is attributable to the data rather than to differential model tuning. No alternative classifier is compared, which is appropriate given that no classification-performance claim is being made against competing methods." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-082" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Essentially nothing on the acquisition side - no microphone, recording device, sampling rate, room or distance is specified for either site, which for a study built on amplitude-envelope spectra is the most consequential reporting omission. Analysis tools are named: the MATLAB Audio Toolbox together with scripts from four prior published studies for the envelope spectrum, intrinsic mode function and temporal modulation spectrum extraction, with further technical detail relegated to supplementary material. The intonation pipeline extracts time-normalised f0 contours at 20 points per utterance. Principal component analysis and a linear-kernel SVM complete the chain. The reliance on published scripts rather than bespoke implementations, together with the OSF deposit of the analysis code, makes the computational half of the pipeline substantially more reproducible than the acquisition half." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-082" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "ADOS-2 with research-reliable administration and scoring, using Overall, Social Affect and Restricted and Repetitive Behavior calibrated severity scores. This is a real observational instrument correctly administered, but it is the only diagnostic evidence described - there is no ADI-R, no DSM criterion application, no clinician best-estimate procedure and no statement of who made the final determination. For a subset of the Cantonese sample recruited through 'employment programmes particularly designed for adults previously diagnosed with Asperger syndrome or high-functioning Autism', the original diagnosis was made elsewhere under unknown procedures and the ADOS-2 serves as confirmation. The comparison groups are handled more stringently than in most studies here, screened for personal or family history of autism or related genetic disorders such as fragile X - a criterion that follows from the English cohort's family-genetic origins and that excludes the broader autism phenotype in relatives. General exclusions of brain injury, major psychiatric disorder, known genetic syndrome and other neurodevelopmental disorders were applied to all participants." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-082" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "One narration per participant of a 24-page wordless picture book, from which 20 utterances were selected for analysis" ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-linguistic case-control classification with within-language and combined-language models, comparing two feature classes on the same participants" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "English-speaking and Cantonese-speaking autistic individuals with IQ in the average range" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 55 recruited in English and 28 in Cantonese; 33 English and 24 Cantonese analysed; comparison: 39 recruited in English and 24 in Cantonese; 33 English and 24 Cantonese analysed" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "Model 1, within language: English rhythm AUC 0.900 (accuracy 0.819, sensitivity 0.788, specificity 0.849), p < 0.001; Cantonese rhythm AUC 0.962 (accuracy 0.880, sensitivity 0.917, specificity 0.833), p < 0.001; English intonation AUC 0.695 (accuracy 0.683, sensitivity 0.758, specificity 0.606), p = 0.007; Cantonese intonation AUC 0.620 (accuracy 0.605), p = 0.507, not significant. Model 2, langua",
                        "Rhythm-relevant acoustic features classified autism at AUC 0.900 in English and 0.962 in Cantonese, and continued to classify at 0.886 when the two languages were pooled into a single model despite their typological distance - English being a stress-timed Indo-European non-tone language and Cantonese a syllable-timed Sino-Tibetan tone language. Intonation features behaved entirely differently: the" ;
                    slr:outcome "Median AUC across 5,001 iterations with permutation-derived p values, accuracy, sensitivity and specificity for all six model configurations, boxplots of the AUC distributions, and aggregated confusion matrices" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "Model 1, within language: English rhythm AUC 0.900 (accuracy 0.819, sensitivity 0.788, specificity 0.849), p < 0.001; Cantonese rhythm AUC 0.962 (accuracy 0.880, sensitivity 0.917, specificity 0.833), p < 0.001; English intonation AUC 0.695 (accuracy 0.683, sensitivity 0.758, specificity 0.606), p = 0.007; Cantonese intonation AUC 0.620 (accuracy 0.605), p = 0.507, not significant. Model 2, langua; Rhythm-relevant acoustic features classified autism at AUC 0.900 in English and 0.962 in Cantonese, and continued to classify at 0.886 when the two languages were pooled into a single model despite their typological distance - English being a stress-timed Indo-European non-tone language and Cantonese a syllable-timed Sino-Tibetan tone language. Intonation features behaved entirely differently: the" ] ] ;
