            slr:overall_judgment "low" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-022-00598-6> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "ARTICLE OPEN Evaluation of an artiﬁcial intelligence-based medical device for diagnosis of autism spectrum disorder Jonathan T. Megerian1,2, Sangeeta Dey3,4, Raun D. Melmed5, Daniel L. Coury6,7, Marc Lerner1,2, Christopher J. Nicholls8,9, Kristin Sohl 10, Rambod Rouhbakhsh11,12, Anandhi Narasimhan13, Jonathan Romain1,2, Sailaja Golla14, Saﬁullah Shareef15, Andrey Ostrovsky 16,17, Jennifer Shannon18, Colleen Kraft18, Stuart Liu-Mayo18, Halim Abbas 18, Diana E. Gal-Szabo18, Dennis P. Wall18,19 and Sharief Taraman 1,2,18,20✉ Autism spectrum disorder (ASD) can be reliably diagnosed at 18 months, yet signiﬁcant diagnostic delays persist in the United States. This double-blinded, multi-site, prospective, active comparator cohort study tested the accuracy of an artiﬁcial intelligence- based Software as a Medical Device designed to aid primary care healthcare providers (HCPs) in diagnosing ASD. The Device combines behavioral features from three distinct inputs (a caregiver questionnaire, analysis of two short home videos, and an HCP questionnaire) in a gradient boosted decision tree machine learning algorithm to produce either an ASD positive, ASD negative, or indeterminate output. This study compared Device outputs to diagnostic agreement by two or more independent specialists in a cohort of 18–72-month-olds with developmental delay concerns (425 study completers, 36% female, 29% ASD prevalence). Device output PPV for all study completers was 80.8% (95% conﬁdence intervals (CI), 70.3%–88.8%) and NPV was 98.3% (90.6%–100%). For the 31.8% of participants who received a determinate output (ASD positive or negative) Device sensitivity was 98.4% (91.6%–100%) and speciﬁcity was 78.9% (67.6%–87.7%). The Device’s indeterminate output acts as a risk control measure when inputs are insufﬁciently granular to make a determinate recommendation with conﬁdence. If this risk control measure were removed, the sensitivity for all study completers would fall to 51.6% (63/122) (95% CI 42.4%, 60.8%), and speciﬁcity would fall to 18.5% (56/303) (95% CI 14.3%, 23.3%). Among participants for whom the Device abstained from providing a result, specialists identiﬁed that 91% had one or more complex neurodevelopmental disorders. No signiﬁcant differences in Device performance were found across participants’ sex, race/ethnicity, income, or education level. For nearly a third of this primary care sample, the Device enabled timely diagnostic evaluation with a high degree of accuracy. The Device shows promise to signiﬁcantly increase the number of children able to be diagnosed with ASD in a primary care setting, potentially facilitating earlier intervention and more efﬁcient use of specialist resources. npj Digital Medicine (2022) 5:57 ; https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-022-00598-6" ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Jonathan T. Megerian (2022). Evaluation of an artificial intelligence-based medical device for diagnosis of autism spectrum disorder. *npj Digital Medicine*. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-022-00598-6" ;
    dcterms:creator "Jonathan T. Megerian" ;
    dcterms:title "Evaluation of an artificial intelligence-based medical device for diagnosis of autism spectrum disorder" ;
    bibo:doi "10.1038/s41746-022-00598-6" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_361b59fb" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2022 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are participants and attrition fully described?" ;
                            slr:justification "Flow from 711 to 425 with reasons, COVID-19 impact quantified, and completers compared against all enrolled on every demographic variable." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is cognitive level characterised?" ;
                            slr:justification "No IQ testing, with the reason given as most participants being below the age of reliable non-verbal assessment." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the sampling frame the intended use population?" ;
                            slr:justification "Consecutive referrals with developmental concerns from primary care across 14 sites, at 28.7% autism prevalence." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the clinicians representative of the intended users?" ;
                            slr:justification "Fifteen general pediatrics or family medicine physicians with 1-38 years experience, not selected for autism interest or expertise." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the comparison group clinically appropriate?" ;
                            slr:justification "61.9% had a non-autism developmental or behavioural condition and only 9.4% were neurotypical - the discrimination that matters in practice." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the index test fixed before evaluation?" ;
                            slr:justification "Model and abstention thresholds locked on prior training and testing data, with FDA-agreed PPV and NPV floors." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the behavioural input automated?" ;
                            slr:justification "The video channel is scored by trained human analysts; the authors identify reducing this dependence as a scalability priority." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the raters of the video input qualified and standardised?" ;
                            slr:justification "Master's degree minimum with five years autism experience, standardised training and certification against performance guarantees on unseen videos." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the inputs blinded from one another and from the output?" ;
                            slr:justification "Caregivers, video analysts and clinicians were blinded to each other's input and to the Device output." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the reference standard appropriate and independently applied?" ;
                            slr:justification "Specialist DSM-5 diagnosis with structured observation and standardised instruments, independently confirmed by a blinded reviewer with third-specialist adjudication." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is reference-standard reliability quantified?" ;
                            slr:justification "79% first-reviewer agreement, 21% requiring a third opinion, and specialist certainty ratings reported by diagnostic outcome." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the error composition characterised?" ;
                            slr:justification "Three-by-three confusion matrix showing all 15 false positives came from the other-condition group and none from neurotypical children." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is per-input contribution reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "No ablation across the three channels in this cohort; the fusion benefit is cited from prior publications." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is uncertainty quantified?" ;
                            slr:justification "Clopper-Pearson exact 95% intervals on every estimate including all demographic strata." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are predictive values reported at the deployment prevalence?" ;
                            slr:justification "28.7% prevalence in the intended referral population, so PPV and NPV are directly interpretable without prevalence correction." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are endpoints pre-specified?" ;
                            slr:justification "PPV and NPV as primary endpoints with FDA-agreed thresholds, under prospective ClinicalTrials.gov registration." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the counterfactual performance without abstention reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Sensitivity 51.6% and specificity 18.5% across all completers if the indeterminate output were removed." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are subgroup analyses adequately powered?" ;
                            slr:justification "Acknowledged; differences assessed by interval overlap, with female PPV 60% against male 88% dismissed on that basis." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are equity and access issues addressed?" ;
                            slr:justification "English-only operation, smartphone requirement, cost and reimbursement uncertainty, and Medicaid sustainability all raised explicitly." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are the limits of scope stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "'The goal of the Device is not to enable diagnosis of all presentations of ASD' - only the subset it can determine confidently." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the dataset accessible or its access conditions stated?" ;
                            slr:justification "Withheld on patient-sensitivity grounds but offered to qualified researchers under a data-sharing agreement." ;
                            slr:rating "Partly" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is code available?" ;
                            slr:justification "Explicitly proprietary and not publicly available." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is the trial prospectively registered?" ;
                            slr:justification "NCT04151290 before initiation, with the full protocol publicly available." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are ethics and consent reported?" ;
                            slr:justification "Centralised IRB with named body, protocol number and approval date, and informed consent from all caregivers." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Is longitudinal stability established?" ;
                            slr:justification "Identified as the subject of a planned registrational study; NPV is currently cross-sectional rather than prognostic." ;
                            slr:rating "No" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Are commercial interests transparent?" ;
                            slr:justification "Seven authors affiliated with the manufacturer and the corresponding author reachable at a company address." ;
                            slr:rating "Yes" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "some concerns" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Substantial and candid, with the central limitation stated as a headline result rather than buried. The abstention rate is not framed as a caveat but as a design feature whose cost is quantified: the Device declined to classify 68.2% of children, and the paper states plainly what would happen otherwise - sensitivity 51.6%, specificity 18.5%. The stated scope follows: 'the goal of the Device is not to enable diagnosis of all presentations of ASD, but to aid primary care HCPs to diagnose the subset of children the Device can confidently determine a recommendation for'. Other limitations acknowledged: not powered for covariate inference; no IQ testing, with the reason and the need for follow-up stated; English only, with non-English families excluded and translations in development; parental education higher and income lower than the general population; smartphone access required; cost and reimbursement data absent, with explicit concern about equitable access for low-income and uninsured families; reliance on human video analysts limiting scalability; and the expectation that abstention rates will vary with the population's composition, being lower in more neurotypical or higher-prevalence populations. What receives insufficient weight: the sex difference in PPV, dismissed on interval overlap in an admittedly underpowered analysis; and the fact that the reference standard's own 21% disagreement rate bounds the achievable ceiling, which is reported but not carried into the interpretation of the results." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-086" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Prospective and prospectively registered (NCT04151290) before initiation, conducted August 2019 to June 2020, with each child assessed once by the Device and once by specialists. Ethics reporting is complete and specific: centralised IRB review by IntegReview under protocol number Q170886, approved 19 July 2019, with the subsequent acquisition by Advarra noted, and informed consent from all caregivers. The COVID-19 disruption is disclosed in detail rather than smoothed over - the date of the national emergency, the number of sites closed (9 of 14), the reduced availability of those remaining, the estimate that 100 specialist visits could not be completed, the 97 that were completed afterwards, and the estimated drop-out rate absent the pandemic (26.2%). Data are withheld from public release on patient-sensitivity grounds but offered to qualified researchers under a data-sharing agreement, with the full protocol public on ClinicalTrials.gov. The code is proprietary and not available." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-086" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The validation design is the study's principal contribution to this review and differs in kind from everything else charted here. Rather than resampling within a dataset, the model was locked with thresholds fixed on prior training and testing data, and then evaluated prospectively on consecutively enrolled children whose diagnoses were established afterwards by an independent blinded specialist consensus. All three Device inputs were blinded from one another and from the output; the specialists were blinded to the Device. The trial was registered before initiation, so the endpoints - PPV and NPV with FDA-agreed floors - were pre-specified rather than selected post hoc. Participant flow is fully documented with attrition causes including the COVID-19 disruption quantified. The two features that make this the corpus's benchmark are the population and the disclosure. The population is the referral cohort the tool is meant for, with 28.7% prevalence and only 9.4% neurotypical, so predictive values are directly deployable rather than requiring prevalence correction - the calculation Kong and colleagues perform hypothetically is here unnecessary because the design supplies it directly. The disclosure is the counterfactual: sensitivity 51.6% and specificity 18.5% without abstention, against 98.4% and 78.9% with it. No other paper in this corpus reports what its performance would be under a less favourable operating decision. What remains open is the temporal question the authors themselves identify - whether children given indeterminate or negative outputs at 18-72 months go on to receive autism diagnoses later - which the planned registrational study is designed to address and without which the NPV of 98.3% is a cross-sectional rather than a prognostic figure." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-086" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Behaviour is captured through three complementary observational vantage points rather than a single measurement channel, and the design rationale is explicitly clinical rather than technical. Caregiver report captures behaviour across settings and time; home video captures 'rich insight into the child's natural behavior outside of the clinic setting'; and the clinician questionnaire captures structured professional observation within a visit. The constructs probed are enumerated in full and are recognisably the constructs of a clinical autism evaluation - joint attention, integration of communication forms, reciprocal communication, quality of social responses and overtures, directed and responsive smiling, eye contact appropriateness, pretend and creative play alone and with others, imitation, shared interests, offering comfort, repetitive mannerisms, sensory interests, unusual interests - rather than proxy signals such as gaze coordinates or acoustic features. This is the one study in the corpus where the computational input is the same behavioural vocabulary a clinician would use, which is why its outputs can be interpreted directly and why its false positives can be characterised as children with other conditions that would benefit from similar intervention." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-086" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The strongest reference standard in the corpus, and the one against which others should be judged. Diagnosis was made by board-certified child and adolescent psychiatrists, child neurologists, developmental-behavioural pediatricians or child psychologists with more than five years of autism diagnostic experience, using structured clinical observation (comprising conversation or free play, symbolic interactive play and sensory stimulation), clinician interview and examination, medical and developmental review, and standardised assessment instruments, to reach a DSM-5 determination. That determination was then independently reviewed by a blinded second specialist working from standardised medical history, physical examination findings and video of the initial assessment, with a third specialist adjudicating disagreements and the majority decision defining the standard. The stated purpose is to reduce 'confirmation bias, deindividuation, and homogenized group thinking'. Crucially, the paper also quantifies the reference standard's own reliability rather than treating it as ground truth: the first reviewer agreed with the diagnosing specialist 79% of the time; of the 21% requiring a third opinion, the third sided with the diagnosing specialist 43% of the time and the first reviewer 57%; and diagnosing specialists reported being 'completely certain' only 67% of the time when diagnosing autism, 74% when ruling it out in favour of another condition, and 95% when the child was neurotypical. Reporting the imperfection of one's own gold standard is rare and consequential: it places a ceiling on what any device validated against it can be shown to achieve." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-086" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Predictive values were the pre-specified primary endpoints, and the reason given is exactly right for a diagnostic aid: 'the selection of predictive values as primary endpoints reflects the utility of the Device in a broad spectrum of subjects, as these values measure how often in the study population the Device correctly identifies a patient with ASD and how frequently it correctly determines that a patient does not have ASD'. Because the cohort's 28.7% prevalence is that of the referral population the tool would actually serve, these predictive values are directly interpretable in deployment - which is precisely what the accuracy figures reported throughout the rest of this corpus are not. Clopper-Pearson exact intervals accompany every estimate. The confusion matrix is three-by-three against a three-level reference standard rather than two-by-two, so the composition of the errors is visible: all 15 false positives came from the other-condition column and none from the neurotypical column. Performance is stratified across five demographic dimensions with intervals throughout, severity and comorbidity are cross-tabulated against output, and reference-standard reliability and specialist certainty are reported alongside. The one substantive gap is the absence of per-input performance, which would show how much each channel contributes." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-086" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "One consecutively enrolled cohort rather than contrasted groups, with the three-level reference standard producing the comparison structure. Of 711 enrolled, 425 completed both the Device inputs and the specialist evaluation; specialists classified 122 (28.7%) as autism-positive, 263 (61.9%) as autism-negative with at least one other developmental or behavioural condition, and 40 (9.4%) as autism-negative and neurotypical. That composition is the study's decisive structural advantage over every other study in this corpus: the comparison against which the Device is judged is overwhelmingly children with other developmental concerns rather than typically developing children, which is the discrimination a primary care tool actually has to make. Attrition is documented in a flow diagram and its causes disclosed - 126 dropped out before completing Device inputs and a further 160 after completing inputs but before the specialist evaluation, with the COVID-19 national emergency in March 2020 closing 9 of 14 sites, preventing an estimated 100 specialist visits, and the authors estimating the drop-out rate absent COVID at 26.2%. Completers and non-completers are shown to be demographically similar in a side-by-side table." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-086" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Goal: test whether an AI-based diagnostic aid can allow primary care clinicians to diagnose autism directly, reducing referral load and diagnostic delay. Main findings: for the 31.8% of children on which the Device rendered a verdict, PPV was 80.8% and NPV 98.3%, with sensitivity 98.4% and specificity 78.9%; if abstention were removed, sensitivity across all completers would fall to 51.6% and specificity to 18.5%; 91% of the children the Device declined to classify had at least one neurodevelopmental disorder; all 15 false positives had a non-autism developmental condition and none were neurotypical, with one specialist judging a third of them autistic; there was a single false negative; the mean age at which an autism-positive output was rendered was 2.81 years against a national average diagnosis age above 4; and no performance differences were detected across sex, race and ethnicity, income or education, though the study was not powered to test them." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-086" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No feature-level interpretability, and the algorithm and code are proprietary - so at the level of mechanism the Device is opaque. What the paper offers instead is case-level interpretability, and it is more clinically useful than a feature ranking would be. The indeterminate group is characterised diagnostically: 91.0% had at least one neurodevelopmental disorder, 71.0% were autism-negative with another condition, 20.0% were autism-positive and 9.0% had no developmental diagnosis - so the abstentions concentrate on the genuinely ambiguous rather than falling randomly. Output is cross-tabulated against DSM-5 severity, showing a clear gradient from 44 of 65 Level 3 children classified positive to 1 of 5 Level 1 children. Output is cross-tabulated against every comorbidity, showing for example that phonological disorder yields abstention 82.4% of the time. And specialist certainty is compared between cases the Device classified and cases it declined - 71% versus 64% completely certain, p = 0.40 - testing whether the Device abstains where clinicians are also uncertain, and finding no significant difference. Taken together these establish what the Device is doing at the level a clinician needs, even though they say nothing about how." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-086" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Extensive on comorbidity and severity, absent on cognition. Specialists recorded all comorbid conditions, tabulated across fifteen diagnostic categories with frequencies, and then cross-tabulated against Device output - showing for instance that children with phonological disorder were classified indeterminate 82.4% of the time while those with intellectual disability received a positive output 66.7% of the time. DSM-5 support-level severity was recorded separately for social communication and restricted repetitive behaviour domains in every autism-positive child, using the maximum where site and reviewing specialists differed, and broken down by output: of 121 with scores, 5 were Level 1, 51 Level 2 and 65 Level 3, with the Device classifying 44 of 65 Level 3 children positive against 1 of 5 Level 1 children. That gradient is the clearest evidence in the paper of what the abstention mechanism is doing - declining on milder and more ambiguous presentations. No IQ or developmental quotient was obtained, and the reason given is sound: 'a large portion of participants were below the age threshold where non-verbal cognitive abilities can be reliably tested with traditional measures of intelligence'. The authors nonetheless flag that 'appropriately powered follow-up studies are needed to confirm Device performance across a range of intellectual levels'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-086" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Reported for the whole cohort in unusual demographic depth and used analytically rather than descriptively. Completers were 36.2% female, distributed across five age bands from 18 months to over 5 years, and characterised by race and ethnicity (53.9% non-Hispanic white only, 13.2% Black only, 11.5% Hispanic or Latino only, 13.4% multiple, 4.2% Asian only), parental education across six levels, and parental income across six brackets. Performance is then broken down across every one of these strata with Clopper-Pearson intervals, which no other study in this corpus attempts. The authors are appropriately careful about what this shows: the study 'was not powered for statistical inference on covariates' and differences were assessed 'by examining the overlap of corresponding 95% CIs'. Two age effects did reach significance - a higher determinate rate under 3 (39% versus 26%, p = 0.006) and higher specificity at 3 and over (88% versus 67%, p = 0.03). The sex comparison warrants more caution than the paper gives it: PPV was 60% (12/20) for girls against 88% (51/58) for boys, a 28-point gap resting on 20 determinate positive girls, and reading overlapping intervals as evidence of no difference in an underpowered analysis is the weakest inference in the paper - particularly since diagnostic disparity by sex is one of its stated motivations. The cohort had higher parental education and lower household income than the general US population, both acknowledged, with the income difference attributed plausibly to 'reduced parental participation in the workforce as a result of participants' developmental delays'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-086" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No keyword list appears in the extracted text. The operative vocabulary is Software as a Medical Device, diagnostic aid, active comparator, predictive value, indeterminate output, abstention, risk control and primary care - a regulatory and health-services register rather than a machine-learning one, which accurately reflects a paper whose contribution is a trial rather than an algorithm." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-086" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A gradient boosted decision tree, chosen for a regulated diagnostic aid over deep architectures, and locked before the trial. The model's provenance is described across seven years of prior development: initially trained 'using patient record data from thousands of children with diverse conditions, presentations, and comorbidities' with labels from standardised diagnostic tools, then 'iteratively improved, supplemented with ASD-expert input, and prospectively validated for 7 years prior to this study'. The abstention mechanism is the modelling contribution and is handled with unusual explicitness: thresholds were tuned by cross-validation on training and testing data to hold PPV above 65% and NPV above 85% - floors agreed with the FDA in advance - while minimising the abstention rate. The justification for abstaining rather than always deciding is stated in three parts: it 'provides a safeguard against known machine-learning failure modes, fosters clinician trust through model transparency, and helps flag cases where additional human expertise or data may be needed'. That framing - abstention as a transparency and triage mechanism rather than a performance trick - is the right one, and the paper earns it by quantifying the alternative." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-086" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Fourteen sites across six US states, recruiting children 'representing a population of patients that HCPs would see in their primary care practice' - so the sampling frame is the referral population the tool is intended for, not a research cohort. The participating clinicians are equally representative: 15 physicians trained in general pediatrics or family medicine, spanning 1 to 38 years of post-residency experience, and explicitly 'not recruited on the basis of any special interest or experience of ASD diagnosis, beyond what was delivered as part of their standard training'. That last detail matters more than it might appear: a diagnostic aid evaluated only with enthusiastic autism-interested clinicians would not establish that ordinary primary care physicians can use it. Thirteen point nine percent of clinician assessments were conducted by telemedicine rather than in person, with 'no evidence of performance degradation' when done remotely - an incidental finding produced by the COVID-19 disruption that speaks directly to rural and low-access deployment." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-086" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Reporting is at regulatory-trial standard and exceeds every other study in the corpus. Fully specified: trial registration number and protocol availability; IRB name, protocol number and approval date; site count and states; recruitment population; clinician qualifications and experience distribution; participant flow with reasons at each stage and COVID-19 impact quantified; baseline demographics for both completers and all enrolled across age, sex, race and ethnicity, education and income; the complete list of behavioural constructs probed by the questionnaires; item counts per input per age band; video duration requirements and caregiver guidance; video analyst qualification, training and certification requirements; the reference standard procedure including the blinding and adjudication structure; specialist qualifications; the certainty rating scale; median completion times for two of three inputs with the third's estimation method disclosed; the confusion matrix against a three-level standard; performance across every demographic stratum with intervals; severity and comorbidity breakdowns by output; the statistical software, packages and interval method; and the abstention threshold-setting procedure including the FDA-agreed PPV and NPV floors. Not available: the algorithm itself, the feature weights, and the code, all proprietary; and the specific standardised instruments the specialists used are described generically rather than named." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-086" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three input vectors combined into a single mathematical representation, with 64 questionnaire items per child spread across the channels. The representation is entirely in the vocabulary of clinical behavioural observation rather than in derived signal features, which is what allows the outputs to be audited clinically - the paper can report which comorbidities and which severity levels are associated with each output because the inputs are commensurable with the reference standard's own constructs. The age-banded versions are a considered detail: item content differs slightly between 18-47 and 48-71 months 'in order to best capture the ASD phenotype among children based on developmental trajectories', with the classifier trained to perform equivalently across both, and the trial then reports performance separately by age band and finds a genuine difference in determinate rate and specificity. That is representation design responding to a developmental fact, and it is validated rather than assumed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-086" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "2022. The framing is a health-systems argument rather than a technical or clinical-deficit one, and it is the most concretely evidenced in the corpus. The problem is specified with figures at each link in the chain: US prevalence 1.9%; mean age of diagnosis over 4 years despite reliable diagnosis being possible at 18 months; an estimated three-year delay between first caregiver concern and diagnosis, longer for children who are non-white, female, of lower socioeconomic status or rural; 27% of autistic children still undiagnosed at age 8; waits of up to 18 months between primary care screening and specialist diagnosis; only about 1% of autistic patients diagnosed by primary care clinicians; roughly 60% of children who fail a screen neither referred nor diagnosed; and 61% of 454 children with an eventual autism diagnosis having screened negative on a common screener in a cohort of over 20,000. The barriers to primary care diagnosis are then named specifically - low confidence with diagnostic tools, lack of specialist training, insufficient time to administer or to review results, and inadequate reimbursement - as is the observation that existing instruments risk overdiagnosis 'in populations with a lower prevalence of ASD, as is the case in primary care' and lose reliability when combination instruments are used stand-alone by time-pressured clinicians. The proposed role follows from this analysis and is correspondingly bounded: not to replace specialist assessment but to let primary care resolve the straightforward cases so that specialist capacity concentrates on the complex ones, with the authors observing that 'reducing tertiary referral loads by even a third could significantly shorten wait times for interventions to begin'." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-086" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'children with ASD', 'patients with ASD', 'a child diagnosed with ASD', 'ASD positive' and 'ASD negative' as output labels. Person-first throughout, with the acronym used as an attribute rather than as a noun for people. The framing is diagnostic and health-systems oriented rather than pathologising: the problems named are wait times, referral bottlenecks, diagnostic disparity and specialist capacity, not deficits in children. The paper is notably careful about the population it serves, repeatedly framing autism as one possibility among many overlapping developmental presentations - 'ASD can co-occur with and/or share many overlapping features of other disorders diagnosed in childhood' - and treating the false positives as children who 'had a non-ASD developmental-behavioral pediatric condition that could potentially benefit from similar early interventions as ASD', which is a clinically constructive reading of an error rather than a defensive one." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-086" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Consumer smartphones for caregivers plus web portals for clinicians and analysts - no specialised hardware. The substantive resource requirement is human: video analysts must hold at least a master's degree in psychology, occupational therapy, physical therapy, speech-language pathology, special education or a related field with specific autism training, and have at least five years of professional or clinical experience with autistic children. They receive standardised training, must pass certification on previously unseen videos meeting minimum PPV, NPV and abstention-rate guarantees, and undergo ongoing performance review during the study. This is the element that most complicates the scalability claim, and the authors identify it themselves as a research priority: 'improving the scalability of the video component of the Device by decreasing reliance on human video analysts in subsequent generations of the algorithm'. The Device also requires smartphone access, which the authors note 'not all low-income American families may have', and functions only in English, with Mandarin and Spanish versions in development." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-086" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The most consequential fusion result in the corpus, though the fusion itself is inherited from prior work: 'previously published analysis of earlier algorithm iterations demonstrated that combining inputs significantly improved the performance of the algorithm'. What this trial adds is not a demonstration that fusion helps but a demonstration of what fusion plus abstention achieves under prospective conditions on the clinically relevant population. Two properties of the fusion deserve note. The three inputs are mutually blinded, so no channel is contaminated by another - the caregiver does not know what the clinician said, the video analyst does not know either, and none sees the output. And the channels are deliberately heterogeneous in vantage point rather than in modality, combining retrospective caregiver knowledge, naturalistic home observation and structured clinical observation, which is a different fusion logic from the modality-combining designs elsewhere in this review and is justified by reference to diagnostic guidelines rather than to information theory. The trial does not report per-input performance, so the marginal contribution of each channel in this cohort cannot be assessed - a gap relative to the fusion ablations reported by MacFarlane, Han and Liao." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-086" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Three mutually blinded inputs, deliberately mirroring the components of a standard evaluation: 'use of multimodular Device inputs is consistent with current guidelines and recommendations for an evaluation of ASD that include having both caregiver and clinician input, as well as a structured observation of the child'. The video component is the element that distinguishes it from a questionnaire battery, and the rationale given is behavioural rather than technical - clinicians assess 'within a time-constrained clinical encounter where the child may not display behavior characteristic of that seen in the home environment or may become newly behaviorally reactive due to change in environment', so caregiver-recorded home video supplies observation of the child in their ordinary setting. The practical constraints are modest and reported: two videos of 1.5 to 5 minutes, with in-app guidance on framing and lighting; a caregiver questionnaire taking a median 4 minutes 56 seconds; and a clinician questionnaire taking about 10 minutes after a 30-minute visit. Age-banded questionnaire versions differ slightly to reflect developmental trajectories, with the classifier trained to perform equivalently across both." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-086" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "npj Digital Medicine 5:57 (2022), a digital health journal published in partnership with Seoul National University Bundang Hospital. Authorship spans 20 affiliations including CHOC Children's, UC Irvine, Stanford, Nationwide Children's, Ohio State, University of Missouri, Arizona State, Children's National, and seven authors affiliated with Cognoa Inc., the device manufacturer, with the corresponding author reachable at a cognoa.com address and also holding a CHOC and UC Irvine appointment. The senior author list includes the Stanford investigator whose group also produced the crowdsourced speech study elsewhere in this corpus and who declares founding Cognoa there. The commercial relationship is transparent from the affiliations and the paper is framed as a regulatory submission, which explains both its methodological rigour - a pivotal trial must satisfy FDA review - and its limits, since the algorithm and code are proprietary and unavailable for independent scrutiny." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-086" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Six directions, all operationally concrete. Reduce dependence on human video analysts in future algorithm generations - the scalability bottleneck. Determine what training primary care clinicians need to use the Device confidently in practice, which is a human-factors question the trial did not address since it used a study protocol rather than routine deployment. Run a registrational study monitoring the stability of Device results against diagnosis over time, 'including establishing a diagnosis later in children who at the time of initial evaluation did not meet diagnostic criteria for ASD' - the follow-up that would establish whether indeterminate and negative outputs remain correct as children develop, and the single most important missing evidence for a tool used at 18-72 months. Power future studies for demographic subgroups. Confirm performance across intellectual levels. And obtain cost and reimbursement data to establish whether deployment would be equitable, with explicit reference to Medicaid sustainability and to families without smartphones or insurance." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-086" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:country_region "One assessment episode per child: caregiver questionnaire (median 4 min 56 s), two home videos of 1.5-5 minutes each scored by an analyst (median 10 min 54 s), and a 30-minute clinician visit with a q" ;
                    slr:study_design "Double-blinded, multi-site, prospective, active comparator cohort pivotal trial, prospectively registered on ClinicalTrials.gov (NCT04151290) before initiation" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Children aged 18-72 months for whom a caregiver or clinician had raised a developmental delay concern but who were not yet diagnosed" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: 122 of 425 completers (28.7%); comparison: 303 autism-negative completers, comprising 263 with at least one non-autism condition and 40 neurotypical" ] ;
            slr:section_f_methods_results [ a slr:MethodsAndResults ;
                    slr:key_results "In a prospectively registered, double-blinded, 14-site trial of 425 children aged 18-72 months referred with developmental concerns, a Device combining caregiver report, expert-scored home video and primary care clinician observation achieved PPV 80.8% and NPV 98.3% against blinded specialist consensus - but only for the 31.8% of children on which it was willing to render a verdict. The paper's mo",
                        "The Device returned a determinate output for 31.8% (135/425) of participants. Within that subset: PPV 80.8% (95% CI 70.3-88.8), NPV 98.3% (90.6-100), sensitivity 98.4% (91.6-100), specificity 78.9% (67.6-87.7). The confusion matrix gives 63 true positives, 15 false positives, 1 false negative and 56 true negatives among determinate cases. Critically, the paper reports what happens if abstention is" ;
                    slr:outcome "PPV and NPV as pre-specified primary endpoints with Clopper-Pearson 95% confidence intervals, plus sensitivity, specificity, determinate-result rate, a full three-by-three confusion matrix against a three-level reference standard, performance stratified by age, sex, race and ethnicity, parental educ" ] ;
            slr:section_g_synthesis [ a slr:SynthesisFields ;
                    slr:summary_of_findings "The Device returned a determinate output for 31.8% (135/425) of participants. Within that subset: PPV 80.8% (95% CI 70.3-88.8), NPV 98.3% (90.6-100), sensitivity 98.4% (91.6-100), specificity 78.9% (67.6-87.7). The confusion matrix gives 63 true positives, 15 false positives, 1 false negative and 56 true negatives among determinate cases. Critically, the paper reports what happens if abstention is; In a prospectively registered, double-blinded, 14-site trial of 425 children aged 18-72 months referred with developmental concerns, a Device combining caregiver report, expert-scored home video and primary care clinician observation achieved PPV 80.8% and NPV 98.3% against blinded specialist consensus - but only for the 31.8% of children on which it was willing to render a verdict. The paper's mo" ] ] ;
