            slr:created_at_time "2026-08-14T05:29:15.065734+00:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
            slr:full_text_source "user_supplied_pdf" ;
            slr:media_type "text/plain" ] ;
    slr:journal "IEEE Tr" ;
    slr:risk_of_bias_assessment [ a slr:RiskOfBiasAssessment ;
            slr:domain_judgments [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Index test" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "The index test is largely prespecified. The two anatomical regions were chosen a priori with stated literature rationales; the entropy parameters m = 2 and r = 0.15 sigma were taken from prior work with a supplementary sensitivity analysis; the 15 Hz filter cut-off, the 20-scale integration window and the 40% validity threshold are all justified from domain reasoning or cited sources rather than tuned; and no feature selection occurs. The primary analyses are hypothesis tests with confound adjustment rather than a fitted model. The one in-sample decision is the restriction of the secondary classification analysis to social movies, based on effect sizes observed in the same data." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Patient selection" ;
                    slr:judgment "unclear" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Recruitment is from consecutive well-child visits at four paediatric primary care clinics rather than from a specialist autism service, with autism identified prospectively at 9.2% prevalence through routine M-CHAT-R/F screening and ADOS-T evaluation - a substantially stronger sampling frame than the case-control designs that dominate this corpus, and the reason this domain is not rated high. Exclusion criteria are stated in full and are practical rather than diagnostic. Against this: the groups differ substantially in age (24.21 versus 20.62 months), sex (77.5% versus 48.9% male) and racial and ethnic composition (47.5% versus 77.7% White; 32.5% versus 7.3% Hispanic/Latino), none of which is adjusted for; caregiver education is very high (82.8% college degree); and per-movie analysed samples vary with the 40% validity threshold in ways shown only graphically." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Reference standard" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "A two-stage clinical pathway: universal caregiver-completed M-CHAT-R/F as part of routine care, followed by ADOS-Toddler evaluation by a child psychologist for any child screening positive or prompting caregiver or clinician concern. ADOS-T totals are reported (7.64 +/- 1.69). The comparison group is screened rather than assumed non-autistic, and the single discordant case - one typically developing child who screened positive, was evaluated and was not diagnosed - is reported explicitly. The residual limitation, insufficient to raise the rating, is that screen-negative children without expressed concern did not receive ADOS-T, so comparison-group status rests on a screening instrument." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Flow and timing" ;
                    slr:judgment "unclear" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "All participants received the index test and the reference standard within the same clinical encounter, and each contributes a single feature vector per movie, so leave-one-out cross-validation partitions participants cleanly. Missing data are handled explicitly inside the entropy computation with a stated rule and worked example, the 40% validity threshold is justified from prior literature, missing-data percentage is shown not to differ between groups, and it is entered as an ANCOVA covariate - unusually careful handling. Against this: per-movie analysed sample sizes are reported only in figures, no flow diagram accounts for how many of the 436 were excluded from each movie, and no class-handling procedure is described for the decision-tree analysis despite the reported precision and recall being inconsistent with the 40:396 ratio." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "unclear" ;
            slr:tool_used "QUADAS-2" ] .

<urn:slr:article:7adb257effec> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Advances in computational behavior analysis via artificial intelligence (AI) promise to improve mental healthcare services by providing clinicians with tools to assist diagnosis or measurement of treatment outcomes. This potential has spurred an increasing number of studies in which automated pipelines predict diagnoses of mental health conditions. However, a fundamental question remains unanswered: How do the predictions of the AI algorithms correspond and compare with the predictions of humans? This is a critical question if AI technology is to be used as an assistive tool, because the utility of an AI algorithm would be negligible if it provides little information beyond what clinicians can readily infer. In this paper, we compare the performance of 19 human raters (8 autism experts and 11 non-experts) and that of an AI algorithm in terms of predicting autism diagnosis from short (3-minute) videos of N = 42 participants in a naturalistic conversation. Results show that the AI algorithm achieves an average accuracy of 80.5%, which is comparable to that of clinicians with expertise in autism (83.1%) and clinical research staff without specialized expertise (78.3%). Critically, diagnoses that were inaccurately predicted by most humans (experts and non-experts, alike) were typically correctly predicted by AI. Our results highlight the potential of AI as an assistive tool that can augment clinician diagnostic decision-making." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Sariyanidi E; Zampella CJ; DeJardin E; Herrington JD; Schultz RT; Tunc B (2023). Comparison of Human Experts and AI in Predicting Autism from Facial Behavior. *Joint Proceedings of the ACM IUI 20*." ;
    dcterms:creator "DeJardin E",
        "Herrington JD",
        "Sariyanidi E",
        "Schultz RT",
        "Tunc B",
        "Zampella CJ" ;
    dcterms:title "Comparison of Human Experts and AI in Predicting Autism from Facial Behavior" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_6249bd63" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2023 ;
    slr:appraisal [ a slr:CriticalAppraisal ;
            slr:domain_1_sample [ a slr:SamplePopulationDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Handling of missing data and attrition" ;
                            slr:justification "Two of 44 enrolled participants excluded, with both reasons stated (absent consent for this specific set of experiments; data unavailable for processing), and the resulting 42 fully analysed. The consent-based exclusion indicates granular per-experiment consent." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Participant selection and spectrum representativeness" ;
                            slr:justification "Fifteen autistic adults, verbally fluent native English speakers with IQ comparable to controls, recruited at a specialist research centre and able to hold an unstructured conversation with a stranger. This is the cognitively able end of the adult spectrum, and no severity score is reported to characterise it further." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Confounder control" ;
                            slr:justification "Groups matched on chronological age, sex ratio, WASI-II full-scale IQ and verbal IQ, with a stated test of each - the most complete matching in this corpus. Native English fluency required of all participants. Confederates blind to diagnostic status and unaware of the dependent variables, so elicitation was not differentially shaped. Facial identity is explicitly modelled and removed at the feature stage." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Comparison group adequacy" ;
                            slr:justification "Neurotypical controls recruited specifically as such and described to raters as clear cases. No clinical comparison group. The authors state the limitation explicitly and call for future work on neurodiverse samples, which is the correct diagnosis of the problem but does not remedy it here." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "high" ] ;
            slr:domain_2_data_collection [ a slr:DataCollectionDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reference standard quality" ;
                            slr:justification "Clinical Best Estimate informed by ADOS-2 Module 4 and adhering to DSM-5, established prospectively as part of the study rather than inherited from records. Controls deliberately recruited as neurotypical. The only gaps are the absence of a reported ADOS-2 severity score and of a consensus reliability figure." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Measurement standardisation" ;
                            slr:justification "Identical dual-camera device, resolution, frame rate and placement for every participant; a semi-structured paradigm with specified confederate training rules, timing constraints and a verbatim prompt; camera parameters supplied to the 3DMM fitting to decouple expression from pose. No group-dependent acquisition differences." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Index test definition and prespecification" ;
                            slr:justification "Linear SVM at default C = 1, no feature selection, one hyperparameter tuned in a nested loop over four values. The representation choices - identity removal, localised basis, exclusion of nose and cheek components and of FACS units - are argued a priori on stated principles rather than selected for performance." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Outcome measurement blinding" ;
                            slr:justification "The pipeline is fully automated and label-free at inference. Confederates were blind to diagnostic status and unaware of the dependent variables. Human raters were necessarily unblinded to the task but were told only that cases were clear-cut, not which was which, and viewed videos in randomised order with a single viewing and no revisiting." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "low" ] ;
            slr:domain_3_methods [ a slr:MethodsAnalysisDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Sample size adequacy for the modelling" ;
                            slr:justification "Forty-two participants and 6,272 features. The linear model, fixed regularisation and absence of feature selection mitigate the dimensionality, and the nested participant-level scheme means the estimate is honest, but one participant is 2.4 accuracy points and the error-overlap finding rests on five hard cases with no uncertainty quantification." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Performance estimation and chance level" ;
                            slr:justification "Accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, PPV and NPV are all reported - the most complete metric set here - and per-participant error analyses go beyond summary statistics. But no majority-class baseline (64.3%), permutation test, AUC or confidence interval is given, and the sensitivity of 0.55 is stated once and never discussed while the abstract quotes only accuracy." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data splitting and leakage control" ;
                            slr:justification "One feature vector per participant makes the split unit the person by construction. Nested cross-validation with 10 folds repeated 100 times with shuffled participant order addresses fold-assignment variance. The 3DMM identity-removal step additionally strips the individual-appearance information that drives leakage elsewhere in this corpus." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Preprocessing and feature selection nesting" ;
                            slr:justification "No feature selection is performed, so selection bias cannot arise despite 6,272 features. The single tuned hyperparameter is optimised in the inner loop. Regularisation is left at default rather than searched." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "unclear" ] ;
            slr:domain_4_bias_transparency [ a slr:BiasTransparencyDomain ;
                    slr:items [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Data, code and protocol availability" ;
                            slr:justification "No code, no data, no preregistration, and the modified localised expression basis - the study's most reusable artefact - is not released. The reliance on published components (3DI) partially offsets this for reconstruction but not for verification." ;
                            slr:rating "High" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Ethics, consent and conflicts" ;
                            slr:justification "IRB approval from the Children's Hospital of Philadelphia is stated, and the exclusion of two participants for not consenting to this specific set of experiments evidences granular per-experiment consent - a strong governance signal for identifiable facial video. Four NIH grants are itemised. No competing-interests statement appears in the retrieved text." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Acknowledgement of limitations" ;
                            slr:justification "Six substantive limitations are stated, including several that comparable papers omit entirely: the neurotypical-only comparison group with an explicit call for neurodiverse samples, the adult-only sample, the monadic rather than dyadic analysis, the partial interpretability with a specific statement of where it stops, the sample-size constraint given as the actual reason for the modelling choice, and the need for user research before clinical benefit can be claimed. The unaddressed items are the 0.55 sensitivity, the missing chance baseline, and the unmatched information available to human raters." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Completeness of methods reporting" ;
                            slr:justification "The paradigm (including confederate training rules and verbatim prompt), hardware, 3DMM method and its rationale, basis construction, exact cross-correlation scheme with lag convention and overlap, feature count derivation, classifier and its fixed C, hyperparameter grid, and validation scheme are all fully specified - as is the entire human-rating protocol. Table 1 demographics were not recoverable from the retrieved text." ;
                            slr:rating "Low" ],
                        [ a slr:AppraisalItem ;
                            slr:item_text "Reporting of results and uncertainty" ;
                            slr:justification "Five operating characteristics for the model and three levels of human aggregation, plus two per-participant analyses with a quantified correlation. But no confidence intervals, no AUC, no significance test on the human-versus-AI comparison or the error-overlap claim, and the abstract and conclusion foreground accuracy while omitting the 0.55 sensitivity." ;
                            slr:rating "Unclear" ] ;
                    slr:overall_concern "unclear" ] ] ;
    slr:charting_record [ a slr:ChartingRecord ;
            slr:custom_charting_answer [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Five directions, all following directly from what the study found. Examine which behaviours drive a predicted diagnosis, framed not as a nicety but as a precondition for clinical use - 'clinicians should understand how the AI algorithm makes a prediction before taking this prediction into account' - and requiring more granular expression models than the current localised basis provides. Extend to younger participants, since early diagnosis is where the developmental benefit lies. Move from monadic to dyadic analysis by incorporating the confederate's behaviour, which the recording setup already captures and the paradigm's interactive nature makes theoretically apt. Test on neurodiverse samples rather than autism-versus-neurotypical, the authors' own diagnosis of their key external-validity gap. And conduct user research to determine whether clinician decisions actually improve when AI assistance is available - the step that would convert a demonstrated non-overlap of errors into demonstrated clinical value. Absent from the list: raising sensitivity, external validation on an independent cohort, and analysis of the bored condition." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.9" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-100" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Interpretability is treated as a design constraint rather than a post-hoc report, and the study is the clearest example in this corpus of a modelling choice being made to preserve semantic traceability. The authors note that the 3DI method's PCA expression basis 'limits the interpretability as PCA components are not localized - we cannot associate any PCA component with a specific facial region', and therefore rebuild the basis with 60 localised components before any modelling occurs. The linear SVM then makes weights directly readable. The importance analysis is done at two levels and, critically, against the right baseline: individual top-10 features are named, and the four anatomical categories are compared with their proportion of the full 6,272-feature pool, so the conclusion that mouth features matter is not an artefact of there being many mouth features (25.5% of the pool, 40% of the top 10) and the pose finding is stronger than it looks (5.3% of the pool, 13.3% of the top 10). Findings are then related to independent literature on blinking, nodding, social orienting and backchannel feedback rather than left as bare weights. The authors are equally explicit about the ceiling: they cannot distinguish upper lip from mouth corner, a fuller account would require 3D versions of localised bases, and 'further analysis to uncover why these features are important is beyond the scope of this study'. Finally, the discussion argues that interpretability is a precondition for the assistive use case the paper proposes - a clinician cannot rationally weigh a dissenting AI opinion without knowing which behaviours produced it - which correctly makes explanation a functional requirement rather than a courtesy." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-100" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Among the best-specified methods in this corpus, with the usual artefact gap. Reproducible from the text: the CASS protocol including confederate selection, training rules, timing constraints and the verbatim prompt; the camera hardware, resolution, frame rate and placement; the choice of 3DI with its stated rationale; the construction of the 60-component localised basis and the principled exclusion of nose and cheek components; the exact windowed cross-correlation scheme, including 50% window overlap, forward-lag-only convention and the explicit note that the pair (i,j) is therefore not equivalent to (j,i); the derivation of the 6,272 feature count; the SVM kernel and the fixed C = 1; the single tuned hyperparameter and its four candidate values; and the nested 10-fold cross-validation repeated 100 times with shuffling. The human-rating protocol is equally complete - head-and-neck crops, side-by-side layout, audio removed, randomised order, one viewing per video, no revisiting, completed in one to three sessions. Not available: code, data, the modified expression basis, preregistration, and the Table 1 demographics in the retrieved text. As a workshop paper (CEUR/ACM IUI joint proceedings) it is also shorter than a journal article, which accounts for some of the compression." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.6" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-100" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The retrieved text states that the groups did not differ significantly on mean chronological age, full-scale IQ, verbal IQ or sex ratio, with the statistics themselves in a table not recoverable from the extracted text. That set of matched variables is nonetheless the most complete in this corpus for a study of this size, and two elements are worth emphasising because their absence undermines so many other studies charted here. IQ was measured with a standardised instrument (WASI-II) in both groups and matched on both full-scale and verbal estimates - so the classification cannot be attributed to a cognitive-ability gap, which is an uncontrolled confound in the majority of studies in this review. And sex ratio was matched rather than left uncontrolled or eliminated by sampling only boys. All participants were native and fluent English speakers by inclusion, which removes language proficiency as a confound in a conversational paradigm but also narrows generalisability. What is not reported in the retrieved text: race or ethnicity, socioeconomic status, comorbidity, medication, and - notably given that ADOS-2 was administered - any severity score. The sample is adults, cognitively able enough to complete an unstructured 3-minute conversation with a stranger, and it is a research-registry population at a specialist centre rather than a community sample." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-100" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Facial movement and head movement during spontaneous conversation, and specifically their temporal coordination. The behavioural target is defined broadly and deliberately: the authors reject restricting analysis to the six basic emotions on the grounds that 'other kinds of facial movements (e.g., communicative expressions, speech-related mouth movements) are also important for diagnosing autism', and so quantify all observable facial motion via a morphable model rather than an emotion taxonomy. The coordination framing is grounded in the diagnostic instrument itself: the paper notes that ADOS criteria evaluate how an individual combines speech with gestures and eye contact with facial expression, and reasons by analogy that coordination within the facial channel - across regions, and between face and head - should be similarly informative. Empirically this is borne out. Head-pose-to-pose correlations carry the greatest average classifier weight, pose-eye correlations feature prominently in the top 10, and mouth-related correlations account for six of the top 10 including three mouth-mouth pairs; relative to their share of the 6,272-feature pool, pose features are about 2.5 times over-represented in the top 10 and mouth features about 1.6 times. The authors connect these to established findings on blinking and nodding as conversational signals and on head and eye movement as indices of social attention. Two behaviours present in the recording are excluded by design: speech, because audio was removed to keep the human comparison fair, and the confederate's behaviour, because the analysis is monadic." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-100" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cross-sectional, single session per participant, no follow-up and no test-retest assessment of the coordination features. Ethics governance is well handled: the study was approved by the Institutional Review Board of the Children's Hospital of Philadelphia, and consent was evidently granular rather than blanket, since two of the 44 enrolled participants were excluded specifically because of 'their lack of consent for this particular set of experiments' - a per-experiment consent regime that is a positive marker of data governance in a study involving identifiable facial video. Confederates were blinded to diagnostic status. The human raters were not blinded in the ordinary sense - they were told that every participant was either a confirmed autism case or a deliberately recruited clear control - which is appropriate for calibrating a benchmark but does mean their task was easier than a real screening decision. Funding from four NIH grants is fully itemised. No competing-interests statement appears in the retrieved text, which is a gap given that the work develops a potential clinical tool." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-100" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "No cross-sensor fusion - a single camera stream - but the study is one of the most informative in this corpus about fusion of a different kind, because its entire feature construction is a within-modality coordination analysis. The 56 time series (53 localised facial expression components plus yaw, pitch and roll) are combined pairwise by windowed cross-correlation with directional lag, producing 6,272 features that encode not what the face did but how its parts moved together in time. The results locate the discriminative signal squarely in the cross-channel pairs: the highest-weighted features on average are pose-pose, and pose-eye combinations appear among the top individual features - so the signal is in how head movement coordinates with itself and with eye-region movement, not in either channel alone. This is a concrete demonstration that the useful information in multimodal behaviour can lie in the coordination structure rather than in the constituent signals, which is a stronger and more testable claim than the generic 'fusion improved accuracy' reported by most multimodal studies here. Two limits are stated by the authors. Feature count grows quadratically in the number of time series, which is why Facial Action Coding System units were not added and why nose and cheek components were dropped - a rare instance of a paper declining extra features on principled dimensionality grounds rather than adding everything available. And the analysis is monadic: the confederate's synchronised video was recorded by the same device and discarded, so interpersonal synchrony, which is the natural extension of a within-person coordination analysis and is theoretically central to conversation, remains untested and is named as future work." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ3.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-100" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The Contextual Assessment of Social Skills, slightly modified - a semi-structured assessment of conversational ability designed to mimic a real-life first-time encounter. Each participant held two 3-minute face-to-face conversations with two different confederates: an interested condition, in which the confederate engaged verbally and non-verbally, and a bored condition, in which the confederate signalled disengagement through one-word answers, few follow-up questions, neutral affect and reduced eye contact and gesture. Only the interested condition is analysed here. The confederate protocol is specified to an unusual degree: 10 confederates (3 male, 7 female, all native English speakers, undergraduate or BA-level research staff), blind to diagnostic status and unaware of the dependent variables, trained to speak no more than 50% of the time, to wait 10 s before initiating and 5 s before re-initiating after a pause, and otherwise to converse naturally; the prompt given to both parties is quoted verbatim. That combination - a live, unscripted, genuinely social task with a standardised and blinded partner - is close to the ideal for this literature, since it preserves naturalism without letting the partner's behaviour vary with what they believe about the participant. Two design choices constrain the result. Audio was discarded from both the AI and human conditions, so speech content, prosody and turn-taking timing - all diagnostically informative and all captured on the recording - are excluded by design in order to make the human-AI comparison fair. And only the participant's video enters the model, an approach the authors name explicitly as monadic and identify as a limitation: 'even if the context of the conversation is dyadic, our AI-based analysis is not dyadic since it discards the information from the confederate'. The bored condition, which would test whether the coordination signature survives a change in the partner's behaviour, was collected and left unanalysed." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-100" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A linear support vector machine at the default regularisation C = 1, over 6,272 engineered coordination features. The choice of a simple model is argued rather than defaulted: deep learning was rejected because 'the sample size is insufficient for reliably training deep learning models from scratch', and transfer learning was rejected because, to the authors' knowledge, no publicly available pre-trained model is directly applicable to the problem, so fine-tuning a classification head was also unavailable. Leaving C at its default further shrinks the search space, so the entire modelling procedure has exactly one tuned quantity - the cross-correlation window - and that is tuned inside a nested loop. The result is a pipeline with very few researcher degrees of freedom, which is the main reason its 80.5% is more credible than several higher figures elsewhere in this corpus. The intellectual weight of the paper sits in the representation rather than the classifier: the modelling contribution is the 3DMM-based identity-removed localised expression basis and the directional cross-correlation feature construction, with the SVM as a deliberately transparent read-out." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-100" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Single site: the Center for Autism Research at the Children's Hospital of Philadelphia, with the University of Pennsylvania. This is a specialist research centre, and both arms of the study reflect that - the participants are a research-registry sample assessed by Clinical Best Estimate on site, and the 19 raters are the centre's own doctoral clinicians and BA-level staff. The rater sample is therefore an unusually strong human benchmark (these are people who assess autistic adults professionally) and simultaneously a narrow one (they share an institution, a training culture and, plausibly, prior exposure to similar video material). The recording setting is a laboratory room with a floor-standing dual-camera device between the two conversation partners, and the paradigm is a semi-structured assessment rather than a clinical encounter. The recruitment geometry is case-control at 35.7% prevalence with deliberately clear-cut controls, which the authors flag as the key generalisation limit. Against that, the paradigm itself is deliberately designed for scalability outside such settings: the conversation partner is a non-clinician, the interaction lasts three minutes, and the only equipment is a camera - which is the basis for the claim that this could serve as a screening procedure." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-100" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Published 2023 (author manuscript available in PMC from November 2023), from the Center for Autism Research at the Children's Hospital of Philadelphia and the University of Pennsylvania, funded by four NIH grants (R01MH118327, R01MH122599, 5P50HD105354-02, R21HD102078). The framing is deliberately corrective rather than promotional. The opening premise is that psychiatry, unlike the rest of medicine, relies almost exclusively on observational and paper-and-pencil instruments, and that computational behaviour analysis could supply the missing instrumentation - with the explicit acknowledgement that clinical assessment reliability is currently limited, citing the DSM-5 field trial kappa of 0.69 for autism. The central rhetorical move is to identify a gap the field has left open: 'to our knowledge, no study has directly compared AI algorithms and human raters with respect to overall predictive capacity or specific decisions on individual cases'. That reframing - from 'can AI classify autism?' to 'does AI tell a clinician anything they did not already know?' - is the paper's real contribution and is the question most of this corpus does not ask. The positioning is consistently augmentative: AI as an assistive tool that could prompt a clinician to re-evaluate when the two disagree, with the caveat that this is only workable if the algorithm can say why." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-100" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The stated limitations are unusually well chosen and include several that comparable papers omit: the sample is too small for deep learning, and this is given as the actual reason for the modelling choice rather than left implicit; the interpretability achieved is partial, and the authors specify exactly where it stops (they cannot resolve upper lip from mouth corner); the comparison group is neurotypical, and the conclusion states that a real patient 'may not be NT but may not have ASD either', calling for neurodiverse samples; the participants are adults when early diagnosis is where the clinical value lies; the analysis is monadic and discards the confederate; and whether clinicians actually benefit requires user research not yet performed. What is not addressed matters too. The sensitivity of 0.55 appears in a single sentence alongside three other operating characteristics and is never revisited, yet it is arguably the study's most consequential number: a tool that misses 45% of autistic adults cannot function as the screening technology the conclusion proposes, at least not in the direction the conclusion proposes it - though it could function well in the opposite direction, since NPV 0.79 and specificity 0.95 make a positive prediction informative. The abstract and conclusion report only accuracy. No majority-class baseline is computed, though at 15 versus 27 the floor is 64.3%, so the model's 80.5% represents a 16-point gain rather than the 30-point gain a reader assuming balance would infer. The human and AI conditions are not strictly matched - raters saw participant and confederate side by side while the model saw only the participant, so if anything the humans had more information, which strengthens rather than weakens the paper's conclusion but should be stated. Raters were also told that every case was clear-cut. No confidence intervals accompany any figure, and with 42 participants one case is 2.4 percentage points. And the bored condition was collected and never used." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.7" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-100" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "A single 6,272-dimensional vector per participant, built in three steps. First, frame-wise 3D morphable model fitting via 3DI, which jointly models identity, pose and expression so that the person's facial identity is estimated and removed rather than left to confound the expression signal - the authors cite identity bias explicitly, and this is one of very few studies in this corpus that treats individual identity as something to be subtracted rather than as an accidental source of accuracy. Second, replacement of the model's default PCA expression basis with 60 purpose-built localised components, of which 53 are retained (nose and cheek discarded), so that each dimension corresponds to a nameable facial region; concatenated with yaw, pitch and roll, this gives a 56 x T matrix per video. Third, windowed cross-correlation on every ordered pair of the 56 series, using 50% overlapping windows of length Tw and permitting only forward lag on the second series so that direction is preserved and the (i,j) pair differs from (j,i); each pair contributes the mean and standard deviation of its maximal cross-correlation across windows. The representation is therefore about temporal relationships rather than about static or averaged expression content, and it deliberately discards absolute expression magnitude, which is exactly the quantity most vulnerable to identity and recording differences. The dimensionality - 6,272 features for 42 participants - is extreme, mitigated by a linear model, fixed regularisation, no feature selection and participant-level nested cross-validation." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-100" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The stated goal is not primarily to build a better classifier but to answer a prior question the field has left open: how do AI predictions correspond to human ones? The framing is explicit and is the paper's organising logic - 'the utility of an AI algorithm would be negligible if it provides little information beyond what clinicians can readily infer' - so the study is designed as a benchmark rather than a demonstration. Nineteen raters (8 doctoral-level autism experts, 11 non-experts) and a computer-vision pipeline were given the same 3-minute silent videos of 42 adults in casual conversation. Findings: AI accuracy 80.5%, expert clinicians 83.1%, non-experts 78.3%, all humans 80.3% - so the model performs at approximately expert level on this evidence. The operating characteristics show why accuracy alone is misleading: PPV 0.86, NPV 0.79, specificity 0.95 and sensitivity 0.55, meaning the classifier is highly conservative and detects only just over half the autistic adults. The paper's real result is the error analysis. Of five participants misclassified by a majority of human raters, four were correctly classified by the model, including the hardest case, which only 21% of raters got right. Per-participant accuracy of humans and AI correlated at just rho = 0.35, falling to 0.19 once the participants nearly all raters got right are removed, and the lower-left quadrant of the scatter - hard for both - is essentially empty. Feature weights point to head pose and its coordination with eye movement, and to mouth-related coordination, as the dominant signal, with both categories over-represented in the top-weighted features relative to their share of the feature pool." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-100" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Author keywords: autism; assistive healthcare technologies; digital phenotyping. The choice is revealing. 'Digital phenotyping' locates the work in a measurement tradition rather than a diagnostic one, and 'assistive healthcare technologies' states the intended role explicitly - neither 'diagnosis', 'screening', 'classification', 'machine learning' nor 'deep learning' appears, even though the paper performs classification with machine learning. Terms carried through the body text include computational behavior analysis, 3D morphable model, windowed cross-correlation, monadic versus dyadic analysis, Clinical Best Estimate, Contextual Assessment of Social Skills, and neurodiverse samples." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-100" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The strongest reference standard encountered in this review. Diagnostic status was 'confirmed as part of this study' - established prospectively rather than inherited from records - using the Clinical Best Estimate process, informed by ADOS-2 Module 4 and adhering to DSM-5 criteria. Clinical Best Estimate is the accepted research gold standard precisely because it integrates a standardised observational instrument, developmental history and clinical judgement rather than resting on any single source, and Module 4 is the correct ADOS-2 module for verbally fluent adolescents and adults. Neurotypical status was not merely the absence of a diagnosis: controls were 'recruited specifically as neurotypical controls', and raters were told they were 'clear cases of individuals without autism'. That framing is a genuine strength for a benchmark study - it means the human raters' 80.3% accuracy is not depressed by ambiguous controls - but it is simultaneously the study's central external-validity limit, since the two groups are drawn from the well-separated ends of a distribution that a real screening population fills continuously. The authors say so themselves in the conclusion. Two further points: the ADOS-2 was administered but no calibrated severity score is reported, so the autistic sample's position on the spectrum is unquantified; and the conversation confederates were blind to diagnostic status and unaware of the dependent variables, so the elicitation itself was not differentially shaped by knowledge of group membership - a control that few conversational studies in this corpus implement." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.4" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-100" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "CEUR Workshop Proceedings volume 3359, the ITAH (Intelligent Technologies for Assistive Healthcare) track of the Joint Proceedings of the ACM IUI 2023 Workshops, pages 48-57 - a workshop paper at a human-computer interaction venue rather than a clinical or machine-learning journal. The venue choice fits the contribution: the question of whether AI predictions add anything beyond human judgement is an interaction question, and the paper's framing around assistive rather than autonomous use is native to the IUI community. It also explains some of the reporting compression, since workshop papers are short. The article is deposited as an HHS Public Access author manuscript, reflecting the NIH funding, and is available under CC BY 4.0." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ5.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-100" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Recording used a purpose-built in-house device with two 1080p cameras at 30 fps mounted back to back on a floor stand between the participant and the confederate, so both parties are captured simultaneously from a fixed and identical geometry. Cognitive assessment used the WASI-II. Diagnosis used ADOS-2 Module 4. The analysis stack is the study's technical core and is described precisely: 3DI, a 3D morphable model fitting method chosen for two specific properties - it learns facial identity from multiple frames, so identity bias can be modelled and explained away rather than left to contaminate the expression signal, and it accepts camera parameters as input, which the authors state is critical for decoupling expression from head pose. The default PCA expression basis was replaced with a purpose-built basis of 60 localised components; 7 corresponding to nose and cheek were discarded, leaving 53 expression dimensions plus 3 rotation angles. Classification used a linear SVM at default C. The decision to reject Facial Action Coding System units is argued rather than assumed: the analysis needs continuous intensities for cross-correlation, automated AU detectors supply intensity for only a small number of AUs, and adding AUs would inflate a feature space that already grows quadratically in the number of time series." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ2.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-100" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Cognitive ability was measured properly: WASI-II full-scale and verbal IQ estimates were obtained for the whole sample and compared between groups, with no significant difference. This is the correct handling and it removes the single most pervasive confound in this literature. Language ability is indexed by verbal IQ and by the requirement that all participants be native and fluent English speakers, which matters for a task whose entire stimulus is spontaneous conversation. Autism severity, however, is not reported. ADOS-2 Module 4 was administered as part of the diagnostic process, so calibrated severity scores were almost certainly available, yet none appears - so it is impossible to say where the 15 autistic adults sit on the spectrum, whether the 45% the model failed to detect were the less severely affected, or whether the four participants that humans misjudged but the model caught differed systematically in severity. That last question is the one the paper's own error-overlap analysis makes most interesting, and severity data would have gone a long way towards answering it. No adaptive-functioning, comorbidity or motor measure is reported either." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-100" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "'Individuals with autism spectrum disorder (ASD)' and 'adults with autism' predominate, paired with 'neurotypical (NT) individuals' rather than the more common 'typically developing' or, worse, 'normal'. 'Neurotypical' is a term originating in the autistic community and its use here, in a paper from a major autism research centre, is a meaningful marker: it frames the comparison group as one neurotype among others rather than as a standard of normality. The conclusion goes further, calling for future work 'in predicting ASD diagnosis from neurodiverse samples' - importing 'neurodiverse' as a technical descriptor of a clinically mixed population, which is both an accurate usage and an unusual one in this literature. Clinical description is measured throughout: autism is 'defined by observable behavioral cues - what a person does well or not well, too little or too much', its core traits are 'observable differences in social communication, social reciprocity, nonverbal communication, and relationships', and the word 'differences' rather than 'deficits' is used where most papers in this corpus use the latter. The framing of purpose is also non-replacement: the stated aim is to establish whether AI adds anything beyond clinician judgement, on the explicit premise that 'clinicians would have little interest in or benefit from incorporating AI algorithms if their decisions - and errors - highly overlap with their own'. There is no autistic-community involvement statement." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.2" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-100" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "Forty-four adults enrolled (17 autistic, 27 neurotypical), of whom two were excluded because they had not consented to this particular set of experiments or their data could not be processed, giving a final sample of 42: 15 autistic and 27 neurotypical, i.e. 35.7% autism prevalence. All were native and fluent English speakers. A second sample is central to the design and is reported with equal care: 19 human raters, comprising 8 expert clinicians (doctoral-level, with extensive autism training at the Center for Autism Research) and 11 non-experts (mostly BA-level psychology staff with some familiarity with autism but no specialised training), all of whom rated all 42 videos. A third group also participates without being analysed: 10 conversation confederates (3 male, 7 female, all native English speakers, undergraduate or BA-level research assistants), semi-randomly assigned by availability and clinical judgement, and blind to participant diagnostic status. The unit of analysis for the model is the participant - one 6,272-dimensional vector each - which is why no within-participant leakage is possible." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ1.1" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-100" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The strongest validation design encountered so far in this review, with one important caveat about how the result should be read. Each participant contributes exactly one feature vector, so the unit of splitting is the person by construction and the segment-level leakage that inflates most studies in this corpus is structurally impossible. Validation is nested cross-validation with a single tuned hyperparameter, the correlation window Tw over four candidate values, optimised inside the inner loop; the SVM's regularisation is left at its default rather than tuned, further reducing researcher degrees of freedom; and no feature selection is performed at all, so selection bias cannot arise despite 6,272 features. The outer evaluation is 10-fold cross-validation repeated 100 times with participant order shuffled between repetitions, which addresses fold-assignment variance - a source of instability that very few studies here even acknowledge. The identity-modelling step deserves separate credit as leakage control of a different kind: because the 3DMM estimates and removes facial identity, the representation is explicitly stripped of the individual-appearance information that, elsewhere in this corpus, silently drives high accuracy. The caveat is the base rate and the operating point. At 15 autistic and 27 neurotypical participants, majority-class prediction yields 64.3%, so 80.5% is a 16-point improvement rather than the 30 points a reader assuming balance might infer, and no permutation test or confidence interval is offered. The class imbalance also shapes the classifier: at sensitivity 0.55 and specificity 0.95 the model has learned to be conservative, so its accuracy is carried disproportionately by correctly identifying neurotypical participants. None of this undermines the study's actual claim - which concerns the non-overlap of AI and human errors, evaluated per participant - but it does mean the headline accuracy should not be read as evidence of a strong autism detector." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.5" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-100" ],
                [ a slr:ChartingQuestionAnswer ;
                    slr:answer_text "The most complete metric set in this corpus for a study of this type. The AI model is characterised by accuracy (80.5%), sensitivity (0.55), specificity (0.95), positive predictive value (0.86) and negative predictive value (0.79) - reporting PPV and NPV alongside sensitivity and specificity is rare here and is the reporting that lets a reader see the model's actual clinical shape, namely a conservative rule whose positive calls are trustworthy and whose negative calls are not. Human performance is reported at three levels of aggregation: all raters (80.3%), experts (83.1%) and non-experts (78.3%). Beyond summary metrics, the study reports two per-participant analyses that carry more information than any accuracy figure: a table listing the five participants misclassified by a majority of human raters together with the AI's prediction for each, and a scatter plot of per-participant human accuracy against per-participant AI accuracy with the correlation quantified (rho = 0.35, and rho = 0.19 after excluding participants that 95%+ of raters classified correctly). Missing: AUC, confidence intervals, and any statistical test of the human-versus-AI difference or of the error-overlap claim - the rho values and the empty lower-left quadrant are reported descriptively, with no significance test or bootstrap, and with 42 participants and five hard cases the error-overlap finding rests on a small number of observations." ;
                    slr:question_id "RQ4.3" ;
                    slr:source_id "S-100" ] ;
            slr:section_b_design [ a slr:StudyDesign ;
                    slr:study_design "Cross-sectional diagnostic-classification study with an embedded human-benchmark comparison. The distinguishing design feature is that the same 3-minute silent videos were given to…" ] ;
            slr:section_c_primary_sample [ a slr:PrimarySample ;
                    slr:population_studied "Autism spectrum disorder" ;
                    slr:sample_size_text "ASD/index: ; comparison: 27" ] ] ;
